// Professeur Camille: hand-written guided lessons (generated by build_tutor_lessons.py)
window.TUTOR_LESSONS = {"1-1": {"stageId": "1-1", "title": "Your very first French: hello, your name, where you live", "goal": "By the end you can say hello, give your name, ask how someone is, say where you live and count to 10.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Bonjour» ! I'm Camille, your French teacher from Lyon. This is your very first lesson, so we'll go gently, one small step at a time. You're going to do great!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Let's start with a guess. «bon» means 'good' and «jour» means 'day'. So what do you think «bonjour» means?", "choices": ["Good night", "Hello (literally 'good day')", "Thank you"], "answer": "Hello (literally 'good day')", "right": "Exactly! «bon» + «jour» = 'good day', and French people use it to say hello to anyone, at any time of the day.", "wrong": "Look at the two parts: «bon» (good) + «jour» (day) = 'good day'. That's how French people say hello: «bonjour»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation: «bonjour» sounds like 'bohn-ZHOOR'. The «n» is hummed through your nose, not fully said, and «j» is soft like the 's' in 'measure'. With friends you can say «salut» ('sa-LU'): the final «t» is silent!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "To give your name, say «je m'appelle» + your name. It sounds like 'zhuh ma-PELL' and literally means 'I call myself'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Bonjour, je m'appelle Camille.", "en": "Hello, my name is Camille.", "note": "You can also say «je suis Camille» (I am Camille) or «mon nom est Camille» (my name is Camille)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Your turn! Write in French: My name is Tom.", "answer": "Je m'appelle Tom.", "accept": ["je m'appelle tom", "je m'appelle tom.", "je m’appelle tom", "je m appelle tom", "je suis tom", "mon nom est tom"], "hint": "Starts with «Je m'...»", "right": "Parfait ! «Je m'appelle» + your name: you just introduced yourself in French!", "wrong": "We say «Je m'appelle» + name, with an apostrophe: «me» shortens to «m'» before a vowel. So: «Je m'appelle Tom.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "French has two words for 'you'. To a friend, ask «Comment tu t'appelles ?»; to a stranger, someone older or a group, ask «Comment vous appelez-vous ?» (the «s» of «vous» is silent: 'voo')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You meet your new boss for the first time. How do you politely ask her name?", "choices": ["Comment tu t'appelles ?", "Comment vous appelez-vous ?", "Je m'appelle ?"], "answer": "Comment vous appelez-vous ?", "right": "Très bien ! A new boss is someone you don't know well, so we use the polite «vous».", "wrong": "«tu» is only for friends, family and children. For a boss or a stranger, use the polite «vous»: «Comment vous appelez-vous ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "To ask 'How are you?' casually, say «Comment ça va ?» or just «Ça va ?». The little hook under the «ç» (a cedilla) makes it sound like 's': 'sa va'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A friend asks you «Ça va ?» and you feel good. What's the best answer?", "choices": ["Ça va bien, merci. Et toi ?", "Au revoir.", "Non, ça va mal."], "answer": "Ça va bien, merci. Et toi ?", "right": "Bravo ! «Ça va bien» = I'm fine, «merci» = thank you, «et toi ?» = and you? Very natural!", "wrong": "«Au revoir» means goodbye and «ça va mal» means you're NOT okay. If you feel good, say «Ça va bien, merci. Et toi ?» (I'm fine, thanks. And you?)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "To say where you live: «J'habite à» + a city, and «J'habite en» + most countries, like «en France» or «en Angleterre». The «h» is silent: 'zha-BEET'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I live in France'?", "choices": ["J'habite à France.", "J'habite en France.", "J'habite au France."], "answer": "J'habite en France.", "right": "Excellent ! «France» is a country, so we use «en». «à» is for cities: «J'habite à Paris».", "wrong": "«à» is for cities («à Londres», «à Paris»). Most countries take «en»: «J'habite en France». (A few masculine countries take «au», like «au Japon», and plural ones take «aux», like «aux États-Unis».)"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Numbers 1 to 10: «un, deux, trois, quatre, cinq, six, sept, huit, neuf, dix». Watch out: «sept» sounds like 'set' (silent p) and «huit» sounds like 'weet'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which number is «huit»?", "choices": ["4", "8", "9"], "answer": "8", "right": "Yes! «huit» ('weet') is 8. Great listening memory!", "wrong": "4 is «quatre» and 9 is «neuf». «huit», pronounced 'weet', is 8."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «bonjour» / «salut» to say hello, «je m'appelle…» for your name, «ça va ?» for how are you, «j'habite à» + city, «en» + country. You already know so much!"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with your own name and city: «Bonjour, je m'appelle… J'habite à… Ça va ?»", "fr": "Bonjour, je m'appelle Tom. J'habite à Londres. Ça va ?"}]}, "1-2": {"stageId": "1-2", "title": "Polite words & ways to say goodbye", "goal": "By the end you can say please, thank you, sorry and goodbye in the right situation.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Bienvenue» ! That means 'welcome', and it sounds like 'bee-yan-vuh-NU'. Today we learn the magic polite words and lots of ways to say goodbye."}, {"t": "say", "text": "You know «ça va bien» (I'm fine) and «ça va mal» (I'm not okay). In between, you can say «ça va pas mal» (not bad) or «comme ci comme ça» (so-so, literally 'like this like that')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone asks «Ça va ?» and you feel just so-so. What do you say?", "choices": ["Ça va bien.", "Comme ci comme ça.", "Bienvenue."], "answer": "Comme ci comme ça.", "right": "Yes! «Comme ci comme ça» = so-so. You can even wiggle your hand as you say it, like the French do.", "wrong": "«Ça va bien» means you feel fine, and «bienvenue» means welcome. For 'so-so', say «comme ci comme ça»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«merci» ('mair-SEE') means thank you, and «merci beaucoup» ('boh-KOO') means thank you very much. The answer is «de rien», you're welcome, literally 'it's nothing'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone says to you «Merci beaucoup !». What do you answer?", "choices": ["De rien.", "Bonjour.", "Pardon."], "answer": "De rien.", "right": "Parfait ! «De rien» = you're welcome. It's the natural reply to «merci».", "wrong": "«Bonjour» is hello and «pardon» is sorry. To answer a thank you, say «de rien» (you're welcome)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "'Please' follows the «tu»/«vous» idea: «s'il vous plaît» for strangers or a group, «s'il te plaît» for a friend. It sounds like 'seel voo PLAY' and means 'if it pleases you'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a café, you order from a waiter you don't know: «Un café, …»", "choices": ["s'il te plaît", "s'il vous plaît", "de rien"], "answer": "s'il vous plaît", "right": "Très bien ! The waiter is a stranger, so you use the polite «vous» form: «s'il vous plaît».", "wrong": "«s'il te plaît» is for friends and family, and «de rien» means you're welcome. With a waiter you don't know, say «s'il vous plaît»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«excusez-moi» is the polite 'excuse me' to get a stranger's attention («excuse-moi» with a friend). «pardon» is a quick 'sorry', and «pardonnez-moi» means 'forgive me'. In the Paris metro you hear «pardon» all day long!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You accidentally bump into someone in the street. What's the quick word to say?", "choices": ["Pardon !", "Merci !", "Salut !"], "answer": "Pardon !", "right": "Exactly! «Pardon» is the quick, polite 'sorry' for little accidents.", "wrong": "«Merci» is thank you and «salut» is hi/bye with friends. For a small accident, say «pardon» (sorry)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«au revoir» ('oh ruh-VWAR') is the goodbye that works with everyone, while «salut» is only for friends. You can add «à bientôt» (see you soon) or «à tout à l'heure» (see you later today)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Au revoir, bonne journée !", "en": "Goodbye, have a nice day!", "note": "In the evening, say «bonne soirée» (have a nice evening) instead."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You'll see your friend again in a few days. Which goodbye fits best?", "choices": ["À bientôt !", "À tout à l'heure !", "Bonne nuit !"], "answer": "À bientôt !", "right": "Bravo ! «À bientôt» = see you soon, perfect for a few days.", "wrong": "«À tout à l'heure» means see you later the SAME day, and «bonne nuit» is for bedtime. For a few days, say «à bientôt» (see you soon)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Common mistake: «bonsoir» means 'good evening' and is a HELLO after about 6 pm. «bonne nuit» (good night) is only said when someone is going to bed."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "It's 8 pm and you arrive at a dinner party. How do you greet the hosts?", "choices": ["Bonsoir !", "Bonne nuit !", "Bonne journée !"], "answer": "Bonsoir !", "right": "Parfait ! «Bonsoir» is the evening hello.", "wrong": "«Bonne nuit» is for bedtime and «bonne journée» is a daytime goodbye wish. To say hello in the evening, use «bonsoir»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «merci» / «de rien», «s'il vous plaît» / «s'il te plaît», «pardon», «excusez-moi». To leave: «au revoir», «à bientôt», «bonne journée». Evening hello: «bonsoir»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Thank you very much!", "answer": "Merci beaucoup !", "accept": ["merci beaucoup", "merci beaucoup !", "merci beaucoup!", "merci beaucoup."], "hint": "«Merci» + the word for 'very much'", "right": "Magnifique ! «Merci beaucoup» = thank you very much. And remember the reply: «de rien».", "wrong": "'Thank you' is «merci» and 'very much' is «beaucoup»: «Merci beaucoup !»"}]}, "1-3": {"stageId": "1-3", "title": "Tu or vous? Two ways to say 'you'", "goal": "By the end you know when to say «tu» and when to say «vous».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we look at something very French: there are two words for 'you', «tu» ('tu') and «vous» ('voo', silent s). Choosing the right one is part of being polite in France."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "English uses 'you' for everyone. Have a guess: who would you call «tu», the friendly 'you'?", "choices": ["Your best friend", "A police officer", "Your new boss"], "answer": "Your best friend", "right": "Good instinct! «tu» is the friendly, informal 'you', for people you know well.", "wrong": "A police officer and a new boss deserve respect, so they get «vous». «tu» is the friendly 'you', for a best friend."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«tu» is for ONE person you know well: family, friends, and children. Children and teens also say «tu» to each other."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«vous» shows respect: use it with strangers, older people and in professional situations, like with a doctor, a manager or a teacher."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You're at the doctor's. How do you ask 'How are you?'", "choices": ["Comment vas-tu ?", "Comment allez-vous ?", "Salut, ça va ?"], "answer": "Comment allez-vous ?", "right": "Très bien ! A doctor is a professional situation, so you use «vous»: «Comment allez-vous ?»", "wrong": "«Comment vas-tu ?» and «Salut, ça va ?» are informal, for friends. With a doctor, use the respectful «vous»: «Comment allez-vous ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«vous» has a second job: it is also the plural 'you'. When you talk to a group, even a group of close friends, you say «vous»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You greet three close friends at the same time. Which question do you use?", "choices": ["Comment vas-tu ?", "Comment allez-vous ?"], "answer": "Comment allez-vous ?", "right": "Exactly! They are friends, but there are three of them, so you need the plural «vous».", "wrong": "«tu» is only for ONE person. For a group, even of friends, use the plural «vous»: «Comment allez-vous ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "tu parles / vous parlez", "en": "you speak (friend) / you speak (polite or group)", "note": "The verb ending changes with the pronoun, so choosing «tu» or «vous» matters for grammar too."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "French has verbs for this! «tutoyer» = to say «tu» to someone, «vouvoyer» = to say «vous». If you're not sure, start with «vous»: the other person may then offer «tu»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Je peux vous tutoyer ?» mean?", "choices": ["Can I say 'tu' to you?", "Can I help you?", "You must say 'vous' to me."], "answer": "Can I say 'tu' to you?", "right": "Bravo ! «tutoyer» = to say «tu», so the question asks to switch to the friendly 'you'.", "wrong": "«tutoyer» means 'to say «tu»'. So «Je peux vous tutoyer ?» means 'Can I say tu to you?'. 'You must say vous to me' would be «Vous devez me vouvoyer»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A student talks to their teacher. Which 'you' should the student use?", "choices": ["tu", "vous"], "answer": "vous", "right": "Yes! Students use «vous» with teachers as a sign of respect, even if the teacher says «tu» to them.", "wrong": "A teacher is someone you show respect to, so a student uses «vous». (The teacher, however, may say «tu» to a young student.)"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Culture tip: saying «tu» to a possible employer sounds too familiar, a bit rude. With a child, though, «vous» would sound strange: always «tu»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You're talking to a 6-year-old child. Which 'you'?", "choices": ["tu", "vous"], "answer": "tu", "right": "Parfait ! Adults use «tu» with children.", "wrong": "With one child, adults always use the friendly «tu». «vous» would only be right for a group of children."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «tu» = one person you know well, or a child. «vous» = respect (strangers, older people, work) OR any group. When in doubt, choose «vous»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "You meet your manager in the morning. Write in French: How are you?", "answer": "Comment allez-vous ?", "accept": ["comment allez-vous", "comment allez-vous ?", "comment allez-vous?", "comment allez vous", "comment allez vous ?"], "hint": "Starts with «Comment allez-...»", "right": "Excellent ! A manager gets the respectful «vous»: «Comment allez-vous ?»", "wrong": "With a manager, use the polite «vous» form: «Comment allez-vous ?». («Comment vas-tu ?» is for friends.)"}]}, "2-1": {"stageId": "2-1", "title": "Weather, seasons and days of the week", "goal": "By the end you can say what the weather is like, name the seasons and days, and use «c'est» / «ce sont».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Bonjour», c'est encore moi, Camille! («c'est encore moi» = it's me again.) Today we'll talk about the weather, the seasons and the days of the week."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To ask about the weather, say «Quel temps fait-il ?» ('kel tahn feh-TEEL'). Most answers start with «il fait…»: «il fait beau» (it's nice/sunny), «il fait chaud» (it's hot), «il fait froid» (it's cold)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Aujourd'hui, il fait beau et il fait chaud.", "en": "Today it's sunny and it's hot.", "note": "«aujourd'hui» = today, pronounced 'oh-zhoor-DWEE'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "It's freezing outside and you need a big coat. What's the weather like?", "choices": ["Il fait chaud.", "Il fait froid.", "Il fait beau."], "answer": "Il fait froid.", "right": "Exactly! «froid» = cold. Brrr!", "wrong": "«chaud» means hot and «beau» means nice/sunny. When you need a big coat, «il fait froid» (it's cold)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Careful: on French taps, «C» means «chaud», which is HOT, not cold! Also, rain and snow don't use «il fait»: we say «il pleut» (it's raining) and «il neige» (it's snowing)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'it is raining'?", "choices": ["Il fait pleut.", "Il pleut.", "Il neige."], "answer": "Il pleut.", "right": "Bravo ! «Il pleut» stands alone, with no «fait».", "wrong": "Rain doesn't use «il fait», and «il neige» means it's snowing. 'It is raining' is simply «il pleut»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The four seasons: «le printemps» (spring), «l'été» (summer), «l'automne» (autumn) and «l'hiver» (winter)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Look closely: why do we write «l'été» and not «le été»?", "choices": ["Because «été» starts with a vowel", "Because it's plural", "Because summer is feminine"], "answer": "Because «été» starts with a vowel", "right": "Well spotted! «le» becomes «l'» before a vowel or a silent «h»: «l'été», «l'automne», «l'hiver». It's smoother to say.", "wrong": "It isn't about plural or feminine. «le» shortens to «l'» before a vowel or a silent «h»: «l'été», «l'hiver»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The days of the week: «lundi, mardi, mercredi, jeudi, vendredi, samedi, dimanche». In France the week starts on Monday, «lundi»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which day is missing? «lundi, mardi, …, jeudi»", "choices": ["mercredi", "vendredi", "dimanche"], "answer": "mercredi", "right": "Parfait ! «mercredi» (Wednesday) comes between «mardi» and «jeudi».", "wrong": "The order is «lundi, mardi, mercredi, jeudi, vendredi, samedi, dimanche». So the missing day is «mercredi»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«c'est» means 'it is / this is' for ONE thing: «c'est lundi», «c'est ma sœur». For several things, use «ce sont» (these are): «ce sont mes chats»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'These are my sisters'?", "choices": ["C'est mes sœurs.", "Ce sont mes sœurs."], "answer": "Ce sont mes sœurs.", "right": "Très bien ! «sœurs» is plural, so we use «ce sont».", "wrong": "«c'est» is for one thing. «mes sœurs» means several sisters, so we need «ce sont mes sœurs»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Numbers 11 to 20: «onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize», then «dix-sept» (10+7), «dix-huit», «dix-neuf» and «vingt». «vingt» sounds like 'van': the g and t are silent!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Let's do some maths: «dix + six = ?»", "choices": ["seize", "seize-dix", "quinze"], "answer": "seize", "right": "Yes! 10 + 6 = 16 = «seize» ('sez').", "wrong": "«dix» = 10 and «six» = 6, so the answer is 16 = «seize». «quinze» is 15."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «il fait beau / chaud / froid», «il pleut», «il neige». Seasons: «le printemps, l'été, l'automne, l'hiver». Days: «lundi» to «dimanche». One thing: «c'est»; several: «ce sont»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Today it is Monday and it is cold.", "answer": "Aujourd'hui, c'est lundi et il fait froid.", "accept": ["aujourd'hui, c'est lundi et il fait froid", "aujourd'hui c'est lundi et il fait froid", "aujourd’hui, c’est lundi et il fait froid", "aujourd’hui c’est lundi et il fait froid", "aujourd'hui, c'est lundi et il fait froid."], "hint": "«Aujourd'hui, c'est…» + «et il fait…»", "right": "Magnifique ! You just gave the day and the weather in French!", "wrong": "Today = «aujourd'hui», it is Monday = «c'est lundi» (no capital!), it is cold = «il fait froid»: «Aujourd'hui, c'est lundi et il fait froid.»"}]}, "2-2": {"stageId": "2-2", "title": "Counting from 1 to 20", "goal": "By the end you can recognise, say and write the numbers from 1 to 20.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we'll count to 20 together, like a pro. First, a quick warm-up with the numbers you already know from 1 to 10."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What number is «cinq»?", "choices": ["5", "6", "7"], "answer": "5", "right": "Yes! «cinq» = 5. It sounds like 'sank': the «q» is said like a 'k'.", "wrong": "6 is «six» and 7 is «sept». «cinq», pronounced 'sank', is 5."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation: «deux» is 'duh' with rounded lips, «trois» is 'trwah', «neuf» is 'nuhf'. Alone, «six» and «dix» end with an 's' sound: 'sees', 'dees'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Numbers 11 to 16 are special words: «onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize». Notice that they all end in «-ze»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize", "en": "11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16", "note": "«quatorze» = 'ka-TORZ'; «seize» = 'sez' (not like the English word 'seize'!)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Hint: think of the English word 'dozen'. What number is «douze»?", "choices": ["2", "12", "20"], "answer": "12", "right": "Exactly! «douze» = 12, like a dozen. A dozen eggs is even «une douzaine d'œufs» in French.", "wrong": "2 is «deux» and 20 is «vingt». «douze» is 12, like 'a dozen'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is 15?", "choices": ["quatorze", "quinze", "cinq"], "answer": "quinze", "right": "Bravo ! «quinze» = 15. Memory trick: «cinq» (5) and «quinze» (15) both have the same 'k' sound.", "wrong": "«quatorze» is 14 and «cinq» is 5. 15 is «quinze»; it sounds a bit like «cinq», which helps you remember."}, {"t": "say", "text": "From 17, French builds numbers like a little sum: ten + number. «dix-sept» (10+7), «dix-huit» (10+8), «dix-neuf» (10+9), with a hyphen in the middle."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 19?", "choices": ["dix-neuf", "neuf-dix", "neuf-onze"], "answer": "dix-neuf", "right": "Parfait ! Ten first, then nine: «dix-neuf».", "wrong": "The ten comes first: 19 = 10 + 9 = «dix-neuf»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«vingt» (20) sounds like 'van', said through the nose: the g and the t are both silent. It's the same sound as «vin» (wine)!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the number 18 in French.", "answer": "dix-huit", "accept": ["dix-huit", "dix huit", "dixhuit"], "hint": "10 + 8", "right": "Très bien ! «dix-huit» = 10 + 8. Say it 'deez-WEET'.", "wrong": "18 is 10 + 8, so we write «dix-huit», with a hyphen."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«huit + quatre = ?»", "choices": ["onze", "douze", "quatorze"], "answer": "douze", "right": "Yes! 8 + 4 = 12 = «douze».", "wrong": "«huit» = 8 and «quatre» = 4, so 8 + 4 = 12 = «douze»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«neuf + sept = ?»", "choices": ["quinze", "seize", "dix-sept"], "answer": "seize", "right": "Excellent ! 9 + 7 = 16 = «seize».", "wrong": "«neuf» = 9 and «sept» = 7, so 9 + 7 = 16 = «seize». «quinze» is 15 and «dix-sept» is 17."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: 11 to 16 are special words ending in «-ze»; 17, 18 and 19 are «dix-» + a number; and 20 is «vingt», said 'van'."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Count out loud from 11 to 20: «onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize, dix-sept, dix-huit, dix-neuf, vingt».", "fr": "onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize, dix-sept, dix-huit, dix-neuf, vingt"}]}, "2-3": {"stageId": "2-3", "title": "First, second, third: ordinal numbers", "goal": "By the end you can say first, second, third… in French and use them correctly.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today: 'first, second, third', the ordinal numbers. French makes them with one simple trick."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Let's guess. «trois» means 3. What do you think «troisième» means?", "choices": ["three times", "third", "thirty"], "answer": "third", "right": "Great guess! «troisième» = third. The ending «-ième» turns a number into a position.", "wrong": "The ending «-ième» turns a number into a position, like '-th' in English. «trois» → «troisième» = third."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The trick: add «-ième» (say 'YEM') to the number. «deux» → «deuxième», «six» → «sixième», «huit» → «huitième»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'eighth'?", "choices": ["huitième", "huitème", "huit"], "answer": "huitième", "right": "Parfait ! «huit» + «-ième» = «huitième».", "wrong": "Add the full ending «-ième» to the number: «huit» → «huitième»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "There's one exception: 'first' is «premier» for masculine words and «première» for feminine words. Say them 'pruh-MYAY' and 'pruh-MYAIR'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est arrivé premier.", "en": "He came first."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«fois» (time, occasion) is feminine. How do you say 'the first time'?", "choices": ["la premier fois", "la première fois", "la unième fois"], "answer": "la première fois", "right": "Bravo ! A feminine noun takes «première»: «C'est la première fois.»", "wrong": "'First' never uses «-ième»: it's «premier» (masculine) or «première» (feminine). «fois» is feminine, so «la première fois»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Three spelling changes: drop the final «e» («quatre» → «quatrième»), add a «u» to five («cinq» → «cinquième»), and turn f into v for nine («neuf» → «neuvième»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you write 'ninth'?", "choices": ["neufième", "neuvième", "neuième"], "answer": "neuvième", "right": "Excellent ! The «f» of «neuf» becomes a «v»: «neuvième».", "wrong": "With nine, the «f» turns into a «v» before the ending: «neuf» → «neuvième»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Abbreviations: «1er» / «1re» (first), then just «e»: «2e», «3e», «18e». In Paris you'll see «le 18e arrondissement», the 18th district."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In dates, French uses normal numbers, never «-ième», except for the first of the month: «le 1er décembre» (premier), but «le 15 juillet» (quinze)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "France's national day is July 14th. How do you say it?", "choices": ["le quatorze juillet", "le quatorzième juillet"], "answer": "le quatorze juillet", "right": "Yes! Dates use normal numbers: «le quatorze juillet». Only the 1st is «le premier».", "wrong": "Dates don't use «-ième» (except «le premier»). So it's «le quatorze juillet»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: number + «-ième» (troisième, huitième), first = «premier / première», watch «quatrième, cinquième, neuvième», and dates use normal numbers except «le 1er»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I am the third child.", "answer": "Je suis le troisième enfant.", "accept": ["je suis le troisième enfant", "je suis le troisième enfant.", "je suis le troisieme enfant"], "hint": "«Je suis le…» + 'third' + «enfant»", "right": "Magnifique ! «trois» + «-ième» = «troisième».", "wrong": "'Third' is «trois» + «-ième» = «troisième»: «Je suis le troisième enfant.»"}]}, "2-4": {"stageId": "2-4", "title": "Talking about the weather and the seasons", "goal": "By the end you can describe sun, clouds, rain and fog, and say which season it is.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's become little weather reporters! Remember the key question: «Quel temps fait-il ?», 'What's the weather like?'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Quick review: what does «il fait beau» mean?", "choices": ["It's nice / sunny", "It's cold", "It's raining"], "answer": "It's nice / sunny", "right": "Yes! «il fait beau» = the weather is nice.", "wrong": "'It's cold' is «il fait froid» and 'it's raining' is «il pleut». «il fait beau» means the weather is nice."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Another way to talk about the sun: «il y a du soleil», literally 'there is some sun'. «il y a» ('eel-ya') means 'there is / there are', and it's super useful."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Clouds: «il y a des nuages» (there are clouds) or «c'est nuageux» (it's cloudy). Fog: «il y a du brouillard», pronounced 'broo-YAR'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The sky is grey and full of clouds, but it isn't raining. What do you say?", "choices": ["C'est nuageux.", "Il pleut.", "Il fait chaud."], "answer": "C'est nuageux.", "right": "Très bien ! «nuageux» comes from «nuage», cloud.", "wrong": "«il pleut» means it's raining and «il fait chaud» means it's hot. For a cloudy sky, say «c'est nuageux» or «il y a des nuages»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«il pleut» = it's raining, and «la pluie» = the rain. «para» means 'against'. So what is «un parapluie»?", "choices": ["an umbrella", "a raincoat", "a cloud"], "answer": "an umbrella", "right": "Clever! «para» (against) + «pluie» (rain) = «un parapluie», an umbrella.", "wrong": "Put the pieces together: «para» (against) + «pluie» (rain) = something against the rain: an umbrella!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You can't even see the end of the street because the air is thick and white. What's the weather?", "choices": ["Il y a du brouillard.", "Il y a du soleil.", "Il fait beau."], "answer": "Il y a du brouillard.", "right": "Exactly! «le brouillard» = fog.", "wrong": "«soleil» means sun and «il fait beau» means nice weather. Thick white air is fog: «il y a du brouillard»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To say 'in' a season: «en été», «en automne», «en hiver», but «au printemps». The first three start with a vowel sound; «printemps» doesn't."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ici, il fait beau, c'est l'été.", "en": "Here, it's sunny, it's summer."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'in spring'?", "choices": ["en printemps", "au printemps", "à printemps"], "answer": "au printemps", "right": "Bravo ! «printemps» starts with a consonant, so it's «au printemps».", "wrong": "«en» is used with the seasons that start with a vowel sound («en été», «en hiver»). Spring is the odd one: «au printemps»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il neige et il fait très froid.» Which season is it, probably?", "choices": ["C'est l'été.", "C'est l'hiver.", "C'est le printemps."], "answer": "C'est l'hiver.", "right": "Oui ! Snow and cold: «c'est l'hiver», it's winter.", "wrong": "«il neige» = it's snowing and «il fait très froid» = it's very cold. That sounds like winter: «c'est l'hiver»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, then try it with your own favourite season: «Le printemps est ma saison préférée.»", "fr": "Le printemps est ma saison préférée."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «il y a du soleil», «il y a des nuages», «c'est nuageux», «il y a du brouillard», «il pleut». Seasons: «en été, en automne, en hiver», but «au printemps»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Today it is cold.", "answer": "Aujourd'hui, il fait froid.", "accept": ["aujourd'hui, il fait froid", "aujourd'hui il fait froid", "aujourd’hui, il fait froid", "aujourd’hui il fait froid", "aujourd'hui, il fait froid."], "hint": "«Aujourd'hui, il fait…»", "right": "Parfait ! Today = «aujourd'hui», it is cold = «il fait froid».", "wrong": "Today = «aujourd'hui», and for cold we use «il fait»: «Aujourd'hui, il fait froid.»"}]}, "2-5": {"stageId": "2-5", "title": "Days of the week in real sentences", "goal": "By the end you can name the days and say 'on Monday' or 'every Monday' correctly.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we'll practise the days of the week and how to use them in real sentences."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: most French days come from the sky! «lundi» = Moon day («la lune»), «mardi» = Mars, «mercredi» = Mercury, «jeudi» = Jupiter, «vendredi» = Venus. «dimanche» is the Lord's day."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«la lune» means 'the moon'. So which day is the 'moon day'?", "choices": ["lundi", "mardi", "dimanche"], "answer": "lundi", "right": "Exactly! «lune» → «lundi» (Monday), just like Moon-day → Monday in English.", "wrong": "«mardi» comes from Mars and «dimanche» is the Lord's day. «lune» (moon) gives «lundi», Monday."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Common mistake: in French, days do NOT take a capital letter, unless they start a sentence. And the week starts on «lundi»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence is written correctly?", "choices": ["Aujourd'hui, c'est Lundi.", "Aujourd'hui, c'est lundi."], "answer": "Aujourd'hui, c'est lundi.", "right": "Parfait ! «lundi» with a small «l», because French days have no capital letter.", "wrong": "In French, days of the week are written in lowercase: «Aujourd'hui, c'est lundi.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "No word for 'on' is needed! «lundi» alone means 'this (coming) Monday', and «le lundi» means 'on Mondays', every Monday."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il va à la piscine lundi matin.", "en": "He's going to the swimming pool on Monday morning.", "note": "«matin» = morning, «soir» = evening: «mardi soir» = Tuesday evening."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I go to the restaurant on Fridays' (every Friday)?", "choices": ["Je vais au restaurant vendredi.", "Je vais au restaurant le vendredi.", "Je vais au restaurant sur vendredi."], "answer": "Je vais au restaurant le vendredi.", "right": "Très bien ! «le vendredi» = every Friday, a habit.", "wrong": "«vendredi» alone means just this coming Friday, and French never uses «sur» here. For a habit, add «le»: «le vendredi» = on Fridays."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Another way to say 'every': «tous les» + the day with an «s»: «tous les mercredis» = every Wednesday. The «s» is silent!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Nous marchons ensemble … mercredis.» (We walk together every Wednesday.)", "choices": ["tous les", "le", "sur les"], "answer": "tous les", "right": "Bravo ! «tous les mercredis» = every Wednesday. Notice the «s» on «mercredis».", "wrong": "«le» needs a singular day («le mercredi»), and «sur» isn't used here. With «mercredis» (plural), say «tous les mercredis»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which day comes just before «dimanche»?", "choices": ["vendredi", "samedi", "lundi"], "answer": "samedi", "right": "Oui ! «samedi» (Saturday), then «dimanche» (Sunday): the weekend!", "wrong": "The end of the week goes «vendredi, samedi, dimanche». So the day before «dimanche» is «samedi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «lundi, mardi, mercredi, jeudi, vendredi, samedi, dimanche», no capitals. «lundi» = this Monday; «le lundi» or «tous les lundis» = every Monday."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Today it is Saturday.", "answer": "Aujourd'hui, c'est samedi.", "accept": ["aujourd'hui, c'est samedi", "aujourd'hui c'est samedi", "aujourd’hui, c’est samedi", "aujourd’hui c’est samedi", "aujourd'hui, c'est samedi."], "hint": "«Aujourd'hui, c'est…»", "right": "Magnifique ! And you remembered: no capital letter on «samedi».", "wrong": "Today = «aujourd'hui», it is = «c'est», and Saturday = «samedi» (lowercase): «Aujourd'hui, c'est samedi.»"}]}, "3-1": {"stageId": "3-1", "title": "How you feel, the months and the date", "goal": "By the end you can say how you feel, name the months and say today's date.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Bonjour ! Comment ça va ?» Today you'll learn to say how you feel, name the months and give the date. Let's go!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "To say how you feel, use «je suis» (I am) + a feeling: «je suis content» (happy), «je suis triste» (sad), «je suis fatigué» (tired), «je suis malade» (sick), «je suis fâché» (angry)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You worked all day and you just want to sleep. What do you say?", "choices": ["Je suis fatigué.", "Je suis content.", "Je suis fâché."], "answer": "Je suis fatigué.", "right": "Exactly! «fatigué» = tired. Time for bed!", "wrong": "«content» means happy and «fâché» means angry. When you want to sleep, «je suis fatigué» (I'm tired)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "A woman adds an «e»: «content» → «contente», «fatigué» → «fatiguée». In «contente» you now hear the t ('kon-TAHNT'), while «content» ends silently ('kon-TAHN')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Camille is a woman and she's happy. What does she say?", "choices": ["Je suis content.", "Je suis contente.", "Je suis contents."], "answer": "Je suis contente.", "right": "Bravo ! A woman adds «-e»: «contente». And now the t is pronounced.", "wrong": "«content» is the masculine form and «contents» is plural. A woman talking about herself adds «-e»: «Je suis contente.»"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I am sad.", "answer": "Je suis triste.", "accept": ["je suis triste", "je suis triste."], "hint": "«Je suis…» + the word for sad", "right": "Très bien ! «triste» already ends in «e», so it's the same for men and women.", "wrong": "I am = «je suis», sad = «triste»: «Je suis triste.» Words already ending in «-e», like «triste» and «malade», don't change for women."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The months: «janvier, février, mars, avril, mai, juin, juillet, août, septembre, octobre, novembre, décembre». Like the days, they have no capital letter."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which month is «août»?", "choices": ["April", "August", "October"], "answer": "August", "right": "Yes! «août» = August. It sounds like 'oot': short and sweet.", "wrong": "April is «avril» and October is «octobre». «août», pronounced 'oot', is August."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To give a date, French says day + number + month, with no 'th' and no 'of': «mardi 31 octobre». You can also say «le 31 octobre»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Aujourd'hui, c'est mardi 31 octobre 2018.", "en": "Today is Tuesday 31st October 2018.", "note": "2018 = «deux-mille-dix-huit»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you write 'Wednesday 26th May' in French?", "choices": ["mercredi 26 mai", "mai mercredi 26", "mercredi mai 26"], "answer": "mercredi 26 mai", "right": "Parfait ! Day, number, then month, all in lowercase.", "wrong": "In French, the order is day + number + month: «mercredi 26 mai». The number always comes before the month."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Add «-aine» to a number for 'about': «une dizaine» (about ten), «une vingtaine» (about twenty), «une centaine» (about a hundred). Careful: «une douzaine» is exactly a dozen."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Il y a une vingtaine de personnes» mean?", "choices": ["There are exactly twenty people.", "There are about twenty people.", "There are twenty-one people."], "answer": "There are about twenty people.", "right": "Exactly! «une vingtaine» = about twenty, a round, approximate number.", "wrong": "«-aine» makes a number approximate: «vingt» = 20, «une vingtaine» = about 20."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «je suis content(e) / triste / fatigué(e) / malade / fâché(e)». Months: «janvier» to «décembre», no capitals. Dates: «mardi 31 octobre». About ten: «une dizaine»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, with your own feeling and favourite month: «Aujourd'hui, je suis content(e). Mon mois préféré est juin.»", "fr": "Aujourd'hui, je suis contente. Mon mois préféré est juin."}]}, "3-2": {"stageId": "3-2", "title": "The twelve months", "goal": "By the end you can name all twelve months and say when your birthday is.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today: the twelve months. Good news: many of them look like their English cousins!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Have a guess: what is «octobre»?", "choices": ["August", "October", "November"], "answer": "October", "right": "Easy, right? «octobre» = October.", "wrong": "«octobre» looks just like 'October', and it is! (August is «août» and November is «novembre».)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "These are very close to English: «mars, avril, mai, septembre, octobre, novembre, décembre»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The trickier ones: «janvier» ('zhahn-VYAY'), «février» ('fay-vree-YAY'), «juin» ('zhwan'), «juillet» ('zhwee-YEH') and «août» ('oot')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«juin» and «juillet» are easy to mix up. Which one is July?", "choices": ["juin", "juillet"], "answer": "juillet", "right": "Bravo ! «juillet» = July. Remember France's national day, «le 14 juillet».", "wrong": "«juin» is June. July is «juillet», like France's national day, «le 14 juillet»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which month comes after «février»?", "choices": ["mars", "mai", "avril"], "answer": "mars", "right": "Oui ! «janvier, février, mars»: January, February, March.", "wrong": "The order is «janvier, février, mars, avril, mai…». So after «février» comes «mars»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Remember: in French, months have no capital letter, unless they start a sentence."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence is written correctly?", "choices": ["Mon anniversaire est en Mai.", "Mon anniversaire est en mai."], "answer": "Mon anniversaire est en mai.", "right": "Parfait ! «mai» with a small «m».", "wrong": "French months are written in lowercase in the middle of a sentence: «en mai»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To say 'in' a month, use «en»: «en mai», «en mars», «en août». «mon anniversaire» means 'my birthday'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mon anniversaire est en mars.", "en": "My birthday is in March."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'in August'?", "choices": ["en août", "à août", "au août"], "answer": "en août", "right": "Très bien ! Every month takes «en»: «en août».", "wrong": "With months, we always use «en»: «en août», «en janvier»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone asks you «Quand est votre anniversaire ?». What does it mean?", "choices": ["How old are you?", "When is your birthday?", "What is your favourite month?"], "answer": "When is your birthday?", "right": "Exactly! «quand» = when, «anniversaire» = birthday.", "wrong": "«quand» means 'when' and «anniversaire» means 'birthday': 'When is your birthday?'"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «janvier, février, mars, avril, mai, juin, juillet, août, septembre, octobre, novembre, décembre», always lowercase, and 'in' + month = «en»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: My birthday is in May.", "answer": "Mon anniversaire est en mai.", "accept": ["mon anniversaire est en mai", "mon anniversaire est en mai.", "mon anniversaire, c'est en mai"], "hint": "«Mon anniversaire est…»", "right": "Magnifique ! «en» + month, with a small «m».", "wrong": "My birthday = «mon anniversaire», is = «est», in May = «en mai»: «Mon anniversaire est en mai.»"}]}, "3-3": {"stageId": "3-3", "title": "Big numbers: hundreds, thousands, millions", "goal": "By the end you can read and write big numbers like 567, 2 150 and 3 000 000.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today: big numbers! Hundreds, thousands, millions, and how French writes them."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Warm-up: what is «cent»?", "choices": ["10", "100", "1000"], "answer": "100", "right": "Yes! «cent» = 100. It sounds like 'sahn': the t is silent.", "wrong": "10 is «dix» and 1000 is «mille». «cent», pronounced 'sahn', is 100."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Build big numbers from left to right, like in English, but without 'and': 129 = «cent-vingt-neuf», 567 = «cinq-cent-soixante-sept»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 129?", "choices": ["cent-vingt-neuf", "un-cent-et-vingt-neuf", "cent-neuf-vingt"], "answer": "cent-vingt-neuf", "right": "Parfait ! 100 + 29 = «cent-vingt-neuf». No «un» before «cent» and no 'and'.", "wrong": "French never says «un-cent» and has no 'and' here. 129 = 100 + 29 = «cent-vingt-neuf»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«mille» ('meel') = 1000, and it never takes an «s»: «deux-mille», «dix-mille». Like «cent», there's no «un» in front: just «mille»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you write 2000?", "choices": ["deux-milles", "deux-mille", "mille-deux"], "answer": "deux-mille", "right": "Bravo ! «mille» never takes an «s».", "wrong": "«mille» never takes an «s», and the multiplier comes first: «deux-mille»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Dix-mille personnes ont participé à la manifestation.", "en": "Ten thousand people took part in the demonstration."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«un million» is a noun, so unlike «mille» it DOES take an «s» in the plural: «deux millions»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 3 000 000?", "choices": ["trois millions", "trois million", "trois mille"], "answer": "trois millions", "right": "Excellent ! «million» is a noun, so it takes an «s»: «trois millions».", "wrong": "«trois mille» is only 3000. For millions, «million» takes an «s» in the plural: «trois millions»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "French uses a space between thousands («3 000») and a comma for decimals («1,2 kg»). It's the opposite of English, so look carefully at prices and weights!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a French shop you see «1,5 kg». How much is that?", "choices": ["1.5 kg", "15 kg", "1500 kg"], "answer": "1.5 kg", "right": "Exactly! The French comma is the English decimal point: 1,5 = 1.5.", "wrong": "In French, the comma marks decimals. «1,5 kg» = one and a half kilos (1.5 kg)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«cent» takes an «s» when multiplied and nothing follows. How do you write 630?", "choices": ["six-cent-trente", "six-cents-trente"], "answer": "six-cent-trente", "right": "Très bien ! A number follows «cent», so no «s». Compare «six-cents» (600).", "wrong": "«cent» only takes an «s» when nothing comes after it («six-cents» = 600). With «trente» after it, write «six-cent-trente»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: no «un» before «cent» or «mille»; «mille» never takes an «s»; «millions» does; and «cent» takes an «s» only at the end. French writes 3 000 and 1,5."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: 2 150", "answer": "deux-mille-cent-cinquante", "accept": ["deux-mille-cent-cinquante", "deux mille cent cinquante", "deux-mille cent cinquante"], "hint": "2000 + 100 + 50", "right": "Magnifique ! «deux-mille» + «cent» + «cinquante».", "wrong": "Split it up: 2000 = «deux-mille», 100 = «cent», 50 = «cinquante»: «deux-mille-cent-cinquante»."}]}, "3-4": {"stageId": "3-4", "title": "Counting in tens, from 10 to 300", "goal": "By the end you can count in tens up to 300, including the famous 70, 80 and 90.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we count in tens: 10, 20, 30… all the way to 300. Get ready for some fun French maths!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Here they are: «dix, vingt, trente, quarante, cinquante, soixante». Notice that 40, 50 and 60 all end in «-ante»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«cinquante» looks a bit like «cinq». What do you think it is?", "choices": ["5", "15", "50"], "answer": "50", "right": "Well spotted! «cinq» (5) → «cinquante» (50).", "wrong": "5 is «cinq» and 15 is «quinze». «cinq» + «-ante» gives «cinquante», 50."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«soixante» (60) is pronounced 'swa-SAHNT': here the x sounds like an 's'!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Now the fun part. French doesn't have a special word for 70. How do you think it says it?", "choices": ["septante", "soixante-dix", "sept-dix"], "answer": "soixante-dix", "right": "Yes! 70 = «soixante-dix», 60 + 10. (In Belgium and Switzerland they say «septante»!)", "wrong": "In France, 70 is 60 + 10: «soixante-dix». (Belgians and Swiss say «septante», but in France it's «soixante-dix».)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "80 is «quatre-vingts», four twenties (4 × 20), pronounced 'katr-VAN'. 90 is «quatre-vingt-dix», 4 × 20 + 10."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «quatre-vingt-dix»?", "choices": ["24", "80", "90"], "answer": "90", "right": "Bravo ! 4 × 20 + 10 = 90.", "wrong": "Do the maths: «quatre-vingts» = 4 × 20 = 80, then «+ dix» = 90."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«vingt» and «cent» take an «s» when multiplied, but only if nothing follows: «quatre-vingts», «deux-cents», but «quatre-vingt-dix», «deux-cent-dix»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you write 200?", "choices": ["deux-cent", "deux-cents"], "answer": "deux-cents", "right": "Parfait ! Multiplied and nothing after it, so «cents» takes an «s».", "wrong": "«cent» takes an «s» when it is multiplied and nothing follows: «deux-cents». (But «deux-cent-dix».)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "After 100, just put «cent» in front: «cent-dix» (110), «cent-vingt» (120), «cent-soixante-dix» (170)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 100?", "choices": ["un-cent", "cent", "dix-dix"], "answer": "cent", "right": "Exactly! Just «cent», never «un-cent».", "wrong": "French says just «cent» for 100, without «un» in front."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce fauteuil coûte deux-cent-quatre-vingts euros.", "en": "This armchair costs 280 euros."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Lyon is 350 kilometres away. How do you write 350?", "choices": ["trois-cent-cinquante", "trois-cents-cinquante", "trois-cent-cinquantes"], "answer": "trois-cent-cinquante", "right": "Très bien ! A number follows «cent», so no «s».", "wrong": "«cent» loses its «s» when another number follows, and «cinquante» never takes an «s»: «trois-cent-cinquante»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «dix, vingt, trente, quarante, cinquante, soixante, soixante-dix, quatre-vingts, quatre-vingt-dix, cent», then «deux-cents», «trois-cents»… and «mille» ('meel') = 1000."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write 70 in French.", "answer": "soixante-dix", "accept": ["soixante-dix", "soixante dix", "soixantedix"], "hint": "60 + 10", "right": "Magnifique ! 60 + 10 = «soixante-dix».", "wrong": "70 is 60 + 10 in French: «soixante-dix»."}]}, "3-5": {"stageId": "3-5", "title": "Every number from 1 to 100", "goal": "By the end you can say and write any number from 1 to 100.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today you'll master every number up to 100! The basic pattern is simple: the ten, a hyphen, then the unit."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "vingt-deux, trente-cinq, quarante-huit", "en": "22, 35, 48"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «trente-sept»?", "choices": ["27", "37", "73"], "answer": "37", "right": "Yes! «trente» (30) + «sept» (7) = 37.", "wrong": "«trente» = 30 and «sept» = 7, so «trente-sept» = 37."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With 1, we add «et» (and): «vingt-et-un» (21), «trente-et-un» (31), up to «soixante-et-un» (61). Say it smoothly: 'van-tay-UN'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 41?", "choices": ["quarante-un", "quarante-et-un", "un-quarante"], "answer": "quarante-et-un", "right": "Parfait ! Numbers ending in 1 (from 21 to 61) take «et»: «quarante-et-un».", "wrong": "For 21, 31, 41, 51 and 61, we add «et»: «quarante-et-un»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In the 70s, French keeps «soixante» and adds 10 to 19: 71 = «soixante-et-onze», 72 = «soixante-douze», 77 = «soixante-dix-sept»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 75?", "choices": ["soixante-quinze", "soixante-dix-cinq", "sept-dix-cinq"], "answer": "soixante-quinze", "right": "Bravo ! 75 = 60 + 15 = «soixante-quinze».", "wrong": "Think 60 + 15: «soixante» + «quinze» = «soixante-quinze»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The 80s use «quatre-vingt» + 1 to 9, with no «et»: «quatre-vingt-un» (81). The 90s use «quatre-vingt» + 10 to 19: «quatre-vingt-onze» (91), «quatre-vingt-dix-neuf» (99)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 81?", "choices": ["quatre-vingt-et-un", "quatre-vingt-un", "quatre-vingts-un"], "answer": "quatre-vingt-un", "right": "Excellent ! No «et» and no «s» here: «quatre-vingt-un».", "wrong": "From 81 on, there's no «et», and «vingt» loses its «s» when a number follows: «quatre-vingt-un»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Since a 1990 spelling reform, all the parts of a number can be joined with hyphens («vingt-et-un»). You'll also see the older spelling «vingt et un»: both are fine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 99?", "choices": ["quatre-vingt-dix-neuf", "quatre-vingt-neuf", "neuf-vingt-dix"], "answer": "quatre-vingt-dix-neuf", "right": "Magnifique ! 80 + 19 = «quatre-vingt-dix-neuf». The longest one!", "wrong": "«quatre-vingt-neuf» is only 89. 99 = 80 + 19 = «quatre-vingt-dix-neuf»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Culture: French phone numbers are read in pairs, like «06 72 84…» = «zéro six, soixante-douze, quatre-vingt-quatre…». So you'll hear these numbers all the time!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «quatre-vingt-quatre»?", "choices": ["24", "84", "94"], "answer": "84", "right": "Yes! 4 × 20 + 4 = 84.", "wrong": "«quatre-vingts» = 80, plus «quatre» = 84."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: ten + unit («trente-cinq»), «et» for 21 to 61 and 71, the 70s = «soixante» + 10 to 19, the 80s = «quatre-vingt» + unit, the 90s = «quatre-vingt» + 10 to 19."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write 72 in French.", "answer": "soixante-douze", "accept": ["soixante-douze", "soixante douze", "soixantedouze"], "hint": "60 + 12", "right": "Très bien ! 60 + 12 = «soixante-douze».", "wrong": "72 = 60 + 12 in French: «soixante» + «douze» = «soixante-douze»."}]}, "4-1": {"stageId": "4-1", "title": "Alphabet & pronunciation: first steps", "goal": "Ask someone's first name and how it is spelt, and meet four key French sounds: ou, é, è and the nasal 'in'.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! I'm Camille, and today we start with the basics: how French sounds. First, a very useful question when you meet someone: «Quel est ton prénom ?», which means 'What is your first name?'"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quel est ton prénom ?", "en": "What is your first name?", "note": "prénom = first name"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quel est ton nom ?", "en": "What is your surname?", "note": "In French, «nom» usually means the family name."}, {"t": "say", "text": "French names can be hard to spell, so people often ask: «Comment ça s'écrit ?» Word for word, that's 'How does that write itself?' You answer by spelling it letter by letter. We'll learn the letters in the next stage."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Comment ça s'écrit ?", "en": "How is it spelt?"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«ton» is for friends, children and family. With an adult you don't know, be polite and say «Quel est votre prénom ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You meet a new classmate your age. How do you ask their first name?", "choices": ["Quel est ton prénom ?", "Quel est ton nom ?", "Comment ça s'écrit ?"], "answer": "Quel est ton prénom ?", "right": "Parfait ! «prénom» means first name, and «ton» is friendly.", "wrong": "«prénom» is the first name and «nom» is the surname. «Comment ça s'écrit ?» asks how to spell it. The right question is «Quel est ton prénom ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now for four key sounds. Number one: «ou» sounds like the 'oo' in 'food'. Round your lips and push them forward: «le cou» (the neck), «la poule» (the hen)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«ou» never sounds like the 'ow' in 'cow'. It is always a pure 'oo'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Number two: «é» is a short, crisp 'ay' with no glide at the end. Smile a little as you say it: «le bébé», «vous voulez», «mélanger». Number three: the open 'e' of 'bed': «la forêt», «il est», and often «j'ai»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the 'oo' sound?", "choices": ["la poule", "le bébé", "le pain"], "answer": "la poule", "right": "Yes! «ou» = 'oo'.", "wrong": "The letters «ou» make the 'oo' sound, as in «la poule». «bébé» has the é sound and «pain» has a nasal sound."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Number four is a nasal vowel. In «le pain», «le jardin» and «la peinture», the letters ain, in and ein make one sound that goes through your nose, and you do NOT say the n. Try starting from the 'a' in 'cat' and sending it into your nose: «pain»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Nasal test: pinch your nose and say «pain». If your nose buzzes, you've got it! Don't add an 'n' or 'ng' at the end."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How is the end of «le jardin» pronounced?", "choices": ["Like English 'din', with a clear n", "A nasal vowel with no clear n", "Like 'deen'"], "answer": "A nasal vowel with no clear n", "right": "Exactly! The n just tells you the vowel is nasal. You don't say it.", "wrong": "In «jardin», 'in' is one nasal vowel and the n is not pronounced. Air goes through your nose, and your tongue does not touch the roof of your mouth."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the crisp 'ay' sound of é?", "choices": ["le bébé", "la forêt", "le cou"], "answer": "le bébé", "right": "Bravo! «bébé» has two crisp é sounds.", "wrong": "«é» (the accent slopes up to the right) is the crisp 'ay', as in «bébé». «forêt» has the open 'e' of 'bed', and «cou» has 'oo'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Quel est ton prénom ?» and «Comment ça s'écrit ?». ou = 'oo'. é = crisp 'ay'. è/ê = 'e' as in 'bed'. ain/in/ein = a nasal sound with no n."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: How is it spelt?", "answer": "Comment ça s'écrit ?", "accept": ["comment ça s'écrit", "Comment ça s'écrit?", "comment ça s’écrit", "Comment ça s’écrit ?"], "hint": "Comment ça s'...", "right": "Perfect, including the ç and the é!", "wrong": "The answer is «Comment ça s'écrit ?». Note the cedilla on ç and the accent on é."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quel est ton prénom ? Comment ça s'écrit ?»", "fr": "Quel est ton prénom ? Comment ça s'écrit ?"}]}, "4-2": {"stageId": "4-2", "title": "The French alphabet", "goal": "Say the 26 letters of the French alphabet and spell a name out loud.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's learn the French alphabet. You'll need it to spell your name on the phone, at a hotel or at the doctor's. The letters look English, but many sound different!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "A to G: «A» (aah), «B» (bay), «C» (say), «D» (day), «E» (uh, like 'er' without the r), «F» (eff), «G» (zhay, with the 'zh' sound in 'measure')."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The biggest trap: the French letter «E» sounds like 'uh', and the French letter «I» sounds like 'ee'. English speakers mix these up all the time!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "H to N: «H» (ash), «I» (ee), «J» (zhee), «K» (kah), «L» (ell), «M» (em), «N» (en)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "G and J are almost swapped compared with English. G = 'zhay' and J = 'zhee'. To remember, think of alphabetical order: G comes first and says 'ay'; J comes later and says 'ee'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say the French letter I?", "choices": ["ee", "eye", "uh"], "answer": "ee", "right": "Yes! French I = 'ee'.", "wrong": "French I sounds like 'ee'. 'uh' is the letter E, and 'eye' isn't used in French at all."}, {"t": "say", "text": "O to U: «O» (oh), «P» (pay), «Q» (kü), «R» (air, with a soft r at the back of the throat), «S» (ess), «T» (tay), «U» (ü)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "How to say French U: say 'ee', then round your lips as if for a kiss, but keep your tongue where it is. That's «u». It is NOT 'you'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "V to Z: «V» (vay), «W» (doo-bluh-vay, which means 'double V'), «X» (eeks), «Y» (ee-grek, which means 'Greek i'), «Z» (zed)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which letter is called «i grec»?", "choices": ["Y", "W", "G"], "answer": "Y", "right": "Correct! «i grec» = Y, the 'Greek i'.", "wrong": "«i grec» is Y. W is «double vé» and G is 'zhay'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do French people say the letter H?", "choices": ["aitch", "ash", "hay"], "answer": "ash", "right": "Yes, 'ash'! And as a sound, French h is silent: «l'hôtel».", "wrong": "The letter H is called 'ash' in French. And as a sound, the h is silent, as in «l'hôtel»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Useful extras for spelling: «deux L» or «double L» for LL, «accent aigu» for é, «accent grave» for è. For example, Anne is spelt «A, deux N, E»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ça s'écrit P, I, E, R, R, E.", "en": "It's spelt P, I, E, R, R, E.", "note": "Pierre: pay, ee, uh, air, air, uh"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone spells a name: 'zhay, ah, bay, air, ee, uh, ell'. What is the first letter?", "choices": ["G", "J", "Z"], "answer": "G", "right": "Yes, G! The name is Gabriel.", "wrong": "'zhay' is G (and 'zhee' is J). The name is Gabriel: G, A, B, R, I, E, L."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: E = 'uh', I = 'ee', G = 'zhay', J = 'zhee', H = 'ash', U = 'ee' with rounded lips, W = «double vé», Y = «i grec»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the French name of the letter W.", "answer": "double vé", "accept": ["double vé", "double v", "double ve", "Double vé"], "hint": "It means 'double V'.", "right": "Exactly: «double vé»!", "wrong": "W is called «double vé», which means 'double V' (not 'double U' like in English)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Spell my name out loud: «C, A, M, I, deux L, E»", "fr": "C, A, M, I, deux L, E"}]}, "4-3": {"stageId": "4-3", "title": "The alphabet in words: A to E", "goal": "Pronounce everyday words from A to E, and learn the hard and soft C and the different sounds of E.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's bring the alphabet to life with real words from A to E. Each letter has its own 'personality' in French."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A is easy: it's always a clear 'ah', never the 'ay' of English 'apron'. «amoureux» (in love), «apéritif» (a drink before dinner; the French love their «apéro»!)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis amoureux.", "en": "I'm in love.", "note": "A woman says «Je suis amoureuse.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "B is just like English: «la bière» (beer), «belle blonde» (beautiful blonde, also a nickname for a light beer!), and «bof», the French shrug word for 'meh, so-so'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Bof» is very everyday French. Shrug your shoulders and push your lips out as you say it, just like the French do!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "C has two voices. Before a, o or u it's a hard 'k': «le camion» (truck), «coquine» (naughty), «la couche» (nappy). Before e, i or y it's a soft 's': «le cinéma», «la cerise» (cherry)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How is the C in «camion» pronounced?", "choices": ["k", "s", "sh"], "answer": "k", "right": "Yes! It's 'ka-myon'.", "wrong": "Before a, o or u, C is a hard 'k', so «camion» is 'ka-myon'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has a soft C (sounds like 's')?", "choices": ["coquine", "le cinéma", "la couche"], "answer": "le cinéma", "right": "Bravo! Before i, C sounds like 's'.", "wrong": "C is soft ('s') before e, i or y, as in «le cinéma». In «coquine» and «couche», C comes before o, so it's hard 'k'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "D is like English, but your tongue touches the back of your top teeth: «drôle» (funny), «débordé» (overwhelmed). In «dynamique», the Y simply sounds like 'ee'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis débordé !", "en": "I'm swamped!", "note": "A woman writes «débordée». It sounds the same."}, {"t": "say", "text": "E is the tricky one. With an accent, «é» is a crisp 'ay': «élégante». E + n makes a nasal 'ahn' sound: «enfant». And E before a double consonant usually sounds like the 'e' in 'bed': «ecclésiastique»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word starts with a nasal sound (no clear n)?", "choices": ["enfant", "élégante", "ecclésiastique"], "answer": "enfant", "right": "Exactly! «en» is nasal, so it's 'ahn-fahn'.", "wrong": "«enfant» starts with the nasal sound «en», and you don't say the n. «élégante» starts with é ('ay'), and «ecclésiastique» starts with the 'e' of 'bed'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In «enfant», 'en' and 'an' make the same nasal sound, and the final t is silent: 'ahn-fahn'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «dynamique», the Y sounds like…", "choices": ["'ee' as in 'see'", "'eye' as in 'dye'", "'uh'"], "answer": "'ee' as in 'see'", "right": "Yes! It's 'dee-na-meek'.", "wrong": "French Y (as a vowel) sounds like 'ee', so «dynamique» is 'dee-na-meek'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: A = 'ah'. C = 'k' before a/o/u and 's' before e/i/y. Y = 'ee'. é = 'ay'. en = nasal 'ahn'. And «bof» = meh!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The truck is funny.", "answer": "Le camion est drôle.", "accept": ["le camion est drôle", "Le camion est drôle", "le camion est drole"], "hint": "le camion / drôle", "right": "Très bien! Don't forget the little hat on «drôle».", "wrong": "The answer is «Le camion est drôle.» Note the ô in drôle."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «L'enfant est amoureux, et moi, je suis débordé !»", "fr": "L'enfant est amoureux, et moi, je suis débordé !"}]}, "4-4": {"stageId": "4-4", "title": "Acute & grave accents", "goal": "Pronounce é and è correctly and use à and où to tell words apart.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French accents aren't decoration: they change either the sound or the meaning. Today we look at the acute accent «é» (accent aigu) and the grave accent «è, à, ù» (accent grave)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«é»: smile, keep your mouth almost closed, and say a short 'ay' with no glide (not 'ay-ee'). «le bébé», «l'été» (summer), «le café», «l'épaule» (shoulder)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The acute accent only goes on E. Picture it as a smile: the line goes up, and your lips spread wide."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "En été, je bois un café.", "en": "In summer, I drink a coffee."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«è»: open your mouth wider, like the 'e' in 'bed' or 'get'. «le père», «la mère», «le frère», «très», «la crème»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the open 'e' of 'bed'?", "choices": ["la mère", "le café", "l'été"], "answer": "la mère", "right": "Yes! è = the open 'e' of 'bed'.", "wrong": "«è» is the open sound, as in «la mère». «café» and «été» have the crisp, smiling é."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Two é's can sit side by side, and each one is pronounced: «créé» (created) is 'cray-ay'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How many syllables does «créé» have?", "choices": ["1", "2", "3"], "answer": "2", "right": "Correct: cré-é. Each é is its own syllable.", "wrong": "«créé» has 2 syllables: cré-é. Every é is pronounced."}, {"t": "say", "text": "On A and U, the grave accent doesn't change the sound at all. It changes the MEANING! «a» = has, «à» = at/to. «ou» = or, «où» = where."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a un frère à Paris.", "en": "He has a brother in Paris.", "note": "a = has / à = in, at, to"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Où est la crème ?", "en": "Where is the cream?", "note": "ù only appears in this one word: «où»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: Je vais ___ Paris.", "choices": ["a", "à", "á"], "answer": "à", "right": "Parfait! «à» = to.", "wrong": "«à» means 'to/at'. Without the accent, «a» means 'has'. «á» doesn't exist in French."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: Tu veux du thé ___ du café ? (tea OR coffee)", "choices": ["ou", "où", "oú"], "answer": "ou", "right": "Yes! No accent: «ou» = or.", "wrong": "'Or' is «ou» without an accent. «où» means 'where', and «oú» doesn't exist."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: é = crisp, smiling 'ay'. è = open 'e' as in 'bed'. à and où sound the same as a and ou; the accent just changes the meaning."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Where is my brother?", "answer": "Où est mon frère ?", "accept": ["où est mon frère", "Où est mon frère?", "Où est mon frère", "où est mon frère ?"], "hint": "Où ... frère", "right": "Excellent! Two accents in the right places.", "wrong": "The answer is «Où est mon frère ?». You need ù for 'where' and è in «frère»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Mon père et ma mère boivent un café en été.»", "fr": "Mon père et ma mère boivent un café en été."}]}, "4-5": {"stageId": "4-5", "title": "The circumflex accent", "goal": "Recognise the circumflex (â ê î ô û), know how it changes the sound, and use the 'hidden S' trick to guess meanings.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Meet the little hat «^». It's called the accent circonflexe, and it can sit on any vowel: â, ê, î, ô, û. Its secret? It often marks an S that disappeared from the spelling hundreds of years ago!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la forêt", "en": "the forest", "note": "Old French: forest → forêt"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "l'hôpital", "en": "the hospital", "note": "hospital → hôpital"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Detective trick: when you see a circumflex, add an S after the vowel and you'll often find the English word! «la fête» → feast, «la pâte» → paste, «le maître» → master, «l'hôtel» → hostel, «le mât» → mast."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le maître mange des pâtes.", "en": "The master is eating pasta."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Use the S trick: what does «la côte» mean?", "choices": ["the coast", "the coat", "the cot"], "answer": "the coast", "right": "Great detective work! côte → coste → coast.", "wrong": "Add an S after the ô: côte → coste → coast. «la côte» = the coast."}, {"t": "say", "text": "What about the sound? «ê» = the open 'e' of 'bed', just like è: «la fête», «la forêt». «ô» = a closed 'oh' with rounded lips: «l'hôtel», «l'hôpital». «â» is a deeper 'ah' for some speakers, but most people today say it like a normal a: «la pâte». «î» and «û» don't change the sound at all."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How does «la fête» sound?", "choices": ["'fet' (e as in 'bed')", "'fate'", "'feet'"], "answer": "'fet' (e as in 'bed')", "right": "Yes! ê sounds just like è.", "wrong": "«ê» is the open 'e' of 'bed', so «fête» sounds like 'fet'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Sometimes the hat is there to tell two words apart: «sur» (on) / «sûr» (sure), «du» (some) / «dû» (owed), «mur» (wall) / «mûr» (ripe). They sound identical!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis sûr.", "en": "I'm sure.", "note": "A woman says «Je suis sûre.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one means 'sure'?", "choices": ["sur", "sûr", "sùr"], "answer": "sûr", "right": "Correct! «sûr» with a hat = sure.", "wrong": "'Sure' is «sûr». «sur» without the hat means 'on', and «sùr» doesn't exist."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Listen for the ô: «notre» (our) has an open o, like in 'not', but «le nôtre» (ours) has a closed 'oh'. The hat closes the sound."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has a closed 'oh' sound, like 'go' without the glide?", "choices": ["l'hôtel", "la fête", "la pâte"], "answer": "l'hôtel", "right": "Yes! ô = closed 'oh'.", "wrong": "«ô» gives the closed 'oh', as in «l'hôtel». «fête» has the open 'e' and «pâte» has 'ah'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: the circumflex often replaces an old S (forêt = forest). ê = the è sound, ô = closed 'oh', and â/î/û barely change. It also tells pairs like sur/sûr apart."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The hotel is in the forest.", "answer": "L'hôtel est dans la forêt.", "accept": ["l'hôtel est dans la forêt", "L'hôtel est dans la forêt", "L’hôtel est dans la forêt.", "l’hôtel est dans la forêt"], "hint": "L'h_tel ... la for_t", "right": "Superb! Both hats are in the right place.", "wrong": "The answer is «L'hôtel est dans la forêt.», with ô in hôtel and ê in forêt."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «La fête est à l'hôtel, près de la forêt.»", "fr": "La fête est à l'hôtel, près de la forêt."}]}, "4-6": {"stageId": "4-6", "title": "The tréma", "goal": "Understand that the tréma (¨) splits two vowels so each one is pronounced, as in maïs, Noël and naïve.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Meet the tréma «¨», two little dots. Its job is simple: it says 'pronounce this vowel separately from the one before it'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Normally «ai» makes one sound, as in «mais» (but), which sounds like 'meh'. But in «maïs» (sweetcorn), the dots split it into two: 'ma-ees'. And yes, here you say the s!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le maïs", "en": "sweetcorn", "note": "ma-ïs: 2 syllables"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word means 'but'?", "choices": ["mais", "maïs", "maïse"], "answer": "mais", "right": "Yes! «mais» = but (one syllable: 'meh').", "wrong": "'But' is «mais», one sound: 'meh'. «maïs» (ma-ees) is sweetcorn."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More examples: «naïve» (na-eev), «haïr» (to hate: a-eer), «laïque» (secular: la-eek), «la faïence» (earthenware: fa-yahns)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "It works on E too: «Noël» (Christmas) is 'no-el', not 'nole'. «le canoë» is 'ka-no-ay'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How many syllables are in «Noël»?", "choices": ["1", "2", "3"], "answer": "2", "right": "Correct: No-ël!", "wrong": "The dots split o and e: No-ël, so 2 syllables."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Without the dots, «ai» or «oe» would blend into one sound. Think of the tréma as a little wall between two vowels."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «naïve»?", "choices": ["'nave' (one syllable)", "'na-eev' (two syllables)", "'nay-vee'"], "answer": "'na-eev' (two syllables)", "right": "Parfait! na-ïve.", "wrong": "The tréma on ï splits the vowels: 'na-eev'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A special case: «aiguë» or «aigüe» (high-pitched, feminine). Without the dots, «gue» would sound like the plain 'g' at the end of «la langue». With the dots, you pronounce the u: 'ay-gü'. Both spellings are correct; «aigüe» is the newer one."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle a une voix aiguë.", "en": "She has a high-pitched voice."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Why is there a tréma in «haïr»?", "choices": ["To make the h pronounced", "To pronounce a and i separately", "To make the r silent"], "answer": "To pronounce a and i separately", "right": "Exactly! a-ïr, two sounds.", "wrong": "The tréma always splits vowels: «haïr» = a-eer. The h stays silent and the r is pronounced."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Here's a sentence to practise them all at once:"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Anaïs mange du maïs à Noël sur son canoë.", "en": "Anaïs eats sweetcorn at Christmas in her canoe."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: ¨ = say the two vowels separately. maïs (ma-ees) vs mais (meh), Noël, naïve, haïr, canoë."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Merry Christmas!", "answer": "Joyeux Noël !", "accept": ["joyeux noël", "Joyeux Noël!", "Joyeux Noël", "joyeux Noël"], "hint": "Joyeux N...", "right": "Joyeux Noël to you too! 🎄", "wrong": "The answer is «Joyeux Noël !», with ë in Noël."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Anaïs mange du maïs à Noël sur son canoë.»", "fr": "Anaïs mange du maïs à Noël sur son canoë."}]}, "4-7": {"stageId": "4-7", "title": "The cedilla", "goal": "Know that ç always sounds like 's' and is used only before a, o and u.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Remember: before a, o or u, C sounds like 'k' (as in «camion»). But what if we want an 's' sound there? We add a little tail: «ç». It's called the cédille."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le garçon", "en": "the boy", "note": "gar-SON. Without the cédille it would be 'gar-kon'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More examples: «la leçon» (lesson), «le reçu» (receipt), «la façade» (front of a building), «le caleçon» (boxer shorts), and of course «ça va ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«ça» (that) and «sa» (his/her) sound exactly the same. The cédille only changes the spelling."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How is «ç» pronounced?", "choices": ["s", "k", "ch"], "answer": "s", "right": "Yes! ç is always 's'.", "wrong": "ç is always pronounced 's'. That's its only job!"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "You will NEVER see ç before e or i, because C is already soft there: «acide», «décevant», «recevoir»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which spelling is correct?", "choices": ["la leçon", "la lecon", "la lesçon"], "answer": "la leçon", "right": "Bravo! We need ç before o to get the 's' sound.", "wrong": "It's «la leçon». Without the cédille, «lecon» would sound like 'luh-kon'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The cédille matters most in verbs. In «recevoir» (to receive), c comes before e, so it's already 's'. But in «je reçois», c comes before o, so we need ç to keep the 's' sound."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je reçois un message.", "en": "I receive a message."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Same thing with «remplacer» (to replace) → «remplaçant» (replacing, or a substitute player), and «commencer» (to start) → «nous commençons»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: nous commen__ons (we start)", "choices": ["c", "ç", "ss"], "answer": "ç", "right": "Perfect: «commençons». The ç keeps the 's' sound before o.", "wrong": "Before o we need ç to keep the 's' sound: «nous commençons»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word needs NO cédille?", "choices": ["gar_on", "fa_ade", "dé_evant"], "answer": "dé_evant", "right": "Exactly! Before e, C is already 's': «décevant».", "wrong": "«décevant» has c before e, so it's already soft. «garçon» and «façade» need ç because the next letter is o or a."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le garçon français reçoit un caleçon pour Noël.", "en": "The French boy gets boxer shorts for Christmas."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: ç = 's' before a, o or u, and never before e or i. Watch out for verbs: recevoir → je reçois, commencer → nous commençons."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The boy is French.", "answer": "Le garçon est français.", "accept": ["le garçon est français", "Le garçon est français"], "hint": "gar_on ... fran_ais", "right": "Très bien: two cédilles!", "wrong": "The answer is «Le garçon est français.», with ç in both garçon and français."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le garçon français reçoit un caleçon pour Noël.»", "fr": "Le garçon français reçoit un caleçon pour Noël."}]}, "4-8": {"stageId": "4-8", "title": "AI & EI sounds", "goal": "Pronounce ai and ei as the open è sound, as in père.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Two letters, one sound! In French, «ai» and «ei» usually sound like the «è» in «père», the open 'e' of 'bed'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la laine", "en": "the wool", "note": "sounds like 'len'"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la reine", "en": "the queen", "note": "sounds like 'ren'"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Words with ai: «faire» (to do/make), «je t'aime», «je parlais» (I was talking), «le balai» (broom), «j'ai». Words with ei: «la neige» (snow), «treize» (13), «la peine» (sorrow)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Never say 'eye' or 'ay-ee'! «aime» is not 'aim'; it's 'em'. Keep your jaw still, with no glide."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How does «je t'aime» sound?", "choices": ["zhuh tem", "zhuh time", "zhuh taym"], "answer": "zhuh tem", "right": "Yes! ai = è, and the final e is silent.", "wrong": "«ai» sounds like è, so «je t'aime» = 'zhuh tem'. No 'eye', no 'ay'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In «j'ai» and in future endings like «je parlerai», many speakers use a more closed é. Both versions are understood, so don't worry about it for now."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the same vowel sound as «père»?", "choices": ["la neige", "le bébé", "le cou"], "answer": "la neige", "right": "Correct! ei = è.", "wrong": "«ei» sounds like the è in «père», as in «la neige». «bébé» has the crisp é, and «cou» has 'oo'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two things to watch out for. When ai or ei comes before n or m plus a consonant (or the end of the word), it becomes nasal: «le pain», «plein». We'll study that soon. And «ail» sounds like 'eye': «le travail» (work)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word does NOT have the è sound?", "choices": ["faire", "le pain", "treize"], "answer": "le pain", "right": "Well spotted! «ain» is a nasal sound.", "wrong": "In «le pain», ai + n makes a nasal sound. «faire» and «treize» have the open è."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ça me fait de la peine.", "en": "It makes me sad.", "note": "Literally: 'it gives me pain'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai de la laine pour faire un pull à la Reine des neiges.", "en": "I have some wool to make a jumper for the Snow Queen."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which spelling means 'thirteen'?", "choices": ["treize", "traize", "trèze"], "answer": "treize", "right": "Yes: «treize», with ei.", "wrong": "'Thirteen' is «treize». The other spellings sound the same but don't exist; that's why spelling French takes practice!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: ai and ei = the open è of «père». No 'eye', no 'ay'. Be careful with ain/ein (nasal) and ail ('eye')."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I love snow.", "answer": "J'aime la neige.", "accept": ["j'aime la neige", "J'aime la neige", "J’aime la neige.", "j’aime la neige"], "hint": "J'aime ... neige", "right": "Moi aussi! Perfect spelling.", "wrong": "The answer is «J'aime la neige.», with ai in aime and ei in neige."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'ai de la laine pour faire un pull à la Reine des neiges.»", "fr": "J'ai de la laine pour faire un pull à la Reine des neiges."}]}, "4-9": {"stageId": "4-9", "title": "AU & EAU sounds", "goal": "Pronounce au and eau as a pure, closed 'oh', and use au/aux meaning 'to the'.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Another 'many letters, one sound' trick: «au» and «eau» both sound like a closed 'oh'. It's like the 'o' in 'go', but without the 'w' glide at the end. Round your lips and keep them still."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "de l'eau", "en": "some water", "note": "«eau» = three letters, one sound: 'oh'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With eau: «beau» (beautiful), «le bateau» (boat), «le chapeau» (hat), «le gâteau» (cake), «le bureau» (office/desk), «beaucoup» (a lot). With au: «l'auto» (car), «chaud» (hot), «mauvais» (bad), «le cauchemar» (nightmare)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English speakers tend to say 'oh-oo'. In French, freeze your lips in the 'o' shape. The sound is short and pure."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the 'oh' sound?", "choices": ["le chapeau", "le cou", "la poule"], "answer": "le chapeau", "right": "Yes! «eau» = 'oh'.", "wrong": "«eau» makes the 'oh' sound, as in «chapeau». «cou» and «poule» have «ou», which is 'oo'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How many sounds are in «eau»?", "choices": ["1", "2", "3"], "answer": "1", "right": "Exactly: three letters, one sound, 'oh'.", "wrong": "«eau» is just ONE sound: 'oh'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For the plural, most -eau words add an x, and the x is silent: «un bateau» → «des bateaux». They sound the same!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai deux chapeaux.", "en": "I have two hats.", "note": "chapeaux = 'sha-po', with a silent x"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«au» is also a little word! à + le always becomes «au», and à + les becomes «aux». Both mean 'to the' or 'at the'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On va au restaurant ?", "en": "Shall we go to the restaurant?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: Je vais ___ cinéma.", "choices": ["au", "à le", "eau"], "answer": "au", "right": "Parfait! à + le = au.", "wrong": "«à le» always contracts to «au»: «Je vais au cinéma.» «eau» means water."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Don't confuse «au» with «ou»! au = 'oh', ou = 'oo'. Listen to the difference: «beau» (beautiful) / «bout» (end)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has 'oo', not 'oh'?", "choices": ["le bout", "le bateau", "l'auto"], "answer": "le bout", "right": "Well heard! «ou» = 'oo'.", "wrong": "«le bout» has «ou», which is 'oo'. «bateau» and «auto» have the 'oh' sound."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: au and eau = a pure, closed 'oh'. The plural x is silent (bateaux). au = à + le, aux = à + les. And au ('oh') is not ou ('oo')!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The cake is beautiful.", "answer": "Le gâteau est beau.", "accept": ["le gâteau est beau", "Le gâteau est beau", "le gateau est beau"], "hint": "gâteau / beau", "right": "Délicieux! Perfect.", "wrong": "The answer is «Le gâteau est beau.»: two words with eau = 'oh'."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il fait chaud : un gâteau et de l'eau sur le bateau !»", "fr": "Il fait chaud : un gâteau et de l'eau sur le bateau !"}]}, "4-10": {"stageId": "4-10", "title": "The nasal IN sound (ain, aim, ein, in, im, yn, ym)", "goal": "Recognise the seven spellings of the nasal 'in' sound and know when the sound is NOT nasal.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Time for a famous French nasal sound: the one in «le pain» and «le vin». Seven spellings, ONE sound: ain, aim, ein, in, im, yn, ym."}, {"t": "say", "text": "How to say it: start from the 'a' of 'cat', then let the air flow through your nose as well. Your tongue must NOT touch the roof of your mouth, so there's no n and no ng!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le pain", "en": "the bread"}, {"t": "say", "text": "ain/aim: «le train», «le copain» (friend), «demain» (tomorrow), «ainsi» (thus), «la faim» (hunger). in/im: «le vin», «le raisin» (grape), «le prince», «la fin» (the end), «malin» (clever), «intéressant», «un timbre» (stamp), «imbécile». ein: «plein» (full), «la peinture» (paint). ym/yn: «le thym» (thyme), «sympa» (nice)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Before b or p, the n becomes m: «un timbre», «imbécile», «impossible». It's the same sound."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word does NOT have the nasal 'in' sound?", "choices": ["la faim", "le vin", "la laine"], "answer": "la laine", "right": "Yes! In «laine», the n is followed by a vowel (e), so ai = è: 'len'.", "wrong": "«la laine» = 'len': the n is followed by an e, so it isn't nasal. «faim» and «vin» are nasal."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The golden rule: the vowel stops being nasal when the n or m is followed by a vowel or is doubled. «fin» (nasal) → «fine» ('feen'). «américain» → «américaine» ('ken'). «plein» → «pleine» ('plen')."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le vin est américain, la bière est américaine.", "en": "The wine is American, the beer is American.", "note": "américain = nasal / américaine = clear n"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «fine», is 'in' nasal?", "choices": ["Yes, it's nasal", "No, it's 'feen' with a clear n", "The n is silent"], "answer": "No, it's 'feen' with a clear n", "right": "Exactly! An e after the n cancels the nasal.", "wrong": "In «fine», the n is followed by e, so you say a clear 'ee' + n: 'feen'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the nasal sound?", "choices": ["image", "immense", "impossible"], "answer": "impossible", "right": "Bravo! im + p = nasal.", "wrong": "«impossible»: im + consonant (p) = nasal. In «image», m + vowel isn't nasal ('ee-mazh'), and in «immense» the double m isn't nasal either ('ee-mahns')."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Many French people today pronounce «un» exactly like this 'in' sound, so «brun» (brown) and «brin» (blade of grass) often sound alike."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai faim, je veux du pain !", "en": "I'm hungry, I want some bread!", "note": "Literally: 'I have hunger'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «J'ai faim» mean?", "choices": ["I'm hungry", "I'm tired", "I'm cold"], "answer": "I'm hungry", "right": "Yes! The French 'have' hunger: «avoir faim».", "wrong": "«J'ai faim» = I'm hungry (literally 'I have hunger'). Note «faim» = nasal 'in'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: ain, aim, ein, in, im, yn, ym = one nasal sound, with no n pronounced. If a vowel follows the n/m, or it's doubled, it's no longer nasal: fin / fine."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the bread and the wine", "answer": "le pain et le vin", "accept": ["le pain et le vin", "Le pain et le vin", "Le pain et le vin."], "hint": "pain / vin", "right": "Très français! Perfect.", "wrong": "The answer is «le pain et le vin»: two different spellings, same nasal sound."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Demain, mon copain prend le train pour acheter du pain et du vin.»", "fr": "Demain, mon copain prend le train pour acheter du pain et du vin."}]}, "4-11": {"stageId": "4-11", "title": "The CH sound", "goal": "Pronounce ch as 'sh', and recognise the Greek-origin words where ch = 'k'.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In French, «ch» almost always sounds like English 'sh', never like the 'ch' in 'church'!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Listen: «le chat» (cat), «le chapeau» (hat), «la bouche» (mouth), «la douche» (shower), «une écharpe» (scarf), «la chimie» (chemistry), «la chirurgie» (surgery)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le chat a un chapeau.", "en": "The cat has a hat.", "note": "'luh sha a un sha-po'"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«chimie» is 'shee-mee', even though English 'chemistry' has a k! And «architecte» is 'ar-shee-tekt'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «le chat»?", "choices": ["luh sha", "luh tcha", "luh ka"], "answer": "luh sha", "right": "Parfait! ch = sh, and the t is silent.", "wrong": "ch = 'sh' and the final t is silent, so it's 'luh sha'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The exceptions: some words from Greek, often scientific ones, have a 'k' sound: «l'orchestre», «la chronologie», «le chronomètre», «le psychologue», «un archéologue», «un archange», «le chœur» (choir), «le chaos»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "An easy clue: ch before r or l = 'k' (chrono, chlore). Otherwise, for scientific words you'll need to learn them one by one, because «chimie» and «architecte» are 'sh'!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has a 'k' sound?", "choices": ["l'orchestre", "la bouche", "le chapeau"], "answer": "l'orchestre", "right": "Yes! «orchestre» = 'or-kestr'.", "wrong": "«l'orchestre» comes from Greek and is pronounced 'or-kestr'. «bouche» and «chapeau» have the usual 'sh'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How does «la chronologie» start?", "choices": ["kro", "shro", "tchro"], "answer": "kro", "right": "Correct! ch + r = 'k'.", "wrong": "When ch comes before r, it's 'k': «chronologie» = 'kro-no-lo-zhee'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Trap! Which one has the 'sh' sound?", "choices": ["un architecte", "un archéologue", "both"], "answer": "un architecte", "right": "Bravo! 'ar-shee-tekt' but 'ar-kay-o-log'.", "wrong": "«architecte» = 'ar-shee-tekt' (sh), while «archéologue» = 'ar-kay-o-log' (k). You just have to learn these."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le psychologue écoute l'orchestre.", "en": "The psychologist listens to the orchestra.", "note": "Two 'k' sounds: psee-ko-log, or-kestr"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The English 'ch' of 'church' only appears in borrowed words spelt «tch»: «le match», «tchao» (bye)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: ch = 'sh' (chat, chapeau, chimie, architecte). Greek words and ch + r = 'k' (orchestre, chronologie, psychologue)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The cat is in the shower.", "answer": "Le chat est sous la douche.", "accept": ["le chat est sous la douche", "Le chat est sous la douche", "le chat est dans la douche", "Le chat est dans la douche."], "hint": "French says 'under the shower': sous la douche", "right": "Pauvre chat! Perfect French: you're 'under' the shower in French.", "wrong": "The answer is «Le chat est sous la douche.» The French say 'under the shower' (sous)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le chat met une écharpe et un chapeau pour écouter l'orchestre.»", "fr": "Le chat met une écharpe et un chapeau pour écouter l'orchestre."}]}, "4-12": {"stageId": "4-12", "title": "CH tongue twister: l'archiduchesse", "goal": "Master the s/sh contrast with France's most famous tongue twister.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's have fun! French children practise their 's' and 'sh' sounds with a famous «virelangue» (tongue twister):"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les chaussettes de l'archiduchesse sont-elles sèches, archi-sèches ?", "en": "Are the archduchess's socks dry, extra dry?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Let's break it down. «les chaussettes» (the socks): 'lay sho-SET'. ch = sh, ss = s, and the final s is silent."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "les chaussettes", "en": "the socks"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«l'archiduchesse» (the archduchess): 'lar-shee-dü-SHESS'. There are two 'sh' sounds, and the u is the French ü (say 'ee' with rounded lips)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How many 'sh' sounds are in «archiduchesse»?", "choices": ["1", "2", "3"], "answer": "2", "right": "Yes: ar-CHI-du-CHESSE!", "wrong": "There are 2: ar-CHI-du-CHESSE. Each ch = 'sh'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«sèches, archi-sèches» (dry, extra dry): 'sesh, ar-shee-sesh'. In everyday French, «archi-» means 'super / extremely': «C'est archi-faux !» (That's totally wrong!)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «sèches», what sound does «ch» make?", "choices": ["sh", "k", "tch"], "answer": "sh", "right": "Correct: 'sesh'.", "wrong": "ch = 'sh' here, so «sèches» = 'sesh'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«sont-elles ?» (are they?) is a question with the verb first. Link the t: 'son-TEL'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The t in «sont» is normally silent, but here it links to «elles». This is called a liaison. Say 'son-tel', not 'son… el'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «sont-elles» ?", "choices": ["son-tel", "son-el", "sont-ells (every letter)"], "answer": "son-tel", "right": "Perfect liaison!", "wrong": "The silent t comes back to life before a vowel: «sont-elles» = 'son-tel'. The final s stays silent."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «archi-sèches» mean?", "choices": ["extra dry", "architect-dry", "a bit dry"], "answer": "extra dry", "right": "Yes! archi- = super/extremely.", "wrong": "«archi-» is an intensifier: «archi-sèches» = extra dry."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Slowly first: «Les chaussettes de l'archiduchesse»", "fr": "Les chaussettes de l'archiduchesse"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Now the second half: «sont-elles sèches, archi-sèches ?»", "fr": "sont-elles sèches, archi-sèches ?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: ch = 'sh', s/ss = 's', the final s is silent, and there's a liaison in «sont-elles». Start slowly, then speed up!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the socks", "answer": "les chaussettes", "accept": ["les chaussettes", "Les chaussettes"], "hint": "ch... + ss + tt", "right": "Bravo! Double s, double t.", "wrong": "The answer is «les chaussettes», with ch, double s and double t."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Now the whole thing, as fast as you can: «Les chaussettes de l'archiduchesse sont-elles sèches, archi-sèches ?»", "fr": "Les chaussettes de l'archiduchesse sont-elles sèches, archi-sèches ?"}]}, "4-13": {"stageId": "4-13", "title": "É, ET, EZ, ER & ED sounds", "goal": "Recognise the spellings that make the crisp é sound (é, -er, -ez, -ed, et), plus the common -er exceptions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The crisp 'ay' sound of «é» has many spellings: é, -er, -ez, -ed, and the word «et». Learn them and you'll read French much more easily!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "The spelling «é»: «le bébé», «l'armée» (army), «le cinéma», «le déjeuner» (lunch), «les céréales»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "At the end of a word: -er (most verbs and many nouns): «marcher», «préparer», «le déjeuner». -ez: «vous marchez», «le nez» (nose), «répétez !». -ed: «le pied» (foot), «le trépied» (tripod). The r, z and d are all silent!"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Bonus: «marcher», «marché» and «marchez» all sound exactly the same: 'mar-shay'. Only the spelling changes the meaning."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What about the z in «vous marchez»?", "choices": ["The z is silent", "The z is pronounced", "The ch is silent"], "answer": "The z is silent", "right": "Yes! «marchez» = 'mar-shay'.", "wrong": "Final -ez = 'ay' with a silent z, so «vous marchez» = 'voo mar-shay'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Répétez, s'il vous plaît.", "en": "Repeat, please.", "note": "ré-pé-tez: three é sounds!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai mal au pied.", "en": "My foot hurts.", "note": "pied = 'pyay', with a silent d"}, {"t": "say", "text": "A trap! In some short words, -er ends with a pronounced r and an open è: «la mer» (sea), «hier» (yesterday), «cher» (expensive), «fier» (proud), «l'hiver» (winter)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word does NOT end in the 'ay' sound?", "choices": ["la mer", "le nez", "manger"], "answer": "la mer", "right": "Well spotted! «mer» = 'mair', and you say the r.", "wrong": "«la mer» is one of the exceptions: 'mair', with the r pronounced. «nez» and «manger» end in 'ay'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«et» (and) is always 'ay', and the t is silent: «toi et moi». The t is never linked, even before a vowel: «et un» = 'ay un'. Nouns ending in -et, like «le jouet» (toy), are often closer to è."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say «toi et moi»?", "choices": ["twa ay mwa", "twa et mwa", "twa ett mwa"], "answer": "twa ay mwa", "right": "Parfait! «et» = 'ay'.", "wrong": "«et» is always 'ay' with a silent t: 'twa ay mwa'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «le rez-de-chaussée» (the ground floor), how is «rez» pronounced?", "choices": ["ray", "rez", "ruh"], "answer": "ray", "right": "Yes! The z is silent.", "wrong": "«rez» = 'ray': -ez sounds like é and the z is silent."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which group sounds exactly the same?", "choices": ["marcher / marché / marchez", "mer / marché / marchez", "hier / marcher / pied"], "answer": "marcher / marché / marchez", "right": "Exactly: all three are 'mar-shay'.", "wrong": "«marcher», «marché» and «marchez» are all 'mar-shay'. «mer» and «hier» have a pronounced r."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: é, -er, -ez, -ed and «et» = crisp 'ay', with a silent final letter. Exceptions: mer, hier, cher, fier, hiver = 'air' with the r pronounced."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (polite form): Repeat, please.", "answer": "Répétez, s'il vous plaît.", "accept": ["répétez, s'il vous plaît", "répétez s'il vous plaît", "Répétez s'il vous plaît.", "répétez, s’il vous plaît", "Répétez, s'il vous plaît"], "hint": "Répét..., s'il vous plaît", "right": "Très bien! Three é sounds in a row.", "wrong": "The answer is «Répétez, s'il vous plaît.»: é, é, then -ez."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Vous marchez au marché et vous préparez le déjeuner.»", "fr": "Vous marchez au marché et vous préparez le déjeuner."}]}, "4-14": {"stageId": "4-14", "title": "EN, EM, AN & AM sounds", "goal": "Pronounce the nasal 'ahn' sound (an, am, en, em) and avoid the main traps: silent -ent in verbs, and 'en' after i.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Our second nasal sound: «an», «am», «en» and «em» all sound the same! Open your mouth as for the 'a' in 'father', and push the air through your nose. Don't say the n at the end."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Listen: «la plante», «blanc» (white), «pendant» (during), «trente» (30), «cent» (100), «le vent» (wind), «lentement» (slowly), «enchanté». Before b or p, n becomes m: «ambre» (amber), «ensemble» (together), «longtemps» (a long time)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Enchanté !", "en": "Nice to meet you!", "note": "Both 'en' and 'an' = the same nasal sound."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Nasal sounds love silent letters: in «cent», «le vent», «blanc» and «longtemps», the final t, c and ps are silent."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the nasal 'ahn' sound?", "choices": ["le vent", "le vin", "le cou"], "answer": "le vent", "right": "Yes! en = 'ahn'.", "wrong": "«le vent» has 'en' = 'ahn'. «vin» has the other nasal ('in'), and «cou» has 'oo'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Don't confuse it with «on», where the lips are rounded, as in «bon». Listen to these pairs: «lent» (slow) / «long» (long), «sans» (without) / «son» (sound), «le vent» / «ils vont» (they go)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«sans» means 'without'. How is it different from «son»?", "choices": ["sans = open 'ahn', son = rounded 'on'", "They sound identical", "sans has a clear n"], "answer": "sans = open 'ahn', son = rounded 'on'", "right": "Exactly! Open mouth for an/en, rounded lips for on.", "wrong": "They are two different nasal sounds: «sans» = open 'ahn', «son» = rounded 'on'. Neither one has a clear n."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Trap 1: «-ent» at the end of an ils/elles verb is completely silent: «ils parlent» = 'eel parl'. But in «lentement» or «le vent», -ent is nasal."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say «elles chantent»?", "choices": ["el shahnt", "el shahn-tahn", "el shan-tent"], "answer": "el shahnt", "right": "Perfect! The verb ending -ent is silent.", "wrong": "In verbs, the ils/elles ending -ent is silent: «elles chantent» = 'el shahnt'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Trap 2: after i, «en» sounds like the 'in' of «pain»: «bien», «le chien». And it isn't nasal when a vowel or a double n/m follows: «une année» ('a-nay')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word does NOT have the 'ahn' sound?", "choices": ["le chien", "la chambre", "trente"], "answer": "le chien", "right": "Well done! After i, 'ien' sounds like 'yin'.", "wrong": "In «le chien», ien = 'yin' (like in «pain»). «chambre» and «trente» have 'ahn'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pendant ce temps, le vent souffle lentement.", "en": "Meanwhile, the wind blows slowly."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: an, am, en, em = one nasal 'ahn', with no n pronounced. Careful: ils parlent (silent -ent), bien/chien ('yin'), and an/en ≠ on."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: thirty plants", "answer": "trente plantes", "accept": ["trente plantes", "Trente plantes"], "hint": "trente / plante", "right": "Bravo! Two nasal sounds, two silent endings.", "wrong": "The answer is «trente plantes». Both words have the nasal 'ahn', and the final -es is silent."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Nous chantons ensemble pendant longtemps.»", "fr": "Nous chantons ensemble pendant longtemps."}]}, "4-15": {"stageId": "4-15", "title": "The EU sound", "goal": "Pronounce the closed eu (peu, bleu) and the open eu (peur, jeune), and avoid confusing de/deux.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Now a sound English doesn't have: «eu». Here's the recipe: say «é», keep your tongue where it is, and round your lips as if to whistle. Voilà: «peu», «bleu», «deux»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "That's the closed «eu». You use it when nothing is pronounced after it: «peu» (little), «le feu» (fire), «il veut» (he wants), «mieux» (better), «vieux» (old), «deux» (two)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le ciel est bleu.", "en": "The sky is blue."}, {"t": "say", "text": "When a pronounced consonant follows, the eu opens up: drop your jaw a little and keep your lips rounded. It's close to the 'u' in British 'fur'. «la peur» (fear), «le beurre» (butter), «la couleur» (colour), «jeune» (young), «neuf» (nine), «le voleur» (thief)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the open eu (with a pronounced consonant after it)?", "choices": ["la peur", "peu", "bleu"], "answer": "la peur", "right": "Yes! The r is pronounced, so the eu opens.", "wrong": "In «la peur», the r is pronounced, so the eu is open. «peu» and «bleu» end on the sound, so they have the closed eu."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«peu» (little) and «peur» (fear) differ only by the r and how open your mouth is. «J'ai un peu peur» = I'm a bit scared."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai un peu peur.", "en": "I'm a little scared."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word does NOT contain the eu sound?", "choices": ["deux", "jeune", "douze"], "answer": "douze", "right": "Correct! «douze» has 'oo'.", "wrong": "«douze» (12) has «ou» = 'oo'. «deux» and «jeune» have eu."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Be careful with «de» and «deux»: «de» is a short, relaxed 'duh', but «deux» needs rounded, tense lips. «deux cafés» (two coffees) is very different from «de café»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which number is «neuf»?", "choices": ["9", "2", "12"], "answer": "9", "right": "Yes! «neuf» = 9 (it also means 'new').", "wrong": "«neuf» = 9 (and also 'new'). 2 is «deux» and 12 is «douze»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Big exception: «eu», the past participle of avoir, is pronounced like the French u: «j'ai eu» = 'zhay ü'. It's a completely different sound!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How is «eu» pronounced in «j'ai eu froid» (I was cold)?", "choices": ["like ü (French u)", "like «deux»", "like 'you'"], "answer": "like ü (French u)", "right": "Great! «eu» from avoir = ü.", "wrong": "The past participle «eu» (from avoir) is pronounced ü, like the French u: «j'ai eu» = 'zhay ü'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le vieux voleur a peur du feu.", "en": "The old thief is afraid of fire.", "note": "vieux, feu = closed / voleur, peur = open"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: closed eu at the end of a sound (peu, bleu, deux, feu). Open eu before a pronounced consonant (peur, beurre, jeune, neuf). de ≠ deux, and «j'ai eu» = ü."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The young thief is afraid.", "answer": "Le jeune voleur a peur.", "accept": ["le jeune voleur a peur", "Le jeune voleur a peur"], "hint": "jeune / voleur / avoir peur", "right": "Excellent! Three open eu sounds.", "wrong": "The answer is «Le jeune voleur a peur.» In French you 'have fear': avoir peur."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le vieux voleur a un peu peur du feu bleu.»", "fr": "Le vieux voleur a un peu peur du feu bleu."}]}, "4-16": {"stageId": "4-16", "title": "G & J sounds", "goal": "Pronounce J as a soft 'zh', know when G is soft or hard, and understand the silent e and u spelling tricks.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "J is easy in French: it's always the soft 'zh' sound in 'measure' or 'beige', never the 'dj' of English 'jam'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Listen: «jeune» (young), «joli» (pretty), «jaloux» (jealous), «jamais» (never), «le journal» (newspaper), «le déjeuner» (lunch), «ajuster» (to adjust)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Test yourself: no 'd' at the start! «Jean» is 'zhahn', not 'djeen'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How does «jamais» start?", "choices": ["zh (as in 'measure')", "dj (as in 'jam')", "y (as in 'yes')"], "answer": "zh (as in 'measure')", "right": "Yes! French J = soft 'zh'.", "wrong": "French J is always the soft 'zh' of 'measure', so «jamais» = 'zha-meh'. There's no d sound."}, {"t": "say", "text": "G has two voices, just like C. Before e, i or y it's a soft 'zh' (the same as J): «généralement», «gentiment» (nicely), «la magie», «la luge» (sledge), «une agence», «rougir» (to blush), «la gym». Before a, o, u or a consonant it's a hard 'g', as in 'go': «la gare» (station), «le goût» (taste), «grand»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has a SOFT g?", "choices": ["la magie", "la gare", "grand"], "answer": "la magie", "right": "Correct! G before i = 'zh': 'ma-zhee'.", "wrong": "G is soft before e, i or y: «la magie» = 'ma-zhee'. In «gare» (g + a) and «grand» (g + r), the g is hard."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has a HARD g?", "choices": ["le goût", "gentiment", "rougir"], "answer": "le goût", "right": "Yes! G before o = hard 'g'.", "wrong": "G before o is hard: «le goût» = 'luh goo'. In «gentiment» and «rougir», g comes before e or i, so it's soft."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Trick 1: to keep g soft before a or o, French adds a silent e: «manger» → «nous mangeons», «Georges», «il nageait» (he was swimming)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous mangeons à midi.", "en": "We eat at noon.", "note": "The e is silent; it just keeps the g soft."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Trick 2: to make g hard before e or i, add a silent u: «la guerre» (war), «la guitare», «la langue» (tongue/language)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: nous man___ons (we eat)", "choices": ["g", "ge", "gu"], "answer": "ge", "right": "Parfait! «mangeons»: the e keeps the 'zh' sound.", "wrong": "Before o, g would turn hard, so we add a silent e: «nous mangeons»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How is «la guitare» pronounced?", "choices": ["gee-tar (hard g, silent u)", "zhee-tar", "gwee-tar"], "answer": "gee-tar (hard g, silent u)", "right": "Exactly! The u just keeps the g hard.", "wrong": "In «guitare», the u is silent and only makes the g hard: 'gee-tar'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Généralement, le jeune joue gentiment.", "en": "Usually, the young boy plays nicely.", "note": "Every g and j here = 'zh'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: J = always 'zh'. G = 'zh' before e/i/y and hard 'g' before a/o/u or a consonant. Add a silent e to keep it soft (mangeons) and a silent u to keep it hard (guitare)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We're having lunch. (nous)", "answer": "Nous déjeunons.", "accept": ["nous déjeunons", "Nous déjeunons", "on déjeune", "On déjeune.", "nous dejeunons"], "hint": "déjeuner → nous ...", "right": "Bon appétit! Perfect.", "wrong": "The answer is «Nous déjeunons.» (or, more casually, «On déjeune.»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le jeune Georges mange une orange à la gare.»", "fr": "Le jeune Georges mange une orange à la gare."}]}, "4-17": {"stageId": "4-17", "title": "GN sound", "goal": "Pronounce French «gn» as one soft 'ny' sound, like in 'canyon', and recognise it in everyday words.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! Today we learn a lovely French sound: «gn». It is NOT a 'g' plus an 'n'. It is one soft sound, like the 'ny' in 'canyon' or the 'ni' in 'onion'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "How to make it: put the tip of your tongue behind your lower teeth. Then press the MIDDLE of your tongue up against the roof of your mouth and say 'n'. That is «gn»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la montagne", "en": "the mountain", "note": "Sounds like 'mon-ta-nyuh'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le champignon", "en": "the mushroom", "note": "'cham-pee-nyon' — no hard g!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «gn» in «montagne»?", "choices": ["Like 'g' + 'n', as in 'signal'", "Like 'ny' in 'canyon'", "Like a simple 'n'"], "answer": "Like 'ny' in 'canyon'", "right": "Exactement ! «gn» is one soft 'ny' sound: «montagne».", "wrong": "In French, «gn» is one sound: 'ny', like in 'canyon'. So «montagne» sounds like 'mon-ta-nyuh'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Feel the difference: «peine» (pain, sorrow) ends with a normal 'n'. «peigne» (comb) ends with 'ny'. Only your tongue changes!"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, slowly: «peine» … «peigne».", "fr": "peine, peigne"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est mignon, ce petit agneau !", "en": "This little lamb is cute!", "note": "Two «gn» sounds: «mignon», «agneau»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word contains the «gn» sound?", "choices": ["la signature", "la sirène", "le singe"], "answer": "la signature", "right": "Bravo ! «signature» = 'see-nya-tur'.", "wrong": "Look for the letters «g» + «n» together: «la signature» → 'see-nya-tur'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il y a une araignée dans la baignoire !", "en": "There's a spider in the bathtub!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «magnifique»?", "choices": ["'mag-nee-feek'", "'ma-nyee-feek'", "'ma-nee-feek'"], "answer": "'ma-nyee-feek'", "right": "Parfait ! Not like English 'magnificent' — the French «gn» is soft: 'ma-nyee-feek'.", "wrong": "English speakers often say a hard 'g' because of 'magnificent'. In French it's 'ma-nyee-feek', with the soft «gn»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Funny word: «oignon» (onion). The «i» is silent! Say 'o-nyon', just like the new spelling «ognon». Also, a few learned words keep a hard g + n: «diagnostic» ('dee-ag-nos-teek')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «oignon», which letter is silent?", "choices": ["the «g»", "the «i»", "the «n»"], "answer": "the «i»", "right": "Oui ! «oignon» = 'o-nyon'. The «i» is silent.", "wrong": "The «i» is silent: «oignon» sounds like 'o-nyon'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «gn» = 'ny', tongue in the middle of the roof of your mouth. «montagne», «champignon», «mignon», «agneau», «peigne», «magnifique». Never a hard 'g'!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the mountain", "answer": "la montagne", "accept": ["la montagne", "montagne"], "hint": "It ends in -agne.", "right": "Très bien ! «la montagne».", "wrong": "It's «la montagne» — m-o-n-t-a-g-n-e."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le magnifique rossignol chante dans la montagne.»", "fr": "Le magnifique rossignol chante dans la montagne."}]}, "4-18": {"stageId": "4-18", "title": "IEN sound", "goal": "Pronounce the nasal «ien» sound (as in «chien») and the related «ein» sound, and know when «-ienne» is not nasal.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's learn «ien», like in «chien» (dog). It is two parts glued together: a quick 'y' sound + the nasal vowel «in»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A nasal vowel means the air goes out through your nose AND mouth. For «in», smile a little, open your mouth, and say 'a' as in 'cat' while letting air through your nose. Do NOT say the 'n' at the end!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un chien", "en": "a dog", "note": "Sounds like 'shyan' (nasal), no 'n' at the end."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "rien", "en": "nothing", "note": "'ryan' (nasal) — one syllable."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «chien», do you pronounce the final «n»?", "choices": ["Yes, clearly", "No, the vowel becomes nasal", "Only before a consonant"], "answer": "No, the vowel becomes nasal", "right": "Exactement ! The «n» makes the vowel nasal, but you don't hear an 'n'.", "wrong": "The «n» is not pronounced. It turns the vowel into a nasal sound: «chien» = 'shyan' through the nose."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le musicien a un chien appelé Sébastien.", "en": "The musician has a dog called Sebastian."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Big trap: in the feminine «-ienne», the sound is NOT nasal. «musicien» (nasal) → «musicienne» = 'mu-zee-syen', with a clear 'n'. Same with «chien» → «chienne»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has a clear 'n' sound (not nasal)?", "choices": ["pharmacien", "pharmacienne", "bientôt"], "answer": "pharmacienne", "right": "Bravo ! «-ienne» = 'yen' with a real 'n'.", "wrong": "«-ien» is nasal, but «-ienne» has a real 'n': «pharmacienne» = 'far-ma-syen'."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say the pair: «musicien» … «musicienne».", "fr": "musicien, musicienne"}, {"t": "say", "text": "The nasal «in» sound can also be written «ein» or «ain»: «plein» (full), «teint» (complexion), «ceinture» (belt), «pain» (bread). Same sound, different spelling."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the same vowel sound as the end of «chien»?", "choices": ["plein", "pleine", "plan"], "answer": "plein", "right": "Oui ! «plein» = nasal «in», like the end of «chien».", "wrong": "«plein» has the nasal «in» sound. «pleine» has a real 'n', and «plan» is a different nasal («an»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Lucien est électricien, il vient de passer un entretien.", "en": "Lucien is an electrician; he has just had an interview."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How many syllables in «bientôt»?", "choices": ["2: 'byan-to'", "3: 'bee-an-to'", "4: 'bee-en-to-t'"], "answer": "2: 'byan-to'", "right": "Parfait ! The 'y' glides into the nasal: 'byan-to'.", "wrong": "The «i» is a quick 'y' glide, not a separate syllable: «bientôt» = 'byan-to', and the final «t» is silent."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ien» = quick 'y' + nasal «in», no 'n' at the end: «chien», «rien», «bientôt», «ancien». But «-ienne» has a real 'n'. And «ein» / «ain» sound like «in»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: a dog", "answer": "un chien", "accept": ["un chien", "chien"], "hint": "It starts with «ch».", "right": "Très bien ! «un chien».", "wrong": "It's «un chien» — c-h-i-e-n."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le musicien a un chien appelé Sébastien.»", "fr": "Le musicien a un chien appelé Sébastien."}]}, "4-19": {"stageId": "4-19", "title": "ILL sound", "goal": "Pronounce «ill» as a 'y' sound (as in «fille») and remember the common exceptions where it sounds like 'l' (ville, mille, tranquille).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Here is a surprise for English speakers: in French, «ill» usually sounds like 'y' in 'yes'. No 'l' at all!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "une fille", "en": "a girl", "note": "Sounds like 'fee-y' — NOT 'fill'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "une famille", "en": "a family", "note": "'fa-mee-y'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «fille»?", "choices": ["Like English 'fill'", "'fee-y'", "'feel'"], "answer": "'fee-y'", "right": "Exactement ! «ill» = 'ee' + 'y': «fille».", "wrong": "«ill» makes a 'y' sound. «fille» = 'fee-y'. Saying 'feel' or 'fill' is a very common mistake."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un papillon", "en": "a butterfly", "note": "'pa-pee-yon'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ma gentille fille aime la vanille.", "en": "My kind daughter likes vanilla."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «fille, famille, papillon, vanille».", "fr": "fille, famille, papillon, vanille"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the exceptions! A few common words keep a real 'l' sound: «ville» (town), «village», «mille» (thousand), «million», «milliard», «tranquille» (calm)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: 'a quiet town of a thousand millions' → «tranquille», «ville», «mille», «million», «milliard». These say 'l'. Almost everything else with «ill» says 'y'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word is pronounced with a real 'l' sound?", "choices": ["la fille", "la ville", "la famille"], "answer": "la ville", "right": "Bravo ! «ville» = 'veel'. It's one of the exceptions.", "wrong": "«ville» is an exception: 'veel', with an 'l'. «fille» and «famille» use the 'y' sound."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «gentille» (kind, feminine)?", "choices": ["'jan-teel'", "'jan-tee-y'", "'jen-till'"], "answer": "'jan-tee-y'", "right": "Parfait ! «gentille» uses the 'y' sound.", "wrong": "«gentille» follows the normal rule: 'y' sound → 'jan-tee-y' (nasal «en»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "After a vowel, «il» or «ille» also makes 'y': «travail» ('tra-va-y'), «soleil» ('so-lay'), «abeille» (bee)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Mille» means 1,000. How do you say it?", "choices": ["'meel'", "'mee-y'", "'mill-uh'"], "answer": "'meel'", "right": "Oui ! «mille» = 'meel' — an exception.", "wrong": "«mille» is an exception: 'meel'. Remember: «ville, village, mille, million, milliard, tranquille»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ill» = 'y' (fille, famille, papillon, aiguille, brouillon). Exceptions with 'l': ville, village, mille, million, milliard, tranquille."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: a girl", "answer": "une fille", "accept": ["une fille", "fille"], "hint": "Double «l» at the end.", "right": "Très bien ! «une fille».", "wrong": "It's «une fille» — f-i-l-l-e."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ma famille habite dans un village tranquille.»", "fr": "Ma famille habite dans un village tranquille."}]}, "4-20": {"stageId": "4-20", "title": "Œ sound", "goal": "Read the letter «œ» and pronounce the rounded 'eu' sound in words like «sœur», «cœur» and «œuf».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Look at this letter: «œ». It's an «o» and an «e» tied together. You find it in very common words like «sœur» (sister) and «cœur» (heart)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The sound: say 'uh' as in 'fur' (without the r), then round your lips like for a kiss. That's it! Your tongue stays low and relaxed."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "ma sœur", "en": "my sister", "note": "Rhymes with «peur» (fear)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "mon cœur", "en": "my heart"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the same vowel sound as «sœur»?", "choices": ["la fleur", "la sur", "le sous"], "answer": "la fleur", "right": "Bravo ! «œu» in «sœur» and «eu» in «fleur» make the same sound.", "wrong": "The spelling «eu» often makes the same sound as «œu»: «fleur», «peur», «sœur», «cœur»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un œuf", "en": "an egg", "note": "Say the «f»: 'uhf' with rounded lips."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Magic trick: in the plural, «œufs» and «bœufs» lose the «f» sound AND the vowel closes: «des œufs» = 'day-zuh', «des bœufs» = 'day buh'. Natives do this all the time."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «des œufs» (eggs)?", "choices": ["'day-zuhf'", "'day-zuh'", "'day-oof'"], "answer": "'day-zuh'", "right": "Parfait ! Plural «œufs»: the «f» is silent, and the «s» of «des» links with a 'z'.", "wrong": "In the plural, the «f» is silent: «des œufs» = 'day-zuh' (with a liaison 'z')."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un œil", "en": "an eye", "note": "'uh-y' — the plural is totally different: «des yeux»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ma sœur veut un œuf.", "en": "My sister wants an egg."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which spelling is correct for 'heart'?", "choices": ["le cœur", "le cuer", "le coer"], "answer": "le cœur", "right": "Oui ! «cœur» with «œ».", "wrong": "It's «le cœur», with the tied letters «œ» + «u» + «r»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "On a keyboard without «œ», people write «oe»: «soeur», «coeur». It's accepted, but «œ» is the proper spelling."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«l'œuvre de Picasso» — what does «œuvre» mean?", "choices": ["the eye", "the body of work", "the egg"], "answer": "the body of work", "right": "Exactement ! «une œuvre» = a work (of art). English borrowed it as 'oeuvre'.", "wrong": "«l'œuvre» = the work / body of work. English uses 'oeuvre' too."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «œ» = 'uh' with rounded lips: «sœur», «cœur», «œuf», «bœuf», «œuvre». Plural «œufs», «bœufs» → 'uh', 'buh' with a silent «f»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: my sister", "answer": "ma sœur", "accept": ["ma sœur", "ma soeur", "sœur", "soeur"], "hint": "«ma» + the word with «œ».", "right": "Très bien ! «ma sœur».", "wrong": "It's «ma sœur» (you may type «ma soeur»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ma sœur veut un œuf.»", "fr": "Ma sœur veut un œuf."}]}, "4-21": {"stageId": "4-21", "title": "OI sound", "goal": "Pronounce «oi» as 'wa' (as in «moi», «roi») and recognise common homophones like «voix / voie».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Easy one today! In French, «oi» is always pronounced 'wa', like in 'what' or 'water'. «moi» = 'mwa', «toi» = 'twa'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le roi", "en": "the king", "note": "'luh rwa' — with the French throat «r»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la joie", "en": "joy", "note": "'jwa' — the final «e» is silent."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «moi»?", "choices": ["'moy'", "'mwa'", "'mo-ee'"], "answer": "'mwa'", "right": "Exactement ! «oi» = 'wa': «moi» = 'mwa'.", "wrong": "«oi» is always 'wa' in French. «moi» = 'mwa', never 'moy'."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «moi, toi, le roi, la joie».", "fr": "moi, toi, le roi, la joie"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Because final letters are often silent, many words sound exactly the same: «soi», «soit», «la soie» (silk) are all 'swa'. «la voix» (voice) and «la voie» (path, lane) are both 'vwa'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which two words sound exactly the same?", "choices": ["la voix / la voie", "le roi / la reine", "la joie / le jouet"], "answer": "la voix / la voie", "right": "Bravo ! Both are 'vwa'. Only the spelling and context differ.", "wrong": "«la voix» (voice) and «la voie» (path) are both pronounced 'vwa' — the endings are silent."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La boîte est droite.", "en": "The box is straight.", "note": "Here the «t» is pronounced: 'bwat', 'drwat'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch the endings: «droit» ('drwa') has a silent «t», but «droite» ('drwat') ends in «e», so you hear the «t». Same in «maladroit» ('ma-la-drwa')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «Le roi est maladroit», is the final «t» of «maladroit» pronounced?", "choices": ["Yes", "No", "Only in the plural"], "answer": "No", "right": "Oui, it's silent: 'ma-la-drwa'.", "wrong": "A final consonant without «e» after it is usually silent: «maladroit» = 'ma-la-drwa'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word contains the 'wa' sound?", "choices": ["la boîte", "la botte", "le bout"], "answer": "la boîte", "right": "Parfait ! «boîte» = 'bwat'.", "wrong": "Look for «oi»: «la boîte» = 'bwat'. The other words have no «oi»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le roi est maladroit.", "en": "The king is clumsy."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «oi» = 'wa', always: «moi», «toi», «roi», «joie», «voix», «boîte», «droite». Final consonants are usually silent unless an «e» follows."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the king", "answer": "le roi", "accept": ["le roi", "roi"], "hint": "Three letters, with «oi».", "right": "Très bien ! «le roi».", "wrong": "It's «le roi» — r-o-i."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le roi est maladroit, mais la boîte est droite.»", "fr": "Le roi est maladroit, mais la boîte est droite."}]}, "4-22": {"stageId": "4-22", "title": "OIN sound", "goal": "Pronounce «oin» as 'w' + nasal «in» (as in «loin», «moins») and tell it apart from «oi».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "You know «oi» = 'wa'. Add an «n» and it changes: «oin» = 'w' + the nasal vowel «in». Think 'w' + 'an' through the nose, with no 'n' at the end."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For the nasal «in»: smile a little, open your mouth as for 'a' in 'cat', and let air out through your nose. Don't touch your tongue to your teeth — no 'n'!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "loin", "en": "far", "note": "'lwan' (nasal)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le coin", "en": "the corner", "note": "'kwan' (nasal)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «loin», what do you hear at the end?", "choices": ["A clear 'n' sound", "A nasal vowel, no 'n'", "A 'g' sound"], "answer": "A nasal vowel, no 'n'", "right": "Exactement ! The «n» only makes the vowel nasal.", "wrong": "You don't pronounce the «n». It makes the vowel nasal: «loin» = 'lwan' through the nose."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Minimal pairs train your ear: «moi» ('mwa', me) vs «moins» ('mwan', less); «la loi» (law) vs «loin» (far); «la foi» (faith) vs «le foin» (hay)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say the pairs: «moi … moins», «loi … loin», «foi … foin».", "fr": "moi, moins, loi, loin, foi, foin"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word means 'less'?", "choices": ["moi", "moins", "mois"], "answer": "moins", "right": "Bravo ! «moins» = 'mwan' (nasal), and the «s» is silent.", "wrong": "«moins» (less) has the nasal «oin» sound. «moi» = me, «mois» = month (both 'mwa')."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un point", "en": "a point / a dot", "note": "Silent «t»: 'pwan'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "L'adjoint est dans le coin, loin de la botte de foin.", "en": "The deputy is in the corner, far from the haystack."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word contains the «oin» sound?", "choices": ["la joie", "joindre", "le jour"], "answer": "joindre", "right": "Parfait ! «joindre» = 'jwan-dr'.", "wrong": "Look for «oin»: «joindre» (to join) = 'jwan-dr'. «joie» = 'jwa'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How many sounds are pronounced in «soin» (care)?", "choices": ["'swa' + 'n'", "'swan' (nasal, no 'n')", "'so-een'"], "answer": "'swan' (nasal, no 'n')", "right": "Oui ! «soin» = 's' + 'w' + nasal «in».", "wrong": "«soin» = 'swan' with a nasal vowel, and no 'n' pronounced."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «oin» = 'w' + nasal «in»: «loin», «coin», «point», «moins», «soin», «foin», «joindre». Compare with «oi» = 'wa'."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: far", "answer": "loin", "accept": ["loin"], "hint": "4 letters, with «oin».", "right": "Très bien ! «loin».", "wrong": "It's «loin» — l-o-i-n."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «L'adjoint est dans le coin, loin de la botte de foin.»", "fr": "L'adjoint est dans le coin, loin de la botte de foin."}]}, "4-23": {"stageId": "4-23", "title": "ON & OM sounds", "goal": "Pronounce the nasal vowel «on/om» (as in «bon», «ombre») and know when «on» is not nasal (as in «bonne»).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«on» is one of the most common French sounds: «bon», «non», «mon», «le monde». It's a nasal vowel: round your lips like for 'oh', then let air out through your nose. No 'n' at the end!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "bon", "en": "good", "note": "Not 'bonn' — just a round nasal 'oh'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le bonbon", "en": "the sweet / candy", "note": "Two nasal sounds: 'bon-bon'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «non»?", "choices": ["Like English 'non' with a clear 'n' at the end", "A nasal 'oh' after the first 'n', no final 'n'", "'noon'"], "answer": "A nasal 'oh' after the first 'n', no final 'n'", "right": "Exactement ! Only the first «n» is a real 'n'. The final one makes the vowel nasal.", "wrong": "The final «n» isn't pronounced: «non» = 'n' + nasal «on»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Before «b» or «p», French writes «om» instead of «on», but the sound is the same: «ombre» (shade), «bombe», «le pompier» (firefighter), «comprendre»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le pompier ne comprend pas.", "en": "The firefighter doesn't understand."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In which word does «om» sound like «on»?", "choices": ["l'ombre", "l'homme", "la pomme"], "answer": "l'ombre", "right": "Bravo ! «om» + «b» → nasal: «ombre».", "wrong": "«om» is nasal only before «b» or «p»: «ombre». In «homme» and «pomme», the double «m» is a real 'm' sound."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Big trap: if «n» is doubled or followed by a vowel, it's NOT nasal. «bon» (nasal) but «bonne» = 'bonn', «bonheur» = 'bo-nur'. Same: «son» vs «sonner»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word does NOT have a nasal sound?", "choices": ["bon", "bonne", "bonbon"], "answer": "bonne", "right": "Parfait ! «bonne» = 'bonn', with a clear 'n'.", "wrong": "«nn» + vowel = a real 'n', no nasal: «bonne» = 'bonn'. «bon» and «bonbon» are nasal."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say the pair: «bon … bonne».", "fr": "bon, bonne"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le plomb» (lead, the metal): is the final «b» pronounced?", "choices": ["Yes", "No"], "answer": "No", "right": "Oui, it's silent: «plomb» = 'plon' (nasal).", "wrong": "The «b» is silent: «plomb» = 'plon' (nasal)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mon garçon conduit un long camion marron sur le pont.", "en": "My boy drives a long brown truck on the bridge.", "note": "Count the nasal «on» sounds: there are six!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «on» / «om» (before b, p) = nasal round 'oh', no 'n': «bon», «monde», «oncle», «ombre», «pompier». But «onne», «omme», «on» + vowel = not nasal."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the world", "answer": "le monde", "accept": ["le monde", "monde"], "hint": "It starts with «mon».", "right": "Très bien ! «le monde».", "wrong": "It's «le monde» — m-o-n-d-e."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Mon garçon conduit un long camion marron sur le pont.»", "fr": "Mon garçon conduit un long camion marron sur le pont."}]}, "4-24": {"stageId": "4-24", "title": "OU sound", "goal": "Pronounce «ou» as in English 'food' and clearly distinguish it from the French «u» sound.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Good news: «ou» sounds like 'oo' in 'food' or 'you'. «nous» = 'noo', «pour» = 'poor'. Push your lips forward into a small round circle — more than in English."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "nous", "en": "we", "note": "Final «s» silent: 'noo'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le jour", "en": "the day"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «pour» (for)?", "choices": ["'pour' like English 'pour'", "'poor' with a round 'oo'", "'pur' like French «pur»"], "answer": "'poor' with a round 'oo'", "right": "Exactement ! «ou» = 'oo', with very round lips.", "wrong": "«ou» = 'oo' as in 'food': «pour» = 'poor' (with a French «r»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The real challenge: don't confuse «ou» with the French «u». For «u», say 'ee' and then round your lips without moving your tongue. «tu» (you) vs «tout» (all) are different words!"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Minimal pairs: «tu / tout», «vu / vous», «la rue (street) / la roue (wheel)», «dessus (on top) / dessous (underneath)». Mixing them changes the meaning!"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say the pairs: «tu … tout», «la rue … la roue».", "fr": "tu, tout, la rue, la roue"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word means 'the wheel'?", "choices": ["la rue", "la roue", "la ruse"], "answer": "la roue", "right": "Bravo ! «la roue» = 'roo'. «la rue» is the street.", "wrong": "«ou» = 'oo': «la roue» is the wheel. «la rue» (with French «u») is the street."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word contains the «ou» sound?", "choices": ["la jupe", "le hibou", "le bus"], "answer": "le hibou", "right": "Parfait ! «hibou» (owl) = 'ee-boo', and the «h» is silent.", "wrong": "Look for «ou»: «le hibou» = 'ee-boo'. «jupe» and «bus» have the French «u»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La mouche se mouche.", "en": "The fly blows its nose.", "note": "A fun tongue-twister: «la mouche» (fly), «se moucher» (to blow your nose)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«ou» (or) and «où» (where) sound exactly the same: 'oo'. The accent only shows the meaning."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu viens avec nous ?» Which words have the «ou» sound?", "choices": ["only «tu»", "only «nous»", "«tu» and «nous»"], "answer": "only «nous»", "right": "Oui ! «nous» = 'noo'. «tu» has the French «u».", "wrong": "Only «nous» has «ou». «tu» uses the French «u» (say 'ee' with round lips)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je mouline toujours la soupe pour nous.", "en": "I always blend the soup for us."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ou» = 'oo' with lips pushed forward: «nous», «jour», «pour», «hibou», «soupe». Don't mix it up with «u»: «tu / tout», «rue / roue»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: we", "answer": "nous", "accept": ["nous"], "hint": "4 letters, silent «s».", "right": "Très bien ! «nous».", "wrong": "It's «nous» — n-o-u-s."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je mouline toujours la soupe pour nous.»", "fr": "Je mouline toujours la soupe pour nous."}]}, "4-25": {"stageId": "4-25", "title": "PSY, PSO & PSEU sounds", "goal": "Pronounce the «p» in French words starting with «ps-» and say «psych-» with a 'k' sound.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In English, the 'p' in 'psychology' is silent. In French, it's NOT! You say both letters: 'ps'. «psychologie» = 'psee-ko-lo-jee'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Trick: close your lips for 'p', then open them straight into 's', like at the end of 'tips' or 'cups'. Now put that at the start of the word."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la psychologie", "en": "psychology", "note": "'psee-ko-lo-jee'."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Practise the start: «ps… ps… psy».", "fr": "psy, psy, psy"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In French «psychologie», is the «p» pronounced?", "choices": ["Yes, you hear 'ps'", "No, it's silent like in English", "Only in formal speech"], "answer": "Yes, you hear 'ps'", "right": "Exactement ! French says the «p»: 'psee-ko-lo-jee'.", "wrong": "Unlike English, French pronounces the «p»: «psychologie» = 'psee-ko-lo-jee'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Second point: in «psych-», the «ch» usually sounds like 'k', not 'sh'. «psychiatre» = 'psee-kyatr', «psychose» = 'psee-koz', «psychanalyse» = 'psee-ka-na-leez'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How is «ch» pronounced in «la psychose»?", "choices": ["'sh'", "'k'", "'tch'"], "answer": "'k'", "right": "Bravo ! «psychose» = 'psee-koz'.", "wrong": "In «psych-» words, «ch» = 'k': «psychose» = 'psee-koz'. The «s» between vowels sounds like 'z'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le / la psychologue", "en": "the psychologist", "note": "Same form for a man or a woman."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le psoriasis", "en": "psoriasis", "note": "'pso-rya-zees' — the final «s» is pronounced here."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un pseudonyme", "en": "a pseudonym", "note": "'pseu-do-neem'. In daily life people say «un pseudo» — a username or nickname online."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Natives shorten a lot: «mon psy» = my therapist (very common, familier). «Quel est ton pseudo ?» = What's your username?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Je vais chez mon psy» mean?", "choices": ["I'm going to my therapist's.", "I'm going to my psychology class.", "I'm going to my friend's."], "answer": "I'm going to my therapist's.", "right": "Parfait ! «psy» is the everyday short form for psychologist or psychiatrist.", "wrong": "«mon psy» = my therapist (psychologist/psychiatrist). «chez» = at someone's place."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which pronunciation of «psychiatre» is correct?", "choices": ["'see-kyatr'", "'psee-kyatr'", "'psee-shyatr'"], "answer": "'psee-kyatr'", "right": "Oui ! 'p' pronounced, «ch» = 'k'.", "wrong": "Two rules: say the «p», and «ch» = 'k': «psychiatre» = 'psee-kyatr'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: French «ps-» = say both 'p' and 's': «psychologie», «psoriasis», «pseudo». In «psych-», «ch» = 'k'. And «mon psy» is how natives say 'my therapist'."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: (the) psychology", "answer": "la psychologie", "accept": ["la psychologie", "psychologie"], "hint": "Starts with «psych-», ends with «-logie».", "right": "Très bien ! «la psychologie».", "wrong": "It's «la psychologie» — p-s-y-c-h-o-l-o-g-i-e."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Mon amie doit aller voir un psychologue.»", "fr": "Mon amie doit aller voir un psychologue."}]}, "4-26": {"stageId": "4-26", "title": "R sound", "goal": "Produce the French throat «r» and know when a final «r» is silent (as in «parler»).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The French «r» is famous! It's not rolled like Spanish, and not like English 'r'. It's made at the back of your throat — a soft, light friction, a bit like a gentle gargle."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Step by step: 1) Put the tip of your tongue down behind your lower teeth and keep it there. 2) Say 'k' or 'g' — feel where the back of your tongue touches? 3) Now don't touch completely: just let air rub there. That rough, soft sound is the French «r»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Practice trick: say 'ag-a', then make the 'g' softer and softer, with air passing: 'a-ra'. Gargling water is great practice too!"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Try it: «ra, ré, ri, ro, ru».", "fr": "ra, ré, ri, ro, ru"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Where is the tip of your tongue for the French «r»?", "choices": ["Curled up, like English 'r'", "Down, behind the lower teeth", "Tapping the top of the mouth"], "answer": "Down, behind the lower teeth", "right": "Exactement ! The tongue tip stays down; the sound is made at the back.", "wrong": "Keep the tongue tip down behind your lower teeth. The French «r» is made at the back of the throat, not with the tip of the tongue."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "rouge", "en": "red", "note": "Start straight from the throat."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il arrivera demain.", "en": "He will arrive tomorrow.", "note": "Double «rr» sounds like a single «r»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The hardest positions: «r» after a consonant («très», «grand», «prendre») and at the end of a word («bonjour», «mer»). Keep it light — don't force it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «parler» (to speak), is the final «r» pronounced?", "choices": ["Yes", "No, «-er» verbs sound like 'é'", "Only in questions"], "answer": "No, «-er» verbs sound like 'é'", "right": "Bravo ! «parler» = 'par-lé'. Infinitives in «-er» end with an 'é' sound.", "wrong": "In «-er» infinitives, the «r» is silent: «parler» = 'par-lé'. But in short words like «la mer», «hiver», «fer», you do say it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «la mer» (the sea), is the final «r» pronounced?", "choices": ["Yes", "No"], "answer": "Yes", "right": "Oui ! «mer» = 'mair' with a French «r». Final «r» is usually pronounced, except in «-er» verbs and many «-er» nouns like «le boulanger».", "wrong": "Here it's pronounced: «la mer». The silent «r» is mainly in «-er» verbs («parler») and words like «le boulanger»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le rhododendron", "en": "rhododendron", "note": "«rh» = just «r». The «h» is silent."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «rh» in «le rhume» (a cold)?", "choices": ["Like a French «r»", "Like 'h'", "Like 'rh' with a breath"], "answer": "Like a French «r»", "right": "Parfait ! «h» is silent in French: «rhume» = 'rum' (with French «u»).", "wrong": "The «h» is silent, so «rh» = «r»: «le rhume»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: French «r» = tongue tip down, soft friction at the back of the throat. «rr» = «r». «rh» = «r». Final «-er» in verbs is silent («parler»)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: red", "answer": "rouge", "accept": ["rouge"], "hint": "Starts with «r», ends with «-ge».", "right": "Très bien ! «rouge».", "wrong": "It's «rouge» — r-o-u-g-e."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il arrivera et réparera la voiture rouge.»", "fr": "Il arrivera et réparera la voiture rouge."}]}, "4-27": {"stageId": "4-27", "title": "S, SS & SC sounds", "goal": "Know when «s» sounds like 's' or 'z', and how to pronounce «ss» and «sc».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The letter «s» has two sounds in French: 's' (as in 'sun') or 'z' (as in 'zoo'). There are simple rules!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 1: one «s» between two vowels = 'z'. «rose» = 'roz', «la musique» = 'mu-zeek', «amusant» = 'a-mu-zan'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la musique", "en": "music", "note": "'mu-zeek'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 2: at the start of a word, or next to a consonant, «s» = 's': «salut», «le silence», «sage», «l'espoir», «insulter». Rule 3: «ss» = always 's'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Famous trap: «le poison» ('pwa-zon') vs «le poisson» ('pwa-son', fish). Say the wrong one at a restaurant and you'll get funny looks!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word means 'fish'?", "choices": ["le poison", "le poisson", "la boisson"], "answer": "le poisson", "right": "Bravo ! «poisson» with «ss» = 's' sound. «poison» with one «s» = 'z'.", "wrong": "«ss» = 's': «le poisson» = fish. «le poison» ('pwa-zon') = poison. «la boisson» = drink."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say the pair: «le poison … le poisson».", "fr": "le poison, le poisson"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How is the «s» pronounced in «la franchise»?", "choices": ["'s'", "'z'", "silent"], "answer": "'z'", "right": "Exactement ! «s» between two vowels = 'z': 'fran-sheez'.", "wrong": "The «s» is between «i» and «e» → 'z': «franchise» = 'fran-sheez'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 4: in a liaison, a final «s» linking to a vowel also sounds like 'z': «ils ont» = 'eel-zon', «nous aimons» = 'noo-zay-mon'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«ils ont» (they have) vs «ils sont» (they are). How is «ils ont» pronounced?", "choices": ["'eel-son'", "'eel-zon'", "'eel-on'"], "answer": "'eel-zon'", "right": "Parfait ! The liaison makes a 'z'. That's how you tell «ils ont» from «ils sont» ('eel-son').", "wrong": "Liaison «s» = 'z': «ils ont» = 'eel-zon'. «ils sont» = 'eel-son' (they are). Very important difference!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 5: «sc» before «e», «i», «y» = 's': «la science», «la piscine» (pool). Before «a», «o», «u» = 'sk': «l'escargot», «scolaire»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «sc» in «la piscine»?", "choices": ["'sk'", "'s'", "'sh'"], "answer": "'s'", "right": "Oui ! «sc» + «i» = 's': 'pee-seen'.", "wrong": "«sc» before «e» or «i» = 's': «piscine» = 'pee-seen'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «s» between vowels = 'z' (rose, musique). Start of word, next to a consonant, or «ss» = 's' (salut, espoir, poisson). Liaison «s» = 'z' (ils ont). «sc» + e/i = 's'."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the fish", "answer": "le poisson", "accept": ["le poisson", "poisson"], "hint": "Double «s» for the 's' sound.", "right": "Très bien ! «le poisson».", "wrong": "It's «le poisson» — with «ss»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Nous aimons la musique et le poisson.»", "fr": "Nous aimons la musique et le poisson."}]}, "4-28": {"stageId": "4-28", "title": "TH sound", "goal": "Pronounce French «th» as a simple 't' and avoid the English 'th' sound.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "This one is easy — but English speakers forget it all the time! French has no 'th' sound. «th» is simply pronounced 't'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "du thé", "en": "some tea", "note": "'tay' — exactly like the letter «t» + «é»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le théâtre", "en": "the theatre", "note": "'té-atr'. No tongue between the teeth!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «thé»?", "choices": ["Like 'the' in English", "'tay' (just «t» + «é»)", "'say'"], "answer": "'tay' (just «t» + «é»)", "right": "Exactement ! «th» = 't'.", "wrong": "French «th» = 't', never the English 'th': «thé» = 'tay'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Keep your tongue behind your teeth. If your tongue comes out between your teeth, you're doing the English 'th' — stop it there! The French «t» is also softer than English: no puff of air."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «le thé, le thème, la théorie».", "fr": "le thé, le thème, la théorie"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "du thon", "en": "some tuna", "note": "Sounds exactly like «ton» (your)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word sounds exactly like «du thym» (thyme)?", "choices": ["«teint» (complexion)", "«thème»", "«temps» (time)"], "answer": "«teint» (complexion)", "right": "Bravo ! «thym» and «teint» are both 't' + nasal «in».", "wrong": "«thym» = 't' + nasal «in», just like «teint». «temps» has a different nasal («an»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "les mathématiques", "en": "mathematics", "note": "'ma-té-ma-teek'. Natives say «les maths» ('mat') — and the «s» is silent!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do natives usually say «les mathématiques» in everyday French?", "choices": ["«les maths», pronounced 'mat'", "«les maths», pronounced 'maths' like English", "«la math»"], "answer": "«les maths», pronounced 'mat'", "right": "Parfait ! «J'aime les maths» = 'jem lay mat'.", "wrong": "Everyday French says «les maths», pronounced 'mat': «th» = 't', and the final «s» is silent."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un athlète", "en": "an athlete", "note": "'at-let'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word starts with a 't' sound?", "choices": ["la thérapie", "la chaise", "le jour"], "answer": "la thérapie", "right": "Oui ! «thérapie» = 'té-ra-pee'.", "wrong": "«th» = 't': «la thérapie» = 'té-ra-pee'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: French «th» = 't', tongue behind the teeth: «thé», «théâtre», «thème», «thèse», «thon», «thym», «athlète», «maths»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: some tea", "answer": "du thé", "accept": ["du thé", "du the", "thé"], "hint": "«du» + a word with «th» and «é».", "right": "Très bien ! «du thé».", "wrong": "It's «du thé» — t-h-é."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je bois du thé avant le théâtre.»", "fr": "Je bois du thé avant le théâtre."}]}, "4-29": {"stageId": "4-29", "title": "TION & SSION sounds", "goal": "Pronounce «-tion» and «-ssion» as 'syon' (not English 'shun'), and spot the «-stion» exception.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Hundreds of French words end in «-tion»: «nation», «préparation», «réputation». Good news: you already know their meaning from English! But the sound is different."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In English, '-tion' = 'shun'. In French, «-tion» = 'syon': an 's', a quick 'y', then the nasal «on». «nation» = 'na-syon'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la nation", "en": "the nation", "note": "'na-syon' (nasal at the end)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «la nation»?", "choices": ["'nay-shun'", "'na-syon'", "'na-tee-on'"], "answer": "'na-syon'", "right": "Exactement ! «-tion» = 'syon', with a nasal «on».", "wrong": "French «-tion» = 'syon': «nation» = 'na-syon'. Never 'shun'."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «la nation, la natation, la préparation».", "fr": "la nation, la natation, la préparation"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«-ssion» sounds exactly the same: 'syon'. «la pression» = 'pré-syon', «la progression» = 'pro-gré-syon'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Do «-tion» and «-ssion» sound different?", "choices": ["Yes, «-ssion» is 'shun'", "No, both are 'syon'", "Yes, «-tion» has a 't'"], "answer": "No, both are 'syon'", "right": "Bravo ! Same sound, different spelling.", "wrong": "Both endings sound 'syon': «nation», «pression»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Exception: after an «s», the «t» is a real 't': «-stion» = 'styon'. «la question» = 'kes-tyon', «la digestion». Never 'kes-syon'!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «la question»?", "choices": ["'kes-tyon'", "'kes-syon'", "'kwes-shun'"], "answer": "'kes-tyon'", "right": "Parfait ! After «s», «-tion» keeps its 't': 'kes-tyon'.", "wrong": "«-stion» = 'styon': «question» = 'kes-tyon'. And French «qu» = 'k', no 'w'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Gender trick: nouns ending in «-tion» and «-ssion» are almost always feminine: «la nation», «la pression», «une question»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which article goes with «réputation»?", "choices": ["le", "la", "les"], "answer": "la", "right": "Oui ! «la réputation» — «-tion» nouns are feminine.", "wrong": "Words in «-tion» are feminine: «la réputation»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ma prononciation progresse !", "en": "My pronunciation is improving!", "note": "Spelling trap: «prononciation» — no «u», unlike English 'pronunciation' has no 'o'… compare carefully!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which spelling is correct?", "choices": ["la prononciation", "la pronounciation", "la prononsiation"], "answer": "la prononciation", "right": "Très bien ! «pro-non-cia-tion», like the verb «prononcer».", "wrong": "It's «la prononciation» — think of the verb «prononcer» (to pronounce)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «-tion» and «-ssion» = 'syon' (nasal): «nation», «natation», «pression». After «s»: 'styon' («question»). These nouns are feminine."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the pronunciation", "answer": "la prononciation", "accept": ["la prononciation", "prononciation"], "hint": "Think of «prononcer».", "right": "Excellent ! «la prononciation».", "wrong": "It's «la prononciation» — p-r-o-n-o-n-c-i-a-t-i-o-n."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «La préparation de la natation demande de la concentration.»", "fr": "La préparation de la natation demande de la concentration."}]}, "4-30": {"stageId": "4-30", "title": "Vowel sounds", "goal": "Pronounce the basic French vowels a, e, i, o and u clearly and without English 'gliding'.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French vowels are pure and steady. In English, 'no' slides into 'noh-oo'. In French, the vowel stays the same from start to end. Keep your mouth still while you say it!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«a» = open mouth, like 'a' in 'father' but shorter: «un ami», «agréable». «i» = like 'ee' in 'see', but short and with a big smile: «le lit», «le livre»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un ami", "en": "a friend", "note": "Liaison: 'uh-na-mee'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le livre", "en": "the book", "note": "Short, smiling «i»: 'leevr'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«e» without accent in short words («le», «me», «te») is a soft, relaxed 'uh', with slightly rounded lips. «é» is different: 'ay' without the 'y' glide, lips spread."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which pair sounds different?", "choices": ["«le» / «les»", "«lit» / «lie»", "«mot» / «maux»"], "answer": "«le» / «les»", "right": "Bravo ! «le» = 'luh', «les» = 'lay'. This difference tells singular from plural!", "wrong": "«le» ('luh') and «les» ('lay') sound different — that's how you hear singular vs plural. The other pairs sound the same."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say the pair: «le livre … les livres».", "fr": "le livre, les livres"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«o» in «rose», «le dos», «le mot», «trop» = a closed 'oh', lips very round — and no 'oo' at the end! Not 'roh-ooz', just 'roz'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «le lit» (bed), is the final «t» pronounced?", "choices": ["Yes", "No"], "answer": "No", "right": "Exactement ! «lit» = 'lee'. Final consonants are often silent.", "wrong": "The «t» is silent: «le lit» = 'luh lee'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the special one: French «u». It doesn't exist in English. Say 'ee', keep your tongue there, then round your lips like for a kiss. «la jupe» (skirt), «tu»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Check yourself in a mirror: for «u», lips are round and pushed forward, but your tongue is at the front like for 'ee'. If you say 'oo', you've moved your tongue back."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the French «u» sound?", "choices": ["la jupe", "la soupe", "le loto"], "answer": "la jupe", "right": "Parfait ! «jupe» = 'ee' tongue + round lips.", "wrong": "«la jupe» has the French «u». «soupe» has «ou» ('oo'), «loto» has «o»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «tu, la jupe, la lune».", "fr": "tu, la jupe, la lune"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How should you say «trop» (too much)?", "choices": ["'troh-oo' with a glide", "'tro', pure and short", "'trop' with the «p»"], "answer": "'tro', pure and short", "right": "Oui ! Pure «o», silent «p».", "wrong": "French vowels don't glide, and the final «p» is silent: «trop» = 'tro'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «a» open, «i» smiling, «e» soft 'uh', «é» = 'ay', «o» round and pure, «u» = 'ee' tongue + round lips. No gliding!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the book", "answer": "le livre", "accept": ["le livre", "livre"], "hint": "«le» + l-i-v-r-e.", "right": "Très bien ! «le livre».", "wrong": "It's «le livre»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Mon amie a une jupe rose et un livre.»", "fr": "Mon amie a une jupe rose et un livre."}]}, "4-31": {"stageId": "4-31", "title": "What NOT to pronounce", "goal": "Recognise silent letters in French: most final consonants, final «e», «h», and the «-ent» verb ending.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French spelling has many silent letters. Knowing what NOT to pronounce will make you sound much more natural. Let's learn the main rules."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 1: most final consonants are silent. «grand» = 'gran', «le loup» = 'loo', «la forêt» = 'fo-ray', «vert» = 'vair', «long» = 'lon'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le grand loup est dans la forêt.", "en": "The big wolf is in the forest.", "note": "Silent: d, p, t."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «le loup» (the wolf)?", "choices": ["'luh loop'", "'luh loo'", "'luh lowp'"], "answer": "'luh loo'", "right": "Exactement ! The final «p» is silent.", "wrong": "The final «p» is silent: «le loup» = 'luh loo'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: the consonants in the word CaReFuL — C, R, F, L — are often pronounced at the end: «le sac», «la mer», «neuf», «le sel». Most others are silent."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In which word do you hear the final consonant?", "choices": ["le sac", "le dos", "le mot"], "answer": "le sac", "right": "Bravo ! «c» is one of the CaReFuL letters: «sac» = 'sak'.", "wrong": "«C, R, F, L» are usually pronounced at the end: «le sac» = 'sak'. «dos» and «mot» have silent endings."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 2: a final «e» is silent, but it makes the consonant before it heard. «vert» = 'vair', «verte» = 'vairt'. «grand» = 'gran', «grande» = 'grand'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 3: the verb ending «-ent» with «ils/elles» is completely silent. «ils chantent» sounds exactly like «il chante» = 'eel shant'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «Les oiseaux chantent»?", "choices": ["'chan-tent' with a nasal at the end", "'shant' — «-ent» is silent", "'chan-tay'"], "answer": "'shant' — «-ent» is silent", "right": "Parfait ! Verb ending «-ent» = silent.", "wrong": "For «ils/elles» verbs, «-ent» is silent: «chantent» = 'shant'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Careful! In other words, «-ent» is NOT a verb ending, and you say a nasal 'an' (only the «t» is silent): «lentement» = 'lant-man', «un compliment», «décent»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How does «lentement» (slowly) end?", "choices": ["Silent, like a verb", "With a nasal 'an' sound, «t» silent", "With a clear 'ent'"], "answer": "With a nasal 'an' sound, «t» silent", "right": "Oui ! It's an adverb, not a verb: 'lant-man'.", "wrong": "«lentement» is not a verb, so «-ent» = nasal 'an' (silent «t»): 'lant-man'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 4: the letter «h» is always silent: «habiter» = 'a-bee-tay', «haut» = 'oh'. Some «h» words block the liaison: «en haut» = 'an oh', no 'n' link."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les oiseaux chantent en haut des arbres.", "en": "The birds sing at the top of the trees.", "note": "Silent: «-ent», «h», «t» of «haut», «s» of «arbres»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: final consonants mostly silent (except CaReFuL), final «e» silent, verb «-ent» silent, «h» silent. Less is more!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the wolf", "answer": "le loup", "accept": ["le loup", "loup"], "hint": "Silent «p» at the end.", "right": "Très bien ! «le loup».", "wrong": "It's «le loup» — l-o-u-p."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le grand loup est à côté du long serpent vert.»", "fr": "Le grand loup est à côté du long serpent vert."}]}, "4-32": {"stageId": "4-32", "title": "Les liaisons obligatoires", "goal": "Make the mandatory liaisons in French (after determiners, before nouns, with pronouns, in fixed expressions) and avoid forbidden ones.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "A «liaison» is when a normally silent final consonant is pronounced because the next word starts with a vowel or silent «h». «les» = 'lay', but «les amis» = 'lay-za-mee'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Liaison sounds: «s», «x», «z» → 'z' («deux oranges» = 'deu-zo-ranj'). «n» → 'n' («un ami» = 'uh-na-mee'). «t», «d» → 't' («un grand homme» = 'uh gran-tom')."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "les olives", "en": "the olives", "note": "'lay-zo-leev'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «les amis»?", "choices": ["'lay a-mee'", "'lay-za-mee'", "'lace a-mee'"], "answer": "'lay-za-mee'", "right": "Exactement ! The «s» links with a 'z' sound.", "wrong": "After «les», liaison is obligatory, and «s» sounds 'z': «les amis» = 'lay-za-mee'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Obligatory liaison 1: determiner + noun or adjective: «un ami», «des enfants», «mes anciens élèves». Also numbers: «deux oranges», «trois anciens camarades». And adjective before a noun: «mes nouveaux amis», «un petit ami»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "mes nouveaux amis", "en": "my new friends", "note": "Two liaisons: 'may noo-vo-za-mee'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Obligatory liaison 2: pronouns with the verb: «nous avons», «ils ont», «elles y sont allées», «nous en avons parlé». And in inversion: «aiment-ils», «comment allez-vous ?»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous en avons déjà parlé.» How many liaisons?", "choices": ["One: «nous en»", "Two: «nous en» + «en avons»", "None"], "answer": "Two: «nous en» + «en avons»", "right": "Bravo ! 'noo-zan-na-von'. Pronouns link with the verb.", "wrong": "There are two: «nous en» ('noo-zan') and «en avons» ('an-na-von') → 'noo-zan-na-von'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Obligatory liaison 3: after short prepositions and adverbs: «chez elle», «dans un appartement», «très important», «bien assimilé». And fixed expressions: «c'est-à-dire», «de temps en temps», «tout à coup», «plus ou moins»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il habite dans un appartement.", "en": "He lives in an apartment.", "note": "'dan-zuh-na-par-tuh-man'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Forbidden liaisons: never after «et» («toi et elle» = 'twa ay el'), never after a singular noun («un étudiant intelligent»), and never before an aspirated «h»: «les haricots» = 'lay a-ree-ko' (a liaison here sounds like 'les zaricots' — a classic joke!)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one has NO liaison?", "choices": ["les enfants", "les haricots", "deux oranges"], "answer": "les haricots", "right": "Parfait ! «haricot» has an aspirated «h», so no liaison: 'lay a-ree-ko'.", "wrong": "«haricot» begins with an aspirated «h», which blocks liaison: 'lay a-ree-ko'. «les enfants» and «deux oranges» need the 'z'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je vais chez elle.» Do you make the liaison?", "choices": ["Yes: 'shay-zel'", "No: 'shay el'", "Only in writing"], "answer": "Yes: 'shay-zel'", "right": "Oui ! After «chez», liaison is obligatory.", "wrong": "Liaison is obligatory after short prepositions like «chez» and «dans»: «chez elle» = 'shay-zel'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle est grande et intelligente.» Liaison after «et»?", "choices": ["Yes, 't' sound", "No, never after «et»", "Only in formal speech"], "answer": "No, never after «et»", "right": "Exactement ! «et» never links — the «t» stays silent.", "wrong": "Liaison after «et» is forbidden: 'grand ay an-tay-lee-jant'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: link after determiners and numbers, adjective + noun, pronoun + verb, inversion, short prepositions/adverbs, fixed expressions. Never after «et», a singular noun, or before an aspirated «h»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: my new friends", "answer": "mes nouveaux amis", "accept": ["mes nouveaux amis", "mes nouvelles amies"], "hint": "«nouveau» in the plural ends in «-x».", "right": "Très bien ! And say it 'may noo-vo-za-mee'.", "wrong": "It's «mes nouveaux amis» (or «mes nouvelles amies» for female friends)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with all the liaisons: «Nous en avons parlé avec mes anciens élèves.»", "fr": "Nous en avons parlé avec mes anciens élèves."}]}, "5-1": {"stageId": "5-1", "title": "The little words before nouns: le, un, du", "goal": "By the end you can choose the right word for 'the', 'a' and 'some' in French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! I'm Camille. Here's a surprise: in French every noun is masculine or feminine. Even a chair, «une chaise», is feminine!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "English has only one word for 'the'. How many do you think French has?", "choices": ["1", "2", "4"], "answer": "4", "right": "Yes! «le», «la», «l'» and «les». The word changes with the noun's gender and number.", "wrong": "French actually has 4: «le» (masculine), «la» (feminine), «l'» (before a vowel or h) and «les» (plural)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le stylo, la chaise, l'ordinateur, les stylos", "en": "the pen, the chair, the computer, the pens"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«ordinateur» (computer) starts with a vowel. How do you say 'the computer'?", "choices": ["le ordinateur", "l'ordinateur", "la ordinateur"], "answer": "l'ordinateur", "right": "Perfect! Before a vowel or h, «le» and «la» lose their vowel and become «l'». It makes the word easier to say.", "wrong": "Before a vowel or an h, «le» and «la» shorten to «l'»: «l'ordinateur», «l'hôpital»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "A gender clue: nouns ending in -e are often feminine (about 75% of the time), and nouns ending in -tion are always feminine: «la formation». Still, learn every noun WITH its article!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now 'a/an': «un» goes with masculine nouns, «une» with feminine nouns, and «des» (some) goes with plurals."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«chaise» is feminine. How do you say 'a chair'?", "choices": ["un chaise", "une chaise", "des chaise"], "answer": "une chaise", "right": "Bravo! The noun is feminine, so it takes «une».", "wrong": "«un» is for masculine nouns. «chaise» is feminine, so it's «une chaise». «des» is only for plurals."}, {"t": "say", "text": "French also has a word for 'some', used for things you don't count, like food: «du» (masculine), «de la» (feminine), «de l'» (before a vowel) and «des» (plural). English often drops 'some', but French never does!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je voudrais du fromage et de l'eau.", "en": "I would like (some) cheese and (some) water."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«confiture» (jam) is feminine. How do you say 'I would like some jam'?", "choices": ["Je voudrais du confiture.", "Je voudrais de la confiture.", "Je voudrais confiture."], "answer": "Je voudrais de la confiture.", "right": "Excellent! A feminine noun takes «de la». You also remembered that French can't skip 'some'.", "wrong": "«du» is only for masculine nouns, and French can't leave 'some' out. «confiture» is feminine, so it's «de la confiture»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "After a quantity, «du», «de la» and «des» all shrink to just «de»: «un kilo de pommes» (a kilo of apples), «un litre de lait» (a litre of milk)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Last idea: when «à» (to/at) or «de» (of) meets «le» or «les», the two words join together. «à + le = au», «à + les = aux», «de + le = du», «de + les = des»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'at the theatre': à + le théâtre = ?", "choices": ["à le théâtre", "au théâtre", "aux théâtre"], "answer": "au théâtre", "right": "Yes! «à + le» always becomes «au». «à le» never appears in French.", "wrong": "«à + le» must join up as «au». «aux» is for plurals («aux sports d'hiver»). So the answer is «au théâtre»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je voudrais du pain et de la confiture.»", "fr": "Je voudrais du pain et de la confiture."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: 'the' = «le / la / l' / les». 'a' = «un / une», 'some' (plural) = «des». 'some' (food) = «du / de la / de l'». After a quantity, use just «de»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I would like some bread. («pain» is masculine)", "answer": "Je voudrais du pain.", "accept": ["je voudrais du pain", "je voudrais du pain."], "hint": "Starts with «Je voudrais…» + the masculine 'some'", "right": "Magnifique ! «pain» is masculine, so it's «du pain».", "wrong": "For a masculine noun, 'some' is «du»: «Je voudrais du pain.»"}]}, "5-2": {"stageId": "5-2", "title": "The four ways to say 'the'", "goal": "By the end you can choose le, la, l' or les for any noun.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's review the four ways to say 'the': «le», «la», «l'» and «les». The article always matches its noun."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le cinéma», «le sac», «le vélo»: what do these nouns have in common?", "choices": ["They are feminine", "They are masculine", "They are plural"], "answer": "They are masculine", "right": "Exactly! «le» means the noun is masculine and singular.", "wrong": "«le» only goes with masculine singular nouns. A feminine noun would take «la», and a plural noun would take «les»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la table, la chaise, la trousse", "en": "the table, the chair, the pencil case"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«arbre» (tree) starts with a vowel. How do you say 'the tree'?", "choices": ["le arbre", "la arbre", "l'arbre"], "answer": "l'arbre", "right": "Yes! Before a vowel or h, the article becomes «l'», whatever the gender: «l'arbre», «l'école», «l'hôpital».", "wrong": "Before a vowel or h, «le» and «la» both become «l'». So it's «l'arbre»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With plurals, gender doesn't matter: it's always «les». «les tables», «les arbres»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Before a vowel, the s of «les» is pronounced like a z: «les arbres» sounds like 'lay-zarbr'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'the chairs'?", "choices": ["la chaises", "les chaises", "le chaises"], "answer": "les chaises", "right": "Bravo! For any plural noun, use «les».", "wrong": "«la» and «le» are only for singular nouns. A plural noun always takes «les»: «les chaises»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A gender clue: words ending in -e or -ion are mostly feminine: «la nature», «la bouteille», «la nation»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch out for traps! «le livre», «le téléphone» and «l'arbre» end in -e but are masculine. Words ending in -age, -ège or -isme are usually masculine too: «le nettoyage», «le manège»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«nation» ends in -ion. How do you say 'the nation'?", "choices": ["le nation", "la nation", "les nation"], "answer": "la nation", "right": "Great detective work! Words ending in -ion are feminine, so it's «la nation».", "wrong": "Words ending in -ion are feminine, so we use «la». «les» would need a plural noun."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «le vélo, la tasse, l'école, les arbres».", "fr": "le vélo, la tasse, l'école, les arbres"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «le» = masculine, «la» = feminine, «l'» = before a vowel or h, «les» = plural. Learn every noun with its article!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the bottle («bouteille» is feminine)", "answer": "la bouteille", "accept": ["la bouteille"], "hint": "Feminine singular → «la…»", "right": "Parfait ! Feminine singular takes «la».", "wrong": "«bouteille» is feminine and singular, so it's «la bouteille»."}]}, "5-3": {"stageId": "5-3", "title": "Saying 'a' and 'some': un, une, des", "goal": "By the end you can say 'a cat', 'a house' and 'apples' in French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "'a/an' in French is «un» for masculine nouns and «une» for feminine nouns. For plurals, use «des» (some)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What do you think «un chat» means?", "choices": ["the cat", "a cat", "some cats"], "answer": "a cat", "right": "Yes! «un» means 'a' with a masculine noun.", "wrong": "'The cat' would be «le chat». «un» means 'a', so «un chat» = a cat."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "une maison", "en": "a house", "note": "«maison» is feminine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'a car'? («voiture» is feminine)", "choices": ["un voiture", "une voiture", "des voiture"], "answer": "une voiture", "right": "Bravo! Feminine noun → «une».", "wrong": "«un» is for masculine nouns, and «des» is for plurals. A feminine noun takes «une»: «une voiture»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For more than one, use «des»: «des pommes» = (some) apples. English often drops 'some', but French never drops «des»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai des pommes.", "en": "I have (some) apples."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I have cats'?", "choices": ["J'ai chats.", "J'ai des chats.", "J'ai un chats."], "answer": "J'ai des chats.", "right": "Excellent! A plural noun needs «des», even when English uses no word at all.", "wrong": "French needs an article here. For a plural noun, it's «des»: «J'ai des chats.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Endings in -e and -ion are often feminine: «une tasse», «une nation». But some are traps: «un livre», «un arbre», «un téléphone»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«livre» (book) ends in -e, but it's one of the traps. How do you say 'a book'?", "choices": ["une livre", "un livre"], "answer": "un livre", "right": "Well remembered! «livre» is masculine, even though it ends in -e.", "wrong": "«livre» is a trap: it ends in -e but it is masculine. So it's «un livre»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation: «un» is a nasal sound (say 'uh' through your nose). «une» ends with a clear n. Before a vowel, «un» links up: «un arbre» sounds like 'uh-narbr'."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «un chat, une maison, des pommes».", "fr": "un chat, une maison, des pommes"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «un» = a (masculine), «une» = a (feminine), «des» = some (any plural)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: a mug («tasse» is feminine)", "answer": "une tasse", "accept": ["une tasse"], "hint": "Feminine → «une…»", "right": "Super ! Feminine noun → «une tasse».", "wrong": "«tasse» is feminine, so it's «une tasse»."}]}, "5-4": {"stageId": "5-4", "title": "Partitive articles: some bread, some jam", "goal": "By the end you can say 'some' with food, and use «de» after a negative or a quantity.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Partitive articles mean 'some', for an amount you don't count. They're used mostly with food: «du», «de la», «de l'» and «des»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«beurre» (butter) is masculine. How do you say 'some butter'?", "choices": ["de la beurre", "du beurre", "des beurre"], "answer": "du beurre", "right": "Yes! Masculine → «du» («de + le» always becomes «du»).", "wrong": "«de la» is for feminine nouns, and «des» is for plurals. A masculine noun takes «du»: «du beurre»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai de la confiture sur ma tranche de pain.", "en": "I have (some) jam on my slice of bread."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«eau» (water) starts with a vowel. How do you say 'Do you want some water?'", "choices": ["Voulez-vous du eau ?", "Voulez-vous de l'eau ?", "Voulez-vous de la eau ?"], "answer": "Voulez-vous de l'eau ?", "right": "Perfect! Before a vowel or silent h, it's «de l'».", "wrong": "Before a vowel, «du» and «de la» become «de l'». That's why we say «de l'eau»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Big rule number 1: in a negative sentence («ne… pas»), all these words become just «de» (or «d'» before a vowel)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je mange du pain. → Je ne mange pas de pain.", "en": "I eat bread. → I don't eat bread."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I don't drink lemonade'?", "choices": ["Je ne bois pas de la limonade.", "Je ne bois pas de limonade.", "Je ne bois pas limonade."], "answer": "Je ne bois pas de limonade.", "right": "Bravo! After «ne… pas», «de la» becomes just «de».", "wrong": "After a negative, «du / de la / des» all become «de». And you can't drop it completely. So: «Je ne bois pas de limonade.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Big rule number 2: after a quantity, use just «de» too: «500 grammes de confiture», «un kilo de pommes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Fill the gap: «Il faut 500 grammes ___ fruits.»", "choices": ["des", "de", "du"], "answer": "de", "right": "Yes! «500 grammes» is a quantity, so we use only «de».", "wrong": "After a quantity like «500 grammes», we always use «de», never «des» or «du»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "A handy idiom: «Il a de la chance» = he is lucky. Word for word, it says 'he has some luck'!"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «Il prend toujours du sucre dans son café.» (He always takes sugar in his coffee.)", "fr": "Il prend toujours du sucre dans son café."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: 'some' = «du / de la / de l' / des». After «ne… pas» or after a quantity, use just «de» or «d'»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I don't eat bread.", "answer": "Je ne mange pas de pain.", "accept": ["je ne mange pas de pain", "je ne mange pas de pain."], "hint": "«Je ne mange pas…» + the short word", "right": "Excellent ! After a negative, «du pain» becomes «de pain».", "wrong": "In a negative sentence, «du» becomes «de»: «Je ne mange pas de pain.»"}]}, "5-5": {"stageId": "5-5", "title": "Why gender matters", "goal": "By the end you understand how a noun's gender changes the words around it.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Why does it matter if a word is masculine or feminine? Because lots of the words around it change to match!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le stylo vert» = the green pen. Now guess: «la fleur ___» = the green flower.", "choices": ["vert", "verte", "verts"], "answer": "verte", "right": "Well guessed! A feminine noun gets a feminine adjective: add an -e.", "wrong": "«fleur» is feminine, so the adjective adds an -e: «verte». «verts» would be masculine plural."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le stylo vert est petit. / La fleur verte est petite.", "en": "The green pen is small. / The green flower is small."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Even with «les», the adjective still shows the gender: «les stylos verts» but «les fleurs vertes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'The green flowers are small'?", "choices": ["Les fleurs verts sont petits.", "Les fleurs vertes sont petites.", "Les fleurs vertes sont petits."], "answer": "Les fleurs vertes sont petites.", "right": "Bravo! Feminine plural means adding -es to BOTH adjectives.", "wrong": "«fleurs» is feminine plural, so every adjective takes -es: «vertes», «petites»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Possessives change too: «mon stylo» (my pen) but «ma fleur» (my flower). So does 'this': «ce stylo» but «cette fleur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'this flower'?", "choices": ["ce fleur", "cette fleur", "ces fleur"], "answer": "cette fleur", "right": "Yes! «cette» goes with feminine nouns.", "wrong": "«ce» is for masculine nouns and «ces» is for plurals. For a feminine noun, it's «cette fleur»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "French has no word for 'it'! A pen is «il» (he) and a flower is «elle» (she): «Le stylo ? Il est vert.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«La fleur est verte.» → «___ est verte.»", "choices": ["Il", "Elle", "Ils"], "answer": "Elle", "right": "Exactly! «la fleur» is feminine, so it becomes «elle».", "wrong": "A feminine noun is replaced by «elle». «il» replaces masculine nouns, and «ils» is plural."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Question words change too: «Quel stylo ?» but «Quelle fleur ?» (which pen? which flower?)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Endings help. -age, -isme, -eau, -ment and -oir are usually masculine («le village», «le bateau»). -tion, -té, -ette and -esse are usually feminine («la natation», «la liberté»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «Mon stylo est vert. Ma fleur est verte.»", "fr": "Mon stylo est vert. Ma fleur est verte."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: gender changes the adjectives («vert/verte»), the possessives («mon/ma»), 'this' («ce/cette»), 'which' («quel/quelle») and 'it' («il/elle»)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: This flower is green.", "answer": "Cette fleur est verte.", "accept": ["cette fleur est verte", "cette fleur est verte."], "hint": "Feminine 'this' + feminine adjective", "right": "Magnifique ! «cette» and «verte» both match the feminine «fleur».", "wrong": "«fleur» is feminine, so we need «cette» and «verte»: «Cette fleur est verte.»"}]}, "5-6": {"stageId": "5-6", "title": "Spotting feminine words", "goal": "By the end you can recognise common feminine endings, and their traps.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's become gender detectives! Certain endings usually mean a word is feminine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«la vie», «la salade», «la tasse»: which ending do they share?", "choices": ["-e", "-o", "-r"], "answer": "-e", "right": "Yes! Most nouns ending in -e are feminine.", "wrong": "Look at the last letter: vie, salade, tasse all end in -e. That's the most common feminine ending."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Traps that end in -e but are masculine: «le livre», «le monde», «le téléphone», «l'homme»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la lumière, la mère, la terre", "en": "the light, the mother, the earth", "note": "-ère/-erre → feminine (trap: «le verre», the glass)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Words ending in -ette are feminine. How do you say 'the bike' («bicyclette»)?", "choices": ["le bicyclette", "la bicyclette", "les bicyclette"], "answer": "la bicyclette", "right": "Bravo! -ette → feminine → «la».", "wrong": "Words ending in -ette are feminine, so they take «la». «les» needs a plural."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More feminine endings: -esse («la tendresse»), -elle («la poubelle», the bin), -oie («la joie»), -té («la liberté», «la santé»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«liberté» ends in -té. How do you say 'the freedom'?", "choices": ["le liberté", "la liberté", "les liberté"], "answer": "la liberté", "right": "Yes! Words ending in -té are usually feminine.", "wrong": "The ending -té usually means feminine, so it takes «la»: «la liberté»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Careful with -é and -ée: «l'été» (summer), «le musée» and «le lycée» (high school) are masculine!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is the trap (a masculine word)?", "choices": ["santé", "société", "musée"], "answer": "musée", "right": "Sharp eyes! «le musée» is masculine even though it ends in -ée.", "wrong": "«la santé» and «la société» follow the rule (feminine). The trap is «le musée»: masculine!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la robe verte, les robes vertes", "en": "the green dress, the green dresses", "note": "Feminine noun → feminine adjective."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «la planète, la joie, la poubelle».", "fr": "la planète, la joie, la poubelle"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: -e, -ère, -esse, -ette, -elle, -oie and -té usually mean feminine. Watch for traps like «le livre», «le verre», «le musée» and «l'été»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the light («lumière»)", "answer": "la lumière", "accept": ["la lumière", "la lumiere"], "hint": "-ère → feminine", "right": "Parfait ! -ère → feminine → «la lumière».", "wrong": "«lumière» ends in -ère, so it's feminine: «la lumière»."}]}, "5-7": {"stageId": "5-7", "title": "Spotting masculine words", "goal": "By the end you know which categories and endings are usually masculine.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Now for the masculine words! Some whole groups are masculine: days, seasons, languages and trees."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le lundi, le mardi", "en": "Monday, Tuesday (and every Monday or Tuesday as a habit)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Days of the week are masculine. How do you say 'Monday' with an article?", "choices": ["la lundi", "le lundi", "les lundi"], "answer": "le lundi", "right": "Yes! All the days are masculine.", "wrong": "Days are masculine and singular here, so they take «le»: «le lundi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Seasons are masculine too: «l'hiver», «le printemps», «l'été», «l'automne». That's true even though the word «saison» itself is feminine!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'a winter'?", "choices": ["une hiver", "un hiver"], "answer": "un hiver", "right": "Bravo! Seasons are masculine, so it's «un».", "wrong": "All the seasons are masculine, so we say «un hiver»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'apprends le français et le japonais.", "en": "I am learning French and Japanese.", "note": "Languages are masculine and have no capital letter."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Trees are masculine, even when the fruit is feminine: «la pomme» (apple) → «le pommier» (apple tree)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«la cerise» = the cherry. How do you say 'the cherry tree'?", "choices": ["la cerisier", "le cerisier"], "answer": "le cerisier", "right": "Exactly! Trees ending in -ier are masculine.", "wrong": "The fruit may be feminine, but the tree is always masculine: «le cerisier»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Masculine endings: -ier («le papier»), -o/-ot («le vélo»), -ment («le médicament»), -an («l'océan»), -isme («le féminisme»), -ou («le genou»). Traps: «la moto», «la météo», «la jument»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word is the trap (it's feminine)?", "choices": ["vélo", "moto", "chariot"], "answer": "moto", "right": "Well spotted! «la moto» is feminine even though it ends in -o.", "wrong": "«le vélo» and «le chariot» follow the rule. «la moto» is the feminine trap."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «le printemps, le pommier, le gouvernement».", "fr": "le printemps, le pommier, le gouvernement"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: days, seasons, languages and trees are masculine. So are words ending in -ier, -o, -ment, -an, -isme and -ou, with a few traps."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: the apple tree", "answer": "le pommier", "accept": ["le pommier"], "hint": "«pomme» + -ier", "right": "Super ! Trees are masculine: «le pommier».", "wrong": "A tree is masculine: «le pommier»."}]}, "5-8": {"stageId": "5-8", "title": "Gender quiz: un or une?", "goal": "By the end you can use your clues to guess the gender of new words.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Quiz time! Use your detective clues to choose «un» or «une». Ready?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "___ solution", "choices": ["un", "une"], "answer": "une", "right": "Yes! Words ending in -tion are feminine.", "wrong": "Words ending in -tion are always feminine: «une solution»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "___ féminisme", "choices": ["un", "une"], "answer": "un", "right": "Correct! Words ending in -isme are masculine, even this one.", "wrong": "The ending -isme is masculine: «un féminisme»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Categories help: days, seasons and languages are masculine. «un dimanche», «un automne», «l'italien» (the Italian language)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "___ dimanche", "choices": ["un", "une"], "answer": "un", "right": "Bravo! Days of the week are masculine.", "wrong": "Even though it ends in -e, «dimanche» is a day, and days are masculine: «un dimanche»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "___ voiture", "choices": ["un", "une"], "answer": "une", "right": "Yes! It ends in -e and is feminine.", "wrong": "«voiture» follows the -e rule: it's feminine, «une voiture»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Words ending in a consonant are usually masculine: «un nez» (nose), «un citron» (lemon), «un projet» (project)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "___ projet", "choices": ["un", "une"], "answer": "un", "right": "Correct! It ends in a consonant, so it's masculine.", "wrong": "Most words ending in a consonant are masculine: «un projet»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "une bicyclette, un vélo", "en": "a bike, a bike", "note": "Same object, different gender! The gender belongs to the word, not the thing."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "___ journée (day)", "choices": ["un", "une"], "answer": "une", "right": "Yes! -ée is usually feminine (but remember the trap «le musée»).", "wrong": "The ending -ée is usually feminine: «une journée»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «un écran, une enveloppe, un cou».", "fr": "un écran, une enveloppe, un cou"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: -tion/-sion/-ée/-ette → «une». -isme/consonant/days/seasons/languages → «un». When in doubt, learn the word with its article!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with the right article: a mission", "answer": "une mission", "accept": ["une mission"], "hint": "-ssion → feminine", "right": "Excellent ! -ssion → feminine → «une mission».", "wrong": "Words ending in -ssion are feminine: «une mission»."}]}, "5-9": {"stageId": "5-9", "title": "Fruit", "goal": "By the end you can name 10 fruits with the right article.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Miam ! Let's learn the names of fruits. Always learn the article together with the noun."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "une pomme, une poire, une banane", "en": "an apple, a pear, a banana"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What do you think «une fraise» is?", "choices": ["a strawberry", "a lemon", "a cherry"], "answer": "a strawberry", "right": "Yes! «une fraise» = a strawberry.", "wrong": "«une fraise» is a strawberry. A lemon is «un citron» and a cherry is «une cerise»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un citron, un abricot, un ananas", "en": "a lemon, an apricot, a pineapple"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Careful: «un ananas» is a pineapple, not a banana! A banana is «une banane»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'a pineapple'?", "choices": ["une banane", "un ananas", "un abricot"], "answer": "un ananas", "right": "Bravo! «un ananas» = a pineapple. It's masculine.", "wrong": "«une banane» is a banana and «un abricot» is an apricot. A pineapple is «un ananas»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which fruit is masculine?", "choices": ["cerise", "citron", "tomate"], "answer": "citron", "right": "Yes! «un citron»: it ends in a consonant sound, so it's masculine.", "wrong": "«cerise» and «tomate» end in -e and are feminine. «un citron» is the masculine one."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Notice that most fruits ending in -e are feminine: «une cerise», «une tomate», «une orange»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation: «un abricot» links up and sounds like 'uh-nabriko'. The final t is silent."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'a cherry'?", "choices": ["un cerise", "une cerise"], "answer": "une cerise", "right": "Perfect! It ends in -e, so it's feminine: «une cerise».", "wrong": "«cerise» ends in -e and is feminine: «une cerise»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «une pomme, une fraise, un citron».", "fr": "une pomme, une fraise, un citron"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: most fruits ending in -e are feminine (pomme, poire, fraise, cerise, banane, orange, tomate). «citron», «abricot» and «ananas» are masculine."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: a pear", "answer": "une poire", "accept": ["une poire"], "hint": "Feminine, starts with «une p…»", "right": "Super ! «une poire».", "wrong": "A pear is «une poire». It's feminine."}]}, "5-10": {"stageId": "5-10", "title": "Meat and fish", "goal": "By the end you can order meat and fish using du, de la, de l' and des.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "At the butcher's! We usually buy 'some' meat, so we use the partitive articles: «du», «de la», «de l'» and «des»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "du poulet, du bœuf, du porc", "en": "some chicken, some beef, some pork"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«poisson» (fish) is masculine. How do you say 'some fish'?", "choices": ["de la poisson", "du poisson", "des poisson"], "answer": "du poisson", "right": "Yes! Masculine → «du».", "wrong": "«de la» is for feminine nouns and «des» is for plurals. A masculine noun takes «du»: «du poisson»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "de l'agneau", "en": "some lamb", "note": "It starts with a vowel, so we use «de l'»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«agneau» starts with a vowel. Which form is right?", "choices": ["du agneau", "de l'agneau"], "answer": "de l'agneau", "right": "Bravo! Before a vowel, we use «de l'».", "wrong": "Before a vowel, «du» becomes «de l'»: «de l'agneau»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Things you can count use «un»: «un hamburger», «un hotdog». For plurals, use «des»: «des saucisses»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'some sausages'?", "choices": ["du saucisses", "des saucisses", "de la saucisses"], "answer": "des saucisses", "right": "Yes! A plural noun takes «des».", "wrong": "«saucisses» is plural, so we use «des saucisses»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«du saucisson» is a dried cured sausage, a French apéritif favourite. «des saucisses» are the sausages you cook."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Remember: after a negative, everything becomes «de»: «Alice est végétarienne, elle ne mange pas de viande.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I don't eat ham'?", "choices": ["Je ne mange pas du jambon.", "Je ne mange pas de jambon."], "answer": "Je ne mange pas de jambon.", "right": "Excellent! After «ne… pas», «du» becomes «de».", "wrong": "After a negative, «du» becomes «de»: «Je ne mange pas de jambon.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «Je voudrais un hotdog avec de la moutarde, s'il vous plaît.»", "fr": "Je voudrais un hotdog avec de la moutarde, s'il vous plaît."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «du poulet», «du bœuf», «du jambon», «de l'agneau», «des saucisses», «un hamburger». In a negative, use «pas de viande»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: some chicken", "answer": "du poulet", "accept": ["du poulet"], "hint": "Masculine 'some'", "right": "Parfait ! «du poulet».", "wrong": "«poulet» is masculine, so 'some chicken' = «du poulet»."}]}, "5-11": {"stageId": "5-11", "title": "Drinks (non-alcoholic)", "goal": "By the end you can order a drink politely in French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Thirsty? We don't count drinks, so we use «du», «de la» or «de l'»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "du café, du thé, du lait", "en": "some coffee, some tea, some milk"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «du lait»?", "choices": ["some milk", "some tea", "some juice"], "answer": "some milk", "right": "Yes! «le lait» = milk.", "wrong": "«du lait» is some milk. Tea is «du thé» and juice is «du jus»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "de la limonade, de la tisane", "en": "some lemonade, some herbal tea"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«eau» (water) starts with a vowel. How do you say 'some water'?", "choices": ["du eau", "de l'eau", "de la eau"], "answer": "de l'eau", "right": "Bravo! Before a vowel, use «de l'».", "wrong": "Before a vowel, we use «de l'»: «de l'eau»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In a restaurant: «de l'eau plate» = still water, «de l'eau gazeuse» = fizzy water, «de l'eau du robinet» = tap water. Ask for «une carafe d'eau» and the tap water is free!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Juice is «le jus», which is masculine: «du jus d'orange», «du jus de pomme»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'some apple juice'?", "choices": ["de la jus de pomme", "du jus de pomme"], "answer": "du jus de pomme", "right": "Yes! «jus» is masculine, so it takes «du».", "wrong": "The main word is «jus», which is masculine. So it's «du jus de pomme»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To order politely, say «Je voudrais…» (I would like…) and finish with «s'il vous plaît»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «Je voudrais du thé, s'il vous plaît.»", "fr": "Je voudrais du thé, s'il vous plaît."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I drink orange juice'?", "choices": ["Je bois du jus d'orange.", "Je bois jus d'orange."], "answer": "Je bois du jus d'orange.", "right": "Excellent! French needs «du» here, even though English has no word.", "wrong": "French can't drop 'some': «Je bois du jus d'orange.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «du café / thé / lait / jus», «de la limonade / tisane», «de l'eau». To order: «Je voudrais… s'il vous plaît»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I would like some coffee.", "answer": "Je voudrais du café.", "accept": ["je voudrais du café", "je voudrais du cafe", "je voudrais du café."], "hint": "«Je voudrais…» + masculine 'some'", "right": "Magnifique ! «Je voudrais du café.»", "wrong": "«café» is masculine: «Je voudrais du café.»"}]}, "5-12": {"stageId": "5-12", "title": "Savoury food", "goal": "By the end you can name savoury foods with du, de la and des.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's learn some savoury foods, «la nourriture salée». Remember to use 'some'!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "du pain, du riz, du sel, du poivre", "en": "some bread, some rice, some salt, some pepper"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«riz» (rice) is masculine. How do you say 'some rice'?", "choices": ["de la riz", "du riz", "des riz"], "answer": "du riz", "right": "Yes! Masculine → «du riz».", "wrong": "A masculine noun takes «du»: «du riz»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "des frites, des chips, des pâtes, des légumes", "en": "some fries (UK chips), some crisps (US chips), some pasta, some vegetables"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False friends! «des frites» are fries (British 'chips'), and «des chips» are crisps (American 'chips')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You want a bag of crisps. What do you ask for?", "choices": ["des frites", "des chips"], "answer": "des chips", "right": "Yes! In French, «des chips» are crisps.", "wrong": "«des frites» are hot fries. Crisps are «des chips»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some are feminine: «de la viande» (some meat), «de la pizza» (some pizza)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I am eating some meat'?", "choices": ["Je mange du viande.", "Je mange de la viande."], "answer": "Je mange de la viande.", "right": "Bravo! «viande» is feminine, so it takes «de la».", "wrong": "«viande» is feminine: «Je mange de la viande.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Pourrais-tu me passer le sel, s'il te plaît ?» Here it's «le sel» (THE salt, that salt shaker on the table), not «du sel»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I am making pasta'?", "choices": ["Je fais des pâtes.", "Je fais de la pâtes."], "answer": "Je fais des pâtes.", "right": "Yes! «pâtes» is plural in French, so it takes «des».", "wrong": "«pâtes» is plural, so we use «des»: «Je fais des pâtes.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «Je voudrais du riz et des légumes.»", "fr": "Je voudrais du riz et des légumes."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «du pain / riz / sel / poivre / poisson», «de la viande / pizza», «des frites / chips / pâtes / légumes»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: some bread", "answer": "du pain", "accept": ["du pain"], "hint": "Masculine 'some'", "right": "Super ! «du pain».", "wrong": "«pain» is masculine: «du pain»."}]}, "5-13": {"stageId": "5-13", "title": "Sweet food", "goal": "By the end you can talk about sweets and desserts, including flavours.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Time for dessert! Let's learn some sweet food, «la nourriture sucrée»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "du chocolat, du sucre, de la confiture", "en": "some chocolate, some sugar, some jam"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «de la confiture»?", "choices": ["some jam", "some sugar", "some chocolate"], "answer": "some jam", "right": "Yes! «la confiture» = jam. It's feminine.", "wrong": "«de la confiture» is some jam. Sugar is «du sucre»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un gâteau, une glace, une tarte, un bonbon", "en": "a cake, an ice cream, a tart, a sweet"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'an ice cream'?", "choices": ["un glace", "une glace"], "answer": "une glace", "right": "Bravo! «glace» is feminine.", "wrong": "«glace» ends in -e and is feminine: «une glace»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Flavours use «à la», «au» or «aux»: «une glace à la vanille», «une glace à la fraise», «la tarte aux pommes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'a strawberry ice cream'?", "choices": ["une glace de fraise", "une glace à la fraise"], "answer": "une glace à la fraise", "right": "Yes! Flavours use «à» + article.", "wrong": "For flavours, French uses «à la / au / aux»: «une glace à la fraise»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "With love/like/hate verbs («aimer», «adorer», «détester»), use «le / la / les», not «du»: «J'adore la tarte aux pommes.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I love chocolate'?", "choices": ["J'adore du chocolat.", "J'adore le chocolat."], "answer": "J'adore le chocolat.", "right": "Excellent! After «adorer», we use «le».", "wrong": "After verbs like «adorer» or «aimer», use «le / la / les»: «J'adore le chocolat.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous mangeons des céréales, du pain et de la confiture au petit-déjeuner.", "en": "We eat cereal, bread and jam at breakfast."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «J'adore la tarte aux pommes, c'est mon dessert préféré.»", "fr": "J'adore la tarte aux pommes, c'est mon dessert préféré."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «du chocolat / sucre», «de la confiture», «un gâteau», «une glace à la vanille». And «J'adore le / la…» for things you love."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: a cake", "answer": "un gâteau", "accept": ["un gâteau", "un gateau"], "hint": "Masculine, ends in -eau", "right": "Parfait ! «un gâteau» (-eau words are usually masculine).", "wrong": "A cake is «un gâteau». It's masculine."}]}, "5-14": {"stageId": "5-14", "title": "Vegetables", "goal": "By the end you can name 10 vegetables in French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's fill our basket with vegetables, «les légumes»!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "une carotte, une courgette, une salade", "en": "a carrot, a courgette (zucchini), a lettuce"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What do you think «un champignon» is?", "choices": ["a mushroom", "an onion", "a leek"], "answer": "a mushroom", "right": "Yes! «un champignon» = a mushroom (think 'champignon' on pizza menus).", "wrong": "«un champignon» is a mushroom. An onion is «un oignon» and a leek is «un poireau»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "un oignon, un poireau, un poivron", "en": "an onion, a leek, a (bell) pepper", "note": "«oignon» sounds like 'onion': the i is silent."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Careful: «un poivron» is a bell pepper (the vegetable) and «du poivre» is pepper (the spice)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You want a red bell pepper for your salad. What do you say?", "choices": ["du poivre rouge", "un poivron rouge"], "answer": "un poivron rouge", "right": "Bravo! «un poivron» is the vegetable.", "wrong": "«du poivre» is the spice. The vegetable is «un poivron»: «un poivron rouge»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Potato is «une pomme de terre». The casual word is «une patate»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Word for word, what does «pomme de terre» mean?", "choices": ["earth apple", "potato bread", "green apple"], "answer": "earth apple", "right": "Yes! «pomme» = apple, «terre» = earth. An apple from the ground!", "wrong": "«pomme» = apple and «terre» = earth, so word for word it's an 'apple of the earth'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Garlic and broccoli are usually 'some': «de l'ail», «du brocoli»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«ail» (garlic) starts with a vowel. How do you say 'some garlic'?", "choices": ["du ail", "de l'ail"], "answer": "de l'ail", "right": "Excellent! Before a vowel, use «de l'».", "wrong": "Before a vowel, «du» becomes «de l'»: «de l'ail»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say out loud: «une carotte, un oignon, de l'ail».", "fr": "une carotte, un oignon, de l'ail"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «une carotte / courgette / salade / pomme de terre», «un champignon / oignon / poireau / poivron», «de l'ail», «du brocoli»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: a potato", "answer": "une pomme de terre", "accept": ["une pomme de terre", "une patate"], "hint": "An 'apple of the earth'…", "right": "Super ! «une pomme de terre» (or casually «une patate»).", "wrong": "A potato is «une pomme de terre» (or «une patate»)."}]}, "6-1": {"stageId": "6-1", "title": "I, you, he, she + to be & to have", "goal": "By the end you can use French subject pronouns, the verbs être and avoir, and make an adjective agree.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! I'm Camille. In French, 'I' is «je», 'you' (to a friend) is «tu», 'he' is «il» and 'she' is «elle»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "French has no special word for 'it'. «la table» is feminine. Which word do you think replaces it?", "choices": ["il", "elle", "on"], "answer": "elle", "right": "Yes! A feminine noun becomes «elle», even for objects. So «il» and «elle» can both mean 'it'.", "wrong": "A masculine noun becomes «il», a feminine noun becomes «elle». «la table» is feminine, so it's «elle»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the plural: «nous» = we, «vous» = you (a group, or one person politely), «ils» = they (masculine or mixed), «elles» = they (all feminine). And «on» = 'we / people' in everyday chat."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Charlotte et Tom» — a girl and a boy. Which pronoun replaces them?", "choices": ["elles", "ils", "nous"], "answer": "ils", "right": "Exactly! A mixed group takes «ils». Even one boy in a group of girls makes it «ils».", "wrong": "«elles» is only for groups that are all feminine. As soon as there's one masculine person or noun, use «ils»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«tu» or «vous»? Use «tu» with friends, family and children. Use «vous» with strangers, older people, shop staff — and with any group. When in doubt, choose «vous»!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the super-verb «avoir» (to have): «j'ai, tu as, il/elle/on a, nous avons, vous avez, ils/elles ont»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai un chat.", "en": "I have a cat.", "note": "«je» + «ai» → «j'ai»: «je» drops its e before a vowel."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'they have' (a group of boys)?", "choices": ["ils a", "ils ont", "ils sont"], "answer": "ils ont", "right": "Great! «ont» is avoir with «ils/elles». Careful: «sont» is a different verb — 'they are'.", "wrong": "With «ils», avoir becomes «ont»: «ils ont». «ils sont» means 'they are' — that's the verb être!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "The second super-verb is «être» (to be): «je suis, tu es, il/elle/on est, nous sommes, vous êtes, ils/elles sont»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Nous ___ français.» (We are French.)", "choices": ["sommes", "êtes", "avons"], "answer": "sommes", "right": "Bravo ! «nous sommes» = we are. «nous avons» would mean 'we have'.", "wrong": "With «nous», être is «sommes». «êtes» goes with «vous», and «avons» means 'have'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "A handy expression: «il y a» = there is / there are. It never changes: «Il y a un chat», «Il y a trois chats»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Last idea: adjectives agree with their noun and usually come AFTER it. Add -e for feminine and -s for plural: «petit, petite, petits, petites»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Camille is a woman. How does she say 'I am small'?", "choices": ["Je suis petit.", "Je suis petite.", "Je suis petits."], "answer": "Je suis petite.", "right": "Yes! Camille is feminine, so the adjective takes an -e: «petite». Now you can even hear the t!", "wrong": "The adjective agrees with the speaker. A woman adds -e: «Je suis petite». The -s is only for plural."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Bonjour, je m'appelle Camille. J'ai un chat, il est blanc et noir.»", "fr": "Bonjour, je m'appelle Camille. J'ai un chat, il est blanc et noir."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: pronouns «je, tu, il, elle, on, nous, vous, ils, elles». «avoir» = to have («j'ai»), «être» = to be («je suis»). Adjectives agree and usually follow the noun."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: There are three white cats.", "answer": "Il y a trois chats blancs.", "accept": ["il y a trois chats blancs", "il y a trois chats blancs.", "il y a 3 chats blancs", "il y a 3 chats blancs."], "hint": "Starts with «Il y a…». The colour goes after the noun and agrees.", "right": "Magnifique ! «il y a» never changes, and «blancs» takes -s to agree with «chats».", "wrong": "Use «Il y a» + number + noun + adjective. The colour goes after the noun and takes -s: «Il y a trois chats blancs.»"}]}, "6-2": {"stageId": "6-2", "title": "Swapping names for pronouns", "goal": "By the end you can replace any subject with the right subject pronoun.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we practise one skill: swapping a name or a noun for a pronoun, just like 'Luc' → 'he' in English. It makes your French sound natural."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Luc range les jouets.» (Luc tidies the toys.) Which pronoun replaces «Luc»?", "choices": ["il", "elle", "ils"], "answer": "il", "right": "Yes! Luc is one boy → «il»: «Il range les jouets.»", "wrong": "One male person = «il». «elle» is for one female, «ils» for a group."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now a trickier one: what if YOU are in the group? «Tom et moi» = Tom and me. Who is that, in one word?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom et moi mangeons ensemble.» → ?", "choices": ["Ils mangeons ensemble.", "Nous mangeons ensemble.", "Vous mangeons ensemble."], "answer": "Nous mangeons ensemble.", "right": "Exactly! Any group that includes «moi» (me) becomes «nous». And look: the verb already ends in -ons, the «nous» ending.", "wrong": "If «moi» (me) is in the group, it's 'we' → «nous». Notice the verb «mangeons»: -ons is the «nous» ending."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Verb endings are clues! -ons almost always goes with «nous», and -ez with «vous». Check the verb to confirm your pronoun."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Same idea with «toi» (you): a group that includes the person you're talking to, but not you, becomes «vous»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Paul et toi allez voir un film.» → ?", "choices": ["Vous allez voir un film.", "Ils allez voir un film.", "Nous allez voir un film."], "answer": "Vous allez voir un film.", "right": "Bravo ! Paul + you = 'you all' → «vous». The -ez ending of «allez» confirms it.", "wrong": "«toi» means 'you', so Paul + you = «vous». The verb «allez» ends in -ez, the «vous» ending."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Alice et Charlotte nagent tous les jeudis. → Elles nagent tous les jeudis.", "en": "Alice and Charlotte swim every Thursday. → They swim every Thursday.", "note": "Two girls → «elles»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Charlotte et Rémi habitent très loin.» → ?", "choices": ["Elles habitent très loin.", "Ils habitent très loin.", "Nous habitent très loin."], "answer": "Ils habitent très loin.", "right": "Yes! One boy in the group makes it «ils».", "wrong": "Charlotte is a girl but Rémi is a boy. A mixed group always takes «ils»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ces gens sont incroyables !» (These people are incredible!) → ?", "choices": ["Ils sont incroyables !", "Elles sont incroyables !", "Il est incroyable !"], "answer": "Ils sont incroyables !", "right": "Right! «les gens» (people) is plural and masculine → «ils».", "wrong": "«gens» means 'people', which is plural, so not «il». Grammatically it's masculine → «Ils sont incroyables !»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Nous mangeons ensemble.» Remember: the s of «nous» is silent here!", "fr": "Nous mangeons ensemble."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: one boy → «il», one girl → «elle», group with me → «nous», group with you → «vous», mixed or masculine group → «ils», all-female group → «elles»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Replace the subject with a pronoun: «Charlotte arrive à 20 h.» Write the new sentence.", "answer": "Elle arrive à 20 h.", "accept": ["elle arrive à 20 h", "elle arrive à 20 h.", "elle arrive a 20 h", "elle arrive à 20h", "elle arrive a 20h", "elle arrive à 20h."], "hint": "Charlotte is one girl…", "right": "Parfait ! One girl → «elle». The verb stays exactly the same.", "wrong": "Charlotte is one female person → «elle»: «Elle arrive à 20 h.»"}]}, "6-3": {"stageId": "6-3", "title": "«On» vs «nous»: two ways to say 'we'", "goal": "By the end you can use «on» for 'we / people / someone' and know when to choose «nous».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Meet the friendliest pronoun in French: «on». It can mean 'we', 'people', 'someone' or 'one'. French people use it all the time!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "En France, on aime le pain.", "en": "In France, people like bread.", "note": "Here «on» = people in general."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Look at the verb in «on aime». Which pronoun uses the same verb form?", "choices": ["nous (nous aimons)", "il/elle (il aime)", "vous (vous aimez)"], "answer": "il/elle (il aime)", "right": "Well spotted! «on» always takes the il/elle form of the verb, even when it means 'we'.", "wrong": "«on» is grammatically singular, so its verb looks like the il/elle form: «on aime», «on est», «on a»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "So 'we are' can be «nous sommes» or «on est» — same meaning! «on» is casual and spoken; «nous» is more formal and used in writing."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You're texting a friend: 'Shall we go?' Which is most natural?", "choices": ["On y va ?", "Nous y allons ?", "Il y va ?"], "answer": "On y va ?", "right": "Exactly! In casual chat, French people almost always say «on» for 'we'. «On y va ?» is super common.", "wrong": "«Nous y allons ?» is correct but sounds very formal in a text. Among friends, use «on»: «On y va ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«On y va !» (Let's go!) is one of the most useful phrases in French. You'll hear it everywhere — in shops, in families, at the end of meetings."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«on» can also mean 'someone', when you don't know who did something."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On a sonné.", "en": "Someone rang the bell."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «On dit que c'est un bon restaurant» mean?", "choices": ["People say it's a good restaurant.", "He says it's a good restaurant.", "I say it's a good restaurant."], "answer": "People say it's a good restaurant.", "right": "Yes! Here «on» means people in general — like English 'they say…'.", "wrong": "«on» isn't 'he' or 'I'. In «On dit que…», it means people in general: 'People say / They say…'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Careful: «on» can ONLY be a subject. 'She gives us an apple' is «Elle nous donne une pomme», never «Elle on donne une pomme»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a formal letter: 'We are available on Monday.' Which fits best?", "choices": ["On est disponibles lundi.", "Nous sommes disponibles lundi.", "Nous est disponibles lundi."], "answer": "Nous sommes disponibles lundi.", "right": "Perfect! In formal writing, «nous» is the polite choice, with its own verb form «sommes».", "wrong": "For formal writing, choose «nous» — and «nous» takes «sommes», not «est». «On est…» is fine with friends but too casual here."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, like a French friend: «On y va ?»", "fr": "On y va ?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «on» = we / people / someone / one. It takes the il/elle verb form («on est», «on a»). Use «on» in casual speech, «nous» in formal speech and writing."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French, the casual way: We are at Tom's. («chez» = at someone's place)", "answer": "On est chez Tom.", "accept": ["on est chez tom", "on est chez tom."], "hint": "Starts with «On…» + the il/elle form of être", "right": "Bravo ! «on» + «est» = the casual 'we are'.", "wrong": "Casual 'we' is «on», and it takes the il/elle form of être: «On est chez Tom.»"}]}, "6-4": {"stageId": "6-4", "title": "What time is it? (hours & minutes)", "goal": "By the end you can ask the time and say any time on the hour, with minutes, in the 24-hour clock.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's tell the time! The question is «Quelle heure est-il ?» — What time is it? The answer always starts with «Il est…»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you think you say 'It is two o'clock'?", "choices": ["C'est deux heures.", "Il est deux heures.", "Il a deux heures."], "answer": "Il est deux heures.", "right": "Yes! Time always uses «il est» + number + «heures».", "wrong": "French doesn't use «c'est» or «il a» for the time. It's always «Il est…»: «Il est deux heures.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est une heure.", "en": "It is one o'clock.", "note": "One hour = «une heure» (no s). From two onwards: «heures»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "'O'clock' doesn't exist in French! The word «heures» does the job: «Il est huit heures» = It is eight (o'clock)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "12:00 has special words: «midi» (midday) and «minuit» (midnight). No «heures» after them!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "It's 12 noon, time for lunch. What do you say?", "choices": ["Il est midi heures.", "Il est midi.", "Il est minuit."], "answer": "Il est midi.", "right": "Bravo ! «midi» = midday («mi» = middle + «di» = day). «minuit» would be midnight — very late for lunch!", "wrong": "12 noon is «Il est midi», with no «heures» after it. «minuit» is midnight."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In France, people often use the 24-hour clock, especially for trains, shops and TV. After midi, keep counting: 13:00 = «treize heures», 15:00 = «quinze heures», 20:00 = «vingt heures»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your train leaves at 4 pm. The announcer says…", "choices": ["seize heures", "quatorze heures", "quatre heures"], "answer": "seize heures", "right": "Yes! 4 pm = 12 + 4 = 16 → «seize heures».", "wrong": "In the 24-hour clock, add 12 to afternoon times: 4 pm → 16 → «seize heures». «quatorze» is 14, i.e. 2 pm."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For minutes, just add the number after «heures». No need for the word 'minutes'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est dix-huit heures vingt-cinq.", "en": "It is 6:25 pm."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What time is «Il est quatorze heures cinquante» ?", "choices": ["2:50 pm", "4:50 pm", "2:15 pm"], "answer": "2:50 pm", "right": "Perfect! «quatorze» = 14 = 2 pm, and «cinquante» = 50.", "wrong": "«quatorze heures» = 14:00 = 2 pm, and «cinquante» = 50. So it's 2:50 pm."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Not using the 24-hour clock? Add «du matin» (in the morning), «de l'après-midi» (in the afternoon) or «du soir» (in the evening): «huit heures du soir» = 8 pm."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quelle heure est-il ? Il est vingt heures.»", "fr": "Quelle heure est-il ? Il est vingt heures."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Quelle heure est-il ?» → «Il est» + number + «heures». «midi» and «minuit» stand alone. The 24-hour clock is normal. Minutes go at the end."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It is 10 pm. (use the 24-hour clock)", "answer": "Il est vingt-deux heures.", "accept": ["il est vingt-deux heures", "il est vingt-deux heures.", "il est vingt deux heures", "il est 22 heures", "il est 22h", "il est 22 h"], "hint": "10 pm + 12 = ?", "right": "Excellent ! 10 pm = 22:00 = «vingt-deux heures».", "wrong": "Add 12 to 10 pm → 22 → «Il est vingt-deux heures.»"}]}, "6-5": {"stageId": "6-5", "title": "Half past, quarter past, quarter to", "goal": "By the end you can say times with «et quart», «et demie», «moins le quart» and minutes to the hour.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Now the friendly way to say times: half past, quarter past, quarter to. French adds little words after the hour."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«demi» means half. What do you think «Il est trois heures et demie» means?", "choices": ["It is 3:30.", "It is 3:15.", "It is 2:30."], "answer": "It is 3:30.", "right": "Yes! «et demie» = 'and a half' → half past three.", "wrong": "«et demie» = 'and a half', added after the hour: three hours and a half = 3:30."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "After «midi» and «minuit», write «et demi» without the final -e: «midi et demi» (12:30)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Quarter past is «et quart»: «Il est deux heures et quart» = 2:15."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'It is a quarter past three'?", "choices": ["Il est trois heures et quart.", "Il est trois heures moins le quart.", "Il est trois heures et demie."], "answer": "Il est trois heures et quart.", "right": "Bravo ! Quarter past = «et quart», literally 'and a quarter'.", "wrong": "Quarter PAST = «et quart» ('and a quarter'). «moins le quart» is quarter TO, and «et demie» is half past."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Quarter to uses «moins» (minus): «Il est quatre heures moins le quart» = four minus a quarter = 3:45. Notice: you name the NEXT hour!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What time is «Il est dix heures moins le quart» ?", "choices": ["10:15", "9:45", "10:45"], "answer": "9:45", "right": "Yes! Ten minus a quarter = 9:45. French counts back from the coming hour.", "wrong": "«moins» = minus. Ten o'clock minus a quarter of an hour = 9:45."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Same trick for other minutes after the half hour: next hour + «moins» + minutes. «Il est deux heures moins vingt» = 1:40."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'It is twenty-five to three' (2:35)?", "choices": ["Il est deux heures moins vingt-cinq.", "Il est trois heures moins vingt-cinq.", "Il est trois heures et vingt-cinq."], "answer": "Il est trois heures moins vingt-cinq.", "right": "Exactly! The next hour (three) minus twenty-five.", "wrong": "With «moins», name the NEXT hour: 2:35 = three minus twenty-five → «trois heures moins vingt-cinq». And never put «et» before plain minutes."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "With the 24-hour clock, keep it simple and just read the numbers: 17:30 = «dix-sept heures trente», 14:15 = «quatorze heures quinze», 15:45 = «quinze heures quarante-cinq»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "On a train timetable you see 17:30. What does the announcer say?", "choices": ["dix-sept heures trente", "sept heures trente", "dix-huit heures moins trente"], "answer": "dix-sept heures trente", "right": "Yes! With 24-hour times, just read the numbers: 17 = «dix-sept», 30 = «trente».", "wrong": "17 = «dix-sept» and 30 = «trente». With the 24-hour clock, just read the numbers: «dix-sept heures trente»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est onze heures dix.", "en": "It is ten past eleven.", "note": "Minutes past the hour: just add the number."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il est quatre heures et demie.»", "fr": "Il est quatre heures et demie."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «et quart» = quarter past, «et demie» = half past, «moins le quart» = quarter to (name the next hour!), «moins» + minutes for 'to'. With the 24-hour clock, just read the numbers."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It is a quarter to one.", "answer": "Il est une heure moins le quart.", "accept": ["il est une heure moins le quart", "il est une heure moins le quart.", "il est 1 heure moins le quart"], "hint": "Name the next hour («une heure»), then subtract.", "right": "Parfait ! One o'clock minus a quarter = 12:45.", "wrong": "Name the next hour, then subtract a quarter: «Il est une heure moins le quart.»"}]}, "6-6": {"stageId": "6-6", "title": "Everyday expressions with «être»", "goal": "By the end you can say you're late, early, on time, back, about to do something, and that you agree.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«être» (to be) appears in lots of everyday expressions. Today you'll learn a handful you can use straight away."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«retard» means delay. What do you think «Je suis en retard» means?", "choices": ["I am late.", "I am early.", "I am back."], "answer": "I am late.", "right": "Yes! «être en retard» = to be late. Very useful for apologising!", "wrong": "«retard» means delay, so «être en retard» = to be late. 'Early' is «en avance»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A time trio: «être en avance» = to be early, «être à l'heure» = to be on time, «être en retard» = to be late. And when you come home: «être de retour» = to be back."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The meeting is at 9:00 and you arrive at 8:50. You are…", "choices": ["en avance", "en retard", "à l'heure"], "answer": "en avance", "right": "Bravo ! Ten minutes before = «en avance» (early).", "wrong": "Arriving before the time = early = «en avance». «à l'heure» means exactly on time."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Culture: in France, being «à l'heure» matters for trains and work, but for dinner at a friend's home, arriving 10–15 minutes late is considered polite!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Another everyday one: «être d'accord» = to agree. «Je suis d'accord !» = I agree! People often shorten it to just «D'accord !» = OK!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'Tom doesn't agree with Paul'?", "choices": ["Tom n'a pas d'accord avec Paul.", "Tom n'est pas d'accord avec Paul.", "Tom ne fait pas d'accord avec Paul."], "answer": "Tom n'est pas d'accord avec Paul.", "right": "Exactly! It's an «être» expression: «être d'accord» → «il n'est pas d'accord».", "wrong": "English just says 'agree', but French uses «être»: «être d'accord». So: «Tom n'est pas d'accord avec Paul.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis en train de faire mes devoirs.", "en": "I am in the middle of doing my homework.", "note": "«être en train de» + verb = to be busy doing something right now."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous sommes sur le point de partir.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["We are about to leave.", "We have just left.", "We are on the way."], "answer": "We are about to leave.", "right": "Yes! «être sur le point de» = to be about to (literally 'on the point of').", "wrong": "«sur le point de» = on the point of → about to. The action hasn't happened yet: 'We are about to leave.'"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Feelings and health: «être en forme» = to be fit / in good shape, «être enrhumé(e)» = to have a cold, «être en colère» = to be angry."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your brother sneezes all day. He…", "choices": ["est enrhumé", "est en forme", "est en colère"], "answer": "est enrhumé", "right": "Right! «être enrhumé» = to have a cold. Notice: English says 'have', French says 'be'.", "wrong": "Sneezing = a cold = «être enrhumé». «en forme» means fit, «en colère» means angry."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«être bien dans sa peau» literally means 'to be good in your skin' — a lovely way to say you feel comfortable with who you are."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je suis désolé, je suis en retard !» (Sorry, I'm late!)", "fr": "Je suis désolé, je suis en retard !"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «en avance / à l'heure / en retard», «de retour», «d'accord», «en train de», «sur le point de», «en forme», «enrhumé», «en colère». All of them use «être»!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: My parents are back.", "answer": "Mes parents sont de retour.", "accept": ["mes parents sont de retour", "mes parents sont de retour."], "hint": "«mes parents» = they → «sont», then «de retour»", "right": "Super ! «mes parents» = «ils» → «sont», + «de retour».", "wrong": "«mes parents» = they → «sont», then «de retour»: «Mes parents sont de retour.»"}]}, "6-7": {"stageId": "6-7", "title": "Hungry, cold, right: expressions with «avoir»", "goal": "By the end you can say your age and how you feel using «avoir».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Here's a big surprise for English speakers: many feelings that use 'to be' in English use «avoir» (to have) in French."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In French you don't 'be' 15 — you 'have' 15 years. How do you say 'I am 15 years old'?", "choices": ["Je suis 15 ans.", "J'ai 15 ans.", "J'ai 15."], "answer": "J'ai 15 ans.", "right": "Yes! Age uses «avoir» + number + «ans» (years).", "wrong": "Age uses «avoir», and «ans» (years) can't be dropped: «J'ai 15 ans». «Je suis 15 ans» is a classic beginner mistake."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Body feelings also use «avoir»: «avoir faim» (to be hungry), «avoir soif» (thirsty), «avoir chaud» (hot), «avoir froid» (cold), «avoir sommeil» (sleepy)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai faim et j'ai soif !", "en": "I'm hungry and I'm thirsty!", "note": "Literally: I have hunger and I have thirst."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "It's winter and you forgot your coat. What do you say?", "choices": ["Je suis froid.", "J'ai froid.", "J'ai chaud."], "answer": "J'ai froid.", "right": "Parfait ! «avoir froid» = to feel cold.", "wrong": "Feeling cold = «avoir froid». «Je suis froid» would mean you're a cold, unfriendly person!"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Careful: «Je suis chaud» does NOT mean 'I feel hot'. It's slang for 'I'm up for it!' and can even sound flirty. To talk about temperature, always say «J'ai chaud»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Being right or wrong also uses «avoir»: «avoir raison» = to be right, «avoir tort» = to be wrong."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend says Paris is the capital of France. You reply…", "choices": ["Tu as raison.", "Tu es raison.", "Tu as tort."], "answer": "Tu as raison.", "right": "Bravo ! «Tu as raison» = You're right. «avoir raison» uses avoir.", "wrong": "'To be right' = «avoir raison» → «Tu as raison». «Tu as tort» would mean 'You're wrong'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two super-useful ones with «de»: «avoir besoin de» = to need, «avoir envie de» = to feel like."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai besoin d'un café. J'ai envie de dormir.", "en": "I need a coffee. I feel like sleeping.", "note": "«de» becomes «d'» before a vowel."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les enfants ont sommeil.» means…", "choices": ["The children are sleepy.", "The children are asleep.", "The children have a dream."], "answer": "The children are sleepy.", "right": "Yes! «avoir sommeil» = to feel sleepy (not asleep yet).", "wrong": "«sommeil» means sleep, and «avoir sommeil» = to feel sleepy. The children aren't asleep yet!"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "More to collect: «avoir de la chance» (to be lucky), «avoir peur» (to be scared), «avoir mal» (to hurt). «Nous avons de la chance, il fait beau !» = We're lucky, it's sunny!"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'ai faim, j'ai soif et j'ai sommeil !»", "fr": "J'ai faim, j'ai soif et j'ai sommeil !"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: age, hunger, thirst, hot/cold, sleepy, right/wrong, lucky, scared → all with «avoir» in French. Think 'I HAVE hunger', not 'I am hungry'."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: You are right. (to a friend)", "answer": "Tu as raison.", "accept": ["tu as raison", "tu as raison.", "tu as raison !"], "hint": "«tu» + avoir + «raison»", "right": "Excellent ! «avoir raison» → «tu as raison».", "wrong": "'To be right' uses «avoir»: «Tu as raison.»"}]}, "6-8": {"stageId": "6-8", "title": "Drilling «être» in the present", "goal": "By the end you can choose the right form of «être» for any subject, even nouns and names.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«être» (to be) is the most important verb in French — and it's irregular, so we learn it by heart. Singular: «je suis, tu es, il/elle/on est»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Tu ___ content.» (You are happy.)", "choices": ["es", "est", "êtes"], "answer": "es", "right": "Yes! «tu es». «es» and «est» sound the same, but «tu» always takes «es».", "wrong": "With «tu», être is «es»: «tu es». «est» is for il/elle/on, and «êtes» for vous."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«tu es», «il est» and «elle est» all sound exactly the same: [è]. Only the spelling changes!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the plural: «nous sommes, vous êtes, ils/elles sont»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Vous ___ courageux.» (You are brave.)", "choices": ["sommes", "êtes", "sont"], "answer": "êtes", "right": "Bravo ! «vous êtes». The little hat on the «ê» replaces an old letter s.", "wrong": "«vous» always goes with «êtes». «sommes» is for nous, «sont» for ils/elles."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The tricky part: when the subject is a noun or a name, find the matching pronoun in your head first. «Le chat» = il → «Le chat est dans son panier»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Les filles ___ grandes.»", "choices": ["est", "sont", "sommes"], "answer": "sont", "right": "Yes! «les filles» = elles → «sont».", "wrong": "«les filles» is feminine plural = «elles», so être becomes «sont»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Mon frère et moi ___ dans la maison.»", "choices": ["sont", "sommes", "êtes"], "answer": "sommes", "right": "Perfect! 'My brother and I' = we = «nous» → «sommes».", "wrong": "A group that includes «moi» (me) = «nous», so the verb is «sommes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Simon et toi ___ très gentils.»", "choices": ["êtes", "sont", "es"], "answer": "êtes", "right": "Excellent ! Simon + you = «vous» → «êtes».", "wrong": "«toi» = you. Simon + you = you all = «vous» → «êtes»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "To make it negative, wrap «ne… pas» around the verb: «Je ne suis pas en forme» = I'm not in good shape."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On est chez Tom.", "en": "We are at Tom's.", "note": "«on» takes «est», like il/elle."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say the whole verb out loud: «je suis, tu es, il est, elle est, on est, nous sommes, vous êtes, ils sont, elles sont».", "fr": "je suis, tu es, il est, elle est, on est, nous sommes, vous êtes, ils sont, elles sont"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: noun subject? Find its pronoun, then pick the form. 'X and me' = «nous sommes», 'X and you' = «vous êtes», other groups = «sont»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The children are at school.", "answer": "Les enfants sont à l'école.", "accept": ["les enfants sont à l'école", "les enfants sont à l'école.", "les enfants sont a l'ecole", "les enfants sont a l'école", "les enfants sont à l ecole", "les enfants sont a l ecole"], "hint": "«les enfants» = ils → ?", "right": "Bravo ! «les enfants» = «ils» → «sont».", "wrong": "«les enfants» is plural = «ils», so: «Les enfants sont à l'école.»"}]}, "6-9": {"stageId": "6-9", "title": "Using «être» to describe and locate", "goal": "By the end you can describe people and say where they are with «être», with good pronunciation.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's use «être» in real life: to describe people and to say where things are."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis grand. Elle est très belle.", "en": "I am tall. She is very pretty."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A man says «Je suis grand». What does a woman say?", "choices": ["Je suis grand.", "Je suis grande.", "Je suis grands."], "answer": "Je suis grande.", "right": "Yes! After «être», the adjective agrees with the person: a woman adds -e.", "wrong": "The adjective after «être» agrees with the subject. For a woman, add -e: «Je suis grande»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«être» + a little place word tells where someone is: «dans» (in), «sous» (under), «sur» (on), «entre» (between), «près de» (near)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elles sont sous la Tour Eiffel.» Where are they?", "choices": ["under the Eiffel Tower", "on the Eiffel Tower", "near the Eiffel Tower"], "answer": "under the Eiffel Tower", "right": "Exactly! «sous» = under. On top would be «sur».", "wrong": "«sous» means under. Don't confuse it with «sur» (on). 'Near' is «près de»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«sous» or «sur»? Think «sur» = 'surface' (on top) and «sous» = 'sous-chef' (the cook under the chef)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'We are near the library'?", "choices": ["Nous sommes près de la bibliothèque.", "Nous sommes sous la bibliothèque.", "Nous êtes près de la bibliothèque."], "answer": "Nous sommes près de la bibliothèque.", "right": "Parfait ! «nous sommes» + «près de» = we are near.", "wrong": "'Near' is «près de», and «nous» takes «sommes» (never «êtes»): «Nous sommes près de la bibliothèque.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Pronunciation time! In «vous êtes», the silent s of «vous» wakes up and sounds like a z: «vou-z-êtes». This link is called a liaison."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «vous êtes» ?", "choices": ["vou-êtes (no link)", "vou-z-êtes", "vouss-êtes"], "answer": "vou-z-êtes", "right": "Yes! The s links to the next vowel with a z sound.", "wrong": "Before a vowel, the silent s of «vous» is pronounced as a z: «vou-z-êtes»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Your ears can tell verbs apart: «ils sont» (they are) has a hard s sound, while «ils ont» (they have) has a z liaison: «il-z-ont»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with the liaison: «Vous êtes presque là.» (You are nearly there.)", "fr": "Vous êtes presque là."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ils sont entre Lyon et Paris.", "en": "They are between Lyon and Paris."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «être» + an agreeing adjective describes; «être» + «dans / sous / sur / entre / près de» locates. And remember the liaison in «vous êtes»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: You are nice and clever. (to a male friend; «sympa» = nice, «intelligent» = clever)", "answer": "Tu es sympa et intelligent.", "accept": ["tu es sympa et intelligent", "tu es sympa et intelligent."], "hint": "Starts with «Tu es…»", "right": "Génial ! «tu es» + two adjectives in the masculine.", "wrong": "To a friend, use «tu es», then the adjectives: «Tu es sympa et intelligent.»"}]}, "6-10": {"stageId": "6-10", "title": "Drilling «avoir» in the present", "goal": "By the end you can choose the right form of «avoir» for any subject and use it in the negative.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«avoir» (to have) is the other super-verb: «j'ai, tu as, il/elle/on a, nous avons, vous avez, ils/elles ont»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Why is it «j'ai» and not «je ai» ?", "choices": ["«je» drops its e before a vowel", "It's a different verb", "It's slang"], "answer": "«je» drops its e before a vowel", "right": "Yes! «je» + vowel → «j'». Same in «j'aime», «j'habite».", "wrong": "«je» loses its e before a vowel sound to make it easier to say: «je + ai = j'ai»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Mon frère ___ un vélo rouge.»", "choices": ["a", "as", "ont"], "answer": "a", "right": "Yes! «mon frère» = il → «a».", "wrong": "«mon frère» is one person = «il», so avoir = «a». «as» goes with «tu»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«il a» (he has) vs «à» (to/at): same sound, different meaning. The verb has NO accent: «il a un chat», «il va à Paris»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Alex et Nathalie ___ beaucoup d'amis.»", "choices": ["a", "avez", "ont"], "answer": "ont", "right": "Bravo ! Two people = «ils» → «ont».", "wrong": "Alex and Nathalie = they = «ils» → «ont»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Watch the endings: «nous avons» ends in -ons, «vous avez» ends in -ez. These endings come back with almost every French verb!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Fred et toi ___ de la chance.»", "choices": ["avons", "avez", "ont"], "answer": "avez", "right": "Perfect! Fred + you = «vous» → «avez». And «avoir de la chance» = to be lucky.", "wrong": "«toi» (you) is in the group, but not «moi», so it's «vous» → «avez»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Mon père et moi ___ les mêmes yeux.»", "choices": ["avons", "ont", "avez"], "answer": "avons", "right": "Yes! 'My father and I' = «nous» → «avons».", "wrong": "A group with «moi» (me) is «nous», so avoir becomes «avons»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With «ne… pas»: «ai», «as», «a» start with a vowel, so «ne» becomes «n'»: «Tu n'as pas de frères.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je n'ai pas de chat.", "en": "I don't have a cat.", "note": "After «pas», «un / une» becomes «de»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Two handy extras: «avoir l'air» = to look/seem («Tu as l'air content» = You look happy) and «avoir mal à la tête» = to have a headache."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud — notice the z liaison in «nous avons»: «Nous avons trois chiens et deux chats.»", "fr": "Nous avons trois chiens et deux chats."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «j'ai, tu as, il/elle/on a, nous avons, vous avez, ils/elles ont». Find the pronoun hidden in the subject, then pick the form. Negative: «je n'ai pas de…»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We have three dogs and two cats. (use «nous»)", "answer": "Nous avons trois chiens et deux chats.", "accept": ["nous avons trois chiens et deux chats", "nous avons trois chiens et deux chats.", "nous avons 3 chiens et 2 chats"], "hint": "«nous» + avoir = ?", "right": "Magnifique ! «nous avons» + plural nouns with -s.", "wrong": "With «nous», avoir is «avons»: «Nous avons trois chiens et deux chats.»"}]}, "7-1": {"stageId": "7-1", "title": "Describing yourself: age, colours, hair & eyes", "goal": "By the end you can give your age, use colours and linking words, and describe yourself or a family member.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! Today you'll learn to describe yourself and your family: age, hair, eyes — plus little linking words to join your ideas."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«et» = and. What do you think «mais» means in «J'ai un chat mais pas de chien» ?", "choices": ["but", "or", "so"], "answer": "but", "right": "Yes! «mais» = but. It introduces a contrast.", "wrong": "«mais» = but: it introduces a contrast. 'Or' is «ou» and 'so' is «donc»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "French kids remember 7 linking words with a silly question: «Mais ou et donc or ni car ?» ('But where is Ornicar?') → mais (but), ou (or), et (and), donc (so), or (yet), ni (neither), car (because)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Age! In French you HAVE your years: «avoir» + number + «ans». The question is «Quel âge as-tu ?» (How old are you?)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your sister is 20. How do you say 'She is 20 years old'?", "choices": ["Elle est vingt ans.", "Elle a vingt ans.", "Elle a vingt."], "answer": "Elle a vingt ans.", "right": "Bravo ! «avoir» for age, and never forget «ans».", "wrong": "Age uses «avoir» plus the word «ans»: «Elle a vingt ans». You can't drop «ans» like in English."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mon anniversaire est le dix-sept mai.", "en": "My birthday is on the 17th of May.", "note": "No 'th' and no 'of': just «le» + number + month."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Colours are adjectives, so they come after the noun and agree: «un pull vert», «une robe verte», «des robes vertes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«robe» (dress) is feminine. How do you say 'a black dress'?", "choices": ["une noire robe", "une robe noir", "une robe noire"], "answer": "une robe noire", "right": "Perfect! The colour goes after the noun and takes -e for feminine.", "wrong": "Colours go AFTER the noun and agree. Feminine → add -e: «une robe noire»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Some colours never change: «marron» (brown) and «orange» are also nouns (a chestnut, an orange), so «des yeux marron», «des robes orange». Two-word colours don't change either: «des chemises bleu foncé»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Most adjectives go after the noun, but a few short, common ones go BEFORE. Remember BAGS: Beauty, Age, Goodness, Size → «un joli chat», «un vieux chat», «un bon chat», «un petit chat»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is correct for 'three little cats'?", "choices": ["trois chats petits", "trois petits chats", "trois petit chats"], "answer": "trois petits chats", "right": "Yes! «petit» is a Size adjective (BAGS), so it goes before — and it still agrees: -s.", "wrong": "«petit» is a BAGS adjective (Size), so it goes before the noun, and agrees with the plural: «trois petits chats»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To describe hair and eyes, use «avoir les…»: «J'ai les cheveux noirs et les yeux verts.» Note: «cheveux» (hair) is always plural in French!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'He has brown eyes'?", "choices": ["Il a les yeux marron.", "Il a les yeux marrons.", "Il est les yeux marron."], "answer": "Il a les yeux marron.", "right": "Excellent ! «avoir» + «les yeux» + colour, and «marron» never takes -s.", "wrong": "Use «avoir les yeux…», and remember «marron» never changes: «Il a les yeux marron»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Describe yourself out loud, for example: «J'ai trente ans. J'ai les cheveux bruns et les yeux bleus.»", "fr": "J'ai trente ans. J'ai les cheveux bruns et les yeux bleus."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: linking words «et, mais, ou, donc, car». Age = «avoir … ans». Colours follow the noun and agree (except «marron», «orange»). BAGS adjectives go before. Hair and eyes = «avoir les cheveux / les yeux…»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I have a sister. She is 20 years old.", "answer": "J'ai une sœur. Elle a vingt ans.", "accept": ["j'ai une sœur. elle a vingt ans.", "j'ai une sœur. elle a vingt ans", "j'ai une soeur. elle a vingt ans", "j'ai une soeur. elle a vingt ans.", "j'ai une sœur, elle a vingt ans", "j'ai une soeur, elle a vingt ans", "j'ai une sœur elle a vingt ans", "j'ai une soeur elle a vingt ans", "j'ai une sœur. elle a 20 ans", "j'ai une soeur. elle a 20 ans"], "hint": "«sœur» = sister. Age uses «avoir» + «ans».", "right": "Magnifique ! «J'ai une sœur» + age with «avoir» and «ans».", "wrong": "Use «avoir» twice: «J'ai une sœur. Elle a vingt ans.» Don't forget «ans»!"}]}, "7-2": {"stageId": "7-2", "title": "Before or after? The BAGS adjectives", "goal": "By the end you can place adjectives correctly before or after the noun.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In French, most adjectives come AFTER the noun. But a small group of very common adjectives jumps in front."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess: which is normal French for 'a red car'?", "choices": ["une rouge voiture", "une voiture rouge"], "answer": "une voiture rouge", "right": "Yes! The default spot for an adjective is after the noun.", "wrong": "The normal French order is noun + adjective: «une voiture rouge»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The exceptions fit the word BAGS: Beauty, Age, Goodness, Size."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La jolie fille est dans le salon.", "en": "The pretty girl is in the living room.", "note": "B = Beauty: «beau / belle», «joli / jolie»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A = Age. How do you say 'an old house'? («maison» is feminine)", "choices": ["une maison vieille", "une vieille maison", "une vieux maison"], "answer": "une vieille maison", "right": "Yes! «vieux» is an Age adjective, so it goes before, and its feminine is «vieille».", "wrong": "Age adjectives go before the noun. «maison» is feminine, so «vieux» → «vieille»: «une vieille maison»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "G = Goodness: «bon / bonne», «mauvais / mauvaise», «gentil / gentille». S = Size: «grand / grande», «petit / petite», «gros / grosse»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Tom gave the right answer.' Which is correct?", "choices": ["Tom a donné la réponse bonne.", "Tom a donné la bonne réponse.", "Tom a donné la bon réponse."], "answer": "Tom a donné la bonne réponse.", "right": "Bravo ! «bon» is a Goodness adjective → before the noun, in the feminine «bonne».", "wrong": "«bon» is a BAGS adjective (Goodness), so it goes before, and agrees with «réponse» (feminine): «la bonne réponse»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'We have a small cat but also a big dog.'", "choices": ["Nous avons un chat petit mais aussi un chien grand.", "Nous avons un petit chat mais aussi un grand chien.", "Nous avons un petit chat mais aussi un chien grand."], "answer": "Nous avons un petit chat mais aussi un grand chien.", "right": "Perfect! «petit» and «grand» are both Size adjectives, so both go before.", "wrong": "Size adjectives («petit», «grand») go before the noun: «un petit chat», «un grand chien»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Special forms before a vowel or silent h: «beau» → «bel», «vieux» → «vieil». «un bel appartement», «un vieil homme». It keeps the words flowing!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'a beautiful flat'? («appartement» is masculine)", "choices": ["un beau appartement", "un bel appartement", "un belle appartement"], "answer": "un bel appartement", "right": "Excellent ! Before a vowel, masculine «beau» becomes «bel» (it sounds like «belle»).", "wrong": "«appartement» starts with a vowel, so «beau» changes to «bel»: «un bel appartement». «belle» is the feminine form."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Bonus: a few adjectives change meaning with position. «un ancien ami» = a former friend, «un ami ancien» = an old (long-time) friend. «un grand homme» = a great man, «un homme grand» = a tall man."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«ma propre voiture» means…", "choices": ["my clean car", "my own car", "my old car"], "answer": "my own car", "right": "Yes! Before the noun, «propre» = own. After it, «une voiture propre» = a clean car.", "wrong": "Position matters: «propre» before the noun = own; after the noun = clean. «ma propre voiture» = my own car."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'ai un petit chat et un grand chien.»", "fr": "J'ai un petit chat et un grand chien."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: adjectives usually follow the noun. BAGS adjectives (Beauty, Age, Goodness, Size) go before. «bel» and «vieil» before a vowel. Some adjectives change meaning with position."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: a pretty girl", "answer": "une jolie fille", "accept": ["une jolie fille", "une jolie fille."], "hint": "«joli» is a Beauty adjective, and «fille» is feminine.", "right": "Parfait ! Beauty → before the noun, feminine → «jolie».", "wrong": "«joli» goes before the noun (B for Beauty) and agrees with «fille»: «une jolie fille»."}]}, "7-3": {"stageId": "7-3", "title": "Two ways to say 'because': «parce que» & «car»", "goal": "By the end you can explain why with «parce que» and «car» and know which to use.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French has two common ways to say 'because': «parce que» and «car». Let's see how they differ."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis malade parce que j'ai trop mangé.", "en": "I am ill because I ate too much."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «parce qu'elle a froid», why «qu'» and not «que» ?", "choices": ["«que» drops its e before a vowel", "It's a typo", "It means 'that'"], "answer": "«que» drops its e before a vowel", "right": "Yes! Like «je → j'», «que» becomes «qu'» before a vowel: «parce qu'il», «parce qu'elle».", "wrong": "«que» loses its e before a vowel: «parce que + elle → parce qu'elle»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«parce que» answers the question «Pourquoi ?» (Why?). It's the everyday spoken choice, and it can even start a sentence."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone asks «Pourquoi ?». The most natural spoken answer starts with…", "choices": ["Parce que…", "Car…", "Donc…"], "answer": "Parce que…", "right": "Exactly! «parce que» can open an answer — «car» can't.", "wrong": "To answer «Pourquoi ?», say «Parce que…». «car» can't start a sentence, and «donc» means 'so'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«car» also means 'because' (or 'for'). It's a bit more formal and common in writing. It links two parts of a sentence, usually after a comma."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Alice n'est pas à l'école, car elle est malade.", "en": "Alice is not at school because she is sick."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Where can «car» go?", "choices": ["In the middle, linking two parts", "At the very beginning of a sentence", "At the end of a sentence"], "answer": "In the middle, linking two parts", "right": "Yes! «car» joins two clauses: «…, car…». It never starts a sentence.", "wrong": "«car» links two clauses: «Je reste, car il pleut». If you want to start with 'because', use «parce que»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Fun fact: «un car» is also a noun meaning a coach (a long-distance bus). The context always makes it clear!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Choose the best sentence for a formal written report:", "choices": ["Le président ne peut pas venir, car il est absent.", "Car il est absent, le président ne peut pas venir.", "Le président ne peut pas venir, car qu'il est absent."], "answer": "Le président ne peut pas venir, car il est absent.", "right": "Parfait ! Formal writing loves «car», placed in the middle after a comma.", "wrong": "«car» can't start a sentence, and it's never followed by «que». The right one: «…, car il est absent»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je reste à la maison parce qu'il pleut.» (I'm staying home because it's raining.)", "fr": "Je reste à la maison parce qu'il pleut."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: both mean 'because'. «parce que» = everyday, answers «Pourquoi ?», can start a sentence. «car» = more formal, only in the middle after a comma."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I am ill because I ate too much. (use «parce que»)", "answer": "Je suis malade parce que j'ai trop mangé.", "accept": ["je suis malade parce que j'ai trop mangé", "je suis malade parce que j'ai trop mangé.", "je suis malade parce que j'ai trop mange", "je suis malade parce que j ai trop mangé", "je suis malade parce que j ai trop mange"], "hint": "«malade» = ill, «trop mangé» = ate too much", "right": "Bravo ! «parce que» + «j'ai» — «que» stays whole here because «j'ai» starts with a consonant.", "wrong": "«Je suis malade» + «parce que» + «j'ai trop mangé»: «Je suis malade parce que j'ai trop mangé.»"}]}, "7-4": {"stageId": "7-4", "title": "What are you wearing? Clothes", "goal": "By the end you can name common clothes and say what you're wearing, with colours.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's get dressed in French! First, the key verb: «Je porte…» = I wear / I'm wearing."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je porte un pantalon et une veste.", "en": "I'm wearing trousers and a jacket.", "note": "«un pantalon» is singular in French, unlike English 'trousers'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What do you think «un chapeau» is? Picture a 'chap' wearing one on his head…", "choices": ["a hat", "a shoe", "a coat"], "answer": "a hat", "right": "Oui ! «un chapeau» = a hat. The «eau» sounds like 'oh'.", "wrong": "«un chapeau» = a hat. A coat is «un manteau», and shoes are «des chaussures»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Feet first: «des chaussures» (shoes), «des chaussettes» (socks), «des baskets» (trainers), «des sandales» (sandals)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which do you put on your feet FIRST?", "choices": ["des chaussettes", "des chaussures", "des gants"], "answer": "des chaussettes", "right": "Exactly! Socks first: «des chaussettes», then shoes: «des chaussures».", "wrong": "Socks go on first — «des chaussettes». «chaussures» are shoes, and «gants» are gloves for your hands."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory hook: «chaussettes» are the small ones (socks). The ending «-ette» often means 'little' in French, like «une maisonnette» (a little house)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Winter wear: «un manteau» (coat), «une écharpe» (scarf), «des gants» (gloves), «un pull» (jumper), «un gilet» (cardigan)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "It's freezing outside. What do you wear?", "choices": ["un manteau, une écharpe, des gants", "un maillot de bain, un short, des sandales", "un pyjama, des pantoufles, un peignoir"], "answer": "un manteau, une écharpe, des gants", "right": "Bien joué ! Coat, scarf and gloves — perfect for the cold.", "wrong": "For cold weather: «un manteau» (coat), «une écharpe» (scarf), «des gants» (gloves). The others are for the beach or for bed!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Add colours after the clothing, and make them agree: «une ceinture bleue», «des sandales rouges»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I am wearing red sandals'?", "choices": ["Je porte des rouges sandales.", "Je porte des sandales rouges.", "Je porte des sandales rouge."], "answer": "Je porte des sandales rouges.", "right": "Bravo ! Colour after the noun, plus -s for plural.", "wrong": "Colours follow the noun and agree. «sandales» is plural, so «rouges»: «des sandales rouges»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False friends! «une veste» is a jacket (not a vest), and «un costume» is a man's suit (not a costume). A woman's suit is «un tailleur»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Describe what you're wearing now, for example: «Je porte un t-shirt blanc et un pantalon noir.»", "fr": "Je porte un t-shirt blanc et un pantalon noir."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Je porte…» + clothing + colour (after it, agreeing). Feet: «chaussures, chaussettes, baskets». Winter: «manteau, écharpe, gants, pull». Watch the false friends «veste» and «costume»!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: He is wearing a cap. («une casquette» = a cap)", "answer": "Il porte une casquette.", "accept": ["il porte une casquette", "il porte une casquette."], "hint": "«il» + «porte» + …", "right": "Super ! «Il porte» = he is wearing — French uses one simple verb.", "wrong": "'He is wearing' is just «il porte» in French: «Il porte une casquette.»"}]}, "7-5": {"stageId": "7-5", "title": "The face and head", "goal": "By the end you can name the parts of the face and describe someone's eyes and hair.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's learn the face! «le visage» = the face, «la tête» = the head."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Think of 'amuse-bouche' (a tiny snack before dinner). What is «la bouche» ?", "choices": ["the mouth", "the nose", "the chin"], "answer": "the mouth", "right": "Yes! An amuse-bouche 'amuses the mouth'. «la bouche» = the mouth.", "wrong": "«la bouche» = the mouth (an amuse-bouche 'amuses the mouth'). The nose is «le nez», the chin «le menton»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More: «le nez» (nose), «les oreilles» (ears), «le front» (forehead), «le menton» (chin), «les joues» (cheeks)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «le nez» ?", "choices": ["with a z sound at the end", "like «le né» (silent z)", "like «le nèze»"], "answer": "like «le né» (silent z)", "right": "Yes! The final z is silent: «le nez» sounds like «le né».", "wrong": "Final consonants are often silent in French. «nez» sounds just like «né»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The eye is tricky: one eye = «l'œil», two eyes = «les yeux». Completely different words!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I have green eyes'?", "choices": ["J'ai les œils verts.", "J'ai les yeux verts.", "J'ai les yeux vert."], "answer": "J'ai les yeux verts.", "right": "Bravo ! The plural of «œil» is «yeux», and «verts» takes -s to agree.", "wrong": "«œil» has an irregular plural: «yeux». And the colour agrees: «les yeux verts»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«les cheveux» (hair) is ALWAYS plural in French. «un cheveu» is one single hair — so 'There's a hair in my soup!' = «Il y a un cheveu dans ma soupe !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'My sister has short hair'?", "choices": ["Ma sœur a le cheveu court.", "Ma sœur a les cheveux courts.", "Ma sœur est les cheveux courts."], "answer": "Ma sœur a les cheveux courts.", "right": "Perfect! «avoir les cheveux…», plural, and «courts» agrees.", "wrong": "Hair is plural in French and uses «avoir»: «Ma sœur a les cheveux courts»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Around the eyes and mouth: «les cils» (eyelashes), «les sourcils» (eyebrows), «les lèvres» (lips), «la langue» (tongue), «les dents» (teeth)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le dentiste regarde mes dents.", "en": "The dentist looks at my teeth.", "note": "«dent» → «dentiste»: easy to remember!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«les sourcils» (eyebrows) sit just above «les cils». So what are «les cils» ?", "choices": ["eyelashes", "lips", "cheeks"], "answer": "eyelashes", "right": "Yes! «les cils» = eyelashes, and «les sourcils» are the hairs above them: eyebrows.", "wrong": "«les cils» are the eyelashes. «sourcils» = the hairs above the «cils» → eyebrows."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Point and say: «la tête, le nez, la bouche, les yeux, les oreilles.»", "fr": "la tête, le nez, la bouche, les yeux, les oreilles"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «le visage, la tête, le nez (silent z), la bouche, les oreilles, le menton, les joues». One «œil», two «yeux». «les cheveux» is always plural."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I have green eyes and brown hair.", "answer": "J'ai les yeux verts et les cheveux marron.", "accept": ["j'ai les yeux verts et les cheveux marron", "j'ai les yeux verts et les cheveux marron.", "j'ai les yeux verts et les cheveux bruns", "j'ai les yeux verts et les cheveux bruns.", "j ai les yeux verts et les cheveux marron", "j ai les yeux verts et les cheveux bruns"], "hint": "«J'ai les yeux…» + «et les cheveux…»", "right": "Excellent ! Plural «yeux» and «cheveux», and the colours agree («marron» never changes).", "wrong": "Use «avoir les…» for both: «J'ai les yeux verts et les cheveux marron.»"}]}, "7-6": {"stageId": "7-6", "title": "Neck, back and tummy", "goal": "By the end you can name parts of the upper body and say where it hurts.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's move down the body: «le cou» (neck), «les épaules» (shoulders), «le dos» (back), «le ventre» (tummy)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you pronounce «le dos» ?", "choices": ["like 'doss'", "like 'doh' (silent s)", "like 'doz'"], "answer": "like 'doh' (silent s)", "right": "Yes! The final s is silent: «le dos» = 'doh'.", "wrong": "Final s is usually silent in French: «le dos» sounds like 'doh'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Funny one: «un os» (a bone) — you pronounce the s in the singular ('oss'), but NOT in the plural: «les os» sounds like 'lay-zoh'!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai mal au ventre.» What's the problem?", "choices": ["a tummy ache", "a backache", "a sore neck"], "answer": "a tummy ache", "right": "Yes! «avoir mal à» + body part = to have a pain in… «le ventre» = tummy.", "wrong": "«le ventre» is the tummy, so «J'ai mal au ventre» = I have a tummy ache. A backache would be «mal au dos»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Remember: «à + le = au». So «mal au ventre», «mal au dos». With «la» it stays: «mal à la tête». With a plural: «mal aux épaules»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I have a backache'?", "choices": ["J'ai mal à le dos.", "J'ai mal au dos.", "J'ai mal aux dos."], "answer": "J'ai mal au dos.", "right": "Perfect! «à + le dos» → «au dos».", "wrong": "«à + le» always merges into «au»: «J'ai mal au dos». «aux» is for plurals."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A few more: «le nombril» (belly button), «la pomme d'Adam» (Adam's apple), «le derrière» (bum — literally 'the behind')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«derrière» is also a little place word. What does «derrière la maison» mean?", "choices": ["behind the house", "in front of the house", "inside the house"], "answer": "behind the house", "right": "Exactly! «derrière» = behind — so «le derrière» is your behind!", "wrong": "«derrière» = behind. So «derrière la maison» = behind the house, and «le derrière» = your behind (bum)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle a un long cou.", "en": "She has a long neck.", "note": "Use «avoir» to describe someone's body."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«l'épaule» = the shoulder. How do you say 'the shoulders'?", "choices": ["les épaules", "les épauls", "le épaules"], "answer": "les épaules", "right": "Yes! «l'» becomes «les», and the noun takes a silent -s.", "wrong": "In the plural, «l'» becomes «les» and the noun adds a silent -s: «les épaules»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'ai mal au dos et au cou.»", "fr": "J'ai mal au dos et au cou."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «le cou, les épaules, le dos (silent s), le ventre, le nombril, un os». Pain = «avoir mal à»: «mal au dos», «mal à la tête», «mal aux épaules»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I have a tummy ache.", "answer": "J'ai mal au ventre.", "accept": ["j'ai mal au ventre", "j'ai mal au ventre.", "j ai mal au ventre"], "hint": "«avoir mal à» + «le ventre» → ?", "right": "Bravo ! «à + le ventre» = «au ventre».", "wrong": "Use «avoir mal à», and remember «à + le = au»: «J'ai mal au ventre.»"}]}, "7-7": {"stageId": "7-7", "title": "Arms, hands, legs and feet", "goal": "By the end you can name the parts of your arms and legs and describe them.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Now arms and legs! «le bras» (arm — silent s), «la main» (hand), «le doigt» (finger), «le pouce» (thumb), «le coude» (elbow)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le doigt» has silent letters. How do you think you say it?", "choices": ["'dwah' (rhymes with «moi»)", "'doigt' with g and t", "'doyg'"], "answer": "'dwah' (rhymes with «moi»)", "right": "Yes! The g and t are silent: «doigt» rhymes with «moi».", "wrong": "In «doigt», both g and t are silent. It sounds like 'dwah' and rhymes with «moi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Legs: «la jambe» (leg), «la cuisse» (thigh), «le genou» (knee), «la cheville» (ankle), «le pied» (foot)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What's the plural of «le genou» (knee)?", "choices": ["les genous", "les genoux", "les genoues"], "answer": "les genoux", "right": "Bravo ! «genou» is one of the few words that take -x in the plural.", "wrong": "«genou» takes an -x in the plural: «les genoux». It belongs to a small special group of words."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "French kids learn the 7 nouns in -ou that take -x: «bijou, caillou, chou, genou, hibou, joujou, pou». Almost every other noun in -ou takes -s."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Toes have two names. Which one literally means 'fingers of feet'?", "choices": ["les doigts de pieds", "les orteils", "les ongles"], "answer": "les doigts de pieds", "right": "Yes! «les doigts de pieds» = toes, literally. The other name is «les orteils».", "wrong": "«les doigts de pieds» = 'fingers of the feet' → toes. «les orteils» is the other word for toes, and «les ongles» are nails."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mon père a de grands pieds.", "en": "My father has big feet.", "note": "«des» becomes «de» when an adjective comes before a plural noun."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'She has long legs'?", "choices": ["Elle a de longues jambes.", "Elle a de long jambes.", "Elle a des longs jambes."], "answer": "Elle a de longues jambes.", "right": "Excellent ! «longues» agrees (feminine plural), and «des» becomes «de» before the adjective.", "wrong": "«jambes» is feminine plural, so «long → longues». And «des» becomes «de» before an adjective: «de longues jambes»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Hands: «la main» (hand) is feminine even though it ends in a consonant, and «l'ongle» = the fingernail. «main» also gives us «manucure» (manicure)!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word is NOT part of the leg?", "choices": ["la cheville", "le genou", "le coude"], "answer": "le coude", "right": "Yes! «le coude» is the elbow, in the middle of your arm.", "wrong": "«la cheville» (ankle) and «le genou» (knee) are on the leg. «le coude» is the elbow — on your arm."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Point and say: «la main, le bras, le coude, la jambe, le genou, le pied.»", "fr": "la main, le bras, le coude, la jambe, le genou, le pied"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: arm «le bras», hand «la main», finger «le doigt», thumb «le pouce», elbow «le coude». Leg «la jambe», knee «le genou» (plural «genoux»), ankle «la cheville», foot «le pied», toes «les orteils»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: My father has big feet.", "answer": "Mon père a de grands pieds.", "accept": ["mon père a de grands pieds", "mon père a de grands pieds.", "mon pere a de grands pieds", "mon père a des grands pieds", "mon pere a des grands pieds"], "hint": "«grand» goes before the noun (Size), and «des» → «de»", "right": "Magnifique ! «grands» before the noun, agreeing, with «de».", "wrong": "«grand» is a Size adjective, so it goes first: «Mon père a de grands pieds.»"}]}, "7-8": {"stageId": "7-8", "title": "Colours, part 1", "goal": "By the end you can name 7 basic colours and make them agree with the noun.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's paint in French! «rouge» (red), «jaune» (yellow), «vert» (green), «bleu» (blue), «noir» (black), «blanc» (white), «orange» (orange)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a French restaurant you order «du vin rouge». What colour is your wine?", "choices": ["red", "white", "pink"], "answer": "red", "right": "Yes! «rouge» = red. Santé !", "wrong": "«rouge» = red. White wine would be «du vin blanc»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Colours are adjectives, so they come AFTER the noun: «le perroquet vert» (the green parrot), never «le vert perroquet»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'the blue soap'?", "choices": ["le bleu savon", "le savon bleu"], "answer": "le savon bleu", "right": "Exactly! Noun first, then the colour.", "wrong": "Colours always come after the noun: «le savon bleu»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Colours agree: feminine + e, masculine plural + s, feminine plural + es. «vert, verte, verts, vertes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«table» is feminine. How do you say 'the green tables'?", "choices": ["les tables vert", "les tables vertes", "les tables verts"], "answer": "les tables vertes", "right": "Bravo ! Feminine plural → «vertes».", "wrong": "«tables» is feminine AND plural, so add -es: «les tables vertes»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Colours already ending in -e («rouge», «jaune», «orange») don't add another e: «une robe rouge». But watch «blanc»: its feminine is «blanche»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'the white table'?", "choices": ["la table blanc", "la table blance", "la table blanche"], "answer": "la table blanche", "right": "Yes! «blanc» has a special feminine: «blanche».", "wrong": "«blanc» is irregular: feminine = «blanche». So: «la table blanche»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le zèbre est noir et blanc.", "en": "The zebra is black and white.", "note": "After «être», the colour still agrees with the subject."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «rouge, jaune, vert, bleu, noir, blanc.» Make the r in «rouge» soft, from the back of your throat.", "fr": "rouge, jaune, vert, bleu, noir, blanc"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: 7 colours. They go after the noun and agree (+e, +s, +es). «blanc» → «blanche». Colours ending in -e don't change in the feminine."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The parrot is green. («le perroquet» = the parrot)", "answer": "Le perroquet est vert.", "accept": ["le perroquet est vert", "le perroquet est vert."], "hint": "«Le perroquet est…»", "right": "Parfait ! «perroquet» is masculine, so «vert» stays as it is.", "wrong": "«perroquet» is masculine singular, so the colour has no ending: «Le perroquet est vert.»"}]}, "7-9": {"stageId": "7-9", "title": "Colours, part 2", "goal": "By the end you can use pink, grey, purple, brown, turquoise, silver and gold, including the colours that never change.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "More colours: «rose» (pink), «gris» (grey), «violet» (purple), «marron» (brown), «turquoise», plus «argent» (silver) and «or» (gold)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«une souris» (a mouse) is feminine. How do you say 'the grey mouse'?", "choices": ["la souris gris", "la souris grise", "la souris grisse"], "answer": "la souris grise", "right": "Yes! «gris» + e = «grise» — and now you hear the s, like a z: 'greez'.", "wrong": "Feminine nouns add -e: «gris → grise». So: «la souris grise»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«violet» doubles its t in the feminine: «violette». Just like the flower — «une violette» is a violet!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'a purple dress'?", "choices": ["une robe violet", "une robe violete", "une robe violette"], "answer": "une robe violette", "right": "Bravo ! «violet» → «violette», with a double t.", "wrong": "«violet» doubles its t in the feminine: «une robe violette»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Big exception: «marron» (brown — literally 'chestnut') and «turquoise» never change. «des yeux marron», «des chaussures marron», «une armoire marron»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'brown shoes'? («chaussures» is feminine plural)", "choices": ["des chaussures marrons", "des chaussures marronnes", "des chaussures marron"], "answer": "des chaussures marron", "right": "Parfait ! «marron» never changes — no -e, no -s.", "wrong": "«marron» comes from the noun 'chestnut', so it never changes: «des chaussures marron»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«or» (gold) and «argent» (silver) are nouns. To use them as colours, say «la couleur or» or «la couleur argent». And «argent» also means money!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le flamant rose est rose.» What colour is the flamingo?", "choices": ["pink", "red", "purple"], "answer": "pink", "right": "Yes! «rose» = pink, like the flower «une rose».", "wrong": "«rose» = pink, like the flower «une rose». Red is «rouge», purple is «violet»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le lit violet est à côté de l'armoire marron.", "en": "The purple bed is next to the brown wardrobe.", "note": "«armoire» is feminine, but «marron» doesn't change."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «rose, gris, grise, violet, violette, marron.»", "fr": "rose, gris, grise, violet, violette, marron"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «gris → grise», «violet → violette». «marron» and «turquoise» never change. «or» and «argent» are used as «la couleur or / argent»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The koala is grey.", "answer": "Le koala est gris.", "accept": ["le koala est gris", "le koala est gris."], "hint": "«koala» is masculine.", "right": "Excellent ! Masculine singular → «gris», no ending.", "wrong": "«koala» is masculine, so the colour stays «gris»: «Le koala est gris.»"}]}, "7-10": {"stageId": "7-10", "title": "My family, part 1", "goal": "By the end you can name your close family members with «mon» and «ma».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's meet the family! «la mère» (mother), «le père» (father), and the cuter «maman» and «papa»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "We say «mon père» but «ma mère». Why «mon» vs «ma» ?", "choices": ["«mon» for masculine nouns, «ma» for feminine", "«mon» is formal, «ma» is informal", "There's no reason"], "answer": "«mon» for masculine nouns, «ma» for feminine", "right": "Exactly! 'My' agrees with the noun: «mon père», «ma mère».", "wrong": "'My' changes with the noun's gender: «mon» + masculine, «ma» + feminine."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Children: «le fils» (son) and «la fille» (daughter). «fille» also means 'girl'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation: «fils» = 'feess' (the l is silent, the s is spoken!), «fille» = 'fee-y'. Don't mix them up!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I have a son'?", "choices": ["J'ai un fils.", "J'ai une fille.", "J'ai un frère."], "answer": "J'ai un fils.", "right": "Yes! «un fils» = a son. «une fille» would be a daughter.", "wrong": "'Son' = «un fils». «une fille» is a daughter, and «un frère» is a brother."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Siblings: «le frère» (brother), «la sœur» (sister). Note the special letters «œ» in «sœur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'my sister'?", "choices": ["mon sœur", "ma sœur", "ma sœure"], "answer": "ma sœur", "right": "Bravo ! «sœur» is feminine → «ma sœur». No extra e needed.", "wrong": "«sœur» is feminine, so 'my' = «ma»: «ma sœur». It doesn't end in -e, but it's still feminine."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Couples: «le mari» (husband), «la femme» (wife). «femme» also means 'woman', but «mari» only means husband — 'a man' is «un homme»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«ma femme» means…", "choices": ["my wife", "my mother", "my sister"], "answer": "my wife", "right": "Yes! With 'my', «ma femme» = my wife. «une femme» alone = a woman.", "wrong": "«femme» = woman or wife. With 'my', «ma femme» = my wife. Mother is «mère», sister is «sœur»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La fille de ma sœur s'appelle Sarah.", "en": "My sister's daughter is called Sarah.", "note": "French has no 's: say 'the daughter OF my sister'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'my mother's brother'?", "choices": ["ma mère's frère", "le frère de ma mère", "le frère ma mère"], "answer": "le frère de ma mère", "right": "Bravo ! «le frère de ma mère» — French uses «de» instead of 's.", "wrong": "French has no 's. Put the thing owned first, then «de» + the owner: «le frère de ma mère»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Mon père, ma mère, mon frère et ma sœur.»", "fr": "Mon père, ma mère, mon frère et ma sœur."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «mon père / ma mère, mon fils / ma fille, mon frère / ma sœur, mon mari / ma femme». «mon» + masculine, «ma» + feminine. Use «de» instead of 's."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I have a brother and a sister.", "answer": "J'ai un frère et une sœur.", "accept": ["j'ai un frère et une sœur", "j'ai un frère et une sœur.", "j'ai un frère et une soeur", "j'ai un frère et une soeur.", "j'ai un frere et une soeur", "j ai un frère et une sœur", "j ai un frere et une soeur"], "hint": "«J'ai un…» + «et une…»", "right": "Super ! «un frère» (masculine) and «une sœur» (feminine).", "wrong": "Use «un» with masculine «frère» and «une» with feminine «sœur»: «J'ai un frère et une sœur.»"}]}, "7-11": {"stageId": "7-11", "title": "My family, part 2", "goal": "By the end you can name your extended family: aunts, uncles, cousins, grandparents, nephews and nieces.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's grow the family tree: «la tante» (aunt), «l'oncle» (uncle), «le cousin / la cousine» (cousin)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your aunt's daughter is your…", "choices": ["ma cousine", "mon cousin", "ma tante"], "answer": "ma cousine", "right": "Yes! A female cousin is «la cousine» → «ma cousine». Unlike English, French shows the gender.", "wrong": "Your aunt's daughter is a female cousin: «ma cousine». «mon cousin» is a male cousin."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In families you'll constantly hear «tata» (aunty) and «tonton» (uncle) — especially from children!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Grandparents: «la grand-mère», «le grand-père» — literally 'big mother', 'big father'. Familiar names: «mamie» / «papi»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What do you think «les grands-parents» means?", "choices": ["grandparents", "great parents", "tall parents"], "answer": "grandparents", "right": "Yes! «grand» + «parents» = grandparents. Easy!", "wrong": "«les grands-parents» = grandparents, built just like «grand-mère» and «grand-père»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now flip it: grandchildren are 'little': «le petit-fils» (grandson), «la petite-fille» (granddaughter)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«ma petite-fille» (with a hyphen) means…", "choices": ["my granddaughter", "my little girl", "my niece"], "answer": "my granddaughter", "right": "Yes! With the hyphen, «petite-fille» is one word: granddaughter. Without it, «ma petite fille» = my little girl.", "wrong": "The hyphen makes «petite-fille» one word: granddaughter. Without it, «ma petite fille» means my little girl."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Your siblings' children: «le neveu» (nephew), «la nièce» (niece)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La fille de ma sœur s'appelle Sarah, c'est ma nièce.", "en": "My sister's daughter is called Sarah, she's my niece.", "note": "«c'est» introduces who someone is."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your brother's son is…", "choices": ["mon neveu", "ma nièce", "mon cousin"], "answer": "mon neveu", "right": "Bravo ! Your sibling's son = «mon neveu».", "wrong": "Your brother's or sister's son = nephew = «mon neveu». «ma nièce» is a niece; a cousin is your aunt's or uncle's child."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'My cousins live in Paris'?", "choices": ["Mes cousins habitent à Paris.", "Mon cousins habitent à Paris.", "Ma cousins habitent à Paris."], "answer": "Mes cousins habitent à Paris.", "right": "Parfait ! Plural 'my' is «mes», for both genders.", "wrong": "For plural nouns, 'my' = «mes»: «mes cousins», «mes tantes»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Mon grand-père s'appelle Daniel.»", "fr": "Mon grand-père s'appelle Daniel."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ma tante, mon oncle, mon cousin / ma cousine, ma grand-mère, mon grand-père, mon petit-fils, ma petite-fille, mon neveu, ma nièce». Plural: «mes»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: my grandmother", "answer": "ma grand-mère", "accept": ["ma grand-mère", "ma grand mère", "ma grand-mere", "ma grand mere"], "hint": "«mère» is feminine, so 'my' = ?", "right": "Excellent ! Feminine → «ma», and don't forget the hyphen.", "wrong": "«grand-mère» is feminine, so 'my' = «ma»: «ma grand-mère»."}]}, "7-12": {"stageId": "7-12", "title": "The rainbow song", "goal": "By the end you can sing a short French song and name the colours of the rainbow.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's sing! Songs are a brilliant way to remember colours. Here's the first line of «la chanson de l'arc-en-ciel» (the rainbow song)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Rouge et orange et jaune et vert,", "en": "Red and orange and yellow and green,"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«ciel» means sky. What does «arc-en-ciel» literally mean?", "choices": ["arch in the sky", "rain in the sky", "colours of the sky"], "answer": "arch in the sky", "right": "Yes! A rainbow is an 'arch in the sky' — beautiful, isn't it?", "wrong": "«arc» = arch, «en» = in, «ciel» = sky → 'arch in the sky'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Violet, rose et bleu.", "en": "Purple, pink and blue."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which colour in the song is pink?", "choices": ["rose", "rouge", "violet"], "answer": "rose", "right": "Yes! «rose» = pink, like the flower.", "wrong": "«rose» = pink. «rouge» is red and «violet» is purple."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The chorus: «Je connais les couleurs» = I know the colours. «connaître» = to know (to be familiar with)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Toutes les couleurs» mean?", "choices": ["All the colours", "Some colours", "Two colours"], "answer": "All the colours", "right": "Yes! «toutes» = all — think of English 'total'.", "wrong": "«toutes» = all (think 'total'). «Toutes les couleurs» = All the colours."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Celles de l'arc-en-ciel» = 'Those of the rainbow'. It's «celles» (feminine plural) because «couleur» is feminine: «la couleur»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«couleur» is feminine. So how do you say 'a red colour'?", "choices": ["un couleur rouge", "une couleur rouge", "une rouge couleur"], "answer": "une couleur rouge", "right": "Bravo ! Feminine → «une», and the colour goes after the noun.", "wrong": "«couleur» is feminine, so it takes «une», and the colour goes after: «une couleur rouge»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Sing or say the chorus: «Je connais les couleurs, toutes les couleurs, celles de l'arc-en-ciel.»", "fr": "Je connais les couleurs, toutes les couleurs, celles de l'arc-en-ciel."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: the rainbow is «l'arc-en-ciel», and its colours are «rouge, orange, jaune, vert, violet, rose, bleu». «Je connais les couleurs» = I know the colours."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I know the colours.", "answer": "Je connais les couleurs.", "accept": ["je connais les couleurs", "je connais les couleurs."], "hint": "Starts with «Je connais…»", "right": "Magnifique ! You know the colours — and a French song!", "wrong": "It's the chorus line: «Je connais les couleurs.»"}]}, "8-1": {"stageId": "8-1", "title": "Faire, jouer & how often you do things", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate «faire» and «jouer», talk about your hobbies and say how often you do them.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Bonjour» ! Today we meet two super useful verbs for hobbies: «faire» (to do / to make) and «jouer» (to play). We'll also learn to say 'every week' or 'twice a month'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je fais un gâteau.» You know «je» = I and «un gâteau» = a cake. What do you think «fais» means here?", "choices": ["eat", "make", "buy"], "answer": "make", "right": "Yes! «faire» means both 'to do' and 'to make', so «je fais un gâteau» = I'm making a cake.", "wrong": "«fais» comes from «faire», which means 'to do' AND 'to make'. So «je fais un gâteau» = I'm making a cake."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«faire» is irregular, so we learn it by heart: «je fais», «tu fais», «il/elle/on fait», «nous faisons», «vous faites», «ils/elles font»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Good news: «fais», «fais» and «fait» all sound the same: 'feh'. Watch out: it's «vous faites», never 'vous faisez', a very common learner mistake!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Vous ___ vos devoirs.» (You do your homework.)", "choices": ["faisez", "faites", "font"], "answer": "faites", "right": "Bravo! «vous faites» is irregular, just like «vous êtes» (you are).", "wrong": "With «vous», «faire» becomes «faites» (not 'faisez'). «font» is for «ils/elles»: «ils font»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For an activity you practise regularly, use «faire de». It becomes «du» before a masculine word, «de la» before a feminine word and «de l'» before a vowel."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je fais du vélo et ma sœur fait de la natation.", "en": "I ride my bike and my sister swims.", "note": "«le vélo» → «du vélo», «la natation» → «de la natation»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«l'équitation» (horse riding) starts with a vowel. How do you say 'We go horse riding'?", "choices": ["Nous faisons du équitation.", "Nous faisons de la équitation.", "Nous faisons de l'équitation."], "answer": "Nous faisons de l'équitation.", "right": "Exactly! Before a vowel, «du» and «de la» both become «de l'».", "wrong": "French avoids two vowels bumping into each other, so before a vowel we use «de l'»: «de l'équitation»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now «jouer» (to play), a regular verb: «je joue», «tu joues», «il joue», «nous jouons», «vous jouez», «ils jouent». Instruments take «jouer de», sports and games take «jouer à» («à + le» = «au», «à + les» = «aux»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je joue de la guitare et mon frère joue au football.", "en": "I play the guitar and my brother plays football."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«les cartes» = cards. Which sentence means 'I play cards'?", "choices": ["Je joue des cartes.", "Je joue aux cartes.", "Je joue à les cartes."], "answer": "Je joue aux cartes.", "right": "Parfait ! Cards are a game, so «jouer à», and «à + les» always contracts to «aux».", "wrong": "Games and sports take «jouer à», and «à + les» must contract to «aux»: «je joue aux cartes». «jouer de» is only for instruments."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To say how often: «tous les jours» (every day), «tous les mercredis» (every Wednesday), «toutes les semaines» (every week). For 'once' or 'twice', say «une fois par semaine», «deux fois par mois»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Why do we say «toutes les semaines» but «tous les jours»?", "choices": ["Because «semaine» is feminine", "Because «semaine» is longer", "No reason, both forms are fine"], "answer": "Because «semaine» is feminine", "right": "Yes! «tout» agrees with the noun: «tous» for masculine plural («les jours»), «toutes» for feminine plural («les semaines»).", "wrong": "«tout» agrees with its noun: «le jour» is masculine → «tous les jours»; «la semaine» is feminine → «toutes les semaines»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «faire de» + activity («du», «de la», «de l'»), «jouer de» + instrument, «jouer à» + sport or game («au», «aux»). Add «tous les…» or «une fois par…» to say how often!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I swim every Tuesday.", "answer": "Je fais de la natation tous les mardis.", "accept": ["je fais de la natation tous les mardis", "je fais de la natation tous les mardis."], "hint": "Starts with «Je fais de la…» and ends with «tous les…»", "right": "Super ! «faire de la natation» + «tous les mardis» (days are masculine, so «tous»).", "wrong": "Swimming is «la natation», so «faire de la natation». 'Every Tuesday' = «tous les mardis» (no capital letter for days in French)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je fais du vélo tous les mercredis.» Then try it with your own hobby!", "fr": "Je fais du vélo tous les mercredis."}]}, "8-2": {"stageId": "8-2", "title": "Everyday expressions with «faire»", "goal": "By the end you can use common «faire» expressions for chores, weather, feelings and daily life.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French uses «faire» in lots of everyday expressions where English uses other verbs. The trick: don't translate word by word, learn them as little blocks!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«la vaisselle» means 'the dishes'. So what do you think «faire la vaisselle» means?", "choices": ["to buy dishes", "to wash up", "to break dishes"], "answer": "to wash up", "right": "Yes! 'Doing the dishes' = washing up. Another chore: «faire la cuisine» = to cook.", "wrong": "«faire la vaisselle» = 'to do the dishes', in other words, to wash up."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je fais la cuisine et tu fais la vaisselle.", "en": "I cook and you wash up.", "note": "A fair deal!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "The weather also uses «faire»: «il fait beau» (it's nice/sunny), «il fait chaud» (it's hot), «il fait froid» (it's cold), «il fait mauvais» (the weather's bad)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "It's January in Paris and it's 0°C. What do you say?", "choices": ["Il fait chaud.", "Il fait froid.", "Il est froid."], "answer": "Il fait froid.", "right": "Exactly! For the weather, French uses «il fait» + adjective.", "wrong": "For the weather, use «il fait» + adjective: «il fait froid». «Il est froid» would describe a person or a thing (he/it is cold), and «chaud» means hot."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«faire la grasse matinée» literally means 'to do the fat morning'. It means to sleep in, a favourite French Sunday activity!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "On Sunday, I sleep until noon. Which expression fits?", "choices": ["Je fais la queue.", "Je fais la grasse matinée.", "Je fais la tête."], "answer": "Je fais la grasse matinée.", "right": "Bravo! «faire la grasse matinée» = to sleep in / have a lie-in.", "wrong": "Sleeping in is «faire la grasse matinée». «faire la queue» = to queue, and «faire la tête» = to sulk."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some expressions about feelings and behaviour: «faire peur» (to scare), «faire la tête» (to sulk), «faire la grimace» (to make a face), «faire attention» (to be careful)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Fais attention sur la route !", "en": "Be careful on the road!", "note": "To give an order to a friend, just drop «tu»: «Fais…». And «Ne fais pas la tête !» = Don't sulk!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend is crossing a busy street without looking. You shout:", "choices": ["Fais attention !", "Fais la fête !", "Fais peur !"], "answer": "Fais attention !", "right": "Yes! «faire attention» = to be careful.", "wrong": "'Be careful!' is «Fais attention !». «faire la fête» = to party, and «faire peur» = to scare."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it with feeling, as if you're warning a friend: «Fais attention !»", "fr": "Fais attention !"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two more for daily life: «faire les courses» (to do the shopping) and «faire la queue» (to queue), like at the bakery on a Sunday morning!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Nous ___ les courses le samedi.» (We do the shopping on Saturdays.)", "choices": ["faisons", "faites", "font"], "answer": "faisons", "right": "Parfait ! «nous» → «faisons».", "wrong": "With «nous», «faire» becomes «faisons». «vous faites», «ils font»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Culture: «faire le pont» = 'to make the bridge'. If a public holiday falls on a Thursday, many French people take Friday off too, to 'bridge' to the weekend!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «faire la vaisselle», «faire la cuisine», «faire les courses», «faire la grasse matinée», «faire attention», and for the weather «il fait beau / froid»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I wash up and you cook.", "answer": "Je fais la vaisselle et tu fais la cuisine.", "accept": ["je fais la vaisselle et tu fais la cuisine", "je fais la vaisselle et tu fais la cuisine.", "je fais la vaisselle, et tu fais la cuisine"], "hint": "Use «faire» twice: «Je fais la v…» and «tu fais la c…»", "right": "Excellent ! Two «faire» expressions in one sentence.", "wrong": "Washing up = «faire la vaisselle», cooking = «faire la cuisine»: «Je fais la vaisselle et tu fais la cuisine.»"}]}, "8-3": {"stageId": "8-3", "title": "Percussion instruments", "goal": "By the end you can name percussion instruments and say which one you play.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's make some noise! Today: percussion instruments, «les instruments à percussion»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Many names look like English: «un xylophone», «un triangle», «des maracas». What do you think «une batterie» is in music?", "choices": ["a battery", "a drum kit", "a guitar"], "answer": "a drum kit", "right": "Yes! In music, «une batterie» is a drum kit. (Outside music it can also mean a battery!)", "wrong": "In music, «une batterie» is a drum kit, 'the drums'. It can mean a battery too, but not in a band!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "The family: «un tambour» (a drum), «une batterie» (drums), «une cymbale» (a cymbal), «un xylophone», «un triangle», «des maracas», «des castagnettes» (castanets), «des timbales» (kettledrums)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Always learn a noun with «un» or «une»: the gender changes the little word after «jouer»! And remember: «un tambour» is ONE drum, «une batterie» is the whole drum kit."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the gender of «batterie»?", "choices": ["masculine (un)", "feminine (une)"], "answer": "feminine (une)", "right": "Right: «une batterie». Many nouns ending in -ie are feminine.", "wrong": "It's «une batterie», feminine. Many nouns ending in -ie are feminine."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To say you play an instrument: «jouer» + «du» (masculine), «de la» (feminine), «de l'» (vowel or silent h) or «des» (plural)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je joue du xylophone et mon frère joue de la batterie.", "en": "I play the xylophone and my brother plays the drums."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«un tambour» is masculine. Complete: «Il joue ___ tambour.»", "choices": ["du", "de la", "au"], "answer": "du", "right": "Bravo! Masculine instrument → «du» (de + le).", "wrong": "Masculine instrument → «du» (de + le). «au» is for sports and games («jouer au foot»), never for instruments."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«une cymbale» is feminine. Complete: «Elle joue ___ cymbale.»", "choices": ["du", "de la", "des"], "answer": "de la", "right": "Yes! Feminine → «de la».", "wrong": "Feminine instrument → «de la»: «elle joue de la cymbale». «du» is masculine, «des» is plural."}, {"t": "say", "text": "You can also use «faire de» + instrument: it means you practise it regularly, like a hobby or lessons. That's why nobody says «faire du triangle»: we say «jouer du triangle»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your sister takes drum lessons every week. Which sentence sounds the most natural?", "choices": ["Elle fait de la batterie.", "Elle fait du batterie.", "Elle joue à la batterie."], "answer": "Elle fait de la batterie.", "right": "Exactly! Regular practice → «faire de», and «batterie» is feminine → «de la».", "wrong": "Regular practice → «faire de», and «batterie» is feminine → «de la batterie». «jouer à» is for sports, not instruments."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les maracas sont souvent associées à la musique mexicaine.", "en": "Maracas are often associated with Mexican music.", "note": "«maracas» is feminine in French, hence «associées»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «un tambour», «une batterie», «une cymbale», «un xylophone», «un triangle», «des maracas». «jouer du / de la / de l' / des» + instrument, and «faire de» for regular practice."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I play the drums.", "answer": "Je joue de la batterie.", "accept": ["je joue de la batterie", "je joue de la batterie.", "je fais de la batterie", "je fais de la batterie."], "hint": "Drums = «une batterie» (feminine)", "right": "Super ! «batterie» is feminine, so «de la».", "wrong": "The drums = «une batterie», feminine, so «jouer de la»: «Je joue de la batterie.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with rhythm: «Je joue du triangle !»", "fr": "Je joue du triangle !"}]}, "8-4": {"stageId": "8-4", "title": "Wind instruments", "goal": "By the end you can name wind instruments and say which one you play with «du», «de la» or «de l'».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Take a deep breath: today we learn wind instruments, «les instruments à vent» («le vent» = the wind)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«une flûte traversière» or «une flûte à bec»? «traverser» means 'to go across'. Which one do you hold sideways?", "choices": ["la flûte traversière", "la flûte à bec"], "answer": "la flûte traversière", "right": "Yes! It goes across your face: that's the flute. «une flûte à bec» ('beak flute') is a recorder, the one kids learn at school.", "wrong": "«traversière» = across: you hold the flute sideways. «une flûte à bec» ('beak flute') is the recorder you blow straight into."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Feminine: «une trompette», «une clarinette», «une flûte». Masculine: «un saxophone», «un trombone», «un hautbois» (oboe), «un accordéon», «un harmonica»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Fun fact: «un trombone» is also a paper clip in French! And «hautbois» sounds 'oh-BWAH': it literally means 'high wood'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Use «du» for masculine and «de la» for feminine. «Je joue ___ trompette.»", "choices": ["du", "de la", "de l'"], "answer": "de la", "right": "Bravo! «une trompette» is feminine → «de la».", "wrong": "«une trompette» is feminine, so «de la trompette». «du» is for masculine nouns, «de l'» for nouns starting with a vowel."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Thomas joue de la clarinette dans l'orchestre.", "en": "Thomas plays the clarinet in the orchestra."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«un accordéon» starts with a vowel. «Mon grand-père joue ___ accordéon.»", "choices": ["du", "de la", "de l'"], "answer": "de l'", "right": "Exactly! Before a vowel → «de l'accordéon».", "wrong": "Before a vowel, «du» and «de la» become «de l'»: «de l'accordéon»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To ask a friend which instrument they play: «De quel instrument joues-tu ?» To be polite, say «De quel instrument jouez-vous ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mon instrument à vent préféré est le saxophone.", "en": "My favourite wind instrument is the saxophone.", "note": "«préféré» (favourite) comes after the noun."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I play the saxophone'?", "choices": ["Je joue au saxophone.", "Je joue du saxophone.", "Je joue le saxophone."], "answer": "Je joue du saxophone.", "right": "Parfait ! Instrument → «jouer de», and «saxophone» is masculine → «du».", "wrong": "Instruments always take «de»: «jouer du saxophone». «jouer au» is for sports, and «jouer le saxophone» isn't French."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English speakers often say 'je joue le piano'. Avoid it! Memory trick: Instruments → «de», Sports → «à»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «une trompette», «une clarinette», «une flûte», «un saxophone», «un trombone», «un accordéon». «jouer du» (masc.), «de la» (fem.), «de l'» (vowel)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I play the trumpet.", "answer": "Je joue de la trompette.", "accept": ["je joue de la trompette", "je joue de la trompette.", "je fais de la trompette", "je fais de la trompette."], "hint": "«trompette» is feminine", "right": "Super ! Feminine instrument → «de la».", "wrong": "«une trompette» is feminine, so: «Je joue de la trompette.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Ask a friend which instrument they play: «De quel instrument joues-tu ?»", "fr": "De quel instrument joues-tu ?"}]}, "8-5": {"stageId": "8-5", "title": "String instruments", "goal": "By the end you can name string instruments and talk about the ones you play.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today: «les instruments à cordes», string instruments («une corde» = a string or a rope)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«un violoncelle» is the big cousin of the «violon». Which instrument do you think it is?", "choices": ["a cello", "a harp", "a double bass"], "answer": "a cello", "right": "Yes! «violoncelle» = violoncello, which English shortened to 'cello'.", "wrong": "«violoncelle» = violoncello, shortened to 'cello' in English. The harp is «la harpe» and the double bass is «la contrebasse»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Feminine: «une guitare», «une harpe», «une contrebasse» (double bass). Masculine: «un violon», «un alto» (viola), «un violoncelle», «un piano»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False friend: «un alto» is a viola, not an alto voice. And we say «la harpe», never «l'harpe»: this «h» blocks the apostrophe."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«un piano» is masculine. «Je joue ___ piano.»", "choices": ["du", "de la", "de l'"], "answer": "du", "right": "Bravo! Masculine → «du piano».", "wrong": "«un piano» is masculine, so «du piano». «de la» is for feminine nouns."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je joue de la guitare et du piano.", "en": "I play the guitar and the piano.", "note": "Repeat «de» before each instrument."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Elle joue ___ violon et ___ harpe.»", "choices": ["du / de la", "de la / du", "du / de l'"], "answer": "du / de la", "right": "Exactly! «le violon» → «du», «la harpe» → «de la».", "wrong": "«le violon» is masculine → «du violon»; «la harpe» is feminine and keeps «la» → «de la harpe»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two useful extras: «un archet» (a bow) and «une partition» (sheet music, a score). And remember, «faire de» + instrument = you practise it regularly."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Alice fait du violon depuis l'âge de 8 ans.", "en": "Alice has been playing the violin since she was 8.", "note": "«depuis» + present tense = 'has been …ing since'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What do you use to play the violin?", "choices": ["un archet", "une partition", "un piano"], "answer": "un archet", "right": "Yes! «un archet» is the bow. «une partition» is the music you read.", "wrong": "The bow is «un archet». «une partition» is sheet music: you read it, you don't play with it!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which of these words is feminine?", "choices": ["violoncelle", "contrebasse", "alto"], "answer": "contrebasse", "right": "Well spotted: «la contrebasse». Careful though: «le violoncelle» also ends in -e but is masculine!", "wrong": "It's «la contrebasse». «le violoncelle» ends in -e but is masculine, so always learn the article with the noun."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «une guitare», «une harpe», «une contrebasse», «un violon», «un alto», «un violoncelle», «un piano». «jouer du / de la» + instrument."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I play the cello.", "answer": "Je joue du violoncelle.", "accept": ["je joue du violoncelle", "je joue du violoncelle.", "je fais du violoncelle", "je fais du violoncelle."], "hint": "«violoncelle» is masculine", "right": "Parfait ! Masculine → «du violoncelle».", "wrong": "«le violoncelle» is masculine, so: «Je joue du violoncelle.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je joue de la guitare et du piano.»", "fr": "Je joue de la guitare et du piano."}]}, "8-6": {"stageId": "8-6", "title": "Sports (1): «jouer à» or «faire de»", "goal": "By the end you can name ball sports and say you play them with «jouer au» or «faire du».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Sports time! Most ball sports in French come from English and are masculine: «le football», «le basket», «le tennis», «le golf», «le rugby»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«la table» = the table. What do you think «le tennis de table» is?", "choices": ["table tennis", "a tennis court", "table football"], "answer": "table tennis", "right": "Yes! «le tennis de table» = table tennis, also called «le ping-pong».", "wrong": "«le tennis de table» = 'tennis of table': table tennis, also called «le ping-pong»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More: «le volley» (volleyball), «le baseball», «le badminton», «le hockey». Many have a short form: «le foot», «le basket», «le volley»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«la glace» means ice. So «le hockey sur glace» is…", "choices": ["field hockey", "ice hockey", "ice skating"], "answer": "ice hockey", "right": "Exactly! «sur glace» = on ice.", "wrong": "«sur glace» = on ice, so «le hockey sur glace» = ice hockey. Ice skating is «le patinage»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In France, «le football» means soccer! American football is «le football américain». Most people just say «le foot»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two ways to talk about these sports: «jouer à» (to play) or «faire de» (to do regularly). Contractions: «à + le» = «au» and «de + le» = «du»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je joue au football tous les mercredis. = Je fais du football tous les mercredis.", "en": "I play football every Wednesday."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Il joue ___ volley dans son école.»", "choices": ["au", "du", "à le"], "answer": "au", "right": "Bravo! «jouer à» + «le volley» → «au volley».", "wrong": "With «jouer», use «à», and «à + le» must contract to «au»: «il joue au volley». «du» goes with «faire»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Nous faisons ___ golf le samedi.»", "choices": ["au", "du", "de le"], "answer": "du", "right": "Yes! «faire de» + «le golf» → «du golf».", "wrong": "With «faire», use «de», and «de + le» must contract to «du»: «nous faisons du golf»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Careful: some sports work only with «faire»: «faire du ski», «faire de la natation» (swimming), «faire de la danse». Just like in English, you don't 'play' swimming!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence is correct?", "choices": ["Je joue à la natation.", "Je fais de la natation.", "Je joue de la natation."], "answer": "Je fais de la natation.", "right": "Parfait ! Swimming only works with «faire de», and «natation» is feminine → «de la».", "wrong": "You can't «jouer» swimming: use «faire de la natation». «jouer de» is for instruments."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: for games with a ball, both «jouer à» and «faire de» work. For ski, swimming or dance, only «faire de» works."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «jouer au foot / au tennis / au basket» or «faire du foot / du tennis / du basket». But only «faire du ski» and «faire de la natation»!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I play tennis every Saturday.", "answer": "Je joue au tennis tous les samedis.", "accept": ["je joue au tennis tous les samedis", "je joue au tennis tous les samedis.", "je fais du tennis tous les samedis", "je fais du tennis tous les samedis."], "hint": "«Je joue au…» + «tous les…»", "right": "Excellent ! «jouer au tennis» + «tous les samedis».", "wrong": "«jouer à» + «le tennis» → «au tennis», and 'every Saturday' = «tous les samedis»: «Je joue au tennis tous les samedis.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je joue au basket et je fais du ski.»", "fr": "Je joue au basket et je fais du ski."}]}, "8-7": {"stageId": "8-7", "title": "Sports (2): «faire du, de la, de l'»", "goal": "By the end you can name individual sports and say you do them with «faire du / de la / de l'».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "More sports today! For these we use «faire de»: «du» (masculine), «de la» (feminine), «de l'» (vowel)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«courir» = to run and «le pied» = the foot. What do you think «la course à pied» is?", "choices": ["running", "football", "hiking"], "answer": "running", "right": "Yes! 'The race on foot' = running.", "wrong": "«la course à pied» = 'the race on foot', so running. Hiking is «la randonnée»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Masculine sports: «le ski», «le judo», «le karaté», «le cyclisme» (cycling), «le patinage» (ice skating), «le patin à roulettes» (roller skating)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je fais du karaté et du cyclisme.", "en": "I do karate and cycling."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le ski» → «Nous faisons ___ ski tous les hivers.»", "choices": ["du", "de la", "de l'"], "answer": "du", "right": "Bravo! «le ski» is masculine → «du ski».", "wrong": "«le ski» is masculine, and «de + le» = «du»: «nous faisons du ski»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Feminine sports: «la natation» (swimming), «la boxe», «la randonnée» (hiking), «la gymnastique», «la plongée sous-marine» (scuba diving), «la planche à voile» (windsurfing)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il fait de la gymnastique le dimanche matin.", "en": "He does gymnastics on Sunday mornings."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«la randonnée» → «Je fais ___ randonnée.»", "choices": ["du", "de la", "de l'"], "answer": "de la", "right": "Yes! Feminine → «de la randonnée».", "wrong": "«la randonnée» is feminine, so «de la randonnée»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two sports start with a vowel: «l'équitation» (horse riding) and «l'escrime» (fencing). So we say «de l'»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I do fencing'?", "choices": ["Je fais du escrime.", "Je fais de l'escrime.", "Je fais de la escrime."], "answer": "Je fais de l'escrime.", "right": "Parfait ! Before a vowel → «de l'».", "wrong": "«escrime» starts with a vowel, so «de l'escrime»: no «du» or «de la» before a vowel."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False friend alert: «la natation» = swimming, nothing to do with 'nation'! And the bike itself is «le vélo»: «faire du vélo» = to ride a bike."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je fais de la boxe le jeudi.» What does «le jeudi» mean here?", "choices": ["on Thursdays (every Thursday)", "last Thursday", "only this Thursday"], "answer": "on Thursdays (every Thursday)", "right": "Yes! «le» + day = a regular habit. «jeudi» alone = this Thursday.", "wrong": "«le jeudi» = every Thursday, a habit. Without «le», «jeudi» means just this coming Thursday."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «faire du ski / du judo / du cyclisme», «faire de la natation / de la boxe / de la randonnée», «faire de l'équitation / de l'escrime»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I go horse riding.", "answer": "Je fais de l'équitation.", "accept": ["je fais de l'équitation", "je fais de l'équitation.", "je fais de l’équitation", "je fais de l'equitation", "je fais du cheval"], "hint": "Horse riding = «l'équitation»", "right": "Super ! Vowel → «de l'équitation».", "wrong": "«l'équitation» starts with a vowel, so «de l'»: «Je fais de l'équitation.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Tell me about your week: «Je fais de la natation le samedi.»", "fr": "Je fais de la natation le samedi."}]}, "8-8": {"stageId": "8-8", "title": "Time words (1): units, next & last, early & late", "goal": "By the end you can name units of time, say 'next week / last month' and say if you're early, late or on time.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's talk about time: «une seconde», «une minute», «une heure» (an hour), «un jour», «une semaine», «un mois» (a month), «un an» or «une année» (a year)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "We write «l'heure», not «la heure». Why?", "choices": ["Because the h is silent", "Because «heure» is masculine", "Because it's plural"], "answer": "Because the h is silent", "right": "Yes! The «h» is silent, so the word really starts with a vowel sound: «la» becomes «l'».", "wrong": "«heure» is feminine, but its «h» is silent, so it sounds like a vowel: «la» shortens to «l'»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Bigger units: «une décennie» (a decade) and «un siècle» (a century)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«un an» counts time («j'ai 20 ans»), «une année» stresses the duration («toute l'année», all year). Same pair: «le jour / la journée», so «Bonne journée !» = Have a nice day!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Leaving a shop in the morning, you say:", "choices": ["Bon an !", "Bonne journée !", "Bon siècle !"], "answer": "Bonne journée !", "right": "Parfait ! «Bonne journée !» wishes someone a good whole day.", "wrong": "We say «Bonne journée !» (Have a nice day!) because «la journée» is the day as a duration."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Next and last: «prochain / prochaine» = next, «dernier / dernière» = last. They go after the noun: «la semaine prochaine», «le mois dernier»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je vais en vacances la semaine prochaine.", "en": "I'm going on holiday next week."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'last year'?", "choices": ["l'année dernière", "l'année prochaine", "l'an prochaine"], "answer": "l'année dernière", "right": "Bravo! «dernière» (feminine, to agree with «année») goes after the noun.", "wrong": "'Last' = «dernier / dernière», after the noun: «l'année dernière». «prochaine» means next."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«un mois» is masculine. 'Next month' is «le mois ___».", "choices": ["prochain", "prochaine", "dernière"], "answer": "prochain", "right": "Yes! Masculine noun → «prochain».", "wrong": "«mois» is masculine, so «prochain» (no -e): «le mois prochain». «prochaine» is for feminine nouns like «la semaine»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Being punctual: «tôt» = early, «tard» = late, «être en retard» = to be late (for something), «être à l'heure» = to be on time."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis en retard !", "en": "I'm late!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your train leaves at 9:00 and you arrive at 9:00 exactly. You are…", "choices": ["en retard", "à l'heure", "tard"], "answer": "à l'heure", "right": "Exactly! «être à l'heure» = to be on time.", "wrong": "Arriving exactly on time = «être à l'heure». «en retard» means late."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«tard» vs «en retard»: «Il est tard» = it's late (in the day); «Je suis en retard» = I'm late (for an appointment). English uses 'late' for both!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «une heure», «un jour / une journée», «un an / une année», «prochain» (next), «dernier» (last), «tôt», «tard», «en retard», «à l'heure»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I am on time.", "answer": "Je suis à l'heure.", "accept": ["je suis à l'heure", "je suis à l'heure.", "je suis à l’heure", "je suis a l'heure"], "hint": "«Je suis à l'…»", "right": "Super ! «être à l'heure» = to be on time.", "wrong": "'To be on time' = «être à l'heure»: «Je suis à l'heure.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je vais en vacances le mois prochain.»", "fr": "Je vais en vacances le mois prochain."}]}, "8-9": {"stageId": "8-9", "title": "Time words (2): yesterday, today, tomorrow", "goal": "By the end you can say yesterday, today, tomorrow, this morning/afternoon/evening, and 'in two weeks' or 'two weeks ago'.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Three key words: «hier» (yesterday), «aujourd'hui» (today), «demain» (tomorrow)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«aujourd'hui» is one word with an apostrophe inside: 'oh-zhoor-DWEE'. «hier» sounds like 'yair'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «demain» mean?", "choices": ["yesterday", "today", "tomorrow"], "answer": "tomorrow", "right": "Yes! «demain» = tomorrow.", "wrong": "«demain» = tomorrow. «hier» = yesterday and «aujourd'hui» = today."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Add a part of the day after it: «demain matin» (tomorrow morning), «demain après-midi» (tomorrow afternoon), «hier soir» (yesterday evening)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'yesterday evening'?", "choices": ["demain soir", "hier soir", "ce soir"], "answer": "hier soir", "right": "Parfait ! «hier» + «soir».", "wrong": "Yesterday = «hier», evening = «soir»: «hier soir». «demain soir» = tomorrow evening."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For 'this': «ce matin», «ce soir», but «cet après-midi». Before a masculine word starting with a vowel, «ce» becomes «cet»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «___ après-midi, je vais chez mon oncle.»", "choices": ["Ce", "Cet", "Cette"], "answer": "Cet", "right": "Bravo! «après-midi» starts with a vowel, so «cet».", "wrong": "«après-midi» is masculine and starts with a vowel, so «ce» becomes «cet»: «cet après-midi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "One step further: «après-demain» (the day after tomorrow) and «avant-hier» (the day before yesterday). «après» = after, «avant» = before."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Today is Wednesday. «avant-hier» was…", "choices": ["Monday", "Tuesday", "Friday"], "answer": "Monday", "right": "Yes! Yesterday was Tuesday, so the day before yesterday was Monday.", "wrong": "«avant-hier» = the day before yesterday. Yesterday was Tuesday, so «avant-hier» was Monday."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«dans» + time = in (future): «dans deux semaines». «il y a» + time = ago: «il y a deux semaines»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je viens dans deux semaines.", "en": "I'm coming in two weeks."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis venu il y a deux semaines.", "en": "I came two weeks ago.", "note": "This is the past tense; you'll learn it later."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Three days ago' is…", "choices": ["dans trois jours", "il y a trois jours", "trois jours avant"], "answer": "il y a trois jours", "right": "Exactly! «il y a» + time = ago. Note that it comes BEFORE the time, unlike English.", "wrong": "'Ago' = «il y a», placed before the time: «il y a trois jours». «dans trois jours» = in three days (future)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «hier», «aujourd'hui», «demain», «ce matin», «cet après-midi», «ce soir», «avant-hier», «après-demain», «dans…» (in) and «il y a…» (ago)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: tomorrow afternoon", "answer": "demain après-midi", "accept": ["demain après-midi", "demain apres-midi", "demain après midi", "demain apres midi"], "hint": "«demain» + «après-…»", "right": "Super ! «demain» + «après-midi».", "wrong": "Tomorrow = «demain», afternoon = «après-midi»: «demain après-midi»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Aujourd'hui, je fais du sport et demain, je joue de la guitare.»", "fr": "Aujourd'hui, je fais du sport et demain, je joue de la guitare."}]}, "9-1": {"stageId": "9-1", "title": "Going places: «aller» + to the", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate «aller» and say where you are going (to the shop, to a friend's, to a country, to a town).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! Today's verb is «aller», which means 'to go'. It's a rebel verb with no regular pattern, so let's learn it like a little song: «je vais, tu vas, il va, elle va, on va»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Here's the plural: «nous allons, vous allez, ils vont, elles vont». Notice that only «nous» and «vous» start with «all-», like the infinitive «aller»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which form goes with «nous»?", "choices": ["allons", "allez", "vont"], "answer": "allons", "right": "Exactly! «nous» forms nearly always end in «-ons», so it's «nous allons».", "wrong": "Here's the pattern: «nous» → «-ons» (nous allons). «allez» goes with «vous», and «vont» goes with «ils/elles»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For 'to the' after «aller», French uses «à», and it changes with the noun. With a feminine noun it's «à la». With a masculine noun, «à + le» merges into «au». («à le» never exists.)"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je vais au supermarché.", "en": "I am going to the supermarket.", "note": "le supermarché → à + le = au"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le cinéma» is masculine. How do you say 'I'm going to the cinema'?", "choices": ["Je vais à le cinéma.", "Je vais au cinéma.", "Je vais à la cinéma."], "answer": "Je vais au cinéma.", "right": "Bravo! «à + le» always merges into «au».", "wrong": "French never says «à le». It becomes «au». And «cinéma» is masculine, so «à la» doesn't work either: «Je vais au cinéma»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Three more forms: «à l'» before a vowel or h (à l'hôtel), «aux» before a plural (aux toilettes), and «chez» for someone's home or a professional (chez ma sœur, chez le dentiste)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Have a guess: 'I'm going to my sister's house' is…", "choices": ["Je vais à ma sœur.", "Je vais chez ma sœur.", "Je vais au sœur."], "answer": "Je vais chez ma sœur.", "right": "Perfect! «chez» + a person means 'to/at that person's place'. You don't need a word for 'house'.", "wrong": "When you go to a PERSON (their home, or a doctor or dentist), French uses «chez»: «chez ma sœur», «chez le docteur»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Countries work the same way. Masculine countries take «au» (au Japon, au Portugal). Feminine countries, and masculine ones starting with a vowel, take «en» (en France, en Italie, en Irak). Plural countries take «aux» (aux États-Unis)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le Canada» is masculine. 'I'm going to Canada' is…", "choices": ["Je vais en Canada.", "Je vais au Canada.", "Je vais à Canada."], "answer": "Je vais au Canada.", "right": "Yes! A masculine country starting with a consonant takes «au».", "wrong": "«en» is for feminine countries (or ones starting with a vowel). «le Canada» is masculine and starts with C, so it's «au Canada»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: most countries ending in «-e» are feminine (la France, l'Italie, la Chine), so they take «en». Towns are easier: always «à» (à Paris, à Londres)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je vais à Rome, en Italie.", "en": "I am going to Rome, in Italy.", "note": "town → à, feminine country → en"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We are going to the hospital. (hôpital starts with h)", "answer": "Nous allons à l'hôpital.", "accept": ["nous allons à l'hôpital", "nous allons a l'hopital", "nous allons à l hôpital", "nous allons a l hopital", "nous allons à l'hopital"], "hint": "«nous» + aller, then «à l'» before the h", "right": "Excellent! «nous allons», plus «à l'» because «hôpital» starts with a silent h.", "wrong": "With «nous», «aller» becomes «allons». Before a vowel or silent h, use «à l'»: «Nous allons à l'hôpital»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «je vais, tu vas, il va, nous allons, vous allez, ils vont». For 'to the': «à la», «au», «à l'», «aux», or «chez» + a person. Countries take «en», «au» or «aux», and towns take «à»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'My sister goes to the library and my children go to the cinema.'", "fr": "Ma sœur va à la bibliothèque et mes enfants vont au cinéma."}]}, "9-2": {"stageId": "9-2", "title": "«en» or «dans»? Two ways to say 'in'", "goal": "By the end you know when to use «en» and when to use «dans».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«en» and «dans» can both mean 'in', but you can't swap them. Let's work out when each one is used."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'habite en France, en Provence.", "en": "I live in France, in Provence.", "note": "«en» + feminine countries and regions"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«en» also goes with transport (en train, en avion), languages (en français), materials (en or, 'made of gold'), states of mind or body (en colère, en forme) and years or months (en 2016, en juin)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'by plane'?", "choices": ["dans avion", "en avion", "à l'avion"], "answer": "en avion", "right": "Yes! For means of transport French uses «en»: en avion, en train, en bus.", "wrong": "For a means of transport, use «en» with no article: «en avion», «en train»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«dans» is for a specific place, usually with «le/la/un/une»: «dans la maison», «dans une banlieue tranquille». Compare that with the general «J'habite en banlieue» ('I live in the suburbs')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I live in a quiet suburb' is…", "choices": ["J'habite en une banlieue tranquille.", "J'habite dans une banlieue tranquille."], "answer": "J'habite dans une banlieue tranquille.", "right": "Well spotted! «une banlieue tranquille» is a particular suburb with an article, so it takes «dans».", "wrong": "«en» normally comes without an article and is general («en banlieue»). A specific place with «une» takes «dans»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "A time trick that confuses English speakers: «en 20 minutes» means 'it takes 20 minutes'. «dans 20 minutes» means '20 minutes from now'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je vais au bureau dans 20 minutes.", "en": "I am going to the office in 20 minutes (from now)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend texts «J'arrive dans dix minutes». What does it mean?", "choices": ["I'll be there ten minutes from now.", "My journey takes ten minutes."], "answer": "I'll be there ten minutes from now.", "right": "Exactly! «dans» + time = when something will START.", "wrong": "«dans» + time tells you when something happens, counting from now. 'The trip takes ten minutes' would be «en dix minutes»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Last one: decades use «dans les»: «dans les années 80» = 'in the 80s'. A single year uses «en»: «en 1985»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Ce texte est ___ français.", "choices": ["en", "dans"], "answer": "en", "right": "Bravo! Languages take «en»: en français, en anglais.", "wrong": "To say what language something is in, use «en»: «Ce texte est en français»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «en» = countries and regions, transport, languages, materials, states, years, and how long something takes. «dans» = a specific place, 'from now' for time, and decades."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I am travelling by train.", "answer": "Je voyage en train.", "accept": ["je voyage en train"], "hint": "Starts with «Je voyage…»", "right": "Perfect! Transport takes «en».", "wrong": "Means of transport take «en» with no article: «Je voyage en train»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'I live in France, in a small house.'", "fr": "J'habite en France, dans une petite maison."}]}, "9-3": {"stageId": "9-3", "title": "Where is it? On, under, next to…", "goal": "By the end you can say where something or someone is (on, under, behind, near…).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Picture a cat and a table. If the cat is IN a box it's «dans», ON the table it's «sur», and UNDER the table it's «sous»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le chat se cache sous le fauteuil.", "en": "The cat is hiding under the armchair."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "He puts the book ON the shelf: Il place le livre ___ l'étagère.", "choices": ["sous", "sur", "dans"], "answer": "sur", "right": "Yes! «sur» = on top of.", "wrong": "«sous» means under and «dans» means inside. On top of is «sur»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory hook: «sur» is in English 'surface' (on top). «sous» is in 'sous-chef', the chef UNDER the head chef. Your mouth also stays more open for «sur»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More place words: «devant» = in front of, «derrière» = behind, «entre» = between, «contre» = against, «vers» = towards."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The sofa is against the wall: Le sofa est ___ le mur.", "choices": ["contre", "vers", "sous"], "answer": "contre", "right": "Correct! «contre» = against, touching something.", "wrong": "«vers» means towards (movement) and «sous» means under. Against the wall is «contre le mur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The children run towards their mother: Les enfants courent ___ leur mère.", "choices": ["vers", "entre", "sur"], "answer": "vers", "right": "Great! «vers» shows the direction of a movement.", "wrong": "When someone moves in a direction, use «vers» (towards). «entre» means between."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Three of these end in «de»: «à côté de» = next to, «près de» = near, «loin de» = far from. «de» becomes «d'» before a vowel, and «de + le» becomes «du» (à côté du beurre)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La poste est près de la bibliothèque.", "en": "The post office is near the library."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Tom lives far from here' is…", "choices": ["Tom habite loin de ici.", "Tom habite loin d'ici.", "Tom habite loin du ici."], "answer": "Tom habite loin d'ici.", "right": "Perfect! «de» drops its e before the vowel of «ici»: «loin d'ici».", "wrong": "«de» + vowel → «d'». «du» is only for «de + le». So it's «loin d'ici»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «dans, sur, sous, devant, derrière, entre, contre, vers», plus «à côté de, près de, loin de» (watch out for «d'» and «du»)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The cat is under the table.", "answer": "Le chat est sous la table.", "accept": ["le chat est sous la table"], "hint": "«Le chat est…»", "right": "Well done! «sous» = under.", "wrong": "Under = «sous»: «Le chat est sous la table»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'Alice is sitting next to Paul.'", "fr": "Alice est assise à côté de Paul."}]}, "9-4": {"stageId": "9-4", "title": "In or to a town, a country, someone's place", "goal": "By the end you can choose «à», «en», «au», «aux» or «chez» for any place.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Good news: French uses the SAME little word for where you are and where you are going. «J'habite en France» (I live in France) and «Je vais en France» (I go to France)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Cities, towns and villages always take «à»: «à Paris», «à Lyon»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Nicole habite ___ Toulouse.", "choices": ["en", "à", "au"], "answer": "à", "right": "Yes! A town takes «à».", "wrong": "Toulouse is a town, and towns always take «à»: «à Toulouse»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Some town names include «Le», like Le Havre. Then «à + le» merges into «au»: «Je vais au Havre»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«en» goes with feminine countries and regions (en France, en Provence), with continents (en Asie, en Afrique), and with masculine countries starting with a vowel (en Iran)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Elles sont ___ Afrique en ce moment.", "choices": ["en", "au", "à"], "answer": "en", "right": "Correct! Continents always take «en».", "wrong": "Africa is a continent, and continents take «en»: «en Afrique», «en Europe», «en Asie»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Masculine countries take «au» (au Mexique, au Japon). Plural places take «aux» (aux États-Unis, aux Pays-Bas, aux Antilles)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "L'école organise un voyage ___ Japon.", "choices": ["en", "au", "aux"], "answer": "au", "right": "Bravo! «le Japon» is masculine singular, so it takes «au».", "wrong": "«le Japon» is masculine and starts with a consonant, so it's «au». «aux» is only for plurals like «aux États-Unis»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ils partent aux Antilles.", "en": "They are going to the West Indies.", "note": "les Antilles is plural → aux"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«chez» is for a person's home or business: «chez Fred», «chez le coiffeur», «chez moi» (at my place)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Let's meet at my place' is…", "choices": ["Retrouvons-nous chez moi.", "Retrouvons-nous à moi."], "answer": "Retrouvons-nous chez moi.", "right": "Perfect! «chez moi» = at my home.", "wrong": "For someone's home, use «chez» + the person or a pronoun: «chez moi», «chez toi», «chez Fred»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: towns → «à», feminine countries, regions and continents → «en», masculine countries → «au», plurals → «aux», people → «chez»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We live in Paris.", "answer": "Nous habitons à Paris.", "accept": ["nous habitons à paris", "nous habitons a paris"], "hint": "Paris is a town…", "right": "Excellent! A town takes «à».", "wrong": "Towns take «à»: «Nous habitons à Paris»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say where you live, for example: 'I live in Lyon, in France.'", "fr": "J'habite à Lyon, en France."}]}, "9-5": {"stageId": "9-5", "title": "20 handy little words: with, without, before, after…", "goal": "By the end you can use key prepositions like «avec, sans, avant, après, pendant, pour, sauf».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Prepositions are the small words that glue sentences together. You know some already (à, dans, sur, sous, chez). Today we'll learn new ones in opposite pairs."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Pair 1: «avant» = before, «après» = after. Pair 2: «avec» = with, «sans» = without."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "We are playing tennis without you: Nous jouons au tennis ___ toi.", "choices": ["avec", "sans", "sauf"], "answer": "sans", "right": "Yes! «sans» = without.", "wrong": "«avec» means with and «sauf» means except. Without is «sans»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous irons nager après le déjeuner.", "en": "We will go swimming after lunch."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "He will leave before me: Il partira ___ moi.", "choices": ["après", "avant", "pendant"], "answer": "avant", "right": "Correct! «avant» = before.", "wrong": "«après» means after and «pendant» means during. Before is «avant»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«de… à…» means 'from… to…' (de 8 h à 17 h). «pendant» = during or for (a stretch of time). «pour» = for, or 'in order to' before a verb."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'achète des œufs pour faire un gâteau.", "en": "I buy eggs to make a cake.", "note": "pour + infinitive = in order to"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Charlotte stayed for a week: Charlotte est restée ___ une semaine.", "choices": ["pendant", "pour", "sans"], "answer": "pendant", "right": "Great! For a length of time that really happened, use «pendant».", "wrong": "«pour» + duration is only for future plans (Nous partons pour une semaine). For a stay that actually happened, use «pendant»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«en face de» means opposite (across the street), so it isn't about faces at all: «La boucherie est en face de la boulangerie». And «sauf» = except."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I called everybody except my mother' is…", "choices": ["J'ai appelé tout le monde sauf ma mère.", "J'ai appelé tout le monde sans ma mère."], "answer": "J'ai appelé tout le monde sauf ma mère.", "right": "Exactly! «sauf» = except, so she's left out of the group.", "wrong": "«sans» means without, as in doing something without someone. To exclude one person from a group, use «sauf» (except)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «avant/après», «avec/sans», «de… à…», «pendant», «pour», «en face de», «entre», «sauf». Plus the place words you already know: «dans, sur, sous, devant, derrière, chez»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I am going to the cinema with my sister.", "answer": "Je vais au cinéma avec ma sœur.", "accept": ["je vais au cinéma avec ma sœur", "je vais au cinema avec ma soeur", "je vais au cinéma avec ma soeur", "je vais au cinema avec ma sœur"], "hint": "«Je vais au…» + with = «avec»", "right": "Wonderful! «au cinéma» + «avec».", "wrong": "«cinéma» is masculine, so «au cinéma». With = «avec»: «Je vais au cinéma avec ma sœur»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'They work from 8am to 5pm.'", "fr": "Ils travaillent de 8 h à 17 h."}]}, "9-6": {"stageId": "9-6", "title": "Holiday places: sea, mountains, countryside", "goal": "By the end you can name 10 holiday places and say where you are going.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Holiday time! Some of these you can guess: «la plage» (beach), «la mer» (sea), «la montagne»…"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«la montagne» looks like an English word. What is it?", "choices": ["the mountain", "the countryside", "the lake"], "answer": "the mountain", "right": "Yes! «montagne» ↔ mountain. Many French words are cousins of English ones.", "wrong": "Look at the letters: «mont-agne» ↔ 'mount-ain'. It's the mountain."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More words: «la campagne» = countryside, «la rivière» = river, «le lac» = lake, «la ville» = town, «le village» = village, «le camping» = campsite, «l'hôtel» = hotel."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch out: «le camping» is the PLACE (the campsite). Also, «la mer» (sea) and «la mère» (mother) sound exactly the same!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le lac» is masculine. 'I'm going to the lake' is…", "choices": ["Je vais au lac.", "Je vais à la lac."], "answer": "Je vais au lac.", "right": "Bravo! Masculine → «à + le» = «au».", "wrong": "«le lac» is masculine, and «à + le» merges into «au»: «Je vais au lac»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je vais à la mer pour les vacances et je vais dormir à l'hôtel.", "en": "I'm going to the seaside for the holidays and I'm going to sleep at the hotel."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Why is it «à l'hôtel» and not «à le hôtel»?", "choices": ["Because «hôtel» starts with a silent h", "Because «hôtel» is plural"], "answer": "Because «hôtel» starts with a silent h", "right": "Exactly! Before a vowel or silent h, «le/la» shortens to «l'», so it's «à l'».", "wrong": "«hôtel» is singular. The h is silent, so «le» shortens to «l'», which gives «à l'hôtel»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je vais à la campagne, dans un camping.", "en": "I'm going to the countryside, to a campsite."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «la ville, le village, la campagne, la mer, la plage, la rivière, le lac, la montagne, le camping, l'hôtel». Feminine → «à la», masculine → «au», vowel → «à l'»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I am going to the beach.", "answer": "Je vais à la plage.", "accept": ["je vais à la plage", "je vais a la plage"], "hint": "«plage» is feminine", "right": "Perfect! Feminine → «à la».", "wrong": "«la plage» is feminine, so «à la»: «Je vais à la plage»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'I'm going to the mountains and my friends are going to the sea.'", "fr": "Je vais à la montagne et mes amis vont à la mer."}]}, "9-7": {"stageId": "9-7", "title": "Around town: bank, church, pool…", "goal": "By the end you can name 13 town places and ask where one is.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's walk through a French town! Many places look almost like English: «la banque», «l'aéroport», «le théâtre», «l'hôpital»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «l'église»?", "choices": ["the church", "the school", "the airport"], "answer": "the church", "right": "Yes! «l'église» = the church (think 'ecclesiastical').", "wrong": "The school is «l'école» and the airport is «l'aéroport». «l'église» is the church."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Buildings: «l'immeuble» = block of flats, «le bâtiment» = building, «le centre-ville» = town centre, «la ferme» = farm."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The little hat (^) often marks a lost 's': «hôpital» = hospital, «hôtel» = hostel/hotel, «forêt» = forest. Picture the s hiding under the hat!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Places for fun: «la piscine» = swimming pool, «la patinoire» = ice rink, «la salle de sport» = gym, «la boîte de nuit» = nightclub."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Where do you go to swim?", "choices": ["à la piscine", "à la patinoire", "à la ferme"], "answer": "à la piscine", "right": "Correct! «la piscine» = the pool. (A false friend: it isn't a fish place!)", "wrong": "«la patinoire» is for ice-skating and «la ferme» is a farm. Swimming happens «à la piscine»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Où est la banque ?", "en": "Where is the bank?", "note": "«Où est… ?» = Where is…?"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Where is the church?", "answer": "Où est l'église ?", "accept": ["où est l'église", "ou est l'eglise", "où est l église", "ou est l eglise", "où est l'église?", "ou est l'église"], "hint": "«Où est…»", "right": "Perfect! «Où est» + the place.", "wrong": "Use «Où est» + the place: «Où est l'église ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "I'll wait for you in the town centre: Je t'attendrai ___ centre-ville.", "choices": ["au", "à la", "en"], "answer": "au", "right": "Bravo! «le centre-ville» is masculine → «au».", "wrong": "«le centre-ville» is masculine, so «à + le» = «au centre-ville»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «l'immeuble, l'aéroport, la banque, l'église, le bâtiment, la boîte de nuit, le centre-ville, la ferme, la patinoire, la piscine, la salle de sport, le théâtre, l'hôpital». Ask with «Où est… ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "You're lost. Say it out loud: 'Excuse me, where is the hospital?'", "fr": "Excusez-moi, où est l'hôpital ?"}]}, "9-8": {"stageId": "9-8", "title": "More town places: library, post office, town hall", "goal": "By the end you can name more key places and avoid the «librairie» trap.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Some easy ones first: «la maison» (house), «le cinéma», «le musée», «le parc», «le zoo», «le bar»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le musée» means…", "choices": ["the museum", "the music", "the mouse"], "answer": "the museum", "right": "Yes! «musée» ↔ museum.", "wrong": "Music is «la musique». «le musée» is the museum (le musée du Louvre!)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Official places: «la bibliothèque» = library, «la poste» = post office, «la mairie» = town hall, «le commissariat de police» = police station, «le centre commercial» = shopping centre."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Classic trap: «la librairie» is a BOOKSHOP (you pay). The library, where you borrow, is «la bibliothèque»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You want to borrow a book for free. Where do you go?", "choices": ["à la bibliothèque", "à la librairie"], "answer": "à la bibliothèque", "right": "Well done! Borrowing happens at «la bibliothèque».", "wrong": "«la librairie» is a shop where you buy books. To borrow, go to «la bibliothèque»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two more: «le bureau» = the office (and also the desk!). «le jardin public» = a public park, the same as «le parc»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu dois poster une lettre à la poste.", "en": "You must post a letter at the post office."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Do you want to go to the cinema tomorrow night? Veux-tu aller ___ cinéma demain soir ?", "choices": ["au", "à la", "aux"], "answer": "au", "right": "Great! «le cinéma» → «au cinéma».", "wrong": "«cinéma» is masculine singular, so «à + le» = «au»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Où est le café le plus proche ?", "en": "Where is the nearest café?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «la maison, la bibliothèque, le cinéma, le musée, le bureau, le parc, le commissariat de police, la poste, le centre commercial, le zoo, la mairie, le bar, le café». Remember: librairie = bookshop!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I am going to the town hall.", "answer": "Je vais à la mairie.", "accept": ["je vais à la mairie", "je vais a la mairie"], "hint": "town hall = «la mairie»", "right": "Perfect! «la mairie» is feminine → «à la».", "wrong": "Town hall = «la mairie» (feminine), so «Je vais à la mairie»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'I'm going to borrow a book at the library.'", "fr": "Je vais emprunter un livre à la bibliothèque."}]}, "9-9": {"stageId": "9-9", "title": "Shopping street: bakery, cheese shop, chemist's", "goal": "By the end you can name French shops and say which shop you're going to.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French shop names often end in «-erie». Start from the product: «le pain» → «la boulangerie» (bakery), «la pâtisserie» = cake shop."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«le fromage» = cheese. So «la fromagerie» is…", "choices": ["the cheese shop", "the fish shop", "the bakery"], "answer": "the cheese shop", "right": "Exactly! Product + «-erie» = the shop that sells it.", "wrong": "Look inside the word: «fromag-erie» → «fromage» (cheese) + «-erie» (shop). It's the cheese shop."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«le poisson» (fish) → «la poissonnerie». «la boucherie» sells raw meat, «la charcuterie» sells prepared meats (ham, sausages), and «l'épicerie» is the grocer's."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You want ham and saucisson. Where do you go?", "choices": ["à la charcuterie", "à la boucherie", "à la bijouterie"], "answer": "à la charcuterie", "right": "Yes! Prepared meats like ham and sausage come from «la charcuterie».", "wrong": "«la boucherie» sells raw meat and «la bijouterie» sells jewellery. Ham and saucisson come from «la charcuterie»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More shops: «la pharmacie» (chemist's), «la bijouterie» (jeweller's), «la quincaillerie» (hardware store), «la librairie» (bookshop), «le pressing» (dry cleaner's), «le bureau de tabac» (newsagent's)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Handy rule: every shop name ending in «-erie» is feminine (la boulangerie, la boucherie…). In France, look for the green cross for «la pharmacie»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Other shops use «le magasin de…»: «le magasin de vêtements» (clothes), «de chaussures» (shoes), «de jouets» (toys), «de bricolage» (DIY)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "It's my nephew's birthday, I'm going to the toy shop: Je vais ___", "choices": ["au magasin de jouets.", "à la magasin de jouets.", "au magasin de chaussures."], "answer": "au magasin de jouets.", "right": "Bravo! «le magasin» is masculine → «au», and toys = «jouets».", "wrong": "«magasin» is masculine, so «au magasin». Toys are «jouets» («chaussures» are shoes)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai acheté un marteau et des clous à la quincaillerie.", "en": "I bought a hammer and some nails at the hardware store."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«La boulangerie n'est pas loin de la fromagerie.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["The bakery is not far from the cheese shop.", "The bakery is next to the cheese shop."], "answer": "The bakery is not far from the cheese shop.", "right": "Correct! «pas loin de» = not far from.", "wrong": "«loin de» means far from, and «n'… pas» makes it 'not far from'. Next to would be «à côté de»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «la boulangerie, la pâtisserie, la boucherie, la charcuterie, la poissonnerie, l'épicerie, la fromagerie, la pharmacie, la librairie», plus «le magasin de…» and «le supermarché»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I am going to the bakery.", "answer": "Je vais à la boulangerie.", "accept": ["je vais à la boulangerie", "je vais a la boulangerie"], "hint": "-erie shops are feminine", "right": "Perfect! «-erie» → feminine → «à la».", "wrong": "«la boulangerie» is feminine (all -erie shops are), so «Je vais à la boulangerie»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'I'm going to the bakery, then to the cheese shop.'", "fr": "Je vais à la boulangerie, puis à la fromagerie."}]}, "10-1": {"stageId": "10-1", "title": "Verbs 101: infinitives, groups & the present tense", "goal": "By the end you can recognise the 3 verb groups and conjugate group 1 (-er) and group 2 (-ir) verbs in the present.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "A verb in its dictionary form is called the infinitive: «parler» (to speak), «finir» (to finish), «prendre» (to take), «voir» (to see). It's the verb before you put a person in front of it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Look at «parler, finir, prendre, voir». Which of these is NOT a French infinitive ending?", "choices": ["-er", "-ir", "-re", "-ing"], "answer": "-ing", "right": "Well noticed! French has no '-ing' form at all.", "wrong": "French infinitives end in -er, -ir, -re or -oir. There's no '-ing' form in French."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Because there's no '-ing', 'I walk' and 'I am walking' are the same in French: «je marche». Never try to translate 'am'!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Verbs also have 'moods': the indicatif for facts, the impératif for orders, the conditionnel for 'would', the subjonctif for wishes and doubts. Don't worry, we'll start with the présent of the indicatif."}, {"t": "say", "text": "French sorts verbs into 3 groups. Group 1 = regular -er verbs (parler). Group 2 = regular -ir verbs whose '-ing' form ends in «-issant» (finir → finissant). Group 3 = irregular verbs (prendre, voir, dormir… and even «aller»!)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which group does «marcher» (to walk) belong to?", "choices": ["Group 1", "Group 2", "Group 3"], "answer": "Group 1", "right": "Yes! It ends in -er and it's regular, so it's group 1.", "wrong": "Regular verbs ending in -er are group 1. «marcher» ends in -er, so it's group 1."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To conjugate a group 1 verb, take off -er to get the stem (march-), then add: «-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je marche, tu marches, il marche, nous marchons, vous marchez, ils marchent", "en": "I walk, you walk, he walks, we walk, you walk, they walk"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation secret: «-e, -es, -e, -ent» are all SILENT. «je marche», «tu marches» and «ils marchent» sound exactly the same. You only hear «-ons» and «-ez»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "My children only speak English: Mes enfants ___ seulement anglais. (parler)", "choices": ["parle", "parlent", "parlez"], "answer": "parlent", "right": "Bravo! «mes enfants» = they → «-ent».", "wrong": "«mes enfants» means 'they' (ils), and the ending for ils is «-ent»: «parlent»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Group 2: take off -ir and add «-is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent». Careful: «dormir» isn't group 2, because there's no -issant (we say «dormant»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous choisissons un livre.", "en": "We choose / We are choosing a book."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "ils ___ (finir)", "choices": ["finent", "finissent", "finisent"], "answer": "finissent", "right": "Perfect! Group 2 plurals have «-iss-»: ils finissent.", "wrong": "The group 2 plural endings are «-issons, -issez, -issent», with a double s: «ils finissent»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: infinitive = the dictionary form. Group 1 (-er): -e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent. Group 2 (-ir, -issant): -is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent. Group 3 = irregular. And there's no '-ing'!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We choose a book.", "answer": "Nous choisissons un livre.", "accept": ["nous choisissons un livre"], "hint": "choisir is group 2 → nous …issons", "right": "Excellent! «choisir» → «nous choisissons».", "wrong": "«choisir» is group 2: stem «chois-» + «-issons» → «Nous choisissons un livre»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'My husband and I speak English and French.'", "fr": "Mon mari et moi parlons anglais et français."}]}, "10-2": {"stageId": "10-2", "title": "Group 1 -er verbs + the «mangeons / commençons» trick", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate any -er verb in the present, including -ger and -cer verbs.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Every group 1 verb follows the same recipe: take off -er to find the stem, then add the endings. «chanter» → stem «chant-»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the stem of «regarder» (to watch)?", "choices": ["regard", "regarde", "regarder"], "answer": "regard", "right": "Yes! Take off -er and «regard-» is left.", "wrong": "To find the stem, take off the last two letters, -er: «regarder» → «regard-»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The endings: «je -e, tu -es, il/elle/on -e, nous -ons, vous -ez, ils/elles -ent»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je chante, tu chantes, nous chantons, vous chantez.", "en": "I sing, you sing, we sing, you sing."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "tu ___ (chanter)", "choices": ["chante", "chantes", "chantez"], "answer": "chantes", "right": "Correct! «tu» always takes an -s: tu chantes.", "wrong": "With «tu», the ending is «-es»: «tu chantes». «-ez» goes with «vous»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now a spelling quirk with «manger». A g followed by o sounds hard, like 'go'. To keep the soft 'zh' sound with «nous», French adds an e: «nous mangeons»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which spelling is correct?", "choices": ["nous mangons", "nous mangeons", "nous mangeont"], "answer": "nous mangeons", "right": "Great! The e keeps the g soft before «-ons».", "wrong": "Without the e, «mangons» would sound like 'man-GONE'. Add an e to keep the soft sound: «nous mangeons»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Same idea with «commencer». c + o sounds like k, so French adds a cedilla (ç) to keep the 's' sound: «nous commençons»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous commençons à 9 heures.", "en": "We start at 9 o'clock."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Only «nous» changes, because it's the only ending that starts with o. Other examples: nager → nous nageons, voyager → nous voyageons, lancer → nous lançons."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "nous ___ (nager = to swim)", "choices": ["nagons", "nageons", "naçons"], "answer": "nageons", "right": "Bravo! -ger verb + nous → «-geons».", "wrong": "«nager» ends in -ger, so «nous» needs an e after the g: «nous nageons». The ç is only for -cer verbs."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: stem + «-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent». With «nous»: -ger → «-geons», -cer → «-çons»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We are eating.", "answer": "Nous mangeons.", "accept": ["nous mangeons"], "hint": "Don't forget the e!", "right": "Perfect! «nous mangeons», with the e.", "wrong": "«manger» is a -ger verb, so «nous mangeons» keeps an e after the g."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'I eat, we eat, we begin.'", "fr": "Je mange, nous mangeons, nous commençons."}]}, "10-3": {"stageId": "10-3", "title": "Practice: -er verbs in real sentences", "goal": "By the end you can quickly pick the right -er ending for any subject, including 'Tom and I'.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Practice day! You already know the -er endings. Let's check your memory before I show them again."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the -er ending for «vous»?", "choices": ["-ons", "-ez", "-ent"], "answer": "-ez", "right": "Yes! vous → -ez (vous chantez).", "wrong": "«vous» takes «-ez» (vous parlez). «-ons» is for nous and «-ent» is for ils/elles."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Here they are: «je -e, tu -es, il/elle/on -e, nous -ons, vous -ez, ils/elles -ent»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Charlotte ___ dans la forêt avec son chien. (marcher)", "choices": ["marche", "marches", "marchent"], "answer": "marche", "right": "Correct! One person = elle → «-e».", "wrong": "Charlotte = elle, and elle takes «-e»: «Charlotte marche»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Subject trick: two names = they → «-ent» (Tom et Sam jouent). 'X and me' = nous → «-ons». 'X and you' = vous → «-ez»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Rob and I are calling our grandma: Rob et moi ___ à notre grand-mère. (téléphoner)", "choices": ["téléphonent", "téléphonons", "téléphonez"], "answer": "téléphonons", "right": "Bravo! 'Rob and I' = we → «-ons».", "wrong": "'Rob and me' includes you, so it means nous, which takes «-ons»: «téléphonons»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Rob and you watch TV every evening: Rob et toi ___ la télé tous les soirs. (regarder)", "choices": ["regardez", "regardons", "regardent"], "answer": "regardez", "right": "Perfect! 'Rob and you' = vous → «-ez».", "wrong": "'Rob and you' = you (plural) = vous, so «-ez»: «regardez»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les enfants nagent à la piscine régulièrement.", "en": "The children swim at the pool regularly.", "note": "nagent: -ent is silent!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two bonus structures. Questions can swap the verb and «tu»: «Écoutes-tu souvent de la musique ?» Negatives wrap the verb: «Tom ne coupe pas le gâteau»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Je ___ une pizza au fromage. (manger)", "choices": ["mange", "manges", "mangeons"], "answer": "mange", "right": "Yes! je → «-e».", "wrong": "With «je», the ending is «-e»: «je mange». The extra e only appears in «nous mangeons»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: find the subject first (who?), then add the ending. 'X et moi' → -ons, 'X et toi' → -ez, two people → -ent."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (use «tu»): You speak two languages.", "answer": "Tu parles deux langues.", "accept": ["tu parles deux langues"], "hint": "tu → -es", "right": "Great job! «tu parles».", "wrong": "«tu» takes «-es»: «Tu parles deux langues»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'You sing in a choir every Wednesday evening.'", "fr": "Vous chantez dans une chorale tous les mercredis soir."}]}, "10-4": {"stageId": "10-4", "title": "Group 2 -ir verbs: finir, choisir, grandir", "goal": "By the end you can spot a group 2 verb and conjugate it in the present.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Group 2 verbs pass two tests: they end in -ir, AND their '-ing' form ends in «-issant» (finir → finissant). If you can say '-issant', it's group 2."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is a group 2 verb?", "choices": ["choisir (choisissant)", "dormir (dormant)", "partir (partant)"], "answer": "choisir (choisissant)", "right": "Exactly! «choisissant» has the -issant ending, so it's group 2.", "wrong": "«dormant» and «partant» have no «-iss-», so they're group 3. Only «choisissant» passes the test."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Take off -ir and add: «-is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je finis, tu finis, il finit, nous finissons, vous finissez, ils finissent", "en": "I finish, you finish, he finishes, we finish, you finish, they finish"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Listening trick: «je finis», «tu finis» and «il finit» sound the same ('fee-nee'). If you hear the «-iss-» sound, it's plural!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The children are growing fast: Les enfants ___ vite. (grandir)", "choices": ["grandit", "grandissent", "grandissons"], "answer": "grandissent", "right": "Bravo! They → «-issent».", "wrong": "«les enfants» = ils, and ils takes «-issent»: «grandissent»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The cat jumps onto the table: Le chat ___ sur la table. (bondir)", "choices": ["bondis", "bondit", "bondissent"], "answer": "bondit", "right": "Correct! il/elle → «-it».", "wrong": "«le chat» = il, and il takes «-it»: «bondit». «-is» is for je/tu."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je remplis une bouteille d'eau.", "en": "I am filling a bottle with water."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "My friends and I are finishing our homework: Mes amis et moi ___ nos devoirs. (finir)", "choices": ["finissons", "finissez", "finissent"], "answer": "finissons", "right": "Perfect! 'My friends and I' = nous → «-issons».", "wrong": "'My friends and I' = we = nous, so «-issons»: «finissons»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: group 2 = -ir + -issant. Endings: «-is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent». Common ones: finir, choisir, grandir, remplir, agir, bâtir."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We choose the best films.", "answer": "Nous choisissons les meilleurs films.", "accept": ["nous choisissons les meilleurs films"], "hint": "nous → -issons", "right": "Excellent!", "wrong": "«choisir» + nous → «choisissons»: «Nous choisissons les meilleurs films»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'I finish, we finish, they finish.'", "fr": "Je finis, nous finissons, ils finissent."}]}, "10-5": {"stageId": "10-5", "title": "Verb detective: group 1, 2 or 3?", "goal": "By the end you can sort any verb into its group in a few seconds.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's play detective and sort verbs into their 3 groups. You only need three clues."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Clue 1: ends in -er → group 1 (with one famous exception). Clue 2: ends in -ir AND has -issant → group 2. Clue 3: everything else (-re, -oir, other -ir) → group 3."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«sauter» (to jump)?", "choices": ["Group 1", "Group 2", "Group 3"], "answer": "Group 1", "right": "Yes! An -er ending → group 1.", "wrong": "«sauter» ends in -er and is regular, so it's group 1."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«rougir» (to blush)? Hint: «rougissant».", "choices": ["Group 1", "Group 2", "Group 3"], "answer": "Group 2", "right": "Correct! -ir + -issant = group 2.", "wrong": "«rougir» ends in -ir and gives «rougissant», so it passes both tests: group 2."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«descendre» (to go down)?", "choices": ["Group 1", "Group 2", "Group 3"], "answer": "Group 3", "right": "Bravo! Every -re verb is group 3.", "wrong": "«descendre» ends in -re, and -re verbs are always group 3."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The famous exception: «aller» ends in -er but it's group 3, because «je vais, nous allons, ils vont» follow no pattern!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "So… «aller»?", "choices": ["Group 1", "Group 2", "Group 3"], "answer": "Group 3", "right": "You remembered the trap! «aller» = group 3.", "wrong": "«aller» is the exception: it ends in -er but it's totally irregular, so it's group 3."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je pense, donc je suis.", "en": "I think, therefore I am.", "note": "Descartes! «penser» = group 1, «être» = group 3"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which list contains ONLY group 3 verbs?", "choices": ["voir, écrire, croire", "parler, penser, voir", "grandir, rougir, finir"], "answer": "voir, écrire, croire", "right": "Excellent detective work! -oir and -re verbs are group 3.", "wrong": "«parler» and «penser» are group 1, and «grandir, rougir, finir» are group 2. «voir, écrire, croire» are all group 3."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: -er → group 1 (except «aller»). -ir + -issant → group 2. -re, -oir and other -ir verbs → group 3."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the infinitive of the group 1 verb meaning 'to think'.", "answer": "penser", "accept": ["penser"], "hint": "Remember Descartes: «Je pense…»", "right": "Yes! «penser», an -er verb from group 1.", "wrong": "'To think' is «penser» (je pense), a regular -er verb."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'Parler: group one. Finir: group two. Prendre: group three.'", "fr": "Parler : groupe un. Finir : groupe deux. Prendre : groupe trois."}]}, "10-6": {"stageId": "10-6", "title": "«essayer» & «payer»: the y/i switch", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate «essayer» (to try) and «payer» (to pay) in the present.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«essayer» = to try (or try on) and «payer» = to pay. They're group 1 verbs, with one small twist on the letter y."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Before a silent ending (-e, -es, -ent), the y can turn into an i. You can write «je paie» OR «je paye», and both are correct!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je paie. / Je paye.", "en": "I pay. (both spellings are correct)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is NOT correct?", "choices": ["je paie", "je paye", "nous paions"], "answer": "nous paions", "right": "Well spotted! «nous» and «vous» always keep the y: nous payons.", "wrong": "«nous» and «vous» never change: «nous payons», «vous payez». So «nous paions» is wrong."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Full «essayer»: «j'essaie/j'essaye, tu essaies/essayes, il essaie/essaye, nous essayons, vous essayez, ils essaient/essayent»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "They are trying to do their best: Ils ___ de faire de leur mieux.", "choices": ["essaient", "essayons", "essaiez"], "answer": "essaient", "right": "Correct! «ils essaient» (or «ils essayent»).", "wrong": "For «ils», the ending is «-ent», and the y may become i: «essaient». «essayons» goes with nous."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«essayer» also means to try ON clothes: «Charlotte essaie sa nouvelle robe». In a shop, ask «Je peux essayer ?» (Can I try it on?). And «essayer de» + verb = to try to."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu paies en liquide ou avec ta carte de crédit ?", "en": "Are you paying in cash or by credit card?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "vous ___ (payer)", "choices": ["payez", "paiez", "payons"], "answer": "payez", "right": "Perfect! vous keeps the y: «vous payez».", "wrong": "With «vous», there's no y/i change: «vous payez». («payons» goes with nous.)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: with je, tu, il and ils you can choose i or y (je paie/paye). With nous and vous, always y: «nous payons, vous essayez»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We pay.", "answer": "Nous payons.", "accept": ["nous payons"], "hint": "nous keeps the y", "right": "Great! «nous payons».", "wrong": "«nous» always keeps the y: «Nous payons»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: 'I try on the dress and I pay with my card.'", "fr": "J'essaie la robe et je paie avec ma carte."}]}, "11-1": {"stageId": "11-1", "title": "Irregular verbs: prendre, pouvoir, vouloir & more", "goal": "By the end you can use key irregular verbs, talk about the weather, say what you're busy doing and give simple orders.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today: irregular verbs, also called group 3 verbs. There's no single pattern, but here's good news: «nous» almost always ends in -ons, «vous» in -ez and «ils/elles» in -ent."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Look: «nous prenons», «nous buvons», «nous pouvons». Which ending do they share?", "choices": ["-ons", "-ez", "-ent"], "answer": "-ons", "right": "Exactly! The «nous» form ends in -ons for almost every verb (except «nous sommes» from «être»).", "wrong": "Look at the end of each word: «pren-ons», «buv-ons», «pouv-ons». «nous» almost always ends in -ons."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«prendre» (to take): «je prends», «tu prends», «il prend», «nous prenons», «vous prenez», «ils prennent». Watch the double «n» in «prennent»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Nous ___ le train de 18 h.»", "choices": ["prenons", "prennent", "prendons"], "answer": "prenons", "right": "Bravo! «nous prenons»: the «d» disappears in the plural.", "wrong": "With «nous», «prendre» becomes «prenons» (no «d»). «prennent» is for «ils/elles»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«pouvoir» (can) and «vouloir» (to want) are twins: «je peux / je veux», «tu peux / tu veux», «il peut / il veut», «nous pouvons / voulons», «vous pouvez / voulez», «ils peuvent / veulent»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«je peux», «tu peux», «je veux», «tu veux» end in -x, not -s! They sound like 'puh' and 'vuh'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous prenons le train de 18 h car nous voulons aller à Paris.", "en": "We're taking the 6 pm train because we want to go to Paris.", "note": "After «vouloir» or «pouvoir», the next verb stays in the infinitive: «aller»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence means 'I can come'?", "choices": ["Je peux viens.", "Je peux venir.", "Je peut venir."], "answer": "Je peux venir.", "right": "Parfait ! «je peux» (with -x) + infinitive «venir».", "wrong": "«je» takes «peux» (with -x), and the second verb stays in the infinitive: «Je peux venir.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Weather verbs are impersonal: they only work with «il», which here means 'it': «il pleut» (it's raining), «il neige» (it's snowing). And «il faut…» = you must / it is necessary."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Look out of the window and say what the weather's like: «Il pleut !» or «Il neige !»", "fr": "Il pleut !"}, {"t": "say", "text": "To stress that something is happening right now, use «être en train de» + infinitive: «Je suis en train de regarder la télé» = I'm (busy) watching TV."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'She is (busy) drinking a lemonade'?", "choices": ["Elle est en train de boire une limonade.", "Elle est en train boire une limonade.", "Elle a en train de boire une limonade."], "answer": "Elle est en train de boire une limonade.", "right": "Exactly! «être» + «en train de» + infinitive. Don't forget the little «de».", "wrong": "The structure is «être en train de» + infinitive: «elle est en train de boire». It uses «être» (not «avoir») and always needs «de»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The imperative gives orders: take the «tu», «nous» or «vous» form and drop the pronoun. «Partons !» = Let's go! «Arrêtez !» = Stop! For -er verbs, the «tu» form loses its -s: «tu marches» → «Marche !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Tell a friend: 'Walk in front of me!'", "choices": ["Marches devant moi !", "Marche devant moi !", "Tu marche devant moi !"], "answer": "Marche devant moi !", "right": "Bravo! No pronoun, and -er verbs drop the -s in the «tu» imperative.", "wrong": "In the imperative we drop «tu», and -er verbs lose their -s: «tu marches» → «Marche devant moi !»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «prendre» (nous prenons, ils prennent), «pouvoir / vouloir» (je peux, je veux + infinitive), «il pleut», «être en train de», and orders like «Partons !»"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We want to go to Paris.", "answer": "Nous voulons aller à Paris.", "accept": ["nous voulons aller à paris", "nous voulons aller à paris.", "nous voulons aller a paris", "on veut aller à paris", "on veut aller a paris"], "hint": "«Nous voulons…» + infinitive", "right": "Super ! «nous voulons» + the infinitive «aller».", "wrong": "«vouloir» with «nous» = «voulons», then the infinitive: «Nous voulons aller à Paris.»"}]}, "11-2": {"stageId": "11-2", "title": "Group 3 verbs: the four ending families", "goal": "By the end you can recognise group 3 verbs and pick the right present-tense ending.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Group 3 verbs are the irregular ones. They end in -ir («dormir», «sortir»), -oir («savoir», «vouloir») or -re («prendre», «mettre»), plus the verb «aller»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which of these is a group 3 verb?", "choices": ["parler", "finir", "vendre"], "answer": "vendre", "right": "Yes! «vendre» (to sell) ends in -re, so it's group 3.", "wrong": "«parler» is group 1 (-er) and «finir» is group 2 (nous finissons). «vendre» ends in -re: group 3."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Most group 3 verbs take -s, -s, -t, -ons, -ez, -ent: «je dors», «tu dors», «il dort», «nous dormons», «vous dormez», «ils dorment»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je lis un livre et il dort.", "en": "I'm reading a book and he's sleeping."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«lire» (to read) follows -s, -s, -t. Complete: «Il ___ le journal.»", "choices": ["lis", "lit", "lid"], "answer": "lit", "right": "Bravo! «il» → -t: «il lit».", "wrong": "In this family, «il» takes -t: «je lis», «tu lis», «il lit»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Verbs in -dre («prendre», «attendre», «vendre», «répondre») keep their «d»: -ds, -ds, -d. So «il prend», «il attend», with no -t!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«attendre» (to wait). Complete: «Elle ___ le bus.»", "choices": ["attend", "attendt", "attends"], "answer": "attend", "right": "Exactly! -dre verbs end in -d with «il/elle».", "wrong": "-dre verbs keep the «d» and add nothing for «il/elle»: «elle attend». «attends» is for «je» and «tu»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«pouvoir» and «vouloir» use -x, -x, -t: «je peux», «tu veux», «il peut»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "And «ouvrir» (to open) behaves like an -er verb: «j'ouvre», «tu ouvres», «il ouvre», «nous ouvrons»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete with «ouvrir»: «Tu ___ la fenêtre ?»", "choices": ["ouvres", "ouvris", "ouvrs"], "answer": "ouvres", "right": "Parfait ! «ouvrir» takes -e, -es, -e, like «parler».", "wrong": "«ouvrir» takes the -er endings: «j'ouvre», «tu ouvres», «il ouvre»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The stem often changes with «nous» and «vous»: «je viens» → «nous venons», «je bois» → «nous buvons». And watch out: «je suis» can mean 'I am' («être») or 'I follow' («suivre»)!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«venir» (to come): «je viens», «nous ___».", "choices": ["viennons", "venons", "viens"], "answer": "venons", "right": "Yes! The stem goes back to «ven-» with «nous»: «nous venons».", "wrong": "With «nous», the stem changes back to «ven-»: «nous venons». «viens» is for «je» and «tu»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je peux faire un gâteau au chocolat.", "en": "I can make a chocolate cake."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: -s/-s/-t («je dors»), -ds/-ds/-d («il prend»), -x/-x/-t («je peux»), -e/-es/-e («j'ouvre»). And «nous» / «vous» almost always end in -ons / -ez."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We are learning French. (apprendre)", "answer": "Nous apprenons le français.", "accept": ["nous apprenons le français", "nous apprenons le français.", "nous apprenons le francais", "on apprend le français", "on apprend le francais"], "hint": "«apprendre» works like «prendre»: «nous appren…»", "right": "Excellent ! «apprendre» follows «prendre»: «nous apprenons».", "wrong": "«apprendre» is conjugated like «prendre»: «nous apprenons». Languages take «le»: «le français»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Ask someone politely: «Pouvez-vous parler français ?»", "fr": "Pouvez-vous parler français ?"}]}, "11-3": {"stageId": "11-3", "title": "Practice: conjugating irregular verbs", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate common irregular verbs in the present without hesitating.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Practice time! Quick reminder of the four ending families: -s/-s/-t, -ds/-ds/-d, -x/-x/-t and -e/-es/-e."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«courir» (to run) uses -s, -s, -t. «Il ___ dans le parc.»", "choices": ["cours", "court", "coure"], "answer": "court", "right": "Bravo! «il» → -t: «il court».", "wrong": "In the -s/-s/-t family, «il» takes -t: «je cours», «tu cours», «il court»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les enfants courent dans le parc.", "en": "The children are running in the park.", "note": "«les enfants» = «ils» → -ent."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom et moi ___ le train de 20 h.» (prendre)", "choices": ["prenons", "prennent", "prenez"], "answer": "prenons", "right": "Yes! «Tom et moi» = «nous» → «prenons».", "wrong": "«Tom et moi» means 'we', so «nous»: «nous prenons». «prennent» is for «ils», «prenez» for «vous»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Spot the hidden pronoun first: «Tom et moi» = «nous», «Alice et Luc» = «ils», «Charlotte» = «elle»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Est-ce que vous ___ dîner chez Michel ce soir ?» (aller)", "choices": ["allez", "vont", "allons"], "answer": "allez", "right": "Parfait ! «vous allez».", "wrong": "«aller» with «vous» = «allez». «vont» is «ils», «allons» is «nous»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Charlotte ___ l'anglais.» (apprendre)", "choices": ["apprend", "apprends", "apprent"], "answer": "apprend", "right": "Exactly! -dre verbs end in -d with «il/elle».", "wrong": "«Charlotte» = «elle», and -dre verbs end in -d: «elle apprend»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«vouloir»: «je veux», «tu veux», «il veut», «nous voulons», «vous voulez», «ils veulent». The negative wraps around the verb: «il ne veut pas»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il ne ___ pas manger.»", "choices": ["veux", "veut", "veulent"], "answer": "veut", "right": "Bravo! «il» → «veut».", "wrong": "With «il», «vouloir» = «veut» (with -t). «veux» is for «je/tu»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu ___ trop de sucre sur le gâteau.» (mettre)", "choices": ["mets", "met", "mettes"], "answer": "mets", "right": "Yes! «tu mets», with an -s.", "wrong": "«mettre» follows -s/-s/-t: «je mets», «tu mets», «il met»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«offrir» (to offer, to give as a gift) works like «ouvrir»: «j'offre», «tu offres», «il offre», «nous offrons», «ils offrent»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Alice et Luc ___ des fleurs à leur mère.»", "choices": ["offrent", "offre", "offrons"], "answer": "offrent", "right": "Parfait ! «Alice et Luc» = «ils» → «offrent».", "wrong": "«Alice et Luc» = «ils», so -ent: «ils offrent»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: find the pronoun, pick the family, add the ending. «ils courent», «nous prenons», «vous allez», «elle apprend», «il veut», «tu mets»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I can prepare lunch. (pouvoir)", "answer": "Je peux préparer le déjeuner.", "accept": ["je peux préparer le déjeuner", "je peux préparer le déjeuner.", "je peux preparer le dejeuner"], "hint": "«Je peux…» + infinitive", "right": "Super ! «je peux» + infinitive «préparer».", "wrong": "«je peux» (with -x) + infinitive: «Je peux préparer le déjeuner.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Nous vendons notre maison.»", "fr": "Nous vendons notre maison."}]}, "11-4": {"stageId": "11-4", "title": "Groups 1, 2 & 3: which family?", "goal": "By the end you can quickly tell which group a French verb belongs to.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French verbs come in three families. Group 1: -er verbs («parler»). Group 2: regular -ir verbs like «finir» («nous finissons»). Group 3: all the irregular ones."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«ajouter» (to add) belongs to…", "choices": ["group 1", "group 2", "group 3"], "answer": "group 1", "right": "Yes! It ends in -er, so group 1, the biggest and easiest family.", "wrong": "«ajouter» ends in -er, so it's group 1."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "One big exception: «aller» ends in -er but it's group 3, totally irregular («je vais», «nous allons»)!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "So «aller» belongs to…", "choices": ["group 1", "group 2", "group 3"], "answer": "group 3", "right": "Well remembered! «aller» is the only -er verb in group 3.", "wrong": "«aller» looks like group 1, but it's irregular («je vais», «ils vont»), so group 3."}, {"t": "say", "text": "How to spot group 2? Try «nous»: if you get -issons, it's group 2. «finir» → «nous finissons», «choisir» → «nous choisissons»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous choisissons un gâteau.", "en": "We're choosing a cake."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«dormir» ends in -ir, but gives «nous dormons». So it's…", "choices": ["group 2", "group 3"], "answer": "group 3", "right": "Exactly! No -iss-, so it's irregular: group 3.", "wrong": "Group 2 needs -iss- with «nous» («finissons»). «nous dormons» has none, so «dormir» is group 3."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Group 3 also includes all verbs in -re («vendre», «mettre», «prendre») and -oir («voir», «savoir», «apercevoir»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«vendre» (to sell) belongs to…", "choices": ["group 1", "group 2", "group 3"], "answer": "group 3", "right": "Yes! -re verbs are always group 3.", "wrong": "«vendre» ends in -re, and -re verbs are group 3."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«acheter» (to buy) belongs to…", "choices": ["group 1", "group 2", "group 3"], "answer": "group 1", "right": "Bravo! The accent changes in «j'achète», but it's still a regular -er verb: group 1.", "wrong": "«acheter» ends in -er, so group 1 (even if «j'achète» takes an accent)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«se réjouir» (to be delighted) gives «nous nous réjouissons». Group?", "choices": ["group 1", "group 2", "group 3"], "answer": "group 2", "right": "Yes! -ir + -iss- = group 2.", "wrong": "Look at «réjou-iss-ons»: the -iss- shows it's group 2."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: -er = group 1 (except «aller»), -ir with -iss- = group 2, everything else (-re, -oir, other -ir) = group 3."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the «nous» form of «finir».", "answer": "nous finissons", "accept": ["nous finissons", "finissons"], "hint": "Group 2: add -issons", "right": "Parfait ! «nous finissons», the group 2 trademark.", "wrong": "Group 2 verbs add -iss- with «nous»: «nous finissons»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say one verb from each group: «Je parle, je finis, je prends.»", "fr": "Je parle, je finis, je prends."}]}, "11-5": {"stageId": "11-5", "title": "Verbs and their opposites", "goal": "By the end you can use common verbs in pairs of opposites, like open/close and buy/sell.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Learning words in pairs of opposites doubles your vocabulary! For example, «aller» (to go) ↔ «venir» (to come)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«ouvrir» = to open. What do you think the opposite is?", "choices": ["fermer", "sortir", "finir"], "answer": "fermer", "right": "Yes! «ouvrir» ↔ «fermer». Shops show «ouvert» (open) and «fermé» (closed).", "wrong": "The opposite of «ouvrir» (open) is «fermer» (close). «sortir» = to go out, «finir» = to finish."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More pairs: «acheter» (buy) ↔ «vendre» (sell), «commencer» (start) ↔ «finir» (finish), «arriver» (arrive) ↔ «partir» (leave), «entrer» (go in) ↔ «sortir» (go out)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the opposite of «acheter» (to buy)?", "choices": ["vendre", "prendre", "donner"], "answer": "vendre", "right": "Exactly! «acheter» ↔ «vendre».", "wrong": "To buy ↔ to sell: «acheter» ↔ «vendre». «prendre» = to take, «donner» = to give."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'arrive à 9 h et je pars à 17 h.", "en": "I arrive at 9 am and I leave at 5 pm."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The prefix «dé-» or «dés-» often works like 'un-' or 'dis-': «obéir» ↔ «désobéir», «habiller» ↔ «déshabiller»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "If «obéir» = to obey, then «désobéir» = …", "choices": ["to disobey", "to obey again", "to give orders"], "answer": "to disobey", "right": "Yes! «dés-» = 'dis-': to disobey.", "wrong": "«dés-» works like English 'dis-': «désobéir» = to disobey."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Door trap in France: «Tirez» = Pull and «Poussez» = Push («tirer» ↔ «pousser»). Many tourists push when they should pull!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A shop door says «Poussez». What do you do?", "choices": ["push", "pull", "knock"], "answer": "push", "right": "Bravo! «pousser» = to push.", "wrong": "«Poussez» comes from «pousser», to push. «Tirez» would mean pull."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Around the house: «allumer» (switch on) ↔ «éteindre» (switch off), «monter» (go up) ↔ «descendre» (go down), «remplir» (fill) ↔ «vider» (empty)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You're leaving the room. Ask your friend to switch off the light: «___ la lumière, s'il te plaît.»", "choices": ["Éteins", "Allume", "Monte"], "answer": "Éteins", "right": "Parfait ! «éteindre» = to switch off; «Éteins» is the «tu» imperative.", "wrong": "Switch off = «éteindre», so «Éteins la lumière». «Allume» means switch on."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Borrow vs lend: «emprunter» (to borrow) ↔ «prêter» (to lend). «Tu me prêtes ton stylo ?» = Can you lend me your pen?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«emprunter» means…", "choices": ["to borrow", "to lend", "to give"], "answer": "to borrow", "right": "Yes! «emprunter» = to borrow, «prêter» = to lend.", "wrong": "«emprunter» = to borrow (you take it for a while). «prêter» = to lend (you give it for a while)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ouvrir / fermer», «acheter / vendre», «arriver / partir», «entrer / sortir», «allumer / éteindre», «tirer / pousser», «emprunter / prêter»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the opposite of «commencer».", "answer": "finir", "accept": ["finir", "terminer"], "hint": "It's a group 2 verb: «f…»", "right": "Super ! «commencer» ↔ «finir» (or «terminer»).", "wrong": "The opposite of «commencer» (to start) is «finir» or «terminer» (to finish)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'ouvre la porte et je ferme la fenêtre.»", "fr": "J'ouvre la porte et je ferme la fenêtre."}]}, "11-6": {"stageId": "11-6", "title": "Useful everyday verbs", "goal": "By the end you can use a set of very common verbs, without adding extra English prepositions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today, a toolbox of everyday verbs. Most end in -er, so they're regular: «aider» (to help), «chercher» (to look for), «oublier» (to forget), «montrer» (to show), «garder» (to keep)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Careful with «chercher». What does it mean?", "choices": ["to look for", "to look at", "to find"], "answer": "to look for", "right": "Yes! «chercher» = to look for. 'To look at' is «regarder».", "wrong": "«chercher» = to look for (searching). 'To look at' is «regarder»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Common mistake: 'je cherche pour mes clés'. No «pour»! «chercher» already contains 'for': «Je cherche mes clés.» Same with «attendre» (wait for) and «écouter» (listen to)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I'm waiting for the bus'?", "choices": ["J'attends le bus.", "J'attends pour le bus.", "J'attends à le bus."], "answer": "J'attends le bus.", "right": "Parfait ! «attendre» already means 'wait for', so no extra word.", "wrong": "«attendre» = to wait FOR, so no «pour»: «J'attends le bus.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Alice aide Paul à préparer le repas.", "en": "Alice helps Paul (to) prepare the meal.", "note": "«aider quelqu'un à» + infinitive."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some verbs need «à» before the person: «donner à» (give to), «montrer à» (show to), «demander à» (ask). «Je donne un livre à Paul.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I'm showing the photo to my mum'?", "choices": ["Je montre la photo à ma mère.", "Je montre ma mère la photo.", "Je montre la photo pour ma mère."], "answer": "Je montre la photo à ma mère.", "right": "Bravo! The thing first, then «à» + the person.", "wrong": "French says: «montrer» + thing + «à» + person: «Je montre la photo à ma mère»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ces livres appartiennent à ma grand-mère.", "en": "These books belong to my grandmother.", "note": "«appartenir à» = to belong to."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Useful irregular ones: «dire» (to say), «écrire» (to write), «lire» (to read), «mettre» (to put / put on), «perdre» (to lose)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "It's cold. «Je ___ mon manteau.» (I put on my coat.)", "choices": ["mets", "dis", "lis"], "answer": "mets", "right": "Yes! «mettre» = to put on (clothes): «je mets».", "wrong": "To put on clothes = «mettre»: «je mets mon manteau». «dis» = say, «lis» = read."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two more: «essayer de» + infinitive = to try to, and «oublier de» + infinitive = to forget to. «J'essaie de comprendre.» = I'm trying to understand."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'She forgets her keys'?", "choices": ["Elle oublie ses clés.", "Elle perd ses clés.", "Elle garde ses clés."], "answer": "Elle oublie ses clés.", "right": "Exactly! «oublier» = to forget.", "wrong": "To forget = «oublier»: «elle oublie ses clés». «perdre» = to lose, «garder» = to keep."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «aider», «chercher» (no «pour»!), «attendre», «montrer à», «donner à», «mettre», «essayer de», «oublier»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I'm looking for my phone.", "answer": "Je cherche mon téléphone.", "accept": ["je cherche mon téléphone", "je cherche mon téléphone.", "je cherche mon telephone", "je cherche mon portable"], "hint": "«chercher», without «pour»", "right": "Super ! And no «pour», well done.", "wrong": "«chercher» already means 'look for': «Je cherche mon téléphone.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it proudly: «J'essaie de parler français !»", "fr": "J'essaie de parler français !"}]}, "11-7": {"stageId": "11-7", "title": "Savoir vs connaître: two ways to 'know'", "goal": "By the end you can choose between «savoir» and «connaître» correctly.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "English has one 'to know'; French has two: «savoir» and «connaître». Let's find out the difference!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je sais nager.» («nager» = to swim). What do you think it means?", "choices": ["I know how to swim.", "I know a swimmer.", "I like swimming."], "answer": "I know how to swim.", "right": "Yes! «savoir» + infinitive = to know how to do something.", "wrong": "«savoir» + a verb = a skill: «Je sais nager» = I know how to swim (I can swim)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«savoir» + infinitive = to know how to (a skill). French doesn't translate 'how': «Je sais cuisiner» = I know how to cook."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mes enfants savent faire du vélo.", "en": "My children know how to ride a bike."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I know how to count'?", "choices": ["Je sais compter.", "Je sais de compter.", "Je connais compter."], "answer": "Je sais compter.", "right": "Parfait ! «savoir» + infinitive, with nothing in between.", "wrong": "A skill = «savoir» + infinitive directly: «Je sais compter». No «de», and «connaître» never takes a verb."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«savoir» also introduces a fact with «que», «où», «quand» or «combien»: «Je sais que Tom est en France.» = I know (that) Tom is in France."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«savoir»: «je sais», «tu sais», «il sait», «nous savons», «vous savez», «ils savent». «connaître»: «je connais», «tu connais», «il connaît», «nous connaissons», «vous connaissez», «ils connaissent»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Est-ce que tu ___ où est ma clé ?»", "choices": ["sais", "connais"], "answer": "sais", "right": "Yes! «où» introduces a fact, so «savoir».", "wrong": "After the verb comes «où…» (a fact), so we need «savoir»: «tu sais où…»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«connaître» + noun = to know a person, a place or a thing (to be familiar with it). «Je connais Lyon.» «Nous ne connaissons pas Paul.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Connaissez-vous ce restaurant ?", "en": "Do you know this restaurant?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I know Paul'?", "choices": ["Je sais Paul.", "Je connais Paul."], "answer": "Je connais Paul.", "right": "Bravo! A person → «connaître».", "wrong": "For people, places and things, use «connaître»: «Je connais Paul»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Quick test: next word is a verb, «que», «où» or «quand»? → «savoir». Next word is a person, place or thing? → «connaître»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous ___ Paris ?»", "choices": ["savez", "connaissez"], "answer": "connaissez", "right": "Exactly! Paris is a place → «connaître».", "wrong": "Paris is a place, so «connaître»: «Vous connaissez Paris ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «savoir» + infinitive (skill) or + «que / où / quand» (fact). «connaître» + person, place or thing."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I know how to swim.", "answer": "Je sais nager.", "accept": ["je sais nager", "je sais nager."], "hint": "«savoir» + infinitive", "right": "Super ! «Je sais nager», no word for 'how'.", "wrong": "A skill = «savoir» + infinitive: «Je sais nager.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je connais Lyon et je sais parler français !»", "fr": "Je connais Lyon et je sais parler français !"}]}, "11-8": {"stageId": "11-8", "title": "Il y a: there is, there are (and 'ago')", "goal": "By the end you can say what there is or isn't somewhere, and use «il y a» to mean 'ago'.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«il y a» is a tiny super-expression: it means both 'there is' AND 'there are'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il y a des fourchettes sur la table.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["There are forks on the table.", "He has forks on the table.", "The forks are on the table."], "answer": "There are forks on the table.", "right": "Yes! «il y a» = there is / there are.", "wrong": "«il y a» is a fixed expression meaning 'there is / there are', so: There are forks on the table."}, {"t": "say", "text": "It's made of «il» + «y» + «a» (from «avoir»). Unlike English, it never changes for plural: «il y a une assiette», «il y a 30 élèves»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'There are 30 pupils in the class'?", "choices": ["Il y a 30 élèves dans la classe.", "Il y ont 30 élèves dans la classe.", "Ils sont 30 élèves dans la classe."], "answer": "Il y a 30 élèves dans la classe.", "right": "Bravo! «il y a» stays the same, even with a plural.", "wrong": "«il y a» never changes: «Il y a 30 élèves dans la classe.» No «ont», even for plurals."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Negative: «il n'y a pas de…». «un», «une» and «des» all become «de» («d'» before a vowel)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il n'y a pas d'assiette sur la table.", "en": "There's no plate on the table."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Make it negative: «Il y a des fourchettes.»", "choices": ["Il n'y a pas des fourchettes.", "Il n'y a pas de fourchettes.", "Il y a pas de fourchettes."], "answer": "Il n'y a pas de fourchettes.", "right": "Parfait ! «n'» before «y», and «des» becomes «de».", "wrong": "«ne» becomes «n'» before «y», and after «pas», «des» becomes «de»: «Il n'y a pas de fourchettes.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«il y a» is great for weather: «il y a du soleil» (it's sunny), «il y a du vent» (it's windy), «il y a du brouillard» (it's foggy)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The trees are moving and hats are flying away. You say:", "choices": ["Il y a du vent.", "Il y a du soleil.", "Il y a du brouillard."], "answer": "Il y a du vent.", "right": "Yes! «le vent» = the wind.", "wrong": "Flying hats = wind: «Il y a du vent». «soleil» = sun, «brouillard» = fog."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Other tenses: «il y aura» = there will be, «il y avait» = there was / there used to be. Just recognise them for now!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il y aura du vent demain.", "en": "It will be windy tomorrow."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Finally, «il y a» + a time = ago: «il y a 10 ans» = 10 years ago."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Two hours ago' is…", "choices": ["il y a deux heures", "dans deux heures", "deux heures il y a"], "answer": "il y a deux heures", "right": "Exactly! «il y a» comes BEFORE the time.", "wrong": "'Ago' = «il y a», placed before the time: «il y a deux heures». «dans deux heures» = in two hours."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «il y a» = there is/are (never plural), «il n'y a pas de» = there isn't/aren't any, «il y a du vent», and «il y a» + time = ago."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: There is a cat in the garden.", "answer": "Il y a un chat dans le jardin.", "accept": ["il y a un chat dans le jardin", "il y a un chat dans le jardin."], "hint": "«Il y a un…»", "right": "Super ! «Il y a» + «un chat».", "wrong": "'There is' = «Il y a»: «Il y a un chat dans le jardin.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Describe your room: «Dans ma chambre, il y a un lit et une table.»", "fr": "Dans ma chambre, il y a un lit et une table."}]}, "12-1": {"stageId": "12-1", "title": "Saying no: simple negations", "goal": "By the end you can make sentences negative with «ne… pas», «jamais», «plus», «rien», «personne», «aucun» and «ni… ni».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we learn to say no in French! The basic negation is a sandwich: «ne» + verb + «pas»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le chat est sur la table.» → «Le chat n'est pas sur la table.» Where do «ne» and «pas» go?", "choices": ["around the verb", "before the subject", "at the end of the sentence"], "answer": "around the verb", "right": "Exactly! «ne» goes just before the verb and «pas» just after it, like bread around a filling.", "wrong": "Look again: «n'» + «est» + «pas». The two parts wrap around the verb, like a sandwich."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«ne» becomes «n'» before a vowel or a silent h: «je n'aime pas», «il n'habite pas ici»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Make it negative: «J'aime le café.»", "choices": ["Je n'aime pas le café.", "Je ne aime pas le café.", "Je pas aime le café."], "answer": "Je n'aime pas le café.", "right": "Parfait ! «aime» starts with a vowel, so «ne» → «n'», and «pas» follows the verb.", "wrong": "Before a vowel, «ne» shortens to «n'», and «pas» comes after the verb: «Je n'aime pas le café.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Swap «pas» for another word to change the meaning: «ne… jamais» (never), «ne… plus» (no longer, not anymore), «ne… rien» (nothing), «ne… personne» (nobody)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je ne regarde plus la télévision.", "en": "I don't watch TV anymore."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I never cry'?", "choices": ["Je ne pleure plus.", "Je ne pleure jamais.", "Je ne pleure rien."], "answer": "Je ne pleure jamais.", "right": "Bravo! «ne… jamais» = never.", "wrong": "'Never' = «ne… jamais». «ne… plus» means 'not anymore' and «ne… rien» means 'nothing'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je vois quelqu'un.» (I see someone.) How do you make it negative?", "choices": ["Je ne vois personne.", "Je ne vois rien.", "Je ne vois quelqu'un pas."], "answer": "Je ne vois personne.", "right": "Yes! The opposite of «quelqu'un» (someone) is «ne… personne» (nobody).", "wrong": "For people, use «ne… personne»: «Je ne vois personne» = I don't see anybody. «rien» is for things."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«ne… aucun / aucune» = no… at all, and it agrees with the noun: «Je n'ai aucune idée.» «ne… ni… ni…» = neither… nor: «Je n'ai ni pain ni beurre.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Big rule: after a negation, «un», «une», «des», «du», «de la» and «de l'» become «de» (or «d'» before a vowel). «Je mange du pain» → «Je ne mange pas de pain»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mon frère ne mange plus de viande car il est végétarien.", "en": "My brother doesn't eat meat anymore because he's vegetarian.", "note": "«de la viande» → «plus de viande»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai des amis.» How do you make it negative?", "choices": ["Je n'ai pas des amis.", "Je n'ai pas d'amis.", "Je n'ai pas de amis."], "answer": "Je n'ai pas d'amis.", "right": "Exactly! «des» → «de», and before a vowel → «d'».", "wrong": "After «pas», «des» becomes «de», and «de» shortens to «d'» before a vowel: «Je n'ai pas d'amis.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "On signs, «ne pas» stays together before an infinitive: «Ne pas fumer» (No smoking), «Ne pas déranger» (Do not disturb). In casual speech people often drop «ne» («J'sais pas» = dunno), but always write it!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ne… pas» (not), «jamais» (never), «plus» (no longer), «rien» (nothing), «personne» (nobody), «aucun» (none), «ni… ni» (neither… nor). And after a negation, articles become «de»!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I don't drink tea.", "answer": "Je ne bois pas de thé.", "accept": ["je ne bois pas de thé", "je ne bois pas de thé.", "je ne bois pas de the"], "hint": "«Je ne bois pas…» and remember «du» → «de»", "right": "Super ! «ne… pas» around «bois», and «du thé» → «de thé».", "wrong": "«ne» + «bois» + «pas», and «du thé» becomes «de thé» after a negation: «Je ne bois pas de thé.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Shrug and say: «Je n'ai aucune idée !»", "fr": "Je n'ai aucune idée !"}]}, "12-2": {"stageId": "12-2", "title": "un, une, des: in positive and negative sentences", "goal": "By the end you can choose «un», «une» or «des», and change them to «de» after a negation.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Indefinite articles mean 'a', 'an' or 'some': «un» (masculine), «une» (feminine), «des» (plural)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«maison» (house) is feminine. How do you say 'a house'?", "choices": ["un maison", "une maison", "des maison"], "answer": "une maison", "right": "Yes! Feminine singular → «une».", "wrong": "«maison» is feminine and singular, so «une maison». «un» is masculine, «des» is plural."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai un chat, une maison et des pommes.", "en": "I have a cat, a house and some apples."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il y a ___ livres sur la table.»", "choices": ["un", "une", "des"], "answer": "des", "right": "Bravo! «livres» is plural → «des».", "wrong": "«livres» ends in -s: it's plural, so «des livres»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English often drops 'some' ('I'm buying flowers'), but French always needs an article: «Nous achetons des fleurs.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'Tom is wearing black shoes'?", "choices": ["Tom porte chaussures noires.", "Tom porte des chaussures noires.", "Tom porte une chaussures noires."], "answer": "Tom porte des chaussures noires.", "right": "Parfait ! French needs «des» even where English says nothing.", "wrong": "French can't leave the noun without an article. Plural → «des»: «Tom porte des chaussures noires.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "After a negation, «un», «une» and «des» all become «de»: «J'ai un chat» → «Je n'ai pas de chat»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai des pommes. → Je n'ai pas de pommes.", "en": "I have some apples. → I don't have any apples."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous n'avez pas ___ voiture.»", "choices": ["une", "de", "des"], "answer": "de", "right": "Exactly! After «pas», «une» becomes «de».", "wrong": "The sentence is negative («n'… pas»), so «une» becomes «de»: «Vous n'avez pas de voiture.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Before a vowel or a silent h, «de» becomes «d'»: «Je n'ai pas d'ordinateur.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai une télévision mais je n'ai pas ___ ordinateur.»", "choices": ["de", "d'", "un"], "answer": "d'", "right": "Yes! Negative → «de», and before a vowel → «d'».", "wrong": "After «pas» we need «de», which shortens to «d'» before the vowel of «ordinateur»: «pas d'ordinateur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«As-tu ___ frères ?» (Do you have any brothers?)", "choices": ["des", "de", "un"], "answer": "des", "right": "Well spotted! It's a question, not a negation, so «des» stays.", "wrong": "There's no «ne… pas» here: it's just a question. So the plural article stays «des»: «As-tu des frères ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «un» (masc.), «une» (fem.), «des» (plural). After a negation, all three become «de» («d'» before a vowel)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I don't have a car.", "answer": "Je n'ai pas de voiture.", "accept": ["je n'ai pas de voiture", "je n'ai pas de voiture.", "je n’ai pas de voiture"], "hint": "«Je n'ai pas de…»", "right": "Super ! «une voiture» → «pas de voiture».", "wrong": "After «pas», «une» becomes «de»: «Je n'ai pas de voiture.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it with enthusiasm: «C'est une bonne idée !»", "fr": "C'est une bonne idée !"}]}, "12-3": {"stageId": "12-3", "title": "Practice: ne… pas, ne… plus, ne… jamais", "goal": "By the end you can turn any simple sentence into a negative one, even with two verbs or with «c'est».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's practise our three favourite negations: «ne… pas» (not), «ne… plus» (no longer), «ne… jamais» (never). They wrap around the conjugated verb."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Alice est petite.» (ne… pas)", "choices": ["Alice n'est pas petite.", "Alice ne est pas petite.", "Alice est ne pas petite."], "answer": "Alice n'est pas petite.", "right": "Bravo! «n'» before the vowel of «est», «pas» after it.", "wrong": "«ne» shortens to «n'» before a vowel and the two parts wrap around the verb: «Alice n'est pas petite.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous habitons à Paris.» (ne… plus)", "choices": ["Nous n'habitons plus à Paris.", "Nous ne habitons plus à Paris.", "Nous n'habitons pas plus à Paris."], "answer": "Nous n'habitons plus à Paris.", "right": "Yes! Silent h → «n'», and «plus» replaces «pas».", "wrong": "The «h» of «habitons» is silent, so «n'». And «plus» replaces «pas», never both: «Nous n'habitons plus à Paris.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "With two verbs (like «pouvoir» + infinitive), the negation wraps around the FIRST, conjugated verb: «Elle ne peut pas venir.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu ne peux pas conduire parce que tu n'as pas 18 ans.", "en": "You can't drive because you're not 18."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle peut venir.» How do you make it negative?", "choices": ["Elle ne peut pas venir.", "Elle peut ne venir pas.", "Elle ne peut venir pas."], "answer": "Elle ne peut pas venir.", "right": "Parfait ! «ne… pas» goes around «peut»; the infinitive stays at the end.", "wrong": "Wrap the conjugated verb «peut»: «Elle ne peut pas venir.» The infinitive «venir» comes after."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Remember: after a negation, «un», «une», «des», «du» and «de la» become «de»: «Les enfants ne mangent jamais de gâteaux.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous avons une voiture.» (ne… pas)", "choices": ["Nous n'avons pas une voiture.", "Nous n'avons pas de voiture.", "Nous ne avons pas de voiture."], "answer": "Nous n'avons pas de voiture.", "right": "Exactly! «n'» before the vowel, and «une» → «de».", "wrong": "Two things: «ne» → «n'» before «avons», and «une» → «de» after a negation: «Nous n'avons pas de voiture.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Exception: after «être» (and «devenir»), the article does NOT change: «Mon chat n'est pas un chat siamois.» You're saying what something isn't, not that something is missing."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is correct?", "choices": ["Ce n'est pas un problème.", "Ce n'est pas de problème."], "answer": "Ce n'est pas un problème.", "right": "Yes! With «être», «un» stays «un».", "wrong": "With «être», the article stays the same: «Ce n'est pas un problème.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Notice that «c'est» becomes «ce n'est pas»: «C'est joli !» → «Ce n'est pas joli !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous êtes en retard.» (ne… jamais)", "choices": ["Vous n'êtes jamais en retard.", "Vous ne êtes jamais en retard.", "Vous êtes jamais en retard."], "answer": "Vous n'êtes jamais en retard.", "right": "Bravo! «n'» + «êtes» + «jamais».", "wrong": "«ne» → «n'» before «êtes», and in writing we always keep it: «Vous n'êtes jamais en retard.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: wrap the conjugated verb, use «n'» before a vowel, change «un / une / des / du» to «de» (except after «être»), and «c'est» → «ce n'est pas»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Make it negative with «ne… plus»: «Il parle à Tom.»", "answer": "Il ne parle plus à Tom.", "accept": ["il ne parle plus à tom", "il ne parle plus à tom.", "il ne parle plus a tom"], "hint": "«Il ne parle…»", "right": "Super ! «ne… plus» around «parle».", "wrong": "Wrap the verb: «Il ne parle plus à Tom.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je ne prends jamais le bus.»", "fr": "Je ne prends jamais le bus."}]}, "12-4": {"stageId": "12-4", "title": "Repeat after me: negations out loud", "goal": "By the end you can say negative sentences smoothly, with natural French pronunciation.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "This lesson is all about saying negations out loud, smoothly and naturally. Ready to talk?"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In speech, «ne» is very light: «je ne» sounds like 'zhuhn'. «n'est pas» flows as one word, 'nay-PAH', and the «s» of «pas» is silent!"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Repeat: «Le chat n'est pas sur la table.»", "fr": "Le chat n'est pas sur la table."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which negation means 'never'?", "choices": ["ne… plus", "ne… jamais", "ne… rien"], "answer": "ne… jamais", "right": "Yes! «jamais» = never.", "wrong": "'Never' = «ne… jamais». «plus» = no longer, «rien» = nothing."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous ne mangeons jamais ensemble.", "en": "We never eat together."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Repeat: «Je ne pleure jamais.»", "fr": "Je ne pleure jamais."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je ne regarde plus la télévision.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["I don't watch TV anymore.", "I never watch TV.", "I watch more TV."], "answer": "I don't watch TV anymore.", "right": "Exactly! «ne… plus» = not anymore, no longer.", "wrong": "In a negation, «plus» means 'anymore' (not 'more'): I don't watch TV anymore."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation trick: in «ne… plus», the «s» is silent: 'plu'. If you pronounce the «s» ('pluss'), it means 'more'!"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Repeat, with a silent «s»: «Je ne regarde plus la télévision.»", "fr": "Je ne regarde plus la télévision."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«ne… rien» = nothing, not anything: «Je ne vois rien.» = I can't see anything."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Si vous ne faites rien dimanche, venez manger chez nous.", "en": "If you're not doing anything on Sunday, come and eat at our place."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je mange des épinards.» → «Je ne mange pas ___ épinards.»", "choices": ["des", "de", "d'"], "answer": "d'", "right": "Parfait ! Negative → «de», and before a vowel → «d'».", "wrong": "After «pas», «des» becomes «de», and before the vowel of «épinards» it shortens to «d'»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Repeat: «Je ne mange pas de viande.»", "fr": "Je ne mange pas de viande."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ne… pas», «ne… jamais», «ne… plus» (silent «s»!), «ne… rien», and «de / d'» after a negation. Keep «ne» light and quick."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Tom doesn't work on Tuesdays.", "answer": "Tom ne travaille pas le mardi.", "accept": ["tom ne travaille pas le mardi", "tom ne travaille pas le mardi.", "tom ne travaille pas les mardis"], "hint": "«le mardi» = on Tuesdays", "right": "Super ! «ne… pas» around «travaille», and «le mardi» = every Tuesday.", "wrong": "Wrap the verb and use «le mardi» for a habit: «Tom ne travaille pas le mardi.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Last one, nice and smooth: «Je ne bois jamais de limonade.»", "fr": "Je ne bois jamais de limonade."}]}, "12-5": {"stageId": "12-5", "title": "Adjectives and their opposites", "goal": "By the end you can describe things with common adjectives, their opposites and their feminine forms.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Adjectives come in handy pairs of opposites: «grand» (big, tall) ↔ «petit» (small), «chaud» (hot) ↔ «froid» (cold)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«facile» means easy. What do you think the opposite is?", "choices": ["difficile", "faible", "triste"], "answer": "difficile", "right": "Yes! «facile» ↔ «difficile», easy to remember!", "wrong": "Easy ↔ difficult: «facile» ↔ «difficile». «faible» = weak, «triste» = sad."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Most adjectives add -e for the feminine: «grand» → «grande», «content» → «contente». If they already end in -e, nothing changes: «triste», «facile», «jeune»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ma sœur est ___.» (tall)", "choices": ["grand", "grande", "grands"], "answer": "grande", "right": "Bravo! «sœur» is feminine → «grande».", "wrong": "The adjective agrees with «ma sœur» (feminine, singular): add -e → «grande»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some feminine forms are irregular: «beau» → «belle», «vieux» → «vieille», «gentil» → «gentille», «long» → «longue», «sec» → «sèche», «faux» → «fausse»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le château est vieux, mais la maison est neuve.", "en": "The castle is old, but the house is brand new."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What's the feminine of «vieux» (old)?", "choices": ["vieuse", "vieille", "vieuxe"], "answer": "vieille", "right": "Parfait ! «vieux» → «vieille»: «une vieille maison».", "wrong": "«vieux» is irregular: its feminine is «vieille», as in «une vieille maison»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«neuf / neuve» = brand new (just made), «nouveau / nouvelle» = new to you. «Une voiture neuve» comes straight from the factory!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The restaurant isn't «vide» (empty), it's…", "choices": ["plein", "propre", "léger"], "answer": "plein", "right": "Yes! «vide» ↔ «plein» (full).", "wrong": "The opposite of «vide» (empty) is «plein» (full). «propre» = clean, «léger» = light."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More pairs: «propre» (clean) ↔ «sale» (dirty), «léger» (light) ↔ «lourd» (heavy), «rapide» (fast) ↔ «lent» (slow), «gentil» (nice) ↔ «méchant» (mean)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Use a negation! «Ce sac n'est pas léger, il est ___.»", "choices": ["lourd", "lent", "sale"], "answer": "lourd", "right": "Exactly! Not light → heavy: «lourd».", "wrong": "The opposite of «léger» (light) is «lourd» (heavy). «lent» = slow, «sale» = dirty."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False friend: «large» means wide, not 'large'! For 'large' or 'big', say «grand». The opposite of «large» is «étroit» (narrow)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Cette rue est étroite.» (This street is narrow.) What's the opposite?", "choices": ["Cette rue est large.", "Cette rue est petite.", "Cette rue est longue."], "answer": "Cette rue est large.", "right": "Well done! «étroit» ↔ «large» (wide).", "wrong": "«étroit» (narrow) ↔ «large» (wide). Remember, «large» is a false friend!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «grand / petit», «facile / difficile», «vide / plein», «léger / lourd», «étroit / large», and irregular feminine forms like «belle», «vieille», «gentille»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The soup is not hot, it is cold.", "answer": "La soupe n'est pas chaude, elle est froide.", "accept": ["la soupe n'est pas chaude, elle est froide", "la soupe n'est pas chaude, elle est froide.", "la soupe n'est pas chaude elle est froide", "la soupe n’est pas chaude, elle est froide"], "hint": "«soupe» is feminine: «chaude», «froide»", "right": "Super ! A negation and two feminine adjectives in one sentence.", "wrong": "«la soupe» is feminine, so «chaude» and «froide», and «it» = «elle»: «La soupe n'est pas chaude, elle est froide.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it with conviction: «Le français n'est pas difficile, il est facile !»", "fr": "Le français n'est pas difficile, il est facile !"}]}, "13-1": {"stageId": "13-1", "title": "Describing and comparing: bigger, smaller, the biggest", "goal": "By the end you can make adjectives agree, compare two people with plus/moins/aussi… que, and say 'the most' and 'the best'.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Bonjour» ! Today we describe and compare people and things. First, a reminder: in French, an adjective changes to match its noun, a bit like a chameleon: «un livre intéressant», but «des histoires intéressantes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«petit» means 'small'. «La chambre» (the bedroom) is feminine. Which form do you think goes with it?", "choices": ["petit", "petite", "petits"], "answer": "petite", "right": "Yes! For most adjectives, you just add «-e» for a feminine noun: «une petite chambre».", "wrong": "«chambre» is feminine and singular, so we add «-e» to the adjective: «petit» → «petite». «petits» would be masculine plural."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Some endings change in the feminine: «heureux» → «heureuse» (happy), «sportif» → «sportive» (sporty), «beau» → «belle» (beautiful), «bon» → «bonne» (good). If the adjective already ends in «-e», nothing changes: «facile» → «facile»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Ma sœur est très ___.» (sportif)", "choices": ["sportif", "sportive", "sportife"], "answer": "sportive", "right": "Bravo ! Adjectives ending in «-if» become «-ive» in the feminine: «sportive».", "wrong": "«sœur» is feminine, and adjectives ending in «-if» change to «-ive»: «sportif» → «sportive». «sportife» does not exist."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Now let's compare! «plus» means 'more'. Guess the meaning of «Lucie est plus drôle que Tom.»", "choices": ["Lucie is funnier than Tom.", "Lucie is as funny as Tom.", "Lucie is less funny than Tom."], "answer": "Lucie is funnier than Tom.", "right": "Exactly! «plus» + adjective + «que» = 'more … than', so 'funnier than'.", "wrong": "«plus» means 'more', and «que» means 'than'. So «plus drôle que» = 'more funny than' = 'funnier than'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "There are three ways to compare: «plus … que» (more … than), «moins … que» (less … than) and «aussi … que» (as … as). The adjective goes in the middle, like a sandwich, and it still agrees: «Ma sœur est plus grande que mon frère.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Lucie est aussi drôle que Tom.", "en": "Lucie is as funny as Tom.", "note": "It works with adverbs too: «Lucie court plus vite que Tom.» = Lucie runs faster than Tom."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say: 'Lucie runs as fast as Tom'?", "choices": ["Lucie court plus vite que Tom.", "Lucie court aussi vite que Tom.", "Lucie court moins vite que Tom."], "answer": "Lucie court aussi vite que Tom.", "right": "Parfait ! 'as … as' is «aussi … que».", "wrong": "'as … as' is «aussi … que». «plus» = more and «moins» = less. So: «Lucie court aussi vite que Tom.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«bon» means 'good'. In English we don't say 'more good', we say 'better'. Which one is correct? «Mon gâteau est ___ que ton gâteau.»", "choices": ["plus bon", "meilleur", "mieux"], "answer": "meilleur", "right": "Très bien ! Just like 'better' in English, «bon» has a special form: «meilleur». We never say «plus bon».", "wrong": "«bon» is irregular: 'better' is «meilleur» (feminine «meilleure»). «plus bon» is a typical mistake, and «mieux» is the comparative of «bien» (well), not «bon»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To say 'the most' or 'the least', put «le», «la» or «les» before «plus» or «moins»: «Ma maison est la plus grande.» If the adjective comes after the noun, you repeat the article: «le chanteur le plus doué» (the most talented singer)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est la question la moins difficile.", "en": "It is the least difficult question.", "note": "«la» because «question» is feminine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'He has the best bike.' Which is correct?", "choices": ["Il a le plus bon vélo.", "Il a le meilleur vélo.", "Il a le mieux vélo."], "answer": "Il a le meilleur vélo.", "right": "Super ! 'the best' = «le meilleur» (and «la meilleure», «les meilleurs», «les meilleures»).", "wrong": "For «bon», 'the best' is «le meilleur», never «le plus bon» (like 'the goodest' in English!). «vélo» is masculine, so: «le meilleur vélo»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«mauvais» (bad) also has a special form: «pire» (worse) and «le pire» (the worst). «plus mauvais» is also correct. And remember: «mieux» (better) is for «bien» (well): «Il se sent mieux.» = He feels better."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: adjectives agree; «plus / moins / aussi + adjective + que» to compare two things; «le / la / les plus + adjective» for 'the most'; and «bon» → «meilleur» → «le meilleur»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: My sister is taller than my brother.", "answer": "Ma sœur est plus grande que mon frère.", "accept": ["ma sœur est plus grande que mon frère", "ma soeur est plus grande que mon frère", "ma soeur est plus grande que mon frere", "ma sœur est plus grande que mon frère."], "hint": "«Ma sœur est plus …» Don't forget the feminine «-e»!", "right": "Excellent ! «plus grande que»: «grande» agrees with «sœur».", "wrong": "Use «plus» + adjective + «que», and make the adjective feminine for «sœur»: «Ma sœur est plus grande que mon frère.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: my sister is taller than my brother, but my father is the tallest.", "fr": "Ma sœur est plus grande que mon frère, mais mon père est le plus grand."}]}, "13-2": {"stageId": "13-2", "title": "Bon or bien? Good vs well", "goal": "By the end you can choose between «bon» (good) and «bien» (well) in everyday sentences.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Many learners mix up «bon» and «bien». The simple rule: «bon» means 'good' and describes a thing (a noun); «bien» means 'well' and describes an action (a verb)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce gâteau est bon. Ma mère va bien.", "en": "This cake is good. My mother is well.", "note": "The cake is a thing → «bon». How my mother is doing → «bien»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'We ate well.' Is eating a thing or an action? Choose: «Nous avons ___ mangé.»", "choices": ["bon", "bien", "bonne"], "answer": "bien", "right": "Yes! «manger» is an action, so we use «bien» (well): «Nous avons bien mangé.»", "wrong": "Eating is an action (a verb), so we need 'well' = «bien». «bon / bonne» describe things: «un bon repas» (a good meal)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "As an adjective, «bon» agrees like any adjective: «bon», «bonne», «bons», «bonnes». For example: «une bonne soirée» (a nice evening), «de bons élèves» (good students)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous passons de ___ vacances.» (We are having a nice holiday.) Tip: «vacances» is feminine plural.", "choices": ["bon", "bons", "bonnes"], "answer": "bonnes", "right": "Très bien ! Feminine plural → «bonnes»: double «n» + «-es».", "wrong": "«vacances» is feminine plural, so «bon» becomes «bonnes» (double the «n», then add «-es»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«bon» can also mean 'right' or 'correct'. At the station, you might hear: «Ce n'est pas le bon train.» = This is not the right train."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'This is not the right way'? («la route» = the road/way)", "choices": ["Ce n'est pas la bien route.", "Ce n'est pas la bonne route.", "Ce n'est pas le bon route."], "answer": "Ce n'est pas la bonne route.", "right": "Parfait ! «route» is feminine, so «la bonne route».", "wrong": "'right' here is the adjective «bon», and it agrees with «route» (feminine): «la bonne route». «bien» can't be used like this, and «le» would be masculine."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "After «sentir» (to smell) and in «il fait bon» (the weather is nice), «bon» never changes: «Ces fleurs sentent bon.» Also useful when shopping: «bon marché» = cheap, and it never changes either!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Ces fleurs sentent ___.» (These flowers smell nice.)", "choices": ["bon", "bonnes", "bons"], "answer": "bon", "right": "Bravo ! After «sentir», «bon» works like an adverb, so it stays «bon», even with «fleurs».", "wrong": "Tricky one! After «sentir» (to smell), «bon» describes the action of smelling, so it never changes: «Ces fleurs sentent bon.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "And sometimes «bien» describes a person or a thing, after «être» or «c'est». Then it means 'good, decent, nice' and it NEVER changes: «C'est une fille bien.», «Ce sont des gens bien.», «Je me sens bien.» (I feel good.)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'These are good (decent) people.' «Ce sont des gens ___.»", "choices": ["bien", "biens", "bienne"], "answer": "bien", "right": "Exactly! In this use, «bien» is invariable: no «-s», no «-e».", "wrong": "When «bien» means 'good, decent' about people, it never changes: «des gens bien», «une fille bien»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Both words can be nouns too: «un bon d'achat» = a voucher, «un bon de commande» = an order form, and «Je fais ça pour ton bien.» = I'm doing this for your own good."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «bon» = good (a thing, it agrees: «bonne», «bons», «bonnes»), and also 'right'. «bien» = well (an action), or 'good/decent' after «être», and it never changes."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: My mother is well.", "answer": "Ma mère va bien.", "accept": ["ma mère va bien", "ma mere va bien", "ma mère va bien."], "hint": "Use the verb «aller»: «Ma mère va …»", "right": "Super ! In French, health uses «aller» + «bien»: «Ça va bien», «Ma mère va bien».", "wrong": "To talk about health, French uses «aller» (to go) + «bien» (well): «Ma mère va bien.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: this cake is good and I feel good!", "fr": "Ce gâteau est bon et je me sens bien !"}]}, "13-3": {"stageId": "13-3", "title": "Adjectives: matching and placing them", "goal": "By the end you can make adjectives agree with their noun and know when to put them before or after it.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's practise adjectives! Remember: a French adjective matches its noun in gender (masculine/feminine) and number (singular/plural)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je suis dans une ___ chambre.» (petit)", "choices": ["petit", "petite", "petits"], "answer": "petite", "right": "Yes! «une chambre» is feminine singular, so «petite».", "wrong": "Look at «une»: «chambre» is feminine singular. Add «-e»: «une petite chambre»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For the plural, most adjectives just add «-s»: «Ils sont jolis.» If an adjective already ends in «-x» or «-s», it stays the same: «un enfant heureux» → «des enfants heureux»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ce sont des filles ___.» (charmant)", "choices": ["charmants", "charmante", "charmantes"], "answer": "charmantes", "right": "Bravo ! Feminine (+e) and plural (+s): «charmantes».", "wrong": "«filles» is feminine AND plural, so we add both «-e» and «-s»: «des filles charmantes»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Adjectives ending in «-eux» become «-euse» in the feminine: «heureux» → «heureuse». So in the feminine plural: «heureuses»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elles sont ___ de partir en vacances.» (heureux)", "choices": ["heureux", "heureuses", "heureuxes"], "answer": "heureuses", "right": "Parfait ! «elles» = feminine plural, so «-eux» → «-euse» + «s» = «heureuses».", "wrong": "«elles» is feminine plural. «heureux» → «heureuse» (feminine) → «heureuses» (plural). «heureuxes» doesn't exist."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now, where does the adjective go? Usually AFTER the noun, the opposite of English: «un film américain» (an American film), «un sofa bleu» (a blue sofa)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai regardé un film américain hier.", "en": "I watched an American movie yesterday.", "note": "Nationalities and colours always go after the noun."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick «BAGS»: adjectives of Beauty, Age, Goodness and Size usually go BEFORE the noun: «une belle maison», «un jeune homme», «un bon film», «une petite chambre»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'A big house': which is correct? («grand» = big)", "choices": ["une maison grande", "une grande maison", "une grande maisonne"], "answer": "une grande maison", "right": "Excellent ! «grand» is a Size adjective (the S of BAGS), so it goes before: «une grande maison».", "wrong": "Size adjectives like «grand» go before the noun (BAGS). And only the adjective changes, not the noun: «une grande maison»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom est beau mais Charlotte est encore plus ___.» (Tom is handsome but Charlotte is even more beautiful.)", "choices": ["beau", "belle", "beaue"], "answer": "belle", "right": "Super ! «beau» has a special feminine: «belle».", "wrong": "«beau» is irregular: its feminine is «belle» (not «beaue»). Charlotte is a woman, so: «encore plus belle»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«bon» doubles its «n» in the feminine: «Cette crêpe est très bonne.» Same for «ancien» → «ancienne» and «gros» → «grosse» (double «s»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: add «-e» for feminine and «-s» for plural; watch special endings («-eux» → «-euse», «beau» → «belle», «bon» → «bonne»); most adjectives go after the noun, except BAGS adjectives."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: They are pretty. (talking about girls: «elles»)", "answer": "Elles sont jolies.", "accept": ["elles sont jolies", "elles sont jolies."], "hint": "«Elles sont joli…»: feminine + plural!", "right": "Bravo ! «jolies»: «-e» for feminine and «-s» for plural.", "wrong": "With «elles» (feminine plural), «joli» takes «-e» and «-s»: «Elles sont jolies.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: I am in a small bedroom with a blue sofa.", "fr": "Je suis dans une petite chambre avec un sofa bleu."}]}, "13-4": {"stageId": "13-4", "title": "Comparing: more, less, as… as", "goal": "By the end you can compare people and things with plus… que, moins… que, aussi… que, and use meilleur and pire.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's practise comparing! The three patterns are «plus … que» (more … than), «moins … que» (less … than) and «aussi … que» (as … as). The adjective or adverb goes between the two words."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Tom runs faster than Marc. «Tom court ___ Marc.» (vite = fast)", "choices": ["plus vite que", "vite plus que", "plus que vite"], "answer": "plus vite que", "right": "Yes! «plus» + adverb + «que»: «plus vite que».", "wrong": "The word goes in the middle of the 'sandwich': «plus» + «vite» + «que»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Don't forget agreement: the adjective matches the person or thing you describe. «Emma est plus sérieuse que sa sœur.» Emma is a woman, so «sérieuse»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'This programme is as long as a film.' «Cette émission est aussi ___ qu'un film.» («émission» is feminine)", "choices": ["long", "longue", "longs"], "answer": "longue", "right": "Bravo ! «émission» is feminine, so «long» → «longue».", "wrong": "The adjective agrees with «émission», which is feminine: «long» → «longue»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«que» becomes «qu'» before a vowel: «aussi grand qu'un parc», «qu'elle». It's easier to say!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "To compare with a person, use «moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux» after «que», not «je» or «tu»: «Emma est plus sérieuse que moi.» = Emma is more serious than me."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Arthur is older than you.'", "choices": ["Arthur est plus âgé que toi.", "Arthur est plus âgé que tu.", "Arthur est plus âgé que te."], "answer": "Arthur est plus âgé que toi.", "right": "Parfait ! After «que» we use the strong pronoun «toi».", "wrong": "After «que», use the strong pronouns: «moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux». So: «que toi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two special forms: 'better' is «meilleur(e)» (never «plus bon»), and 'worse' is «pire» (or «plus mauvais»). But «moins bon» and «aussi bon» are normal!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The strawberry tart is better than the galette. «La tarte aux fraises est ___ la galette.»", "choices": ["plus bonne que", "meilleure que", "meilleur que"], "answer": "meilleure que", "right": "Excellent ! 'better' = «meilleur», and with «tarte» (feminine) it becomes «meilleure».", "wrong": "We never say «plus bon(ne)»: 'better' is «meilleur». «tarte» is feminine, so add «-e»: «meilleure que»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The tomato soup is not as good as the onion soup. «La soupe à la tomate n'est pas ___ la soupe à l'oignon.»", "choices": ["aussi bonne que", "aussi meilleure que", "aussi bon que"], "answer": "aussi bonne que", "right": "Very good! For equality, «bon» stays regular: «aussi bonne que» (feminine for «soupe»).", "wrong": "«meilleur» only replaces «plus bon». For 'as good as', use «aussi bon que», and make it feminine for «soupe»: «aussi bonne que»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Sa situation est pire que la mienne.", "en": "His situation is worse than mine.", "note": "You can also say «plus mauvaise que la mienne»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "It works with adverbs of frequency too: «Vous allez à la piscine moins souvent que nous.» = You go to the pool less often than us."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «plus / moins / aussi» + adjective or adverb + «que»; agree the adjective; use «moi, toi…» after «que»; «bon» → «meilleur», «mauvais» → «pire»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The blue car is less fast than the red car.", "answer": "La voiture bleue est moins rapide que la voiture rouge.", "accept": ["la voiture bleue est moins rapide que la voiture rouge", "la voiture bleue est moins rapide que la rouge", "la voiture bleue est moins rapide que la voiture rouge."], "hint": "«La voiture bleue est moins …»", "right": "Bravo ! «moins rapide que» = less fast than. And «bleue» agrees with «voiture».", "wrong": "'less … than' = «moins … que». Colours go after the noun and agree: «La voiture bleue est moins rapide que la voiture rouge.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Compare yourself with a friend out loud, for example: Emma is more serious than me.", "fr": "Emma est plus sérieuse que moi."}]}, "13-5": {"stageId": "13-5", "title": "Tout, toute, tous, toutes", "goal": "By the end you can say 'all', 'every' and 'the whole' with the correct form of «tout».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today's little word is «tout»: it means 'all', 'every' or 'the whole'. It has four forms: «tout» (masc. sing.), «toute» (fem. sing.), «tous» (masc. plural) and «toutes» (fem. plural)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«les jours» = the days. What do you think «tous les jours» means?", "choices": ["every day", "one day", "the whole night"], "answer": "every day", "right": "Exactly! «tous les jours» = 'all the days' = every day. Very useful!", "wrong": "«tous les jours» is literally 'all the days', which means 'every day'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«tout» agrees with the noun that follows: «tout le gâteau» (the whole cake), «toute la ville» (the whole town), «tous les matins» (every morning), «toutes les filles» (all the girls)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les enfants ont mangé tout le gâteau.", "en": "The children ate the whole cake.", "note": "«gâteau» is masculine singular → «tout»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous avons visité ___ la ville.» (We visited the whole town.)", "choices": ["tout", "toute", "toutes"], "answer": "toute", "right": "Yes! «la ville» is feminine singular, so «toute».", "wrong": "Look at «la ville»: feminine singular. So «tout» takes an «-e»: «toute la ville»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation: «tout» and «tous» (before a noun) both sound like 'too'. «toute» and «toutes» sound like 'toot', because you hear the «t»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je prends le métro ___ les jours.»", "choices": ["tous", "toutes", "tout"], "answer": "tous", "right": "Bravo ! «les jours» is masculine plural → «tous».", "wrong": "«jours» is masculine plural, so we need «tous» («toutes» is for feminine plural)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les enfants ont mangé ___ les brioches.» («brioche» is feminine)", "choices": ["tous", "toutes", "toute"], "answer": "toutes", "right": "Parfait ! Feminine plural → «toutes».", "wrong": "«les brioches» is feminine AND plural, so we need «toutes»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A very common expression: «tout le monde» = everybody (literally 'all the world'). Careful, the verb is singular: «Tout le monde est là.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Everybody is here.' Which is correct?", "choices": ["Tout le monde est là.", "Tous le monde sont là.", "Toute le monde est là."], "answer": "Tout le monde est là.", "right": "Super ! «le monde» is masculine singular, so «tout le monde», with a singular verb: «est».", "wrong": "«le monde» is masculine singular → «tout le monde», and the verb stays singular: «est». So: «Tout le monde est là.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «tout» + masculine singular, «toute» + feminine singular, «tous» + masculine plural, «toutes» + feminine plural. Look at the noun after it to choose!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I run every morning. («courir» = to run, «le matin» = the morning)", "answer": "Je cours tous les matins.", "accept": ["je cours tous les matins", "je cours tous les matins."], "hint": "«Je cours … les matins.»", "right": "Excellent ! «matins» is masculine plural → «tous les matins».", "wrong": "'every morning' = «tous les matins» («matins» is masculine plural). So: «Je cours tous les matins.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: all the boys and all the girls in my class.", "fr": "Tous les garçons et toutes les filles de ma classe."}]}, "14-1": {"stageId": "14-1", "title": "My, your, his… and this, that, these", "goal": "By the end you can use possessive adjectives (mon, ma, mes…), demonstrative adjectives (ce, cet, cette, ces) and say 'my father's car' in French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today: two families of little words. Possessives like «mon, ma, mes» (my) and demonstratives like «ce, cette, ces» (this, that, these). Both change to match the noun that follows."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For 'my': «mon» + masculine noun, «ma» + feminine noun, «mes» + plural noun. Same pattern for 'your' (informal): «ton, ta, tes», and 'his/her': «son, sa, ses»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est mon père et ma mère.", "en": "It's my father and my mother.", "note": "«père» is masculine → «mon»; «mère» is feminine → «ma»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Here's a puzzle: «C'est son manteau.» Emma owns the coat, and «manteau» is masculine. What does the sentence mean?", "choices": ["It's her coat.", "It's his coat.", "It's my coat."], "answer": "It's her coat.", "right": "Yes! «son» can mean 'his' OR 'her'. It agrees with «manteau» (masculine), not with Emma.", "wrong": "«son» means 'his' or 'her'. The form depends on the noun («manteau» is masculine), not on the owner. Emma owns it, so: 'It's her coat.'"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Big difference from English: the possessive matches the THING, not the owner. «sa voiture» can be 'his car' or 'her car', because «voiture» is feminine."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For 'our', 'your' (formal/plural) and 'their', there are only two forms: «notre / nos», «votre / vos», «leur / leurs». The singular form works for both masculine and feminine nouns: «notre voiture», «notre village»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Ce soir, mon frère et moi allons chez ___ grands-parents.' (our)", "choices": ["notre", "nos", "leurs"], "answer": "nos", "right": "Parfait ! «grands-parents» is plural, so 'our' = «nos».", "wrong": "«grands-parents» is plural, so we need the plural 'our': «nos». («notre» is for one thing, «leurs» means 'their'.)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "French has no «'s». To say 'my father's car', flip it around with «de»: «la voiture de mon père» (literally 'the car of my father')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'Paul's bottle'?", "choices": ["la Paul's bouteille", "la bouteille de Paul", "de Paul la bouteille"], "answer": "la bouteille de Paul", "right": "Bravo ! Thing first, then «de» + owner: «la bouteille de Paul».", "wrong": "French has no «'s». Say the thing first, then «de» + the owner: «la bouteille de Paul»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now 'this/that': «ce» + masculine noun («ce livre»), «cette» + feminine noun («cette chanson»), «ces» + any plural noun («ces fleurs»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Before a masculine noun starting with a vowel or a silent «h», «ce» becomes «cet», so it's easier to say: «cet arbre», «cet homme»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ enfant est petit.» (This child is small.)", "choices": ["Ce", "Cet", "Cette"], "answer": "Cet", "right": "Excellent ! «enfant» is masculine and starts with a vowel, so «cet».", "wrong": "«enfant» is masculine but starts with a vowel, so «ce» becomes «cet»: «Cet enfant est petit.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ chaise est confortable.» (That chair is comfortable.)", "choices": ["Ce", "Cette", "Ces"], "answer": "Cette", "right": "Super ! «chaise» is feminine singular → «cette».", "wrong": "«chaise» is feminine singular, so we use «cette». «ces» is only for plurals."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «mon/ma/mes, ton/ta/tes, son/sa/ses, notre/nos, votre/vos, leur/leurs» agree with the thing owned; «ce/cet/cette/ces» = this/that/these/those; and «de» replaces «'s»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: These pens are blue. («un stylo» = a pen)", "answer": "Ces stylos sont bleus.", "accept": ["ces stylos sont bleus", "ces stylos sont bleus."], "hint": "Plural → «Ces …», and don't forget the «-s» on «bleu».", "right": "Bravo ! «ces» for plural, and «bleus» agrees with «stylos».", "wrong": "For plural nouns, 'these' = «ces», and the colour agrees: «Ces stylos sont bleus.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: this evening, my brother and I are going to our grandparents' to celebrate their wedding anniversary.", "fr": "Ce soir, mon frère et moi allons chez nos grands-parents pour célébrer leur anniversaire de mariage."}]}, "14-2": {"stageId": "14-2", "title": "Ce, cet, cette, ces: pointing at things", "goal": "By the end you can choose the right word for 'this', 'that', 'these' and 'those' every time.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's practise pointing at things! «ce, cet, cette, ces» replace «le, la, les» when you mean 'this/that' or 'these/those'. They match the noun's gender and number."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "ce stylo, cet arbre, cette fleur, ces fleurs", "en": "this pen, this tree, this flower, these flowers"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ pommes sont délicieuses.» (These apples are delicious.)", "choices": ["Ce", "Cette", "Ces"], "answer": "Ces", "right": "Yes! «pommes» is plural, so «ces», whatever the gender.", "wrong": "Look at the «-s» on «pommes»: it's plural. For all plural nouns, use «ces»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'aime beaucoup les livres de ___ écrivain.» (I really like this writer's books.)", "choices": ["ce", "cet", "cette"], "answer": "cet", "right": "Bravo ! «écrivain» is masculine and starts with a vowel, so «cet».", "wrong": "«écrivain» is masculine but starts with a vowel «é». «ce écrivain» is hard to say, so French uses «cet écrivain»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«cet» and «cette» sound exactly the same: 'set'. Only the spelling shows the gender, so think about it when you write!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«On dit que ___ hôtel est très bien.» (They say this hotel is very good.)", "choices": ["ce", "cet", "cette"], "answer": "cet", "right": "Parfait ! The «h» of «hôtel» is silent, so it acts like a vowel: «cet hôtel».", "wrong": "«hôtel» is masculine and its «h» is silent, so it behaves like a vowel: «cet hôtel»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je connais bien ___ famille.» (I know this family well.)", "choices": ["ce", "cet", "cette"], "answer": "cette", "right": "Très bien ! «famille» is feminine → «cette».", "wrong": "«famille» is feminine singular, so we use «cette»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«ce» is also very handy for time: «ce matin» (this morning), «ce soir» (this evening), «cette semaine» (this week), «cet été» (this summer)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you ask 'What are you doing this evening?'", "choices": ["Qu'est-ce que tu fais cette soir ?", "Qu'est-ce que tu fais ce soir ?", "Qu'est-ce que tu fais cet soir ?"], "answer": "Qu'est-ce que tu fais ce soir ?", "right": "Super ! «soir» is masculine and starts with a consonant → «ce soir».", "wrong": "«soir» is masculine and starts with «s», a consonant, so just «ce»: «ce soir»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Avez-vous vu ___ homme ?» (Have you seen this man?)", "choices": ["ce", "cet", "ces"], "answer": "cet", "right": "Excellent ! «homme» is masculine with a silent «h» → «cet homme».", "wrong": "«homme» is masculine singular and its «h» is silent, so: «cet homme»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ce» + masculine, «cet» + masculine starting with a vowel or silent «h», «cette» + feminine, «ces» + all plurals."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: These children are noisy! («turbulent» = noisy, unruly)", "answer": "Ces enfants sont turbulents !", "accept": ["ces enfants sont turbulents", "ces enfants sont turbulents !", "ces enfants sont turbulents!"], "hint": "Plural: «Ces enfants …»", "right": "Bravo ! «ces» for plural, and listen: you link the «s» to the vowel, 'say-zan-fan'.", "wrong": "«enfants» is plural, so it's «ces» (never «cets»): «Ces enfants sont turbulents !»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: can you give me this newspaper, please?", "fr": "Est-ce que tu peux me donner ce journal, s'il te plaît ?"}]}, "14-3": {"stageId": "14-3", "title": "This and that in real life", "goal": "By the end you can use ce, cet, cette, ces naturally in everyday sentences and tell them apart from «c'est».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In this lesson we use «ce, cet, cette, ces» in real sentences. They replace the article to point at one specific thing: «le livre» (the book) → «ce livre» (this book)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je trouve ce livre très intéressant.", "en": "I find this book very interesting."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How would you say 'I find these books very interesting'?", "choices": ["Je trouve ce livres très intéressants.", "Je trouve ces livres très intéressants.", "Je trouve cettes livres très intéressants."], "answer": "Je trouve ces livres très intéressants.", "right": "Yes! Plural → «ces», and «intéressants» also takes an «-s».", "wrong": "For plural nouns, 'these' is always «ces». «cettes» doesn't exist. So: «ces livres»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Don't confuse «ce» in «c'est» with «ce livre». In «c'est», «ce» just means 'it/that' and goes with the verb: «C'est beau.» = It's beautiful."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In which sentence is «ce» used to point at a noun (= 'this')?", "choices": ["C'est mon ami.", "Ce chat est noir.", "C'est super !"], "answer": "Ce chat est noir.", "right": "Exactly! «ce chat» = this cat. In «c'est», «ce» just means 'it/that'.", "wrong": "In «c'est», «ce» means 'it/that' and goes with «est». Only «Ce chat est noir.» points at a noun: 'This cat is black.'"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Remember the agreement in a full sentence: «Il a porté cette veste toute la journée.» (He wore this jacket all day.) «veste» is feminine, so «cette»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'This man is wearing this blue tie.' («une cravate» = a tie)", "choices": ["Cet homme porte cette cravate bleue.", "Ce homme porte cet cravate bleue.", "Cette homme porte ce cravate bleu."], "answer": "Cet homme porte cette cravate bleue.", "right": "Parfait ! «cet» for «homme» (silent h), «cette» for «cravate», and «bleue» agrees with «cravate».", "wrong": "«homme» is masculine with a silent «h» → «cet». «cravate» is feminine → «cette», and the colour agrees: «bleue»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«ce» can mean 'this' or 'that'. To insist, add «-ci» (here) or «-là» (there): «ce livre-ci» = this book here, «ce livre-là» = that book there."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a shop, you point at a jumper far away. Which do you say for 'that jumper there'? («un pull» = a jumper)", "choices": ["ce pull-ci", "ce pull-là", "cette pull-là"], "answer": "ce pull-là", "right": "Bravo ! «-là» = there, so «ce pull-là» = that jumper there.", "wrong": "«-ci» is for 'here', «-là» is for 'there'. «pull» is masculine, so: «ce pull-là»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'This flower smells nice.'", "choices": ["Ce fleur sent bon.", "Cette fleur sent bon.", "Cet fleur sent bon."], "answer": "Cette fleur sent bon.", "right": "Super ! «fleur» is feminine → «cette». And «sent bon» = smells nice.", "wrong": "«fleur» is feminine, so we use «cette fleur». «cet» is only for masculine nouns starting with a vowel or silent h."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ce, cet, cette, ces» point at a specific noun; «c'est» is something different (it is); add «-ci / -là» for 'this here / that there'."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: These trees are very big. («un arbre» = a tree)", "answer": "Ces arbres sont très grands.", "accept": ["ces arbres sont très grands", "ces arbres sont tres grands", "ces arbres sont très grands."], "hint": "«Ces arbres sont très …»", "right": "Excellent ! «ces arbres» (you link: 'say-zarbr'), and «grands» agrees.", "wrong": "Plural → «ces», and the adjective takes «-s»: «Ces arbres sont très grands.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: the cat has been in this tree since this morning.", "fr": "Le chat est sur cet arbre depuis ce matin."}]}, "14-4": {"stageId": "14-4", "title": "Possessives in context: mon, ma, mes and friends", "goal": "By the end you can choose the right possessive adjective, even for tricky cases like «mon école» and «son frère et sa sœur».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's practise possessives. The golden rule: in French, the possessive agrees with the noun that follows, NOT with the owner. «Il mange sa pomme.» = He is eating his apple («pomme» is feminine)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Paul a une table. C'est ___ table.» Paul is a man, but «table» is feminine. Which word?", "choices": ["son", "sa", "ses"], "answer": "sa", "right": "Yes! «table» is feminine → «sa table», even if Paul is a man. It means 'his table'.", "wrong": "Forget the owner! Look at the noun: «table» is feminine singular → «sa». «sa table» = his table."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Here is the full family: «mon/ma/mes» (my), «ton/ta/tes» (your), «son/sa/ses» (his/her), «notre/nos» (our), «votre/vos» (your, formal or plural), «leur/leurs» (their)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ frère et ___ sœur sont grands.» (his / his)", "choices": ["Son … son", "Son … sa", "Sa … sa"], "answer": "Son … sa", "right": "Bravo ! «frère» is masculine → «son», «sœur» is feminine → «sa».", "wrong": "Each noun chooses its own form: «frère» (masculine) → «son», «sœur» (feminine) → «sa»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Tricky one! Before a feminine noun starting with a vowel or silent «h», use «mon, ton, son» to make it easier to say: «mon école», «son amie», «son habitude»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ école est grande.» (My school is big.) «école» is feminine…", "choices": ["Ma", "Mon", "Mes"], "answer": "Mon", "right": "Excellent ! «école» starts with a vowel, so «ma» becomes «mon»: «mon école».", "wrong": "Even though «école» is feminine, it starts with a vowel, so we say «mon école» (not «ma école», which is hard to say)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Paul et Tom ont préparé ___ voyage.» (their trip)", "choices": ["leur", "leurs", "son"], "answer": "leur", "right": "Parfait ! There are two owners, but only one trip: «leur voyage».", "wrong": "'their' is «leur» for one thing and «leurs» for several things. One trip → «leur voyage»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In the plural, there's no masculine/feminine difference: «mes stylos», «mes pommes», «ses yeux» (his/her eyes)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ livres sont vieux.» (your, formal)", "choices": ["Votre", "Vos", "Tes"], "answer": "Vos", "right": "Très bien ! Formal 'your' + plural noun → «vos».", "wrong": "Formal 'your' is «votre» for one thing and «vos» for several. «livres» is plural → «vos». («tes» is informal.)"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ma sœur habite à Paris mais son fils habite à Londres.", "en": "My sister lives in Paris but her son lives in London.", "note": "«son fils» = her son: «fils» is masculine."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: look at the noun, not the owner; use «mon/ton/son» before a vowel, even for feminine nouns; plurals use «mes, tes, ses, nos, vos, leurs»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: My hat is in your car. (informal 'your'; «un chapeau», «une voiture»)", "answer": "Mon chapeau est dans ta voiture.", "accept": ["mon chapeau est dans ta voiture", "mon chapeau est dans ta voiture."], "hint": "«chapeau» is masculine, «voiture» is feminine.", "right": "Super ! «mon» for «chapeau» (masculine), «ta» for «voiture» (feminine).", "wrong": "«chapeau» is masculine → «mon», «voiture» is feminine → «ta»: «Mon chapeau est dans ta voiture.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: my uncle, his wife and his son are coming to my house.", "fr": "Mon oncle, sa femme et son fils vont venir chez moi."}]}, "14-5": {"stageId": "14-5", "title": "Whose is it? Possession with «de»", "goal": "By the end you can say 'Camille's cat' and 'Camille's sister's cat' in French, and swap them for son/sa/ses.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In English we say 'Camille's cat'. French has no «'s», so we turn the phrase around and use «de»: «le chat de Camille» (the cat of Camille)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'It's Paul's bottle'?", "choices": ["C'est la bouteille de Paul.", "C'est Paul's bouteille.", "C'est de Paul bouteille."], "answer": "C'est la bouteille de Paul.", "right": "Yes! The thing first, then «de» + the owner.", "wrong": "Turn it around: the thing first, then «de» + owner: «C'est la bouteille de Paul.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "With a name, use «de» alone: «le chat de Camille». With a normal noun, keep its little word: «le chat de la sœur de Camille» = Camille's sister's cat."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Remember: «de + le» becomes «du»: «la voiture du père de mon ami» = my friend's father's car. And «de + les» = «des»: «la maison des voisins» = the neighbours' house."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The teacher's book' («le professeur»):", "choices": ["le livre de le professeur", "le livre du professeur", "le livre de professeur"], "answer": "le livre du professeur", "right": "Bravo ! «de + le» always becomes «du».", "wrong": "We never say «de le»: it contracts to «du». So: «le livre du professeur»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now a trick to swap «de + owner» for a possessive: the first letter shows the owner («s-» = his/her), and the ending shows the thing (gender and number). «la bouteille de Paul» → «sa bouteille»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est le chat de Camille. → C'est son chat.", "en": "It's Camille's cat. → It's her cat.", "note": "«chat» is masculine → «son», even though Camille is a woman."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Replace with a possessive: «les clés de Marc» (Marc's keys) →", "choices": ["son clés", "sa clés", "ses clés"], "answer": "ses clés", "right": "Parfait ! «s-» for Marc (his), and «-es» because «clés» is plural.", "wrong": "«s-» shows the owner (his/her), and the ending matches the thing: «clés» is plural → «ses clés»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Replace with a possessive: «la maison de mes parents» (my parents' house) →", "choices": ["leur maison", "leurs maison", "sa maison"], "answer": "leur maison", "right": "Excellent ! Several owners → 'their' = «leur», and one house → no «-s».", "wrong": "My parents are two people, so 'their' → «leur». There's only one house, so no «-s»: «leur maison»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: thing + «de» + owner («le chat de Camille»), «de + le» = «du», «de + les» = «des», and you can swap it for «son / sa / ses / leur / leurs»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It's Camille's sister's cat.", "answer": "C'est le chat de la sœur de Camille.", "accept": ["c'est le chat de la sœur de camille", "c'est le chat de la soeur de camille", "c est le chat de la soeur de camille", "c'est le chat de la sœur de camille."], "hint": "Start with the cat: «C'est le chat de la …»", "right": "Bravo ! Thing → owner → owner's owner: «le chat de la sœur de Camille».", "wrong": "Turn the English around: the cat, of the sister, of Camille: «C'est le chat de la sœur de Camille.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: it's my father's car.", "fr": "C'est la voiture de mon père."}]}, "15-1": {"stageId": "15-1", "title": "The passé composé: talking about the past", "goal": "By the end you can say what you did yesterday, using «avoir» or «être» and a past participle.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we learn the main past tense in French: the «passé composé». It is used for actions that happened and are finished, like «j'ai joué» (I played)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mes enfants ont joué au parc.", "en": "My children played in the park.", "note": "Also: have played / did play."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "English has 'I ate', 'I have eaten' and 'I did eat'. How many of those can «j'ai mangé» mean?", "choices": ["Only 'I ate'", "'I ate', 'I have eaten' and 'I did eat'", "Only 'I have eaten'"], "answer": "'I ate', 'I have eaten' and 'I did eat'", "right": "Yes! French uses one form, the passé composé, for all three.", "wrong": "French uses just one form, «j'ai mangé», for all three English versions: 'I ate', 'I have eaten' and 'I did eat'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The recipe has 3 parts: subject + «avoir» or «être» in the present + past participle. Most verbs use «avoir»: «j'ai», «tu as», «il a», «nous avons», «vous avez», «ils ont»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai mangé une pomme.", "en": "I ate an apple.", "note": "«j'ai» (avoir) + «mangé» (past participle)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Past participles: -er verbs change to «-é» («jouer» → «joué»), and -ir verbs like «finir» drop the r («fini»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess the past participle of «choisir» (to choose).", "choices": ["choisé", "choisi", "choisu"], "answer": "choisi", "right": "Well done! «choisir» is like «finir»: drop the r → «choisi».", "wrong": "-ir verbs like «finir» just drop the r: «finir» → «fini», so «choisir» → «choisi»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Irregular verbs must be learnt by heart: «faire» → «fait», «prendre» → «pris», «boire» → «bu», «voir» → «vu», «avoir» → «eu», «être» → «été»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous avons ___ du café.» (boire, to drink)", "choices": ["boiré", "bu", "bois"], "answer": "bu", "right": "Perfect! «boire» is irregular: «nous avons bu» = we drank.", "wrong": "«boire» is irregular. Its past participle is «bu»: «nous avons bu». «bois» is the present tense."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Movement verbs use «être» instead: «aller», «venir», «partir», «arriver», «sortir», «rester», «tomber»… Remember them with DR & MRS VANDERTRAMP."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis allée au supermarché.", "en": "I went to the supermarket.", "note": "A woman is speaking, so «allée» takes an extra -e."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence is correct for 'They (women) went to the cinema'?", "choices": ["Elles ont allé au cinéma.", "Elles sont allées au cinéma.", "Elles sont allé au cinéma."], "answer": "Elles sont allées au cinéma.", "right": "Excellent! «aller» uses «être», and the participle agrees: feminine + plural = «-es».", "wrong": "«aller» is a movement verb, so it takes «être» («elles sont»). With «être», the participle agrees with the subject: «allées» (-e for feminine, -s for plural)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch out: «marcher» (to walk) feels like movement but uses «avoir»: «j'ai marché»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: subject + «avoir» (most verbs) or «être» (movement verbs) + participle. With «être», add -e / -s / -es to agree."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Charlotte ate with me.", "answer": "Charlotte a mangé avec moi.", "accept": ["charlotte a mangé avec moi", "charlotte a mange avec moi"], "hint": "Starts with «Charlotte a…»", "right": "Bravo! «manger» uses «avoir»: «elle a mangé».", "wrong": "«manger» uses «avoir»: Charlotte → «a», then «mangé»: «Charlotte a mangé avec moi.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Hier, je suis allé au parc et j'ai joué au foot.»", "fr": "Hier, je suis allé au parc et j'ai joué au foot."}]}, "15-2": {"stageId": "15-2", "title": "Saying what you did NOT do", "goal": "By the end you can make any passé composé sentence negative with «ne… pas».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "To say what you didn't do, put «ne… pas» around the auxiliary («avoir» or «être»), not around the participle."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je n'ai pas mangé.", "en": "I didn't eat. / I haven't eaten."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Where does «pas» go?", "choices": ["Je n'ai mangé pas.", "Je n'ai pas mangé.", "Je ne pas ai mangé."], "answer": "Je n'ai pas mangé.", "right": "Yes! «ne» and «pas» go around the auxiliary «ai», and the participle comes after.", "wrong": "The rule is ne + auxiliary + pas + participle: «Je n'ai pas mangé.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«ne» becomes «n'» before a vowel or h: «je n'ai pas», «il n'a pas», «nous n'avons pas». The same pattern works with «être»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je ne suis pas allé en France le mois dernier.", "en": "I didn't go to France last month.", "note": "ne + suis + pas + allé"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Make this negative: «Il est parti.»", "choices": ["Il n'est pas parti.", "Il ne est pas parti.", "Il n'est parti pas."], "answer": "Il n'est pas parti.", "right": "Great! «ne» becomes «n'» before «est», and «pas» comes before the participle.", "wrong": "«ne» becomes «n'» before a vowel, and «pas» goes right after the auxiliary: «Il n'est pas parti.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "After a negative, «des» becomes «de»: «J'ai mangé des bananes» → «Je n'ai pas mangé de bananes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je n'ai pas acheté ___ pommes.»", "choices": ["des", "de", "du"], "answer": "de", "right": "Correct! After «pas», «des» becomes «de».", "wrong": "After a negative, «des» (and «du», «de la») becomes «de»: «pas de pommes»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In casual speech, French people often drop «ne»: «J'ai pas mangé», «Je sais pas». It's fine in conversation, but write the full form."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«ne pas» + infinitive is used on signs and in instructions: «Ne pas fumer» (No smoking), «Ne pas déranger» (Do not disturb)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does a park sign saying «Ne pas marcher sur la pelouse» mean?", "choices": ["Do not walk on the grass", "Walk on the grass", "The grass is wet"], "answer": "Do not walk on the grass", "right": "Yes! «ne pas» + infinitive = an instruction not to do something.", "wrong": "«ne pas» + infinitive gives a 'do not' instruction: «ne pas marcher» = do not walk, «la pelouse» = the grass."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: ne (n') + auxiliary + pas + participle, and «des» → «de» after the negative."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I didn't understand the question.", "answer": "Je n'ai pas compris la question.", "accept": ["je n'ai pas compris la question", "je n’ai pas compris la question", "je nai pas compris la question"], "hint": "«comprendre» → «compris». Starts with «Je n'ai…»", "right": "Parfait! n' + ai + pas + compris.", "wrong": "Wrap «ne… pas» around «ai»: «Je n'ai pas compris la question.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je n'ai pas mangé de bananes.»", "fr": "Je n'ai pas mangé de bananes."}]}, "15-3": {"stageId": "15-3", "title": "-er verbs in the passé composé", "goal": "By the end you can put any regular -er verb into the passé composé.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "-er verbs are the biggest verb family. To make the past participle, drop «-er» and add «-é»: «marcher» → «marché»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess the past participle of «parler» (to speak).", "choices": ["parlé", "parli", "parlu"], "answer": "parlé", "right": "Yes! Drop -er, add -é → «parlé».", "wrong": "For -er verbs, drop -er and add -é: «parler» → «parlé»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai joué au tennis hier.", "en": "I played tennis yesterday."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Most -er verbs use «avoir»: «j'ai», «tu as», «il/elle/on a», «nous avons», «vous avez», «ils/elles ont»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom et moi ___ parlé à notre professeur.»", "choices": ["ont", "avons", "avez"], "answer": "avons", "right": "Well done! «Tom et moi» = «nous», so «nous avons parlé».", "wrong": "«Tom et moi» (Tom and I) means «nous», and «avoir» with «nous» is «avons»: «avons parlé»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu as dévoré le chocolat.", "en": "You devoured the chocolate."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«parlé», «parler» and «parlez» all sound the same ('par-lay'). Only the spelling changes, so check which one you need when you write."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A few -er verbs are movement verbs and use «être»: «rentrer», «rester», «arriver», «tomber», «entrer»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je ___ rentrée de Paris hier soir.»", "choices": ["ai", "suis", "est"], "answer": "suis", "right": "Exactly! «rentrer» is a movement verb, so it takes «être»: «je suis rentrée».", "wrong": "«rentrer» (to come back) is a movement verb, so it uses «être». With «je», that's «suis»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous sommes restées à la maison hier soir.", "en": "We stayed at home last night.", "note": "Only women, so «-es»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A group of men and women says 'We stayed at home'. Which is right?", "choices": ["Nous sommes restés à la maison.", "Nous sommes restées à la maison.", "Nous avons resté à la maison."], "answer": "Nous sommes restés à la maison.", "right": "Bravo! A mixed group takes the masculine plural, «-s».", "wrong": "«rester» uses «être». A mixed group (men and women) takes the masculine plural: «restés». «restées» is for women only."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: -er → -é, usually with «avoir». Movement verbs take «être» and agree with the subject."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: She liked the film a lot.", "answer": "Elle a beaucoup aimé le film.", "accept": ["elle a beaucoup aimé le film", "elle a beaucoup aime le film"], "hint": "«beaucoup» goes between «a» and «aimé».", "right": "Superb! Short adverbs like «beaucoup» go between the auxiliary and the participle.", "wrong": "«beaucoup» goes between the auxiliary and the participle: «Elle a beaucoup aimé le film.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Charlotte a cuisiné pour nous hier.»", "fr": "Charlotte a cuisiné pour nous hier."}]}, "15-4": {"stageId": "15-4", "title": "-ir verbs (group 2) in the passé composé", "goal": "By the end you can use verbs like «finir» and «choisir» in the past.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Group 2 verbs end in -ir and have «-iss-» in the plural present («nous finissons»). For the past participle, just drop the r."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the past participle of «finir»?", "choices": ["finé", "fini", "finu"], "answer": "fini", "right": "Yes! «finir» minus the r = «fini».", "wrong": "For group 2 verbs, just drop the final r: «finir» → «fini»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous avons choisi un film.", "en": "We chose a film."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le professeur a puni son élève.", "en": "The teacher punished his pupil.", "note": "«punir» → «puni»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai ___ le seau.» (remplir, to fill)", "choices": ["remplé", "rempli", "remplit"], "answer": "rempli", "right": "Correct! «remplir» → «rempli».", "wrong": "Drop the r: «remplir» → «rempli». «remplit» is the present tense («il remplit»), and -é is only for -er verbs."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«il finit» (he finishes) and «il a fini» (he finished) sound almost the same. The little «a» is what makes it past!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Careful: not every -ir verb is group 2. «dormir», «sortir», «ouvrir», «offrir», «mourir» and «venir» are irregular (group 3)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is the past participle of «ouvrir» (to open)?", "choices": ["ouvri", "ouvert", "ouvré"], "answer": "ouvert", "right": "Well spotted! «ouvrir» is irregular: «j'ai ouvert la porte».", "wrong": "«ouvrir» is not a group 2 verb. Its participle is irregular: «ouvert» («J'ai ouvert la porte»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Negatives work the usual way: «Charlotte n'a pas fini son assiette» (Charlotte didn't finish her plate)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous ___ depuis l'année dernière.» (grossir, to put on weight)", "choices": ["avez grossi", "êtes grossi", "avez grossé"], "answer": "avez grossi", "right": "Great! Group 2 verbs use «avoir», and «grossir» → «grossi».", "wrong": "Group 2 verbs take «avoir» («vous avez»), and the participle drops the r: «grossi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: group 2 -ir verbs: drop the r (fini, choisi, puni), always with «avoir»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Have you fed the cat this morning? (use «tu», «nourrir»)", "answer": "Tu as nourri le chat ce matin ?", "accept": ["tu as nourri le chat ce matin ?", "tu as nourri le chat ce matin?", "tu as nourri le chat ce matin"], "hint": "«nourrir» → «nourri». Starts with «Tu as…»", "right": "Bravo! «tu as nourri», and the rising voice makes it a question.", "wrong": "«nourrir» → «nourri», with «avoir»: «Tu as nourri le chat ce matin ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'ai fini mes devoirs et j'ai choisi un film.»", "fr": "J'ai fini mes devoirs et j'ai choisi un film."}]}, "15-5": {"stageId": "15-5", "title": "Irregular verbs (group 3) in the passé composé", "goal": "By the end you know the most common irregular past participles and can use them.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Group 3 verbs are irregular: there's no single rule for their past participles. But many of them fall into small families."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Families: -u: «bu», «vu», «eu», «voulu», «pu», «rendu», «descendu». -is: «pris», «appris», «compris», «mis». -it: «fait», «dit»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the past participle of «voir» (to see)?", "choices": ["voi", "vu", "vit"], "answer": "vu", "right": "Yes! «voir» → «vu», part of the -u family.", "wrong": "«voir» belongs to the -u family: «vu» («j'ai vu»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai vu le dernier film de Steven Spielberg hier.", "en": "I saw the latest Steven Spielberg film yesterday."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the past participle of «prendre» (to take)?", "choices": ["prendu", "pris", "prit"], "answer": "pris", "right": "Correct! «prendre» → «pris», like «apprendre» → «appris».", "wrong": "«prendre» and its relatives end in -is: «pris», «appris», «compris»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ils ont eu de la chance.", "en": "They were lucky.", "note": "«eu» (from avoir) sounds like «u»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some irregular verbs are movement verbs, so they use «être»: «aller» → «allé», «venir» → «venu», «partir» → «parti», «sortir» → «sorti», «descendre» → «descendu»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom et moi ___ à Paris le mois dernier.»", "choices": ["avons allé", "sommes allés", "sommes allé"], "answer": "sommes allés", "right": "Excellent! «aller» uses «être», and «nous» (plural) adds -s: «allés».", "wrong": "«aller» is a movement verb, so it uses «être»: «nous sommes». The participle agrees with the plural subject: «allés»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai ouvert la porte.", "en": "I opened the door."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il ___ une mauvaise nouvelle.» (recevoir, to receive)", "choices": ["a reçu", "a recevu", "est reçu"], "answer": "a reçu", "right": "Bravo! «recevoir» → «reçu», with «avoir».", "wrong": "«recevoir» is not a movement verb, so it uses «avoir». Its participle is «reçu» (note the ç)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: learn irregular participles by family (-u, -is, -it). Most take «avoir»; movement verbs take «être»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: You didn't believe in him. (use «tu», «croire»)", "answer": "Tu n'as pas cru en lui.", "accept": ["tu n'as pas cru en lui", "tu n’as pas cru en lui", "tu nas pas cru en lui"], "hint": "«croire» → «cru». Starts with «Tu n'as…»", "right": "Parfait! n' + as + pas + cru.", "wrong": "«croire» → «cru», and the negative goes around «as»: «Tu n'as pas cru en lui.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Alice et Tom ont fait un gâteau ensemble.»", "fr": "Alice et Tom ont fait un gâteau ensemble."}]}, "16-1": {"stageId": "16-1", "title": "Asking questions in French", "goal": "By the end you can ask yes/no questions three ways and use où, quand, comment, pourquoi and combien.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French has no 'do' or 'did' for questions. Instead there are 3 ways to ask: rising intonation, «est-ce que», or inversion."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu aimes les bananes ?", "en": "Do you like bananas?", "note": "Just make your voice go up at the end. Casual."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How would you casually ask 'Do you play the guitar?'", "choices": ["Fais-tu jouer la guitare ?", "Tu joues de la guitare ?", "Tu fais joues de la guitare ?"], "answer": "Tu joues de la guitare ?", "right": "Yes! There's no 'do' in French. Keep the sentence and raise your voice.", "wrong": "Don't translate 'do'. French has no helper verb for questions: «Tu joues de la guitare ?» with rising intonation."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Est-ce que vous allez à Paris ?", "en": "Are you going to Paris?", "note": "«Est-ce que» + normal word order. Works everywhere."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Allez-vous à Paris ?", "en": "Are you going to Paris?", "note": "Inversion: verb-hyphen-subject. More formal."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which question uses inversion?", "choices": ["Vous allez à Paris ?", "Est-ce que vous allez à Paris ?", "Allez-vous à Paris ?"], "answer": "Allez-vous à Paris ?", "right": "Correct! The verb comes before the subject, joined by a hyphen.", "wrong": "Inversion means swapping the verb and the subject: «Allez-vous…», with a hyphen in between."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Question words: «où» (where), «quand» (when), «comment» (how), «pourquoi» (why), «combien» (how much), «combien de» (how many)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «pourquoi» mean?", "choices": ["Where", "Why", "When"], "answer": "Why", "right": "Yes! «pourquoi» = why, literally 'for what'.", "wrong": "«pourquoi» means why («pour» = for, «quoi» = what). Where = «où», when = «quand»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Où est-ce que tu vas ? / Où vas-tu ? / Tu vas où ?", "en": "Where are you going?", "note": "Neutral / formal / informal"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "When the verb ends in a vowel before «il», «elle» or «on», add «-t-» to make it easier to say: «Combien y a-t-il de personnes ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is correct for 'Am I eating with you?'", "choices": ["Mange-je avec toi ?", "Est-ce que je mange avec toi ?", "Je mange-je avec toi ?"], "answer": "Est-ce que je mange avec toi ?", "right": "Well done! Inversion with «je» is avoided, so use «est-ce que».", "wrong": "Don't invert with «je» (only «ai-je», «suis-je», «dois-je», «puis-je» work). Use «est-ce que»: «Est-ce que je mange avec toi ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: intonation (casual), «est-ce que» (neutral), inversion (formal), plus a question word at the start or, informally, at the end."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French using «est-ce que»: Where is he?", "answer": "Où est-ce qu'il est ?", "accept": ["où est-ce qu'il est ?", "où est-ce qu'il est", "ou est-ce qu'il est", "où est-ce qu’il est ?", "où est ce qu'il est"], "hint": "«que» becomes «qu'» before «il».", "right": "Parfait! «Où» + «est-ce qu'» + «il est».", "wrong": "Question word + «est-ce que» + normal order, with «que» → «qu'» before a vowel: «Où est-ce qu'il est ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quand allez-vous en France et comment est-ce que vous allez voyager ?»", "fr": "Quand allez-vous en France et comment est-ce que vous allez voyager ?"}]}, "16-2": {"stageId": "16-2", "title": "The magic question starter: «est-ce que»", "goal": "By the end you can turn any sentence into a question with «est-ce que».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Est-ce que» has no translation: it just signals a question. Put it in front of a normal sentence and keep the word order."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu es français.» How do you make it a question?", "choices": ["Est-ce que tu es français ?", "Est-ce tu es français ?", "Est-ce que es-tu français ?"], "answer": "Est-ce que tu es français ?", "right": "Yes! «Est-ce que» + the unchanged sentence.", "wrong": "You need the full «est-ce que», and the word order doesn't change: «Est-ce que tu es français ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Est-ce que tu viens au cinéma avec nous ce soir ?", "en": "Are you coming to the cinema with us tonight?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Before a vowel or h, «que» becomes «qu'»: «Est-ce qu'il…», «Est-ce qu'elle…», «Est-ce qu'on…»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ elles ont fini leurs devoirs ?»", "choices": ["Est-ce que", "Est-ce qu'", "Est-que"], "answer": "Est-ce qu'", "right": "Correct! «elles» starts with a vowel, so «que» → «qu'».", "wrong": "«elles» starts with a vowel, so «que» shortens to «qu'»: «Est-ce qu'elles ont fini… ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Est-ce que les enfants sont allés à la piscine hier ?", "en": "Did the children go to the swimming pool yesterday?", "note": "Works in the passé composé too."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Say «est-ce que» as one quick chunk: 'ess-kuh'. It's very common in everyday French and never sounds wrong."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Translate: Do you have a brother?", "choices": ["Est-ce que tu as un frère ?", "Est-ce que tu fais avoir un frère ?", "Est-ce que as-tu un frère ?"], "answer": "Est-ce que tu as un frère ?", "right": "Bravo! No 'do', no inversion, just «est-ce que» + «tu as».", "wrong": "Don't translate 'do', and don't invert after «est-ce que»: «Est-ce que tu as un frère ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Est-ce que» + normal sentence + ? It's neutral, so it fits anywhere."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French with «est-ce que»: Have you eaten your vegetables? (tu)", "answer": "Est-ce que tu as mangé tes légumes ?", "accept": ["est-ce que tu as mangé tes légumes ?", "est-ce que tu as mangé tes légumes", "est-ce que tu as mange tes legumes", "est ce que tu as mangé tes légumes"], "hint": "«tu as mangé» = you have eaten.", "right": "Excellent! «Est-ce que» + passé composé.", "wrong": "Put «Est-ce que» before the normal sentence «tu as mangé tes légumes»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Est-ce que vous mangez au restaurant aujourd'hui ?»", "fr": "Est-ce que vous mangez au restaurant aujourd'hui ?"}]}, "16-3": {"stageId": "16-3", "title": "Practice: questions with «est-ce que»", "goal": "By the end you can turn statements, present or past, into «est-ce que» questions quickly.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's practise! The recipe: «Est-ce que» + unchanged sentence + ?, with «qu'» before a vowel."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Arthur est allé au cinéma. → Est-ce qu'Arthur est allé au cinéma ?", "en": "Did Arthur go to the cinema?", "note": "«qu'» before a name starting with a vowel too."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Make a question: «Ils jouent au football.»", "choices": ["Est-ce qu'ils jouent au football ?", "Est-ce que ils jouent au football ?", "Est-ce qu'ils jouent-ils au football ?"], "answer": "Est-ce qu'ils jouent au football ?", "right": "Yes! «qu'» before «ils», and no other change.", "wrong": "«que» → «qu'» before «ils», and don't add inversion: «Est-ce qu'ils jouent au football ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Translate: Is he tall?", "choices": ["Est-ce qu'il est grand ?", "Est-ce qu'est-il grand ?", "Est-ce que il grand ?"], "answer": "Est-ce qu'il est grand ?", "right": "Great! «Est-ce qu'» + «il est grand».", "wrong": "Keep the full sentence «il est grand» and put «Est-ce qu'» in front."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English often drops 'some', but French needs it: 'Do you eat meat?' → «de la viande»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Translate: Do you eat meat? (vous)", "choices": ["Est-ce que vous mangez viande ?", "Est-ce que vous mangez de la viande ?", "Est-ce que vous mangez des viande ?"], "answer": "Est-ce que vous mangez de la viande ?", "right": "Correct! French needs «de la» (some) before «viande».", "wrong": "French needs a 'some' word: «viande» is feminine and singular, so it takes «de la viande»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Est-ce que tu as de l'argent sur toi ?", "en": "Have you got any money on you?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "For past questions, just keep the passé composé after «est-ce que», with the right auxiliary."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Translate: Did you go to Paris? (tu)", "choices": ["Est-ce que tu as allé à Paris ?", "Est-ce que tu es allé à Paris ?", "Est-ce que tu vas à Paris ?"], "answer": "Est-ce que tu es allé à Paris ?", "right": "Bravo! «aller» uses «être»: «tu es allé».", "wrong": "«aller» is a movement verb, so it uses «être»: «tu es allé». «tu vas» is the present."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «est-ce que» never changes the sentence. Just watch «qu'» and your verb tenses."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Make it a question with «est-ce que»: «Tom et Sophie sont partis en vacances.»", "answer": "Est-ce que Tom et Sophie sont partis en vacances ?", "accept": ["est-ce que tom et sophie sont partis en vacances ?", "est-ce que tom et sophie sont partis en vacances", "est ce que tom et sophie sont partis en vacances"], "hint": "Just add «Est-ce que» in front.", "right": "Perfect! Nothing else changes.", "wrong": "Put «Est-ce que» in front and keep everything else: «Est-ce que Tom et Sophie sont partis en vacances ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Est-ce qu'elle vient avec nous ?»", "fr": "Est-ce qu'elle vient avec nous ?"}]}, "16-4": {"stageId": "16-4", "title": "Questions by inversion", "goal": "By the end you can ask formal questions by swapping verb and subject, using «-t-» when needed.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Inversion means putting the verb before the subject pronoun, with a hyphen between them. It sounds a bit formal and is common in writing."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess: «Vous parlez français.» as an inversion question?", "choices": ["Parlez-vous français ?", "Vous-parlez français ?", "Parlez vous-français ?"], "answer": "Parlez-vous français ?", "right": "Yes! The verb «parlez» comes first, then a hyphen, then «vous».", "wrong": "Swap the verb and the pronoun, joined by a hyphen: «Parlez-vous français ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Parlent-ils français ?", "en": "Do they speak French?", "note": "Pronounce the t: 'parl-teel'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "If the verb ends in a vowel and the subject is «il», «elle» or «on», add «-t-» between them. It just makes the words flow."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "A-t-elle un chien ?", "en": "Does she have a dog?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle a un chien.» → inversion?", "choices": ["A-elle un chien ?", "A-t-elle un chien ?", "At-elle un chien ?"], "answer": "A-t-elle un chien ?", "right": "Correct! «a» ends in a vowel, so we add «-t-» before «elle».", "wrong": "«a» ends in a vowel and is followed by «elle», so add «-t-» with hyphens on both sides: «A-t-elle… ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il mange.» → inversion?", "choices": ["Mange-t-il ?", "Mange-il ?", "Mang-t-il ?"], "answer": "Mange-t-il ?", "right": "Great! «mange» ends in a vowel (e), so «-t-» is added.", "wrong": "«mange» ends with a vowel sound before «il», so add «-t-»: «Mange-t-il ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "With «je», inversion only works with a few verbs: «ai-je», «suis-je», «dois-je», «puis-je». For all other verbs, use «est-ce que»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you ask 'Do I eat?'", "choices": ["Mange-je ?", "Est-ce que je mange ?", "Je mange-je ?"], "answer": "Est-ce que je mange ?", "right": "Exactly! «je» + ordinary verbs doesn't invert, so use «est-ce que».", "wrong": "«Mange-je» isn't used in French. With «je», use «est-ce que»: «Est-ce que je mange ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "An English '-ing' question uses the plain present: 'Are you going to the theatre tonight?' → «Vas-tu au théâtre ce soir ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: verb-hyphen-pronoun, «-t-» between vowels, and no inversion with «je» except ai-je, suis-je, dois-je, puis-je."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Make an inversion question: «Nous allons chez Alice.»", "answer": "Allons-nous chez Alice ?", "accept": ["allons-nous chez alice ?", "allons-nous chez alice", "allons nous chez alice"], "hint": "Starts with the verb «Allons…»", "right": "Bravo! Verb, hyphen, pronoun.", "wrong": "Swap verb and pronoun with a hyphen: «Allons-nous chez Alice ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ai-je raison ?» (Am I right?)", "fr": "Ai-je raison ?"}]}, "16-5": {"stageId": "16-5", "title": "Asking where: «où»", "goal": "By the end you can ask 'where' questions in formal, neutral and informal style, present and past.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«où» means where. Watch the accent: «ou» without the accent means 'or'!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Où est le restaurant ?» mean?", "choices": ["Where is the restaurant?", "Or is the restaurant?", "When is the restaurant?"], "answer": "Where is the restaurant?", "right": "Yes! «où» with an accent = where.", "wrong": "«où» with the accent means where («ou» without it means 'or'). When = «quand»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Three styles: «Où» + inversion (formal), «Où est-ce que» + normal order (neutral), or «où» at the end (informal)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Où vas-tu ? / Où est-ce que tu vas ? / Tu vas où ?", "en": "Where are you going?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is the informal style?", "choices": ["Où vas-tu ?", "Où est-ce que tu vas ?", "Tu vas où ?"], "answer": "Tu vas où ?", "right": "Correct! Putting «où» at the end with rising intonation is casual.", "wrong": "The informal style keeps the normal order and puts «où» at the end: «Tu vas où ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Où mange-t-il ?", "en": "Where does he eat?", "note": "«-t-» because «mange» ends in a vowel."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Where does he eat?' with inversion:", "choices": ["Où mange-il ?", "Où mange-t-il ?", "Où il mange-t-il ?"], "answer": "Où mange-t-il ?", "right": "Great! Inversion plus the linking «-t-».", "wrong": "With inversion, add «-t-» between a vowel and «il»: «Où mange-t-il ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "In the passé composé, invert the auxiliary only, and the participle stays after: «Où as-tu acheté tes chaussures ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Where did you go?' with inversion:", "choices": ["Où es-tu allé ?", "Où as-tu allé ?", "Où tu es-allé ?"], "answer": "Où es-tu allé ?", "right": "Excellent! «aller» uses «être», and we invert «es-tu».", "wrong": "«aller» takes «être», and you invert the auxiliary: «Où es-tu allé ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In real life you'll often hear «Il est où, le restaurant ?». It's very natural in spoken French."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Où + inversion», «Où est-ce que…», or informal «… où ?». In the past, invert the auxiliary."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with «est-ce que»: Where did you buy your shoes? (tu)", "answer": "Où est-ce que tu as acheté tes chaussures ?", "accept": ["où est-ce que tu as acheté tes chaussures ?", "où est-ce que tu as acheté tes chaussures", "ou est-ce que tu as achete tes chaussures", "où est ce que tu as acheté tes chaussures"], "hint": "«Où est-ce que» + «tu as acheté…»", "right": "Parfait! Neutral and correct.", "wrong": "«Où est-ce que» + normal order: «Où est-ce que tu as acheté tes chaussures ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Excusez-moi, où est le restaurant ?»", "fr": "Excusez-moi, où est le restaurant ?"}]}, "16-6": {"stageId": "16-6", "title": "Practice: building «où» questions", "goal": "By the end you can create the «où» question that matches a given answer.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "A practice trick: find the place in the answer (en ville, à Paris…), drop it and start the question with «où»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous mangeons en ville à midi. → Où mangez-vous à midi ?", "en": "We eat in town at lunchtime. → Where do you eat at lunchtime?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Same answer, «Nous mangeons en ville à midi.» Which «est-ce que» question matches?", "choices": ["Où est-ce que vous mangez à midi ?", "Où est-ce que mangez-vous à midi ?", "Où vous mangez est-ce que à midi ?"], "answer": "Où est-ce que vous mangez à midi ?", "right": "Yes! «Où est-ce que» + normal order.", "wrong": "After «où est-ce que», keep the normal order and don't invert: «Où est-ce que vous mangez à midi ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "French people often use «on» for 'we': «Où mange-t-on ?» or, very casually, «On mange où ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«On mange où à midi ?» What style is this?", "choices": ["Formal", "Informal", "Incorrect"], "answer": "Informal", "right": "Right! «où» at the end with «on» is relaxed, everyday French.", "wrong": "It's correct but informal: normal word order with «où» at the end is how people chat."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For past answers, keep the passé composé and invert just the auxiliary."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle a marché dans les bois hier. → Où a-t-elle marché hier ?", "en": "Where did she walk yesterday?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone says «J'ai acheté ces chaussures à Paris.» What question did you ask them?", "choices": ["Où as-tu acheté ces chaussures ?", "Où ai-je acheté ces chaussures ?", "Où as-tu achetées ces chaussures ?"], "answer": "Où as-tu acheté ces chaussures ?", "right": "Great! You ask «tu», and the participle stays «acheté».", "wrong": "You're asking the other person, so use «tu»: «as-tu». Here the participle stays «acheté» because «ces chaussures» comes after it."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: remove the place, add «où», then choose inversion, «est-ce que», or informal order."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with «est-ce que»: Where did she walk yesterday?", "answer": "Où est-ce qu'elle a marché hier ?", "accept": ["où est-ce qu'elle a marché hier ?", "où est-ce qu'elle a marché hier", "ou est-ce qu'elle a marche hier", "où est-ce qu’elle a marché hier ?", "où est ce qu'elle a marché hier"], "hint": "«que» → «qu'» before «elle».", "right": "Bravo! And you remembered «qu'».", "wrong": "«Où est-ce qu'» (before a vowel) + «elle a marché hier»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «On mange où à midi ?»", "fr": "On mange où à midi ?"}]}, "16-7": {"stageId": "16-7", "title": "Asking why: «pourquoi»", "goal": "By the end you can ask 'why' questions, including negative and past ones.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«pourquoi» = why. You answer with «parce que» (because). Look at its parts: «pour» + «quoi»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the literal meaning of «pour» + «quoi»?", "choices": ["for what", "where", "how much"], "answer": "for what", "right": "Yes! 'For what?' = why.", "wrong": "«pour» = for, «quoi» = what, so «pourquoi» is literally 'for what', meaning why."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pourquoi es-tu ici ? / Pourquoi est-ce que tu es ici ? / Tu es ici pourquoi ?", "en": "Why are you here?", "note": "Formal / neutral / informal"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A friend asks «Pourquoi tu es fatigué ?» How does your answer begin?", "choices": ["Parce que…", "Pour quoi…", "Quand…"], "answer": "Parce que…", "right": "Correct! «parce que» = because.", "wrong": "You answer a «pourquoi» question with «parce que» (because), e.g. «Parce que j'ai mal dormi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Negative + inversion: «ne» goes before the inverted verb-pronoun, «pas» after it: «ne manges-tu pas»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pourquoi ne manges-tu pas de carottes ?", "en": "Why don't you eat carrots?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Why don't you eat carrots?' with «est-ce que»:", "choices": ["Pourquoi est-ce que tu ne manges pas de carottes ?", "Pourquoi est-ce que tu ne manges de carottes pas ?", "Pourquoi est-ce que tu manges ne pas des carottes ?"], "answer": "Pourquoi est-ce que tu ne manges pas de carottes ?", "right": "Great! «ne… pas» wraps the verb, and «des» → «de».", "wrong": "«ne… pas» wraps around the verb («ne manges pas»), and «des» becomes «de» after a negative."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Handy reply: «Pourquoi pas ?» = Why not? Remember, after a negative, «des» becomes «de»: «pas de carottes»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pourquoi est-il allé à la piscine s'il était malade ?", "en": "Why did he go to the swimming pool if he was sick?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Why did he go?' with inversion:", "choices": ["Pourquoi est-il allé ?", "Pourquoi a-t-il allé ?", "Pourquoi il est-il allé ?"], "answer": "Pourquoi est-il allé ?", "right": "Excellent! «aller» takes «être», so we invert «est-il».", "wrong": "«aller» uses «être», so you invert the auxiliary: «est-il allé»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Pourquoi» + inversion / «est-ce que» / informal ending. Answer with «parce que»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with «est-ce que»: Why are you here? (tu)", "answer": "Pourquoi est-ce que tu es ici ?", "accept": ["pourquoi est-ce que tu es ici ?", "pourquoi est-ce que tu es ici", "pourquoi est ce que tu es ici"], "hint": "«Pourquoi est-ce que» + «tu es ici».", "right": "Parfait!", "wrong": "«Pourquoi est-ce que» + normal order: «Pourquoi est-ce que tu es ici ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Pourquoi est-ce que tu ne manges pas de carottes ?»", "fr": "Pourquoi est-ce que tu ne manges pas de carottes ?"}]}, "16-8": {"stageId": "16-8", "title": "Practice: building «pourquoi» questions", "goal": "By the end you can turn 'because' answers into «pourquoi» questions, even with names.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Practice time! Drop the «parce que…» part of the answer and start with «pourquoi»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai oublié mon sac parce que j'étais pressée. → Pourquoi as-tu oublié ton sac ?", "en": "Why did you forget your bag?", "note": "«mon» becomes «ton» because you're asking."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Answer: «Je suis fatiguée parce que j'ai mal dormi.» Which question was asked?", "choices": ["Pourquoi es-tu fatiguée ?", "Pourquoi suis-je fatiguée ?", "Pourquoi tu es-tu fatiguée ?"], "answer": "Pourquoi es-tu fatiguée ?", "right": "Yes! The question was put to «tu»: «es-tu».", "wrong": "The person answered with «je», so the question was asked with «tu»: «Pourquoi es-tu fatiguée ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Tricky part: with a name, you can't invert the name itself. Keep the name, then add the matching pronoun after the verb: «George va-t-il»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is correct?", "choices": ["Pourquoi va George à la gym ?", "Pourquoi George va-t-il à la gym ?", "Pourquoi George va-il à la gym ?"], "answer": "Pourquoi George va-t-il à la gym ?", "right": "Excellent! Name + verb + «-t-il».", "wrong": "With a name, keep it before the verb and add «-t-il» after: «Pourquoi George va-t-il à la gym ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Shortcut: «est-ce que» avoids this problem: «Pourquoi est-ce que George va à la gym ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pourquoi chante-t-il sous la douche ?", "en": "Why is he singing in the shower?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Translate: Why did we go there?", "choices": ["Pourquoi y sommes-nous allés ?", "Pourquoi avons-nous allés y ?", "Pourquoi nous y sommes allés-nous ?"], "answer": "Pourquoi y sommes-nous allés ?", "right": "Superb! «aller» takes «être», and «y» (there) goes before the auxiliary.", "wrong": "«aller» uses «être» («sommes»), and «y» (there) goes before the inverted auxiliary: «Pourquoi y sommes-nous allés ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: remove the reason, add «pourquoi». With a name, use name + verb-t-il, or just use «est-ce que»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with inversion: Why do you like bananas? (tu)", "answer": "Pourquoi aimes-tu les bananes ?", "accept": ["pourquoi aimes-tu les bananes ?", "pourquoi aimes-tu les bananes", "pourquoi aimes tu les bananes"], "hint": "Invert «tu aimes» → «aimes-tu».", "right": "Bravo!", "wrong": "Invert «tu aimes» to «aimes-tu»: «Pourquoi aimes-tu les bananes ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Pourquoi est-ce que George va à la gym tous les soirs ?»", "fr": "Pourquoi est-ce que George va à la gym tous les soirs ?"}]}, "16-9": {"stageId": "16-9", "title": "Asking how: «comment»", "goal": "By the end you can ask how someone is, their name, and how things are done.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«comment» = how. French also uses it where English says 'what', for example when asking someone's name."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess: «Comment t'appelles-tu ?» means…", "choices": ["How are you?", "What's your name?", "Where do you live?"], "answer": "What's your name?", "right": "Yes! Literally 'How do you call yourself?'", "wrong": "Literally it's 'How do you call yourself?', which means 'What's your name?'. 'How are you?' = «Comment vas-tu ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Comment vas-tu ?", "en": "How are you?", "note": "Also «Comment ça va ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Same three styles as always: «Comment» + inversion, «Comment est-ce que…», or informal «… comment ?» at the end."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Comment faites-vous un gâteau au chocolat ?", "en": "How do you make a chocolate cake?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is informal?", "choices": ["Comment faites-vous un gâteau ?", "Vous faites un gâteau comment ?", "Comment est-ce que vous faites un gâteau ?"], "answer": "Vous faites un gâteau comment ?", "right": "Correct! Normal order with «comment» at the end is casual.", "wrong": "The informal style keeps the normal order and puts «comment» last: «Vous faites un gâteau comment ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Didn't hear someone? Say «Comment ?». It's a polite 'Sorry?' and much nicer than «Quoi ?»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Comment est-elle arrivée ici ?", "en": "How did she get here?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'How did she get here?' with «est-ce que»:", "choices": ["Comment est-ce qu'elle est arrivée ici ?", "Comment est-ce qu'elle a arrivée ici ?", "Comment est-ce que elle est arrivée ici ?"], "answer": "Comment est-ce qu'elle est arrivée ici ?", "right": "Great! «arriver» takes «être», and «que» → «qu'» before «elle».", "wrong": "«arriver» is a movement verb, so it uses «être» («elle est arrivée»), and «que» becomes «qu'» before a vowel."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «comment» = how (and 'what' for names). Use inversion, «est-ce que», or put it at the end."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with «est-ce que»: What is your name? (tu)", "answer": "Comment est-ce que tu t'appelles ?", "accept": ["comment est-ce que tu t'appelles ?", "comment est-ce que tu t'appelles", "comment est-ce que tu t’appelles ?", "comment est ce que tu t'appelles"], "hint": "«Comment est-ce que» + «tu t'appelles».", "right": "Parfait!", "wrong": "Use «comment» (not 'what') + «est-ce que» + «tu t'appelles»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Bonjour ! Comment allez-vous ?»", "fr": "Bonjour ! Comment allez-vous ?"}]}, "16-10": {"stageId": "16-10", "title": "Practice: «comment» questions", "goal": "By the end you can understand and write «comment» questions in present, past and future.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's practise understanding and building «comment» questions. First, some reading."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Comment allez-vous ?» means…", "choices": ["How are you?", "How do you go?", "Where are you going?"], "answer": "How are you?", "right": "Yes! In greetings, «aller» means 'to be doing'.", "wrong": "In greetings, «aller» describes how you're doing: «Comment allez-vous ?» = How are you?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Comment faut-il manger ce plat ?", "en": "How should we eat this dish?", "note": "«il faut» = one must; inverted: «faut-il»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Comment a-t-il fait ça ?» means…", "choices": ["How does he do this?", "How did he do this?", "How will he do this?"], "answer": "How did he do this?", "right": "Correct! «a… fait» is the passé composé.", "wrong": "«a-t-il fait» is the passé composé (auxiliary + participle), so it's past: 'How did he do this?'"}, {"t": "say", "text": "'One' in a general question is «on»: «Comment sert-on le thé ?» = How does one serve tea?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Translate: How does one serve tea?", "choices": ["Comment sert-on le thé ?", "Comment on-sert le thé ?", "Comment sert-il on le thé ?"], "answer": "Comment sert-on le thé ?", "right": "Great! Verb, hyphen, «on».", "wrong": "Invert the verb and «on» with a hyphen: «sert-on». So: «Comment sert-on le thé ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "With a name: «Comment Tom cuisine-t-il ce plat ?» or, more simply, «Comment est-ce que Tom cuisine ce plat ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Comment ira-t-elle en France ?", "en": "How will she go to France?", "note": "«ira» = will go (future); «-t-» again"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Comment est-elle allée chez sa mère ?» means…", "choices": ["How did she go to her mother's house?", "How is her mother?", "Where did she go?"], "answer": "How did she go to her mother's house?", "right": "Excellent! «est allée» = went, and «chez sa mère» = to her mother's.", "wrong": "«est-elle allée» is the passé composé of «aller» (went), and «chez sa mère» = to her mother's house."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: read the verb tense to translate correctly, use «on» for 'one', and use name + verb-t-il with names."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with inversion: How did you do this? (tu)", "answer": "Comment as-tu fait ça ?", "accept": ["comment as-tu fait ça ?", "comment as-tu fait ça", "comment as-tu fait ca", "comment as tu fait ça"], "hint": "Invert the auxiliary: «as-tu».", "right": "Bravo!", "wrong": "In the passé composé, invert the auxiliary: «Comment as-tu fait ça ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Comment est-ce que Tom cuisine ce plat ?»", "fr": "Comment est-ce que Tom cuisine ce plat ?"}]}, "16-11": {"stageId": "16-11", "title": "Asking when: «quand»", "goal": "By the end you can ask 'when' questions in three styles, present and past.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«quand» = when. The d is silent, but before a vowel it links with a t sound: «Quand est-ce que» sounds like 'kan-tess-kuh'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Quand commence le spectacle ?» means…", "choices": ["When does the show start?", "Where does the show start?", "How does the show start?"], "answer": "When does the show start?", "right": "Yes! «quand» = when, «commencer» = to start.", "wrong": "«quand» means when (where = «où», how = «comment»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quand est-ce que le spectacle commence ?", "en": "When does the show start?"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "With «quand» and a noun subject, you can simply swap verb and noun: «Quand commence le spectacle ?». It's elegant and common."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quand arrive-t-elle à la gare ?", "en": "When is she arriving at the station?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is correct?", "choices": ["Quand arrive-elle ?", "Quand arrive-t-elle ?", "Quand arrivé-t-elle ?"], "answer": "Quand arrive-t-elle ?", "right": "Great! «arrive» ends in a vowel, so we add «-t-».", "wrong": "«arrive» ends with a vowel before «elle», so add «-t-». «arrivé» is the past participle, not the present."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Informal speech puts «quand» at the end: «Elle arrive quand ?» or «Le spectacle, il commence quand ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'When did she arrive at the station?' with inversion:", "choices": ["Quand est-elle arrivée à la gare ?", "Quand a-t-elle arrivée à la gare ?", "Quand elle est-elle arrivée à la gare ?"], "answer": "Quand est-elle arrivée à la gare ?", "right": "Excellent! «arriver» takes «être», and «arrivée» agrees with «elle».", "wrong": "«arriver» is a movement verb, so it uses «être»: invert «est-elle», then «arrivée» (agreeing with «elle»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle est arrivée quand à la gare ?", "en": "When did she arrive at the station?", "note": "Informal"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Quand» + inversion, «Quand est-ce que…», or «… quand ?» informally."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with «est-ce que»: When does the show start?", "answer": "Quand est-ce que le spectacle commence ?", "accept": ["quand est-ce que le spectacle commence ?", "quand est-ce que le spectacle commence", "quand est ce que le spectacle commence"], "hint": "«Quand est-ce que» + «le spectacle commence».", "right": "Parfait!", "wrong": "«Quand est-ce que» + normal order: «Quand est-ce que le spectacle commence ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quand est-ce que tu viens ?»", "fr": "Quand est-ce que tu viens ?"}]}, "16-12": {"stageId": "16-12", "title": "Practice: building «quand» questions", "goal": "By the end you can turn answers with a time into «quand» questions, including ones with «devoir».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Practice trick: find the time in the answer (à midi, tous les mercredis…), take it out and start with «quand»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il mange au restaurant tous les mercredis. → Quand mange-t-il au restaurant ?", "en": "When does he eat at the restaurant?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «Charlotte et moi avons rendez-vous à midi», which part does «quand» replace?", "choices": ["Charlotte et moi", "rendez-vous", "à midi"], "answer": "à midi", "right": "Yes! «à midi» is the time, so that's what «quand» asks about.", "wrong": "«quand» asks about time, and the time here is «à midi» (at midday)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You ask Charlotte and her friend. Which question is right?", "choices": ["Quand avez-vous rendez-vous ?", "Quand a-vous rendez-vous ?", "Quand vous avez-vous rendez-vous ?"], "answer": "Quand avez-vous rendez-vous ?", "right": "Great! «vous avez» → «avez-vous».", "wrong": "You speak to two people, so use «vous»: «vous avez» inverts to «avez-vous»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Vous avez rendez-vous quand ?» is fine when speaking, but it's colloquial, so don't write it in formal French."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With «devoir» + infinitive, invert «devoir» only and leave the infinitive after it."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quand doivent-elles aller chez leur tante ?", "en": "When must they go to their aunt's?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Answer: «J'ai rendez-vous chez le docteur à 15 heures.» Which question was asked?", "choices": ["Quand as-tu rendez-vous chez le docteur ?", "Quand ai-je rendez-vous chez le docteur ?", "Quand tu as rendez-vous-tu chez le docteur ?"], "answer": "Quand as-tu rendez-vous chez le docteur ?", "right": "Correct! The answer uses «je», so the question uses «tu»: «as-tu».", "wrong": "The person answered with «je», so you asked with «tu»: «Quand as-tu rendez-vous… ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: take out the time, add «quand», and use inversion or «est-ce que». Only invert the conjugated verb."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with «est-ce que»: When must they (women) go to their aunt's?", "answer": "Quand est-ce qu'elles doivent aller chez leur tante ?", "accept": ["quand est-ce qu'elles doivent aller chez leur tante ?", "quand est-ce qu'elles doivent aller chez leur tante", "quand est-ce qu’elles doivent aller chez leur tante ?", "quand est ce qu'elles doivent aller chez leur tante"], "hint": "«qu'» before «elles».", "right": "Bravo!", "wrong": "«Quand est-ce qu'» + «elles doivent aller chez leur tante»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quand est-ce qu'on mange ?»", "fr": "Quand est-ce qu'on mange ?"}]}, "16-13": {"stageId": "16-13", "title": "Asking how much / how many: «combien»", "goal": "By the end you can ask prices and quantities with «combien» and «combien de».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«combien» = how much (often a price). «combien de» + noun = how many / how much of something."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Combien ça coûte ?» means…", "choices": ["How much does it cost?", "How many are there?", "When does it cost?"], "answer": "How much does it cost?", "right": "Yes! «coûter» = to cost.", "wrong": "«combien» alone with «coûter» (to cost) asks a price: How much does it cost?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est combien ?", "en": "How much is it?", "note": "Informal: perfect at the market"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«combien de» stays «de» even before plurals, and becomes «d'» before a vowel: «combien de pommes», «combien d'élèves»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Combien de pommes as-tu ?", "en": "How many apples have you got?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ élèves y a-t-il dans cette classe ?»", "choices": ["Combien des", "Combien d'", "Combien de les"], "answer": "Combien d'", "right": "Correct! «de» → «d'» before the vowel in «élèves».", "wrong": "It's always «combien de» (never «des»/«les»), and «de» becomes «d'» before a vowel: «Combien d'élèves…»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Combien y a-t-il de personnes dans la salle ?", "en": "How many people are there in the room?", "note": "«combien» and «de…» can be split."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«il y a» (there is/are) becomes «y a-t-il» in inversion. You'll hear it all the time."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'How much is this book?'", "choices": ["Combien coûte ce livre ?", "Combien de coûte ce livre ?", "Combien de livre coûte ?"], "answer": "Combien coûte ce livre ?", "right": "Great! A price uses plain «combien» + «coûter».", "wrong": "For a price, use «combien» without «de»: «Combien coûte ce livre ?». «combien de» is for counting things."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Combien cette maison a-t-elle coûté ?", "en": "How much did this house cost?", "note": "Noun + verb + «-t-elle»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «combien» (+ coûter) for prices, «combien de/d'» + noun for quantities."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with «est-ce que»: How many apples have you got? (tu)", "answer": "Combien de pommes est-ce que tu as ?", "accept": ["combien de pommes est-ce que tu as ?", "combien de pommes est-ce que tu as", "combien de pommes est ce que tu as"], "hint": "«Combien de pommes» + «est-ce que tu as».", "right": "Parfait!", "wrong": "Start with «Combien de pommes», then «est-ce que tu as»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud at a market: «Bonjour, c'est combien, s'il vous plaît ?»", "fr": "Bonjour, c'est combien, s'il vous plaît ?"}]}, "16-14": {"stageId": "16-14", "title": "Practice: understanding «combien» questions", "goal": "By the end you can quickly tell 'how much' from 'how many' and translate «combien» questions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Decoding tip: «combien de/d'» + noun usually means how many. «combien» alone, or with «coûter», asks how much (price)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Combien d'animaux domestiques avez-vous ?» means…", "choices": ["How many pets do you have?", "How much do your pets cost?", "Where are your pets?"], "answer": "How many pets do you have?", "right": "Yes! «combien d'» + noun = how many.", "wrong": "«combien d'» + a countable noun asks how many: How many pets do you have?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Combien as-tu de paires de chaussures ?", "en": "How many pairs of shoes do you have?", "note": "«combien… de» split around the verb"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«C'était combien ?» means…", "choices": ["How much was it?", "How many were there?", "How much is it?"], "answer": "How much was it?", "right": "Correct! «c'était» is the past of «c'est».", "wrong": "«c'était» = it was (past of «c'est»), so: How much was it?"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«c'était» (it was) and «coûtaient» (cost) are in the imperfect, a past tense you'll meet soon. For now, just read them as past."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Combien coûtaient ces tomates ?", "en": "How much did these tomatoes cost?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Combien est-ce que vous avez d'enfants ?» means…", "choices": ["How many children do you have?", "How much do your children cost?", "When do you have children?"], "answer": "How many children do you have?", "right": "Great! «combien… d'enfants» = how many children.", "wrong": "«combien… d'enfants» counts children: How many children do you have?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now asking: price → «combien» + «coûter». Quantity → «combien de» + noun."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You want to know the price of a computer. Which do you ask?", "choices": ["Combien est-ce que cet ordinateur coûte ?", "Combien d'ordinateurs est-ce que vous avez ?", "Combien de cet ordinateur ?"], "answer": "Combien est-ce que cet ordinateur coûte ?", "right": "Perfect! Price = «combien» + «coûter».", "wrong": "For a price, use «combien» + «coûter»: «Combien est-ce que cet ordinateur coûte ?». The others ask about quantity or aren't correct French."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «de» + noun → how many; price/«coûter» → how much. Check the verb tense for past or present."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write with inversion: How many children do you have? (vous)", "answer": "Combien d'enfants avez-vous ?", "accept": ["combien d'enfants avez-vous ?", "combien d'enfants avez-vous", "combien d’enfants avez-vous ?", "combien d'enfants avez vous"], "hint": "«Combien d'enfants» + «avez-vous».", "right": "Bravo!", "wrong": "«Combien d'» (before a vowel) + «enfants» + inverted «avez-vous»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Combien de frères et sœurs as-tu ?»", "fr": "Combien de frères et sœurs as-tu ?"}]}, "16-15": {"stageId": "16-15", "title": "The tag question «n'est-ce pas ?»", "goal": "By the end you can add «n'est-ce pas ?» or «non ?» to check that something is true.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "English tags change all the time: isn't it? didn't they? won't you? French has one tag that never changes: «n'est-ce pas ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est un beau poème, n'est-ce pas ?", "en": "It's a beautiful poem, isn't it?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Natasha et Louis sont venus le mois dernier, ___»", "choices": ["ne sont-ils pas ?", "n'est-ce pas ?", "n'ont-ils pas ?"], "answer": "n'est-ce pas ?", "right": "Yes! «n'est-ce pas ?» never changes, even in the past or plural.", "wrong": "Unlike English, the French tag doesn't follow the verb. It's always «n'est-ce pas ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "You use it when you expect the answer 'yes'. In casual speech, people just say «non ?» at the end."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est un beau poème, non ?", "en": "It's a beautiful poem, isn't it?", "note": "Informal"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous accompagnerez les enfants à l'école demain, n'est-ce pas ?» Best English?", "choices": ["You will take the children to school tomorrow, won't you?", "You will take the children to school tomorrow, isn't it?", "You will take the children to school tomorrow, don't you?"], "answer": "You will take the children to school tomorrow, won't you?", "right": "Great! In English the tag follows 'will', so 'won't you?'. French simply keeps «n'est-ce pas ?»", "wrong": "English matches the tag to the verb (will → won't you?). French doesn't: it's always «n'est-ce pas ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«n'est-ce pas» literally means 'is it not' and sounds like 'ness-pah'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which tag is the most informal?", "choices": ["n'est-ce pas ?", "non ?", "est-ce que ?"], "answer": "non ?", "right": "Correct! «non ?» is the casual version.", "wrong": "«non ?» is the relaxed everyday tag. «n'est-ce pas ?» is more formal, and «est-ce que» starts questions rather than ending them."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: statement + «n'est-ce pas ?» (formal) or «non ?» (casual), whatever the tense."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It's a beautiful poem, isn't it? (formal tag)", "answer": "C'est un beau poème, n'est-ce pas ?", "accept": ["c'est un beau poème, n'est-ce pas ?", "c'est un beau poème, n'est-ce pas", "c'est un beau poeme n'est-ce pas", "c’est un beau poème, n’est-ce pas ?"], "hint": "Ends with «n'est-ce pas ?»", "right": "Parfait!", "wrong": "Statement + the unchanging tag: «C'est un beau poème, n'est-ce pas ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il fait beau aujourd'hui, n'est-ce pas ?»", "fr": "Il fait beau aujourd'hui, n'est-ce pas ?"}]}, "16-16": {"stageId": "16-16", "title": "«C'est» or «il est»?", "goal": "By the end you can choose between «c'est», «ce sont» and «il/elle est» correctly.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«C'est» and «il est» can both mean 'it is' or 'he is'. Which one you use depends on the word that comes next."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Use «c'est» + a determiner (un, une, le, mon…) + noun to present someone or something."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est un beau jardin.", "en": "This is a beautiful garden."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nicole est pianiste. ___ une excellente musicienne.»", "choices": ["C'est", "Elle est", "Il est"], "answer": "C'est", "right": "Yes! «une» + noun follows, so we use «c'est».", "wrong": "There's an article + noun («une excellente musicienne») after the gap, and that needs «c'est»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Use «il est / elle est» + an adjective, or + a job with no article. Use «il est» for the time too."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Voici mon frère. Il est boulanger.", "en": "This is my brother. He is a baker.", "note": "No «un» before a job after «il est»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Mon père donne des cours à l'université. ___ prof de sciences.»", "choices": ["C'est", "Il est", "Ce sont"], "answer": "Il est", "right": "Correct! A job without an article takes «il est».", "wrong": "«prof» is a job with no article, so use «il est». 'C'est un prof' would also work, but it needs «un»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Plural: «ce sont» + plural noun: «Ce sont mes amis». For a general opinion, use «c'est» + masculine adjective: «La Provence ? C'est très beau !» (not «belle»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'adore Paris, ___ une belle ville !»", "choices": ["c'est", "il est", "elle est"], "answer": "c'est", "right": "Great! «une belle ville» = article + noun → «c'est».", "wrong": "The gap is followed by «une» + noun, which needs «c'est»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ 16 heures, les enfants sortent de l'école.»", "choices": ["C'est", "Il est", "Elle est"], "answer": "Il est", "right": "Exactly! The time always uses «il est».", "wrong": "To tell the time, French always uses «il est»: «Il est 16 heures»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «c'est/ce sont» + determiner + noun, or a general opinion. «il/elle est» + adjective, job, or the time."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: She is Italian.", "answer": "Elle est italienne.", "accept": ["elle est italienne", "elle est italienne."], "hint": "Nationality adjectives have no capital letter in French.", "right": "Bravo! Adjective → «elle est», and «italienne» has no capital.", "wrong": "A nationality adjective follows «elle est», in lower case and feminine: «Elle est italienne.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je te présente Francesca. Elle est italienne. C'est une très bonne chanteuse.»", "fr": "Je te présente Francesca. Elle est italienne. C'est une très bonne chanteuse."}]}, "17-1": {"stageId": "17-1", "title": "Who, what, which? Question words", "goal": "By the end you can ask 'who?', 'what?' and 'which (one)?' with «qui», «que», «quoi», «quel» and «lequel».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! Today we learn the little words that start questions. Imagine someone knocks on your door: you call out «Qui est là ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone knocks and you say «Qui est là ?». What do you think «qui» means?", "choices": ["Who", "What", "Where"], "answer": "Who", "right": "Yes! «qui» = who. We use it for people: «Qui est là ?» = Who is there?", "wrong": "Think about the situation: a knock on the door, you want to know which PERSON it is. «qui» = who, always for people."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For things, we use «que» (or «qu'» before a vowel). The most common form is «Qu'est-ce que…?»: «Qu'est-ce que tu veux ?» = What do you want?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Qu'est-ce que tu manges ?", "en": "What are you eating?", "note": "Same meaning with inversion: «Que manges-tu ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In relaxed speech, French people love «quoi» at the END: «C'est quoi ?» = What is it? Alone, «Quoi ?» = What? (a bit casual, like 'What?!')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You see a strange object. How do you ask 'What is it?' in a casual way?", "choices": ["Qui est-ce ?", "C'est quoi ?", "Quel est ?"], "answer": "C'est quoi ?", "right": "Exactly! «C'est quoi ?» is the casual way. «Qu'est-ce que c'est ?» is the standard way.", "wrong": "«qui» is for people, and «quel» needs a noun. For a thing, say «C'est quoi ?» (casual) or «Qu'est-ce que c'est ?»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now «quel» means 'which' or 'what' + a noun, when there are options. Like an adjective, it changes: «quel» (m.), «quelle» (f.), «quels» (m. pl.), «quelles» (f. pl.). All four sound the same!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«couleur» is feminine singular. Complete: «___ couleur préfères-tu ?»", "choices": ["Quel", "Quelle", "Quelles"], "answer": "Quelle", "right": "Bravo ! «couleur» is feminine singular, so «quelle».", "wrong": "«quel» must match its noun. «couleur» is feminine singular → «quelle». «quelles» would be for several colours."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Last one: «lequel» = which one. It replaces the noun, so you don't repeat it: «J'ai trois jupes. Laquelle préfères-tu ?» Forms: «lequel», «laquelle», «lesquels», «lesquelles»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Des pommes ? Lesquelles, rouges ou vertes ?", "en": "Apples? Which ones, red or green?", "note": "«pommes» is feminine plural → «lesquelles»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Regarde ces livres. ___ aimes-tu ?» (one book, «livre» is masculine)", "choices": ["Lequel", "Laquelle", "Quelle"], "answer": "Lequel", "right": "Super ! One masculine thing → «lequel» = which one.", "wrong": "We want 'which one' without repeating the noun, so we need a form of «lequel». «livre» is masculine singular → «lequel»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «qui» = who (people), «que / qu'est-ce que / quoi» = what (things), «quel» + noun = which/what, «lequel» = which one."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: What do you want? (use «Qu'est-ce que»)", "answer": "Qu'est-ce que tu veux ?", "accept": ["qu'est-ce que tu veux", "qu'est-ce que tu veux ?", "qu’est-ce que tu veux", "qu est-ce que tu veux"], "hint": "Starts with «Qu'est-ce que tu…»", "right": "Parfait ! «Qu'est-ce que tu veux ?»", "wrong": "'What' for a thing = «Qu'est-ce que», then «tu veux»: «Qu'est-ce que tu veux ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Qui est là ? C'est moi.»", "fr": "Qui est là ? C'est moi."}]}, "17-2": {"stageId": "17-2", "title": "Negative questions with inversion", "goal": "By the end you can ask questions like 'Don't you eat meat?' with «ne… pas» and inversion.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Remember inversion? You put the verb before the subject with a hyphen: «Manges-tu ?» = Do you eat? French has no 'do/did', the verb simply goes first."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you think we say 'Do you eat meat?' with inversion?", "choices": ["Tu manges de la viande-tu ?", "Manges-tu de la viande ?", "Fais-tu manger de la viande ?"], "answer": "Manges-tu de la viande ?", "right": "Yes! Verb + hyphen + subject: «Manges-tu ?» No need for 'do'.", "wrong": "In French there's no 'do' helper. Just swap: verb first, hyphen, subject: «Manges-tu de la viande ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now add a negation. «ne… pas» wraps around the whole block verb+subject: «ne» + «manges-tu» + «pas»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ne manges-tu pas de viande ?", "en": "Don't you eat meat?", "note": "After a negation, «de la» becomes «de»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Where does «pas» go in a negative inverted question?", "choices": ["Before «ne»", "After the subject pronoun", "Between the verb and the pronoun"], "answer": "After the subject pronoun", "right": "Exactly! «Ne manges-tu pas…» : «pas» comes after «tu», because «manges-tu» is one block.", "wrong": "«manges-tu» is a single block joined by a hyphen, so nothing goes inside it. «ne» comes before it and «pas» after: «Ne manges-tu pas… ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«ne» becomes «n'» before a vowel or h: «N'avons-nous pas de chat ?» = Haven't we got a cat? (French uses «avoir» for 'have got'.)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "With «il», «elle», «on», if the verb ends in a vowel, add «-t-» so it sounds smooth: «a-t-elle», «va-t-il»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "N'a-t-elle pas de beaux yeux ?", "en": "Hasn't she got beautiful eyes?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Choose the correct question: 'Hasn't he got a car?'", "choices": ["N'a-il pas de voiture ?", "N'a-t-il pas de voiture ?", "Ne a-t-il pas de voiture ?"], "answer": "N'a-t-il pas de voiture ?", "right": "Parfait ! «n'» before the vowel, and «-t-» between «a» and «il».", "wrong": "Two rules: «ne» → «n'» before «a», and «a» ends with a vowel so we add «-t-» before «il»: «N'a-t-il pas de voiture ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ne» + verb-subject + «pas» + the rest. Use «n'» before a vowel and «-t-» between a vowel and «il/elle/on»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Haven't we got a cat?", "answer": "N'avons-nous pas de chat ?", "accept": ["n'avons-nous pas de chat", "n'avons-nous pas de chat ?", "n’avons-nous pas de chat", "n avons-nous pas de chat"], "hint": "Starts with «N'avons-nous…»", "right": "Bravo ! «N'avons-nous pas de chat ?»", "wrong": "«ne» → «n'» before «avons», then «avons-nous», then «pas de chat»: «N'avons-nous pas de chat ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ne manges-tu pas de viande ?»", "fr": "Ne manges-tu pas de viande ?"}]}, "17-3": {"stageId": "17-3", "title": "Inversion in the passé composé", "goal": "By the end you can ask 'Have you read…?' and 'Didn't you buy…?' with inversion in the past.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The passé composé has two parts: an auxiliary («avoir» or «être») + a past participle: «tu as lu» = you read / have read. Which part do you think moves in a question?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "To ask 'Have you read this book?', which word goes before «tu»?", "choices": ["lu", "as", "ce"], "answer": "as", "right": "Yes! Only the auxiliary swaps with the subject: «As-tu lu ce livre ?»", "wrong": "Only the auxiliary (the conjugated verb) moves: «tu as lu» → «As-tu lu ce livre ?». The participle «lu» stays after."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "As-tu lu ce livre ?", "en": "Have you read this book?", "note": "Polite or plural: «Avez-vous lu ce livre ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English uses 'did' or 'have' here; French uses neither. «As-tu acheté ton billet ?» can mean 'Did you buy your ticket?' or 'Have you bought your ticket?'"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Has he eaten this morning?' Which is right?", "choices": ["A-il mangé ce matin ?", "A-t-il mangé ce matin ?", "Il a-t-mangé ce matin ?"], "answer": "A-t-il mangé ce matin ?", "right": "Très bien ! «a» ends in a vowel, so we add «-t-» before «il».", "wrong": "«a» + «il» would clash (two vowels), so French adds «-t-»: «A-t-il mangé ce matin ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "For a negative question: «ne» + auxiliary-subject + «pas» + participle. The participle always comes last."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "N'as-tu pas acheté ton billet de train ?", "en": "Didn't you buy your train ticket?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Put it in order: 'Hasn't he eaten this morning?'", "choices": ["N'a-t-il mangé pas ce matin ?", "N'a-t-il pas mangé ce matin ?", "Ne pas a-t-il mangé ce matin ?"], "answer": "N'a-t-il pas mangé ce matin ?", "right": "Super ! «n'» + «a-t-il» + «pas» + «mangé».", "wrong": "«ne… pas» wraps the auxiliary block only, and the participle comes after «pas»: «N'a-t-il pas mangé ce matin ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: positive: auxiliary-subject + participle («As-tu lu ?»). Negative: «n'» + auxiliary-subject + «pas» + participle («N'as-tu pas lu ?»)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (tu): Haven't you read this book?", "answer": "N'as-tu pas lu ce livre ?", "accept": ["n'as-tu pas lu ce livre", "n'as-tu pas lu ce livre ?", "n’as-tu pas lu ce livre", "n as-tu pas lu ce livre"], "hint": "Starts with «N'as-tu pas…»", "right": "Parfait ! «N'as-tu pas lu ce livre ?»", "wrong": "«n'» + «as-tu» + «pas» + «lu» + «ce livre»: «N'as-tu pas lu ce livre ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «As-tu acheté ton billet de train ?»", "fr": "As-tu acheté ton billet de train ?"}]}, "17-4": {"stageId": "17-4", "title": "Casual question tags: hein ? d'accord ?", "goal": "By the end you can turn a sentence into a question with «d'accord ?», «hein ?», «pas vrai ?», «c'est bien ça ?» and «non ?».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In English we add tags like 'isn't he?' or 'right?' to a sentence. French does it too, and it's much easier: the tag never changes!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«On va manger une pizza, d'accord ?» What do you think «d'accord ?» means here?", "choices": ["OK?", "Why?", "When?"], "answer": "OK?", "right": "Yes! «d'accord» = OK / agreed. As a tag, it asks for agreement.", "wrong": "«d'accord» means 'agreed / OK'. At the end of a sentence it asks 'OK?': «On va manger une pizza, d'accord ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu vas venir avec moi, hein ?", "en": "You're coming with me, huh?", "note": "«hein» sounds like a nasal 'an'. Very casual!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«pas vrai ?» (literally 'not true?') and «non ?» both work like 'isn't it?' or 'aren't they?'. You want the other person to confirm."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ils viennent nous voir demain, pas vrai ?", "en": "They're coming to see us tomorrow, aren't they?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You want to check a fact: 'You have a brother, right?'", "choices": ["Tu as un frère, c'est bien ça ?", "Tu as un frère, qui ?", "Tu as un frère, quoi ça ?"], "answer": "Tu as un frère, c'est bien ça ?", "right": "Bravo ! «c'est bien ça ?» = 'is that right?', perfect to check information.", "wrong": "To check if something is correct, French says «c'est bien ça ?» (is that right?): «Tu as un frère, c'est bien ça ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English tags change all the time (isn't he, didn't I, haven't we…). French tags are fixed: «non ?» works after any sentence. Easy win!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which tag is the most casual, almost like 'huh?'", "choices": ["d'accord ?", "hein ?", "c'est bien ça ?"], "answer": "hein ?", "right": "Exactly! «hein ?» is very informal; use it with friends, not in a job interview.", "wrong": "«hein ?» is the very casual one, like 'huh?'. «d'accord ?» asks for agreement and «c'est bien ça ?» checks facts."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: add «d'accord ?», «hein ?», «pas vrai ?», «c'est bien ça ?» or «non ?» at the end of any sentence. Your voice goes up at the end."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We are going to eat a pizza, OK?", "answer": "On va manger une pizza, d'accord ?", "accept": ["on va manger une pizza, d'accord ?", "on va manger une pizza, d'accord", "on va manger une pizza d'accord", "on va manger une pizza, d’accord ?", "nous allons manger une pizza, d'accord ?"], "hint": "«On va manger… , d'accord ?»", "right": "Parfait ! «On va manger une pizza, d'accord ?»", "wrong": "'We are going to' in casual French = «On va», then the tag «d'accord ?»: «On va manger une pizza, d'accord ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with a rising voice at the end: «Ils viennent demain, non ?»", "fr": "Ils viennent demain, non ?"}]}, "17-5": {"stageId": "17-5", "title": "Asking 'who?' and 'with whom?'", "goal": "By the end you can ask 'Who…?' and 'With whom…?' in formal, standard and casual French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«qui» = who. There are several ways to ask «Who are you?», from formal to very casual. Let's look at them."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Qui es-tu ?", "en": "Who are you?", "note": "Also: «Qui est-ce que tu es ?», «Tu es qui ?» (casual), «T'es qui ?» (very casual)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which version is the most casual?", "choices": ["Qui es-tu ?", "Qui est-ce que tu es ?", "T'es qui ?"], "answer": "T'es qui ?", "right": "Yes! Statement word order + «qui» at the end, and «tu es» squeezed to «t'es»: very casual spoken French.", "wrong": "Inversion («Qui es-tu ?») is the most formal. Putting «qui» at the end and shortening «tu es» to «t'es» is very casual: «T'es qui ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "When «qui» is the subject (the person doing the action), just put the verb after it, in the «il» form: «Qui travaille avec vous ?» = Who works with you?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Who ate my chocolate?' Complete: «Qui ___ mangé ma tablette de chocolat ?»", "choices": ["a", "as", "ont"], "answer": "a", "right": "Bravo ! «qui» as a subject takes the «il» form: «Qui a mangé… ?»", "wrong": "When «qui» is the subject, the verb is like «il»: «il a» → «Qui a mangé ma tablette de chocolat ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Long version for subject «qui»: «Qui est-ce qui…?» → «Qui est-ce qui travaille avec vous ?» Note «qui» at the end, not «que», because we ask about a person doing the action."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For 'with whom', put «avec» in front: «Avec qui vas-tu au cinéma ?» In English we'd often say 'Who are you going with?', but French never ends with the preposition… except in casual speech!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu vas au cinéma avec qui ?", "en": "Who are you going to the cinema with?", "note": "Casual: «avec qui» at the end."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Who did you go to the cinema with?' (standard, with est-ce que)", "choices": ["Qui est-ce que tu es allé au cinéma avec ?", "Avec qui est-ce que tu es allé au cinéma ?", "Avec qui tu es allé est-ce que au cinéma ?"], "answer": "Avec qui est-ce que tu es allé au cinéma ?", "right": "Super ! «Avec qui» + «est-ce que» + normal order.", "wrong": "In French, «avec» stays glued to «qui» at the start: «Avec qui est-ce que tu es allé au cinéma ?» You can't leave «avec» alone at the end like English 'with'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Qui es-tu ?» / «Qui est-ce que tu es ?» / «Tu es qui ?». Subject: «Qui a mangé… ?». With: «Avec qui… ?»"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French with inversion: With whom are you going to the cinema?", "answer": "Avec qui vas-tu au cinéma ?", "accept": ["avec qui vas-tu au cinéma", "avec qui vas-tu au cinéma ?", "avec qui vas-tu au cinema", "avec qui vas-tu au cinema ?"], "hint": "«Avec qui vas-…»", "right": "Parfait ! «Avec qui vas-tu au cinéma ?»", "wrong": "«Avec qui» + inversion «vas-tu» + «au cinéma»: «Avec qui vas-tu au cinéma ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Qui travaille avec vous ?»", "fr": "Qui travaille avec vous ?"}]}, "17-6": {"stageId": "17-6", "title": "Practice: questions with qui and avec qui", "goal": "By the end you can turn an answer into the matching 'who' or 'with whom' question.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we play detective: I give you an answer, you find the question! Trick: replace the person with «qui»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tom part en vacances demain. → Qui part en vacances demain ?", "en": "Tom is going on holiday tomorrow. → Who is going on holiday tomorrow?", "note": "«Tom» is replaced by «qui»; the rest stays the same."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Answer: «Charlotte a mangé mon gâteau.» What is the question?", "choices": ["Qui a mangé mon gâteau ?", "Avec qui a mangé mon gâteau ?", "Que a mangé mon gâteau ?"], "answer": "Qui a mangé mon gâteau ?", "right": "Yes! Charlotte (a person, the subject) → «qui»: «Qui a mangé mon gâteau ?»", "wrong": "Charlotte is a person doing the action, so we replace her with «qui»: «Qui a mangé mon gâteau ?». «que» is for things."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Answer: «Richard a regardé la télé toute la journée.» Which question with «est-ce qui» is correct?", "choices": ["Qui est-ce que a regardé la télé toute la journée ?", "Qui est-ce qui a regardé la télé toute la journée ?", "Qui a-t-il regardé la télé toute la journée ?"], "answer": "Qui est-ce qui a regardé la télé toute la journée ?", "right": "Bravo ! When «qui» is the subject, the long form is «Qui est-ce qui».", "wrong": "«qui» here is the subject (who did it?), so the long form ends in «qui»: «Qui est-ce qui a regardé la télé… ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch out! If the answer contains «avec + person», the question needs «avec qui»: «Il a joué avec Tom.» → «Avec qui a-t-il joué ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Answer: «Il a joué avec Tom.» What is the question?", "choices": ["Qui a joué avec Tom ?", "Avec qui a-t-il joué ?", "Avec qui a-il joué ?"], "answer": "Avec qui a-t-il joué ?", "right": "Super ! We ask about Tom, who comes after «avec», so «Avec qui», and don't forget «-t-».", "wrong": "The unknown person is Tom, after «avec», so we need «Avec qui». With inversion, «a» + «il» needs «-t-»: «Avec qui a-t-il joué ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Avec qui est-ce qu'il a joué ?", "en": "Who did he play with?", "note": "Same question with «est-ce que»: no inversion needed."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: subject person → «Qui… ?» or «Qui est-ce qui… ?». Person after «avec» → «Avec qui… ?» or «Avec qui est-ce que… ?»"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Answer: «Je suis allé au cinéma avec Sophie.» Write the question with inversion (tu).", "answer": "Avec qui es-tu allé au cinéma ?", "accept": ["avec qui es-tu allé au cinéma", "avec qui es-tu allé au cinéma ?", "avec qui es-tu allé au cinema", "avec qui es-tu allée au cinéma ?", "avec qui es-tu allée au cinéma"], "hint": "«Avec qui es-…»", "right": "Parfait ! «Avec qui es-tu allé au cinéma ?»", "wrong": "Sophie comes after «avec» → «Avec qui». «je suis» becomes «es-tu»: «Avec qui es-tu allé au cinéma ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Qui part en vacances demain ?»", "fr": "Qui part en vacances demain ?"}]}, "17-7": {"stageId": "17-7", "title": "Practice: choosing the right form of quel", "goal": "By the end you can pick «quel», «quelle», «quels» or «quelles» quickly and correctly.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Quick reminder: «quel» = which/what, and it matches its noun like a chameleon. Your job: find the noun, check its gender and number."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ fruits préfèrent-ils ?» («fruit» is masculine)", "choices": ["Quel", "Quels", "Quelles"], "answer": "Quels", "right": "Yes! «fruits» is masculine plural → «quels».", "wrong": "Look for the «s»: «fruits» is plural, and «fruit» is masculine → «quels»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Sometimes the noun comes after «être»: «___ est le résultat du match ?» The noun is «résultat», masculine singular."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quel est le résultat du match ?", "en": "What is the match result?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'What is your favourite colour?' («couleur» is feminine)", "choices": ["Quel est ta couleur préférée ?", "Quelle est ta couleur préférée ?", "Quels est ta couleur préférée ?"], "answer": "Quelle est ta couleur préférée ?", "right": "Bravo ! «couleur» is feminine singular → «quelle», and «préférée» agrees too.", "wrong": "The noun after «est» is «couleur», feminine singular, so «quelle»: «Quelle est ta couleur préférée ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Good news for your ears: «quel», «quelle», «quels», «quelles» all sound the same: [kel]. The difference is only in writing."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Which shoes are you going to take?' («chaussure» is feminine)", "choices": ["Quels chaussures vas-tu prendre ?", "Quelle chaussures vas-tu prendre ?", "Quelles chaussures vas-tu prendre ?"], "answer": "Quelles chaussures vas-tu prendre ?", "right": "Super ! Feminine plural → «quelles».", "wrong": "«chaussures» is feminine AND plural, so we need both «-le» and «-s»: «quelles»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: find the noun → masculine or feminine? one or several? → «quel / quelle / quels / quelles»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: What is this tool? («outil» is masculine)", "answer": "Quel est cet outil ?", "accept": ["quel est cet outil", "quel est cet outil ?"], "hint": "«Quel est c… outil ?»", "right": "Parfait ! «outil» is masculine singular → «quel». And «cet» because «outil» starts with a vowel.", "wrong": "«outil» is masculine singular → «quel». 'this' before a vowel is «cet»: «Quel est cet outil ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quel genre de musique aimez-vous ?»", "fr": "Quel genre de musique aimez-vous ?"}]}, "17-8": {"stageId": "17-8", "title": "Questions with quel: time, books, favourites", "goal": "By the end you can ask 'What time is it?' and other «quel» questions, with inversion, est-ce que, or «être».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "One of the most useful questions in French uses «quel». How do you think you ask the time? «heure» means hour, and it's feminine…"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'What time is it?'", "choices": ["Quel heure est-il ?", "Quelle heure est-il ?", "Qu'est-ce que l'heure ?"], "answer": "Quelle heure est-il ?", "right": "Yes! «heure» is feminine → «Quelle heure est-il ?» Learn it by heart!", "wrong": "«heure» is feminine, so «quelle». The fixed phrase is «Quelle heure est-il ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "With other verbs you have two choices: inversion or «est-ce que». Both mean the same."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quel livre lis-tu en ce moment ?", "en": "What book are you reading at the moment?", "note": "Or: «Quel livre est-ce que tu lis en ce moment ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is the correct «est-ce que» version of «Quels légumes manges-tu ?»", "choices": ["Quels légumes est-ce que tu manges ?", "Quels légumes est-ce que manges-tu ?", "Est-ce que quels légumes tu manges ?"], "answer": "Quels légumes est-ce que tu manges ?", "right": "Bravo ! «quel» + noun, then «est-ce que», then normal order «tu manges».", "wrong": "With «est-ce que» we don't invert: «Quels légumes» + «est-ce que» + «tu manges»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«quel» can also be separated from its noun by «être»: «Quels sont tes jeux préférés ?» It still agrees with «jeux»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quelle est la solution à ce problème, à ton avis ?", "en": "What is the solution to this problem, in your opinion?"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Don't use «est-ce que» with «quel + être»: say «Quel est ton sport préféré ?», never «Quel est-ce que est…». And note: «orange» never changes: «Quels légumes sont orange ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'What shoes are you going to wear?'", "choices": ["Quelles chaussures vas-tu mettre ?", "Quels chaussures vas-tu mettre ?", "Quelle chaussures vas-tu mettre ?"], "answer": "Quelles chaussures vas-tu mettre ?", "right": "Super ! «chaussures» = feminine plural → «quelles». «mettre» = to put on / wear.", "wrong": "«chaussures» is feminine plural, so «quelles»: «Quelles chaussures vas-tu mettre ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Quelle heure est-il ?», «quel» + noun + inversion or «est-ce que», and «Quel(le)(s) + être + noun» with inversion only."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: What are your (tu) favourite games?", "answer": "Quels sont tes jeux préférés ?", "accept": ["quels sont tes jeux préférés", "quels sont tes jeux préférés ?", "quels sont tes jeux preferes", "quels sont tes jeux preferes ?"], "hint": "«Quels sont tes…»", "right": "Parfait ! «jeux» is masculine plural → «quels» and «préférés».", "wrong": "«jeux» is masculine plural → «Quels sont tes jeux préférés ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quelle heure est-il, s'il vous plaît ?»", "fr": "Quelle heure est-il, s'il vous plaît ?"}]}, "17-9": {"stageId": "17-9", "title": "Quel after prepositions: at what time? in which…?", "goal": "By the end you can ask questions like 'At what time…?' and 'In which area…?' with «à / chez / avec / dans + quel».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In English you can say 'Which area do you live in?'. In French the preposition must come FIRST, before «quel»: «Dans quel quartier habites-tu ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you think we say 'At what time are you going to leave?'", "choices": ["Quelle heure allez-vous partir à ?", "À quelle heure allez-vous partir ?", "À quel heure allez-vous partir ?"], "answer": "À quelle heure allez-vous partir ?", "right": "Yes! «à» goes first, and «heure» is feminine → «À quelle heure… ?»", "wrong": "Two things: the preposition «à» comes first, and «heure» is feminine → «quelle»: «À quelle heure allez-vous partir ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "À quelle heure est-ce que vous allez partir ?", "en": "At what time are you going to leave?", "note": "Same question with «est-ce que»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«chez» means 'at someone's place', and also 'at' for some shops/jobs: «chez le coiffeur» = at the hairdresser's."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Chez quel coiffeur as-tu rendez-vous ?", "en": "At which hairdresser's do you have an appointment?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'With which tool do we cut grass?' («outil» is masculine)", "choices": ["Avec quel outil coupe-t-on l'herbe ?", "Avec quelle outil coupe-on l'herbe ?", "Quel outil avec coupe-t-on l'herbe ?"], "answer": "Avec quel outil coupe-t-on l'herbe ?", "right": "Bravo ! «avec» first, «quel» for masculine «outil», and «-t-» between «coupe» and «on».", "wrong": "«avec» comes first, «outil» is masculine → «quel», and «coupe» + «on» needs «-t-»: «Avec quel outil coupe-t-on l'herbe ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Answer: «On coupe l'herbe avec une tondeuse.» (a lawnmower). Answering with the same preposition is a good habit: «Dans quel quartier… ?» → «Dans le quartier nord.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'In which part of town do you live?' («quartier» is masculine)", "choices": ["Dans quelle quartier habites-tu ?", "Dans quel quartier habites-tu ?", "Quel quartier habites-tu dans ?"], "answer": "Dans quel quartier habites-tu ?", "right": "Super ! «dans» + «quel» (masculine) + inversion.", "wrong": "The preposition can't stay at the end in French. «quartier» is masculine: «Dans quel quartier habites-tu ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: preposition («à», «chez», «avec», «dans») + «quel/quelle/quels/quelles» + noun + question. Never leave the preposition at the end!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French with est-ce que: In which part of town do you live? (tu)", "answer": "Dans quel quartier est-ce que tu habites ?", "accept": ["dans quel quartier est-ce que tu habites", "dans quel quartier est-ce que tu habites ?"], "hint": "«Dans quel quartier est-ce que…»", "right": "Parfait !", "wrong": "«Dans quel quartier» + «est-ce que» + «tu habites»: «Dans quel quartier est-ce que tu habites ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «À quelle heure allez-vous partir ?»", "fr": "À quelle heure allez-vous partir ?"}]}, "17-10": {"stageId": "17-10", "title": "Quel or que? Both mean 'what'", "goal": "By the end you can choose between «quel» (+ noun) and «que» (+ verb).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«quel» and «que» can both translate 'what'. So how do we choose? Look at the word that comes right after!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quel fruit préfères-tu ?", "en": "What fruit do you prefer?", "note": "«quel» + a NOUN («fruit»)"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Que fais-tu ce soir ?", "en": "What are you doing this evening?", "note": "«que» + a VERB («fais»)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "So what's the rule?", "choices": ["«quel» + noun, «que» + verb", "«quel» + verb, «que» + noun", "They are always interchangeable"], "answer": "«quel» + noun, «que» + verb", "right": "Exactly! «quel» is an adjective, so it goes with a noun. «que» is a pronoun, followed by a verb or «est-ce que».", "wrong": "Look again: «Quel fruit» (noun) vs «Que fais-tu» (verb). «quel» + noun, «que» + verb (or «est-ce que»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«que» never changes its spelling, except «qu'» before a vowel: «Qu'aimez-vous regarder à la télé ?», «Qu'est-ce que vous aimez lire ?». «quel» has four spellings."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ journal lis-tu le matin ?»", "choices": ["Que", "Qu'", "Quel"], "answer": "Quel", "right": "Bravo ! «journal» is a noun (masculine) → «quel».", "wrong": "After the blank comes «journal», a noun. Nouns take «quel»: «Quel journal lis-tu le matin ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ est-ce que vous aimez lire ?»", "choices": ["Quel", "Qu'", "Quelle"], "answer": "Qu'", "right": "Super ! Before «est-ce que» we use «que», shortened to «qu'».", "wrong": "There's no noun here, just «est-ce que». So we need «que», which becomes «qu'» before the vowel: «Qu'est-ce que vous aimez lire ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Careful with «être»: «Quelle est ta couleur préférée ?» uses «quelle», because the question is really about the noun «couleur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Which computer is the most powerful?'", "choices": ["Quel ordinateur est le plus puissant ?", "Qu'ordinateur est le plus puissant ?", "Que ordinateur est le plus puissant ?"], "answer": "Quel ordinateur est le plus puissant ?", "right": "Parfait ! «ordinateur» is a noun → «quel».", "wrong": "«ordinateur» is a noun, so «quel» (masculine): «Quel ordinateur est le plus puissant ?». «que» never sits in front of a noun."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: noun next? → «quel(le)(s)». Verb or «est-ce que» next? → «que / qu'»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French with inversion: What are you eating? (tu)", "answer": "Que manges-tu ?", "accept": ["que manges-tu", "que manges-tu ?"], "hint": "«Que …-tu ?»", "right": "Parfait ! A verb follows, so «que».", "wrong": "A verb comes next, so we use «que» + inversion: «Que manges-tu ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say both: «Quel fruit préfères-tu ? Que fais-tu ce soir ?»", "fr": "Quel fruit préfères-tu ? Que fais-tu ce soir ?"}]}, "17-11": {"stageId": "17-11", "title": "Lequel? Which one?", "goal": "By the end you can ask 'which one(s)?' with «lequel», «laquelle», «lesquels» and «lesquelles».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Look at «lequel»: it's «le» + «quel» glued together! It means 'which one', and it replaces a noun you already mentioned."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«laquelle» = «la» + «quelle». So what kind of noun does it replace?", "choices": ["Masculine singular", "Feminine singular", "Feminine plural"], "answer": "Feminine singular", "right": "Yes! «la» and «quelle» are both feminine singular, so «laquelle» replaces a feminine singular noun.", "wrong": "Look at the parts: «la» (feminine singular) + «quelle» (feminine singular) → «laquelle» = which one (feminine)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "So the four forms are: «lequel» (m.), «laquelle» (f.), «lesquels» (m. pl.), «lesquelles» (f. pl.)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai beaucoup de disques. Lequel veux-tu écouter ?", "en": "I have lots of records. Which one do you want to listen to?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ de ces chemises vas-tu porter ?» (one shirt; «chemise» is feminine)", "choices": ["Lequel", "Laquelle", "Lesquelles"], "answer": "Laquelle", "right": "Bravo ! One feminine shirt → «laquelle».", "wrong": "You will wear ONE shirt, and «chemise» is feminine → «laquelle»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Common pattern: «lequel / laquelle… de ces + noun»: «Laquelle de ces chemises… ?» = Which one of these shirts…? Or «Parmi…» (among): «Parmi tous ces livres, lesquels préfères-tu ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a shop: «Nous avons plusieurs paires de chaussures. ___ voulez-vous essayer ?» (several shoes)", "choices": ["Lesquels", "Lesquelles", "Laquelle"], "answer": "Lesquelles", "right": "Super ! «chaussures» is feminine plural → «lesquelles».", "wrong": "We're talking about several «chaussures» (feminine plural) → «lesquelles»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «lequel» = «le» + «quel»; choose the form to match the noun you replace. Then the verb is usually inverted: «Lequel veux-tu ?»"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Among all these books, which ones do you prefer? (tu)", "answer": "Parmi tous ces livres, lesquels préfères-tu ?", "accept": ["parmi tous ces livres, lesquels préfères-tu ?", "parmi tous ces livres, lesquels préfères-tu", "parmi tous ces livres lesquels préfères-tu", "parmi tous ces livres, lesquels preferes-tu ?"], "hint": "«Parmi tous ces livres, les…»", "right": "Parfait ! «livres» is masculine plural → «lesquels».", "wrong": "«livres» is masculine plural → «lesquels»: «Parmi tous ces livres, lesquels préfères-tu ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Laquelle de ces chemises vas-tu porter ?»", "fr": "Laquelle de ces chemises vas-tu porter ?"}]}, "17-12": {"stageId": "17-12", "title": "Oui or si? Two ways to say yes", "goal": "By the end you know when to say «oui» and when to say «si», and the other meanings of «si».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«oui» is the normal word for yes. You use it to answer a positive question: «Est-ce que tu aimes chanter ? — Oui, j'aime chanter.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "But French has a secret weapon: «si». It's a 'yes' that contradicts a NEGATIVE question. English needs a whole sentence: 'Yes, I do!'"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu ne me crois pas ? — Si, je te crois.", "en": "You don't believe me? — Yes, I do believe you."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous ne voulez pas venir avec nous ?» You DO want to come. You answer:", "choices": ["Oui, bien sûr.", "Si, bien sûr.", "Non, bien sûr."], "answer": "Si, bien sûr.", "right": "Parfait ! The question is negative («ne… pas») and you disagree with it, so «si».", "wrong": "The question has «ne… pas», so it's negative. To say 'yes, I do' against a negative question, French uses «si»: «Si, bien sûr.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Est-ce que vous parlez français ?» You do. You answer:", "choices": ["Si, nous parlons français.", "Oui, nous parlons français.", "Non, nous parlons français."], "answer": "Oui, nous parlons français.", "right": "Yes! A positive question → «oui».", "wrong": "There is no «ne… pas» in this question, so it's positive. A positive question gets «oui»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: «si» = 'yes, on the contrary!'. You can even say it alone, with emotion: «Il n'a pas dit ça ?! — Si !» = He didn't say that?! — Yes, he did!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«si» has other jobs too. It can mean 'if': «Je viendrai si je peux.» It can mean 'so': «Je suis si fatigué.» And 'as' in a negative comparison: «Il n'est pas si drôle qu'il le croit.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «si» mean in «Je viendrai si je peux»?", "choices": ["yes", "if", "so"], "answer": "if", "right": "Bravo ! 'I will come if I can.'", "wrong": "Here «si» links two ideas with a condition: 'I will come IF I can.' «si» = if."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: positive question → «oui». Negative question, and you disagree → «si». And «si» can also mean 'if', 'so' or 'as'."}, {"t": "type", "q": "«Tu n'aimes pas le chocolat ?» You love it! Write: Yes, I like chocolate.", "answer": "Si, j'aime le chocolat.", "accept": ["si, j'aime le chocolat.", "si, j'aime le chocolat", "si j'aime le chocolat", "si, j’aime le chocolat."], "hint": "The question is negative…", "right": "Parfait ! «Si» contradicts the negative question.", "wrong": "The question is negative and you disagree, so start with «si»: «Si, j'aime le chocolat.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Tu ne me crois pas ? — Si, je te crois.»", "fr": "Tu ne me crois pas ? Si, je te crois."}]}, "18-1": {"stageId": "18-1", "title": "Tricky plurals: chevaux, yeux, beaux, vieux", "goal": "By the end you can make irregular nouns and adjectives plural, and know adjectives that change meaning before/after the noun.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Most French nouns just add «-s» in the plural: «un livre» → «des livres». But some endings play by other rules. Let's play guess-the-plural!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«un journal» (a newspaper) → «des journaux». So what's the plural of «un cheval» (a horse)?", "choices": ["des chevals", "des chevaux", "des chevaus"], "answer": "des chevaux", "right": "Yes! Most nouns in «-al» become «-aux»: «cheval» → «chevaux», «animal» → «animaux».", "wrong": "Like «journal» → «journaux», nouns in «-al» usually become «-aux»: «des chevaux». Exception: «des festivals»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Nouns in «-au», «-eau» and «-eu» take «-x»: «un cadeau» → «des cadeaux», «un jeu» → «des jeux», «un cheveu» → «des cheveux». Exceptions: «des pneus» (tyres), «des bleus» (bruises)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les enfants ont reçu des cadeaux et des jeux.", "en": "The children received presents and games."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Nouns in «-ou» take «-s» («des trous»), except seven that take «-x». Memory chant: «bijou, caillou, chou, genou, hibou, joujou, pou» → «des bijoux, des cailloux, des choux, des genoux, des hiboux, des joujoux, des poux»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which plural is correct?", "choices": ["des genous", "des genoux", "des genaux"], "answer": "des genoux", "right": "Bravo ! «genou» is one of the famous seven «-ou» nouns that take «-x».", "wrong": "«genou» (knee) is in the list of seven: bijou, caillou, chou, genou, hibou, joujou, pou. They all take «-x»: «des genoux»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Nouns ending in «-s», «-x» or «-z» don't change at all: «le prix» → «les prix», «un nez» → «des nez». And a few are totally irregular: «un œil» → «des yeux», «monsieur» → «messieurs», «madame» → «mesdames»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'She has blue eyes.' Complete: «Elle a les ___ bleus.»", "choices": ["œils", "yeux", "œuils"], "answer": "yeux", "right": "Super ! «un œil», «des yeux»: a completely different word in the plural.", "wrong": "«œil» has a special plural: «yeux». So: «Elle a les yeux bleus.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Adjectives follow similar patterns: «normal» → «normaux», «nouveau» → «nouveaux», «beau» → «beaux». But «vieux», «frais» and «jaloux» already end in «-x» or «-s», so the masculine plural doesn't change."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai acheté un nouveau chapeau pas cher pour remplacer ma vieille casquette.", "en": "I bought a cheap new hat to replace my old cap.", "note": "Before a vowel or silent h: «un bel homme», «un vieil homme», «un nouvel ami»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The old man walks in the garden.' Choose:", "choices": ["Le vieux homme marche dans le jardin.", "Le vieil homme marche dans le jardin.", "Le vieille homme marche dans le jardin."], "answer": "Le vieil homme marche dans le jardin.", "right": "Parfait ! Before a masculine noun starting with a vowel or silent h, «vieux» becomes «vieil».", "wrong": "«homme» starts with a silent h, so «vieux» changes to «vieil» to sound smooth: «le vieil homme». «vieille» is feminine."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Last trick: some adjectives change meaning with their position! Before the noun = figurative, after = literal: «sa propre maison» (his own house) vs «une maison propre» (a clean house)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«une ancienne église» means…", "choices": ["an old church", "a former church", "a clean church"], "answer": "a former church", "right": "Bravo ! «ancien» before the noun = former. After the noun, «une église ancienne» = an old church.", "wrong": "Position matters: «ancien» BEFORE the noun means 'former' (it's not a church any more). After the noun it means 'old'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Other pairs: «un cher ami» (a dear friend) / «un livre cher» (an expensive book); «une grande femme» (a great woman) / «une femme grande» (a tall woman); «un pauvre homme» (a poor wretch) / «un homme pauvre» (a man with no money)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «-al» → «-aux», «-eau/-eu» → «-x», seven «-ou» → «-x», «-s/-x/-z» don't change, «œil» → «yeux». «beau/nouveau/vieux» → «bel/nouvel/vieil» before a vowel. Position can change meaning."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I bought fresh pasta. («les pâtes» is feminine plural)", "answer": "J'ai acheté des pâtes fraîches.", "accept": ["j'ai acheté des pâtes fraîches", "j'ai acheté des pâtes fraîches.", "j’ai acheté des pâtes fraîches", "j'ai acheté des pâtes fraiches", "j'ai achete des pates fraiches"], "hint": "«frais» → feminine «fraîche»…", "right": "Parfait ! «frais» → «fraîche» → «fraîches».", "wrong": "«pâtes» is feminine plural, and «frais» becomes «fraîche» in the feminine, so: «des pâtes fraîches»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il a sa propre voiture.»", "fr": "Il a sa propre voiture."}]}, "18-2": {"stageId": "18-2", "title": "Useful adjectives to describe people and things", "goal": "By the end you can use common adjectives in the masculine and feminine and avoid classic traps like «neuf/nouveau» and «vite/rapide».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today: a toolbox of useful adjectives! First, notice the patterns between masculine and feminine; they make learning 100 adjectives much easier."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«dangereux» → «dangereuse», «ennuyeux» → «ennuyeuse». So what is the feminine of «délicieux»?", "choices": ["délicieuxe", "délicieuse", "délicieusse"], "answer": "délicieuse", "right": "Yes! «-eux» becomes «-euse»: «une tarte délicieuse».", "wrong": "Follow the pattern: «-eux» → «-euse». «délicieux» → «délicieuse»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Other patterns: «actif» → «active», «dernier» → «dernière». And adjectives ending in «-e» don't change: «facile», «drôle», «bête», «honnête»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ma sœur est drôle, bavarde et très active.", "en": "My sister is funny, talkative and very active.", "note": "«drôle» stays the same; «bavard» → «bavarde»; «actif» → «active»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Cette femme est très ___.» (gentil = kind)", "choices": ["gentile", "gentille", "gentil"], "answer": "gentille", "right": "Bravo ! «gentil» doubles the L: «gentille».", "wrong": "«gentil» is a little irregular: it doubles the L in the feminine → «gentille». Same idea: «fou» → «folle», «gros» → «grosse»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Fun colloquial words: «chouette» = great/fantastic (also the word for an owl!), «barbant» = boring. Very common among French friends."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Trap 1: 'new' has two words. «neuf» = brand new (never used); «nouveau» = new to me / different. «une voiture neuve» is fresh from the factory."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai acheté une voiture neuve plutôt qu'une voiture d'occasion.", "en": "I bought a new car rather than a second-hand car."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Trap 2: «vite» is an adverb (how you do something), «rapide» is an adjective (what something is). Complete: «Cet athlète court très ___.»", "choices": ["rapide", "vite", "rapides"], "answer": "vite", "right": "Super ! «court» (runs) is a verb, so we describe it with the adverb «vite». «Il est rapide» uses the adjective.", "wrong": "We're describing the action «court» (runs), so we need the adverb «vite». «rapide» describes a noun: «Il est rapide.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ce bâtiment est une ancienne église.» What is this building?", "choices": ["An old church that is still a church", "A former church", "An expensive church"], "answer": "A former church", "right": "Exactly! «ancien» before the noun = former.", "wrong": "Remember: «ancien» before the noun = former. «C'est une peinture ancienne» (after) = an old painting."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «-eux» → «-euse», «-if» → «-ive», «-er» → «-ère», «-e» stays. Watch «gentille», «folle», «grosse», «neuf/nouveau» and «vite/rapide»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: He is a calm and hardworking pupil.", "answer": "C'est un élève tranquille et travailleur.", "accept": ["c'est un élève tranquille et travailleur", "c'est un élève tranquille et travailleur.", "c’est un élève tranquille et travailleur", "c'est un eleve tranquille et travailleur", "il est un élève tranquille et travailleur"], "hint": "«C'est un élève tranquille et…»", "right": "Parfait ! «C'est un élève tranquille et travailleur.»", "wrong": "Use «C'est un…» before a noun with adjectives: «C'est un élève tranquille et travailleur.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Describe yourself out loud with three adjectives, e.g. «Je suis drôle, gentil et un peu bavard.» (women: «gentille», «bavarde»)", "fr": "Je suis drôle, gentil et un peu bavard."}]}, "19-1": {"stageId": "19-1", "title": "Adverbs: slowly, well, often", "goal": "By the end you can make adverbs from adjectives with «-ment», know the irregular ones (bien, mal, mieux) and put adverbs in the right place.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "An adverb tells you HOW, WHEN, WHERE or HOW MUCH. Good news: adverbs never change, so no gender, no plural! «Il marche lentement.» = He walks slowly."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "English adverbs often end in '-ly'. Look at «lentement» (slowly). Which French ending matches '-ly'?", "choices": ["-ment", "-ent", "-té"], "answer": "-ment", "right": "Yes! «-ment» is the French '-ly': «lentement», «clairement», «sérieusement».", "wrong": "Compare «lent» (slow) and «lentement» (slowly): the extra part is «-ment», the French version of '-ly'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The recipe: take the FEMININE adjective and add «-ment». «lent» → «lente» → «lentement»; «sérieux» → «sérieuse» → «sérieusement»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«heureux» → «heureuse». So 'fortunately' is…", "choices": ["heureuxment", "heureusement", "heureument"], "answer": "heureusement", "right": "Bravo ! Feminine «heureuse» + «-ment» = «heureusement».", "wrong": "Start from the feminine «heureuse», then add «-ment»: «heureusement». Its opposite: «malheureusement» (unfortunately)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "If the masculine adjective ends in a vowel, just add «-ment»: «vrai» → «vraiment», «poli» → «poliment», «rapide» → «rapidement». No feminine needed."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two special endings: «-ent» → «-emment» and «-ant» → «-amment». Both sound like «-amment»: «évident» → «évidemment», «courant» → «couramment»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle parle couramment français.", "en": "She speaks French fluently."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«patient» → ?", "choices": ["patientement", "patiemment", "patiamment"], "answer": "patiemment", "right": "Super ! «-ent» → «-emment»: «patiemment». It's pronounced «pa-sya-man».", "wrong": "Adjectives in «-ent» swap it for «-emment»: «patient» → «patiemment». («-ant» would give «-amment».)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some essential adverbs are irregular, learn them by heart: «bon» → «bien» (well), «mauvais» → «mal» (badly), «meilleur» → «mieux» (better), «gentil» → «gentiment», «bref» → «brièvement»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Where does the adverb go? 'I often played tennis.'", "choices": ["J'ai joué souvent au tennis.", "J'ai souvent joué au tennis.", "Souvent j'ai au tennis joué."], "answer": "J'ai souvent joué au tennis.", "right": "Parfait ! With a compound tense, short adverbs like «souvent», «bien», «mal», «toujours» go between the auxiliary and the past participle.", "wrong": "In the passé composé, short adverbs («souvent», «bien», «déjà»…) sit between «ai» and «joué»: «J'ai souvent joué au tennis.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Simple tense: adverb right after the verb: «Il court vite.» Before an adjective or adverb: «très gentilles», «très souvent». And after quantity adverbs, «du/de la/des» become «de»: «beaucoup d'amis», «trop de limonade»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: feminine adjective + «-ment»; vowel ending + «-ment»; «-ent» → «-emment», «-ant» → «-amment»; irregulars «bien», «mal», «mieux». Short adverbs go after the verb or between auxiliary and participle."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Make the adverb from «gentil» (kind).", "answer": "gentiment", "accept": ["gentiment"], "hint": "It's irregular: no double L…", "right": "Bravo ! «gentiment» (kindly), an irregular one.", "wrong": "«gentil» is irregular: the adverb is «gentiment», not «gentillement»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je parle assez doucement et je marche très lentement car mes enfants dorment paisiblement.»", "fr": "Je parle assez doucement et je marche très lentement car mes enfants dorment paisiblement."}]}, "19-2": {"stageId": "19-2", "title": "Bon or bien? Good or well?", "goal": "By the end you can choose between «bon» (good, adjective) and «bien» (well, adverb), including the tricky exceptions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "English speakers often mix these up, just like 'good' and 'well'. Basic rule: «bon» describes a noun, «bien» describes a verb."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce pain est bon.", "en": "This bread is good.", "note": "«bon» agrees: «Ces cerises sont bonnes.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le bébé a bien dormi.", "en": "The baby slept well.", "note": "«bien» describes the verb «dormir» and never changes."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ma mère cuisine ___.»", "choices": ["bon", "bonne", "bien"], "answer": "bien", "right": "Yes! We describe HOW she cooks (the verb), so «bien».", "wrong": "«cuisine» here is the verb 'cooks'. To say HOW she cooks we need the adverb «bien»: «Ma mère cuisine bien.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Cette soupe est très ___.»", "choices": ["bien", "bonne", "bon"], "answer": "bonne", "right": "Bravo ! It's about the taste of the soup (a noun, feminine), so «bonne».", "wrong": "We describe «soupe», a feminine noun, so we need the adjective «bon» in the feminine: «bonne»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Exception 1: «bon» works like an adverb after «sentir» (smell), «faire» (weather) and «tenir» (hold), and it doesn't change: «Ces roses sentent bon.», «Il fait bon.», «Tiens bon !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'This perfume smells nice.'", "choices": ["Ce parfum sent bien.", "Ce parfum sent bon.", "Ce parfum sent bonne."], "answer": "Ce parfum sent bon.", "right": "Super ! After «sentir», use «bon» for a nice smell.", "wrong": "It's a fixed exception: with «sentir», a nice smell is «sentir bon»: «Ce parfum sent bon.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Exception 2: «bien» can describe a noun for a moral or quality judgement, and it still never changes: «C'est une femme bien.» = She's a good (decent) woman. «Ce programme est très bien.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Classic combo: «Tu as une bonne note, c'est bien !» = You have a good mark, well done! «bonne» for the mark (noun), «c'est bien» as a judgement."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'He is a good doctor.'", "choices": ["C'est un bien docteur.", "C'est un bon docteur.", "C'est un docteur bon."], "answer": "C'est un bon docteur.", "right": "Parfait ! Before a noun, «bon» is the normal adjective, and it goes before the noun.", "wrong": "Describing the noun «docteur» → adjective «bon», placed before the noun: «C'est un bon docteur.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «bon/bonne/bons/bonnes» + noun; «bien» + verb (never changes). Exceptions: «sentir bon», «il fait bon», «tenir bon», and «c'est bien / une femme bien»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We have understood well. (use «avons»)", "answer": "Nous avons bien compris.", "accept": ["nous avons bien compris", "nous avons bien compris."], "hint": "The adverb goes between «avons» and «compris».", "right": "Bravo ! «bien» sits between the auxiliary and the participle.", "wrong": "«bien» describes the verb and goes between «avons» and «compris»: «Nous avons bien compris.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Tu vas bien ? Il fait bon ce matin !»", "fr": "Tu vas bien ? Il fait bon ce matin !"}]}, "19-3": {"stageId": "19-3", "title": "Practice: making adverbs of manner", "goal": "By the end you can quickly turn adjectives into «-ment» adverbs, including the «-ément» ones.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Practice time! Remember the recipe: masculine → feminine → add «-ment». Let's train your reflexes."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "doux → douce → doucement", "en": "soft → softly / gently", "note": "«Parle doucement !» = Speak softly!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«vif» (lively, sharp) → feminine «vive» → adverb?", "choices": ["vifment", "vivement", "vivment"], "answer": "vivement", "right": "Yes! «vive» + «-ment» = «vivement» (sharply, vividly).", "wrong": "Always build from the feminine: «vif» → «vive» → «vivement»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«frais» → «fraîche» → ?", "choices": ["fraisement", "fraîchement", "fraîchment"], "answer": "fraîchement", "right": "Bravo ! «fraîchement» = freshly/newly: «fraîchement peint» = freshly painted.", "wrong": "The feminine is «fraîche», so the adverb is «fraîche» + «-ment» = «fraîchement». Keep the «e»!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Adjectives already ending in «-e» stay the same in the feminine, so just add «-ment»: «admirable» → «admirablement», «paisible» → «paisiblement»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "A few adverbs take «-ément» with an accent: «énorme» → «énormément», «précis» → «précisément», «intense» → «intensément». «J'aime énormément le chocolat !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«précis» → «précise» → ?", "choices": ["précisement", "précisément", "précisemment"], "answer": "précisément", "right": "Super ! This one takes an accent: «précisément».", "wrong": "«précis» is one of the special ones: the «e» gets an accent → «précisément»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a souri mystérieusement.", "en": "He smiled mysteriously.", "note": "«mystérieux» → «mystérieuse» → «mystérieusement»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: feminine + «-ment» («lentement», «grandement»), «-e» adjectives just add «-ment», and watch the accented ones: «énormément», «précisément», «intensément»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Make the adverb from «sec» (dry; feminine «sèche»).", "answer": "sèchement", "accept": ["sèchement", "sechement"], "hint": "Feminine «sèche» + …", "right": "Parfait ! «sèchement» = drily, curtly.", "wrong": "Start from the feminine «sèche» and add «-ment»: «sèchement»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Make the adverb from «lent» (slow).", "answer": "lentement", "accept": ["lentement"], "hint": "Feminine «lente» + …", "right": "Bravo ! «lente» + «-ment» = «lentement».", "wrong": "«lent» → «lente» → «lentement»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say these three out loud: «heureusement, doucement, énormément».", "fr": "heureusement, doucement, énormément"}]}, "19-4": {"stageId": "19-4", "title": "Everyday adverbs: time, frequency, place, quantity", "goal": "By the end you can use common adverbs like «déjà», «souvent», «ici», «trop» and «beaucoup» to tell a short story.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Some adverbs are tiny but you'll use them every day. Let's group them: time, frequency, manner, place and quantity."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Time: «aujourd'hui» (today), «hier» (yesterday), «demain» (tomorrow), «maintenant» (now), «bientôt» (soon), «déjà» (already), «tôt» (early), «tard» (late), «longtemps» (for a long time)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«À bientôt !» is a common goodbye. What does it mean?", "choices": ["See you soon!", "See you tomorrow!", "See you never!"], "answer": "See you soon!", "right": "Yes! «bientôt» = soon. «À demain !» = See you tomorrow!", "wrong": "«bientôt» means 'soon', so «À bientôt !» = See you soon! 'Tomorrow' would be «demain»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Frequency: «toujours» (always), «souvent» (often), «parfois» (sometimes), «jamais» (never, with «ne»: «Je ne mange jamais de viande.»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle va très souvent au cinéma.", "en": "She goes to the cinema very often."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Place: «ici» = here, «là-bas» = ?", "choices": ["over there", "everywhere", "nowhere"], "answer": "over there", "right": "Bravo ! «là-bas» = over there. «partout» = everywhere.", "wrong": "«là» = there, and «là-bas» = over there (further away). Everywhere is «partout»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Quantity: «assez» (enough), «beaucoup» (a lot), «très» (very), «trop» (too much), «plus» (more), «moins» (less). Don't say «très beaucoup»! Just «beaucoup»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'We arrived too late.'", "choices": ["Nous sommes arrivés très tard.", "Nous sommes arrivés trop tard.", "Nous sommes arrivés beaucoup tard."], "answer": "Nous sommes arrivés trop tard.", "right": "Super ! 'too' = «trop». «très tard» would just be 'very late'.", "wrong": "'too' (more than you want) = «trop». «très» = very, and «beaucoup» can't go before an adverb like «tard»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Malheureusement, le musée était déjà fermé.", "en": "Unfortunately, the museum was already closed."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: time («hier, demain, déjà, tôt, tard»), frequency («toujours, souvent, parfois, jamais»), place («ici, là-bas, partout»), quantity («assez, beaucoup, très, trop, plus, moins»)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Tomorrow, we will leave earlier. (use «nous partirons»)", "answer": "Demain, nous partirons plus tôt.", "accept": ["demain, nous partirons plus tôt.", "demain, nous partirons plus tôt", "demain nous partirons plus tôt", "demain, nous partirons plus tot"], "hint": "'earlier' = «plus» + 'early'", "right": "Parfait ! «plus tôt» = earlier.", "wrong": "'earlier' = «plus tôt» (more early): «Demain, nous partirons plus tôt.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Hier, nous avons marché assez vite, mais nous sommes arrivés trop tard.»", "fr": "Hier, nous avons marché assez vite, mais nous sommes arrivés trop tard."}]}, "19-5": {"stageId": "19-5", "title": "Adverbs and their opposites", "goal": "By the end you can use pairs of opposite adverbs like «souvent / rarement», «partout / nulle part» and «gentiment / méchamment».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Learning words in opposite pairs is a great memory trick: one word helps you remember the other. Let's start with time."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "avant-hier → après-demain", "en": "the day before yesterday → the day after tomorrow", "note": "Literally 'before-yesterday' and 'after-tomorrow'!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The opposite of «toujours» (always) is…", "choices": ["souvent", "jamais", "tôt"], "answer": "jamais", "right": "Yes! «toujours» ↔ «jamais». And «souvent» ↔ «rarement».", "wrong": "'always' ↔ 'never': «toujours» ↔ «jamais». «souvent» (often) has its own opposite: «rarement»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Quantity pairs: «beaucoup» ↔ «peu», «plus» ↔ «moins», «trop» ↔ «pas assez». Place pairs: «ici» ↔ «ailleurs» (elsewhere), «partout» ↔ «nulle part»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I can't find my keys anywhere / They are nowhere!' Which adverb means 'nowhere'?", "choices": ["partout", "nulle part", "ailleurs"], "answer": "nulle part", "right": "Bravo ! «nulle part» = nowhere: «Je ne trouve mes clés nulle part.»", "wrong": "«partout» = everywhere and «ailleurs» = elsewhere. 'Nowhere' is «nulle part» (used with «ne»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Many manner opposites just add «mal-» or «in-»: «heureusement» ↔ «malheureusement», «adroitement» ↔ «maladroitement», «consciemment» ↔ «inconsciemment»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The opposite of «adroitement» (skilfully) is…", "choices": ["inadroitement", "maladroitement", "méchamment"], "answer": "maladroitement", "right": "Super ! «mal-» + «adroitement» = «maladroitement» (clumsily).", "wrong": "Here the opposite is made with «mal-»: «maladroitement» (clumsily). «méchamment» is the opposite of «gentiment»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Other useful pairs: «gentiment» ↔ «méchamment» (nastily), «rapidement/vite» ↔ «lentement», «silencieusement» ↔ «bruyamment», «doucement» ↔ «fermement», «joyeusement» ↔ «tristement»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les enfants jouent bruyamment, puis ils dorment silencieusement.", "en": "The children play noisily, then they sleep quietly."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Opposite of «tôt» (early)?", "choices": ["tard", "bientôt", "autrefois"], "answer": "tard", "right": "Parfait ! «tôt» ↔ «tard». «Je me lève tôt, je me couche tard.»", "wrong": "'early' ↔ 'late': «tôt» ↔ «tard». «autrefois» (in the past) is the opposite of «maintenant»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «toujours/jamais», «souvent/rarement», «tôt/tard», «beaucoup/peu», «partout/nulle part», «bien/mal», «vite/lentement», and the «mal-»/«in-» pairs."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the opposite of «gentiment».", "answer": "méchamment", "accept": ["méchamment", "mechamment"], "hint": "From «méchant» (nasty), «-ant» → «-amment»", "right": "Bravo ! «méchant» → «méchamment», like «bruyant» → «bruyamment».", "wrong": "The opposite is «méchamment» (nastily), from «méchant» with the «-ant» → «-amment» rule."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je me lève tôt, mais je me couche tard.»", "fr": "Je me lève tôt, mais je me couche tard."}]}, "20-1": {"stageId": "20-1", "title": "Futur proche & the -ant form", "goal": "By the end you can say what is going to happen with «aller» + infinitive, use «être sur le point de», and form the present participle («en écoutant»).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "You already know «aller» = to go. In French, as in English, 'going to' can talk about the near future: «Je vais manger.» = I'm going to eat. The recipe is «aller» in the present + an infinitive."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Look at «Je vais manger». Which part tells you WHO and WHEN, and which part stays unchanged?", "choices": ["«vais» changes, «manger» stays an infinitive", "Both words change", "«manger» changes, «vais» stays"], "answer": "«vais» changes, «manger» stays an infinitive", "right": "Exactly! Only «aller» is conjugated; the second verb is always the infinitive: «tu vas manger», «ils vont manger».", "wrong": "Only «aller» changes with the person («je vais, tu vas, ils vont»). The action verb stays in the infinitive: «manger», «finir», «prendre»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les filles vont faire un gâteau pour l'anniversaire de leur mère.", "en": "The girls are going to make a cake for their mother's birthday.", "note": "«aller»: je vais, tu vas, il/elle/on va, nous allons, vous allez, ils/elles vont"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Tom and I are going to eat at the restaurant.' Choose the right French.", "choices": ["Tom et moi allons manger au restaurant.", "Tom et moi vont manger au restaurant.", "Tom et moi allons mangé au restaurant."], "answer": "Tom et moi allons manger au restaurant.", "right": "Bravo! «Tom et moi» = «nous», so «allons», then the infinitive «manger».", "wrong": "«Tom et moi» means 'we', so we need «nous allons». And the second verb must be the infinitive «manger», not the participle «mangé»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Even closer to now: «être sur le point de» + infinitive = to be about to. Timeline: «je marche» (now) → «je suis sur le point de marcher» → «je vais marcher» → «je marcherai» (later)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis encore à la maison, mais je suis sur le point de sortir.", "en": "I'm still at home, but I'm about to go out."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'We are about to eat.' Which verb do you conjugate here?", "choices": ["Nous sommes sur le point de manger.", "Nous allons sur le point de manger.", "Nous avons sur le point de manger."], "answer": "Nous sommes sur le point de manger.", "right": "Parfait! The expression uses «être»: «nous sommes sur le point de…».", "wrong": "«Sur le point de» goes with «être» (to be about to): «je suis, tu es, nous sommes sur le point de…»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "With a time word, the plain present can also mean future: «L'avion arrive à 22 h ce soir.» = The plane arrives at 10pm tonight. Just like English!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the present participle, the French '-ing' form. Take the «nous» form of the present, remove «-ons», add «-ant»: «nous parlons» → «parlant», «nous prenons» → «prenant»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«nous finissons» → ?", "choices": ["finant", "finissant", "finisant"], "answer": "finissant", "right": "Super! Stem «finiss-» + «-ant» = «finissant». All -ir verbs like «finir» do this.", "wrong": "Start from «nous finissons», drop «-ons» → «finiss-», then add «-ant»: «finissant»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Only three exceptions: «avoir» → «ayant», «être» → «étant», «savoir» → «sachant»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Its star use: «en» + participle = two actions at the same time (while / by). «Elle cuisine en écoutant la radio.» = She cooks while listening to the radio. «On apprend en faisant des erreurs.» = We learn by making mistakes."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I love swimming.' Careful! Which is correct?", "choices": ["J'adore nageant.", "J'adore en nageant.", "J'adore nager."], "answer": "J'adore nager.", "right": "Well spotted! After a verb like «adorer», English '-ing' becomes a French infinitive: «J'adore nager».", "wrong": "Not every '-ing' becomes «-ant». After verbs like «adorer, aimer», and as a subject, French uses the infinitive: «J'adore nager», «Dormir est bon pour la santé»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «aller» + infinitive = going to; «être sur le point de» + infinitive = about to; «nous» stem + «-ant» = participle; «en» + participle = while/by doing."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Tom is eating while reading the newspaper.", "answer": "Tom mange en lisant le journal.", "accept": ["tom mange en lisant le journal", "tom mange en lisant le journal."], "hint": "«nous lisons» → «lis-ant». Use «en».", "right": "Excellent! «en lisant» = while reading.", "wrong": "Use «en» + participle: «nous lisons» → «lisant». So: «Tom mange en lisant le journal.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Mes voisins vont venir ce soir et mon mari est sur le point d'aller acheter du vin.»", "fr": "Mes voisins vont venir ce soir et mon mari est sur le point d'aller acheter du vin."}]}, "20-2": {"stageId": "20-2", "title": "Depuis or pendant? For, since, during", "goal": "By the end you can choose «depuis» for something still going on and «pendant» for a finished period.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "English 'for' has two French versions. The key question: is the action STILL happening, or is it finished?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je joue au tennis depuis 3 ans.", "en": "I have been playing tennis for 3 years.", "note": "I still play!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Look at the verb in «Je joue au tennis depuis 3 ans». Which tense does French use?", "choices": ["The present", "The passé composé", "The future"], "answer": "The present", "right": "Yes! Because the action is still going on, French uses the present, while English uses 'have been playing'.", "wrong": "«joue» is the present. With «depuis», the action is still going on, so French keeps the present tense (English says 'have been playing')."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«depuis» works with a length of time ('for 3 years') AND a starting point ('since 2016'): «Je joue au tennis depuis 2016.» One word for both!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I have been studying French for 4 years.'", "choices": ["J'ai étudié le français pendant 4 ans.", "J'étudie le français depuis 4 ans.", "J'étudie le français pour 4 ans."], "answer": "J'étudie le français depuis 4 ans.", "right": "Bravo! Still studying → present + «depuis».", "wrong": "You are still studying, so use the present + «depuis»: «J'étudie le français depuis 4 ans.» «Pendant» + passé composé would mean you stopped."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Questions: «Depuis combien de temps…?» (how long, a duration) and «Depuis quand…?» (since when, a starting point). «Depuis quand Hélène habite-t-elle en Angleterre ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«pendant» = for/during a period with a clear end. It usually goes with a past tense: «Tom a étudié le japonais pendant cinq ans.» He doesn't study it any more."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a plu pendant plus de trois heures.", "en": "It rained for more than three hours."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'We played for 3 hours.' (The game is over.)", "choices": ["Nous jouons depuis 3 heures.", "Nous avons joué pendant 3 heures.", "Nous avons joué depuis 3 heures."], "answer": "Nous avons joué pendant 3 heures.", "right": "Parfait! Finished action → passé composé + «pendant».", "wrong": "The game is over, so it's a closed period: passé composé + «pendant». «Nous jouons depuis 3 heures» would mean you are still playing."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«durant» is a more formal twin of «pendant»: «J'ai beaucoup appris durant mon stage.» And never translate this 'for' with «pour»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Paul ne conduit plus depuis son accident.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["Paul drove during his accident.", "Paul hasn't driven since his accident.", "Paul will drive after his accident."], "answer": "Paul hasn't driven since his accident.", "right": "Yes! «depuis» + a starting event = since, and the situation (not driving) continues today.", "wrong": "«depuis son accident» = since his accident; the situation is still true now: he still doesn't drive."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: still true now → present + «depuis» (for/since). Finished period → past + «pendant» (for/during)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Paul has been working here since 2009.", "answer": "Paul travaille ici depuis 2009.", "accept": ["paul travaille ici depuis 2009", "paul travaille ici depuis 2009."], "hint": "Present tense + «depuis».", "right": "Excellent! Present «travaille» + «depuis 2009».", "wrong": "He still works there, so: present + «depuis» → «Paul travaille ici depuis 2009.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud about yourself: «J'apprends le français depuis…» + a duration.", "fr": "J'apprends le français depuis deux ans."}]}, "20-3": {"stageId": "20-3", "title": "Porter, apporter, emporter", "goal": "By the end you can choose between «porter» (carry/wear), «apporter» (bring) and «emporter» (take away) for objects.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "These three verbs share a core: «porter» = to carry, or to wear clothes. The prefix changes the direction."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les élèves portent un uniforme.", "en": "The pupils wear a uniform."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom porte un pull rouge.» What is Tom doing?", "choices": ["He's bringing a red jumper.", "He's wearing a red jumper.", "He's taking away a red jumper."], "answer": "He's wearing a red jumper.", "right": "Yes! With clothes, «porter» = to wear.", "wrong": "«porter» means to carry or, with clothes, to wear. So Tom is wearing a red jumper."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«a-» points to the DESTINATION: «apporter» = to bring something somewhere and leave it there. Think 'a' for 'arrive'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je t'ai apporté des chocolats.", "en": "I brought you some chocolates."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«em-» points to the STARTING POINT: «emporter» = to leave a place taking something with you. Think 'em' = 'away from'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a café you see «Plats à emporter». Guess what it means!", "choices": ["Dishes to wear", "Take-away meals", "Dishes brought by guests"], "answer": "Take-away meals", "right": "Bravo! You leave the café and take the food with you: «à emporter».", "wrong": "«emporter» = to take something away from a place. «Plats à emporter» = take-away meals. «Sur place» = eat in."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'We ate at Paul's. We brought a cake.'", "choices": ["Nous avons emporté un gâteau.", "Nous avons apporté un gâteau.", "Nous avons porté un gâteau."], "answer": "Nous avons apporté un gâteau.", "right": "Parfait! The cake arrives at Paul's and stays there: «apporter».", "wrong": "The focus is the destination (Paul's house), where the cake is left: «apporter». «Emporter» would focus on leaving with it."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«apporter» can also mean to provide: «Le professeur apporte son soutien aux élèves.» = The teacher gives his support to the pupils."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'They are going on holiday and taking only one suitcase.'", "choices": ["Ils partent en vacances et emportent une seule valise.", "Ils partent en vacances et apportent une seule valise.", "Ils partent en vacances et amènent une seule valise."], "answer": "Ils partent en vacances et emportent une seule valise.", "right": "Super! They leave home with the suitcase: «emporter».", "wrong": "They leave a place with the suitcase, so «emporter». «Amener» is only for people, not objects."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "These verbs are for THINGS. For people we use «amener / emmener», the next lesson!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «porter» = carry/wear; «apporter» = bring (to a destination); «emporter» = take away (from a starting point)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Complete: Tu ___ toujours trop de choses quand tu pars en voyage. (you take with you)", "answer": "emportes", "accept": ["emportes"], "hint": "«em-» verb, «tu» form.", "right": "Bravo! «Tu emportes» = you take with you.", "wrong": "You leave with the things, so «emporter»; with «tu»: «emportes»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'ai apporté du vin et j'ai emporté le reste du gâteau.»", "fr": "J'ai apporté du vin et j'ai emporté le reste du gâteau."}]}, "20-4": {"stageId": "20-4", "title": "Mener, amener, emmener", "goal": "By the end you can choose «amener» or «emmener» to take people somewhere, and use «mener» (lead).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Same trick as last lesson, but for PEOPLE and animals. The core verb «mener» means to lead, or to carry out."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce chemin mène au village.", "en": "This path leads to the village."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«La police mène l'enquête.» What does «mène» mean here?", "choices": ["is bringing", "is carrying out", "is taking away"], "answer": "is carrying out", "right": "Yes! «mener une enquête» = to carry out (lead) an investigation.", "wrong": "«mener» = to lead or to carry out. «La police mène l'enquête» = The police are carrying out the investigation."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«amener» (a- = destination) = to take someone to a place, usually leaving them there."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'amène les enfants à la crèche, puis je vais au travail.", "en": "I take the children to the nursery, then I go to work."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Tom is taking his son to school.' (He drops him off.)", "choices": ["Tom amène son fils à l'école.", "Tom apporte son fils à l'école.", "Tom mène son fils à l'école."], "answer": "Tom amène son fils à l'école.", "right": "Bravo! Focus on the destination where the son stays: «amener».", "wrong": "A person taken to a place and left there: «amener». «Apporter» is for objects."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«emmener» (em- = starting point) = to take someone along with you when you leave."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce soir, je t'emmène au restaurant.", "en": "Tonight, I'm taking you out to the restaurant.", "note": "I go too!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Fred always takes his dog with him.'", "choices": ["Fred amène toujours son chien avec lui.", "Fred emmène toujours son chien avec lui.", "Fred emporte toujours son chien avec lui."], "answer": "Fred emmène toujours son chien avec lui.", "right": "Parfait! The dog goes along with Fred: «emmener». (Animals count like people.)", "wrong": "«avec lui» shows the dog comes along: «emmener». «Emporter» is only for objects."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Useful phrase: «Qu'est-ce qui t'amène ?» = What brings you here? And watch the accent: «j'emmène, tu emmènes» but «nous emmenons»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap for people: «amener» = take to (drop off), «emmener» = take along. For things: «apporter / emporter». And «mener» = lead / carry out."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Complete: Ma fille est malade, je dois l'___ chez le médecin.", "answer": "emmener", "accept": ["emmener"], "hint": "Infinitive after «dois»; you go with her.", "right": "Excellent! You go with her: «emmener» (infinitive after «devoir»).", "wrong": "You accompany your daughter, so «emmener», in the infinitive after «je dois»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ce soir, je t'emmène au théâtre.»", "fr": "Ce soir, je t'emmène au théâtre."}]}, "20-5": {"stageId": "20-5", "title": "20 more useful prepositions", "goal": "By the end you can use prepositions like «à cause de», «grâce à», «au lieu de», «malgré», «jusqu'à» and «selon».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's learn prepositions in pairs, it's easier to remember. First: «à cause de» (because of, often negative) vs «grâce à» (thanks to, positive)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous sommes meilleurs en chant grâce à notre professeur de musique.", "en": "We are better at singing thanks to our music teacher."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'He fell down because of you.'", "choices": ["Il est tombé grâce à toi.", "Il est tombé à cause de toi.", "Il est tombé malgré toi."], "answer": "Il est tombé à cause de toi.", "right": "Yes! A negative cause → «à cause de».", "wrong": "Falling is bad news, so «à cause de». «Grâce à» is for good results, and «malgré» means despite."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«au lieu de» = instead of; «malgré» = despite. «J'ai travaillé malgré mon bras cassé.» = I worked despite my broken arm."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Juliette a opté pour la robe rouge au lieu de la bleue.» Which dress did she choose?", "choices": ["The blue one", "The red one", "Both"], "answer": "The red one", "right": "Bravo! «au lieu de» = instead of: red instead of blue.", "wrong": "«au lieu de» = instead of. She chose the red dress instead of the blue one."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Time words: «dès» = from / as soon as, «jusqu'à» = until, «lors de» = during / at the time of, «durant» = during."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tom et Paul restent jusqu'à demain.", "en": "Tom and Paul are staying until tomorrow."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I will go on a diet from tomorrow.' Which word means 'from (right away)'?", "choices": ["dès", "vers", "parmi"], "answer": "dès", "right": "Parfait! «Je me mettrai au régime dès demain.»", "wrong": "«dès» = from / as soon as: «dès demain». «vers» = towards, «parmi» = among."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Opinion: «selon» and «d'après» = according to. Place: «à travers» (through), «près de» (near), «loin de» (far from), «vers» (towards), «parmi» (among)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Handy in shops and flats: «environ» (roughly), «excepté» (except), «hors» (excluding): «650 euros hors charges» = 650 euros not including bills. «par» = per: «10 euros par personne»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Selon les scientifiques, la Terre a 4,6 milliards d'années.» «Selon» means…", "choices": ["despite", "according to", "near"], "answer": "according to", "right": "Yes! «selon» = «d'après» = according to.", "wrong": "«selon» = according to (same as «d'après»). Despite = «malgré», near = «près de»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «à cause de / grâce à», «au lieu de», «malgré», «dès / jusqu'à», «selon / d'après», «à travers», «près de / loin de», «parmi», «environ»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The station is far from here.", "answer": "La gare est loin d'ici.", "accept": ["la gare est loin d'ici", "la gare est loin d ici", "la gare est loin d’ici"], "hint": "«loin de» + «ici» → «d'ici».", "right": "Excellent! «de» + «ici» becomes «d'ici».", "wrong": "«loin de» + «ici»: «de» elides before a vowel → «La gare est loin d'ici.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Grâce à mon professeur, je parle mieux malgré mes erreurs.»", "fr": "Grâce à mon professeur, je parle mieux malgré mes erreurs."}]}, "20-6": {"stageId": "20-6", "title": "Faux amis: false friends", "goal": "By the end you can avoid common false friends like «librairie», «stage», «location», «éventuellement» and «assister à».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "A «faux ami» is a French word that looks like an English word but means something else. They cause funny (or embarrassing!) mistakes."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You want to borrow books for free. Where do you go: a «librairie» or a «bibliothèque»?", "choices": ["une librairie", "une bibliothèque", "un stage"], "answer": "une bibliothèque", "right": "Bravo! A «bibliothèque» is a library. A «librairie» is a bookshop, where you pay!", "wrong": "Trap! «une librairie» = a bookshop. A library is «une bibliothèque»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Work words: «un stage» = an internship, «un patron» = a boss/manager, «un agenda» = a diary (not a meeting list)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mon futur patron m'a recommandé de lire ce livre avant mon stage.", "en": "My future boss recommended that I read this book before my internship."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je dois passer un examen demain.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["I have to pass an exam tomorrow.", "I have to take an exam tomorrow.", "I have to skip an exam tomorrow."], "answer": "I have to take an exam tomorrow.", "right": "Yes! «passer un examen» = to take (sit) an exam. To pass = «réussir un examen».", "wrong": "«passer un examen» only means to take it. If you succeed, you say «réussir un examen»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two verbs: «assister à» = to attend (not to help!), and «éventuellement» = possibly (not 'eventually', which is «finalement»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'assiste à une conférence.» means…", "choices": ["I'm helping at a conference.", "I'm attending a conference.", "I'm organising a conference."], "answer": "I'm attending a conference.", "right": "Parfait! «assister à» = to attend, to be present at.", "wrong": "«assister à» = to attend. To help is «aider»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "More traps: «une location» = a rental, «la monnaie» = loose change, «un coin» = a corner, «un raisin» = a grape (a raisin is «un raisin sec»), «blessé» = injured, «une journée» = a day."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone says «Tu es trop sensible.» What do they mean?", "choices": ["You're too reasonable.", "You're too sensitive.", "You're too sensible about money."], "answer": "You're too sensitive.", "right": "Bravo! «sensible» = sensitive. 'Sensible' in English is «raisonnable».", "wrong": "«sensible» = sensitive (emotionally). The English 'sensible' is «raisonnable»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «librairie» = bookshop, «stage» = internship, «patron» = boss, «agenda» = diary, «passer un examen» = take an exam, «assister à» = attend, «éventuellement» = possibly, «sensible» = sensitive."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I'm going to write the date in my diary.", "answer": "Je vais marquer la date dans mon agenda.", "accept": ["je vais marquer la date dans mon agenda", "je vais noter la date dans mon agenda", "je vais écrire la date dans mon agenda", "je vais marquer la date dans mon agenda.", "je vais noter la date dans mon agenda.", "je vais écrire la date dans mon agenda."], "hint": "Diary = «agenda». Use the futur proche.", "right": "Excellent! «agenda» = diary, a classic faux ami.", "wrong": "Diary = «un agenda». Futur proche: «Je vais marquer (noter) la date dans mon agenda.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Hier, je suis passée à la librairie et j'ai acheté un livre pour mon stage.»", "fr": "Hier, je suis passée à la librairie et j'ai acheté un livre pour mon stage."}]}, "21-1": {"stageId": "21-1", "title": "Le futur simple: I will…", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate regular verbs in the futur simple and use the key irregular stems «ir-», «aur-», «ser-», «fer-».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French has no little word like 'will'. Instead, the verb itself changes: «je parlerai» = I will speak. One word does the whole job!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ellen et Tony mangeront au restaurant ce soir.", "en": "Ellen and Tony will eat at the restaurant tonight."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Look at «mangeront». Which part is the infinitive, and which part is the ending?", "choices": ["«manger» + «-ont»", "«mange» + «-ront»", "«man» + «-geront»"], "answer": "«manger» + «-ont»", "right": "Exactly! For regular verbs the future stem is the whole infinitive, then you add the ending.", "wrong": "Split it as «manger» + «-ont»: the stem is the whole infinitive, and «-ont» is the 'they' ending."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The endings are: «-ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont». Do they remind you of something? They're almost the present of «avoir»: «j'ai, tu as, il a, nous avons, vous avez, ils ont»!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je parlerai, tu parleras, il parlera, nous parlerons, vous parlerez, ils parleront", "en": "I will speak, you will speak, he will speak, we will speak, you will speak, they will speak"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«grandir» (to grow) with «les enfants»:", "choices": ["Les enfants grandiront vite.", "Les enfants grandissent vite.", "Les enfants grandirons vite."], "answer": "Les enfants grandiront vite.", "right": "Bravo! Infinitive «grandir» + «-ont» for «ils» = «grandiront».", "wrong": "«les enfants» = «ils», so the ending is «-ont»: «grandir» + «-ont» = «grandiront». «-ons» is for «nous»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Verbs ending in «-re» drop the final «e» before the ending: «boire» → «je boirai», «prendre» → «je prendrai». The stem always ends in «r»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Adam and Sébastien will drink beer at the bar.' (boire)", "choices": ["Ils boiveront de la bière.", "Ils boiront de la bière.", "Ils boireront de la bière."], "answer": "Ils boiront de la bière.", "right": "Parfait! «boire» loses its «e»: «boir-» + «-ont» = «boiront».", "wrong": "Drop the final «e» of «boire» to get the stem «boir-», then add «-ont»: «boiront»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some very common verbs have a special stem, but the SAME endings: «aller» → «ir-», «avoir» → «aur-», «être» → «ser-», «faire» → «fer-»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous irons au Portugal l'été prochain.", "en": "We will go to Portugal next summer."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Tom and Alice will have a house soon.'", "choices": ["Tom et Alice auront bientôt une maison.", "Tom et Alice avoiront bientôt une maison.", "Tom et Alice seront bientôt une maison."], "answer": "Tom et Alice auront bientôt une maison.", "right": "Bravo! «avoir» → «aur-» + «-ont» = «auront».", "wrong": "«avoir» has the irregular stem «aur-»: «ils auront». («seront» comes from «être».)"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation: «je parlerai» (future) and «je parlerais» (conditional) sound almost the same. Spelling matters: future «je» ends in «-ai»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: stem = infinitive (drop the «e» of «-re»), endings «-ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont». Special stems: «ir-, aur-, ser-, fer-»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Put into the future: «Vous avez le temps d'aller au cinéma.»", "answer": "Vous aurez le temps d'aller au cinéma.", "accept": ["vous aurez le temps d'aller au cinéma", "vous aurez le temps d aller au cinema", "vous aurez le temps d'aller au cinema", "vous aurez le temps d’aller au cinéma"], "hint": "«avoir» → «aur-» + «vous» ending.", "right": "Excellent! «vous aurez» = you will have.", "wrong": "«avoir» → «aur-», and the «vous» ending is «-ez»: «Vous aurez le temps d'aller au cinéma.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ma tante viendra chez moi et nous marcherons le long de la rivière.»", "fr": "Ma tante viendra chez moi et nous marcherons le long de la rivière."}]}, "21-2": {"stageId": "21-2", "title": "Irregular future stems", "goal": "By the end you can use the irregular future stems of «venir», «vouloir», «pouvoir», «savoir», «voir», «recevoir» and make negative future sentences.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Good news first: the future endings NEVER change, even for irregular verbs: «-ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont». Only the stem is tricky."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which list shows the future endings?", "choices": ["-ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont", "-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent", "-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient"], "answer": "-ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont", "right": "Yes! Think of «avoir»: «j'ai, tu as, il a…».", "wrong": "The future endings look like «avoir» in the present: «-ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont». The others are present and imperfect endings."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Many irregular stems end in «-dr-» or «-rr-». «venir» → «viendr-», «tenir» → «tiendr-», «vouloir» → «voudr-»; «pouvoir» → «pourr-», «voir» → «verr-»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je viendrai te voir tous les soirs.", "en": "I will come and see you every evening."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«devenir» is built on «venir». So 'she will become' is…", "choices": ["elle devenira", "elle deviendra", "elle devendra"], "answer": "elle deviendra", "right": "Bravo! Like «venir» → «viendr-», «devenir» → «deviendr-». Same for «obtenir» → «obtiendr-».", "wrong": "«devenir» copies «venir»: «viendr-» → «deviendr-». So «elle deviendra»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Tom and Charlotte will see their aunt.' (voir)", "choices": ["Ils voiront leur tante.", "Ils verront leur tante.", "Ils vouront leur tante."], "answer": "Ils verront leur tante.", "right": "Parfait! «voir» → «verr-» (double r): «ils verront».", "wrong": "«voir» has the stem «verr-» with a double «r»: «ils verront»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two more to learn by heart: «savoir» → «saur-» and «recevoir» → «recevr-»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu sauras quoi faire.", "en": "You will know what to do."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: «aur-» (avoir) and «saur-» (savoir) rhyme, and «pourr-» (pouvoir) and «verr-» (voir) both have a double «rr»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The children won't go swimming this Wednesday.' Where does «ne… pas» go?", "choices": ["Les enfants feront ne pas de natation.", "Les enfants ne feront pas de natation.", "Les enfants ne pas feront de natation."], "answer": "Les enfants ne feront pas de natation.", "right": "Bravo! The future is one word, so «ne… pas» goes around it: «ne feront pas». And «de» after the negative.", "wrong": "In the futur simple the verb is a single word, so «ne» goes before and «pas» after: «ne feront pas de natation»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: same endings always. Stems to know: «ir-, aur-, ser-, fer-, viendr-, voudr-, pourr-, saur-, verr-, recevr-». Regular «dormir» stays «dormir-»: «Tom dormira»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We will want to speak to you. (use «te»)", "answer": "Nous voudrons te parler.", "accept": ["nous voudrons te parler", "nous voudrons te parler."], "hint": "«vouloir» → «voudr-».", "right": "Excellent! «nous voudrons» = we will want.", "wrong": "«vouloir» → «voudr-» + «-ons»: «Nous voudrons te parler.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il sera heureux, tu sauras quoi faire et nous pourrons venir.»", "fr": "Il sera heureux, tu sauras quoi faire et nous pourrons venir."}]}, "21-3": {"stageId": "21-3", "title": "Nominalisation: verbs & adjectives into nouns", "goal": "By the end you can turn verbs and adjectives into nouns with suffixes like «-tion», «-ment», «-age», «-té», «-esse» and spot the exceptions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In English, 'to inform' + '-ation' gives 'information'. French does exactly the same: «informer» → «l'information». This is «la nominalisation»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess the noun from «présenter» (to present).", "choices": ["la présentement", "la présentation", "le présentage"], "answer": "la présentation", "right": "Bravo! «-ation» works just like in English: «la présentation».", "wrong": "Like English 'presentation': stem «présent-» + «-ation» = «la présentation»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Other common verb suffixes: «-ment» («affoler» → «l'affolement», panic), «-age» («repasser» → «le repassage», ironing), «-ure» («brûler» → «la brûlure», burn), «-erie» («garder» → «la garderie», day-care)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Handy gender clue: nouns in «-ment» and «-age» are usually masculine; nouns in «-tion», «-ure» and «-té» are usually feminine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«développer» → ? (to develop → development)", "choices": ["le développement", "la développation", "le développage"], "answer": "le développement", "right": "Parfait! «-ment» (masculine) = English '-ment'.", "wrong": "English 'development' ↔ French «le développement»: suffix «-ment», masculine."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Sometimes you REMOVE the ending: «ajouter» → «l'ajout» (addition), «chanter» → «le chant» (singing). And sometimes the infinitive itself becomes the noun: «rire» → «le rire», «sourire» → «le sourire»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Careful, an exception! What's the noun for «nager» (to swim) as a sport?", "choices": ["le nagement", "la nageation", "la natation"], "answer": "la natation", "right": "Yes! «la natation» looks totally different, just learn it. Same for «acheter» → «l'achat».", "wrong": "Some nouns change completely: «nager» → «la natation» (swimming), «acheter» → «l'achat» (purchase)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Adjectives become nouns too, with «-té», «-esse», «-eur», «-ence/-ance», «-itude», «-isme», «-ie»: «beau» → «la beauté», «exact» → «l'exactitude», «national» → «le nationalisme»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle s'habille avec beaucoup d'élégance.", "en": "She dresses with great elegance.", "note": "«élégant» → «l'élégance»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Work backwards: «la sagesse» (wisdom) comes from which adjective?", "choices": ["sage", "sagement", "sagesse"], "answer": "sage", "right": "Bravo! «sage» (wise) + «-esse» = «la sagesse». Same with «large» → «la largeur».", "wrong": "Remove the suffix «-esse»: «sagesse» → «sage» (wise). «sagement» is the adverb."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: verbs → «-tion, -ment, -age, -ure, -erie», or a cut ending («l'ajout»), or the infinitive («le rire»). Adjectives → «-té, -esse, -eur, -ence, -ance, -itude, -isme». Watch for exceptions like «la natation»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the noun (with its article) from «créer».", "answer": "la création", "accept": ["la création", "la creation"], "hint": "Like English 'creation'.", "right": "Excellent! «créer» → «la création».", "wrong": "«créer» → «la création», feminine like most «-tion» nouns."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «La présentation sur le développement durable était d'une grande clarté.»", "fr": "La présentation sur le développement durable était d'une grande clarté."}]}, "22-1": {"stageId": "22-1", "title": "Reflexive verbs: I wash myself", "goal": "By the end you can use reflexive pronouns («me, te, se, nous, vous») with verbs in the present, the negative, the imperative and the future.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Compare «Je lave la voiture» (I wash the car) and «Je me lave» (I wash myself). When the action comes back to the subject, French adds a reflexive pronoun: these are «les verbes pronominaux»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «Je me lave», what does «me» refer to?", "choices": ["The car", "Myself, the same person as «je»", "Someone else"], "answer": "Myself, the same person as «je»", "right": "Exactly! «me» = myself: the subject does the action to itself.", "wrong": "«me» points back to «je»: I wash MYSELF. That's what makes the verb reflexive."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Each subject has its own partner: «je me, tu te, il/elle/on se, nous nous, vous vous, ils/elles se». The verb itself is conjugated normally."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous nous levons tous les matins à 7 h.", "en": "We get up every morning at 7am."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'They get dressed.' (s'habiller)", "choices": ["Ils se habillent.", "Ils s'habillent.", "Ils leur habillent."], "answer": "Ils s'habillent.", "right": "Bravo! «se» becomes «s'» before a vowel or silent h: «ils s'habillent».", "wrong": "«se» is the pronoun for «ils», and before a vowel or silent h it shortens to «s'»: «ils s'habillent»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Many daily-routine verbs are reflexive in French but not in English: «se réveiller» (wake up), «se lever» (get up), «se doucher», «se coucher» (go to bed), «se reposer» (rest), «se fâcher» (get angry)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With plural subjects, the pronoun can also mean 'each other': «Ils se parlent.» = They speak to each other. «Nous nous écrivons.» = We write to each other."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Negative: 'I'm not getting dressed.' Where do «ne… pas» go?", "choices": ["Je m'habille ne pas.", "Je ne m'habille pas.", "Je m'ne habille pas."], "answer": "Je ne m'habille pas.", "right": "Parfait! «ne» goes before the pronoun, «pas» after the verb: the pronoun sticks to its verb.", "wrong": "The pronoun and verb stay together, and «ne… pas» wraps around them: «Je ne m'habille pas.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Imperative: the pronoun moves AFTER the verb with a hyphen, and «te» becomes «toi»: «Habille-toi !», «Levons-nous !», «Lavez-vous !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "But in a negative order? 'Don't get dressed!'", "choices": ["Ne habille-toi pas !", "Ne t'habille pas !", "Habille-toi pas !"], "answer": "Ne t'habille pas !", "right": "Bravo! In negative orders, the normal pronoun goes back in front: «Ne t'habille pas !»", "wrong": "Negative imperatives use the normal pronoun before the verb: «Ne t'habille pas !», «Ne nous levons pas !»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Futures: futur proche = «aller» + pronoun + infinitive: «Je vais me laver.» Futur simple = pronoun + future verb: «Je me laverai.» The pronoun always matches the subject!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'We are going to go to bed at 10pm.'", "choices": ["Nous allons se coucher à 22 heures.", "Nous allons nous coucher à 22 heures.", "Nous nous allons coucher à 22 heures."], "answer": "Nous allons nous coucher à 22 heures.", "right": "Excellent! The pronoun matches «nous» and sits just before the infinitive.", "wrong": "Keep the pronoun with the infinitive and match it to the subject: «nous allons NOUS coucher». «se» would be for «ils» or «il»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In the passé composé, reflexive verbs take «être»: «Tom s'est douché.» We'll practise this in detail in a later lesson."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: subject + matching pronoun + verb; «ne» before the pronoun; «Lève-toi !» but «Ne te lève pas !»; «Je vais me laver», «Je me laverai»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (futur simple): Tom will get dressed.", "answer": "Tom s'habillera.", "accept": ["tom s'habillera", "tom s'habillera.", "tom s’habillera", "tom s habillera"], "hint": "«s'» + «habiller» + «-a».", "right": "Bravo! «s'habillera» = will get dressed.", "wrong": "Reflexive pronoun «s'» + future «habillera»: «Tom s'habillera.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Nous nous levons à 7 h et nous nous préparons pour aller au travail.»", "fr": "Nous nous levons à 7 h et nous nous préparons pour aller au travail."}]}, "22-2": {"stageId": "22-2", "title": "Reflexive verbs in the present", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate common reflexive verbs in the present, including «me/te/se» → «m'/t'/s'» and «se lever».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Quick reminder: subject + reflexive pronoun + verb. «je me couche, tu te couches, il se couche, nous nous couchons, vous vous couchez, ils se couchent»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Charlotte ___ regarde dans le miroir.»", "choices": ["me", "se", "la"], "answer": "se", "right": "Yes! «Charlotte» = «elle», so «se»: «Charlotte se regarde dans le miroir.»", "wrong": "«Charlotte» is «elle», and the pronoun for «il/elle/on» is «se»: «Charlotte se regarde»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Before a vowel or silent h: «me» → «m'», «te» → «t'», «se» → «s'». That's why you've always said «Je m'appelle…»: it's the reflexive «s'appeler» (literally 'I call myself')!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'You get annoyed.' (s'énerver, «tu»)", "choices": ["Tu te énerves.", "Tu t'énerves.", "Tu s'énerves."], "answer": "Tu t'énerves.", "right": "Bravo! «te» → «t'» before the vowel «é».", "wrong": "With «tu» the pronoun is «te», which becomes «t'» before a vowel: «Tu t'énerves»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«se lever» takes an accent where the ending is silent: «je me lève, tu te lèves, ils se lèvent», but «nous nous levons, vous vous levez»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'What time do you get up?' (tu)", "choices": ["Tu te lèves à quelle heure ?", "Tu te leves à quelle heure ?", "Tu se lèves à quelle heure ?"], "answer": "Tu te lèves à quelle heure ?", "right": "Parfait! «te» for «tu», and «lèves» with the grave accent.", "wrong": "Two things: «tu» goes with «te», and «se lever» has «è» in the «tu» form: «Tu te lèves à quelle heure ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some reflexive verbs have an object too: «se faire un café» = to make oneself a coffee. The verb is conjugated normally: «ils se font un café»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Charlotte et Tom se font un café.", "en": "Charlotte and Tom are making themselves a coffee."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous ___ car le voyage est long.» (se reposer)", "choices": ["nous reposons", "se reposons", "reposons-nous"], "answer": "nous reposons", "right": "Bravo! «nous nous reposons» : yes, «nous» twice!", "wrong": "With «nous», the reflexive pronoun is also «nous»: «Nous nous reposons». «Reposons-nous» is an order."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«vous vous» and «nous nous» look strange to English speakers, but they're normal: the first is the subject, the second is the reflexive pronoun. «Vous vous déguisez en pirate ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: match the pronoun to the subject, shorten before a vowel («m', t', s'»), watch «lève/levons», and conjugate the verb as usual."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I go to bed every day at 10pm.", "answer": "Je me couche tous les jours à 22 h.", "accept": ["je me couche tous les jours à 22 h", "je me couche tous les jours à 22h", "je me couche tous les jours à 22 heures", "je me couche tous les jours a 22 h", "je me couche tous les jours a 22h", "je me couche tous les jours à 22 h.", "je me couche tous les jours à 22 heures."], "hint": "«se coucher»; French uses the 24-hour clock.", "right": "Excellent! «Je me couche… à 22 h.»", "wrong": "«se coucher» with «je» = «je me couche»; 10pm = «22 h»: «Je me couche tous les jours à 22 h.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Describe your morning: «Je me réveille à 7 h, je me lève et je m'habille.»", "fr": "Je me réveille à 7 h, je me lève et je m'habille."}]}, "22-3": {"stageId": "22-3", "title": "Reflexive verbs in the passé composé", "goal": "By the end you can put reflexive verbs in the passé composé with «être» and make the past participle agree correctly.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "All reflexive verbs use «être» in the passé composé. Order: subject + pronoun + «être» + past participle: «Tom s'est douché.» = Tom had a shower."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which auxiliary do you think «se lever» takes? 'I got up.'", "choices": ["Je me suis levé.", "Je m'ai levé.", "J'ai me levé."], "answer": "Je me suis levé.", "right": "Exactly! Reflexive → «être»: «je me suis levé(e)».", "wrong": "Reflexive verbs always use «être», never «avoir»: «Je me suis levé(e).»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "The pattern: «je me suis, tu t'es, il/elle/on s'est, nous nous sommes, vous vous êtes, ils/elles se sont» + participle."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ils se sont pris en photo.", "en": "They took a picture of themselves."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Like other «être» verbs, the participle usually agrees with the subject: add «-e» for feminine, «-s» for plural. «Elle s'est levée.» «Nous nous sommes regardés dans le miroir.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The girls had a rest.' (se reposer)", "choices": ["Les filles se sont reposé.", "Les filles se sont reposées.", "Les filles ont se reposées."], "answer": "Les filles se sont reposées.", "right": "Bravo! Feminine plural subject → «reposées» (-e + -s).", "wrong": "«Les filles» is feminine plural, so the participle takes «-es»: «Les filles se sont reposées.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "The tricky exception: if a direct object comes AFTER the verb (like «les mains», «les dents»), no agreement. «Elles se sont lavées.» but «Elles se sont lavé les mains.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The children brushed their teeth.'", "choices": ["Les enfants se sont brossés les dents.", "Les enfants se sont brossé les dents.", "Les enfants ont brossé ses dents."], "answer": "Les enfants se sont brossé les dents.", "right": "Parfait! «les dents» is the direct object after the verb, so no agreement: «brossé».", "wrong": "«les dents» follows the verb as its direct object, so the participle doesn't agree: «se sont brossé les dents». (Without it: «ils se sont brossés».)"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Notice: French says «se laver LES mains», not «ses mains»: the reflexive pronoun already shows whose hands they are."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Without «se», «laver» is a normal verb. 'I washed my car.'", "choices": ["Je me suis lavé ma voiture.", "J'ai lavé ma voiture.", "Je suis lavé ma voiture."], "answer": "J'ai lavé ma voiture.", "right": "Yes! Not reflexive → «avoir»: «J'ai lavé ma voiture.» Compare «Je me suis lavé(e).»", "wrong": "Washing the car isn't reflexive, so it takes «avoir»: «J'ai lavé ma voiture.» Only «se laver» uses «être»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: pronoun + «être» + participle; agree with the subject («elle s'est levée»), except when a direct object follows («elle s'est lavé les mains»)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "A woman says: 'I went to bed late.' (se coucher, tard)", "answer": "Je me suis couchée tard.", "accept": ["je me suis couchée tard", "je me suis couchée tard.", "je me suis couchee tard"], "hint": "«être» + participle with feminine «-e».", "right": "Excellent! Feminine speaker → «couchée».", "wrong": "«Je me suis couché» + «-e» because the speaker is a woman: «Je me suis couchée tard.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Hier, je me suis levé tôt, je me suis douché et je me suis habillé.» (Women: «levée, douchée, habillée» sound the same!)", "fr": "Hier, je me suis levé tôt, je me suis douché et je me suis habillé."}]}, "22-4": {"stageId": "22-4", "title": "Repeat after me: reflexive verbs out loud", "goal": "By the end you can pronounce reflexive verbs smoothly, with elisions and liaisons, in everyday sentences.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today is a speaking lesson! Reflexive verbs come in little sound blocks: «je me», «tu te», «il se». Say each block smoothly, as if it were one word, without pausing before the verb."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tom se réveille tous les jours à 7 h.", "en": "Tom wakes up every day at 7."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Repeat after me: «Tom se réveille tous les jours à 7 h.»", "fr": "Tom se réveille tous les jours à 7 h."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Before a vowel, the pronoun glides into the verb: «je m'habille» sounds like «jeu-ma-bïy». No pause between «m'» and «habille»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How many syllables do you hear in «je m'habille»?", "choices": ["3: je-ma-bille", "4: je-me-ha-bille", "5: je-me-ha-bi-lle"], "answer": "3: je-ma-bille", "right": "Yes! The «e» of «me» disappears and the «h» is silent: «je-ma-bille».", "wrong": "«me» loses its «e» and the «h» is silent, so you get just three: «je-ma-bille»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Repeat: «Je m'habille avant de prendre mon petit déjeuner.»", "fr": "Je m'habille avant de prendre mon petit déjeuner."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Liaison alert! «nous nous» and «vous vous» before a vowel: the final «s» sounds like «z». «nous nous_habillons» = «nou-nou-za-bi-yon», «vous vous_amusez»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «ils s'habillent», which sound do you hear after «il»?", "choices": ["a 'z' sound", "an 's' sound", "no sound at all"], "answer": "an 's' sound", "right": "Bravo! «s'» is a clear 's': «il-sa-bïy». Compare «ils_aiment» with a 'z' liaison.", "wrong": "«s'» is pronounced 's': «il-sa-bïy». That's how you tell it apart from «ils habillent les enfants» (dressing someone else), which has a 'z' liaison."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Repeat: «Les filles se reposent dans le jardin.»", "fr": "Les filles se reposent dans le jardin."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The «-ent» of «ils se lavent» is silent: «il-se-lav». So «il se lave» and «ils se lavent» sound the same; context tells you who!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You hear «il-se-lav-a-pré-leur-match». Who is it?", "choices": ["Only one person, because of «se»", "Several people, because of «leur»", "You can't tell from «se lave/lavent»"], "answer": "Several people, because of «leur»", "right": "Excellent listening! «se lave(nt)» sounds the same, but «leur match» shows it's «ils»: «Ils se lavent après leur match.»", "wrong": "«il se lave» and «ils se lavent» sound identical, so look for other clues: «leur match» (their match) means several people."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Repeat: «Ils se lavent après leur match de football.»", "fr": "Ils se lavent après leur match de football."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: say «je me / tu te / il se» as one block, glide «m', t', s'» into vowels, «z» liaison after «nous nous / vous vous», silent «-ent»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: You take a picture of yourself in front of the Eiffel Tower. (tu)", "answer": "Tu te prends en photo devant la tour Eiffel.", "accept": ["tu te prends en photo devant la tour eiffel", "tu te prends en photo devant la tour eiffel."], "hint": "«se prendre en photo», «tu» form.", "right": "Bravo! «Tu te prends en photo» = you take a selfie.", "wrong": "«se prendre en photo» with «tu»: «Tu te prends en photo devant la tour Eiffel.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Final challenge: «Tu te prends en photo devant la tour Eiffel.»", "fr": "Tu te prends en photo devant la tour Eiffel."}]}, "23-1": {"stageId": "23-1", "title": "The imparfait: 'used to' and 'was doing'", "goal": "By the end you know when to use the imparfait, how to form it, and how it differs from the passé composé.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! You already know the passé composé for finished events. Today we meet the second big past tense: the «imparfait». Listen: «Quand j'étais jeune, je jouais au tennis.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What do you think «Je jouais au tennis tous les mercredis» means? Look at «tous les mercredis».", "choices": ["I played tennis one Wednesday", "I used to play tennis every Wednesday", "I will play tennis on Wednesdays"], "answer": "I used to play tennis every Wednesday", "right": "Exactly! «tous les mercredis» = every Wednesday, a habit. The imparfait «jouais» = used to play.", "wrong": "«tous les mercredis» means every Wednesday: a repeated habit in the past. That is the job of the imparfait: «je jouais» = I used to play."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The imparfait has four main jobs: habits, background actions (was doing), descriptions (weather, places, people) and feelings or memories."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il faisait beau et les oiseaux chantaient.", "en": "It was sunny and the birds were singing.", "note": "Description = imparfait."}, {"t": "say", "text": "How do you form it? Take the «nous» form of the present, remove «-ons», and you have the stem: «nous parlons» → «parl-», «nous finissons» → «finiss-», «nous voyons» → «voy-»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je parlais, tu parlais, il parlait, nous parlions, vous parliez, ils parlaient", "en": "I / you / he / we / you / they used to speak", "note": "Endings: -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«boire» → «nous buvons». So 'he used to drink' is…", "choices": ["il buvait", "il boivait", "il buvais"], "answer": "il buvait", "right": "Bravo ! Stem «buv-» (from «nous buvons») + «-ait» for «il».", "wrong": "Always start from «nous buvons» → stem «buv-». Then «il» takes «-ait»: «il buvait». («-ais» is for «je» and «tu».)"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Only ONE exception to the stem rule: «être» → «ét-»: «j'étais», «nous étions». Also good news: «-ais», «-ait» and «-aient» all sound the same!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je regardais la télé quand quelqu'un ___ à la porte.» (the bell rang suddenly)", "choices": ["sonnait", "a sonné", "sonnais"], "answer": "a sonné", "right": "Yes! Watching TV = the background (imparfait). The bell = a sudden, finished event (passé composé).", "wrong": "The TV watching was going on (imparfait «regardais»). The bell interrupted it at one moment, so it takes the passé composé: «a sonné»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some words are clues. «tous les», «chaque», «souvent», «parfois» → usually imparfait. «un jour», «tout à coup», «il y a», «d'abord», «enfin» → usually passé composé."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tout à coup, il ___ devant moi.»", "choices": ["sautait", "a sauté", "sautais"], "answer": "a sauté", "right": "Perfect! «tout à coup» (suddenly) signals one quick event → passé composé.", "wrong": "«tout à coup» = suddenly. Something sudden and finished takes the passé composé: «il a sauté»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'étais en train de regarder la télé quand tu es arrivé.", "en": "I was (busy) watching TV when you arrived.", "note": "«être en train de» in the imparfait stresses 'in the middle of'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«venir de» + infinitive = 'just did'. In the imparfait it means 'had just': «Il vient d'arriver» = He has just arrived; «Il venait d'arriver» = He had just arrived."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Je venais de rendre visite à mes grands-parents» mean?", "choices": ["I had just visited my grandparents", "I was coming to visit my grandparents", "I have just visited my grandparents"], "answer": "I had just visited my grandparents", "right": "Great! «venais de» (imparfait) = had just.", "wrong": "«venir de» = just did. In the present «je viens de» = I have just; in the imparfait «je venais de» = I had just."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: imparfait = habits, background, descriptions, feelings. Stem = «nous» form minus «-ons» (except «ét-»). Endings «-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We were in the process of eating when you called.", "answer": "Nous étions en train de manger quand tu as appelé.", "accept": ["nous étions en train de manger quand tu as appelé", "nous etions en train de manger quand tu as appele", "nous étions en train de manger quand vous avez appelé"], "hint": "«Nous étions en train de…» + «quand tu as…»", "right": "Excellent ! Background in the imparfait, interruption in the passé composé.", "wrong": "Background: «Nous étions en train de manger» (imparfait). Interruption: «quand tu as appelé» (passé composé)."}]}, "23-2": {"stageId": "23-2", "title": "Imparfait of -ER verbs", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate any regular -ER verb in the imparfait.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "-ER verbs are the biggest family, and in the imparfait they are very regular. Remove «-er» (or «-ons» from the «nous» form, same result) and add the endings."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«regarder» → «nous regardons». What is the imparfait stem?", "choices": ["regard-", "regarde-", "regardons-"], "answer": "regard-", "right": "Yes! «nous regardons» minus «-ons» = «regard-».", "wrong": "Take «nous regardons» and remove «-ons»: «regard-». Then add the endings."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je regardais, tu regardais, il regardait, nous regardions, vous regardiez, ils regardaient", "en": "I used to watch / was watching…", "note": "«-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English speakers forget the «i» in «nous» and «vous»: «nous regardions», «vous regardiez». Without the «i», it's the present tense!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom ___ au tennis tous les mercredis.» (jouer)", "choices": ["jouait", "jouais", "jouaient"], "answer": "jouait", "right": "Bravo ! «Tom» = «il», so «-ait»: «jouait».", "wrong": "«Tom» is like «il», and «il» takes «-ait»: «Tom jouait». «-ais» is for «je/tu», «-aient» for «ils»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous ___ généralement près de la rivière.» (marcher)", "choices": ["marchez", "marchiez", "marchaient"], "answer": "marchiez", "right": "Super ! «vous» + «-iez» = «marchiez». «marchez» would be the present.", "wrong": "In the imparfait «vous» takes «-iez»: «vous marchiez». «vous marchez» is the present tense."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je cuisinais lorsque tu es arrivé.", "en": "I was cooking when you arrived.", "note": "«lorsque» = «quand» (a bit more formal)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Negatives work like in the present: «ne» before the verb, «pas» after. Reflexive verbs keep their pronoun: «nous nous promenions»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'Electricity didn't exist back then'?", "choices": ["L'électricité n'existait pas à l'époque.", "L'électricité ne existait pas à l'époque.", "L'électricité n'existais pas à l'époque."], "answer": "L'électricité n'existait pas à l'époque.", "right": "Parfait ! «ne» becomes «n'» before a vowel, and «l'électricité» = «elle» → «-ait».", "wrong": "Before a vowel «ne» → «n'». And «l'électricité» is like «elle», so «-ait»: «n'existait pas»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom et Charlotte ___ une lettre à leur tante toutes les semaines.» (poster)", "choices": ["postait", "postaient", "postiez"], "answer": "postaient", "right": "Yes! Two people = «ils» → «-aient».", "wrong": "«Tom et Charlotte» = «ils», and «ils» takes «-aient»: «postaient». It sounds the same as «postait», but the spelling differs."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: -ER stem = infinitive minus «-er». Add «-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient». Watch the «i» in «nous» and «vous»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I was preparing a cake when she arrived.", "answer": "Je préparais un gâteau quand elle est arrivée.", "accept": ["je préparais un gâteau quand elle est arrivée", "je preparais un gateau quand elle est arrivee", "je préparais un gâteau lorsqu'elle est arrivée"], "hint": "«Je prépar…» + «quand elle est…»", "right": "Très bien ! Ongoing action (imparfait) + sudden arrival (passé composé, with «être» and agreement «-e»).", "wrong": "«Je préparais un gâteau» (imparfait, background) + «quand elle est arrivée» (passé composé, «arriver» uses «être», feminine «-e»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quand j'étais petit, je jouais au foot tous les samedis.»", "fr": "Quand j'étais petit, je jouais au foot tous les samedis."}]}, "23-3": {"stageId": "23-3", "title": "Spelling changes: -CER, -GER and -IER verbs", "goal": "By the end you can spell «commencer», «manger» and «crier» correctly in the imparfait.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Some -ER verbs change their spelling to keep their sound. Say «manger»: the «g» is soft, like the s in 'pleasure'. But «g» + «a» sounds hard, like in «gare»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "So to write 'I used to eat' and keep the soft sound, what do you think French does?", "choices": ["je mangais", "je mangeais", "je manjais"], "answer": "je mangeais", "right": "Well spotted! French adds an «e» after «g» before «a» or «o»: «je mangeais».", "wrong": "«ga» sounds hard (like 'gah'). To keep the soft g, French adds an «e»: «je mangeais»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je mangeais, tu mangeais, il mangeait, ils mangeaient — but nous mangions, vous mangiez", "en": "I / you / he / they used to eat — we / you used to eat", "note": "No extra «e» before «i»: «gi» is already soft."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Same idea for -CER verbs like «commencer». «ca» sounds like k, so before «a» or «o» the «c» becomes «ç» to keep the s sound."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which spelling is correct for 'they were beginning'?", "choices": ["ils commencaient", "ils commençaient", "ils commenceaient"], "answer": "ils commençaient", "right": "Bravo ! «c» + «a» needs a cedilla: «ç» keeps the s sound.", "wrong": "Without a cedilla «ca» sounds like 'ka'. Before «a» write «ç»: «ils commençaient»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "And 'we were beginning'?", "choices": ["nous commençions", "nous commencions", "nous commenceions"], "answer": "nous commencions", "right": "Exactly! The ending «-ions» starts with «i», and «ci» is already soft, so no cedilla.", "wrong": "The cedilla is only needed before «a», «o» or «u». «-ions» starts with «i», so «c» stays: «nous commencions»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Last trap: -IER verbs like «crier» (to shout) or «étudier». The stem ends in «i»: «cri-». Add «-ions» or «-iez» and you get TWO i's."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous criions, vous criiez.", "en": "We were shouting, you were shouting.", "note": "Present tense: «nous crions, vous criez» — only one «i»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is the IMPARFAIT?", "choices": ["vous étudiez", "vous étudiiez", "vous étudiaient"], "answer": "vous étudiiez", "right": "Super ! Stem «étudi-» + «-iez» = «étudiiez», double «i».", "wrong": "Stem «étudi-» + imparfait ending «-iez» gives «vous étudiiez» (two i's). «vous étudiez» is the present."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: the imparfait always has an «i» in «nous» and «vous». If the stem already ends in «i», you simply get «ii»!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «-ger» → «geais/geait/geaient»; «-cer» → «çais/çait/çaient»; «-ier» → «iions/iiez». The «nous» and «vous» forms of -ger/-cer need no change."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We used to eat at 7 o'clock.", "answer": "Nous mangions à sept heures.", "accept": ["nous mangions à sept heures", "nous mangions a sept heures", "nous mangions à 7 heures", "nous mangions à 7 h", "on mangeait à sept heures", "on mangeait à 7 heures"], "hint": "Careful: «nous» form, ending «-ions».", "right": "Très bien ! «gi» is already soft, so «nous mangions» needs no extra «e».", "wrong": "«nous» + «-ions»: «mangions». «g» + «i» is already soft, so no extra «e»: «Nous mangions à sept heures»."}]}, "23-4": {"stageId": "23-4", "title": "Imparfait of -IR verbs (finir family)", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate regular -IR verbs like «finir» and «choisir» in the imparfait.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Regular -IR verbs (the «finir» family) have a little surprise in the «nous» form of the present: «nous finissons». Remember the rule: the imparfait stem comes from «nous»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "So what do you think the imparfait stem of «finir» is?", "choices": ["fin-", "finiss-", "fini-"], "answer": "finiss-", "right": "Yes! «nous finissons» minus «-ons» = «finiss-».", "wrong": "Don't start from the infinitive. Use «nous finissons» → remove «-ons» → «finiss-»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je finissais, tu finissais, il finissait, nous finissions, vous finissiez, ils finissaient", "en": "I used to finish / was finishing…", "note": "The «-iss-» appears in every form."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu finissais toujours ton assiette.", "en": "You always used to finish your plate."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«grandir» (to grow up). «Tom ___ vite.»", "choices": ["grandait", "grandissait", "grandissais"], "answer": "grandissait", "right": "Bravo ! «nous grandissons» → «grandiss-» + «-ait».", "wrong": "«nous grandissons» → stem «grandiss-». «Tom» = «il» → «-ait»: «grandissait»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Other verbs in this family: «choisir» (to choose), «remplir» (to fill), «obéir» (to obey), «punir», «guérir» (to heal), «grossir» (to put on weight), «maigrir» (to lose weight)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je ___ toujours à mes parents.» (obéir)", "choices": ["J'obéissais", "Je obéissais", "J'obéissait"], "answer": "J'obéissais", "right": "Parfait ! «je» → «j'» before a vowel, and «je» takes «-ais».", "wrong": "«je» becomes «j'» before a vowel: «j'obéissais». And «je» takes «-ais», not «-ait»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Make it negative: «Elle ___ assez les lapins.» (nourrir)", "choices": ["ne nourrissait pas", "ne nourrissais pas", "nourrissait ne pas"], "answer": "ne nourrissait pas", "right": "Super ! «ne» + verb + «pas», with «elle» → «-ait».", "wrong": "The negation wraps the verb: «ne nourrissait pas». «elle» takes «-ait»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«haïr» (to hate) has a tréma: «nous haïssons» → «nous haïssions». Each vowel is pronounced: «ha-ï»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: for -IR verbs of the «finir» group, use the «nous» stem with «-iss-», then the usual endings."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: He used to choose the same meal every Tuesday evening.", "answer": "Il choisissait le même repas tous les mardis soir.", "accept": ["il choisissait le même repas tous les mardis soir", "il choisissait le meme repas tous les mardis soir", "il choisissait le même repas tous les mardis soirs", "il choisissait le même repas chaque mardi soir"], "hint": "«Il choisiss…» + «tous les mardis soir»", "right": "Excellent ! A habit + «tous les» → imparfait, with «choisiss-».", "wrong": "«choisir» → «nous choisissons» → «Il choisissait». Then «le même repas tous les mardis soir»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je remplissais la baignoire d'eau chaude.»", "fr": "Je remplissais la baignoire d'eau chaude."}]}, "23-5": {"stageId": "23-5", "title": "Imparfait of irregular verbs", "goal": "By the end you can form the imparfait of common irregular verbs like «faire», «prendre», «lire» and «être».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Good news: irregular verbs are not irregular in the imparfait! The same rule works: «nous» form of the present, minus «-ons»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«prendre» → «nous prenons». What is 'I used to take'?", "choices": ["je prendais", "je prenais", "je prennais"], "answer": "je prenais", "right": "Yes! «nous prenons» → «pren-» → «je prenais».", "wrong": "Never use the infinitive. «nous prenons» → «pren-» + «-ais» = «je prenais»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "faire → nous faisons → je faisais; lire → nous lisons → je lisais", "en": "I used to do / I used to read", "note": "«faisais» is pronounced «fe-zè»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«croire» → «nous croyons». 'We used to believe' is…", "choices": ["nous croyions", "nous croions", "nous croyons"], "answer": "nous croyions", "right": "Bravo ! Stem «croy-» + «-ions» = «croyions». «nous croyons» is the present.", "wrong": "Stem «croy-» (from «nous croyons») + «-ions» gives «nous croyions» — with «y» AND «i»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More: «connaître» → «je connaissais», «mettre» → «je mettais», «peindre» → «je peignais», «vivre» → «je vivais», «savoir» → «je savais»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«connaître» → «nous connaissons». 'She used to know' =", "choices": ["elle connaitait", "elle connaissait", "elle connaît"], "answer": "elle connaissait", "right": "Super ! «connaiss-» + «-ait».", "wrong": "From «nous connaissons» the stem is «connaiss-». With «elle»: «elle connaissait»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Only «être» breaks the rule: stem «ét-» → «j'étais, nous étions». «avoir» is regular: «nous avons» → «j'avais»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous étions sur le point de partir lorsque Paul est arrivé.", "en": "We were about to leave when Paul arrived.", "note": "«être sur le point de» = to be about to."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Charlotte et Tom ___ nous voir régulièrement lorsque nous vivions à Londres.» (venir)", "choices": ["venaient", "viennaient", "venait"], "answer": "venaient", "right": "Parfait ! «nous venons» → «ven-» + «-aient».", "wrong": "«nous venons» → «ven-». «Charlotte et Tom» = «ils» → «venaient». No double «n»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «nous» stem for everyone (even irregular verbs), except «être» → «ét-»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Alice used to read at least one book a week.", "answer": "Alice lisait au moins un livre par semaine.", "accept": ["alice lisait au moins un livre par semaine", "alice lisait au moins un livre chaque semaine"], "hint": "«lire» → «nous lisons» → «lis-»", "right": "Très bien ! «lis-» + «-ait», and «par semaine» = a week.", "wrong": "«lire» → «nous lisons» → «Alice lisait». 'a week' = «par semaine»."}]}, "23-6": {"stageId": "23-6", "title": "Verbs in -AYER, -OYER, -UYER", "goal": "By the end you know when «y» changes to «i» in verbs like «envoyer», «payer» and «essuyer».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Verbs like «envoyer» (to send), «nettoyer» (to clean), «essuyer» (to wipe) and «payer» (to pay) play a little game with «y» and «i»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "j'envoie, tu envoies, il envoie, ils envoient — nous envoyons, vous envoyez", "en": "I send… — we send, you send", "note": "«y» becomes «i» before a silent «e»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Look at the pattern. How do you write 'I clean' («nettoyer»)?", "choices": ["je nettoye", "je nettoie", "je nettois"], "answer": "je nettoie", "right": "Well noticed! Before a silent «-e», «y» → «i»: «je nettoie».", "wrong": "With a silent ending («-e, -es, -ent»), «-oyer» and «-uyer» verbs change «y» to «i»: «je nettoie»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In the imparfait there is no silent «e», so the «y» stays everywhere: «j'envoyais», «nous envoyions», «vous envoyiez»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elles ___ leurs chambres quand tu es arrivé.» (nettoyer, imparfait)", "choices": ["nettoiaient", "nettoyaient", "nettoyaient pas"], "answer": "nettoyaient", "right": "Bravo ! Imparfait keeps the «y»: «nettoyaient».", "wrong": "In the imparfait the «y» never changes: stem «nettoy-» + «-aient» = «nettoyaient»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«-ayer» verbs are flexible: «je paie» or «je paye» are both correct. «-oyer» and «-uyer» verbs MUST change: «j'essuie», never «j'essuye»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which form is NOT correct?", "choices": ["je paye", "je paie", "j'essuye"], "answer": "j'essuye", "right": "Exactly! «-uyer» verbs must change: «j'essuie». Only «-ayer» verbs allow both.", "wrong": "«payer» accepts both «paie» and «paye». But «essuyer» must change: «j'essuie», so «j'essuye» is wrong."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Watch the future of «envoyer»: it is irregular, like «voir»: «j'enverrai, tu enverras» (compare «je verrai»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Demain, tu ___ la lettre à ta mère.» (envoyer, futur)", "choices": ["envoieras", "enverras", "envoyeras"], "answer": "enverras", "right": "Super ! «envoyer» has the irregular future stem «enverr-».", "wrong": "The future of «envoyer» is irregular: «enverr-» (like «verr-» for «voir»). So «tu enverras»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «y» → «i» before silent «e» (present: «j'envoie»); the imparfait keeps «y» («j'envoyais»); «-ayer» verbs allow both spellings."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Tom is wiping the table with a cloth.", "answer": "Tom essuie la table avec un torchon.", "accept": ["tom essuie la table avec un torchon"], "hint": "«essuyer» → «il essuie»", "right": "Très bien ! «y» → «i» before the silent «e»: «essuie».", "wrong": "«essuyer» in the present with «il»: «essuie» (y → i before silent e). «Tom essuie la table avec un torchon.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quand j'étais petit, je nettoyais ma chambre le samedi.»", "fr": "Quand j'étais petit, je nettoyais ma chambre le samedi."}]}, "23-7": {"stageId": "23-7", "title": "Verbs in -ELER, -ETER and -EVER", "goal": "By the end you know when «appeler», «jeter», «acheter» and «lever» double a consonant or take an accent.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In «appeler», «jeter», «acheter» and «lever», the «e» before the last consonant is weak. When the next syllable is silent, French makes it strong — in two different ways."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "j'appelle, tu appelles, ils appellent — nous appelons, vous appelez", "en": "I call… — we call, you call", "note": "Double «l» before a silent ending."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«jeter» (to throw) follows «appeler». What is 'I throw'?", "choices": ["je jete", "je jette", "je jète"], "answer": "je jette", "right": "Yes! Like «appelle», «jeter» doubles the consonant: «je jette».", "wrong": "«jeter» works like «appeler»: double the consonant before a silent ending. «je jette»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«acheter» and «lever» choose a different trick: an accent grave instead of a double letter: «j'achète», «je lève»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'They buy' =", "choices": ["ils achettent", "ils achètent", "ils achetent"], "answer": "ils achètent", "right": "Bravo ! «acheter» takes «è», not a double «t».", "wrong": "«acheter» is one of the verbs that use «è»: «ils achètent». Only «jeter»/«appeler»-type verbs double."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the important part: in the imparfait, the endings are never silent, so there is NO change at all: «j'appelais», «je jetais», «j'achetais», «je levais»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I used to call my grandmother every Sunday.' Choose the verb.", "choices": ["j'appellais", "j'appelais", "j'appèlais"], "answer": "j'appelais", "right": "Perfect! The imparfait endings are pronounced, so no double «l»: «j'appelais».", "wrong": "In the imparfait the ending «-ais» is pronounced, so the stem stays simple: «j'appelais». Doubling only happens before a silent ending."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The future keeps the change in every form, because the «e» after the stem is silent: «j'appellerai», «je jetterai», «j'achèterai», «je lèverai»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom et moi ___ un cadeau pour notre mère.» (acheter, futur)", "choices": ["achèterons", "achetterons", "acheterons"], "answer": "achèterons", "right": "Super ! «Tom et moi» = «nous», and the future keeps the «è».", "wrong": "«Tom et moi» = «nous» → «-ons». In the future «acheter» always has «è»: «achèterons»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: present and future change the stem (double letter for «appeler/jeter», «è» for «acheter/lever»); the imparfait never changes."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: They (f.) are lifting the table.", "answer": "Elles soulèvent la table.", "accept": ["elles soulèvent la table", "elles soulevent la table"], "hint": "«soulever» works like «lever» → «è»", "right": "Très bien ! Silent «-ent» → «è»: «soulèvent».", "wrong": "«soulever» is like «lever»: before a silent ending it takes «è». «Elles soulèvent la table.»"}]}, "23-8": {"stageId": "23-8", "title": "Same verb, two past tenses", "goal": "By the end you can put the same verb in the passé composé or the imparfait and feel the difference in meaning.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we take one sentence and put it in both past tenses. Same words, different picture: a finished event or a habit/background."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle a regardé la télévision. / Elle regardait la télévision.", "en": "She watched TV (once, finished). / She was watching / used to watch TV."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous ___ à la mer.» (aller, passé composé, we = men)", "choices": ["sommes allés", "avons allé", "allions"], "answer": "sommes allés", "right": "Bravo ! «aller» takes «être», and the participle agrees: «allés».", "wrong": "«aller» is a movement verb → auxiliary «être»: «nous sommes allés» (plural agreement «-s»). «allions» is the imparfait."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Now the imparfait: «Nous ___ à la mer.» (aller)", "choices": ["allons", "allions", "allaient"], "answer": "allions", "right": "Yes! «nous allons» → «all-» + «-ions»: we used to go.", "wrong": "Stem from «nous allons» = «all-», plus «-ions» for «nous»: «nous allions»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Adam et Sébastien ___ de la bière au bar.» (boire, passé composé)", "choices": ["ont bu", "ont buvé", "buvaient"], "answer": "ont bu", "right": "Super ! The past participle of «boire» is «bu».", "wrong": "«boire» has an irregular participle: «bu». So «ils ont bu». «buvaient» is the imparfait."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Adam et Sébastien buvaient de la bière au bar.", "en": "Adam and Sébastien were drinking / used to drink beer at the bar."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«être» in the passé composé («tu as été») exists, but for descriptions French much prefers the imparfait: «Tu étais pompier» = You were a firefighter (for a period)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Alex ___ sa voiture cet été.» — he changed his car once. Which tense?", "choices": ["a changé", "changeait", "changait"], "answer": "a changé", "right": "Exactly! One finished event → passé composé.", "wrong": "One single, finished action → passé composé: «Alex a changé». (And remember the imparfait is «changeait» with an «e»!)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous ___ un cadeau à votre mère.» (offrir, passé composé)", "choices": ["avez offri", "avez offert", "offriez"], "answer": "avez offert", "right": "Parfait ! «offrir» → participle «offert».", "wrong": "«offrir» has an irregular participle: «offert» (like «ouvrir» → «ouvert»). «vous avez offert»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: passé composé = auxiliary + participle, for finished events. Imparfait = one word with «-ais/-ait…», for habits and background."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French using the imparfait: They used to prepare couscous for lunch.", "answer": "Ils préparaient un couscous pour le déjeuner.", "accept": ["ils préparaient un couscous pour le déjeuner", "ils preparaient un couscous pour le dejeuner", "ils préparaient du couscous pour le déjeuner", "elles préparaient un couscous pour le déjeuner"], "hint": "«prépar-» + «-aient»", "right": "Très bien ! «-aient» for «ils».", "wrong": "«Ils préparaient» (stem «prépar-» + «-aient») «un couscous pour le déjeuner»."}]}, "23-9": {"stageId": "23-9", "title": "Telling a holiday story", "goal": "By the end you can mix passé composé, imparfait, present and future in a short holiday story.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Real stories mix tenses. Today we tell a holiday story: «Ma famille et moi sommes allés en vacances en France le mois dernier.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Why «sommes allés» and not «allions»?", "choices": ["It was one trip, last month", "It was a habit", "It describes the weather"], "answer": "It was one trip, last month", "right": "Yes! «le mois dernier» points to one finished event → passé composé.", "wrong": "«le mois dernier» (last month) marks one specific, finished trip, so we use the passé composé."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous avons décidé de voyager en voiture car nous avons un chien qui n'aime pas prendre l'avion.", "en": "We decided to travel by car because we have a dog who doesn't like flying.", "note": "The dog still has this problem today → present."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le voyage ___ long et désagréable.»", "choices": ["était", "a été", "est"], "answer": "était", "right": "Bravo ! Describing how the trip was → imparfait.", "wrong": "Here we describe the journey (long, unpleasant), so the imparfait: «Le voyage était long»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«…car il ___ trop chaud.»", "choices": ["faisait", "a fait", "fait"], "answer": "faisait", "right": "Super ! Weather in a story = imparfait: «il faisait chaud».", "wrong": "Weather is background description → imparfait: «il faisait trop chaud»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Then the story moves forward with a new event: «Nous sommes arrivés enfin à destination après 10 heures de route.» «enfin» signals the passé composé."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«L'année prochaine nous ___ en Grèce.»", "choices": ["irons", "allions", "sommes allés"], "answer": "irons", "right": "Parfait ! «l'année prochaine» = next year → future: «nous irons».", "wrong": "«l'année prochaine» is next year, so we need the future: «aller» → «nous irons»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Negative future: «ne» + verb + «pas»: «Notre chien ne pourra pas voyager avec nous.» «pouvoir» → future stem «pourr-»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: events = passé composé; descriptions and weather = imparfait; facts true now = present; plans = future."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The journey was long because it was too hot.", "answer": "Le voyage était long car il faisait trop chaud.", "accept": ["le voyage était long car il faisait trop chaud", "le voyage etait long car il faisait trop chaud", "le voyage était long parce qu'il faisait trop chaud"], "hint": "Two descriptions → two imparfaits", "right": "Excellent ! Both parts describe the situation → imparfait.", "wrong": "Both are descriptions: «Le voyage était long car il faisait trop chaud.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «L'année dernière, je suis allé en Italie. Il faisait très beau.»", "fr": "L'année dernière, je suis allé en Italie. Il faisait très beau."}]}, "23-10": {"stageId": "23-10", "title": "Imparfait or passé composé?", "goal": "By the end you can choose between the imparfait and the passé composé in a real text.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Think of a film. The imparfait is the scenery and the background music; the passé composé is the action — what happens, step by step."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je regardais la télévision lorsque quelqu'un a frappé à la porte.» Which part is the action that 'breaks' the scene?", "choices": ["je regardais la télévision", "quelqu'un a frappé à la porte"], "answer": "quelqu'un a frappé à la porte", "right": "Exactly! The knock is a sudden event → passé composé. The TV watching is the background.", "wrong": "The TV watching lasts (imparfait). The knock happens at one moment and interrupts it → passé composé «a frappé»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis allé au cinéma hier après-midi.", "en": "I went to the cinema yesterday afternoon.", "note": "A specific, finished moment → passé composé."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Read this story: «Je suis allé chez ma mère ce week-end car elle m'a téléphoné… pour me dire que mon meilleur ami d'enfance était là.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Why «était là» and not «a été là»?", "choices": ["It describes a situation", "It is a sudden event", "It is in the future"], "answer": "It describes a situation", "right": "Yes! His friend being there is a state, not an action → imparfait.", "wrong": "«être là» describes a situation that was true at the time, so we use the imparfait «était»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous ___ très proches lorsque nous étions petits.»", "choices": ["étions", "avons été", "sommes"], "answer": "étions", "right": "Bravo ! A description of childhood → imparfait.", "wrong": "Describing childhood (how close they were) is background → imparfait «nous étions»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous ___ toujours dans la forêt.» (habit when they were little)", "choices": ["allions", "sommes allés", "irons"], "answer": "allions", "right": "Super ! A childhood habit → imparfait.", "wrong": "«toujours» + childhood = a habit, so the imparfait «nous allions»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«toujours» can go with both tenses: «J'ai toujours aimé Paris» (all my life, whole period seen as finished) vs «J'allais toujours…» (habit)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous ___ une glace et nous avons marché dans la forêt, comme nous le faisions avant.» (this weekend)", "choices": ["mangions", "avons mangé"], "answer": "avons mangé", "right": "Parfait ! This weekend's actions, one after the other → passé composé. «comme nous le faisions avant» = the old habit → imparfait.", "wrong": "These are this weekend's events, in sequence → passé composé «avons mangé». Only «faisions» (the old habit) is imparfait."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: background, habits, descriptions, feelings → imparfait. Events, sequences, interruptions → passé composé."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It was raining when I left.", "answer": "Il pleuvait quand je suis parti.", "accept": ["il pleuvait quand je suis parti", "il pleuvait quand je suis partie", "il pleuvait lorsque je suis parti", "il pleuvait lorsque je suis partie"], "hint": "Weather = imparfait («pleuvoir» → «il pleuvait»); leaving = passé composé.", "right": "Excellent ! Background weather + one event.", "wrong": "Weather is background: «Il pleuvait». Leaving is one event with «être»: «quand je suis parti(e)»."}]}, "24-1": {"stageId": "24-1", "title": "Linking sentences: qui, que, dont, où", "goal": "By the end you can join two sentences with «qui», «que», «dont» and «où», and use «ce qui / ce que / ce dont».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! Relative pronouns are little bridges that join two sentences so you don't repeat a noun. «Le crayon est sur la table. Le crayon est bleu.» → «Le crayon qui est sur la table est bleu.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «Le crayon qui est sur la table est bleu», what comes right after «qui»?", "choices": ["a verb", "a noun", "a pronoun like «je»"], "answer": "a verb", "right": "Yes! «qui» is the subject of its clause, so a verb follows: «qui est».", "wrong": "Look again: «qui EST». «qui» is the subject (it does the action), so it is followed by a verb."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«que» is the OBJECT: something is done to it. So a subject comes after it: «La pomme que je mange est verte.» → I eat the apple."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La voiture que j'ai achetée est bleue, mais je préfère la voiture de Tom qui est rouge.", "en": "The car that I bought is blue, but I prefer Tom's car, which is red.", "note": "«que» + «j'ai», «qui» + «est»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le bol ___ tu m'as donné est blanc.»", "choices": ["qui", "que", "dont"], "answer": "que", "right": "Bravo ! A subject («tu») follows, and the bowl is what you gave → «que».", "wrong": "After the gap comes «tu», a subject. The bowl receives the action (you gave it), so use «que»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Notice «achetée» above: when the direct object comes BEFORE an «avoir» verb, the participle agrees with it: «La pomme que j'ai mangée», «Les élèves que le professeur a rencontrés»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«dont» replaces «de» + something. Use it with verbs built with «de»: «parler de», «avoir besoin de», «avoir envie de», «être membre de»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«avoir besoin de» = to need. «L'outil ___ nous avons besoin ne fonctionne pas.»", "choices": ["que", "dont", "où"], "answer": "dont", "right": "Super ! «avoir besoin DE cet outil» → «dont».", "wrong": "We say «avoir besoin de quelque chose». The «de» means you need «dont»: «l'outil dont nous avons besoin». «que» would be a classic English-speaker mistake."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«où» = where, but also «when» after a time word: «La ville où tu es allé», «l'année où j'ai terminé mes études»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«C'est l'année ___ j'ai terminé mes études.»", "choices": ["où", "quand", "que"], "answer": "où", "right": "Perfect! After a time noun like «l'année» or «le jour», French uses «où».", "wrong": "English says 'the year when', but French uses «où» after time nouns: «l'année où», «le jour où»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "When there is no specific noun, add «ce»: «ce qui» (+ verb), «ce que» (+ subject), «ce dont» (+ «de» verb). They mean 'what' or 'which' and can sum up a whole idea."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il n'a pas réussi son examen, ce qui est dommage.", "en": "He didn't pass his exam, which is a shame.", "note": "«ce qui» refers to the whole idea."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle ne sait pas ___ elle veut.»", "choices": ["ce qui", "ce qu'", "ce dont"], "answer": "ce qu'", "right": "Bravo ! A subject («elle») follows, and «vouloir» takes a direct object → «ce que», shortened to «ce qu'».", "wrong": "«elle» (a subject) follows, so we need the «que» form: «ce que» → «ce qu'» before a vowel."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«quoi» is used after a verb + infinitive: «Nous ne savons pas quoi faire.» = We don't know what to do."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «qui» + verb (subject), «que» + subject (object), «dont» replaces «de…», «où» = place or time. Add «ce» when there's no noun."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The book we talked about is bad. (use «on»)", "answer": "Le livre dont on a parlé est mauvais.", "accept": ["le livre dont on a parlé est mauvais", "le livre dont on a parle est mauvais", "le livre dont nous avons parlé est mauvais"], "hint": "«parler de» → «dont»", "right": "Excellent ! «parler de» → «dont», and no preposition at the end like in English.", "wrong": "«parler de» needs «dont»: «Le livre dont on a parlé est mauvais.» French never ends the clause with a preposition."}]}, "24-2": {"stageId": "24-2", "title": "Verbs + de / à + infinitive", "goal": "By the end you know which common verbs take «de» and which take «à» before an infinitive.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In English: 'I decide to go', 'I learn to drive'. In French, many verbs need a small linking word before the infinitive: «de» or «à». You must learn it with the verb."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tom a décidé d'apprendre à conduire.", "en": "Tom has decided to learn to drive.", "note": "«décider DE» + «apprendre À»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Common «de» verbs: «essayer de», «oublier de», «décider de», «refuser de», «promettre de», «arrêter de», «rêver de», «avoir besoin de», «avoir envie de», «avoir peur de»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai oublié ___ prendre mes clés.»", "choices": ["de", "à", "—"], "answer": "de", "right": "Yes! «oublier de» + infinitive.", "wrong": "«oublier» is a «de» verb: «J'ai oublié de prendre mes clés»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle a arrêté ___ fumer.»", "choices": ["à", "de", "pour"], "answer": "de", "right": "Bravo ! «arrêter de» = to stop doing.", "wrong": "«arrêter» takes «de»: «Elle a arrêté de fumer». English uses '-ing', French uses «de» + infinitive."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Common «à» verbs: «apprendre à», «aider à», «commencer à», «continuer à», «réussir à», «arriver à» (to manage to), «avoir du mal à», «s'habituer à», «penser à», «hésiter à», «servir à»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle a réussi ___ obtenir un poste dans une grande entreprise.»", "choices": ["d'", "à"], "answer": "à", "right": "Super ! «réussir à» = to succeed in / manage to.", "wrong": "«réussir» is an «à» verb: «Elle a réussi à obtenir un poste»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: many «à» verbs describe moving TOWARDS a goal (learning, helping, starting, succeeding). Many «de» verbs describe stopping, avoiding or deciding."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «J'ai du mal à terminer mon travail» mean?", "choices": ["I find it hard to finish my work", "I did badly at my work", "I hate finishing my work"], "answer": "I find it hard to finish my work", "right": "Exactly! «avoir du mal à» = to find it hard to.", "wrong": "«avoir du mal à» + infinitive = to find it hard / to struggle to do something."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce couteau sert à couper le pain.", "en": "This knife is used for cutting bread."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le bruit m'empêche ___ dormir.»", "choices": ["de", "à"], "answer": "de", "right": "Parfait ! «empêcher de» = to prevent from.", "wrong": "«empêcher» (to prevent) takes «de»: «Le bruit m'empêche de dormir»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«de» becomes «d'» before a vowel: «Alice a l'intention d'aller à l'université.» «à» never shortens."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: learn each verb with its little word. «essayer de, oublier de, décider de» vs «apprendre à, réussir à, commencer à»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I dream of becoming a singer. (female speaker)", "answer": "Je rêve de devenir chanteuse.", "accept": ["je rêve de devenir chanteuse", "je reve de devenir chanteuse", "je rêve de devenir chanteur", "je reve de devenir chanteur", "je rêve de devenir chanteur."], "hint": "«rêver de» + infinitive", "right": "Très bien ! «rêver de» + infinitive, and no article before a job.", "wrong": "«rêver» takes «de»: «Je rêve de devenir chanteuse.» No «une» before the job."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'apprends à parler français et j'essaie de pratiquer tous les jours.»", "fr": "J'apprends à parler français et j'essaie de pratiquer tous les jours."}]}, "24-3": {"stageId": "24-3", "title": "Qui or que?", "goal": "By the end you can choose between «qui» and «que» every time.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Quick trick for «qui» vs «que»: look at the word right AFTER the gap. A verb → «qui». A subject (noun or pronoun) → «que»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«La fille ___ parle avec Tom s'appelle Natasha.» What follows the gap?", "choices": ["a verb, so «qui»", "a noun, so «que»"], "answer": "a verb, so «qui»", "right": "Yes! «parle» is a verb, and the girl is the one talking → «qui».", "wrong": "After the gap comes «parle», a verb. The girl does the action, so «qui»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous prenons le train qui part à 10 heures.", "en": "We are taking the train that leaves at 10."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«que» is followed by a subject: «Le livre que j'ai acheté est intéressant.» I bought the book; the book receives the action."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«que» becomes «qu'» before a vowel («le livre qu'Arthur m'a prêté»), but «qui» NEVER shortens: «l'ami qui arrive»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Voici le livre ___ Arthur m'a prêté.»", "choices": ["qui", "que", "qu'"], "answer": "qu'", "right": "Bravo ! A subject («Arthur») follows → «que», shortened to «qu'» before the vowel.", "wrong": "After the gap comes «Arthur», a subject, so «que». Before a vowel it becomes «qu'»: «qu'Arthur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je loue un appartement ___ se trouve près de la gare.»", "choices": ["qui", "que"], "answer": "qui", "right": "Super ! «se trouve» is a (reflexive) verb → «qui».", "wrong": "A reflexive pronoun + verb («se trouve») follows, so «qui». The flat is the subject."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In English you can drop 'that' ('the book I bought'). In French «que» can NEVER be dropped: «le livre que j'ai acheté»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les personnes ___ nous avons rencontrées hier sont sympas.»", "choices": ["qui", "que"], "answer": "que", "right": "Parfait ! «nous» is the subject; the people are what we met. And look: «rencontrées» agrees with «les personnes».", "wrong": "«nous» follows, a subject, so «que». Bonus: the participle «rencontrées» agrees because the object comes before «avoir»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«C'est Natasha ___ m'a dit ça.»", "choices": ["qui", "que"], "answer": "qui", "right": "Exactly! «m'a dit» starts with a pronoun, but the verb is «a dit», and Natasha is the one speaking → «qui».", "wrong": "Careful: «m'» is an object pronoun, not a subject. Natasha did the telling, so «qui»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «qui» = subject, + verb, never shortens. «que» = object, + subject, becomes «qu'», never dropped."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The cat that the children found is black and white.", "answer": "Le chat que les enfants ont trouvé est noir et blanc.", "accept": ["le chat que les enfants ont trouvé est noir et blanc", "le chat que les enfants ont trouve est noir et blanc"], "hint": "«les enfants» is a subject → «que»", "right": "Très bien ! «les enfants» is the subject, the cat is the object → «que».", "wrong": "«les enfants» (subject) follows, so «que»: «Le chat que les enfants ont trouvé est noir et blanc.»"}]}, "24-4": {"stageId": "24-4", "title": "Reading: Manon des Sources", "goal": "By the end you can understand a short film summary and spot relative pronouns in a real text.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's read about a famous French film based on Marcel Pagnol's novel, set in the hills of Provence. Watch for «qui» and «dont»!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Manon des Sources est le deuxième volet du film «Jean de Florette» dont l'histoire est inspirée d'un des romans de Marcel Pagnol.", "en": "Manon des Sources is the second part of the film 'Jean de Florette', whose story is inspired by one of Marcel Pagnol's novels.", "note": "«dont l'histoire» = whose story."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Here «dont» translates as…", "choices": ["whose", "where", "who"], "answer": "whose", "right": "Yes! «dont» + noun often means 'whose': «le film dont l'histoire…» = the film whose story…", "wrong": "«l'histoire DU film» → «dont l'histoire». With a noun after it, «dont» means 'whose'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "L'histoire se passe une décennie après la mort de Jean, le père de Manon.", "en": "The story takes place a decade after the death of Jean, Manon's father.", "note": "«se passer» = to take place."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "When does the story take place?", "choices": ["Ten years after Jean's death", "Ten days after Jean's death", "Before Jean's death"], "answer": "Ten years after Jean's death", "right": "Bravo ! «une décennie» = a decade, ten years.", "wrong": "«une décennie» = a decade (ten years), like 'decade' in English."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La belle Manon, qui est maintenant bergère, mène une vie recluse dans les collines.", "en": "The beautiful Manon, who is now a shepherdess, lives a solitary life in the hills."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is Manon's job?", "choices": ["She is a shepherdess", "She is a teacher", "She is a farmer's wife"], "answer": "She is a shepherdess", "right": "Super ! «bergère» = shepherdess (masculine «berger»).", "wrong": "«bergère» means shepherdess — she looks after sheep. Masculine: «berger»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Notice «refuse de fréquenter les villageois» and «décide de se venger»: two verbs + «de» from the last lesson!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Lorsqu'elle découvre la vérité, elle décide ___ se venger.»", "choices": ["de", "à"], "answer": "de", "right": "Parfait ! «décider de» + infinitive. «se venger» = to take revenge.", "wrong": "«décider» always takes «de»: «elle décide de se venger»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«tomber amoureux de» = to fall in love with: «Ugolin… tombe amoureux d'elle.» Not «avec»!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: you read «dont» = whose, «qui» for extra info, and «de» verbs in a real text. Bravo !"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Ugolin falls in love with her.", "answer": "Ugolin tombe amoureux d'elle.", "accept": ["ugolin tombe amoureux d'elle", "ugolin tombe amoureux d elle"], "hint": "«tomber amoureux de» + «elle»", "right": "Très bien ! «de» + «elle» → «d'elle».", "wrong": "French says «tomber amoureux DE quelqu'un»: «Ugolin tombe amoureux d'elle.»"}]}, "25-1": {"stageId": "25-1", "title": "Mine, this one, me: three kinds of pronouns", "goal": "By the end you can use possessive pronouns («le mien»), demonstrative pronouns («celui-ci») and emphatic pronouns («moi, toi, lui»).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Bonjour ! Listen: «Ma maison est grande mais la sienne est encore plus grande que la mienne.» Two little words replace «maison» so we don't repeat it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What do you think «la mienne» means here?", "choices": ["mine", "hers", "my house is"], "answer": "mine", "right": "Yes! «la mienne» = mine. And «la sienne» = his/hers.", "wrong": "«ma maison» → «la mienne» = mine. It replaces «ma maison». («la sienne» is the one that means his/hers.)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "A possessive pronoun = article + pronoun, and BOTH agree with the thing owned, not the owner: «mon chat» → «le mien», «ma maison» → «la mienne», «mes livres» → «les miens», «mes robes» → «les miennes»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le mien / le tien / le sien / le nôtre / le vôtre / le leur", "en": "mine / yours / his-hers / ours / yours / theirs", "note": "Feminine: «la mienne, la tienne, la sienne, la nôtre, la vôtre, la leur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ce sont tes fleurs ?» (fleurs = feminine plural) — «Oui, ce sont ___.»", "choices": ["les tiens", "les tiennes", "la tienne"], "answer": "les tiennes", "right": "Bravo ! «fleurs» is feminine plural → «les tiennes».", "wrong": "Match the thing owned: «fleurs» is feminine plural, so «les tiennes» (feminine «-nnes» + plural «les»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "After «à» and «de», the article contracts: «à + le mien» = «au mien», «de + les miens» = «des miens». «Je me suis servi de ton téléphone, pas du mien.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Demonstrative pronouns = this one / that one: «celui» (m.), «celle» (f.), «ceux» (m. pl.), «celles» (f. pl.). They need «-ci/-là», «qui/que», or «de» after them."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Mon vélo est bleu. ___ de mon frère est rouge.»", "choices": ["Celui", "Celle", "Ce"], "answer": "Celui", "right": "Super ! «vélo» is masculine singular → «celui de mon frère» = my brother's.", "wrong": "«vélo» is masculine singular, so «celui». «celle» is feminine; «ce» needs a noun after it."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quel gâteau préfères-tu ? Celui-ci ou celui-là ?", "en": "Which cake do you prefer? This one or that one?", "note": "«-ci» = near, «-là» = further away."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Emphatic pronouns: «moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux, elles». Use them for emphasis, alone, after «c'est», and after prepositions."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je vais chez ___.» (them, masculine)", "choices": ["ils", "eux", "leur"], "answer": "eux", "right": "Parfait ! After a preposition, use the emphatic form: «chez eux».", "wrong": "After a preposition like «chez», «avec» or «pour», use the emphatic pronoun: «eux» (not «ils» or «leur»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Add «-même(s)» to say 'myself, themselves': «J'ai préparé ce gâteau moi-même», «Ils ont construit cette maison eux-mêmes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Qui a pris mon livre ?» — «C'est ___.» (her)", "choices": ["elle", "la", "sa"], "answer": "elle", "right": "Yes! After «c'est» → emphatic «elle».", "wrong": "After «c'est» we need an emphatic pronoun: «C'est elle». «la» and «sa» can't stand alone."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «le mien» family = mine…; «celui/celle/ceux/celles» = this one / the one; «moi, toi, lui…» for emphasis and after prepositions."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: My bag is blue, yours is yellow. (use «tu»)", "answer": "Mon sac est bleu, le tien est jaune.", "accept": ["mon sac est bleu, le tien est jaune", "mon sac est bleu le tien est jaune", "mon sac est bleu, le tien est jaune."], "hint": "«sac» is masculine → «le t…»", "right": "Excellent ! «sac» is masculine singular → «le tien».", "wrong": "«sac» is masculine singular, and 'yours' (informal) → «le tien»: «Mon sac est bleu, le tien est jaune.»"}]}, "25-2": {"stageId": "25-2", "title": "Possessive pronouns: masculine singular", "goal": "By the end you can say mine, yours, his, ours, theirs for a masculine singular noun.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today: only masculine singular nouns, like «chat», «crayon», «chien». The article is always «le»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le mien, le tien, le sien, le nôtre, le vôtre, le leur", "en": "mine, yours, his/hers, ours, yours, theirs"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«C'est mon crayon.» → «C'est ___.»", "choices": ["le mien", "la mienne", "le tien"], "answer": "le mien", "right": "Yes! «mon» → «mien», and «crayon» is masculine → «le mien».", "wrong": "«mon crayon» = my pencil → mine. «crayon» is masculine singular → «le mien»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«le sien» means his OR hers. French doesn't care about the owner's gender, only the noun's: «C'est le stylo de Marie» → «C'est le sien»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "It's Sophie's pen («stylo»). «C'est ___.»", "choices": ["la sienne", "le sien", "le leur"], "answer": "le sien", "right": "Bravo ! «stylo» is masculine, so «le sien», even though Sophie is a woman.", "wrong": "Look at the noun, not the owner: «stylo» is masculine → «le sien». Sophie being a woman doesn't matter."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«nôtre» and «vôtre» (pronouns) have a circumflex and an open 'o' sound. «notre» and «votre» (adjectives, + noun) don't: «notre chien» → «le nôtre»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Votre chien est marron, ___ est blanc.» (theirs)", "choices": ["le leur", "le leurs", "leur"], "answer": "le leur", "right": "Super ! One dog → singular «le leur», no «s».", "wrong": "'theirs' for one masculine thing = «le leur». No «s», because there is one dog."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Remember the contraction: «à côté de + le tien» → «à côté du tien»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Mon téléphone est à côté ___.» (yours, informal)", "choices": ["de le tien", "du tien", "au tien"], "answer": "du tien", "right": "Parfait ! «de + le» always becomes «du».", "wrong": "«à côté de» + «le tien»: «de le» is never allowed → «du tien»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English speakers sometimes think «le mien» refers to the owner (me). No: it refers to the person or thing owned. «Ton frère est grand, le mien est petit.» = my brother."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: masculine singular → «le» + «mien, tien, sien, nôtre, vôtre, leur»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It isn't your pencil, it's mine. (use «ton»)", "answer": "Ce n'est pas ton crayon, c'est le mien.", "accept": ["ce n'est pas ton crayon, c'est le mien", "ce n'est pas ton crayon c'est le mien", "ce n est pas ton crayon, c est le mien"], "hint": "«Ce n'est pas…, c'est le m…»", "right": "Très bien ! «crayon» masculine → «le mien».", "wrong": "«Ce n'est pas ton crayon, c'est le mien.» «crayon» is masculine singular → «le mien»."}]}, "25-3": {"stageId": "25-3", "title": "Possessive pronouns: feminine singular", "goal": "By the end you can say mine, yours, his, ours, theirs for a feminine singular noun.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "For a feminine singular noun («maison», «voiture», «pomme»), the article is «la», and «mien/tien/sien» double the «n» and add «-e»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess: «ma pomme» → «___».", "choices": ["la miene", "la mienne", "le mien"], "answer": "la mienne", "right": "Well done! Double «n» + «e»: «la mienne».", "wrong": "«pomme» is feminine. «mien» becomes «mienne» (double n + e), with «la»: «la mienne»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "la mienne, la tienne, la sienne, la nôtre, la vôtre, la leur", "en": "mine, yours, his/hers, ours, yours, theirs", "note": "«nôtre, vôtre, leur» don't change, only the article does."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Sa voiture est rouge, ___ est bleue.» (theirs)", "choices": ["la leur", "le leur", "la leure"], "answer": "la leur", "right": "Bravo ! «voiture» is feminine → «la leur». «leur» never takes an «e».", "wrong": "«voiture» is feminine → article «la». «leur» doesn't add «-e»: «la leur»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Pronunciation: «mien» has a nasal sound, but «mienne» is NOT nasal — the «n» is pronounced clearly, like «mi-enne»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Paul's house («maison»). «C'est ___.»", "choices": ["le sien", "la sienne", "la mienne"], "answer": "la sienne", "right": "Super ! «maison» is feminine → «la sienne», even though Paul is a man.", "wrong": "Only the noun counts: «maison» is feminine → «la sienne» = his."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With «de», «la» doesn't contract: «différente de la tienne», «plus grande que la vôtre»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Notre école est plus grande que ___.» (yours, informal)", "choices": ["la tienne", "le tien", "du tien"], "answer": "la tienne", "right": "Parfait ! «école» is feminine → «la tienne».", "wrong": "«école» is feminine singular → «la tienne». (And «que» never contracts.)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: feminine singular → «la mienne, la tienne, la sienne, la nôtre, la vôtre, la leur»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: My house is very different from yours. (use «tu»)", "answer": "Ma maison est très différente de la tienne.", "accept": ["ma maison est très différente de la tienne", "ma maison est tres differente de la tienne"], "hint": "«différente de la t…»", "right": "Très bien ! And «différente» agrees with «maison» too.", "wrong": "«Ma maison est très différente de la tienne.» Feminine «maison» → «la tienne», and «différente» with «-e»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ta voiture est rapide, mais la mienne est plus confortable.»", "fr": "Ta voiture est rapide, mais la mienne est plus confortable."}]}, "25-4": {"stageId": "25-4", "title": "Possessive pronouns: masculine plural", "goal": "By the end you can say mine, yours, his… for masculine or mixed plural nouns, including «aux» and «des» contractions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "For plural nouns the article is «les». For masculine (or mixed) plurals, just add «-s»: «les miens, les tiens, les siens, les nôtres, les vôtres, les leurs»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«mes livres» → «___»", "choices": ["les miens", "les miennes", "le mien"], "answer": "les miens", "right": "Yes! «livres» is masculine plural → «les miens».", "wrong": "«livres» is masculine plural: article «les» + «miens» (with «s»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Vos enfants sont grands, les leurs sont petits.", "en": "Your children are tall, theirs are small.", "note": "Mixed group (boys + girls) → masculine plural."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai mes livres. As-tu ___ ?»", "choices": ["les tiens", "les vôtres", "le tien"], "answer": "les tiens", "right": "Bravo ! «tu» → «tien», plural books → «les tiens».", "wrong": "With «tu», 'yours' = «tien». Several books (masculine) → «les tiens». «les vôtres» goes with «vous»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now contractions: «à + les» = «aux», «de + les» = «des»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai parlé à tes frères mais pas ___.» (his)", "choices": ["à les siens", "aux siens", "aux siennes"], "answer": "aux siens", "right": "Super ! «à les» → «aux», and «frères» is masculine → «siens».", "wrong": "«à + les» must become «aux». «frères» is masculine plural → «siens»: «aux siens»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mes chiens sont différents des tiens.", "en": "My dogs are different from yours.", "note": "«de + les tiens» → «des tiens»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«les leurs» = theirs (plural things). Don't confuse it with «leur» the object pronoun ('to them'): «Je leur parle» = I speak to them."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: masculine plural → «les miens, les tiens, les siens, les nôtres, les vôtres, les leurs»; «aux» and «des» after «à» and «de»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Your children are tall, theirs are small. (use «vos»)", "answer": "Vos enfants sont grands, les leurs sont petits.", "accept": ["vos enfants sont grands, les leurs sont petits", "vos enfants sont grands les leurs sont petits"], "hint": "Plural → «les leur…»", "right": "Très bien ! Plural → «les leurs».", "wrong": "Several children → «les leurs» (with «s»): «Vos enfants sont grands, les leurs sont petits.»"}]}, "25-5": {"stageId": "25-5", "title": "Possessive pronouns: feminine plural", "goal": "By the end you can say mine, yours, his… for feminine plural nouns like «robes», «clés», «sœurs».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Last form! For feminine plural nouns: «les miennes, les tiennes, les siennes», and the same «les nôtres, les vôtres, les leurs» as the masculine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«mes robes» → «___»", "choices": ["les miens", "les miennes", "la mienne"], "answer": "les miennes", "right": "Yes! «robes» is feminine plural → «les miennes».", "wrong": "«robes» is feminine plural: double «n» + «es» → «les miennes»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai ses clés dans mon sac — j'ai les siennes.", "en": "I have her keys in my bag — I have hers.", "note": "«clé» is feminine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous avons dormi dans nos tentes, pas dans ___.» (theirs)", "choices": ["les leurs", "les leures", "la leur"], "answer": "les leurs", "right": "Bravo ! «les leurs» works for both genders in the plural.", "wrong": "In the plural, 'theirs' is always «les leurs» — masculine or feminine. Never «leures»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ce sont mes sœurs, pas ___.» (yours, informal)", "choices": ["les tiens", "les tiennes", "les tienne"], "answer": "les tiennes", "right": "Super ! «sœurs» is feminine plural → «les tiennes».", "wrong": "«sœurs» is feminine plural → «les tiennes» (double n + es)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Contraction again: «Cette tente appartient à mes sœurs, pas aux tiennes.» → «à + les» = «aux»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je trouve que les tiennes sont plus jolies que ___.» (the flowers, mine)", "choices": ["les miennes", "les miens", "la mienne"], "answer": "les miennes", "right": "Parfait ! «fleurs» is feminine plural → «les miennes».", "wrong": "We're still talking about «les fleurs» (feminine plural) → «les miennes»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Good news for listening: «les miens» and «les miennes» sound different (nasal vs clear «n»), which helps you hear the gender."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: feminine plural → «les miennes, les tiennes, les siennes, les nôtres, les vôtres, les leurs»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I have her keys — I have hers.", "answer": "J'ai ses clés — j'ai les siennes.", "accept": ["j'ai ses clés, j'ai les siennes", "j'ai ses clés - j'ai les siennes", "j'ai ses clés — j'ai les siennes", "j'ai ses cles, j'ai les siennes", "j'ai ses clefs, j'ai les siennes"], "hint": "«ses clés» → «les s…»", "right": "Très bien ! «clés» feminine plural → «les siennes».", "wrong": "«J'ai ses clés — j'ai les siennes.» «clés» is feminine plural → «les siennes»."}]}, "25-6": {"stageId": "25-6", "title": "Possessive pronouns: putting it all together", "goal": "By the end you can choose the right possessive pronoun for any noun and any owner.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Two questions every time: 1) Who owns it? (→ «mien», «tien», «sien», «nôtre», «vôtre», «leur»). 2) What is the gender and number of the THING? (→ article + ending)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les animaux» — «As-tu vu ___ ?» (ours)", "choices": ["les nôtres", "le nôtre", "les notres"], "answer": "les nôtres", "right": "Yes! Plural → «les nôtres», with the circumflex.", "wrong": "«animaux» is plural → «les nôtres». The pronoun always has «ô»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le manteau bleu» — «J'aime beaucoup ___.» (yours, informal)", "choices": ["la tienne", "le tien", "les tiens"], "answer": "le tien", "right": "Bravo ! «manteau» is masculine singular → «le tien».", "wrong": "«manteau» is masculine singular → «le tien»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«La maison» — «Viens dans ___.» (mine)", "choices": ["le mien", "la mienne", "les miennes"], "answer": "la mienne", "right": "Super ! «maison» is feminine singular → «la mienne».", "wrong": "«maison» is feminine singular → «la mienne»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ton chat ressemble au mien.", "en": "Your cat looks like mine.", "note": "«ressembler à + le mien» → «au mien»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«L'idée» (feminine) — «Elle a eu une bonne idée mais ___ est bien plus intéressante.» (ours)", "choices": ["le nôtre", "la nôtre", "la notre"], "answer": "la nôtre", "right": "Parfait ! «idée» is feminine, even though it starts with «l'» → «la nôtre».", "wrong": "«l'idée» hides the gender, but «idée» is feminine → «la nôtre» (with «ô»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "When «l'» hides the gender («l'idée», «l'ordinateur»), check an adjective or your dictionary: «une bonne idée» → feminine; «un ordinateur» → masculine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le livre» — «Le livre que je lis est très bien mais ___ est encore mieux.» (his)", "choices": ["le sien", "la sienne", "le leur"], "answer": "le sien", "right": "Exactly! «livre» is masculine, owner = he → «le sien».", "wrong": "One owner (he) → «sien»; «livre» is masculine → «le sien». «le leur» would be for several owners."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: owner → the stem; thing → article and ending; «à/de» + «le/les» → «au, aux, du, des»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I forgot my book. Can you lend me yours? (use «tu»)", "answer": "J'ai oublié mon livre. Tu peux me prêter le tien ?", "accept": ["j'ai oublié mon livre. tu peux me prêter le tien ?", "j'ai oublié mon livre. tu peux me prêter le tien?", "j'ai oublie mon livre. tu peux me preter le tien ?", "j'ai oublié mon livre. peux-tu me prêter le tien ?", "j'ai oublié mon livre, tu peux me prêter le tien ?", "j'ai oublié mon livre. tu peux me prêter le tien"], "hint": "«…me prêter le t…»", "right": "Très bien ! «livre» masculine → «le tien».", "wrong": "«J'ai oublié mon livre. Tu peux me prêter le tien ?» «livre» is masculine singular → «le tien»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Mon sac est noir, le sien est blanc.»", "fr": "Mon sac est noir, le sien est blanc."}]}, "25-7": {"stageId": "25-7", "title": "C'est, ces or ses?", "goal": "By the end you can tell apart «c'est», «ces» and «ses», which sound almost the same.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«c'est», «ces» and «ses» all sound like 'say'. In writing you must choose! The trick: look at what comes next."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«c'est» = it is / this is. It's a subject + verb, followed by a name, an article + noun, an adjective or an adverb: «C'est Lucie», «C'est un chat», «C'est beau !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Dis-moi, ___ vrai qu'elle est partie ?»", "choices": ["c'est", "ces", "ses"], "answer": "c'est", "right": "Yes! An adjective («vrai») follows and we need a verb → «c'est».", "wrong": "The sentence needs a verb ('is it true'). «ces» and «ses» are adjectives that need a noun. → «c'est vrai»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«ces» = these/those (pointing) and «ses» = his/her/its (owning). Both are followed by a plural noun."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Regarde tous ces nuages ! / Luc range ses jouets.", "en": "Look at all these clouds! / Luc is putting away his toys."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'aime beaucoup cette écrivaine. J'ai lu tous ___ livres.»", "choices": ["ces", "ses", "c'est"], "answer": "ses", "right": "Bravo ! The books belong to the writer → «ses» (her books).", "wrong": "Ask: whose books? The writer's → possessive «ses» = her. «ces» would just point (these books)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Test: replace with «mes». If it works («J'ai lu tous mes livres»), it's ownership → «ses». If «les» works better, pointing → «ces»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ gens sont incroyables !»", "choices": ["Ces", "Ses", "C'est"], "answer": "Ces", "right": "Super ! We're pointing at people → «ces» = these.", "wrong": "A plural noun follows and nobody 'owns' these people — we point at them → «Ces gens»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Paul s'entend bien avec Tom et Jeanne. Ce sont ___ meilleurs amis.»", "choices": ["ces", "ses", "c'est"], "answer": "ses", "right": "Parfait ! They are Paul's best friends → «ses».", "wrong": "Whose best friends? Paul's → «ses meilleurs amis» = his best friends."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu arrives à 20 h, ___ bien ça ?»", "choices": ["c'est", "ces", "ses"], "answer": "c'est", "right": "Exactly! «c'est bien ça ?» = is that right? We need a verb.", "wrong": "No noun follows, and we need 'is that…' → «c'est bien ça ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «c'est» + name/adjective/article (it is); «ces» + plural noun (these); «ses» + plural noun (his/her)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: These clothes on the chair are mine.", "answer": "Ces vêtements sur la chaise sont à moi.", "accept": ["ces vêtements sur la chaise sont à moi", "ces vetements sur la chaise sont a moi", "ces vêtements posés sur la chaise sont à moi", "ces vêtements sur la chaise sont les miens"], "hint": "Pointing → «Ces…»; 'are mine' → «sont à moi»", "right": "Très bien ! Pointing → «ces», and «être à moi» = to be mine.", "wrong": "«Ces vêtements sur la chaise sont à moi.» We point at the clothes → «ces»; «être à moi» = to belong to me."}]}, "26-1": {"stageId": "26-1", "title": "Object pronouns: le, la, les, lui, leur", "goal": "By the end you can replace direct and indirect objects with pronouns and put them in the right place.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we stop repeating nouns! In «Je porte la veste bleue», ask 'I wear WHAT?' The answer, «la veste bleue», is the direct object, with no preposition before it."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je porte la veste bleue. → Je la porte.", "en": "I wear the blue jacket. → I wear it.", "note": "The pronoun goes BEFORE the verb."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess first: how do you say 'I eat it' for «le gâteau»?", "choices": ["Je mange le.", "Je le mange.", "Je mange-le."], "answer": "Je le mange.", "right": "Yes! «gâteau» is masculine, so «le», and it goes before the conjugated verb.", "wrong": "In French the object pronoun goes before the conjugated verb: «Je le mange». «le gâteau» is masculine, so you use «le»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Direct object pronouns: «me, te, le, la, nous, vous, les». «le» and «la» become «l'» before a vowel: «Il attend sa mère» → «Il l'attend»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je vois mes amis.» → ?", "choices": ["Je leur vois.", "Je les vois.", "Je la vois."], "answer": "Je les vois.", "right": "Perfect! «mes amis» is plural with no «à», so it's a direct object: «les».", "wrong": "«voir» takes a direct object (no «à»), and «mes amis» is plural, so you need «les»: «Je les vois»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now a B1 trap. In the passé composé with «avoir», the participle agrees with a direct object pronoun placed BEFORE it."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai mangé la pomme. → Je l'ai mangée.", "en": "I ate the apple. → I ate it.", "note": "«l'» = «la pomme», feminine, so «mangée»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Martine a rencontré ses filles.» → Martine les a…", "choices": ["rencontré", "rencontrés", "rencontrées"], "answer": "rencontrées", "right": "Bravo! «les» stands for «ses filles», feminine plural, so «-es».", "wrong": "«les» comes before «avoir» and means «ses filles» (feminine plural), so the participle takes «-es»: «rencontrées»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Indirect objects come after «à»: «parler à, téléphoner à, écrire à, envoyer à, dire à». Pronouns: «me, te, lui, nous, vous, leur». «lui» covers both 'to him' and 'to her'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu as téléphoné à ta sœur. → Tu lui as téléphoné.", "en": "You called your sister. → You called her."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'envoie une lettre à Alice et Michel.» → ?", "choices": ["Je les envoie une lettre.", "Je leur envoie une lettre.", "Je lui envoie une lettre."], "answer": "Je leur envoie une lettre.", "right": "Exactly! «à» + two people → «leur». Notice there's no «-s»!", "wrong": "«envoyer à» takes an indirect object. For «à» + several people, use «leur» (never «leurs» before a verb)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English speakers often say «je l'ai téléphoné». But French says «téléphoner À quelqu'un», so the answer is «je lui ai téléphoné». Always check the French verb for «à»!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "With two pronouns, the order is «me/te/nous/vous» → «le/la/les» → «lui/leur» → «y» → «en». With «aller» + infinitive, the pronouns go before the infinitive: «Je vais la lui donner»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il donne le livre à la fille.» → ?", "choices": ["Il lui le donne.", "Il le lui donne.", "Il la lui donne."], "answer": "Il le lui donne.", "right": "Excellent! «le» (the book) comes before «lui» (to the girl).", "wrong": "«le/la/les» always come before «lui/leur». «le livre» → «le», «à la fille» → «lui»: «Il le lui donne»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In the affirmative imperative, pronouns go after the verb with hyphens, and «me» becomes «moi»: «Donne-la-lui !», «Parle-moi !». In the negative, the normal order comes back: «Ne me parle pas !»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: direct = no «à» (le, la, les); indirect = «à» + person (lui, leur); pronouns go before the verb; «le/la/les» come before «lui/leur»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Alice is telling it to them. («Alice raconte l'histoire aux enfants.»)", "answer": "Alice la leur raconte.", "accept": ["alice la leur raconte", "alice la leur raconte."], "hint": "«l'histoire» → «la», «aux enfants» → «leur».", "right": "Superbe! «la» before «leur», both before the verb.", "wrong": "«l'histoire» is feminine → «la»; «aux enfants» → «leur». Order: «la leur» + verb: «Alice la leur raconte.»"}]}, "26-2": {"stageId": "26-2", "title": "Y vs. en", "goal": "By the end you can choose between «y» (à + place/thing) and «en» (de + noun, quantities).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Two tiny words, «y» and «en», save you from repeating things. Here's a clue: «y» often replaces «à» + something, and «en» replaces «de» + something."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu vas à la bibliothèque aujourd'hui ? Oui, j'y vais.", "en": "Are you going to the library today? Yes, I'm going there."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom et Alice habitent en France.» How would you say 'They have lived there for a year'?", "choices": ["Ils en habitent depuis 1 an.", "Ils y habitent depuis 1 an.", "Ils habitent y depuis 1 an."], "answer": "Ils y habitent depuis 1 an.", "right": "Yes! A place (en France) → «y», before the verb.", "wrong": "A place introduced by «à, en, dans, chez» is replaced by «y», placed before the verb: «Ils y habitent»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«y» also replaces «à» + a thing or idea with verbs like «penser à», «réfléchir à», «s'intéresser à»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu t'intéresses à la musique ? Non, je ne m'y intéresse pas.", "en": "Are you interested in music? No, I'm not interested in it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu penses à ta copine ?» Which answer is correct?", "choices": ["Oui, j'y pense.", "Oui, je pense à elle.", "Oui, j'en pense."], "answer": "Oui, je pense à elle.", "right": "Well spotted! For a PERSON after «penser à», use «à elle / à lui», not «y».", "wrong": "«y» is for things and places. For a person after «penser à», say «je pense à elle»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "You already know «y» from fixed expressions: «il y a» (there is/are) and «Allons-y !» (Let's go!)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«en» replaces «de/du/des» + noun, meaning 'some', 'of them' or 'it'. It also works with «avoir besoin de», «avoir envie de», «s'occuper de», «parler de»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "As-tu du lait ? Oui, j'en ai.", "en": "Do you have some milk? Yes, I have some."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Avez-vous des livres ?» 'Yes, I have a lot of them.'", "choices": ["Oui, j'en ai beaucoup.", "Oui, j'y ai beaucoup.", "Oui, je les ai beaucoup."], "answer": "Oui, j'en ai beaucoup.", "right": "Bravo! «des livres» (a quantity) → «en», and the quantity word «beaucoup» stays.", "wrong": "«des» + noun is a quantity, so use «en». Keep «beaucoup» at the end: «J'en ai beaucoup»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«As-tu besoin de l'ordinateur ?» 'No, I don't need it.'", "choices": ["Non, je n'y ai pas besoin.", "Non, je n'en ai pas besoin.", "Non, je ne l'ai pas besoin."], "answer": "Non, je n'en ai pas besoin.", "right": "Exactly! «avoir besoin DE» → «en». In the negative, «ne» + «en» = «n'en».", "wrong": "The verb is «avoir besoin de», and «de» + thing → «en». So: «Je n'en ai pas besoin»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «à/en/dans/chez» + place, or «à» + thing → «y». «de» + noun or a quantity → «en». Both go before the verb."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Elle parle du film ? Oui, elle en parle.»", "fr": "Elle parle du film ? Oui, elle en parle."}]}, "26-3": {"stageId": "26-3", "title": "Y or en? Practice in context", "goal": "By the end you can quickly replace phrases with «y» or «en», including in the passé composé and the negative.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Practice time! Ask yourself one question: is there «à / chez / dans / en + place», or «de / du / des»? That tells you «y» or «en»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu es allé chez ton oncle.» → ?", "choices": ["Tu en es allé.", "Tu y es allé.", "Tu es allé y."], "answer": "Tu y es allé.", "right": "Yes! «chez» + person's house = a place → «y». In the passé composé, it goes before the auxiliary.", "wrong": "«chez ton oncle» is a place, so use «y». It goes before the auxiliary «es»: «Tu y es allé»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous achetons des poires. → Nous en achetons.", "en": "We are buying pears. → We are buying some."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai réfléchi à ta proposition.» → ?", "choices": ["J'en ai réfléchi.", "J'y ai réfléchi.", "J'ai y réfléchi."], "answer": "J'y ai réfléchi.", "right": "Parfait! «réfléchir à» + a thing → «y», before «ai».", "wrong": "«réfléchir à» + idea → «y». The pronoun goes before the auxiliary: «J'y ai réfléchi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With reflexive verbs, «y» goes right after the reflexive pronoun: «Nous nous intéressons à l'histoire» → «Nous nous y intéressons»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Alice ne boit pas de thé.» → ?", "choices": ["Alice n'y boit pas.", "Alice n'en boit pas.", "Alice ne boit pas en."], "answer": "Alice n'en boit pas.", "right": "Bravo! «de thé» → «en», and the pronoun sits between «n'» and the verb.", "wrong": "After a negative, «du» becomes «de». «de thé» → «en», placed between «ne» and the verb: «Alice n'en boit pas»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In questions with inversion, «en/y» go before the verb: «Voulez-vous du café ?» → «En voulez-vous ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Mes amis vont en France pour le week-end.» → ?", "choices": ["Mes amis en vont pour le week-end.", "Mes amis y vont pour le week-end.", "Mes amis vont y pour le week-end."], "answer": "Mes amis y vont pour le week-end.", "right": "Careful thinking! Here «en» is the preposition 'to', so «en France» is a place → «y».", "wrong": "Here «en» is just the preposition before a country, not the pronoun. «en France» = a place, so: «Mes amis y vont»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «y» = place or «à» + thing; «en» = «de» + noun or a quantity. Both go before the conjugated verb or the auxiliary."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I ate some. («J'ai mangé des frites.»)", "answer": "J'en ai mangé.", "accept": ["j'en ai mangé", "j'en ai mangé.", "j'en ai mange", "j en ai mangé", "j’en ai mangé"], "hint": "«en» goes before «ai».", "right": "Excellent! And no agreement with «en»: «mangé» stays as it is.", "wrong": "«des frites» → «en», placed before the auxiliary: «J'en ai mangé.» The participle doesn't agree with «en»."}]}, "27-1": {"stageId": "27-1", "title": "The conditional: would, could, should", "goal": "By the end you can form the conditionnel présent and use it for hypotheses, wishes, polite requests and advice.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today's tense is the 'would' tense: the «conditionnel présent». We use it for things that depend on a condition, for wishes, and for polite requests."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Si je gagnais la course, je recevrais une médaille.", "en": "If I won the race, I would receive a medal."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Here's the recipe: the FUTURE stem + the IMPARFAIT endings «-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient». So «je marcherai» (future) becomes «je marcherais» (conditional)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess: if the future of «finir» is «nous finirons», what is 'we would finish'?", "choices": ["nous finissions", "nous finirions", "nous finirons"], "answer": "nous finirions", "right": "Yes! Future stem «finir-» + imperfect ending «-ions».", "wrong": "Keep the future stem «finir-» and add the imperfect ending «-ions»: «nous finirions». «finissions» is the imparfait, and «finirons» is the future."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Irregular verbs keep their irregular future stems: «être» → «ser-», «avoir» → «aur-», «savoir» → «saur-», «aller» → «ir-»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I would be happy to welcome you.'", "choices": ["Je serais heureux de t'accueillir.", "J'étais heureux de t'accueillir.", "Je serai heureux de t'accueillir."], "answer": "Je serais heureux de t'accueillir.", "right": "Parfait! «ser-» (future stem of être) + «-ais» = «serais».", "wrong": "'Would be' = future stem «ser-» + «-ais»: «je serais». «j'étais» = I was; «je serai» = I will be."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Politeness magic: «Je voudrais…» (I would like) sounds much softer than «Je veux…». The same goes for «Pourriez-vous… ?» (Could you…?)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "English 'could' = «pouvoir» in the conditional («je pourrais»). 'Should' = «devoir» in the conditional («je devrais»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'You should do your homework.'", "choices": ["Tu pourrais faire tes devoirs.", "Tu devrais faire tes devoirs.", "Tu dois faire tes devoirs."], "answer": "Tu devrais faire tes devoirs.", "right": "Exactly! 'Should' = conditional of «devoir»: «tu devrais».", "wrong": "'Should' is «devoir» in the conditional: «tu devrais». «pourrais» = could; «dois» = must."}, {"t": "say", "text": "After «si» (if), French NEVER uses the conditional. It uses the imparfait: «si + imparfait, conditionnel»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Tom would speak English if he went to his lessons every day.'", "choices": ["Tom parlerait l'anglais s'il irait à ses cours tous les jours.", "Tom parlerait l'anglais s'il allait à ses cours tous les jours.", "Tom parlait l'anglais s'il allait à ses cours tous les jours."], "answer": "Tom parlerait l'anglais s'il allait à ses cours tous les jours.", "right": "Bravo! Imparfait after «si» («allait»), conditional in the main clause («parlerait»).", "wrong": "Never put a conditional after «si». The pattern is «si» + imparfait («allait»), and the result clause takes the conditional («parlerait»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English 'could/would' about the PAST often becomes the imparfait: «Elle a cru qu'elle pouvait le faire» (She thought she could do it). «Quand j'avais peur, je fermais les yeux» (When I was scared, I would close my eyes) is a past habit."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: future stem + «-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient». 'Could' = «pourrais», 'should' = «devrais», and «si» + imparfait."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Could I have a glass of water please?", "answer": "Pourrais-je avoir un verre d'eau s'il vous plaît ?", "accept": ["pourrais-je avoir un verre d'eau s'il vous plaît ?", "pourrais-je avoir un verre d'eau s'il vous plaît", "pourrais-je avoir un verre d'eau, s'il vous plaît ?", "est-ce que je pourrais avoir un verre d'eau s'il vous plaît ?", "est-ce que je pourrais avoir un verre d'eau, s'il vous plaît ?", "je pourrais avoir un verre d'eau s'il vous plaît ?", "pourrais-je avoir un verre d'eau s'il vous plait ?", "pourrais-je avoir un verre d'eau s'il vous plait"], "hint": "Use the conditional of «pouvoir» with inversion: «Pourrais-je…».", "right": "Très poli! «Pourrais-je» is the elegant, polite form.", "wrong": "'Could I' = «Pourrais-je» (or «Est-ce que je pourrais»): «Pourrais-je avoir un verre d'eau s'il vous plaît ?»"}]}, "27-2": {"stageId": "27-2", "title": "-ER verbs in 5 tenses", "goal": "By the end you can recognise and form «marcher»-type verbs in the présent, passé composé, imparfait, futur and conditionnel.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Let's put five tenses side by side with «marcher» (to walk). Each tense has its own clue to listen for."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je marche · j'ai marché · je marchais · je marcherai · je marcherais", "en": "I walk · I walked · I was walking · I will walk · I would walk"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Présent: drop «-er» and add «-e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent». Passé composé: «avoir/être» + «marché»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which tense is «nous marchions»?", "choices": ["présent", "imparfait", "conditionnel"], "answer": "imparfait", "right": "Yes! The «nous» stem «march-» + «-ions», with no «r» before the ending.", "wrong": "No «-er-» before the ending means it's not the conditional. «march-» + «-ions» is the imparfait («nous marchions»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Imparfait: take the «nous» present form, drop «-ons», and add «-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient». Futur: the infinitive + «-ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Paul will give me a piano lesson tomorrow.'", "choices": ["Paul me donnerait une leçon de piano demain.", "Paul me donnera une leçon de piano demain.", "Paul me donnait une leçon de piano demain."], "answer": "Paul me donnera une leçon de piano demain.", "right": "Perfect! Infinitive «donner» + «-a» = «donnera» (future).", "wrong": "'Will give' is the futur: «donner» + «-a» = «donnera». «donnerait» = would give; «donnait» = was giving."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Conditionnel: the future stem + the imparfait endings. So the futur and conditionnel share the «-er-» sound; only the ending changes."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I would walk' vs 'I will walk': which one is the conditional?", "choices": ["je marcherai", "je marcherais", "je marchais"], "answer": "je marcherais", "right": "Exactly! «-erais» = conditional. «-erai» is the future.", "wrong": "The conditional = future stem + «-ais»: «je marcherais». «marcherai» is the future, and «marchais» is the imparfait."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch out for doubled letters! «appeler» → «j'appellerai» → «j'appellerais». So: «Charlotte appellerait sa mère si elle avait le temps.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: present «-e/-ons», past «ai marché», imparfait «-ais» with no «r», future «-erai», conditional «-erais»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Tom and Charlotte used to go for a walk every day. (se promener)", "answer": "Tom et Charlotte se promenaient tous les jours.", "accept": ["tom et charlotte se promenaient tous les jours", "tom et charlotte se promenaient tous les jours."], "hint": "Imparfait, «ils» form: «-aient».", "right": "Bravo! A habit in the past → imparfait «se promenaient».", "wrong": "'Used to' = imparfait: «nous promenons» → «promen-» + «-aient»: «Tom et Charlotte se promenaient tous les jours.»"}]}, "27-3": {"stageId": "27-3", "title": "-IR verbs (finir) in 5 tenses", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate regular -IR verbs like «finir» in five tenses, including the «-iss-» forms.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Group 2 verbs end in «-ir» and have a secret ingredient: «-iss-» in the plural present and in the imparfait."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je finis · nous finissons · ils finissent", "en": "I finish · we finish · they finish", "note": "Singular: «-is, -is, -it». Plural: «-issons, -issez, -issent»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess the imparfait of «finir» for «je».", "choices": ["je finais", "je finissais", "je finirais"], "answer": "je finissais", "right": "Yes! Imparfait = «nous finissons» minus «-ons» → «finiss-» + «-ais».", "wrong": "Start from «nous finissons», drop «-ons», and add «-ais»: «je finissais». «finirais» is the conditional."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Futur and conditionnel are simple: add the endings straight to the infinitive «finir-». «je finirai» (will), «je finirais» (would)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Paul will put on weight if he carries on eating too many sweets.' (grossir)", "choices": ["Paul grossira", "Paul grossirait", "Paul grossissait"], "answer": "Paul grossira", "right": "Perfect! «si» + present → future in the result: «grossira».", "wrong": "With «si» + present («continue»), the result is in the future: «grossir» + «-a» = «grossira»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Passé composé: «avoir» + participle in «-i». «J'ai réfléchi à ta proposition.» (I thought about your proposal.)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Charlotte would warn her parents if she had their number.' (avertir)", "choices": ["Charlotte avertissait ses parents", "Charlotte avertirait ses parents", "Charlotte avertira ses parents"], "answer": "Charlotte avertirait ses parents", "right": "Bravo! «avertir-» + «-ait» = conditional.", "wrong": "'Would warn' = infinitive stem «avertir-» + «-ait»: «avertirait». «avertissait» is the imparfait."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Other verbs that follow «finir»: «rougir» (to blush), «obéir» (to obey), «grossir» (to put on weight), «agir» (to act), «avertir» (to warn), «réfléchir» (to think)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «-iss-» in the plural present and the imparfait; «finir-» + endings for the future and conditional; «fini» for the passé composé."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Tom and I are finishing our homework.", "answer": "Tom et moi finissons nos devoirs.", "accept": ["tom et moi finissons nos devoirs", "tom et moi finissons nos devoirs.", "tom et moi, nous finissons nos devoirs."], "hint": "«Tom et moi» = «nous».", "right": "Excellent! «Tom et moi» = «nous» → «finissons».", "wrong": "«Tom et moi» works like «nous», so you need «finissons»: «Tom et moi finissons nos devoirs.»"}]}, "27-4": {"stageId": "27-4", "title": "Irregular verbs in 5 tenses", "goal": "By the end you can use key irregular verbs in five tenses, especially their irregular future/conditional stems.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Irregular verbs look scary, but there are patterns. Almost all of them end in «-ons, -ez, -ent» for «nous, vous, ils» in the present."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "je connais · j'ai connu · je connaissais · je connaîtrai · je connaîtrais", "en": "I know · I knew · I used to know · I will know · I would know"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Imparfait: same trick as always. Start from the «nous» present form: «nous prenons» → «je prenais». Only «être» breaks the rule: «j'étais»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Tom and Charlotte used to take the train every day.'", "choices": ["prendraient", "prenaient", "prennent"], "answer": "prenaient", "right": "Yes! «nous prenons» → «pren-» + «-aient».", "wrong": "'Used to' = imparfait: «nous prenons» → «pren-» + «-aient» = «prenaient»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Here's the big thing to learn: the future/conditional stems. «avoir» → «aur-», «être» → «ser-», «aller» → «ir-», «faire» → «fer-», «savoir» → «saur-», «pouvoir» → «pourr-», «voir» → «verr-», «venir» → «viendr-»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Paul will come to my house tomorrow.'", "choices": ["Paul venira chez moi demain.", "Paul viendra chez moi demain.", "Paul viendrait chez moi demain."], "answer": "Paul viendra chez moi demain.", "right": "Parfait! Stem «viendr-» + «-a» (future).", "wrong": "«venir» has the irregular stem «viendr-». 'Will come' = «viendra»; «viendrait» = would come."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Charlotte could go to the swimming pool to relax.'", "choices": ["Charlotte pourrait aller à la piscine pour se détendre.", "Charlotte pouvait aller à la piscine pour se détendre.", "Charlotte pourra aller à la piscine pour se détendre."], "answer": "Charlotte pourrait aller à la piscine pour se détendre.", "right": "Bravo! A suggestion → conditional «pourrait» (double «r»).", "wrong": "A suggestion ('could') = conditional: «pourr-» + «-ait» = «pourrait». «pouvait» = was able to, and «pourra» = will be able to."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Past participles to know by heart: «eu, été, allé, fait, pris, bu, su, pu, vu, venu, dormi, sorti». With «être», the participle agrees: «Les filles sont sorties.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: present = learn it; imparfait = «nous» stem (except «être»); future and conditional = irregular stem + endings."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We would see the sea. (voir)", "answer": "Nous verrions la mer.", "accept": ["nous verrions la mer", "nous verrions la mer.", "on verrait la mer"], "hint": "Stem «verr-» + «-ions».", "right": "Excellent! «verr-» + «-ions» = «verrions».", "wrong": "«voir» → stem «verr-»; for 'would' add «-ions»: «Nous verrions la mer.»"}]}, "27-5": {"stageId": "27-5", "title": "Imparfait or conditionnel?", "goal": "By the end you can tell the imparfait and the conditionnel apart by ear and on paper, and use «si» + imparfait.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Here's a puzzle: the imparfait and the conditional have the SAME endings («-ais, -ait, -ions…»). So what tells them apart?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Look closely: «je marchais» vs «je marcherais». What's the difference?", "choices": ["The ending", "The «r» before the ending", "The subject"], "answer": "The «r» before the ending", "right": "Well spotted! The conditional uses the future stem, so you hear an «r» before the ending.", "wrong": "The endings are identical. The conditional is built on the future stem, so it has an «r» before the ending: «marcheRais»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The imparfait describes the past: habits, ongoing actions, descriptions, feelings. «Je jouais au tennis tous les mercredis.» (I used to play tennis every Wednesday.)"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je regardais la télévision lorsque quelqu'un a frappé à la porte.", "en": "I was watching TV when someone knocked on the door."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The conditional is about 'would': something that depends on a condition. «Je sortirais s'il faisait beau.» (I would go out if the weather was nice.)"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one means 'we would have'?", "choices": ["nous avions", "nous aurons", "nous aurions"], "answer": "nous aurions", "right": "Yes! «aur-» (future stem) + «-ions».", "wrong": "«nous avions» = we had (imparfait); «nous aurons» = we will have; «nous aurions» = we would have."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I would buy a house if I had the money.'", "choices": ["J'achèterais une maison si j'aurais l'argent.", "J'achèterais une maison si j'avais l'argent.", "J'achetais une maison si j'avais l'argent."], "answer": "J'achèterais une maison si j'avais l'argent.", "right": "Perfect! Imparfait after «si», conditional in the result.", "wrong": "After «si», use the imparfait («j'avais»), never the conditional. The result clause gets the conditional: «j'achèterais»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Both tenses can appear in the same sentence: the imparfait in the «si» part, the conditional in the result. «Tom irait en France si ses amis venaient avec lui.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: same endings; an «r» before the ending = conditional; «si» + imparfait, then conditional."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Tom and I would come with you if we had the time.", "answer": "Tom et moi viendrions avec vous si nous avions le temps.", "accept": ["tom et moi viendrions avec vous si nous avions le temps", "tom et moi viendrions avec vous si nous avions le temps.", "tom et moi viendrions avec toi si nous avions le temps", "tom et moi viendrions avec toi si nous avions le temps."], "hint": "«viendr-» + «-ions» … «si nous av-»", "right": "Superbe! «viendrions» (conditional) + «si nous avions» (imparfait).", "wrong": "Result = conditional «viendrions»; condition = «si» + imparfait «avions»: «Tom et moi viendrions avec vous si nous avions le temps.»"}]}, "27-6": {"stageId": "27-6", "title": "-ER verbs: mixed-tense workout", "goal": "By the end you can choose and form the right tense of -ER verbs, including spelling changes.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Workout time! For each sentence, first find the tense, then build the verb. Let's start with the present."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom et moi (ranger) la maison.» (présent)", "choices": ["rangons", "rangeons", "rangent"], "answer": "rangeons", "right": "Yes! We keep an «e» after «g» before «o» so the «g» stays soft.", "wrong": "«Tom et moi» = «nous». Before «-ons», «g» needs an «e» to keep the soft sound: «rangeons»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The same trick applies to «manger» → «nous mangeons», «voyager» → «nous voyageons». And with «c», «commencer» → «nous commençons»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nos amis (accepter) notre invitation.» (passé composé)", "choices": ["ont accepté", "sont acceptés", "acceptaient"], "answer": "ont accepté", "right": "Correct! «accepter» uses «avoir»: «ont accepté».", "wrong": "Most verbs, including «accepter», take «avoir» in the passé composé: «Nos amis ont accepté notre invitation.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les enfants ne (rester) pas longtemps.» (futur)", "choices": ["resteraient", "resteront", "restent"], "answer": "resteront", "right": "Bravo! Infinitive + «-ont» = «resteront».", "wrong": "Futur = infinitive + «-ont» for «ils»: «Les enfants ne resteront pas longtemps.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Reflexive verbs keep their pronoun in every tense. «se lever» in the imparfait: «je me levais». No accent is needed because the ending is pronounced."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je me levais toujours très tôt pour aller au marché.", "en": "I always used to get up very early to go to the market."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Si vous aviez de l'argent, est-ce que vous (voyager) ?» (conditionnel)", "choices": ["voyagiez", "voyagerez", "voyageriez"], "answer": "voyageriez", "right": "Excellent! Future stem «voyager-» + «-iez».", "wrong": "The conditional = «voyager-» + «-iez»: «voyageriez». «voyagiez» is the imparfait, and «voyagerez» is the future."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: present «-ons» (watch the g/c spelling), «avoir» + «-é», future «-eront», imparfait «-ais», conditional «-eriez»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Our friends have accepted our invitation.", "answer": "Nos amis ont accepté notre invitation.", "accept": ["nos amis ont accepté notre invitation", "nos amis ont accepté notre invitation.", "nos amis ont accepte notre invitation"], "hint": "«avoir» + «accepté».", "right": "Parfait!", "wrong": "Passé composé = «ont» + «accepté»: «Nos amis ont accepté notre invitation.»"}]}, "27-7": {"stageId": "27-7", "title": "-IR verbs: mixed-tense workout", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate Group 2 verbs in the present, passé composé, futur, imparfait and conditionnel.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Now the «finir» family. Remember the «-iss-» rule: it appears in the plural present and in the imparfait."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les enfants (désobéir) à leurs parents.» (présent)", "choices": ["désobéient", "désobéissent", "désobéirent"], "answer": "désobéissent", "right": "Yes! Plural present → «-issent».", "wrong": "Group 2 verbs add «-iss-» in the plural: «ils désobéissent»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«obéir/désobéir À quelqu'un»: these verbs take «à», so 'to obey them' = «leur obéir»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le chien (bondir) sur la chaise.» (passé composé)", "choices": ["a bondi", "est bondi", "a bondit"], "answer": "a bondi", "right": "Perfect! «avoir» + participle «bondi» (no «t»).", "wrong": "The past participle of «-ir» verbs ends in «-i», and the verb takes «avoir»: «a bondi»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ils (agir) bientôt.» (futur)", "choices": ["agissent", "agiront", "agiraient"], "answer": "agiront", "right": "Bravo! «agir» + «-ont».", "wrong": "Futur = infinitive + «-ont»: «Ils agiront bientôt.» «agiraient» is the conditional."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Alice rougissait chaque fois qu'elle devait parler devant la classe.", "en": "Alice used to blush every time she had to speak in front of the class.", "note": "A repeated past action → imparfait with «-iss-»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom (maigrir) s'il mangeait moins.» (conditionnel)", "choices": ["maigrissait", "maigrirait", "maigrira"], "answer": "maigrirait", "right": "Excellent! «si» + imparfait → conditional «maigrirait».", "wrong": "With «si» + imparfait («mangeait»), the result is conditional: «maigrir-» + «-ait» = «maigrirait»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «-issent» (present plural), «a + -i» (passé composé), «-iront» (future), «-issait» (imparfait), «-irait» (conditional)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Alice used to blush. (rougir)", "answer": "Alice rougissait.", "accept": ["alice rougissait", "alice rougissait."], "hint": "Imparfait with «-iss-».", "right": "Super! «rougiss-» + «-ait».", "wrong": "«nous rougissons» → «rougiss-» + «-ait»: «Alice rougissait.»"}]}, "27-8": {"stageId": "27-8", "title": "-IR verbs in the conditional", "goal": "By the end you can form the conditionnel présent of Group 2 verbs in hypotheses, advice and wishes.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Good news: Group 2 verbs are the easiest in the conditional. Take the whole infinitive and add «-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Si j'avais des pinceaux, je finirais de peindre la cuisine.", "en": "If I had some brushes, I would finish painting the kitchen."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il ___ (guérir) plus vite s'il prenait ses médicaments.»", "choices": ["guérissait", "guérirait", "guérira"], "answer": "guérirait", "right": "Yes! «guérir» + «-ait» = would get better.", "wrong": "The «si» clause is in the imparfait, so the result takes the conditional: «guérirait»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Si j'étais à sa place, je ___ (réfléchir) à deux fois.»", "choices": ["réfléchirais", "réfléchissais", "réfléchirai"], "answer": "réfléchirais", "right": "Parfait! «Si j'étais à sa place…» is a classic way to give advice + conditional.", "wrong": "After «si j'étais», use the conditional: «je réfléchirais». «réfléchissais» is the imparfait."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«réfléchir à deux fois» = to think twice. «Si j'étais à ta place» = If I were you."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«au cas où» (in case) is also followed by the conditional: «au cas où Sophie choisirait de venir»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom et toi ne ___ (réagir) sûrement pas plus vite.»", "choices": ["réagiraient", "réagiriez", "réagirions"], "answer": "réagiriez", "right": "Bravo! «Tom et toi» = «vous» → «-iez».", "wrong": "«Tom et toi» works like «vous», so the ending is «-iez»: «réagiriez»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous ___ (se divertir) si nous allions au concert.»", "choices": ["nous divertirions", "divertirions", "nous divertissions"], "answer": "nous divertirions", "right": "Excellent! Keep the reflexive «nous» + conditional «divertirions».", "wrong": "Reflexive verbs keep their pronoun: «nous nous divertirions». «divertissions» is the imparfait."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: infinitive + imperfect endings; use it with «si» + imparfait, with «au cas où», and to give advice."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The plants would flower if it was less cold. (fleurir)", "answer": "Les plantes fleuriraient s'il faisait moins froid.", "accept": ["les plantes fleuriraient s'il faisait moins froid", "les plantes fleuriraient s'il faisait moins froid.", "les plantes fleuriraient s’il faisait moins froid"], "hint": "«fleurir» + «-aient», then «si» + imparfait of «faire».", "right": "Superbe!", "wrong": "«fleurir» + «-aient» = «fleuriraient»; «si il» → «s'il faisait»: «Les plantes fleuriraient s'il faisait moins froid.»"}]}, "27-9": {"stageId": "27-9", "title": "Irregular verbs: mixed-tense workout", "goal": "By the end you can conjugate common irregular verbs (ouvrir, savoir, prendre, faire, voir, dormir) in the right tense.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Irregular verbs workout! Look for the time word first («hier», «demain», «tous les matins»), and it will tell you the tense."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les enfants (ouvrir) leurs cadeaux.» (passé composé)", "choices": ["ont ouvri", "ont ouvert", "sont ouverts"], "answer": "ont ouvert", "right": "Yes! «ouvrir» has an irregular participle: «ouvert».", "wrong": "«ouvrir» → past participle «ouvert» (like «offrir» → «offert»), with «avoir»: «ont ouvert»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous (savoir) bientôt la vérité.» (futur)", "choices": ["savrons", "saurons", "saurions"], "answer": "saurons", "right": "Perfect! Irregular stem «saur-» + «-ons».", "wrong": "«savoir» → future stem «saur-»: «nous saurons». «saurions» is the conditional."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom (prendre) le train à 8 heures tous les matins.» (présent)", "choices": ["prend", "prends", "prent"], "answer": "prend", "right": "Correct! «il prend» ends in «-d».", "wrong": "«prendre» in the present: «je prends, tu prends, il prend». So «Tom prend»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two key future stems for today: «faire» → «fer-» and «voir» → «verr-»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Alice et Paul feront un gâteau pour le repas de famille.", "en": "Alice and Paul will make a cake for the family dinner."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Hier soir, Charlotte et moi (dormir) chez ma tante.» (passé composé)", "choices": ["avons dormi", "sommes dormies", "dormions"], "answer": "avons dormi", "right": "Bravo! «dormir» takes «avoir», and «Charlotte et moi» = «nous».", "wrong": "«dormir» uses «avoir» (unlike «sortir»): «Charlotte et moi avons dormi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ont ouvert», «saurons», «prend», «feront», «verrai», «avons dormi». Learn the stems and participles by heart."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I will see my brother on Sunday.", "answer": "Je verrai mon frère dimanche.", "accept": ["je verrai mon frère dimanche", "je verrai mon frère dimanche.", "je verrai mon frere dimanche"], "hint": "Stem «verr-» + «-ai». No word for 'on' before days.", "right": "Excellent! And no «le» before «dimanche» for one specific Sunday.", "wrong": "«voir» → «verr-» + «-ai»: «Je verrai mon frère dimanche.»"}]}, "27-10": {"stageId": "27-10", "title": "Conditional drill: all verb types", "goal": "By the end you can quickly put any common verb into the conditionnel présent.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Final conditional drill! Remember the recipe: future stem + «-ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous ___ à la mer.» (aller)", "choices": ["allerions", "irions", "allions"], "answer": "irions", "right": "Yes! «aller» → stem «ir-» → «nous irions».", "wrong": "«aller» has the irregular stem «ir-»: «nous irions». «allions» is the imparfait."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Adam et Sébastien ___ de la bière au bar.» (boire)", "choices": ["boiraient", "buvraient", "buvaient"], "answer": "boiraient", "right": "Perfect! For «-re» verbs, drop the «e»: «boir-» + «-aient».", "wrong": "For «-re» verbs, drop the final «e» and add the endings: «boir-» + «-aient» = «boiraient»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«-re» verbs drop their final «e» in the future/conditional: «boire» → «boir-», «prendre» → «prendr-», «vendre» → «vendr-»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous ___ un cadeau à votre mère.» (offrir)", "choices": ["offririez", "offriez", "offrirez"], "answer": "offririez", "right": "Bravo! «offrir-» + «-iez».", "wrong": "Conditional = «offrir-» + «-iez»: «vous offririez». «offriez» = imparfait, and «offrirez» = future."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ils prépareraient un couscous pour le déjeuner.", "en": "They would prepare couscous for lunch.", "note": "Yes, «-reraient» really has two «r» sounds!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ça ___ une belle chambre.» (être)", "choices": ["serait", "sera", "était"], "answer": "serait", "right": "Excellent! «ser-» + «-ait» = it would be.", "wrong": "'Would be' = «ser-» + «-ait»: «Ça serait une belle chambre.» «sera» = will be, and «était» = was."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: regular verbs → infinitive + endings; «-re» → drop the «e»; irregular verbs → «ir-, ser-, aur-, fer-, pourr-, verr-…»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je partirais à Londres demain, et Alex changerait sa voiture cet été.»", "fr": "Je partirais à Londres demain, et Alex changerait sa voiture cet été."}]}, "28-1": {"stageId": "28-1", "title": "Complex negations", "goal": "By the end you can use negations like «ne… aucun», «ne… ni… ni», «ne… pas encore», and place them correctly with compound tenses, pronouns, reflexives, imperatives and infinitives.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "You know «ne… pas». Today we upgrade! French has a whole family of negations, and the «ne» part always stays the same."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'aimerais une voiture et un vélo, malheureusement je n'ai ni l'un ni l'autre.", "en": "I'd like a car and a bike; unfortunately I have neither.", "note": "«ni… ni» = neither… nor; «ni l'un ni l'autre» = neither one."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«ne… aucun / aucune» = not a single, no. It agrees with the noun: «Il n'a aucun ami», «Il n'a aucune idée»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'She has no chance.' («une chance»)", "choices": ["Elle n'a aucun chance.", "Elle n'a aucune chance.", "Elle n'a pas aucune chance."], "answer": "Elle n'a aucune chance.", "right": "Yes! «chance» is feminine → «aucune». And «aucune» replaces «pas».", "wrong": "«aucun» agrees with the noun: «une chance» → «aucune». Never use «pas» together with «aucun»: «Elle n'a aucune chance.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "More useful ones: «ne… pas encore» (not yet), «ne… pas du tout» (not at all), «ne… pas assez» (not enough), «ne… guère» (hardly)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Tom has not booked his holiday yet.'", "choices": ["Tom n'a plus réservé ses vacances.", "Tom n'a pas encore réservé ses vacances.", "Tom n'a jamais réservé ses vacances."], "answer": "Tom n'a pas encore réservé ses vacances.", "right": "Parfait! 'Not yet' = «pas encore».", "wrong": "'Not yet' = «ne… pas encore». «ne… plus» = no longer, and «ne… jamais» = never."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In the passé composé, the negation wraps around the AUXILIARY: «ne + avoir/être + pas + participle». And «des» becomes «de»: «Je n'ai pas mangé de bananes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il est allé en France le mois dernier.» → negative?", "choices": ["Il n'est allé pas en France le mois dernier.", "Il n'est pas allé en France le mois dernier.", "Il ne pas est allé en France le mois dernier."], "answer": "Il n'est pas allé en France le mois dernier.", "right": "Bravo! «ne» before the auxiliary, «pas» right after it.", "wrong": "In compound tenses, the negation goes around the auxiliary: «n'est pas allé»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With reflexive or object pronouns, «ne» goes BEFORE the pronoun, and «pas» goes after the verb or the auxiliary. The pronoun sticks to its verb!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je ne le lui ai pas donné.", "en": "I didn't give it to him."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle s'est regardée dans le miroir.» → negative?", "choices": ["Elle ne s'est pas regardée dans le miroir.", "Elle s'est ne pas regardée dans le miroir.", "Elle ne s'est regardée pas dans le miroir."], "answer": "Elle ne s'est pas regardée dans le miroir.", "right": "Excellent! subject + «ne» + «s'» + auxiliary + «pas» + participle.", "wrong": "The order is subject + «ne» + reflexive pronoun + auxiliary + «pas» + participle: «Elle ne s'est pas regardée.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Negative commands keep the normal pronoun order: «Ne me parle pas !», «Ne touche pas à mon téléphone !» Only affirmative commands move the pronoun after the verb («Parle-moi !»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some negations combine: «ne… jamais rien» (never anything), «ne… jamais personne» (never anybody), «ne… plus personne» (no longer anybody), «ne… jamais nulle part» (never anywhere)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I never see anybody.'", "choices": ["Je ne vois personne jamais.", "Je ne vois jamais personne.", "Je ne vois pas jamais personne."], "answer": "Je ne vois jamais personne.", "right": "Yes! «jamais» comes first, then «personne», and there's no «pas».", "wrong": "Combined negations: «ne» + verb + «jamais» + «personne», without «pas»: «Je ne vois jamais personne.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "To negate an INFINITIVE, put «ne pas» together before it: «Il me dit de ne pas faire ça.», «Il est important de ne pas oublier.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ne» + aucun / ni… ni / pas encore / jamais rien. «ne» goes before the pronouns and «pas» after the auxiliary. «ne pas» + infinitive."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: My doctor advised me not to eat too much meat.", "answer": "Mon docteur m'a conseillé de ne pas manger trop de viande.", "accept": ["mon docteur m'a conseillé de ne pas manger trop de viande", "mon docteur m'a conseillé de ne pas manger trop de viande.", "mon médecin m'a conseillé de ne pas manger trop de viande", "mon médecin m'a conseillé de ne pas manger trop de viande.", "mon docteur m'a conseille de ne pas manger trop de viande", "mon docteur m’a conseillé de ne pas manger trop de viande"], "hint": "«conseiller de» + «ne pas» + infinitive.", "right": "Superbe! «ne pas» stays together before the infinitive «manger».", "wrong": "With an infinitive, «ne pas» goes together in front of it: «Mon docteur m'a conseillé de ne pas manger trop de viande.»"}]}, "28-2": {"stageId": "28-2", "title": "Talking about snow and bad weather", "goal": "By the end you can understand and use key weather-report vocabulary about snow, ice and travel conditions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today we read a real-style weather report about snow. First, guess: what might «la neige» and «neigeux» mean?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«un épisode neigeux» means…", "choices": ["a snowy spell", "a TV episode about snow", "a snowman"], "answer": "a snowy spell", "right": "Yes! «neigeux» is the adjective from «la neige»: snowy. «un épisode» here means a spell or period.", "wrong": "«neigeux» = snowy (from «neige»), and «un épisode» in weather talk = a spell or period."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La neige fait des dégâts depuis plus de cinq jours.", "en": "The snow has been causing damage for more than five days.", "note": "«depuis» + present = 'has been …ing for'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'It has been snowing for two days'?", "choices": ["Il neige depuis deux jours.", "Il a neigé depuis deux jours.", "Il neige pendant deux jours."], "answer": "Il neige depuis deux jours.", "right": "Perfect! It's still happening → present + «depuis».", "wrong": "For an action that started in the past and is still going on, French uses the PRESENT + «depuis»: «Il neige depuis deux jours.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Key words: «les chutes de neige» (snowfall), «le verglas» (black ice), «les pluies verglaçantes» (freezing rain), «la couche de neige au sol» (the layer of snow on the ground)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You're driving and the road is covered in invisible ice. That's…", "choices": ["la grêle", "le verglas", "le brouillard"], "answer": "le verglas", "right": "Exactly! «le verglas» = black ice, very dangerous on the roads.", "wrong": "«le verglas» = black ice. «la grêle» = hail, and «le brouillard» = fog."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "France has a colour-coded alert system from Météo-France: «vigilance jaune, orange, rouge». «Trente départements ont été placés en alerte orange» = 30 areas were put on orange alert."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Cet épisode neigeux devrait se poursuivre jusqu'à la semaine prochaine.", "en": "This snowy spell should carry on until next week.", "note": "«devrait» = conditional of «devoir»: should/is expected to."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les températures restent négatives» means…", "choices": ["Temperatures stay below zero.", "People feel negative.", "Temperatures are going up."], "answer": "Temperatures stay below zero.", "right": "Yes! «négatives» = below zero (°C), and «rester» = to stay.", "wrong": "«températures négatives» = below 0 °C; «restent» = stay/remain. So temperatures stay below zero."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «la neige, neigeux, le verglas, les pluies verglaçantes, la couche de neige, les conditions de circulation» (traffic conditions)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it like a weather presenter: «Les conditions de circulation seront donc très difficiles sur l'ensemble du réseau.»", "fr": "Les conditions de circulation seront donc très difficiles sur l'ensemble du réseau."}]}, "28-3": {"stageId": "28-3", "title": "Le Canal du Midi: «ne… plus» and the passive", "goal": "By the end you can understand a short history text and use «ne… plus» and the passive («a été + participle»).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today's text is about the Canal du Midi in the south of France. It was built under Louis XIV and is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le Canal du Midi a été achevé sous le règne de Louis XIV.", "en": "The Canal du Midi was completed under the reign of Louis XIV.", "note": "«a été achevé» = was completed (passive)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The passive: «être» + past participle, and the participle agrees with the subject. «Le canal a été creusé», but «La voie navigable a été creusée»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'This waterway was dug by 12,000 workers.' («une voie navigable»)", "choices": ["Cette voie navigable a été creusé par 12 000 ouvriers.", "Cette voie navigable a été creusée par 12 000 ouvriers.", "Cette voie navigable a creusé par 12 000 ouvriers."], "answer": "Cette voie navigable a été creusée par 12 000 ouvriers.", "right": "Bravo! In the passive, the participle agrees with the feminine subject: «creusée».", "wrong": "Passive = «a été» + participle, agreeing with the subject. «voie» is feminine → «creusée». «par» introduces who did it."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«creuser avec des pelles et des pioches» = to dig with spades and pickaxes. «relier A à B» = to link A to B."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now for this world's grammar: «ne… plus» = no longer, not any more. «De nos jours, ce canal ne sert plus au transport de marchandises.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les chevaux ne sont plus utilisés pour tracter les bateaux.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["Horses are still used to pull boats.", "Horses are no longer used to pull boats.", "Horses were never used to pull boats."], "answer": "Horses are no longer used to pull boats.", "right": "Yes! «ne… plus» = no longer. And «sont utilisés» is a present passive.", "wrong": "«ne… plus» = no longer (they used to be, but not now). «ne… jamais» would mean never."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'This canal is no longer used to transport passengers.'", "choices": ["Ce canal ne sert pas encore au transport de passagers.", "Ce canal ne sert plus au transport de passagers.", "Ce canal ne sert aucun au transport de passagers."], "answer": "Ce canal ne sert plus au transport de passagers.", "right": "Perfect! «servir à» = to be used for; «ne… plus» = no longer.", "wrong": "'No longer' = «ne… plus»: «Ce canal ne sert plus au transport de passagers.» «pas encore» means not yet."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: passive = «être» + agreeing participle («a été achevé, a été creusée»). «ne… plus» = no longer. «de nos jours» = nowadays."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Nowadays, horses are no longer used.", "answer": "De nos jours, les chevaux ne sont plus utilisés.", "accept": ["de nos jours, les chevaux ne sont plus utilisés.", "de nos jours, les chevaux ne sont plus utilisés", "de nos jours les chevaux ne sont plus utilisés", "de nos jours, les chevaux ne sont plus utilises", "aujourd'hui, les chevaux ne sont plus utilisés.", "de nos jours, on n'utilise plus les chevaux."], "hint": "«De nos jours» + passive with «ne… plus» around «sont».", "right": "Excellent! «ne… plus» goes around «sont», and «utilisés» agrees with «chevaux».", "wrong": "«ne» and «plus» go around the conjugated verb «sont»: «De nos jours, les chevaux ne sont plus utilisés.»"}]}, "29-1": {"stageId": "29-1", "title": "Complex comparatives & superlatives", "goal": "By the end you can compare quantities with nouns, strengthen comparisons, and use «plus… plus», «de plus en plus» and noun + superlative structures.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "You can already say «plus grand que». Today: comparing QUANTITIES. With nouns, French adds «de»: «plus de…», «moins de…», «autant de…» + noun + «que»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai autant de livres que mon frère.", "en": "I have as many books as my brother."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I have fewer books than my brother.'", "choices": ["J'ai moins des livres que mon frère.", "J'ai moins de livres que mon frère.", "J'ai moins livres que mon frère."], "answer": "J'ai moins de livres que mon frère.", "right": "Yes! A quantity word + «de» (never «des»), then «que».", "wrong": "After quantity words like «plus, moins, autant», use «de», not «des»: «moins de livres que»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Superlative with a noun: the noun goes in the middle and the article is repeated. «Paul est le garçon le plus grand de la classe.» 'In' after a superlative = «de»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Paris is the most visited city in France.'", "choices": ["Paris est la plus visitée ville en France.", "Paris est la ville la plus visitée de France.", "Paris est la ville plus visitée de France."], "answer": "Paris est la ville la plus visitée de France.", "right": "Parfait! «la ville» + «la plus visitée», with «de» for 'in'.", "wrong": "The pattern is article + noun + article + «plus» + adjective, then «de»: «la ville la plus visitée de France»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To strengthen a comparison, add «bien», «beaucoup», «nettement», «encore» or «tellement» before «plus/moins». For example, «bien plus» = much/way more."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Charlotte a bien plus de livres que toi.", "en": "Charlotte has way more books than you."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The food is much better in this restaurant.'", "choices": ["La nourriture est bien plus bonne dans ce restaurant.", "La nourriture est bien meilleure dans ce restaurant.", "La nourriture est bien plus meilleure dans ce restaurant."], "answer": "La nourriture est bien meilleure dans ce restaurant.", "right": "Excellent! «bon» → «meilleur», never «plus bon». It agrees with «nourriture» → «meilleure».", "wrong": "«bon» has an irregular comparative, «meilleur» (never «plus bon» or «plus meilleur»). «nourriture» is feminine → «bien meilleure»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Same trap with «mauvais»: «pire» already means 'worse', so never say «plus pire». 'So much worse' = «tellement pire»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«tout» strengthens equality: «Il fait tout aussi froid qu'hier» (just as cold), «Elle a tout autant de talent que sa sœur» (just as much talent)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "'The more…, the more…' → «Plus…, plus…», with no article! «Plus je mange, plus j'ai faim.» You can mix them: «Plus elle travaille, moins elle a de temps.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The more he eats, the fatter he gets.'", "choices": ["Le plus il mange, le plus il grossit.", "Plus il mange, plus il grossit.", "Plus il mange, il grossit plus."], "answer": "Plus il mange, plus il grossit.", "right": "Bravo! No «le» in French, and «plus» starts both halves.", "wrong": "Unlike English 'the more', French drops the article: «Plus il mange, plus il grossit.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Gradual change: «de plus en plus» (more and more), «de moins en moins» (less and less), «de mieux en mieux» (better and better), «de mal en pis» (from bad to worse). With a noun, add «de»: «de plus en plus d'amis»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'He sings better and better.'", "choices": ["Il chante de plus en plus bien.", "Il chante de mieux en mieux.", "Il chante de meilleur en meilleur."], "answer": "Il chante de mieux en mieux.", "right": "Yes! «chanter» is a verb, so you need the adverb «mieux»: «de mieux en mieux».", "wrong": "With a verb, 'better' is the adverb «mieux» (not «plus bien» or «meilleur»): «Il chante de mieux en mieux.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «plus/moins/autant DE» + noun; noun + «le plus» + adjective + «de»; «bien meilleur», never «plus bon»; «Plus…, plus…»; «de plus en plus»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The children are just as tired as you. (use «toi»)", "answer": "Les enfants sont tout aussi fatigués que toi.", "accept": ["les enfants sont tout aussi fatigués que toi", "les enfants sont tout aussi fatigués que toi.", "les enfants sont tout aussi fatigues que toi"], "hint": "«tout aussi» + adjective + «que».", "right": "Superbe! «tout aussi» = just as, and «fatigués» agrees with «les enfants». «tout» stays unchanged here because it's an adverb.", "wrong": "'Just as' = «tout aussi»; the adjective agrees with «enfants»: «Les enfants sont tout aussi fatigués que toi.»"}]}, "29-2": {"stageId": "29-2", "title": "La 2 CV: past participles that agree", "goal": "By the end you can understand a short text about the 2 CV and choose the right past participle form (agreement with «être», «avoir» and in the passive).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Meet the Citroën 2 CV, nicknamed «la deuche» or «la deudeuche»: a cheap, simple car made from 1949 to 1990. Our text is full of past participles, so let's master their endings!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Fabriquée par les usines Citroën entre 1949 et 1990, cette voiture…", "en": "Built in Citroën's factories between 1949 and 1990, this car…", "note": "«Fabriquée» agrees with «cette voiture» (feminine)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 1: with «être», including the passive «a été + participle», the participle agrees with the subject. Rule 2: with «avoir», there's usually no agreement."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Cette voiture a été ___ au départ pour les classes populaires.» (concevoir)", "choices": ["conçu", "conçue", "conçues"], "answer": "conçue", "right": "Yes! Passive («a été»), so it agrees with «cette voiture», feminine singular → «conçue».", "wrong": "In the passive, the participle agrees with the subject. «voiture» is feminine singular → «conçue» (keep the «ç» before «u»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Au fil des années, son image a également ___.» (évoluer)", "choices": ["évolué", "évoluée", "évolués"], "answer": "évolué", "right": "Perfect! «avoir» + no direct object before it → no agreement.", "wrong": "With «avoir», the participle doesn't agree with the subject: «son image a évolué»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle est ___ dans les années 70 symbole de liberté.» (devenir)", "choices": ["devenu", "devenue", "devenues"], "answer": "devenue", "right": "Bravo! «devenir» takes «être», so it agrees with «elle» → «devenue».", "wrong": "«devenir» uses «être», so the participle agrees with the subject «elle»: «devenue»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«au fil des années» = as the years went by. «une place à part» = a special/unique place. «mythique» = legendary, iconic."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Plus de 5 millions de 2 CV ont été ___.» (produire)", "choices": ["produit", "produits", "produites"], "answer": "produites", "right": "Excellent! Passive + «2 CV» (= voitures, feminine plural) → «produites».", "wrong": "Passive («ont été») → agree with the subject. «2 CV» are cars («voitures»), feminine plural → «produites»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «être» and the passive → agree («fabriquée, conçue, devenue, produites»); «avoir» → no agreement («évolué»)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Answer in French: Pendant combien d'années la 2 CV a-t-elle été fabriquée ?", "answer": "Pendant 41 ans.", "accept": ["pendant 41 ans", "pendant 41 ans.", "41 ans", "pendant quarante et un ans", "pendant quarante-et-un ans", "quarante et un ans", "elle a été fabriquée pendant 41 ans.", "elle a été fabriquée pendant 41 ans"], "hint": "From 1949 to 1990…", "right": "Exactly! 1990 − 1949 = 41, so «pendant 41 ans».", "wrong": "From 1949 to 1990 is 41 years: «Pendant 41 ans.»"}]}, "30-1": {"stageId": "30-1", "title": "The subjunctive: when and how", "goal": "By the end you can recognise the main subjunctive triggers and form the present subjunctive of regular and key irregular verbs.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The «subjonctif» is the mood of wishes, feelings, necessity and doubt. It lives after «que», usually when the two clauses have different subjects."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je veux que tu viennes.", "en": "I want you to come.", "note": "Two subjects: «je» wants, «tu» comes."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess first: 'I want to come.' One subject only. Which is correct?", "choices": ["Je veux que je vienne.", "Je veux venir.", "Je veux que je viens."], "answer": "Je veux venir.", "right": "Yes! Same subject in both parts → infinitive, no «que» clause.", "wrong": "When the subject doesn't change, French uses the infinitive: «Je veux venir.» The subjunctive appears when someone else does the action: «Je veux que tu viennes.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Formation: take the «ils» form of the present, remove «-ent», then add «-e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent». «ils finissent» → «que je finisse», «ils prennent» → «que je prenne»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Prendre»: «ils prennent». So «Il faut que tu ___ le train.»", "choices": ["prends", "prennes", "preniez"], "answer": "prennes", "right": "Parfait! Stem «prenn-» + «-es» → «que tu prennes».", "wrong": "Start from «ils prennent» → stem «prenn-», then add «-es» for «tu»: «que tu prennes». «Preniez» is the «vous» form."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Nous» and «vous» look just like the imparfait: «que nous parlions», «que vous preniez». Only the context (after «que») tells you it's subjunctive."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Learn the big irregulars by heart: «être» → «que je sois, nous soyons»; «avoir» → «que j'aie, il ait»; «faire» → «que je fasse»; «aller» → «que j'aille, nous allions»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il faut que j'aille faire les courses car mes enfants veulent que je leur fasse un gâteau.", "en": "I have to go shopping because my children want me to make them a cake."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il est désolé que vous ne ___ pas rester.» (pouvoir)", "choices": ["pouvez", "puissiez", "pourriez"], "answer": "puissiez", "right": "Bravo! A feeling («être désolé que») triggers the subjunctive: «pouvoir» → «que vous puissiez».", "wrong": "«Être désolé que» expresses a feeling, so we need the subjunctive. «Pouvoir» is irregular: «que je puisse… que vous puissiez». Also: «vouloir» → «veuille», «savoir» → «sache»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Main triggers: necessity («il faut que, il est essentiel que»), feelings («être content que, avoir peur que»), wishes and orders («vouloir que, exiger que»), doubt («il se peut que, il n'est pas sûr que»), and conjunctions like «pour que, avant que, bien que, à condition que, sans que, à moins que»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which conjunction is followed by the subjunctive?", "choices": ["parce que", "bien que", "pendant que"], "answer": "bien que", "right": "Exactement! «Bien que» (although) is a concession, so subjunctive: «bien qu'il soit fatigué».", "wrong": "«Parce que» and «pendant que» introduce facts, so they take the indicative. «Bien que» (although) takes the subjunctive: «bien qu'il soit fatigué»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Classic trap: «espérer que» takes the INDICATIVE: «J'espère qu'il viendra.» Also, a few list items need fixing: it's «insister pour que» (not «insister que»), and «défendre que» means 'to forbid', not 'to defend'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How would you avoid the subjunctive in «Il est temps que nous partions»?", "choices": ["Il est temps de partir.", "Il est temps que partir.", "Il est temps à partir."], "answer": "Il est temps de partir.", "right": "Oui! «de» + infinitive is a lighter, more general version. It's very common in speech.", "wrong": "You can often replace «que» + subjunctive with «de» + infinitive: «Il est temps de partir.» Use it to sound natural, but you still need the subjunctive in lots of sentences."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: trigger + «que» + new subject → subjunctive. Regular stem from «ils», endings «-e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent», plus the irregulars «sois, aie, fasse, aille, puisse, veuille, sache»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It is necessary that you do your homework. (use «tu» and «il faut»)", "answer": "Il faut que tu fasses tes devoirs.", "accept": ["il faut que tu fasses tes devoirs", "il faut que tu fasses tes devoirs."], "hint": "«Faire» → «que tu fass…»", "right": "Excellent! «Il faut que» + «tu fasses»: the irregular subjunctive of «faire».", "wrong": "«Il faut que» always takes the subjunctive, and «faire» is irregular: «que tu fasses». So: «Il faut que tu fasses tes devoirs.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il est temps que je parte, car le supermarché va bientôt fermer.»", "fr": "Il est temps que je parte, car le supermarché va bientôt fermer."}]}, "30-2": {"stageId": "30-2", "title": "«Falloir» in five tenses", "goal": "By the end you can use «il faut» in the present, passé composé, imparfait, futur and conditionnel, with an infinitive, a noun or «que» + subjunctive.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Falloir» is impersonal: it only exists with «il». It can be followed by an infinitive, a noun, or «que» + subjunctive."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il faut du chocolat pour faire un gâteau au chocolat.", "en": "You need chocolate to make a chocolate cake."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess: what does a shop assistant mean by «Qu'est-ce qu'il vous faut ?»", "choices": ["What do you need?", "What must you do?", "What are you looking at?"], "answer": "What do you need?", "right": "Yes! «Il me/te/vous faut + noun» = I/you need something. Very common in shops.", "wrong": "With a noun and an indirect pronoun, «falloir» means 'to need': «Il me faut un stylo» = I need a pen. So «Qu'est-ce qu'il vous faut ?» = What do you need?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "The five forms: «il faut» (présent), «il a fallu» (passé composé), «il fallait» (imparfait), «il faudra» (futur), «il faudrait» (conditionnel). The «que» clause stays in the present subjunctive."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'You will have to eat before midday.'", "choices": ["Il faudra que tu manges avant midi.", "Il faudra que tu mangeras avant midi.", "Il fallait que tu manges avant midi."], "answer": "Il faudra que tu manges avant midi.", "right": "Parfait! The future is on «faudra»; the verb after «que» stays subjunctive: «tu manges».", "wrong": "Only «falloir» changes tense («il faudra»). After «que» you never use the future: it's always «que tu manges» (subjunctive)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'When I was little, I had to get up at 7 every morning.' (a habit)", "choices": ["il a fallu que je me lève", "il fallait que je me lève", "il faudra que je me lève"], "answer": "il fallait que je me lève", "right": "Exactement! A repeated past habit → imparfait: «il fallait».", "wrong": "Habits and background in the past take the imparfait: «il fallait». «Il a fallu» is for one specific occasion: «Hier, il a fallu que je parte tôt.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Il faudrait que…» is the polite, advisory version: 'you should…'. «Il faut que tu manges» sounds like an order; «Il faudrait que tu manges» sounds like friendly advice."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Il ne faut pas fumer ici» mean?", "choices": ["You don't have to smoke here.", "You must not smoke here.", "You can smoke here."], "answer": "You must not smoke here.", "right": "Bravo! «Il ne faut pas» = must not (prohibition).", "wrong": "Careful, false friend! «Il ne faut pas» = must not. For 'you don't have to', say «Tu n'es pas obligé(e) de…» or, in speech, «Pas besoin de…»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Falloir» and «devoir» often swap: «Je dois partir» = «Il faut que je parte». In speech, «il faut» often loses the «il»: «Faut que j'y aille !»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a fallu que tu manges avant midi.", "en": "You had to eat before midday.", "note": "One specific occasion → passé composé."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «faut, a fallu, fallait, faudra, faudrait» + infinitive, noun, or «que» + present subjunctive. «Il ne faut pas» = must not."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: We will have to leave. (use «il faudra que nous…»)", "answer": "Il faudra que nous partions.", "accept": ["il faudra que nous partions", "il faudra que nous partions."], "hint": "«partir» → «ils partent» → «que nous part…»", "right": "Excellent! «Il faudra» + subjunctive «que nous partions».", "wrong": "Future on «falloir» («il faudra»), subjunctive after «que»: «nous partions». So: «Il faudra que nous partions.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il faudrait que tu manges avant midi.»", "fr": "Il faudrait que tu manges avant midi."}]}, "30-3": {"stageId": "30-3", "title": "Spoken French: going to the cinema", "goal": "By the end you can understand and use casual French to suggest a film, ask what's on and set a time.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today: casual spoken French. Listen to how friends invite each other to the cinema, and how the words get squished together."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Salut chérie, ça te dit un ciné ce soir ?", "en": "Hi honey, do you fancy a movie tonight?", "note": "«ciné» = short for «cinéma»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ça te dit ?» is casual. How would you invite your boss politely?", "choices": ["Ça te dit un café ?", "Ça vous dirait un café ?", "T'es partant pour un café ?"], "answer": "Ça vous dirait un café ?", "right": "Très bien! «vous» + the conditional «dirait» makes it polite and soft.", "wrong": "With a boss, use «vous», and the conditional softens it: «Ça vous dirait un café ?» «Ça te dit» and «t'es partant» are for friends."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In speech, French drops sounds: «y'a quoi ?» = «Qu'est-ce qu'il y a ?», «t'es» = «tu es», «je n'savais pas» = «je ne savais pas»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the standard written form of «T'es partante ?»", "choices": ["Est-ce que tu es partante ?", "Tu as partante ?", "Est-ce que t'as parti ?"], "answer": "Est-ce que tu es partante ?", "right": "Yes! «t'es» = «tu es», and intonation alone makes it a question.", "wrong": "«T'es» is spoken «tu es» (not «tu as», which becomes «t'as»). Standard form: «Est-ce que tu es partante ?» = Are you up for it?"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Partant» is an adjective, so it agrees: a man is «partant», a woman «partante». «Je suis partante !» = I'm in!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Cinema words: «être à l'affiche» = to be showing (an «affiche» is a poster), «la séance» = the screening, «ça vient de sortir» = it has just come out."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je ne savais pas que c'était à l'affiche.» means…", "choices": ["I didn't know it was on.", "I didn't know there was a poster.", "I didn't know it was sold out."], "answer": "I didn't know it was on.", "right": "Exactly! «à l'affiche» = currently showing at the cinema.", "wrong": "«Être à l'affiche» is an expression: the film is showing now. It comes from the posters displayed outside cinemas."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Alors, on dit 19 h 30 devant le cinéma pour qu'on puisse acheter des glaces ?", "en": "So, let's say 7:30 in front of the cinema so we can buy ice cream?", "note": "«on dit» = let's say; «on» = «nous»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Why «pour qu'on puisse» and not «pour qu'on peut»?", "choices": ["«pour que» takes the subjunctive", "«on» always takes the subjunctive", "because it's a question"], "answer": "«pour que» takes the subjunctive", "right": "Bravo! «Pour que» (so that) expresses a purpose → subjunctive «puisse».", "wrong": "It's the conjunction «pour que» (so that) that triggers the subjunctive: «pouvoir» → «qu'on puisse»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«La séance est à 21 h.» What time is the film?", "choices": ["9 pm", "9 am", "11 pm"], "answer": "9 pm", "right": "Yes! France uses the 24-hour clock: 21 h = 9 pm, said «vingt et une heures».", "wrong": "Subtract 12: 21 h = 9 pm. Cinema times, trains and shops all use the 24-hour clock in France."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ça te dit… ?», «y'a quoi ?», «c'est à l'affiche», «t'es partant(e) ?», «on dit 20 h ?», «pour qu'on puisse…»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in casual French: Do you fancy a movie tonight?", "answer": "Ça te dit un ciné ce soir ?", "accept": ["ça te dit un ciné ce soir ?", "ça te dit un ciné ce soir", "ça te dit un cinéma ce soir ?", "ça te dit un cinéma ce soir", "ca te dit un cine ce soir"], "hint": "Starts with «Ça te…»", "right": "Super! Natural, friendly French.", "wrong": "The casual formula is «Ça te dit + noun ?»: «Ça te dit un ciné ce soir ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud (women say «partante»): «Ouais, je suis partant ! On dit 19 h 30 devant le cinéma ?»", "fr": "Ouais, je suis partant ! On dit 19 h 30 devant le cinéma ?"}]}, "31-1": {"stageId": "31-1", "title": "Subjunctive part 2: doubt, the past subjunctive and the «ne explétif»", "goal": "By the end you can use the subjunctive after negative or inverted opinion verbs, form the subjonctif passé, and recognise the «ne explétif».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Some verbs have two stems in the subjunctive: «je, tu, il, ils» come from the «ils» present, «nous» and «vous» from the «nous» present. «ils boivent» → «que je boive», but «nous buvons» → «que nous buvions»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Voir»: «ils voient», «nous voyons». «Il faut que nous ___ ce film.»", "choices": ["voyions", "voyons", "voient"], "answer": "voyions", "right": "Parfait! «nous» comes from «voyons» + «-ions» → «voyions». Same with «nettoyions»: the «yi» is written even if you barely hear it.", "wrong": "For «nous», take the «nous» stem «voy-» and add «-ions»: «que nous voyions». «Voyons» is the present indicative. Same pattern: «tenir» → «que je tienne» but «que nous tenions»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Opinion verbs like «croire, penser, trouver» take the indicative when affirmative (you're fairly sure). In the negative, or in an inverted question, doubt appears, so we switch to the subjunctive."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je crois qu'elle viendra. → Je ne crois pas qu'elle vienne.", "en": "I think she'll come. → I don't think she'll come.", "note": "There is no future subjunctive: «viendra» becomes «vienne»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je ne pense pas que ce ___ super.»", "choices": ["est", "soit", "sera"], "answer": "soit", "right": "Bravo! «ne pas penser que» expresses doubt → subjunctive «soit».", "wrong": "Once «penser» is negative, the subordinate clause goes into the subjunctive: «Je ne pense pas que ce soit super.» Affirmative: «Je pense que c'est super.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Register: subjunctive in questions is mostly written or formal, with inversion: «Penses-tu qu'il soit gentil ?» In everyday speech, people say «Tu penses qu'il est gentil ?» with the indicative."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You're chatting with a friend. Which question sounds most natural?", "choices": ["Tu trouves que c'est un bon film ?", "Trouves-tu que ce soit un bon film ?", "Trouves-tu que c'est soit un bon film ?"], "answer": "Tu trouves que c'est un bon film ?", "right": "Exactement! Intonation question + indicative is the natural spoken choice. The inverted version is correct, but sounds formal.", "wrong": "«Trouves-tu que ce soit…» is correct but formal or written. With a friend, say «Tu trouves que c'est un bon film ?» The third option mixes two verbs, which is never correct."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The «subjonctif passé» shows that the action happened BEFORE the main verb: «aie» or «sois» in the subjunctive + past participle. With «être», the participle agrees."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je doute que les enfants aient fini leurs devoirs.", "en": "I doubt that the children have finished their homework."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'She's happy that you (a woman) went to the cinema with her.'", "choices": ["Elle est contente que tu sois allée au cinéma avec elle.", "Elle est contente que tu aies allé au cinéma avec elle.", "Elle est contente que tu es allée au cinéma avec elle."], "answer": "Elle est contente que tu sois allée au cinéma avec elle.", "right": "Très bien! «aller» uses «être» → «que tu sois allée», with feminine agreement.", "wrong": "«Aller» takes «être», «être content que» needs the subjunctive, and the participle agrees: «que tu sois allée»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The «ne explétif» is a «ne» with NO negative meaning. It's formal and optional, and appears after «craindre que, avoir peur que, éviter que, avant que, à moins que» and after comparisons like «plus… que»: «Il y avait plus de monde qu'on ne le pensait.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Je crains qu'il ne soit trop tard» mean?", "choices": ["I'm afraid it's too late.", "I'm afraid it isn't too late.", "I hope it isn't too late."], "answer": "I'm afraid it's too late.", "right": "Yes! No «pas», so this «ne» isn't a negation; it just sounds elegant.", "wrong": "Without «pas», «ne» here is explétif: it adds no negation. «Je crains qu'il ne soit trop tard» = I fear it IS too late. A real negation needs «pas»: «Je crains qu'il n'ait pas le temps» = I fear he doesn't have time."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: negative or inverted opinion → subjunctive; past subjunctive = «aie/sois» + participle; «ne explétif» = a formal «ne» with no «pas» and no negative meaning."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I don't think he is kind. (use «penser»)", "answer": "Je ne pense pas qu'il soit gentil.", "accept": ["je ne pense pas qu'il soit gentil", "je ne pense pas qu il soit gentil", "je ne pense pas qu'il soit gentil."], "hint": "Negative «penser» → subjunctive of «être».", "right": "Excellent! Negative opinion → «qu'il soit».", "wrong": "«Je ne pense pas que» triggers the subjunctive: «être» → «qu'il soit». So: «Je ne pense pas qu'il soit gentil.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il est peu probable que Tom soit allé voir ce film.»", "fr": "Il est peu probable que Tom soit allé voir ce film."}]}, "31-2": {"stageId": "31-2", "title": "25 French slang words", "goal": "By the end you can understand everyday French slang words and know when (not) to use them.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«L'argot» (slang) is everywhere in casual French: films, songs, friends. Understanding it is essential; using it depends on who you're talking to."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess first: «Mon pote a une nouvelle bagnole.»", "choices": ["My friend has a new car.", "My dad has a new bike.", "My friend has new shoes."], "answer": "My friend has a new car.", "right": "Bien vu! «un pote» = «un ami», «une bagnole» = «une voiture».", "wrong": "«Pote» is slang for «ami» and «bagnole» for «voiture»: My friend has a new car."}, {"t": "say", "text": "People, money and work: «un pote» (friend), «un gamin, une gamine» (kid), «un flic» (cop), «le fric» (money), «le boulot» (work)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai plus de fric, faut que je trouve un boulot.", "en": "I've got no money left, I need to find a job.", "note": "Typical speech: «j'ai plus» = «je n'ai plus», and «faut» = «il faut»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the standard word for «le boulot»?", "choices": ["le travail", "le bureau", "le patron"], "answer": "le travail", "right": "Oui! «le boulot» = «le travail». It's so common that it's almost neutral.", "wrong": "«Le boulot» = «le travail» (work, job). «Métro, boulot, dodo» = commute, work, sleep: the daily grind."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Home and clothes: «la baraque» (house), «la piaule» (bedroom), «le pieu» (bed), «les fringues» (clothes), «les godasses» (shoes), «le froc» (trousers)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Bon, je vais au pieu.» What is he doing?", "choices": ["Going to bed", "Going to work", "Going home"], "answer": "Going to bed", "right": "Exactly! «le pieu» = «le lit».", "wrong": "«Le pieu» is slang for «le lit» (bed). Home is «la baraque», work is «le boulot»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch the gender: «la chose» → «le machin», «le parapluie» → «le pépin», «l'eau» → «la flotte». And «un pépin» also means a pip or a small problem: «J'ai eu un pépin.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il tombe de la flotte, prends ton pépin !»", "choices": ["It's raining, take your umbrella!", "The water is falling, take your pip!", "It's snowing, take your coat!"], "answer": "It's raining, take your umbrella!", "right": "Bravo! «la flotte» = water/rain, «le pépin» = umbrella.", "wrong": "«La flotte» = «l'eau» (here: rain) and «le pépin» = «le parapluie». So: It's raining, take your umbrella!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "More: «la clope» (cigarette), «le pinard» (wine), «la bouffe» (food), «le bouquin» (book), «le frigo» (fridge), «le pif» (nose), «le bide» (belly), «les tifs» (hair), «la taule» (prison), «la bécane» (bike)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word could you safely use with almost anyone, even your boss?", "choices": ["le frigo", "le fric", "la taule"], "answer": "le frigo", "right": "Yes! «le frigo» is so common it's practically standard. «Bouquin» and «boulot» are also mild.", "wrong": "Slang has levels. «Le frigo» is used by everyone, everywhere. «Le fric» and «la taule» are clearly familiar: keep them for friends."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Le bide» also means a flop: «Le film a fait un bide.» And while «flic» is common, don't use it to a police officer's face!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «pote, fric, boulot, bagnole, baraque, pieu, fringues, godasses, bouffe, flotte, pépin, bouquin». Understand them all; use them with friends."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Say it in slang: My friend lost his money. (use «pote» and «fric»)", "answer": "Mon pote a perdu son fric.", "accept": ["mon pote a perdu son fric", "mon pote a perdu son fric."], "hint": "«Mon pote a perdu…»", "right": "Parfait! Very natural, very familiar.", "wrong": "«ami» → «pote», «argent» → «fric»: «Mon pote a perdu son fric.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je prends ma bagnole, mes fringues et mes godasses, et j'arrive !»", "fr": "Je prends ma bagnole, mes fringues et mes godasses, et j'arrive !"}]}, "32-1": {"stageId": "32-1", "title": "Compound prepositions, conjunctions and the past infinitive", "goal": "By the end you can use compound prepositions like «compte tenu de» or «au sein de», choose the right mood after conjunctions, and use «après avoir/être» + participle.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "A «locution prépositionnelle» is several words working as one preposition, often ending in «de»: «à côté de, autour de, loin de, au sein de»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess first: «Je travaille au sein du parlement.»", "choices": ["I work within parliament.", "I work next to parliament.", "I work against parliament."], "answer": "I work within parliament.", "right": "Bien vu! «au sein de» = within, inside an organisation. Quite formal: you'll hear it in the news.", "wrong": "«Au sein de» = within (a group or institution): «au sein de l'entreprise», «au sein de la famille». 'Next to' is «à côté de»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The final «de» contracts as usual: «en haut des escaliers», «à partir du mois prochain», «à la place d'Eric», «au sujet des vacances»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle est en haut ___ escaliers.»", "choices": ["de les", "des", "du"], "answer": "des", "right": "Parfait! «de + les» → «des».", "wrong": "«de + les» always contracts to «des», and «de + le» to «du». «Les escaliers» is plural, so: «en haut des escaliers»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For cause and circumstance, formal French loves: «compte tenu de» (given), «étant donné» (given), «faute de» (for lack of), «grâce à» (thanks to), «malgré / en dépit de» (despite), «en fonction de» (depending on)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'For lack of time, we couldn't see this exhibition.'", "choices": ["Faute de temps, nous n'avons pas pu voir cette exposition.", "Grâce au temps, nous n'avons pas pu voir cette exposition.", "Malgré le temps, nous n'avons pas pu voir cette exposition."], "answer": "Faute de temps, nous n'avons pas pu voir cette exposition.", "right": "Exactement! «faute de» + noun with no article = for lack of.", "wrong": "'For lack of' = «faute de» + noun, with no article: «faute de temps», «faute d'argent». «Grâce à» is for positive causes, and «malgré» means 'despite'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Grâce à» is for good outcomes («grâce à toi»); for bad ones, use «à cause de» («à cause de la pluie»). Two fixes to the list: spell «par-dessus / par-dessous» with a hyphen, and «auprès de» means 'with / to' an authority or 'close to', not 'besides'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Preposition + noun, conjunction + verb: «depuis ton retour» / «depuis que tu es revenu». Moods: «depuis que» and «après que» take the indicative; «pour que» and «bien que» take the subjunctive."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Après qu'il ___ ses devoirs, il ira au cinéma.»", "choices": ["a fini", "ait fini", "finisse"], "answer": "a fini", "right": "Bravo! «Après que» introduces a fact that has happened, so it takes the indicative. Many French people wrongly use the subjunctive here!", "wrong": "«Après que» takes the indicative (the action really happened): «après qu'il a fini». Don't confuse it with «avant que», which takes the subjunctive."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With the same subject, use the past infinitive: «après avoir mangé», «après être allé(e)(s)», «pour avoir aidé» (= for having helped). With «être», the participle agrees with the subject."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The children got dressed after washing.'", "choices": ["Les enfants se sont habillés après s'être lavés.", "Les enfants se sont habillés après avoir lavé.", "Les enfants se sont habillés après se laver."], "answer": "Les enfants se sont habillés après s'être lavés.", "right": "Très bien! Reflexive verb → «s'être» + participle that agrees: «lavés».", "wrong": "«Après» needs a PAST infinitive. «Se laver» is reflexive, so it takes «être»: «après s'être lavés» (plural agreement with «les enfants»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'We were punished for arriving late.'", "choices": ["Nous avons été punis pour être arrivés en retard.", "Nous avons été punis pour arriver en retard.", "Nous avons été punis pour avoir arrivé en retard."], "answer": "Nous avons été punis pour être arrivés en retard.", "right": "Oui! «pour» + past infinitive gives the reason; «arriver» takes «être», so «arrivés».", "wrong": "For a past reason, use «pour» + past infinitive. «Arriver» takes «être»: «pour être arrivés». «Pour arriver» would mean 'in order to arrive'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Après avoir dîné, nous irons visiter soit le jardin botanique, soit le musée du Louvre.", "en": "After having dinner, we'll visit either the botanical garden or the Louvre.", "note": "«soit… soit» = either… or."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: compound prepositions end in «de» (watch «du/des»); «après que» + indicative, «pour que/bien que» + subjunctive; «après avoir/être» + participle when the subject is the same."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I will come to see you after finishing my work. (use «tu»)", "answer": "Je viendrai te voir après avoir fini mon travail.", "accept": ["je viendrai te voir après avoir fini mon travail", "je viendrai te voir après avoir fini mon travail.", "je viendrai te voir apres avoir fini mon travail"], "hint": "«…après avoir + participle»", "right": "Excellent! «après avoir fini»: same subject, past infinitive.", "wrong": "After «après», use the past infinitive: «avoir fini». So: «Je viendrai te voir après avoir fini mon travail.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Compte tenu de la pluie, le match est annulé.»", "fr": "Compte tenu de la pluie, le match est annulé."}]}, "32-2": {"stageId": "32-2", "title": "Real conversation: Camille interviews Lo", "goal": "By the end you can follow a spontaneous French interview and recognise typical features of spoken French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "This is real, unscripted French between two sisters. Notice what native speakers drop, add and repeat when they speak."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On a combien d'années de différence ?", "en": "How many years apart are we?", "note": "Spoken style: «on» = «nous», and the question word sits in the middle."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai 25 ans, toi, tu en as 53.» What does «en» replace?", "choices": ["ans", "sœurs", "la différence"], "answer": "ans", "right": "Exactement! With ages and quantities, French keeps «en»: «Tu en as 53» = you have 53 (of them).", "wrong": "When you drop the noun after a number, «en» replaces it: «tu en as 53» = «tu as 53 ans». You can't just say «tu as 53»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In speech the «ne» disappears: «C'est pas vrai !», «J'ai pas du tout 53 ans». In writing you need it: «Ce n'est pas vrai», «Je n'ai pas du tout 53 ans»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the written version of «Coiffeuse, c'est pas un métier qui t'a plu ?»", "choices": ["Coiffeuse, ce n'est pas un métier qui t'a plu ?", "Coiffeuse, c'est ne pas un métier qui t'a plu ?", "Coiffeuse, ce pas n'est un métier qui t'a plu ?"], "answer": "Coiffeuse, ce n'est pas un métier qui t'a plu ?", "right": "Bravo! «ne» goes before the verb, «pas» after it: «ce n'est pas».", "wrong": "Restore the «ne» before the verb and keep «pas» after: «ce n'est pas». «C'» becomes «ce» before «n'»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Useful bits: «la fille aînée» (the eldest daughter), «c'est chouette» (it's lovely, familiar), «Quel métier peu commun !» (what an unusual job!), «au fait» (by the way)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai pas du tout 53 ans au fait.» What does «au fait» mean?", "choices": ["by the way", "actually", "at the top"], "answer": "by the way", "right": "Oui! «au fait» = by the way. Pronounce the final «t»: «o-fett».", "wrong": "«Au fait» = by the way. Don't confuse it with «en fait» = actually / in fact."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Moi, je voulais être chanteuse et je suis devenue coiffeuse.", "en": "I wanted to be a singer and I became a hairdresser.", "note": "Imparfait for the wish (background), passé composé for what happened."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'She wanted to be a doctor and she became a teacher.'", "choices": ["Elle voulait être médecin et elle est devenue professeure.", "Elle a voulu être médecin et elle devenait professeure.", "Elle voulait être médecin et elle a devenu professeure."], "answer": "Elle voulait être médecin et elle est devenue professeure.", "right": "Parfait! «voulait» (ongoing wish) + «est devenue» (event, with «être» and agreement).", "wrong": "The wish is background → imparfait «voulait». Becoming is an event → passé composé, and «devenir» takes «être»: «elle est devenue»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "After «être», jobs take no article: «Je suis chanteuse», not «Je suis une chanteuse». Feminine forms: «chanteur → chanteuse», «coiffeur → coiffeuse»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Small grammar points in the transcript: «avant que nous commencions» (subjunctive after «avant que»), «ces yeux bleu-vert» (compound colours never agree). Also, languages take a lowercase letter: «en français», not «en Français» as written in the transcript."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ne» drops in speech, «en» replaces numbers of things, imparfait for wishes vs passé composé for events, and «au fait» ≠ «en fait»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "A woman says: 'I wanted to be a singer.'", "answer": "Je voulais être chanteuse.", "accept": ["je voulais être chanteuse", "je voulais être chanteuse.", "je voulais etre chanteuse", "je voulais être chanteur", "je voulais être chanteur.", "je voulais etre chanteur"], "hint": "Imparfait of «vouloir», no article before the job.", "right": "Excellent! Imparfait «voulais» and no article before «chanteuse».", "wrong": "Wish in the past → «je voulais», then job with no article: «Je voulais être chanteuse» (a man: «chanteur»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud (men say «chanteur… coiffeur»): «Moi, je voulais être chanteuse et je suis devenue coiffeuse.»", "fr": "Moi, je voulais être chanteuse et je suis devenue coiffeuse."}]}, "32-3": {"stageId": "32-3", "title": "Reading Maupassant: «Le Horla»", "goal": "By the end you can read the opening of «Le Horla», understand its literary vocabulary, and notice the pronoun «y» and literary inversion.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Le Horla» (1887) by Guy de Maupassant is a short story written as a diary. The first entry is calm and full of love for the countryside, before the horror begins."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quelle journée admirable !", "en": "What a wonderful day!", "note": "«Quel(le) + noun» makes an exclamation."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess first: «J'ai passé toute la matinée étendu sur l'herbe.»", "choices": ["lying stretched out on the grass", "standing on the grass", "reading on the grass"], "answer": "lying stretched out on the grass", "right": "Bien vu! «étendu» = lying stretched out (from «s'étendre»). It agrees with the narrator, a man.", "wrong": "«Étendu» comes from «s'étendre» = to stretch out or lie down. So he spent the morning lying on the grass. (The vocabulary list's 'laid down' isn't quite right: 'lying' is better.)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Maupassant builds rhythm with three verbs: «l'énorme platane qui la couvre, l'abrite et l'ombrage tout entière» = the huge plane tree that covers, shelters and shades it entirely."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «qui la couvre, l'abrite et l'ombrage», what does «la» refer to?", "choices": ["la maison", "l'herbe", "la matinée"], "answer": "la maison", "right": "Exactement! The tree covers the house («devant ma maison»). That's also why «tout entière» is feminine.", "wrong": "The object pronoun «la» refers back to the feminine noun just before: «ma maison». The tree covers, shelters and shades the whole house."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Y» replaces a place introduced by «à» or «dans»: «J'aime ce pays, et j'aime y vivre» = I love living there. «J'y ai mes racines» = I have my roots there."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Where does «y» go? 'I love living there.'", "choices": ["J'aime y vivre.", "J'y aime vivre.", "J'aime vivre y."], "answer": "J'aime y vivre.", "right": "Parfait! «y» belongs to the verb it completes, here «vivre», so it goes right before the infinitive.", "wrong": "The pronoun goes before the verb it relates to. You live THERE, so «y» goes before «vivre»: «J'aime y vivre»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Literary inversion: «la terre où sont nés et morts ses aïeux» = «où ses aïeux sont nés et morts». After «où», writers often put a long subject at the end for rhythm."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «ses aïeux» mean?", "choices": ["his ancestors", "his eyes", "his friends"], "answer": "his ancestors", "right": "Oui! «les aïeux» = ancestors (literary). The land where his ancestors were born and died.", "wrong": "«Aïeux» = ancestors, a literary word (everyday: «les ancêtres»). Don't confuse it with «yeux» (eyes)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More vocabulary: «les racines» (roots, here figurative), «les usages» (customs), «les locutions locales» (local expressions), «les paysans» (country people), «le sol» (the soil)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In this text, «les usages» means…", "choices": ["customs", "uses of tools", "users"], "answer": "customs", "right": "Bravo! «les usages» = customs, traditions. The list's 'habits' is too personal: customs belong to a community.", "wrong": "Here «les usages» = local customs and ways of doing things, alongside food, expressions and accents. 'Users' would be «les usagers»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «étendu» = lying, «la» = the house, «y» = there (before the infinitive), literary inversion after «où», and «aïeux» = ancestors."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I love this country and I love living there.", "answer": "J'aime ce pays et j'aime y vivre.", "accept": ["j'aime ce pays et j'aime y vivre", "j'aime ce pays, et j'aime y vivre", "j'aime ce pays, et j'aime y vivre.", "j'aime ce pays et j'aime y vivre."], "hint": "Put «y» before «vivre».", "right": "Magnifique! Just like Maupassant.", "wrong": "«Y» goes before the infinitive it belongs to: «J'aime ce pays et j'aime y vivre.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Read it aloud slowly, like a storyteller: «J'aime ce pays, et j'aime y vivre parce que j'y ai mes racines.»", "fr": "J'aime ce pays, et j'aime y vivre parce que j'y ai mes racines."}]}, "33-1": {"stageId": "33-1", "title": "Complex relative pronouns: «lequel», «auquel», «duquel», «ce à quoi»", "goal": "By the end you can choose the right complex relative pronoun after a preposition and use «ce + preposition + quoi» to refer to a whole idea.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "After a preposition («sur, avec, dans, pour»), French uses «lequel, laquelle, lesquels, lesquelles» for things. They agree with the noun they refer to."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le fauteuil sur lequel tu es assis appartenait à ma grand-mère.", "en": "The armchair you're sitting on used to belong to my grandmother.", "note": "French never ends a clause with a preposition: «sur lequel», not «…assis sur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«L'agence dans ___ il travaille est très connue.»", "choices": ["lequel", "laquelle", "lesquelles"], "answer": "laquelle", "right": "Parfait! «l'agence» is feminine singular → «laquelle».", "wrong": "Look at the noun: «une agence» is feminine singular, so «dans laquelle». «Lequel» is masculine, «lesquelles» plural."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "For people, «qui» is more natural: «les gens avec qui je fais du yoga». For places, «où» often replaces it: «le fauteuil où tu es assis» (easier, slightly less precise)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With «à», the pronoun contracts: «auquel, auxquels, auxquelles», but «à laquelle» stays separate. Watch for verbs built with «à»: «penser à, tenir à, s'intéresser à, faire face à»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ce sont des photos ___ Tom tient beaucoup.» («tenir à» = to be attached to)", "choices": ["auxquelles", "auxquels", "desquelles"], "answer": "auxquelles", "right": "Bravo! «tenir à» + «les photos» (feminine plural) → «auxquelles».", "wrong": "The verb is «tenir À», and «photos» is feminine plural: «à + lesquelles» → «auxquelles». «Desquelles» would need «de»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With compound prepositions ending in «de» («en face de, près de, à la fin de, à propos de»), use «duquel, desquels, desquelles» or «de laquelle»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le théâtre en face ___ nous habitons va être rénové.»", "choices": ["duquel", "dont", "auquel"], "answer": "duquel", "right": "Exactement! «en face de» + «le théâtre» (masculine singular) → «en face duquel».", "wrong": "After a compound preposition like «en face de», «dont» is impossible; use «duquel» («de + lequel»). «Auquel» would need «à»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Lyon est la ville non loin de laquelle il est né.", "en": "Lyon is the town not far from where he was born."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The same forms ask 'which one?'. «Tu parles de cette jupe ?» → '…which one are you talking about?'", "choices": ["De laquelle parles-tu ?", "Laquelle parles-tu ?", "Duquel parles-tu ?"], "answer": "De laquelle parles-tu ?", "right": "Oui! «parler de» + «la jupe» (feminine) → «De laquelle…?» The preposition starts the question.", "wrong": "«Parler» needs «de», and «jupe» is feminine: «De laquelle parles-tu ?» «Duquel» would be masculine: «Tu parles de ce livre ? – Duquel ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "To refer to a whole idea (not a noun), use «ce + preposition + quoi»: «ce à quoi, ce pour quoi, ce contre quoi, ce sur quoi»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I hate inequality. It's what I've been fighting against for a long time.'", "choices": ["C'est ce contre quoi je lutte depuis longtemps.", "C'est contre lequel je lutte depuis longtemps.", "C'est ce contre que je lutte depuis longtemps."], "answer": "C'est ce contre quoi je lutte depuis longtemps.", "right": "Très bien! «ce contre quoi» refers to the whole idea, not to one noun.", "wrong": "When «what» refers to an idea, use «ce» + preposition + «quoi»: «ce contre quoi je lutte». «Lequel» needs a specific noun, and «que» can't follow a preposition."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Write «ce pour quoi» in three words: «C'est ce pour quoi je suis connue» = that's what I'm known for. «Pourquoi» in one word just means 'why'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: preposition + «lequel» (agreeing); «à» → «auquel/auxquel(le)s/à laquelle»; «de» after compound prepositions → «duquel/desquel(le)s/de laquelle»; ideas → «ce… quoi»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It's a house I'm very fond of. («tenir à»)", "answer": "C'est une maison à laquelle je tiens beaucoup.", "accept": ["c'est une maison à laquelle je tiens beaucoup", "c'est une maison à laquelle je tiens beaucoup.", "c est une maison à laquelle je tiens beaucoup", "c'est une maison a laquelle je tiens beaucoup"], "hint": "«tenir à» + feminine singular noun", "right": "Excellent! «tenir à» + «maison» → «à laquelle».", "wrong": "«Tenir à» + «une maison» (feminine singular) → «à laquelle»: «C'est une maison à laquelle je tiens beaucoup.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le théâtre en face duquel nous habitons va être rénové.»", "fr": "Le théâtre en face duquel nous habitons va être rénové."}]}, "33-2": {"stageId": "33-2", "title": "Reading: a stay in Montreal", "goal": "By the end you can understand a travel account comparing France and Montreal, including comparisons, «celles» and «la plupart des».", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Today's text is a traveller's account comparing France and Montreal. Watch for comparisons, demonstrative pronouns and a relative pronoun you know: «avec lequel»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Montréal est une très grande ville où on voit pas mal de grandes surfaces et de boutiques de mode.", "en": "Montreal is a very big city where you see quite a lot of big stores and fashion boutiques.", "note": "«on» = people in general; «grandes surfaces» = big supermarkets and stores."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess: «pas mal de» means…", "choices": ["quite a lot of", "not many", "not bad"], "answer": "quite a lot of", "right": "Bien vu! «pas mal de» + noun = quite a lot of. Familiar but very common, and NOT a negation.", "wrong": "Despite «pas», «pas mal de» + noun means 'quite a lot of': «pas mal de monde» = quite a lot of people. On its own, «pas mal !» = not bad!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Comparison: «plus que dans les grandes villes françaises mais beaucoup moins que dans celles des États-Unis». «Celles» avoids repeating «les villes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "According to the text, where are there the most fashion boutiques?", "choices": ["in the United States", "in Montreal", "in France"], "answer": "in the United States", "right": "Exactement! Montreal has more than France, but «beaucoup moins» than US cities.", "wrong": "Read the order: more than French cities, «mais beaucoup moins que» (far fewer than) US cities. So the United States has the most."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «celles des États-Unis», what does «celles» replace?", "choices": ["les grandes villes", "les boutiques", "les températures"], "answer": "les grandes villes", "right": "Oui! «celles» (feminine plural) = «les villes»: the cities of the United States.", "wrong": "«Celles» is a demonstrative pronoun that repeats the last comparable noun: «les grandes villes». «Celui, celle, ceux, celles» agree with that noun."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Winter: «Les températures baissent jusqu'à moins 30 degrés. Cependant, malgré le temps, il reste possible de vivre sans sortir dans la neige.» «Il reste possible de» = it's still possible to."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La plupart des immeubles ont accès direct au réseau du métro, avec lequel on peut arriver vite aux entrées souterraines des grands magasins.", "en": "Most buildings have direct access to the metro network, which lets you get quickly to the underground entrances of the department stores.", "note": "«avec lequel» = with which (the network)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«La plupart des immeubles ___ accès direct au métro.»", "choices": ["a", "ont", "avons"], "answer": "ont", "right": "Bravo! «la plupart des» + plural noun takes a plural verb: «ont».", "wrong": "Even though «la plupart» looks singular, the verb agrees with the plural noun: «La plupart des immeubles ont…»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Culture: Montreal's «ville souterraine» (underground city) links metro stations, shops and offices through kilometres of tunnels. Quebec French says «magasiner» for 'to go shopping'. Also, capital letters keep their accents: «États-Unis»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Why can people in Montreal shop all winter without going outside?", "choices": ["Buildings connect directly to the underground metro and shops.", "The shops deliver everything.", "The winter is actually mild."], "answer": "Buildings connect directly to the underground metro and shops.", "right": "Exactly! Direct metro access leads to underground shop entrances.", "wrong": "The text says most buildings have «accès direct au réseau du métro», which leads to «entrées souterraines des grands magasins». The winter is anything but mild: «moins 30 degrés»!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «pas mal de» = quite a lot of, «celles» = those (feminine plural), «la plupart des» + plural verb, «malgré», «il reste possible de», «avec lequel»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Most buildings have direct access to the metro.", "answer": "La plupart des immeubles ont accès direct au métro.", "accept": ["la plupart des immeubles ont accès direct au métro", "la plupart des immeubles ont accès direct au métro.", "la plupart des immeubles ont un accès direct au métro", "la plupart des immeubles ont un accès direct au métro.", "la plupart des immeubles ont acces direct au metro"], "hint": "«La plupart des…» + plural verb", "right": "Parfait! Plural verb after «la plupart des».", "wrong": "«La plupart des immeubles» takes a plural verb: «La plupart des immeubles ont accès direct au métro.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Malgré le temps, il reste possible de vivre sans sortir dans la neige.»", "fr": "Malgré le temps, il reste possible de vivre sans sortir dans la neige."}]}, "33-3": {"stageId": "33-3", "title": "Reading Gaston Leroux: «Le Fantôme de l'Opéra»", "goal": "By the end you can read a gothic literary portrait and understand «être d'une + noun», «si… que», «ne… point» and literary similes.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Le Fantôme de l'Opéra» (1910) by Gaston Leroux describes the Phantom in a terrifying portrait. Literary French loves nouns, comparisons and old negations."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est d'une prodigieuse maigreur.", "en": "He is extraordinarily thin.", "note": "«être d'une + adjective + noun» = an elegant way to say «il est prodigieusement maigre»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Guess: «maigreur» comes from which adjective?", "choices": ["maigre (thin)", "magique (magic)", "majeur (major)"], "answer": "maigre (thin)", "right": "Bien vu! «maigre» + «-eur» → «la maigreur» (thinness), like «grand» → «la grandeur».", "wrong": "Many feminine nouns are made with «-eur» from adjectives: «maigre» → «la maigreur», «grand» → «la grandeur». So: thinness."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Son habit noir flotte sur une charpente squelettique.» Here «un habit» is a formal black suit (a tailcoat), and «une charpente» (a roof frame) is a metaphor for his bony body."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Consequence: «si + adjective + que». «Ses yeux sont si profonds qu'on ne distingue pas bien les prunelles» = his eyes are so deep that you can't really see the pupils."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Son nez est ___ peu de chose qu'il est invisible de profil.»", "choices": ["si", "tant de", "tellement de"], "answer": "si", "right": "Parfait! «si + adjective or adverb + que» expresses a consequence: so small that…", "wrong": "Before an adjective or «peu», use «si» (or «tellement») without «de»: «si peu de chose que…». «Tant de / tellement de» go before a noun: «tant de livres»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Ne… point» is a literary, stronger «ne… pas»: «Sa peau n'est point blanche, mais vilainement jaune» = his skin is not white at all, but an ugly yellow."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the modern equivalent of «n'est point blanche»?", "choices": ["n'est pas blanche", "n'est plus blanche", "est un peu blanche"], "answer": "n'est pas blanche", "right": "Oui! «ne… point» = «ne… pas» (with extra emphasis). You'll find it in classic literature, almost never in speech.", "wrong": "«Ne… point» is simply an old, literary «ne… pas»: «n'est point blanche» = isn't white (at all). «Ne… plus» means 'no longer'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Leroux uses similes with «comme»: «comme une peau de tambour» (like a drum skin), «comme aux crânes des morts». And «vilain» means ugly, but also 'naughty' for children: «Vilain garçon !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Trois ou quatre longues mèches brunes font office de chevelure.» What does «font office de» mean?", "choices": ["serve as", "fall off", "grow into"], "answer": "serve as", "right": "Exactement! «faire office de» = to serve as: three or four locks of hair are all he has for a head of hair.", "wrong": "«Faire office de» = to act as, serve as. Three or four long locks («mèches») are his only «chevelure» (head of hair)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More words: «les prunelles» (pupils), «l'ossature» (bone structure), «une mèche» (lock of hair), «la chevelure» (head of hair). «Tenir à quelque chose comme à la prunelle de ses yeux» = to treasure something."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «être d'une prodigieuse maigreur», «si… que» for consequences, «ne… point» = literary «ne… pas», «comme» similes, «faire office de» = to serve as."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in literary style, with «ne… point»: His skin is not white.", "answer": "Sa peau n'est point blanche.", "accept": ["sa peau n'est point blanche", "sa peau n'est point blanche.", "sa peau n est point blanche"], "hint": "«Sa peau n'est…»", "right": "Magnifique! Very Leroux.", "wrong": "Replace «pas» with «point»: «Sa peau n'est point blanche.» Remember «peau» is feminine: «sa peau», «blanche»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Read it with a spooky voice: «Ses yeux sont si profonds qu'on ne distingue pas bien les prunelles immobiles.»", "fr": "Ses yeux sont si profonds qu'on ne distingue pas bien les prunelles immobiles."}]}, "34-1": {"stageId": "34-1", "title": "Plus-que-parfait: the past before the past", "goal": "Form the plus-que-parfait with avoir or être and use it for an action that happened before another past action.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Imagine two past moments: you phoned, and your friends had already left. The earlier action needs its own tense: the «plus-que-parfait» — English 'had done'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ils étaient déjà partis quand tu as téléphoné.", "en": "They had already left when you phoned.", "note": "Leaving happened first → plus-que-parfait. Phoning → passé composé."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The recipe is simple: imparfait of «avoir» or «être» + past participle. «j'avais mangé», «tu avais fini», «nous avions vu»… and with être verbs: «j'étais parti», «elle était arrivée», «ils étaient venus»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Good news: you choose the auxiliary exactly as in the passé composé. If it's «je suis allé», it becomes «j'étais allé». If it's «j'ai pris», it becomes «j'avais pris». Same agreement rules too."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'When I arrived, she had already left.' Quand je suis arrivé, elle ___.", "choices": ["est déjà partie", "était déjà partie", "avait déjà partie"], "answer": "était déjà partie", "right": "Exactly! «partir» takes être, so imparfait of être + «partie» (agreeing with «elle»).", "wrong": "«Partir» is an être verb, and her leaving happened before my arrival: «elle était déjà partie». «Est partie» is just passé composé, and «avait partie» uses the wrong auxiliary."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous ___ fini nos devoirs avant le dîner.»", "choices": ["avions", "étions", "avons"], "answer": "avions", "right": "Parfait ! «finir» takes avoir → «nous avions fini».", "wrong": "«Finir» uses avoir, and the imparfait of avoir for «nous» is «avions»: «nous avions fini». «Avons» would give a passé composé."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English often lazily skips 'had': 'I realised I forgot my keys.' French is strict — the forgetting came first, so: «Je me suis rendu compte que j'avais oublié mes clés.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je me suis rendu compte que j'avais oublié mes clés.", "en": "I realised I had forgotten my keys."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Reflexive verbs always take être: «Elle s'était levée tôt», «Nous nous étions perdus»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«La veille, ils ___ couchés très tard.»", "choices": ["s'avaient", "s'étaient", "se sont"], "answer": "s'étaient", "right": "Bravo ! Reflexive → être → «ils s'étaient couchés».", "wrong": "Reflexive verbs never take avoir. And «la veille» (the day before) pushes us one step back in the past: «ils s'étaient couchés»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A favourite native use: regrets with «si». «Si j'avais su !» = If only I had known! It also builds unreal past conditions: «Si j'avais su, je serais venu.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Si j'avais su, je serais venu plus tôt.", "en": "If I had known, I would have come earlier."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'If only I had known!'?", "choices": ["Si je savais !", "Si j'avais su !", "Si j'aurais su !"], "answer": "Si j'avais su !", "right": "Yes! After «si», the plus-que-parfait expresses the unreal past.", "wrong": "«Si j'aurais su» is the most famous mistake in French — never put «aurais» after «si». Correct: «Si j'avais su !». «Si je savais» means 'If I knew' (now)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il était fatigué parce qu'il ___ mal dormi.»", "choices": ["a", "avait", "aurait"], "answer": "avait", "right": "Exactly — the bad night came before the tiredness: «il avait mal dormi».", "wrong": "The bad sleep happened before he was tired (both in the past), so we need the plus-que-parfait: «il avait mal dormi»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: imparfait of avoir/être + participle = 'had done'. Use it whenever an action is further back than another past action, and after «si» for past regrets."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I had already eaten.", "answer": "J'avais déjà mangé.", "accept": ["j'avais déjà mangé", "j’avais déjà mangé", "J’avais déjà mangé."], "hint": "imparfait of avoir + mangé, with «déjà» between them", "right": "Perfect! «déjà» sits between the auxiliary and the participle.", "wrong": "It's «J'avais déjà mangé.» — avais (imparfait of avoir) + déjà + mangé."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ils étaient déjà partis quand tu as téléphoné.»", "fr": "Ils étaient déjà partis quand tu as téléphoné."}]}, "34-2": {"stageId": "34-2", "title": "Pluperfect in practice: negation, agreement, timelines", "goal": "Use the plus-que-parfait confidently with negation, past participle agreement and time markers in a past narrative.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "You know the form: «j'avais fait», «j'étais venu». Today we polish it like a native: negation, agreement, and choosing it over the passé composé and imparfait."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Negation wraps around the auxiliary, never the participle: «Je n'avais pas fini», «Elle n'était jamais allée à Paris», «Nous n'avions rien vu»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle n'était jamais allée à Paris avant ce voyage.", "en": "She had never been to Paris before this trip."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'We hadn't understood.' Which is correct?", "choices": ["Nous n'avions pas compris.", "Nous avions ne pas compris.", "Nous n'avions compris pas."], "answer": "Nous n'avions pas compris.", "right": "Yes! «ne… pas» hugs the auxiliary «avions».", "wrong": "In compound tenses, «ne… pas» surrounds the auxiliary: «Nous n'avions pas compris.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Agreement: with être, the participle agrees with the subject: «Elles étaient arrivées». With avoir, it agrees only with a direct object placed before the verb: «les fleurs qu'il avait achetées»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les lettres qu'elle avait écrites sont restées dans un tiroir.", "en": "The letters she had written stayed in a drawer.", "note": "«que» = les lettres (feminine plural), placed before → «écrites»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les gâteaux que nous avions ___ étaient délicieux.» (faire)", "choices": ["faits", "fait", "faites"], "answer": "faits", "right": "Bravo ! «que» stands for «les gâteaux» (masculine plural), placed before the verb → «faits».", "wrong": "The direct object «que» = «les gâteaux» comes before «avions», so the participle agrees: masculine plural → «faits»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Think of a timeline: imparfait = background scenery, passé composé = the event, plus-que-parfait = one step further back. Clue words: «déjà», «la veille» (the day before), «avant», «auparavant», «plus tôt»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quand je suis arrivé, la réunion avait déjà commencé.", "en": "When I arrived, the meeting had already started."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The film had started when we arrived.' Which is correct?", "choices": ["Le film commençait quand nous sommes arrivés.", "Le film avait commencé quand nous sommes arrivés.", "Le film a commencé quand nous étions arrivés."], "answer": "Le film avait commencé quand nous sommes arrivés.", "right": "Exactly. The start came first → plus-que-parfait; our arrival → passé composé.", "wrong": "The start of the film happened before our arrival: «Le film avait commencé quand nous sommes arrivés.» «Commençait» would mean it was just starting as we arrived."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Marie ___ la veille.» (venir)", "choices": ["était venue", "avait venu", "était venu"], "answer": "était venue", "right": "Perfect: être verb + agreement with Marie → «était venue».", "wrong": "«Venir» takes être, and the participle agrees with «Marie»: «Marie était venue la veille.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "A native secret: the plus-que-parfait can soften a request. «J'étais venu vous demander un petit service» sounds more polite and less pushy than «Je viens vous demander…»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'étais venu vous demander un petit service.", "en": "I came to ask you a small favour.", "note": "Politeness by 'distancing' in time."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Vous ___ oublié votre parapluie chez moi.»", "choices": ["aviez", "étiez", "avez"], "answer": "aviez", "right": "Oui ! «oublier» takes avoir → «vous aviez oublié».", "wrong": "«Oublier» uses avoir; imparfait for «vous» is «aviez»: «Vous aviez oublié votre parapluie»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: negation around the auxiliary, être → agree with subject, avoir → agree only with a preceding direct object, and use the plus-que-parfait for anything one step back in the past."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: She had never seen the sea.", "answer": "Elle n'avait jamais vu la mer.", "accept": ["elle n'avait jamais vu la mer", "elle n’avait jamais vu la mer", "Elle n’avait jamais vu la mer."], "hint": "ne… jamais around «avait»", "right": "Magnifique ! «ne… jamais» surrounds the auxiliary.", "wrong": "It's «Elle n'avait jamais vu la mer.» — n' + avait + jamais + vu."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quand je suis arrivé, la réunion avait déjà commencé.»", "fr": "Quand je suis arrivé, la réunion avait déjà commencé."}]}, "35-1": {"stageId": "35-1", "title": "Futur antérieur: 'will have done'", "goal": "Form the futur antérieur and use it after quand/dès que, for deadlines, and to make guesses about the past.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The «futur antérieur» is the 'will have done' tense: an action that will be finished before another future moment. Recipe: future of «avoir» or «être» + past participle."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'aurai mangé avant ton arrivée.", "en": "I will have eaten before your arrival."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Avoir: «j'aurai, tu auras, il aura, nous aurons, vous aurez, ils auront» + participle. Être: «je serai, tu seras, il sera, nous serons, vous serez, ils seront» + participle, with agreement: «elle sera partie»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Demain à midi, nous ___ fini le projet.»", "choices": ["aurons", "aurions", "avons"], "answer": "aurons", "right": "Exactly — future of avoir: «nous aurons fini».", "wrong": "We need the future of avoir: «nous aurons fini». «Aurions» is the conditional (would have)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Trap: «j'aurai» (will have) and «j'aurais» (would have) sound almost identical. When writing, ask yourself: 'will' → «-ai», 'would' → «-ais»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The big difference with English: after «quand», «lorsque», «dès que», «aussitôt que», «une fois que», English says 'when I have finished'. French uses the future logic: «quand j'aurai fini»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Dès que j'aurai fini, je t'appellerai.", "en": "As soon as I have finished, I'll call you."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'As soon as she has arrived, we'll eat.'", "choices": ["Dès qu'elle est arrivée, on mangera.", "Dès qu'elle sera arrivée, on mangera.", "Dès qu'elle aura arrivé, on mangera."], "answer": "Dès qu'elle sera arrivée, on mangera.", "right": "Bravo ! Future-perfect logic + être verb + agreement: «sera arrivée».", "wrong": "The arrival is in the future and completed before eating: «Dès qu'elle sera arrivée». «Arriver» takes être, never avoir."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A very native use: guessing about the past. «Il aura raté son train» = He must have missed his train. It sounds natural and a bit resigned."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Paul n'est pas là ? Il aura encore oublié !", "en": "Paul isn't here? He must have forgotten again!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which form expresses a guess: 'She must have left her phone at home'? «Elle ___ son téléphone à la maison.»", "choices": ["aura laissé", "laissera", "laissait"], "answer": "aura laissé", "right": "Yes! The futur antérieur of probability: «elle aura laissé».", "wrong": "To guess about something already done, French uses the futur antérieur: «Elle aura laissé son téléphone à la maison.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ils ___ partis avant midi.»", "choices": ["seront", "auront", "seraient"], "answer": "seront", "right": "Parfait ! «partir» takes être → «ils seront partis».", "wrong": "«Partir» is an être verb: «ils seront partis». «Seraient» would be the conditional."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'By the time you read these lines, I will have left.'", "choices": ["Quand tu liras ces lignes, je serai parti.", "Quand tu liras ces lignes, je suis parti.", "Quand tu liras ces lignes, j'aurai parti."], "answer": "Quand tu liras ces lignes, je serai parti.", "right": "Beautiful — a classic farewell-letter sentence!", "wrong": "Leaving will be finished before the reading: «je serai parti» (être, futur antérieur)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: future of avoir/être + participle. Use it after «quand / dès que / une fois que» for completed future actions, for deadlines («Dans deux ans, j'aurai terminé mes études»), and for guesses about the past."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: When I have finished, I will call you.", "answer": "Quand j'aurai fini, je t'appellerai.", "accept": ["quand j'aurai fini, je t'appellerai", "quand j’aurai fini, je t’appellerai", "quand j'aurai fini je t'appellerai", "lorsque j'aurai fini, je t'appellerai", "quand j'aurai fini, je vous appellerai", "quand j'aurai terminé, je t'appellerai", "quand j'aurai terminé, je vous appellerai"], "hint": "Not «quand j'ai fini» — think 'will have finished'", "right": "Excellent ! You avoided the English trap.", "wrong": "French says «Quand j'aurai fini, je t'appellerai.» — futur antérieur after «quand», futur simple in the main clause."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Dès que j'aurai fini, je t'appellerai.»", "fr": "Dès que j'aurai fini, je t'appellerai."}]}, "36-1": {"stageId": "36-1", "title": "Le conditionnel passé: 'would have done'", "goal": "Form the conditionnel passé and use it for unreal past conditions, regrets and reproaches, and unconfirmed news.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The «conditionnel passé» is 'would have done' — the tense of what could have happened but didn't. Recipe: conditional of «avoir» or «être» + past participle: «j'aurais aimé», «je serais venu»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'aurais aimé être chanteur.", "en": "I would have liked to be a singer."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Avoir: «j'aurais, tu aurais, il aurait, nous aurions, vous auriez, ils auraient». Être: «je serais, tu serais, il serait, nous serions, vous seriez, ils seraient». Same auxiliary and agreement rules as the passé composé."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Its classic partner: «si» + plus-que-parfait → conditionnel passé. The condition and the result are both imaginary, in the past."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Si j'avais su, je serais venu plus tôt.", "en": "If I had known, I would have come earlier."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Si tu m'avais appelé, je t'___ aidé.»", "choices": ["aurais", "aurai", "avais"], "answer": "aurais", "right": "Exactly — the imaginary result takes the conditionnel passé: «je t'aurais aidé».", "wrong": "Si + plus-que-parfait → conditionnel passé: «je t'aurais aidé». «Aurai» is the future (will have)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The conditional never goes right after «si» meaning 'if'. «Si j'aurais su, j'aurais pas venu» is a famous line from the film «La Guerre des boutons» — said by a child precisely because it's wrong twice!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Regrets and reproaches live here: «J'aurais dû» (I should have), «J'aurais pu» (I could have), «J'aurais voulu» (I would have liked), and the cutting «Tu aurais pu me prévenir !»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu aurais pu me prévenir !", "en": "You could have warned me!", "note": "A reproach — tone matters!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I should have said no.'", "choices": ["Je devrais dire non.", "J'aurais dû dire non.", "J'aurais devoir dire non."], "answer": "J'aurais dû dire non.", "right": "Parfait ! «devoir» in the conditionnel passé = «j'aurais dû» + infinitive.", "wrong": "'Should have' = «j'aurais dû» + infinitive: «J'aurais dû dire non.» «Je devrais dire non» means 'I should say no' (now)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Journalists use it for information not yet confirmed — English 'reportedly' or 'allegedly'. You'll hear this every day on French radio."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Apparemment, il y aurait eu un accident sur l'autoroute ce matin.", "en": "Apparently, there was an accident on the motorway this morning."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "On the news: «Le suspect aurait quitté le pays.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["The suspect would have left the country.", "The suspect has reportedly left the country.", "The suspect will leave the country."], "answer": "The suspect has reportedly left the country.", "right": "Yes! That's the journalistic conditional: unconfirmed fact.", "wrong": "In the news, the conditional signals unconfirmed information: 'The suspect has reportedly left the country.'"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elles ___ venues si elles avaient pu.»", "choices": ["seraient", "auraient", "seront"], "answer": "seraient", "right": "Bravo ! «venir» takes être, conditional → «elles seraient venues».", "wrong": "«Venir» is an être verb: «elles seraient venues». «Seront» is the future."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Mix and match: if the result is in the present, use the conditionnel présent: «Si j'avais pris mon parapluie, je ne serais pas trempé maintenant.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: conditional of avoir/être + participle. Use it after a «si» + plus-que-parfait condition, for regrets («j'aurais dû»), reproaches («tu aurais pu») and unconfirmed news."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I would have liked to come.", "answer": "J'aurais aimé venir.", "accept": ["j'aurais aimé venir", "j’aurais aimé venir", "j'aurais voulu venir", "j’aurais voulu venir"], "hint": "conditional of avoir + aimé + infinitive", "right": "Très bien ! A sentence natives use all the time.", "wrong": "It's «J'aurais aimé venir.» — aurais + aimé + venir."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Si j'avais su, je serais venu plus tôt.»", "fr": "Si j'avais su, je serais venu plus tôt."}]}, "37-1": {"stageId": "37-1", "title": "Le passé simple: the storyteller's tense", "goal": "Recognise the passé simple in literature and news, understand its endings and key irregular forms, and know its spoken equivalent.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The «passé simple» is the past tense of written stories: novels, fairy tales, history books. In speech, natives use the passé composé instead. Your main goal: recognise it instantly, and produce the «il/ils» forms."}, {"t": "say", "text": "-ER verbs: «-ai, -as, -a, -âmes, -âtes, -èrent». So «parler» → «je parlai, tu parlas, il parla, nous parlâmes, vous parlâtes, ils parlèrent»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "En 2015, Tom gagna enfin la coupe du championnat de tennis.", "en": "In 2015, Tom finally won the tennis championship cup."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a novel: «Elle ___ la porte et sortit.» (fermer)", "choices": ["ferma", "fermait", "ferme"], "answer": "ferma", "right": "Yes! A single completed action in a narrative → «elle ferma».", "wrong": "A one-off action in a written story takes the passé simple: «Elle ferma la porte et sortit.» «Fermait» would describe a habit or background."}, {"t": "say", "text": "-IR and most -RE verbs: «-is, -is, -it, -îmes, -îtes, -irent». «finir» → «il finit, ils finirent»; «prendre» → «il prit, ils prirent»; «répondre» → «il répondit»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch out: «il finit» looks exactly like the present tense! Only context tells you. And «je parlai» (passé simple) vs «je parlais» (imparfait) differ by one letter."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Many irregulars use «-us»: «avoir» → «il eut», «être» → «il fut», «pouvoir» → «il put», «savoir» → «il sut», «vouloir» → «il voulut». And a few in «-int»: «venir» → «il vint, ils vinrent», «tenir» → «il tint». Also: «faire» → «il fit», «voir» → «il vit», «naître» → «il naquit», «mourir» → «il mourut»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il était une fois une princesse qui vivait dans un château. Un jour, elle rencontra un loup et eut très peur.", "en": "Once upon a time there was a princess who lived in a castle. One day, she met a wolf and was very frightened.", "note": "Imparfait = background; passé simple = events."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the infinitive of «il fut»?", "choices": ["être", "faire", "falloir"], "answer": "être", "right": "Correct! «fut» = être. «Faire» gives «il fit», «falloir» gives «il fallut».", "wrong": "«Il fut» comes from «être». Don't confuse it with «il fit» (faire)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ils vinrent» comes from…", "choices": ["venir", "voir", "vendre"], "answer": "venir", "right": "Bravo ! «venir» → «ils vinrent». «Voir» → «ils virent», «vendre» → «ils vendirent».", "wrong": "«Vinrent» is from «venir». Compare «ils virent» (voir) and «ils vendirent» (vendre)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In literature, the passé simple plays the role of the passé composé: it moves the story forward, while the imparfait paints the scene. In «Alice au pays des merveilles», chapter 5, you'll read lines like «La Chenille et Alice se regardèrent un moment en silence.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La Chenille et Alice se regardèrent un moment en silence.", "en": "The Caterpillar and Alice looked at each other for a while in silence."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What would a French speaker say in conversation instead of «Nous mangeâmes» ?", "choices": ["Nous avons mangé.", "Nous mangions.", "Nous mangerons."], "answer": "Nous avons mangé.", "right": "Exactly — in speech, the passé simple becomes the passé composé.", "wrong": "The spoken equivalent of the passé simple is the passé composé: «Nous avons mangé.» (or more casually «On a mangé.»)"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Using «nous» or «vous» forms in speech («Vous fûtes charmant») sounds pompous or comic — natives do it only as a joke. But «il/ils» forms are everywhere in books and the press."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il ___ le loup et ___ la fuite.» (voir, prendre)", "choices": ["vit… prit", "voyait… prenait", "vu… pris"], "answer": "vit… prit", "right": "Superbe ! «il vit» (voir) and «il prit» (prendre) — two sudden events.", "wrong": "Two quick events in a story → passé simple: «Il vit le loup et prit la fuite.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: -er → «-a / -èrent»; -ir/-re → «-it / -irent»; irregulars in «-ut» (fut, eut, put) or «-int» (vint, tint). Read it, recognise it, and speak with the passé composé."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Rewrite in spoken French (passé composé): «Elle partit.»", "answer": "Elle est partie.", "accept": ["elle est partie", "Elle est partie"], "hint": "partir takes être — don't forget agreement", "right": "Parfait ! «partit» → «est partie».", "wrong": "«Elle partit» = «Elle est partie.» — être + partie (feminine agreement)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Read it like a storyteller: «Il était une fois une princesse qui vivait dans un château. Un jour, elle rencontra un loup.»", "fr": "Il était une fois une princesse qui vivait dans un château. Un jour, elle rencontra un loup."}]}, "38-1": {"stageId": "38-1", "title": "Active vs. passive voice", "goal": "Build the passive voice in any tense with être + agreeing participle, choose par or de, and know when French prefers on instead.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Active: the subject does the action. Passive: the subject receives it. In French: «être» + past participle (agreeing with the subject) + «par» + the agent."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le lion attrape la gazelle. → La gazelle est attrapée par le lion.", "en": "The lion catches the gazelle. → The gazelle is caught by the lion.", "note": "«attrapée» agrees with «la gazelle»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The magic: «être» carries the tense. «est mangée» (is eaten), «a été mangée» (was/has been eaten), «était mangée» (was being eaten), «sera mangée» (will be eaten), «serait mangée» (would be eaten), «avait été mangée» (had been eaten)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Passive of «Le chat a mangé la souris.»", "choices": ["La souris a été mangée par le chat.", "La souris est mangée par le chat.", "La souris a mangée par le chat."], "answer": "La souris a été mangée par le chat.", "right": "Exactly! Passé composé of être = «a été», + «mangée».", "wrong": "The original is in the passé composé, so être must be too: «a été mangée». «Est mangée» is the present passive."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Passive of «Le jury choisira les gagnants.»", "choices": ["Les gagnants seront choisis par le jury.", "Les gagnants sont choisis par le jury.", "Les gagnants seront choisi par le jury."], "answer": "Les gagnants seront choisis par le jury.", "right": "Bravo ! Future of être + «choisis» (masculine plural).", "wrong": "Future → «seront», and the participle agrees with «les gagnants»: «choisis»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Agreement is automatic with être in the passive: «Les lettres ont été envoyées», «Le colis a été livré». Forgetting it is the number one written mistake."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Par» or «de»? «Par» for an action. «De» for states, feelings and descriptions: «Il est aimé de tous», «La table est couverte de livres», «Elle est connue de tout le quartier»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce professeur est respecté de tous ses élèves.", "en": "This teacher is respected by all his students."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«La montagne est couverte ___ neige.»", "choices": ["de", "par", "avec"], "answer": "de", "right": "Yes! A description → «couverte de».", "wrong": "With verbs of state or description, French uses «de»: «couverte de neige»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Native insight: French uses the passive less than English. It often prefers «on» or a pronominal verb: «On a volé mon vélo» rather than «Mon vélo a été volé»; «Le français se parle au Québec»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On a construit ce pont en 1890. / Ce pont a été construit en 1890.", "en": "This bridge was built in 1890."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I was told that the shop was closed.'", "choices": ["J'ai été dit que le magasin était fermé.", "On m'a dit que le magasin était fermé.", "Je suis dit que le magasin était fermé."], "answer": "On m'a dit que le magasin était fermé.", "right": "Excellent ! Only verbs with a direct object can go passive. «Dire à quelqu'un» → use «on».", "wrong": "In French, you can't make 'I' the subject of a passive «dire» (we say «dire à quelqu'un»). Use «on»: «On m'a dit que…»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'The coffee used to be prepared by Tom.' «Le café ___ préparé par Tom.»", "choices": ["était", "a été", "sera"], "answer": "était", "right": "Parfait — habit in the past → imparfait of être.", "wrong": "'Used to be' = a past habit → imparfait: «Le café était préparé par Tom.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: être (in the tense you need) + agreeing participle + «par» (action) or «de» (state/feeling). And in everyday speech, «on» is often more natural."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (passive): The book was written by Victor Hugo.", "answer": "Le livre a été écrit par Victor Hugo.", "accept": ["le livre a été écrit par victor hugo", "le livre a été écrit par Victor Hugo", "ce livre a été écrit par victor hugo"], "hint": "passé composé of être + écrit", "right": "Magnifique ! «a été écrit».", "wrong": "It's «Le livre a été écrit par Victor Hugo.» — a été + écrit + par."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «La souris a été mangée par le chat.»", "fr": "La souris a été mangée par le chat."}]}, "38-2": {"stageId": "38-2", "title": "Passive gym: transforming both ways", "goal": "Transform sentences from active to passive and back in any tense, including pronoun subjects and agentless passives.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Workout time! Recipe for active → passive: 1) find the direct object, 2) make it the subject, 3) put «être» in the tense of the original verb, 4) add the participle with agreement, 5) add «par» + the old subject."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Charlotte a écrit ce livre. → Ce livre a été écrit par Charlotte.", "en": "Charlotte wrote this book. → This book was written by Charlotte."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Passive of «Tom préparait toujours le café après le déjeuner.»", "choices": ["Le café était toujours préparé par Tom après le déjeuner.", "Le café a toujours été préparé par Tom après le déjeuner.", "Le café était toujours préparée par Tom après le déjeuner."], "answer": "Le café était toujours préparé par Tom après le déjeuner.", "right": "Yes! Imparfait stays imparfait: «était préparé», masculine agreement with «le café».", "wrong": "«Préparait» is imparfait, so être goes in the imparfait: «était préparé». «Café» is masculine → no -e."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Passive of «La chorale a sélectionné Tom et Paul pour représenter leur école.»", "choices": ["Tom et Paul ont été sélectionnés par la chorale pour représenter leur école.", "Tom et Paul a été sélectionné par la chorale pour représenter leur école.", "Tom et Paul sont sélectionnés par la chorale pour représenter leur école."], "answer": "Tom et Paul ont été sélectionnés par la chorale pour représenter leur école.", "right": "Bravo ! Plural subject → «ont été sélectionnés».", "wrong": "Two people = plural: «ont été sélectionnés». The original is passé composé, so «sont sélectionnés» (present) is the wrong tense."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Object pronouns become subject pronouns: «Tout le monde l'admire» → «Il est admiré de tous» or «Elle est admirée de tous». With feelings like «admirer», «de» sounds elegant; «par tout le monde» is fine too."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tout le monde l'admire. → Elle est admirée de tous.", "en": "Everyone admires her. → She is admired by all."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Active form of «Le fromage est mangé par la souris.»", "choices": ["La souris mange le fromage.", "La souris a mangé le fromage.", "Le fromage mange la souris."], "answer": "La souris mange le fromage.", "right": "Exactly! «est mangé» is the present passive → present active «mange».", "wrong": "«Est mangé» is the present tense of the passive, so the active is also present: «La souris mange le fromage.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Active form of «Le gâteau a été mangé par les enfants.»", "choices": ["Les enfants ont mangé le gâteau.", "Les enfants mangent le gâteau.", "Les enfants avaient mangé le gâteau."], "answer": "Les enfants ont mangé le gâteau.", "right": "Parfait ! «a été mangé» → passé composé «ont mangé».", "wrong": "«A été» = passé composé of être, so the active is passé composé: «Les enfants ont mangé le gâteau.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "No agent? Just drop «par…»: «La réunion a été annulée.» To go back to active, use «on»: «On a annulé la réunion.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Active form of «Les résultats seront publiés demain.»", "choices": ["On publiera les résultats demain.", "On publie les résultats demain.", "Ils seront publiés demain."], "answer": "On publiera les résultats demain.", "right": "Excellent ! No agent → «on», and future stays future.", "wrong": "No agent is mentioned, so use «on», keeping the future: «On publiera les résultats demain.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Trap: «La souris est mangée» is the PRESENT passive ('is eaten'), not a passé composé. Don't confuse it with être verbs like «Elle est partie» (she has left)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What tense is «La souris est mangée par le chat» ?", "choices": ["présent (passive)", "passé composé (active)", "imparfait (passive)"], "answer": "présent (passive)", "right": "Well spotted! être in the present + participle = present passive.", "wrong": "«Est» is the present of être, so this is the present passive: 'The mouse is eaten by the cat.'"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Bonus: the pronominal passive, loved in recipes, rules and labels: «Ce vin se boit frais», «Ça ne se fait pas» (That isn't done)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce vin se boit frais.", "en": "This wine is drunk chilled."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: keep the tense, move it onto «être», agree the participle, add «par». Back to active: the agent becomes the subject — or «on» if there's no agent."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Put into the passive: «Le facteur apporte les lettres.»", "answer": "Les lettres sont apportées par le facteur.", "accept": ["les lettres sont apportées par le facteur", "Les lettres sont apportées par le facteur"], "hint": "present of être + feminine plural participle", "right": "Superbe ! «sont apportées» — present tense, feminine plural.", "wrong": "It's «Les lettres sont apportées par le facteur.» — present → «sont», and «apportées» agrees with «les lettres»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ce livre a été écrit par Charlotte.»", "fr": "Ce livre a été écrit par Charlotte."}]}, "39-1": {"stageId": "39-1", "title": "Concordance des temps: matching tenses", "goal": "Choose the right tense in a subordinate clause depending on the tense of the main clause and the time relation between the two actions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Many sentences have two parts: a main clause and a subordinate clause (after «que», «qui», «quand»…). The «concordance des temps» is how the tense of the main clause steers the tense of the subordinate."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai mangé la pomme qui était sur la table.", "en": "I ate the apple which was on the table.", "note": "Main clause: «j'ai mangé la pomme». Subordinate: «qui était sur la table»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Easy case: main clause in the present or future. The subordinate simply uses the logical tense: before → passé composé or imparfait; same time → présent; after → futur. «Tom sait que tu as regardé / regardes / regarderas la télévision.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tom sait que tu regarderas la télévision.", "en": "Tom knows that you will watch television."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tom sait que tu ___ la télévision hier.»", "choices": ["as regardé", "regardes", "regarderas"], "answer": "as regardé", "right": "Yes! «hier» = before now → passé composé.", "wrong": "Main clause present, action before → passé composé: «Tom sait que tu as regardé la télévision hier.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "The tricky case: main clause in the past. Everything shifts back: same time → imparfait; before → plus-que-parfait; after → conditionnel présent (the 'future in the past')."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tom ne savait pas que tu étais en France.", "en": "Tom didn't know that you were in France."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il m'a dit qu'il ___ le lendemain.» (venir — a later action)", "choices": ["viendrait", "viendra", "venait"], "answer": "viendrait", "right": "Exactly! Future seen from the past → conditionnel présent.", "wrong": "Main clause in the past + later action → conditionnel présent: «Il m'a dit qu'il viendrait le lendemain.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je pensais que tu ___.» (partir — an earlier action)", "choices": ["étais déjà parti", "es déjà parti", "seras déjà parti"], "answer": "étais déjà parti", "right": "Parfait ! Earlier than a past moment → plus-que-parfait.", "wrong": "Main clause in the past + earlier action → plus-que-parfait: «Je pensais que tu étais déjà parti.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Native nuance: if something is still true now, French may keep the present. «Tom ne savait pas que tu es en France» = you are still in France today. Same for general truths: «Galilée affirmait que la Terre tourne.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "English trap with «quand»: English says 'when I am older', but French uses the future when the time is future: «Je serai footballeur professionnel quand je serai grand.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je serai footballeur professionnel quand je serai grand.", "en": "I'll be a professional footballer when I grow up."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I'll call you when I arrive.'", "choices": ["Je t'appellerai quand j'arriverai.", "Je t'appellerai quand j'arrivais.", "Je t'appellerai quand j'arriverais."], "answer": "Je t'appellerai quand j'arriverai.", "right": "Bravo ! Future after «quand» for a future event.", "wrong": "The arrival is in the future, so French uses the future after «quand»: «quand j'arriverai». «Arriverais» is the conditional."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je croyais que vous ___ en vacances.» (same time)", "choices": ["étiez", "êtes", "serez"], "answer": "étiez", "right": "Exactly — past main clause + same time → imparfait.", "wrong": "«Je croyais» is past, and being on holiday is simultaneous → imparfait: «vous étiez»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap. Main clause present/future → use the logical tense. Main clause past → imparfait (same time), plus-que-parfait (before), conditionnel présent (after). And future after «quand» for future events."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I knew that you were tired.", "answer": "Je savais que tu étais fatigué.", "accept": ["je savais que tu étais fatigué", "je savais que tu étais fatiguée", "je savais que vous étiez fatigué", "je savais que vous étiez fatiguée", "je savais que vous étiez fatigués", "je savais que vous étiez fatiguées"], "hint": "imparfait in both clauses", "right": "Très bien ! Past main clause + same time → imparfait.", "wrong": "It's «Je savais que tu étais fatigué(e).» — both verbs in the imparfait."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il m'a dit qu'il viendrait le lendemain.»", "fr": "Il m'a dit qu'il viendrait le lendemain."}]}, "40-1": {"stageId": "40-1", "title": "Discours direct et indirect: reporting speech", "goal": "Turn direct speech into indirect speech, adjusting pronouns, tenses, time expressions, questions and commands.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Direct speech quotes the exact words. Indirect speech reports them with «que» — and you adjust pronouns, possessives, sometimes tenses and time words. Useful verbs: «dire», «expliquer», «affirmer», «déclarer», «annoncer», «répondre»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tom explique : « Je lis beaucoup de romans policiers. » → Tom explique qu'il lit beaucoup de romans policiers.", "en": "Tom explains: 'I read a lot of detective novels.' → Tom explains that he reads a lot of detective novels.", "note": "Reporting verb in the present → no tense change, just «je» → «il»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "If the reporting verb is in the past («a dit», «a expliqué»), the tenses shift back: présent → imparfait; passé composé → plus-que-parfait; futur → conditionnel présent. The imparfait stays as it is."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tom a dit qu'il aimait chanter. Charlotte a répondu qu'elle préférait danser.", "en": "Tom said that he liked singing. Charlotte answered that she preferred dancing."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Tom a dit : « Je suis fatigué. » → Tom a dit…", "choices": ["qu'il était fatigué.", "qu'il est fatigué.", "que je suis fatigué."], "answer": "qu'il était fatigué.", "right": "Exactly: «je» → «il», présent → imparfait.", "wrong": "Reporting verb in the past → présent becomes imparfait, and «je» becomes «il»: «Tom a dit qu'il était fatigué.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Elle a dit : « J'ai perdu mes clés. » → Elle a dit…", "choices": ["qu'elle avait perdu ses clés.", "qu'elle a perdu mes clés.", "qu'elle perdait ses clés."], "answer": "qu'elle avait perdu ses clés.", "right": "Bravo ! Passé composé → plus-que-parfait, and «mes» → «ses».", "wrong": "Passé composé shifts to plus-que-parfait, and the possessive changes too: «Elle a dit qu'elle avait perdu ses clés.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Time words move too when the day has passed: «aujourd'hui» → «ce jour-là», «hier» → «la veille», «demain» → «le lendemain», «maintenant» → «à ce moment-là», «ici» → «là»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Last month, he said: « Je partirai demain. » → Il a annoncé…", "choices": ["qu'il partirait le lendemain.", "qu'il partira demain.", "qu'il partait demain."], "answer": "qu'il partirait le lendemain.", "right": "Parfait ! Futur → conditionnel, «demain» → «le lendemain».", "wrong": "Futur becomes conditionnel présent, and since that 'tomorrow' is long gone, «demain» becomes «le lendemain»: «Il a annoncé qu'il partirait le lendemain.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Reported questions: yes/no questions use «si» («s'il» before il/ils); «qu'est-ce que» → «ce que»; «qu'est-ce qui» → «ce qui»; «où, quand, pourquoi, comment» stay — and there's no inversion and no question mark."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il m'a demandé : « Est-ce que tu viens ? » → Il m'a demandé si je venais.", "en": "He asked me: 'Are you coming?' → He asked me if I was coming."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Elle m'a demandé : « Qu'est-ce que tu veux ? » → Elle m'a demandé…", "choices": ["ce que je voulais.", "qu'est-ce que je voulais.", "que voulais-je."], "answer": "ce que je voulais.", "right": "Excellent ! «qu'est-ce que» → «ce que», and présent → imparfait.", "wrong": "In indirect questions, «qu'est-ce que» becomes «ce que», with no inversion: «Elle m'a demandé ce que je voulais.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Commands become «de» + infinitive: «Ferme la porte !» → «Il m'a dit de fermer la porte.» Negative: «Ne crie pas !» → «Il m'a dit de ne pas crier.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Il m'a dit : « Ferme la porte ! » → …", "choices": ["Il m'a dit de fermer la porte.", "Il m'a dit que je ferme la porte.", "Il m'a dit fermer la porte."], "answer": "Il m'a dit de fermer la porte.", "right": "Yes! Imperative → «de» + infinitive.", "wrong": "A reported command uses «dire de» + infinitive: «Il m'a dit de fermer la porte.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: change pronouns and possessives; if the reporting verb is past, shift tenses back; adapt time words; questions → «si / ce que / ce qui»; commands → «de» + infinitive."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Report in the past: Marie a dit : « Je suis contente. »", "answer": "Marie a dit qu'elle était contente.", "accept": ["marie a dit qu'elle était contente", "marie a dit qu’elle était contente", "Marie a dit qu’elle était contente."], "hint": "je → elle, présent → imparfait", "right": "Superbe ! Pronoun and tense both shifted.", "wrong": "It's «Marie a dit qu'elle était contente.» — «je suis» → «elle était»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Tom a dit qu'il aimait chanter. Charlotte a répondu qu'elle préférait danser.»", "fr": "Tom a dit qu'il aimait chanter. Charlotte a répondu qu'elle préférait danser."}]}, "41-1": {"stageId": "41-1", "title": "Native discourse connectors: du coup, en fait, quand même", "goal": "Use the little words that make spoken French flow — du coup, en fait, quand même, bref, figure-toi, j'ai beau, quoi qu'il en soit — in the right register.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "What makes French sound native isn't just grammar — it's the glue words. Let's master the connectors you hear in every Parisian café, and learn which ones to avoid in a formal email."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Du coup» = so / as a result. It's everywhere in speech (courant to familier) — so much that overusing it is a running joke. In formal writing, prefer «donc» or «par conséquent»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Du coup, on fait quoi ce soir ?", "en": "So, what are we doing tonight?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«En fait» = actually / in fact, used to correct an expectation. Pronounce the final t: [ɑ̃fɛt]. Big trap: English 'actually' is NOT «actuellement», which means 'currently'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Actually, I'd rather stay home.'", "choices": ["Actuellement, je préfère rester à la maison.", "En fait, je préfère rester à la maison.", "En effet, je préfère rester à la maison."], "answer": "En fait, je préfère rester à la maison.", "right": "Exactly! «En fait» corrects or nuances what was expected.", "wrong": "'Actually' = «en fait». «Actuellement» means 'currently', and «en effet» means 'indeed' (it confirms rather than corrects)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Quand même» = still / all the same — but natives also use it for emphasis or indignation: «C'est quand même incroyable !», «Tu pourrais quand même dire merci !»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est quand même incroyable, cette histoire !", "en": "This story is really incredible, all the same!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «quand même» add here: «Tu aurais quand même pu m'appeler !» ?", "choices": ["A reproach: 'you could at least have called me!'", "A time: 'you could have called me when…'", "A concession: 'you called me anyway.'"], "answer": "A reproach: 'you could at least have called me!'", "right": "Yes! Here «quand même» expresses indignation — 'at least / really'.", "wrong": "In this sentence «quand même» marks a reproach: 'You could at least have called me!'"}, {"t": "say", "text": "More storytelling glue: «Figure-toi que…» (Would you believe…), «Bref» (Anyway / Long story short), «À vrai dire» (To tell the truth), «Quoi qu'il en soit» (Be that as it may — more formal)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Figure-toi qu'il a démissionné hier ! Bref, tout s'est réglé en cinq minutes.", "en": "Would you believe he resigned yesterday! Anyway, everything got sorted out in five minutes."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You've told a long, messy story and want to wrap it up. Which word?", "choices": ["Bref", "Du coup", "En effet"], "answer": "Bref", "right": "Parfait ! «Bref» = long story short.", "wrong": "To cut a story short, natives say «Bref…». «Du coup» introduces a consequence; «en effet» confirms."}, {"t": "say", "text": "An elegant structure: «avoir beau» + infinitive = no matter how much… «J'ai beau chercher, je ne trouve aucune erreur.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'No matter how hard I try, I can't sleep.'", "choices": ["J'ai beau essayer, je n'arrive pas à dormir.", "J'ai beau d'essayer, je n'arrive pas à dormir.", "Je suis beau essayer, je n'arrive pas à dormir."], "answer": "J'ai beau essayer, je n'arrive pas à dormir.", "right": "Bravo ! «avoir beau» + bare infinitive, no preposition.", "wrong": "The structure is «avoir beau» + infinitive, with no «de»: «J'ai beau essayer, je n'arrive pas à dormir.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Register ladder: familier → «du coup», «bref», «genre»; courant → «en fait», «quand même», «à vrai dire»; soutenu → «quoi qu'il en soit», «par conséquent», «néanmoins». In a job interview, avoid chains of «du coup»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a formal email: 'Regardless, we must move forward.'", "choices": ["Quoi qu'il en soit, nous devons avancer.", "Du coup, faut avancer.", "Bref, on doit avancer."], "answer": "Quoi qu'il en soit, nous devons avancer.", "right": "Excellent register choice!", "wrong": "For a formal email, choose «Quoi qu'il en soit, nous devons avancer.» The others are casual spoken French."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «du coup» (so), «en fait» (actually), «quand même» (still / really!), «bref» (anyway), «figure-toi» (imagine), «j'ai beau» (no matter how), «quoi qu'il en soit» (regardless). Match them to the situation!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Actually, I was thinking of another solution.", "answer": "En fait, je pensais à une autre solution.", "accept": ["en fait, je pensais à une autre solution", "en fait je pensais à une autre solution", "en fait, je pensais plutôt à une autre solution", "en fait, je pensais à une autre solution."], "hint": "Not «actuellement»!", "right": "Très naturel ! You avoided the 'actually' trap.", "wrong": "It's «En fait, je pensais à une autre solution.» — 'actually' = «en fait», and 'was thinking' = imparfait «pensais»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it with feeling: «C'est quand même incroyable, cette histoire !»", "fr": "C'est quand même incroyable, cette histoire !"}]}, "41-2": {"stageId": "41-2", "title": "Fast spoken French: t'as, y'a, chais pas", "goal": "Understand and use the contractions and dropped words of real spoken French, and convert them back to standard written French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Textbook French: «Je ne sais pas, il n'y a personne.» Real Paris French: «Chais pas, y'a personne.» To understand natives, your ear needs to recognise these squeezed forms."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Rule 1: the «ne» disappears in almost all everyday speech: «C'est pas évident», «J'en sais rien». Keep it in writing and formal speech. Rule 2: sounds merge: «tu as» → «t'as», «il y a» → «y'a», «je suis» → «chuis», «je sais pas» → «chais pas», «il faut» → «faut»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "T'as vu le dernier rapport ?", "en": "Did you see the latest report?", "note": "Standard: «Tu as vu…?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Chais pas» mean?", "choices": ["I don't know.", "I'm not at home.", "I know."], "answer": "I don't know.", "right": "Exactly! «Chais pas» = «Je ne sais pas»: the «ne» drops and «je sais» merges into «chais».", "wrong": "«Chais pas» is a squeezed «Je ne sais pas» = I don't know."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Y'a pas de souci, je m'en occupe tout de suite.", "en": "No problem at all, I'll take care of it right away."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Standard written form of «Y'a pas de souci» ?", "choices": ["Il n'y a pas de souci.", "Il n'a pas de souci.", "Il y n'a pas de souci."], "answer": "Il n'y a pas de souci.", "right": "Parfait ! «il» and «ne» both disappear in speech.", "wrong": "«Y'a pas» = «Il n'y a pas». The «ne» goes before «y»: «Il n'y a pas de souci.» «Il n'a pas» means 'he doesn't have'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Faut» = «il faut»: «Faut qu'on se voie avant la fin de la semaine.» Other classics: «T'es où ?» (Où es-tu ?), «Qu'est-ce tu fais ?» (Qu'est-ce que tu fais ?), «I' vient ?» («il» often sounds like «i»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Faut qu'on se voie avant la fin de la semaine.", "en": "We have to meet up before the end of the week.", "note": "Subjunctive «voie» — even natives often misspell it «voit»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You hear: [ʃɥi kʁəve]. What's being said?", "choices": ["Je suis crevé.", "J'ai crevé.", "Je serais crevé."], "answer": "Je suis crevé.", "right": "Bravo ! «Chuis» = «je suis». And «crevé» = exhausted.", "wrong": "[ʃɥi] is the squeezed form of «je suis»: «Je suis crevé.» = I'm exhausted."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Don't mix them up: «t'as» = tu as, «t'es» = tu es. «T'inquiète !» = «Ne t'inquiète pas !» These are fine in texts and with friends, never in a cover letter."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A friend says «T'inquiète !». What does it mean?", "choices": ["Don't worry!", "Are you worried?", "You worry me!"], "answer": "Don't worry!", "right": "Yes! It's a clipped «Ne t'inquiète pas !».", "wrong": "«T'inquiète !» is short for «Ne t'inquiète pas !» = Don't worry!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Everyday expressions you'll hear with these forms: «Ça vaut le coup» (It's worth it), «On se tient au courant» (Let's keep each other posted), «Franchement» (Honestly)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ça vaut vraiment le coup d'essayer.", "en": "It's really worth trying."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Standard written form of «T'es où ?»", "choices": ["Où es-tu ?", "Où as-tu ?", "Où est-il ?"], "answer": "Où es-tu ?", "right": "Exactly — «t'es» = «tu es».", "wrong": "«T'es» = «tu es», so «T'es où ?» = «Où es-tu ?» (or «Où est-ce que tu es ?»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: drop «ne», merge sounds: t'as, t'es, y'a, chuis, chais pas, faut. Understand them all — use them with friends, and switch back to full forms when writing or speaking formally."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the standard written form of: «Chais pas, y'a personne.»", "answer": "Je ne sais pas, il n'y a personne.", "accept": ["je ne sais pas, il n'y a personne", "je ne sais pas, il n’y a personne", "je ne sais pas il n'y a personne"], "hint": "Restore «je», «ne» and «il»", "right": "Parfait ! You can now switch between registers.", "wrong": "Full form: «Je ne sais pas, il n'y a personne.» — both «ne» are restored, plus «je» and «il»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it casually, like a Parisian: «T'inquiète, y'a pas de souci, je m'en occupe.»", "fr": "T'inquiète, y'a pas de souci, je m'en occupe."}]}, "41-3": {"stageId": "41-3", "title": "Verlan, idioms & Parisian banter", "goal": "Understand common verlan words and everyday idioms, and react naturally in casual conversation while knowing when to stay formal.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Verlan» is slang made by flipping syllables — the word itself comes from «l'envers» (the reverse). «fou» → «ouf», «louche» → «chelou», «bizarre» → «zarbi», «lourd» → «relou», «femme» → «meuf», «fête» → «teuf»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est complètement ouf, ce projet !", "en": "This project is completely crazy / amazing!", "note": "«ouf» can be positive or negative — tone decides."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il est trop relou !» — «relou» comes from…", "choices": ["lourd", "roulé", "loup"], "answer": "lourd", "right": "Yes! «lourd» → «relou» = annoying, heavy-going.", "wrong": "«Relou» is verlan for «lourd» — it describes someone annoying or tiresome."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce quartier est devenu super chelou la nuit.", "en": "This neighbourhood has become super sketchy at night.", "note": "«chelou» = verlan for «louche» (shady)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Une meuf» means…", "choices": ["a woman / girl (familiar)", "a party", "a police officer"], "answer": "a woman / girl (familiar)", "right": "Exactly! «femme» → «meuf». A party is «une teuf», a cop «un keuf».", "wrong": "«Meuf» is verlan for «femme» = a woman / girl, very familiar. Careful: «teuf» = party, «keuf» = cop."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Register check: «ouf», «chelou», «relou», «meuf» are mainstream among friends, especially under 40 — but they're familier. Avoid them with your boss, your landlord or at the préfecture. «Keuf» can sound disrespectful."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now classic idioms: «poser un lapin à quelqu'un» = to stand someone up; «se prendre la tête» = to stress / overthink (or argue with someone); «être crevé» = to be exhausted; «avoir la flemme» = can't be bothered; «discuter à bâtons rompus» = to chat freely about everything."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il m'a posé un lapin hier soir.", "en": "He stood me up last night."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'She stood me up.'", "choices": ["Elle m'a posé un lapin.", "Elle m'a donné un lapin.", "Elle m'a laissé un lapin."], "answer": "Elle m'a posé un lapin.", "right": "Bravo ! The verb is always «poser».", "wrong": "The fixed expression is «poser un lapin à quelqu'un»: «Elle m'a posé un lapin.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Arrête de te prendre la tête pour des détails.", "en": "Stop stressing yourself out over details."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai la flemme d'aller au sport.» means…", "choices": ["I can't be bothered to go to the gym.", "I have a fever, I can't go to the gym.", "I'm excited to go to the gym."], "answer": "I can't be bothered to go to the gym.", "right": "Exactly! «avoir la flemme» = to feel too lazy to do something.", "wrong": "«Avoir la flemme (de faire quelque chose)» = can't be bothered. A fever is «avoir de la fièvre»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Banter reactions: «Grave !» (Totally!), «Carrément !» (Absolutely!), «Trop bien !» (So good!), «T'abuses !» (You're pushing it!), «Je suis mort de rire» or «mdr» in texts (I'm dying of laughter)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend says: «Ce resto était trop bon !» A natural, casual way to agree?", "choices": ["Grave !", "Gravement !", "Sérieusement non."], "answer": "Grave !", "right": "Parfait ! «Grave !» = Totally! — pure casual French.", "wrong": "Natives say «Grave !» (Totally!). «Gravement» means 'seriously' as in 'seriously injured'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On a discuté à bâtons rompus pendant deux heures.", "en": "We chatted about anything and everything for two hours.", "note": "This one is neutral — fine in any register."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: verlan flips syllables (ouf, chelou, relou, meuf, teuf); idioms: «poser un lapin», «se prendre la tête», «crevé», «avoir la flemme», «à bâtons rompus»; banter: «Grave !», «Carrément !», «T'abuses !». Use them with friends — and keep them out of formal settings."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (casual): I'm completely exhausted.", "answer": "Je suis complètement crevé.", "accept": ["je suis complètement crevé", "je suis complètement crevée", "chuis complètement crevé", "chuis complètement crevée", "je suis complètement crevé !", "je suis complètement crevée !"], "hint": "«crevé» = exhausted", "right": "Super ! Very natural.", "wrong": "It's «Je suis complètement crevé(e).» — «crevé» is the casual word for exhausted."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it with attitude: «C'est ouf, il m'a encore posé un lapin !»", "fr": "C'est ouf, il m'a encore posé un lapin !"}]}, "42-1": {"stageId": "42-1", "title": "Renting in Paris: le bail & l'état des lieux", "goal": "Understand and use the key vocabulary of renting a flat in France — application file, lease, deposit, guarantor, inspection, notice and insurance.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Renting in Paris is a sport! Key words: «le bail» (lease), «le propriétaire» or «le bailleur» (landlord), «le locataire» (tenant), «le loyer» (rent), «les charges» (service charges), «le dépôt de garantie» (deposit), «le garant» (guarantor), «l'état des lieux» (inspection report), «le préavis» (notice)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "First, «le dossier»: ID, work contract, your three latest payslips («bulletins de salaire» or «fiches de paie»), your last «avis d'imposition» (tax notice) and your guarantor's documents. A complete dossier makes all the difference."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Veuillez trouver ci-joint mes trois derniers bulletins de salaire.", "en": "Please find attached my last three payslips."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "An ad says: «1 200 € charges comprises». What does it mean?", "choices": ["€1,200 including service charges", "€1,200 excluding service charges", "€1,200 deposit included"], "answer": "€1,200 including service charges", "right": "Exactly! «CC» = charges comprises; «HC» = hors charges (excluding).", "wrong": "«Charges comprises» (CC) = service charges included. The opposite is «hors charges» (HC)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les charges comprises incluent le chauffage collectif et l'eau froide.", "en": "The included charges cover central heating and cold water."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "People say «la caution» for the deposit, but the legal term is «le dépôt de garantie»: max one month's rent (excluding charges) unfurnished, two months furnished. Careful: «se porter caution» means to act as a guarantor!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«L'état des lieux d'entrée» is done when you move in, «l'état des lieux de sortie» when you leave. «Contradictoire» means both parties are present and sign. Note every scratch and stain!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous devons dresser l'état des lieux contradictoire avant la remise des clés.", "en": "We must complete the joint inspection report before handing over the keys."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Why is the «état des lieux» so important?", "choices": ["It's the basis for getting your deposit back.", "It's the document that calculates your taxes.", "It renews the lease automatically."], "answer": "It's the basis for getting your deposit back.", "right": "Yes! The landlord compares entry and exit reports to justify any deductions.", "wrong": "The entry and exit «états des lieux» are compared at the end: any new damage can be deducted from your «dépôt de garantie»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Many landlords want «un garant solidaire» — someone who pays if you don't — often living in France. No guarantor? Ask about «Visale», a free guarantee from Action Logement."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le propriétaire exige un garant solidaire résidant en France.", "en": "The landlord requires a joint guarantor residing in France."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'the notice period' when leaving a flat?", "choices": ["le délai de préavis", "la période d'avertissement", "le temps de remarque"], "answer": "le délai de préavis", "right": "Parfait ! «le préavis» or «le délai de préavis».", "wrong": "The term is «le (délai de) préavis». The other options are literal translations no one uses."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Notice period: normally three months for an unfurnished flat, but only one month in a «zone tendue» (high-demand area) like Paris, and one month for furnished flats. Send it by «lettre recommandée avec accusé de réception»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You rent an unfurnished flat in Paris. Your notice period is…", "choices": ["un mois", "trois mois", "six mois"], "answer": "un mois", "right": "Correct! Paris is a «zone tendue», so it's reduced to one month.", "wrong": "Paris is a «zone tendue», so the notice for an unfurnished flat drops from three months to one month."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Last step: tenants must take out home insurance — «une assurance multirisque habitation» — and give the landlord the «attestation» every year."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous avons souscrit une assurance multirisque habitation.", "en": "We took out comprehensive home insurance."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: build your «dossier», check «CC» vs «HC», know the «dépôt de garantie», find a «garant», sign a careful «état des lieux», remember the «préavis», and get your «assurance habitation»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Ask in French (formal): Are the charges included?", "answer": "Les charges sont-elles comprises ?", "accept": ["les charges sont-elles comprises", "les charges sont-elles comprises?", "est-ce que les charges sont comprises", "est-ce que les charges sont comprises ?", "les charges sont comprises ?", "les charges sont incluses ?", "les charges sont-elles incluses"], "hint": "Inversion: les charges sont-elles…", "right": "Très bien ! The perfect question for a visit.", "wrong": "A good formal version: «Les charges sont-elles comprises ?» (or «Est-ce que les charges sont comprises ?»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Nous devons dresser l'état des lieux avant la remise des clés.»", "fr": "Nous devons dresser l'état des lieux avant la remise des clés."}]}, "42-2": {"stageId": "42-2", "title": "Banking, taxes, healthcare & la préfecture", "goal": "Handle French bank, tax, health and préfecture vocabulary and use polite formal phrasing in administrative situations.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Life in France runs on paperwork. Banking first: «le RIB» (relevé d'identité bancaire — your account details with IBAN and BIC), «un virement» (transfer), «un prélèvement» (direct debit), «un découvert» (overdraft), «la carte bancaire»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pourriez-vous me transmettre votre relevé d'identité bancaire (RIB) ?", "en": "Could you send me your bank account details (RIB)?", "note": "Employers, landlords and the CAF will all ask for your RIB."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'A monthly standing order' is…", "choices": ["un virement permanent mensuel", "un prélèvement unique mensuel", "un chèque permanent mensuel"], "answer": "un virement permanent mensuel", "right": "Exactly! «virement permanent» = a transfer that repeats automatically.", "wrong": "A standing order is «un virement permanent». A «prélèvement» is pulled by the creditor (direct debit)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Virement» = you push money out. «Prélèvement» = the creditor pulls it (direct debit). And «le prélèvement à la source» = income tax withheld directly from your salary."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le prélèvement à la source figure directement sur votre fiche de paie.", "en": "Withholding tax appears directly on your payslip."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Taxes: every spring you file «la déclaration de revenus» on impots.gouv.fr. In summer you receive «l'avis d'imposition» — a precious document used as proof of income everywhere."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A landlord asks for proof of your income. Which document?", "choices": ["l'avis d'imposition", "le RIB", "la carte Vitale"], "answer": "l'avis d'imposition", "right": "Parfait ! The tax notice proves your income.", "wrong": "Proof of income = «l'avis d'imposition» (plus payslips). The RIB is bank details; the «carte Vitale» is your health card."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Healthcare: «la Sécurité sociale» / «l'Assurance maladie» (site: ameli.fr) reimburses part of your care; you show your «carte Vitale». Declare «un médecin traitant» (primary care doctor) for better reimbursement. «La mutuelle» covers the rest."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La Sécurité sociale et la mutuelle prennent en charge l'intégralité des soins.", "en": "Social security and top-up insurance cover the full cost of care."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «la mutuelle» ?", "choices": ["top-up health insurance", "the state social security", "a pharmacy chain"], "answer": "top-up health insurance", "right": "Yes! It tops up what the Sécurité sociale reimburses — often provided by your employer.", "wrong": "«La mutuelle» is complementary (top-up) health insurance, covering what the Sécurité sociale doesn't."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mon médecin traitant m'a prescrit un arrêt de travail de trois jours.", "en": "My GP signed me off work for three days."}, {"t": "say", "text": "La préfecture: for your «titre de séjour» (residence permit) you book online, bring originals and copies, sometimes «un timbre fiscal», and you may receive «un récépissé» while waiting. Always required: «un justificatif de domicile» (proof of address)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il me faut un justificatif de domicile de moins de trois mois.", "en": "I need a proof of address dated within the last three months."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is a valid «justificatif de domicile» ?", "choices": ["une facture d'électricité récente", "une carte de bibliothèque", "un ticket de métro"], "answer": "une facture d'électricité récente", "right": "Exactly! A recent utility bill, a «quittance de loyer» or a home insurance certificate all work.", "wrong": "Accepted proofs of address include a recent electricity bill, a «quittance de loyer» (rent receipt) or an «attestation d'assurance habitation»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Admin French loves the conditional: «Je souhaiterais…», «Pourriez-vous…», «Auriez-vous l'amabilité de…». In emails, end with «Cordialement» or, very formally, «Je vous prie d'agréer, Madame, Monsieur, l'expression de mes salutations distinguées.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "At the bank counter, the most appropriate phrase is…", "choices": ["Je souhaiterais ouvrir un compte.", "Je veux un compte, là.", "Donnez-moi un compte."], "answer": "Je souhaiterais ouvrir un compte.", "right": "Parfait ! Polite conditional — doors will open.", "wrong": "In administrative settings, use the polite conditional: «Je souhaiterais ouvrir un compte.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: RIB, virement vs prélèvement, prélèvement à la source, avis d'imposition, carte Vitale, médecin traitant, mutuelle, justificatif de domicile — and always the polite conditional."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I would like to set up a monthly standing order.", "answer": "Je souhaiterais mettre en place un virement permanent mensuel.", "accept": ["je souhaiterais mettre en place un virement permanent mensuel", "je voudrais mettre en place un virement permanent mensuel", "je souhaiterais mettre en place un virement mensuel permanent", "je voudrais mettre en place un virement mensuel permanent", "je souhaiterais mettre en place un virement mensuel", "je voudrais mettre en place un virement mensuel"], "hint": "Je souhaiterais mettre en place…", "right": "Excellent ! Polite and precise.", "wrong": "A natural version: «Je souhaiterais mettre en place un virement permanent mensuel.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Pourriez-vous me transmettre votre RIB, s'il vous plaît ?»", "fr": "Pourriez-vous me transmettre votre RIB, s'il vous plaît ?"}]}, "43-1": {"stageId": "43-1", "title": "Executive Interviews, Pitching & Salary Negotiation", "goal": "Present your career path, results and salary expectations with the polished vocabulary French recruiters expect at executive level.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In a French executive interview you're judged on more than your experience: you're judged on how you phrase it. A few precise verbs instantly signal 'cadre dirigeant': «piloter», «encadrer», «mener à bien», «dégager», «s'inscrire dans»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce poste s'inscrit parfaitement dans la continuité de mon parcours.", "en": "This role fits perfectly with the continuity of my career path.", "note": "«parcours» is the elegant word for your career story."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In startups you'll hear franglais like «J'ai managé une team». In a boardroom interview, «J'ai encadré une équipe» or «J'ai piloté le projet» sounds far more polished."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai piloté une équipe pluridisciplinaire de quinze ingénieurs.", "en": "I led a cross-functional team of fifteen engineers."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence best says 'I successfully led the project' in an executive interview?", "choices": ["J'ai mené à bien le projet.", "J'ai fait le projet.", "J'ai conduit le projet à la fin."], "answer": "J'ai mené à bien le projet.", "right": "Exactly! «Mener à bien» = to carry through to a successful end. Precise and elegant.", "wrong": "«Faire» is too vague and «conduire à la fin» isn't idiomatic. The set phrase is «mener à bien»: «J'ai mené à bien le projet.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the numbers. French executives don't 'make' profit, they «dégagent» it: «dégager une marge», «dégager un bénéfice». Other must-know terms: «le chiffre d'affaires» (revenue), «le retour sur investissement» (ROI), «pérenne» (sustainable, lasting)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous avons dégagé une marge opérationnelle supérieure aux prévisions.", "en": "We generated an operating margin exceeding forecasts."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous avons ___ un bénéfice record cette année.»", "choices": ["dégagé", "fabriqué", "produit"], "answer": "dégagé", "right": "Parfait! «Dégager un bénéfice / une marge» is the standard business collocation.", "wrong": "You don't 'manufacture' profit in French. The natural collocation is «dégager un bénéfice» (or «réaliser un bénéfice»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Cette stratégie présente un retour sur investissement particulièrement pérenne.", "en": "This strategy offers a particularly sustainable return on investment."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Salary talk: in France you always speak in «brut annuel» (gross per year), often with «fixe + variable». Use «je vise» or «mes prétentions salariales se situent autour de...». Never «je veux», which sounds demanding."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Compte tenu de mon expertise, je vise une rémunération brute annuelle de 85 000 euros.", "en": "Given my expertise, I am targeting a gross annual compensation of 85,000 euros."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is the most professional way to state your salary expectations?", "choices": ["Je veux 80 000 euros.", "Mes prétentions salariales se situent autour de 80 000 euros brut par an.", "Donnez-moi 80 000 euros net."], "answer": "Mes prétentions salariales se situent autour de 80 000 euros brut par an.", "right": "Très bien! Indirect, precise, and in gross annual terms, exactly what a French recruiter expects.", "wrong": "«Je veux» and «Donnez-moi» sound blunt, and French salaries are discussed in gross, not net. Say: «Mes prétentions salariales se situent autour de 80 000 euros brut par an.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis opérationnel immédiatement et force de proposition.", "en": "I can hit the ground running, and I'm proactive with ideas.", "note": "«force de proposition» never changes, even for a woman: «Elle est force de proposition.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A woman wants to say she can start right away and is proactive. Which is correct?", "choices": ["Je suis opérationnelle immédiatement et force de proposition.", "Je suis opérationnel immédiatement et forte de proposition.", "Je suis opérationnelle immédiatement et une force de propositions."], "answer": "Je suis opérationnelle immédiatement et force de proposition.", "right": "Bravo! «Opérationnelle» agrees with her, but «force de proposition» is a fixed, invariable expression.", "wrong": "The adjective agrees («opérationnelle»), but «force de proposition» is fixed: no article, no agreement, no plural."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «s'inscrire dans la continuité de mon parcours», «piloter / encadrer une équipe», «dégager une marge», «un ROI pérenne», «je vise une rémunération brute annuelle de...», «opérationnel(le) et force de proposition»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: 'This role fits perfectly with the continuity of my career path.'", "answer": "Ce poste s'inscrit parfaitement dans la continuité de mon parcours.", "accept": ["ce poste s'inscrit parfaitement dans la continuité de mon parcours", "ce poste s’inscrit parfaitement dans la continuité de mon parcours", "ce poste s'inscrit parfaitement dans mon parcours"], "hint": "Ce poste s'inscrit ... dans la continuité de ...", "right": "Impeccable, that's a textbook opening line for a French interview.", "wrong": "The expression is «s'inscrire dans la continuité de»: «Ce poste s'inscrit parfaitement dans la continuité de mon parcours.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with confidence: «Compte tenu de mon expertise, je vise une rémunération brute annuelle de 85 000 euros.»", "fr": "Compte tenu de mon expertise, je vise une rémunération brute annuelle de 85 000 euros."}]}, "43-2": {"stageId": "43-2", "title": "Chairing Boardroom Meetings, Diplomatic Pushback & Formal Emails", "goal": "Run a meeting, disagree diplomatically, and write formal business emails with the right French conventions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French meetings have their own vocabulary: «l'ordre du jour» (agenda), «le tour de table» (round of input), «le compte rendu» (minutes). And French corporate diplomacy relies heavily on the conditional and on «se permettre»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Faisons un tour de table rapide avant de passer à l'ordre du jour.", "en": "Let's go around the table quickly before moving on to the agenda."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the 'agenda' of a meeting in French?", "choices": ["l'agenda", "l'ordre du jour", "le calendrier"], "answer": "l'ordre du jour", "right": "Yes! «L'ordre du jour» = the list of items to discuss.", "wrong": "False friend! «Un agenda» is your diary/planner. A meeting's agenda is «l'ordre du jour»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To steer without sounding bossy, use impersonal conditionals: «Il conviendrait de...» (it would be advisable to), «Il serait opportun de...». They're polite orders."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il conviendrait de hiérarchiser nos priorités avant le lancement officiel.", "en": "It would be advisable to prioritise our objectives before the official launch."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Pushback: never «Vous avez tort» in a boardroom. Natives say «Je me permets de nuancer ce point», «Je partage votre analyse ; toutefois...», or «Si je puis me permettre...»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je me permets de nuancer ce point au vu des derniers chiffres.", "en": "Allow me to qualify this point in light of the latest figures."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You disagree with the CEO. Which is the most diplomatic?", "choices": ["C'est faux.", "Je ne suis pas d'accord du tout.", "Si je puis me permettre, ce point mérite d'être nuancé."], "answer": "Si je puis me permettre, ce point mérite d'être nuancé.", "right": "Parfait! You disagree while keeping everyone's face intact, very French corporate.", "wrong": "The first two are confrontational. Soften: «Si je puis me permettre, ce point mérite d'être nuancé.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Puis» is an old form of «peux». It survives only in fixed phrases like «si je puis me permettre» and in inverted questions: «Puis-je vous interrompre ?» Using it marks you as very polished."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous devons lever les freins qui ralentissent le déploiement.", "en": "We must remove the obstacles slowing down the rollout.", "note": "«lever les freins» = to remove obstacles; very common corporate jargon."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Emails: request with «Pourriez-vous...», set deadlines with «d'ici» (by), and close with «Je reste à votre disposition pour tout complément d'information.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'by the end of the day' (deadline)?", "choices": ["d'ici la fin de la journée", "par la fin du jour", "jusqu'à la fin de la journée"], "answer": "d'ici la fin de la journée", "right": "Exactly! «D'ici» marks a deadline.", "wrong": "«Jusqu'à» means 'until' (a duration), and «par la fin du jour» is an anglicism. For a deadline: «d'ici la fin de la journée»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je vous prie d'agréer, Madame, Monsieur, l'expression de mes salutations distinguées.", "en": "Yours faithfully.", "note": "The full formula for formal letters and official emails."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Everyday emails between colleagues end with «Cordialement» or «Bien cordialement». Avoid «Sincèrement»: it's a calque of 'Sincerely' and sounds odd in French."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which closing fits a quick email to a colleague?", "choices": ["Bien cordialement,", "Sincèrement,", "Je vous prie d'agréer l'expression de mes salutations distinguées."], "answer": "Bien cordialement,", "right": "Oui! Neutral, warm and professional.", "wrong": "The full formula is too solemn for a colleague and «Sincèrement» is an anglicism. Use «Bien cordialement» or «Cordialement»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «l'ordre du jour», «un tour de table», «il conviendrait de...», «je me permets de nuancer...», «si je puis me permettre», «lever les freins», «d'ici la fin de la journée», «Bien cordialement»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (vous): 'Could you get back to me by the end of the day?'", "answer": "Pourriez-vous me faire un retour d'ici la fin de la journée ?", "accept": ["pourriez-vous me faire un retour d'ici la fin de la journée", "pourriez-vous me faire un retour d’ici la fin de la journée", "pourriez-vous me faire un retour d'ici la fin de journée", "pourriez-vous me répondre d'ici la fin de la journée", "pourriez-vous me faire un retour d'ici la fin de la journée?"], "hint": "Pourriez-vous me faire un retour d'ici ...", "right": "Excellent, polite, precise and natural.", "wrong": "Use «faire un retour» (give feedback) and «d'ici» for the deadline: «Pourriez-vous me faire un retour d'ici la fin de la journée ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, calmly: «Je me permets de nuancer ce point au vu des derniers chiffres.»", "fr": "Je me permets de nuancer ce point au vu des derniers chiffres."}]}, "44-1": {"stageId": "44-1", "title": "Mastering Register Shifting (Familier vs Courant vs Soutenu)", "goal": "Recognise and switch between familier, courant and soutenu French in vocabulary, negation and question forms.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French has three main registers: «familier» (friends, street), «courant» (standard, everyday) and «soutenu» (formal writing, speeches, literature). Same idea, three outfits. A native shifts between them constantly, and so must you."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'en sais rien. / Je n'en ai aucune idée. / Je l'ignore.", "en": "Dunno. / I have no idea. / I am unaware of it.", "note": "familier → courant → soutenu"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Trap: «ignorer» in soutenu French means 'not to know', not 'to ignore'. «J'ignore pourquoi il est parti» = I don't know why he left."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ignore la réponse.» means:", "choices": ["I'm ignoring the answer.", "I don't know the answer.", "I forgot the answer."], "answer": "I don't know the answer.", "right": "Exactement! «Ignorer» = not to know. Elegant and very common in writing.", "wrong": "«Ignorer qqch» primarily means not to know it: «J'ignore la réponse» = I don't know the answer."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Vocabulary ladders: «bouquin → livre → ouvrage», «bagnole → voiture → véhicule», «bosser → travailler → œuvrer», «claquer son fric → dépenser tout son argent → dilapider son patrimoine»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a dilapidé l'intégralité de son patrimoine.", "en": "He squandered his entire fortune.", "note": "Familier equivalent: «Il a claqué tout son fric.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence is soutenu?", "choices": ["Ce bouquin m'a scotché.", "Ce livre m'a beaucoup plu.", "Cet ouvrage m'a profondément captivé."], "answer": "Cet ouvrage m'a profondément captivé.", "right": "Oui! «Ouvrage» + «profondément captivé» = high register.", "wrong": "«Bouquin / scotché» is familier, «livre / plu» is courant. Soutenu: «Cet ouvrage m'a profondément captivé.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Grammar also marks register. Soutenu uses: full negation with «ne», inversion in questions, the expletive «ne» after verbs of fear («Je crains qu'il ne soit trop tard»), and «ne» alone with «savoir, pouvoir, oser, cesser» («Je ne saurais vous dire»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je crains que cette hypothèse ne soit erronée.", "en": "I fear that this hypothesis may be wrong.", "note": "This «ne» is explétif: it is NOT a negation."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «Je crains que cette hypothèse ne soit erronée», what does the speaker fear?", "choices": ["That the hypothesis IS wrong", "That the hypothesis is NOT wrong", "Nothing, the sentence is ungrammatical"], "answer": "That the hypothesis IS wrong", "right": "Bravo! The expletive «ne» adds formality, not negation.", "wrong": "After «craindre que», «avoir peur que», «avant que», the lone «ne» is explétif and has no negative meaning. The speaker fears the hypothesis is wrong."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Questions in three registers: «Tu viens quand ?» (familier) → «Quand est-ce que tu viens ?» (courant) → «Quand viendrez-vous ?» (soutenu)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which question is soutenu?", "choices": ["Vous partez où ?", "Où est-ce que vous partez ?", "Où partez-vous ?"], "answer": "Où partez-vous ?", "right": "Exactement! Subject-verb inversion is the hallmark of the soutenu question.", "wrong": "Question word at the end = familier; «est-ce que» = courant; inversion = soutenu: «Où partez-vous ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous sommes confrontés à un dilemme épineux.", "en": "We are faced with a thorny dilemma.", "note": "Familier: «On est dans la galère.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a fait preuve d'une perspicacité remarquable.", "en": "He showed remarkable insight.", "note": "Courant: «Il a été très fin.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The classic advanced-learner mistake is mixing registers: «Je l'ignore, mec» sounds comical. And using soutenu with friends makes you sound «précieux» (pompous). Match the register to the situation."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: three registers, three tools: vocabulary (bouquin / livre / ouvrage), negation (j'sais pas / je ne sais pas / je l'ignore) and questions (intonation / est-ce que / inversion)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Rewrite «Je sais pas.» in the soutenu register (two words, using «ignorer»).", "answer": "Je l'ignore.", "accept": ["je l'ignore", "je l’ignore", "Je l’ignore."], "hint": "Je l'...", "right": "Superbe, that's exactly how an academic or a diplomat would say it.", "wrong": "With «ignorer» + the pronoun «le»: «Je l'ignore.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it with a soutenu tone: «Je crains que cette hypothèse ne soit erronée.»", "fr": "Je crains que cette hypothèse ne soit erronée."}]}, "44-2": {"stageId": "44-2", "title": "Precision Verbs & Nominalization (Éviter 'Faire' et 'Avoir')", "goal": "Replace vague verbs like faire, avoir and dire with precise verbs, and condense clauses through nominalization.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "At C1–C2, examiners hunt for «verbes passe-partout»: «faire, avoir, être, dire, mettre, il y a». Replacing them with precise verbs instantly upgrades your French. The second weapon is nominalization: turning a clause into a dense noun phrase."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Cette décision a suscité une vive polémique.", "en": "This decision sparked a heated controversy.", "note": "Instead of «a fait une grosse polémique»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Upgrade the verb: «Ce film a ___ de nombreuses réactions.»", "choices": ["suscité", "mis", "eu fait"], "answer": "suscité", "right": "Parfait! «Susciter» = to arouse, provoke (reactions, interest, debate).", "wrong": "The precise verb is «susciter»: «Ce film a suscité de nombreuses réactions.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Useful swaps: «avoir du succès → remporter un succès», «avoir une bonne réputation → jouir d'une notoriété», «faire un projet → élaborer un projet», «dire de faire → préconiser», «il y a des problèmes → des problèmes se posent»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il jouit d'une notoriété incontestable dans son domaine.", "en": "He enjoys undeniable renown in his field."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Jouir de» goes only with positive things: «jouir d'une bonne santé, d'un privilège». Never «jouir d'une maladie». And always keep «de + nom»: «jouir» on its own has a sexual meaning!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les experts préconisent une refonte complète du système.", "en": "The experts recommend a complete overhaul of the system."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Nous devons pallier ___ insuffisances du dispositif.»", "choices": ["les", "aux", "à les"], "answer": "les", "right": "Excellent! «Pallier» is transitive: «pallier quelque chose».", "wrong": "Purists insist that «pallier» takes a direct object: «pallier les insuffisances». «Pallier à» is very common but marked as incorrect in careful writing."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous devons pallier les insuffisances du dispositif actuel.", "en": "We must remedy the shortcomings of the current system."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Cette démarche s'est avérée particulièrement fructueuse.", "en": "This approach proved particularly fruitful.", "note": "«s'avérer» = to prove to be. Avoid «s'avérer vrai» (pleonasm); say «se révéler exact»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence has the best written style?", "choices": ["Cette méthode s'est avérée efficace.", "Cette méthode a été efficace finalement.", "Cette méthode, elle a marché."], "answer": "Cette méthode s'est avérée efficace.", "right": "Oui! Precise verb, compact, formal.", "wrong": "The others are acceptable in speech but flat or familier. Written style: «Cette méthode s'est avérée efficace.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Nominalization: «Les prix ont augmenté, ce qui inquiète les ménages» → «La hausse des prix inquiète les ménages.» Denser and more elegant, typical of the press, headlines and the DALF synthesis."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Nominalize «Les salaires ont baissé.»", "choices": ["La baisse des salaires", "Le baissement des salaires", "La baissure des salaires"], "answer": "La baisse des salaires", "right": "Bravo! «Baisser → la baisse», just as «hausser → la hausse».", "wrong": "The noun is «la baisse»: «la baisse des salaires». «Baissement» and «baissure» don't exist."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Common suffixes: «-ment» (licencier → licenciement), «-age» (démarrer → démarrage), «-tion» (réduire → réduction), «-ure» (ouvrir → ouverture), or no suffix (hausser → hausse). There's no fixed rule, so check the dictionary."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «susciter», «préconiser», «pallier qqch», «jouir de», «s'avérer», and nominalize: «la hausse, la baisse, la refonte, l'ouverture»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: 'This decision sparked a heated controversy.'", "answer": "Cette décision a suscité une vive polémique.", "accept": ["cette décision a suscité une vive polémique", "cette décision a suscité une vive controverse", "cette décision a suscité une polémique", "Cette décision a suscité une vive controverse."], "hint": "Use «susciter» and «vive».", "right": "Impeccable, precise verb and the right collocation («vive polémique»).", "wrong": "Avoid «faire»: «Cette décision a suscité une vive polémique.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Les experts préconisent une refonte complète du système.»", "fr": "Les experts préconisent une refonte complète du système."}]}, "45-1": {"stageId": "45-1", "title": "Concession, Restriction & High-Register Subjunctive", "goal": "Use bien que, quoique, à supposer que, pour peu que and non que with the subjunctive, and keep «si» clauses free of the conditional.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Nuance is the heart of C2 French. Concession: «bien que, quoique, encore que». Hypothesis: «à supposer que, pour peu que, pourvu que». Restriction: «ce n'est pas que..., non que...». They all trigger the «subjonctif»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Bien que cette mesure soit impopulaire, elle demeure indispensable.", "en": "Although this measure is unpopular, it remains indispensable."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Bien que le projet ___ coûteux, nous le maintenons.»", "choices": ["est", "soit", "serait"], "answer": "soit", "right": "Parfait! «Bien que» always takes the subjunctive.", "wrong": "«Bien que» + subjonctif, always: «Bien que le projet soit coûteux...»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "You'll hear natives say «bien qu'il est» in casual speech, but in writing it's penalised. Also avoid «malgré que» in formal French: use «bien que + subjonctif» or «malgré + nom»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quoique les résultats paraissent encourageants, la prudence reste de mise.", "en": "Even though the results look encouraging, caution remains in order.", "note": "«quoique» (one word) = bien que."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ vous fassiez, faites-le bien.» (Whatever you do...)", "choices": ["Quoique", "Quoi que", "Bien que"], "answer": "Quoi que", "right": "Bravo! «Quoi que» in two words = whatever.", "wrong": "«Quoique» (one word) = although. 'Whatever' is «quoi que» in two words: «Quoi que vous fassiez...»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "À supposer que le projet aboutisse, quels en seraient les coûts ?", "en": "Assuming the project succeeds, what would its costs be?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pour peu qu'on lui fasse confiance, il accomplit des merveilles.", "en": "Provided you trust him even a little, he works wonders."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «pour peu que» mean?", "choices": ["provided that, even minimally", "so that", "although"], "answer": "provided that, even minimally", "right": "Exactement! A tiny condition is enough to trigger the result.", "wrong": "«Pour peu que» + subjonctif = 'if only / provided that, even slightly'. 'So that' is «pour que»; 'although' is «bien que»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To dismiss a false reason before giving the real one: «Ce n'est pas que + subjonctif, mais...» (courant) or «Non que + subjonctif» (soutenu)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce n'est pas que je veuille m'y opposer, mais le délai est irréaliste.", "en": "It's not that I want to oppose it, but the deadline is unrealistic."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Non qu'il ___ incompétent, mais il manque d'expérience.»", "choices": ["est", "soit", "était"], "answer": "soit", "right": "Très bien! The denied reason goes into the subjunctive.", "wrong": "«Non que» presents a rejected cause, so it takes the subjunctive: «Non qu'il soit incompétent...»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Contrast: a «si» hypothesis never takes the conditional or the subjunctive. Past unreal: «si + plus-que-parfait, conditionnel passé». In literary French you may meet «Si j'eusse su...» or «Eussé-je su...»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Si j'avais eu connaissance de ces éléments, j'aurais agi différemment.", "en": "Had I known about these elements, I would have acted differently."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence is correct?", "choices": ["Si j'aurais su, je serais venu.", "Si j'avais su, je serais venu.", "Si je sache, je serais venu."], "answer": "Si j'avais su, je serais venu.", "right": "Parfait! Remember the rhyme: «Les si n'aiment pas les -rais».", "wrong": "No conditional after «si»: «Si j'avais su, je serais venu.» French kids learn: «Les si n'aiment pas les -rais»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «bien que / quoique / à supposer que / pour peu que / non que» + subjonctif; «quoique» ≠ «quoi que»; «si» + imparfait or plus-que-parfait, never «-rais»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: 'Although this measure is unpopular, it remains indispensable.'", "answer": "Bien que cette mesure soit impopulaire, elle demeure indispensable.", "accept": ["bien que cette mesure soit impopulaire, elle demeure indispensable", "bien que cette mesure soit impopulaire, elle reste indispensable", "quoique cette mesure soit impopulaire, elle demeure indispensable", "quoique cette mesure soit impopulaire, elle reste indispensable"], "hint": "Bien que + subjonctif of «être»...", "right": "Impeccable, subjonctif and elegant verb «demeurer».", "wrong": "«Bien que» + «soit»: «Bien que cette mesure soit impopulaire, elle demeure indispensable.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Pour peu qu'on lui fasse confiance, il accomplit des merveilles.»", "fr": "Pour peu qu'on lui fasse confiance, il accomplit des merveilles."}]}, "45-2": {"stageId": "45-2", "title": "Subtle Logical Links (Or, En revanche, D'autant plus que)", "goal": "Use or, en revanche, d'autant plus... que, force est de and nonobstant with precise logical meaning in formal French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "At C2, connectors must be logically exact. Start with «or»: it is NOT English 'or' (that's «ou»). «Or» introduces a new fact that changes the reasoning, something like 'yet' or 'now'. Classic syllogism: «Tous les hommes sont mortels ; or Socrate est un homme ; donc Socrate est mortel.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il prétendait être absent ; or, deux témoins l'ont aperçu.", "en": "He claimed to be absent; yet two witnesses saw him."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «or» mean in French argumentation?", "choices": ["or (an alternative)", "yet / now (a decisive new fact)", "therefore"], "answer": "yet / now (a decisive new fact)", "right": "Exactement! «Or» brings in the fact that tips the argument.", "wrong": "'Or' (alternative) is «ou», 'therefore' is «donc». «Or» introduces a new decisive fact: 'yet / now'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Another trap in that example: «prétendre» = to claim, NOT to pretend. 'To pretend' is «faire semblant»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«En revanche» and «par contre» both mean 'on the other hand'. «Par contre» has long been criticised by purists. It's fine in speech, but in a DALF essay prefer «en revanche»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce modèle est onéreux ; en revanche, sa fiabilité est absolue.", "en": "This model is expensive; on the other hand, its reliability is total."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a formal essay, which connector is best?", "choices": ["par contre", "en revanche", "au contraire de ça"], "answer": "en revanche", "right": "Oui! «En revanche» is the safe, elegant choice in writing.", "wrong": "«Par contre» is frowned upon in careful writing and «au contraire de ça» is clumsy. Use «en revanche»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«D'autant plus... que» = all the more... because. Structure: «X est d'autant plus + adjectif + que + reason». Short form: «d'autant que» = especially since."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce succès est d'autant plus remarquable que le contexte était hostile.", "en": "This success is all the more remarkable given that the context was hostile."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je suis ___ déçu qu'il m'avait promis de venir.»", "choices": ["d'autant plus", "autant plus", "tellement plus"], "answer": "d'autant plus", "right": "Parfait! «D'autant plus... que» links the intensity to its cause.", "wrong": "The fixed structure is «d'autant plus... que»: «Je suis d'autant plus déçu qu'il m'avait promis de venir.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Force est d'admettre que les résultats dépassent nos espérances.", "en": "One has to admit that the results exceed our expectations.", "note": "«Force est de + infinitif», no article: never «La force est de»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nonobstant les difficultés initiales, le contrat a été honoré.", "en": "Notwithstanding the initial difficulties, the contract was honoured.", "note": "Legal and very formal; «en dépit de» or «malgré» are more natural in essays."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is closest in meaning to «nonobstant»?", "choices": ["en dépit de", "grâce à", "à cause de"], "answer": "en dépit de", "right": "Bravo! «Nonobstant» = «en dépit de» = «malgré».", "wrong": "«Nonobstant» means despite: «en dépit de», «malgré». «Grâce à» = thanks to; «à cause de» = because of."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Punctuation: «or» usually follows a semicolon or a full stop and is followed by a comma; «en revanche» sits between commas or after a semicolon."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «or» (decisive new fact), «en revanche» (contrast, formal), «d'autant plus... que» (stronger because), «force est de» (one has no choice but to), «nonobstant» (despite)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: 'This success is all the more remarkable given that the context was hostile.'", "answer": "Ce succès est d'autant plus remarquable que le contexte était hostile.", "accept": ["ce succès est d'autant plus remarquable que le contexte était hostile", "ce succès est d’autant plus remarquable que le contexte était hostile", "ce succès est d'autant plus remarquable que le contexte était défavorable"], "hint": "... d'autant plus remarquable que ...", "right": "Excellent, a perfectly built «d'autant plus... que».", "wrong": "«Ce succès est d'autant plus remarquable que le contexte était hostile.» Note: «que», not «parce que»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with a pause before «or»: «Il prétendait être absent ; or, deux témoins l'ont aperçu.»", "fr": "Il prétendait être absent ; or, deux témoins l'ont aperçu."}]}, "46-1": {"stageId": "46-1", "title": "Supreme DALF C2 Written Synthesis & Dialectical Markers", "goal": "Structure a DALF C2 synthesis and essay with precise dialectical markers and high-level vocabulary.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The DALF C2 written exam asks you to synthesise a dossier and then argue. The French structure is sacred: introduction (accroche, problématique, annonce du plan) → two or three parts → conclusion (bilan + ouverture)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In the synthesis part: no personal opinion and no copied sentences. You reformulate the documents' ideas with impersonal phrases like «il apparaît que» and «force est de constater que»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Dialectical markers. To establish a fact: «Force est de constater que», «Il est indéniable que». To counter: «Il n'en demeure pas moins que», «Toutefois». To conclude: «En définitive», «Au terme de cette analyse»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Force est de constater que ce phénomène prend une ampleur inédite.", "en": "One cannot help but observe that this phenomenon is reaching an unprecedented scale."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il n'en demeure pas moins que des interrogations éthiques subsistent.", "en": "The fact remains that ethical questions persist."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which marker introduces a counter-argument after a concession?", "choices": ["Il n'en demeure pas moins que", "En définitive", "À l'instar de"], "answer": "Il n'en demeure pas moins que", "right": "Parfait! 'Nevertheless, the fact remains that...'", "wrong": "«En définitive» concludes and «à l'instar de» compares. The counter-point marker is «Il n'en demeure pas moins que»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "À l'instar de ses prédécesseurs, l'auteur remet en cause ce paradigme.", "en": "Like his predecessors, the author challenges this paradigm."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«À l'instar de» means:", "choices": ["like / following the example of", "unlike", "instead of"], "answer": "like / following the example of", "right": "Oui! An elegant replacement for «comme».", "wrong": "«À l'instar de» = like, following the example of. 'Unlike' = «contrairement à»; 'instead of' = «au lieu de»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "En dépit des aléas conjoncturels, la tendance structurelle demeure haussière.", "en": "Despite short-term fluctuations, the underlying trend remains upward.", "note": "«conjoncturel» = short-term, circumstantial; «structurel» = deep, long-term."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Unemployment caused by a temporary crisis is called:", "choices": ["conjoncturel", "structurel", "pérenne"], "answer": "conjoncturel", "right": "Exactement! Tied to the current economic situation, «la conjoncture».", "wrong": "Temporary and circumstantial = «conjoncturel». «Structurel» = rooted in the system; «pérenne» = lasting."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Cette réforme constitue une véritable arme à double tranchant.", "en": "This reform is a real double-edged sword."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "En définitive, il appartient aux pouvoirs publics de trancher cette question.", "en": "Ultimately, it is up to the public authorities to settle this question.", "note": "«il appartient à qqn de + infinitif» = it is up to someone to."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'It is up to citizens to decide.'", "choices": ["Il appartient aux citoyens de décider.", "Il appartient les citoyens à décider.", "Ça appartient aux citoyens décider."], "answer": "Il appartient aux citoyens de décider.", "right": "Bravo! «Il appartient à + personne + de + infinitif».", "wrong": "Structure: «Il appartient à qqn de faire qqch»: «Il appartient aux citoyens de décider.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Vary your verbs: «constituer, représenter, s'avérer, demeurer, se révéler» instead of «être». Examiners reward lexical range."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Force est de constater que» (fact), «Il n'en demeure pas moins que» (counter), «À l'instar de» (comparison), «conjoncturel / structurel», «En définitive, il appartient à... de» (conclusion)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French using «Il n'en demeure pas moins que»: 'The fact remains that ethical questions persist.'", "answer": "Il n'en demeure pas moins que des interrogations éthiques subsistent.", "accept": ["il n'en demeure pas moins que des interrogations éthiques subsistent", "il n’en demeure pas moins que des interrogations éthiques subsistent", "il n'en demeure pas moins que des questions éthiques subsistent", "il n'en demeure pas moins que des questions éthiques persistent", "il n'en demeure pas moins que des interrogations éthiques persistent"], "hint": "... des interrogations éthiques subsistent.", "right": "Superbe, a perfect C2 counter-argument opener.", "wrong": "«Il n'en demeure pas moins que des interrogations éthiques subsistent.» (Indicative after this marker.)"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Force est de constater que ce phénomène prend une ampleur inédite.»", "fr": "Force est de constater que ce phénomène prend une ampleur inédite."}]}, "46-2": {"stageId": "46-2", "title": "Oral Grand Jury Defense: Impromptu Debate & Rhetorical Mastery", "goal": "Handle a jury's objections orally: acknowledge, nuance, reframe false dilemmas and conclude with an opening.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In the DALF C2 oral, you present a reasoned point of view and then defend it against the jury. The winning sequence: acknowledge → nuance → reframe → open up."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Step 1: welcome the objection: «Votre remarque est tout à fait pertinente», «C'est une objection de poids». Step 2: nuance it: «toutefois, elle appelle une précision»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Votre remarque est tout à fait pertinente, toutefois elle appelle une précision.", "en": "Your remark is entirely relevant; however, it calls for a clarification."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A jury member challenges your argument. Best opening?", "choices": ["Non, vous n'avez pas compris.", "C'est une objection de poids, qui mérite qu'on s'y attarde.", "Bon, ben, je sais pas trop."], "answer": "C'est une objection de poids, qui mérite qu'on s'y attarde.", "right": "Excellent! You value the objection and buy thinking time elegantly.", "wrong": "Never contradict the jury bluntly or slip into familier. Acknowledge first: «C'est une objection de poids, qui mérite qu'on s'y attarde.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Buy time with class: «Si je comprends bien votre question...», «Permettez-moi de reformuler...», «C'est une question délicate...». Better than silence or a long «euh»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On ne saurait réduire cette problématique à sa seule dimension économique.", "en": "This issue cannot be reduced to its economic dimension alone.", "note": "«ne saurait» = cannot (soutenu, «ne» without «pas»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«On ne saurait accepter cela» means:", "choices": ["We could not accept that.", "We don't know how to accept that.", "We would know how to accept that."], "answer": "We could not accept that.", "right": "Parfait! «Ne saurait» is a formal, firm 'cannot'.", "wrong": "In soutenu French, «ne saurait + infinitif» = cannot, could not. It's about impossibility, not knowledge."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il s'agit là d'un faux dilemme qu'il convient de dépasser.", "en": "This is a false dilemma that we should move beyond."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The 'false dilemma' move: refuse the either/or the jury offers and show that the two sides complete each other. That's the French dialectical «synthèse»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Loin de s'opposer, ces deux approches se révèlent complémentaires.", "en": "Far from opposing each other, these two approaches prove complementary."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which structure is correct?", "choices": ["Loin de s'opposer, ...", "Loin que s'opposer, ...", "Loin d'opposant, ..."], "answer": "Loin de s'opposer, ...", "right": "Bravo! «Loin de + infinitif».", "wrong": "The structure is «loin de + infinitif»: «Loin de s'opposer, ces approches se complètent.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Finish with a conclusion that opens up: «En guise de conclusion...», «Pour conclure...», then «je souhaiterais ouvrir la réflexion sur...»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "En guise de conclusion, je souhaiterais ouvrir la réflexion sur l'avenir.", "en": "By way of conclusion, I would like to broaden the discussion to the future."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In French academic debate, «la problématique» means:", "choices": ["the central question a topic raises", "a small technical bug", "something that is merely problematic"], "answer": "the central question a topic raises", "right": "Oui! «La problématique» is the guiding question of your whole argument.", "wrong": "«Une problématique» is the central question or issue your argument explores, a key concept in French essays and orals."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Votre remarque est tout à fait pertinente, toutefois...», «On ne saurait...», «Il s'agit d'un faux dilemme», «Loin de s'opposer...», «En guise de conclusion, je souhaiterais ouvrir la réflexion sur...»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: 'Far from opposing each other, these two approaches prove complementary.'", "answer": "Loin de s'opposer, ces deux approches se révèlent complémentaires.", "accept": ["loin de s'opposer, ces deux approches se révèlent complémentaires", "loin de s’opposer, ces deux approches se révèlent complémentaires", "loin de s'opposer, ces deux approches s'avèrent complémentaires", "loin de s'opposer, ces deux approches sont complémentaires"], "hint": "Loin de s'opposer, ... se révèlent ...", "right": "Magnifique, a classic C2 rhetorical move.", "wrong": "«Loin de s'opposer, ces deux approches se révèlent complémentaires.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it calmly and confidently, as if facing the jury: «Votre remarque est tout à fait pertinente, toutefois elle appelle une précision.»", "fr": "Votre remarque est tout à fait pertinente, toutefois elle appelle une précision."}]}, "47-1": {"stageId": "47-1", "title": "Le « ne » qui disparaît", "goal": "Understand and use the everyday spoken negation without «ne», and know when to keep it.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Here's a big secret: in everyday spoken French, natives drop the «ne» almost all the time. «Je ne sais pas» becomes «Je sais pas». The «pas» carries the negation on its own."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je sais pas.", "en": "I don't know."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est pas grave.", "en": "It's no big deal."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Dropping «ne» isn't 'wrong'. It's normal spoken French, even for teachers and politicians in relaxed moments. But keep the «ne» in writing (emails, essays) and in formal situations like job interviews."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How would a French friend most naturally say 'I don't have time' in casual speech?", "choices": ["J'ai pas le temps.", "Je ai pas le temps.", "J'ai ne pas le temps."], "answer": "J'ai pas le temps.", "right": "Exactly! «Je n'ai pas» → «J'ai pas». «Je» still elides before the vowel.", "wrong": "Drop the «ne» but keep the elision: «J'ai pas le temps.» «Je ai» is never possible."}, {"t": "say", "text": "It works with every negation: «pas, jamais, rien, plus, personne». «On sait jamais», «J'ai rien vu», «Y a personne», «J'en peux plus»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On sait jamais.", "en": "You never know."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Casual: 'I didn't see anything.'", "choices": ["J'ai rien vu.", "J'ai vu rien.", "Je vu rien."], "answer": "J'ai rien vu.", "right": "Parfait! «Rien» sits before the past participle, just like «pas».", "wrong": "«Rien» goes between the auxiliary and the participle: «J'ai rien vu.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Listening trap: «On n'a pas faim» and «On a pas faim» sound exactly the same! The liaison «on‿a» hides the «n'». So rely on the «pas»: if you hear «pas», it's negative."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je peux pas ce soir.", "en": "I can't tonight."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "T'inquiète pas.", "en": "Don't worry.", "note": "From «Ne t'inquiète pas». Natives even shorten it to «T'inquiète !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You hear «On a pas faim». It means:", "choices": ["We're not hungry.", "We're hungry.", "Are we hungry?"], "answer": "We're not hungry.", "right": "Oui! The «pas» is all you need to hear.", "wrong": "Even without «ne», «pas» makes it negative: «On a pas faim» = We're not hungry."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Y a pas de souci.", "en": "No problem.", "note": "From «Il n'y a pas de souci»: «il» and «ne» both disappear."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a formal email to a client, you write:", "choices": ["Je ne peux pas assister à la réunion.", "Je peux pas assister à la réunion.", "Je pas peux assister à la réunion."], "answer": "Je ne peux pas assister à la réunion.", "right": "Exactly! In writing, the «ne» comes back.", "wrong": "Dropping «ne» is for speech. In formal writing: «Je ne peux pas assister à la réunion.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: in speech, «ne» disappears (je sais pas, c'est pas grave, j'ai rien vu, t'inquiète pas, y a pas de souci). In writing and formal settings, keep it."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write it the casual spoken way: 'It doesn't work.'", "answer": "Ça marche pas.", "accept": ["ça marche pas", "ca marche pas", "ça fonctionne pas", "ça ne marche pas", "Ça ne marche pas."], "hint": "Ça marche ...", "right": "Parfait, that's exactly how you'd hear it in Paris.", "wrong": "Casual: «Ça marche pas.» (Formal: «Ça ne marche pas.»)"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it like a native, relaxed: «T'inquiète pas, y a pas de souci.»", "fr": "T'inquiète pas, y a pas de souci."}]}, "47-2": {"stageId": "47-2", "title": "Les mots qui fondent (t'as, t'es, y a)", "goal": "Recognise and use the melted forms of spoken French: t'as, t'es, y a, faut, j'suis, chais pas.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Spoken French 'melts' words together: «tu as» → «t'as», «tu es» → «t'es», «il y a» → «y a», «il faut» → «faut», «je suis» → «j'suis» (often «chuis»), «je sais pas» → «chais pas». If you don't know these, native speech sounds like a blur."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "T'as faim ?", "en": "Are you hungry?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "T'es où ?", "en": "Where are you?", "note": "The classic phone question."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«T'es fatigué ?» is short for:", "choices": ["Tu es fatigué ?", "Tu as fatigué ?", "Tu étais fatigué ?"], "answer": "Tu es fatigué ?", "right": "Yes! «T'es» = «tu es» (être).", "wrong": "«T'es» comes from «tu es» (être); «t'as» comes from «tu as» (avoir). So: «Tu es fatigué ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Easy memory trick: «t'as» keeps the A of «tu as» (avoir), and «t'es» keeps the E of «tu es» (être)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Y a du monde.", "en": "It's busy. / There are lots of people.", "note": "«il y a» → «y a», pronounced like 'ya'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Faut y aller.", "en": "We've got to go."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Faut que j'y aille» is short for:", "choices": ["Il faut que j'y aille.", "Je faut y aller.", "Il fait que j'y aille."], "answer": "Il faut que j'y aille.", "right": "Parfait! The «il» simply disappears in speech.", "wrong": "«Faut» = «il faut» with the «il» dropped: «Il faut que j'y aille.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Je» melts before consonants: «j'suis», «j'veux», «j'peux». Before an S sound it even turns into 'ch': «je suis» → «chuis», «je sais pas» → «chais pas». In text messages people write «chui» or «chais pas»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'suis crevé.", "en": "I'm exhausted."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Chais pas.", "en": "Dunno."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A friend texts you: «chui en retard». It means:", "choices": ["I'm late.", "I'm not late.", "He's late."], "answer": "I'm late.", "right": "Exactly! «Chui» = «je suis».", "wrong": "«Chui» is «je suis» melted: «Je suis en retard» = I'm late."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Qu'est-ce que t'en penses ?", "en": "What do you think of it?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is the natural spoken version of «Tu as vu ce qu'il y a ?»", "choices": ["T'as vu c'qu'y a ?", "Tas vu ce qui ya ?", "T'es vu c'qu'il a ?"], "answer": "T'as vu c'qu'y a ?", "right": "Bravo! That's exactly how it sounds in real life.", "wrong": "«Tu as» → «t'as», «ce qu'il y a» → «c'qu'y a»: «T'as vu c'qu'y a ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Don't write these forms in emails or essays. They belong to speech and texting. But you MUST recognise them, and using them with friends makes you sound natural."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: t'as (tu as), t'es (tu es), y a (il y a), faut (il faut), j'suis / chuis (je suis), chais pas (je sais pas)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the melted spoken form: 'Where are you?'", "answer": "T'es où ?", "accept": ["t'es où", "t’es où", "t'es ou", "t'es où?", "tu es où"], "hint": "T'... où ?", "right": "Parfait, that's the most common phone question in France.", "wrong": "«Tu es où ?» melts into «T'es où ?»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it fast and relaxed: «T'as faim ? Faut y aller, y a du monde.»", "fr": "T'as faim ? Faut y aller, y a du monde."}]}, "47-3": {"stageId": "47-3", "title": "« On » à la place de « nous »", "goal": "Use «on» instead of «nous» in everyday speech, with correct verb forms and agreements, and use generic «on» to translate the English passive.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In everyday speech, «on» replaces «nous» almost all the time. «Nous allons au cinéma» → «On va au cinéma». The verb is always 3rd person singular: «on va, on est, on a, on fait»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On y va ?", "en": "Shall we go?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On se voit demain.", "en": "See you tomorrow.", "note": "Literally: we see each other tomorrow."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Casual: 'We're leaving.'", "choices": ["On part.", "On partons.", "On partent."], "answer": "On part.", "right": "Exactly! «On» always takes the «il/elle» form of the verb.", "wrong": "«On» uses the 3rd person singular, even when it means «nous»: «On part.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Agreement trick: when «on» means «nous», adjectives and past participles with «être» usually agree in the plural, and in the feminine for a group of women: «On est fatigués», «On est parties». The verb itself stays singular!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On est fatigués.", "en": "We're tired."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Two women say 'We arrived':", "choices": ["On est arrivées.", "On sont arrivées.", "On a arrivé."], "answer": "On est arrivées.", "right": "Parfait! Singular verb «est», feminine plural participle «arrivées».", "wrong": "Verb in the singular («on est»), participle agreeing with the real people: «On est arrivées.» And «arriver» takes «être»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For emphasis, natives say «Nous, on...». The stressed pronoun is «nous», even with «on». Never «On, on...»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous, on préfère la mer.", "en": "We prefer the seaside.", "note": "«Nous, on...» = as for us."}, {"t": "say", "text": "With «on» = «nous», the possessive stays «notre / nos»: «On a pris notre voiture.» And the reflexive pronoun is «se»: «On s'appelle ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On a mangé chez Paul.", "en": "We ate at Paul's."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Casual: 'We took our car.'", "choices": ["On a pris notre voiture.", "On a pris leur voiture.", "On avons pris notre voiture."], "answer": "On a pris notre voiture.", "right": "Bravo! «On a» + «notre».", "wrong": "Verb in the singular («on a») but the possessive matches 'we': «On a pris notre voiture.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«On» also means 'someone / people' in general. It's the French way to say many English passives: «On m'a dit que c'était fermé» = I was told it was closed."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On m'a dit que c'était fermé.", "en": "I was told it was closed."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«On m'a volé mon portefeuille.» Best translation:", "choices": ["My wallet was stolen.", "We stole my wallet.", "I stole a wallet."], "answer": "My wallet was stolen.", "right": "Exactly! Generic «on» = someone, so English uses the passive.", "wrong": "Here «on» means an unknown person: 'Someone stole my wallet' = «My wallet was stolen.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: on = nous in speech (on va, on est), plural agreement (on est fatigués), «Nous, on...» for emphasis, «notre/nos» possessives, and generic «on» for English passives."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write it the casual way: 'What are we doing tonight?'", "answer": "On fait quoi ce soir ?", "accept": ["on fait quoi ce soir", "on fait quoi ce soir?", "qu'est-ce qu'on fait ce soir", "qu'est-ce qu'on fait ce soir ?", "qu’est-ce qu’on fait ce soir"], "hint": "On fait ... ce soir ?", "right": "Parfait, exactly what French friends text each other on a Friday.", "wrong": "Casual: «On fait quoi ce soir ?» (or «Qu'est-ce qu'on fait ce soir ?»)"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it with a friendly rising tone: «On se voit demain ? On s'appelle !»", "fr": "On se voit demain ? On s'appelle !"}]}, "47-4": {"stageId": "47-4", "title": "Les petits mots : du coup, en fait, bref", "goal": "Use the discourse markers du coup, en fait, bah/ben, quoi, genre, bref and euh like a native speaker.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Native French is full of little words that glue speech together: «du coup, en fait, bah, ben, quoi, genre, bref, euh». They carry little meaning but a lot of naturalness."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Du coup, on fait quoi ?", "en": "So, what do we do?", "note": "«du coup» = so, as a result. Hugely popular, and some people find it overused!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "En fait, c'est simple.", "en": "Actually, it's simple.", "note": "Pronounce the final t: 'en fête'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False friend alert: «actuellement» means 'currently', NOT 'actually'. 'Actually' = «en fait»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Actually, I'm not hungry.'", "choices": ["En fait, j'ai pas faim.", "Actuellement, j'ai pas faim.", "Du fait, j'ai pas faim."], "answer": "En fait, j'ai pas faim.", "right": "Parfait! «En fait» = actually.", "wrong": "«Actuellement» = currently. 'Actually' is «en fait»: «En fait, j'ai pas faim.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Bah» and «Ben» = 'well...' at the start of a sentence. Hesitation: «Ben, je sais pas…». Or 'obviously!': «Bah oui !»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Bah oui !", "en": "Well, yes! (obviously)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Quoi» at the end of a sentence = 'you know', a way of summing up: «Il est sympa, quoi.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est sympa, quoi.", "en": "He's nice, you know."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Where does filler «quoi» go?", "choices": ["At the end of the sentence", "At the beginning of the sentence", "Before the verb"], "answer": "At the end of the sentence", "right": "Exactly! «C'était bien, quoi.»", "wrong": "As a filler, «quoi» closes the sentence: «Il est sympa, quoi.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Genre, il est parti comme ça !", "en": "Like, he just left like that!", "note": "«genre» = 'like' (young people's slang)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Bref, c'était génial.", "en": "Anyway, it was great.", "note": "«bref» = to cut a long story short."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You've been telling a long story and want to wrap it up. You say:", "choices": ["Bref, ...", "Genre, ...", "Euh, ..."], "answer": "Bref, ...", "right": "Oui! «Bref» signals 'long story short'.", "wrong": "«Genre» = like, «euh» = um. To sum up and finish, use «Bref»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "When you're thinking, say «euh», not the English 'um'. It's the fastest way to sound French even while searching for words."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'So, what do we do?' (casual)", "choices": ["Du coup, on fait quoi ?", "Bref, on fait quoi ?", "Actuellement, on fait quoi ?"], "answer": "Du coup, on fait quoi ?", "right": "Bravo! «Du coup» = so, as a consequence.", "wrong": "'So' as a consequence = «du coup». «Bref» = anyway, «actuellement» = currently."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: du coup (so), en fait (actually), bah/ben (well), quoi (you know), genre (like), bref (anyway), euh (um)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: 'Actually, it's simple.'", "answer": "En fait, c'est simple.", "accept": ["en fait, c'est simple", "en fait c'est simple", "en fait, c’est simple"], "hint": "En ..., c'est simple.", "right": "Parfait, and remember to pronounce the t in «fait».", "wrong": "'Actually' = «en fait»: «En fait, c'est simple.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it with natural rhythm: «Bah, en fait, il est sympa, quoi. Bref…»", "fr": "Bah, en fait, il est sympa, quoi. Bref…"}]}, "47-5": {"stageId": "47-5", "title": "Les questions de la rue", "goal": "Ask everyday questions the way French people really do, with intonation and the question word at the end.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French has three ways to ask a question: inversion «Viens-tu ?» (soutenu), «Est-ce que tu viens ?» (courant) and plain intonation «Tu viens ?» (everyday). In the street, intonation wins by far."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu viens ?", "en": "Are you coming?"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Your voice does all the work: «Tu viens.» (falling = statement) vs «Tu viens ?» (rising = question). Same words, different melody."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In casual questions, the question word often goes at the END: «Tu fais quoi ?», «Tu pars quand ?», «Tu habites où ?», «C'est qui ?», «C'est combien ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu fais quoi ?", "en": "What are you doing?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Casual: 'Where do you live?'", "choices": ["Tu habites où ?", "Où tu habites est-ce que ?", "Tu habites que ?"], "answer": "Tu habites où ?", "right": "Parfait! Question word at the end, rising voice.", "wrong": "Casual style puts the question word at the end: «Tu habites où ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Que» goes at the start, «quoi» at the end: «Que fais-tu ?» (soutenu), «Qu'est-ce que tu fais ?» (courant), «Tu fais quoi ?» (familier). But never «Tu fais que ?»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which one is NOT correct?", "choices": ["Tu fais quoi ?", "Qu'est-ce que tu fais ?", "Tu fais que ?"], "answer": "Tu fais que ?", "right": "Exactly! At the end of a question, «que» becomes «quoi».", "wrong": "«Que» can't stand at the end of a sentence. Say «Tu fais quoi ?» or «Qu'est-ce que tu fais ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu pars quand ?", "en": "When are you leaving?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est combien ?", "en": "How much is it?", "note": "Perfect at the market. Also: «Ça coûte combien ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ça te dit ?", "en": "Do you fancy it?", "note": "«Un ciné, ça te dit ?» = Fancy a movie?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A friend asks: «Une pizza ce soir, ça te dit ?» It means:", "choices": ["Do you fancy a pizza tonight?", "What does the pizza say tonight?", "Did you tell them about the pizza?"], "answer": "Do you fancy a pizza tonight?", "right": "Oui! «Ça te dit ?» = Does that appeal to you?", "wrong": "«Ça te dit ?» is an idiom: 'Do you fancy it?' So: Do you fancy a pizza tonight?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On se retrouve où ?", "en": "Where shall we meet?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What register is «Quand pars-tu ?»", "choices": ["soutenu", "familier", "incorrect"], "answer": "soutenu", "right": "Bravo! Inversion = soutenu. In the street you'd say «Tu pars quand ?»", "wrong": "It's correct, just formal: inversion is soutenu. Casual: «Tu pars quand ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: in the street, ask with intonation and put the question word at the end: tu viens ?, tu fais quoi ?, tu pars quand ?, c'est combien ?, c'est qui ?, ça te dit ?, on se retrouve où ?"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Ask casually, with the question word at the end: 'What are you doing?'", "answer": "Tu fais quoi ?", "accept": ["tu fais quoi", "tu fais quoi?", "vous faites quoi", "qu'est-ce que tu fais", "qu'est-ce que tu fais ?"], "hint": "Tu fais ... ?", "right": "Parfait, that's the real street version.", "wrong": "Casual: «Tu fais quoi ?» (question word at the end, rising voice)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it with a rising voice at the end of each question: «Tu fais quoi ce soir ? Un ciné, ça te dit ?»", "fr": "Tu fais quoi ce soir ? Un ciné, ça te dit ?"}]}, "48-1": {"stageId": "48-1", "title": "Surprise & intérêt", "goal": "React to news like a native, picking the right level of surprise and showing interest so the other person keeps talking.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In a French conversation, silence makes people uneasy. Natives react all the time with little sounds and phrases that say 'I'm listening!'. The most useful one is «Ah bon ?»: mild surprise, polite with anyone."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ah bon ? Je ne savais pas.", "en": "Really? I didn't know.", "note": "In speech you'll hear «Ah bon ? Je savais pas.» (no «ne»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Think of it as a ladder. Neutral: «Ah bon ?», «C'est vrai ?», «Vraiment ?». Stronger and casual: «Sérieux ?», «Tu rigoles ?». Total disbelief: «C'est pas vrai !», «Sans blague !»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A colleague says the meeting has moved to Thursday. What's the most natural, neutral reaction?", "choices": ["Ah bon ?", "Sans blague !", "Oh là là !"], "answer": "Ah bon ?", "right": "Exactly! «Ah bon ?» is mild surprise. It fits small news like this, with anyone.", "wrong": "For small news, the neutral reaction is «Ah bon ?». «Sans blague !» is much stronger (and can sound sarcastic), and «Oh là là !» is for trouble or something impressive."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Oh là là» is NOT flirty in French! It mostly means 'oh dear' (something went wrong) or 'wow' (that's a lot). When it's really bad, natives stretch it: «Oh là là là là !»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Oh là là, j'ai oublié mes clés !", "en": "Oh no, I've forgotten my keys!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Sans blague !» literally means 'without joke'. Said with wide eyes, it's 'No kidding!'. Said flat, it's ironic: 'Well, obviously.' Your tone does all the work."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend says, very seriously, «Paris, c'est cher.» You answer flatly: «Sans blague…». What do you mean?", "choices": ["I'm amazed!", "Well, obviously.", "Tell me more!"], "answer": "Well, obviously.", "right": "Yes! Said flat, «Sans blague…» is ironic: everyone knows Paris is expensive.", "wrong": "Said in a flat voice, «Sans blague…» is ironic. It means 'Well, obviously / you don't say'. Only an excited tone makes it real surprise."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To show interest and get more of the story: «Ah oui ? Raconte !», «Et alors ?», «Et après ?». «Raconte !» is the magic word. It's the «tu» command of «raconter», with no -s."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ah oui ? Raconte ! Ça s'est passé comment ?", "en": "Oh yeah? Tell me! How did it go?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your manager, whom you call «vous», tells you some surprising news. Which reaction is safest?", "choices": ["Tu rigoles ?", "C'est vrai ?", "Sérieux ?"], "answer": "C'est vrai ?", "right": "Parfait! «C'est vrai ?» (or «Vraiment ?», «Ah bon ?») works with anyone.", "wrong": "«Tu rigoles ?» uses «tu» and is familiar, and «Sérieux ?» is casual. With a manager, go for «C'est vrai ?», «Vraiment ?» or «Ah bon ?»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«C'est pas vrai !» does NOT accuse anyone of lying. It means 'No way!'. Natives drop the «ne» when speaking. The written form is «Ce n'est pas vrai !»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend announces she's getting married. You shout «C'est pas vrai !». What does it mean?", "choices": ["You're lying.", "No way! That's amazing!", "That's wrong."], "answer": "No way! That's amazing!", "right": "Yes! It's happy disbelief, not an accusation.", "wrong": "«C'est pas vrai !» is an exclamation of disbelief, like 'No way!'. To really say someone is lying, you'd say «Tu mens !»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: neutral «Ah bon ? / C'est vrai ? / Vraiment ?». Strong and familiar: «Sérieux ? / Tu rigoles ? / Sans blague !». Disbelief: «C'est pas vrai !». Interest: «Ah oui ? Raconte !». Trouble or wow: «Oh là là !»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (casual): Seriously? Are you joking?", "answer": "Sérieux ? Tu rigoles ?", "accept": ["sérieux ? tu rigoles", "sérieux? tu rigoles?", "sérieux ? tu rigoles ?", "sérieux? tu rigoles", "serieux ? tu rigoles", "sérieux ? tu plaisantes", "sérieux ? tu plaisantes ?"], "hint": "«Sérieux» + the verb «rigoler» with «tu»", "right": "Très naturel! That's exactly how a friend would react.", "wrong": "The answer is «Sérieux ? Tu rigoles ?». «Rigoler» means to laugh or joke, a very common casual verb."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with lots of feeling: «Ah bon ? C'est pas vrai ! Raconte !»", "fr": "Ah bon ? C'est pas vrai ! Raconte !"}]}, "48-2": {"stageId": "48-2", "title": "D'accord, pas d'accord", "goal": "Agree and disagree naturally, from casual slang to polished, diplomatic French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French has a big range for agreeing. It goes from the polished «Tout à fait» to the teenage «Grave !». The one you choose says a lot about you and the situation."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tout à fait. C'est exactement ça.", "en": "Absolutely. That's exactly it.", "note": "Polished and safe at work, in interviews, with anyone."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Strong casual agreement: «Carrément !» (totally), «C'est clair !» (for sure), «Grave !» (totally, young slang). Neutral and polite: «Exactement», «Absolument», «Tout à fait»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a job interview, the recruiter says teamwork is essential. How do you agree?", "choices": ["Grave !", "Tout à fait.", "Carrément !"], "answer": "Tout à fait.", "right": "Bravo! «Tout à fait» is polished and professional.", "wrong": "«Grave !» and «Carrément !» are casual, fine with friends. In an interview, say «Tout à fait.» or «Absolument.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Grave» normally means 'serious': «C'est grave ?» = Is it serious? And «C'est pas grave» = It doesn't matter. Only as a one-word reply does «Grave !» mean 'totally!'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend says «Ce film était génial !» and you answer «Grave !». What did you say?", "choices": ["It was serious.", "Totally!", "Not really."], "answer": "Totally!", "right": "Yes! As a slang reply, «Grave !» means 'totally!'.", "wrong": "As a one-word reply among young people, «Grave !» means 'totally, absolutely'. It's strong agreement."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Not convinced? Say «Bof…» with a shrug and a little pout. It may be the most French sound there is! It means 'meh'. You'll often hear «Ouais, bof.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le resto ? Bof, c'était pas terrible.", "en": "The restaurant? Meh, it wasn't great.", "note": "«Pas terrible» = not great (it does NOT mean 'not terrible')."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «C'était pas terrible» mean?", "choices": ["It was great.", "It wasn't great.", "It wasn't awful at all."], "answer": "It wasn't great.", "right": "Well done! It's a faux ami: «pas terrible» = mediocre.", "wrong": "«Pas terrible» means 'not great, mediocre'. It's a classic trap for English speakers."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Disagreeing, from soft to strong: «Pas vraiment», «Je ne suis pas sûr(e)», «Je ne suis pas d'accord» (spoken: «Je suis pas d'accord»), «Pas du tout !». Then «N'importe quoi !» (Nonsense!), which is blunt and can sound rude."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "There's no verb for 'to disagree' in everyday French. Say «Je ne suis pas d'accord». Also remember it's «être d'accord», never «avoir d'accord»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a meeting, a colleague suggests something you disagree with. What's the most diplomatic answer?", "choices": ["N'importe quoi !", "Je ne suis pas tout à fait d'accord.", "Bof…"], "answer": "Je ne suis pas tout à fait d'accord.", "right": "Très diplomate! «Pas tout à fait» softens it: 'not quite'.", "wrong": "«N'importe quoi !» is blunt and «Bof…» is dismissive. At work, soften it: «Je ne suis pas tout à fait d'accord.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je ne suis pas tout à fait d'accord : à mon avis, c'est trop cher.", "en": "I don't quite agree: in my opinion, it's too expensive."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap. Agree: «Tout à fait / Absolument» (polished), «Carrément / C'est clair» (casual), «Grave !» (young). Meh: «Bof». Disagree: «Pas vraiment», «Je ne suis pas d'accord», «Pas du tout». Blunt: «N'importe quoi !»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (casual, spoken): I don't agree, not at all.", "answer": "Je suis pas d'accord, pas du tout.", "accept": ["je suis pas d'accord, pas du tout", "je ne suis pas d'accord, pas du tout", "Je ne suis pas d'accord, pas du tout.", "je suis pas d'accord pas du tout", "je ne suis pas d'accord pas du tout"], "hint": "être d'accord + «pas du tout»", "right": "Parfait! With or without «ne», both work. Without it sounds more spoken.", "wrong": "Answer: «Je (ne) suis pas d'accord, pas du tout.» Remember: «être d'accord»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, enthusiastically: «Carrément ! C'est clair !»", "fr": "Carrément ! C'est clair !"}]}, "48-3": {"stageId": "48-3", "title": "Rassurer & encourager", "goal": "Reassure, comfort and encourage people the way French speakers really do.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "When someone apologises, worries or faces something hard, natives react with short, warm phrases. Let's learn the reflexes."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pardon ! — T'inquiète, c'est rien.", "en": "Sorry! — Don't worry, it's nothing.", "note": "Familiar: use it with friends."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«T'inquiète» comes from «Ne t'inquiète pas». Speech drops the «ne», and here even the «pas»! The polite version is «Ne vous inquiétez pas»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your elderly neighbour, whom you call «vous», apologises for the noise. What do you say?", "choices": ["T'inquiète !", "Ne vous inquiétez pas.", "Ne t'inquiète pas."], "answer": "Ne vous inquiétez pas.", "right": "Parfait! «Vous» and the full form: respectful and kind.", "wrong": "With someone you call «vous», use «Ne vous inquiétez pas.» «T'inquiète» is very casual and uses «tu»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To play it down: «C'est rien», «C'est pas grave» (it doesn't matter), «Ça arrive» (it happens), «Ça arrive à tout le monde» (it happens to everyone)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai cassé ton verre… — C'est pas grave, ça arrive !", "en": "I broke your glass… — It doesn't matter, it happens!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend failed their driving test. Which phrase means 'It happens to everyone'?", "choices": ["Ça arrive à tout le monde.", "Ça va arriver.", "Tout le monde arrive."], "answer": "Ça arrive à tout le monde.", "right": "Yes! «Ça arrive» = it happens. Very comforting.", "wrong": "It's «Ça arrive à tout le monde.» «Ça va arriver» = it's going to happen. «Tout le monde arrive» = everyone is arriving."}, {"t": "say", "text": "About the future: «Ça va aller» (It's going to be OK), «Pas de panique» (Don't panic), «Tout va bien se passer» (Everything will go fine)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Courage !» doesn't mean 'be brave'. It means 'hang in there!'. «Bon courage !» is what you say to someone facing something hard: an exam, a long shift, moving house. People also say it as a goodbye to someone heading off to work."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend has three exams tomorrow. What's the best reply?", "choices": ["Bonne chance pour toujours !", "Bon courage pour demain !", "Ça arrive !"], "answer": "Bon courage pour demain !", "right": "Exactly! «Bon courage» is perfect for an effort ahead. «Bonne chance» works too.", "wrong": "For a demanding task ahead, the French reflex is «Bon courage (pour demain) !». «Ça arrive» is for after something went wrong."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To push someone to go for it: «Vas-y !», «Allez !», «Tu peux le faire !». In «Vas-y», the «s» comes back before «y» so you can link the sounds: [va-zi]."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which spelling is correct?", "choices": ["Va-y !", "Vas-y !", "Vas y !"], "answer": "Vas-y !", "right": "Bravo! «Vas-y», with an -s and a hyphen.", "wrong": "It's «Vas-y !». The -s comes back before «y» (for the [z] link), and you need a hyphen."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Allez, vas-y, tu peux le faire !", "en": "Come on, go for it, you can do it!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap. Play it down: «T'inquiète / C'est rien / C'est pas grave / Ça arrive». Comfort: «Ça va aller / Pas de panique». Before something hard: «(Bon) courage !». Push: «Vas-y, tu peux le faire !»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (familiar): Don't worry, it's going to be OK.", "answer": "T'inquiète, ça va aller.", "accept": ["t'inquiète, ça va aller", "t'inquiète ça va aller", "ne t'inquiète pas, ça va aller", "Ne t'inquiète pas, ça va aller.", "t'inquiète pas, ça va aller", "ne t'inquiète pas ça va aller"], "hint": "Short form of «Ne t'inquiète pas» + «aller»", "right": "Super! Warm and natural.", "wrong": "Answer: «T'inquiète, ça va aller.» (or «Ne t'inquiète pas, ça va aller.»)"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Encourage a friend out loud: «Courage ! Ça va aller, tu peux le faire !»", "fr": "Courage ! Ça va aller, tu peux le faire !"}]}, "48-4": {"stageId": "48-4", "title": "Politesse & excuses naturelles", "goal": "Apologise, thank and respond to thanks with the right French ritual phrase for each situation.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French politeness runs on fixed rituals. Use the right phrase at the right moment and you instantly sound natural and well brought up («bien élevé»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Apologising: «Pardon !» is quick (bumping into someone, getting past). «Excuse-moi / Excusez-moi» is standard. «Désolé(e)» shows real regret, and «Je suis vraiment désolé(e)» even more."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Excuse-moi, j'ai pas fait exprès !", "en": "Sorry, I didn't do it on purpose!", "note": "«Faire exprès» = to do on purpose. Careful: «Je ne l'ai pas fait exprès.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You step on a stranger's foot in the metro. What's the quickest natural word?", "choices": ["Pardon !", "Je m'excuse beaucoup.", "Désolé pour le retard."], "answer": "Pardon !", "right": "Exactly! «Pardon !» is the instant reflex for small physical things.", "wrong": "For bumps and small accidents, the reflex is «Pardon !». «Je m'excuse beaucoup» isn't idiomatic, and the third one is for being late."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pardon, je peux passer ?", "en": "Excuse me, can I get past?"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Désolé» agrees: a woman writes «désolée». And watch the structure: «désolé pour» + noun («pour le retard»), but «désolé de» + infinitive («d'être en retard»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A woman writes 'Sorry I'm late'. Which is correct?", "choices": ["Désolée pour le retard.", "Désolé de le retard.", "Désolée pour être en retard."], "answer": "Désolée pour le retard.", "right": "Parfait! Feminine «désolée» + «pour» + noun.", "wrong": "It's «Désolée pour le retard.» (or «Désolée d'être en retard.»). Use «pour» before a noun and «de» before an infinitive."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Replying to «merci»: «De rien» (everyday), «Pas de quoi» (casual), «Avec plaisir» (warm), «Je vous en prie / Je t'en prie» (polished)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Je vous en prie» has two jobs: 'You're welcome' AND 'Please, go ahead / after you' (holding a door, offering a seat)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone holds the door open, gestures for you to go first and says «Je vous en prie.» What do they mean?", "choices": ["I beg you.", "After you.", "You're welcome to leave."], "answer": "After you.", "right": "Yes! Here it means 'please, go ahead'. Reply «Merci !»", "wrong": "With a gesture like this, «Je vous en prie» means 'After you / please go ahead'. After «merci», it means 'You're welcome'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "When someone is kind to you, the natural reply is «Merci, c'est gentil». It's warmer than a plain «merci». To accept an invitation: «Avec plaisir !» or «Ça me ferait plaisir !»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu viens dîner samedi ? — Oh oui, ça me ferait très plaisir, merci !", "en": "Are you coming to dinner on Saturday? — Oh yes, I'd love that, thanks!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A friend offers to drive you home. What's the natural reply?", "choices": ["Merci, c'est gentil.", "De rien.", "Je vous en prie."], "answer": "Merci, c'est gentil.", "right": "Bravo! That's exactly what a native would say.", "wrong": "«De rien» and «Je vous en prie» are replies TO a thank-you. When receiving kindness, say «Merci, c'est gentil.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap. Sorry: «Pardon» (quick), «Excusez-moi», «Désolé(e) pour… / de…». Thanks back: «De rien / Avec plaisir / Je vous en prie». Receiving kindness: «Merci, c'est gentil». Accepting: «Avec plaisir / Ça me ferait plaisir»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Thanks, that's kind.", "answer": "Merci, c'est gentil.", "accept": ["merci, c'est gentil", "merci c'est gentil", "merci, c'est très gentil", "merci, c'est sympa"], "hint": "Merci + c'est + an adjective meaning 'kind'", "right": "Parfait! Use it every day.", "wrong": "Answer: «Merci, c'est gentil.» «Gentil» = kind, nice."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Pardon, je peux passer ? Merci, c'est gentil !»", "fr": "Pardon, je peux passer ? Merci, c'est gentil !"}]}, "48-5": {"stageId": "48-5", "title": "Les émotions du quotidien", "goal": "Express everyday feelings (being fed up, annoyed, lazy, excited, missing someone) with the phrases natives actually use.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Everyday emotions come out in short, punchy phrases. These are the ones you'll hear every single day in France."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Fed up and annoyed: «J'en ai marre !» (I'm fed up!), «J'en ai marre de» + noun or infinitive, «Ça m'énerve !» (That annoys me!)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'en ai marre de ce temps pourri !", "en": "I'm fed up with this rotten weather!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I'm fed up with waiting'?", "choices": ["J'en ai marre d'attendre.", "J'ai marre d'attendre.", "Je suis marre d'attendre."], "answer": "J'en ai marre d'attendre.", "right": "Exactly! «en» is essential, and it's «avoir».", "wrong": "The fixed phrase is «en avoir marre (de)»: «J'en ai marre d'attendre.» Never drop the «en», and don't use «être»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«J'ai la flemme» = I can't be bothered. It's an iconic French feeling of pure laziness! «J'ai la flemme de faire la vaisselle.» It's familiar."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Faux ami alert: «Je suis énervé(e)» = I'm annoyed or irritated, NOT 'nervous'. For nervous, say «Je suis stressé(e)» or «nerveux / nerveuse»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Je suis énervé» mean?", "choices": ["I'm nervous.", "I'm annoyed.", "I'm excited."], "answer": "I'm annoyed.", "right": "Bravo! «Énervé» = annoyed or wound up.", "wrong": "«Énervé» means annoyed or irritated. 'Nervous' would be «stressé» or «nerveux»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Happy and excited: «Je suis trop content(e) !», «J'ai hâte !» (I can't wait!), «J'ai hâte de te voir». Careful with «Je suis excité(e)»: it can have a sexual meaning. Prefer «J'ai hâte» or «Je suis impatient(e)»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I can't wait for the holidays!'?", "choices": ["Je suis excité pour les vacances !", "J'ai hâte d'être en vacances !", "Je ne peux pas attendre les vacances !"], "answer": "J'ai hâte d'être en vacances !", "right": "Parfait! «Avoir hâte de» is THE way to say 'can't wait'.", "wrong": "Natives say «J'ai hâte d'être en vacances !». «Je ne peux pas attendre» is a word-for-word translation, and «excité» can sound sexual."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Manquer» works backwards: «Paris me manque» = I miss Paris. Literally, 'Paris is missing to me'. The thing you miss is the subject."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu me manques.", "en": "I miss you.", "note": "Literally: you are missing to me."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'I miss my parents'?", "choices": ["Je manque mes parents.", "Mes parents me manquent.", "Mes parents manquent moi."], "answer": "Mes parents me manquent.", "right": "Bravo! The parents are the subject, so the verb is plural: «manquent».", "wrong": "Flip it: what you miss is the subject. «Mes parents me manquent.» («Je manque mes parents» would mean you're absent from them!)"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two more: «Ça fait peur» = that's scary. And in casual speech «Je suis dégoûté(e)» = I'm gutted, really disappointed («J'ai raté le concert, je suis dégoûté !»), not only 'disgusted'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «J'en ai marre (de…)», «Ça m'énerve», «J'ai la flemme», «Je suis trop content(e)», «J'ai hâte (de…)», «X me manque», «Je suis dégoûté(e)», «Ça fait peur»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I can't be bothered.", "answer": "J'ai la flemme.", "accept": ["j'ai la flemme", "j'ai trop la flemme", "jai la flemme"], "hint": "avoir + la + a word for laziness", "right": "Super! Very native.", "wrong": "Answer: «J'ai la flemme.» «La flemme» = laziness, with «avoir»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'en ai marre ! Mais j'ai hâte d'être en vacances.»", "fr": "J'en ai marre ! Mais j'ai hâte d'être en vacances."}]}, "49-1": {"stageId": "49-1", "title": "La bise & les salutations", "goal": "Greet and say goodbye like a French person: the mandatory «Bonjour», la bise, and the right farewell for the time of day.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In France, greeting is a ritual, and «Bonjour» is mandatory. Say it when you walk into a shop, a bakery, a bus, a waiting room. Skip it and people will think you're rude, even if the rest of your French is perfect."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The golden rule: «Bonjour» comes before ANY request. «Bonjour, une baguette, s'il vous plaît.» Without the «Bonjour», service can turn frosty."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You walk into a bakery. What's the first thing you say?", "choices": ["Une baguette, s'il vous plaît.", "Bonjour !", "Salut !"], "answer": "Bonjour !", "right": "Exactly! Always «Bonjour» first, then the request.", "wrong": "Always start with «Bonjour !». Going straight to your order feels rude in France, and «Salut» is too familiar with a shopkeeper."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Adding «Madame» or «Monsieur» without a name sounds very polite and natural: «Bonjour, Madame.» «Merci, Monsieur.» In English 'Hello, Madam' sounds stiff, but in French it's everyday courtesy."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«La bise» means touching cheeks and kissing the air. It's for friends, family, friends of friends, and colleagues in many workplaces. The number of kisses depends on the region: 2 in Paris, often 3 around Montpellier, sometimes 4 in parts of the north and centre. Let the other person lead."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On se fait la bise ? — Oui ! Ça fait combien de bises ici ?", "en": "Shall we do the cheek kiss? — Yes! How many kisses is it around here?", "note": "Asking how many is totally normal. Even the French ask!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You meet a new client at a formal business meeting. What do you do?", "choices": ["La bise", "Une poignée de main", "A big hug"], "answer": "Une poignée de main", "right": "Correct! A handshake («une poignée de main») is the formal norm.", "wrong": "In formal or first business meetings, shake hands: «une poignée de main». La bise is for friends and familiar settings, and hugs are rare."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Friendly hellos: «Salut !» and «Coucou !». «Coucou» is very familiar: friends, family, kids, text messages. Goodbyes depend on the time of day: «Bonne journée !» (during the day), «Bonne soirée !» (from late afternoon), «Bonne nuit !» (only when someone's going to bed)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You leave a shop at 7 p.m. What do you say?", "choices": ["Bonne nuit !", "Bonne soirée !", "Bon matin !"], "answer": "Bonne soirée !", "right": "Parfait! In the evening, it's «Bonne soirée !».", "wrong": "In the evening, say «Bonne soirée !». «Bonne nuit» is only for bedtime, and «Bon matin» isn't used in France."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Bon matin» is Québécois, not used in France, where you just say «Bonjour». In the evening, you can greet people with «Bonsoir»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "'See you': «À tout à l'heure» (later today), «À plus tard» or «À plus !» (texted as «A+»), «À bientôt» (soon), «À demain» (tomorrow)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You'll see your colleague again after lunch today. Which goodbye fits?", "choices": ["À tout à l'heure !", "À bientôt !", "Bonne nuit !"], "answer": "À tout à l'heure !", "right": "Yes! «À tout à l'heure» = see you later today.", "wrong": "For later the same day, say «À tout à l'heure !». «À bientôt» is vaguer: 'see you soon', some other day."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Bon, j'y vais. Bonne soirée, à demain !", "en": "Right, I'm off. Have a nice evening, see you tomorrow!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Bonjour» always comes first, often with «Madame / Monsieur». A handshake when it's formal, la bise when it's friendly. «Salut / Coucou» with friends. Leaving: «Bonne journée / Bonne soirée», «À tout à l'heure / À plus / À demain»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Hello, Madam. Have a nice day!", "answer": "Bonjour, Madame. Bonne journée !", "accept": ["bonjour, madame. bonne journée", "bonjour madame, bonne journée", "bonjour, madame, bonne journée", "bonjour madame. bonne journée", "bonjour madame bonne journée", "bonjour, madame. bonne journée !"], "hint": "Greeting + title, then 'good day'", "right": "Très poli! Perfect French courtesy.", "wrong": "Answer: «Bonjour, Madame. Bonne journée !»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud as you leave a shop: «Merci, Monsieur ! Bonne journée, au revoir !»", "fr": "Merci, Monsieur ! Bonne journée, au revoir !"}]}, "49-2": {"stageId": "49-2", "title": "À table", "goal": "Handle French meals with confidence: table manners, l'apéro, toasting, complimenting and restaurant habits.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In France, meals are almost sacred: long, social and full of unwritten codes. Let's go through them so you feel at home at any French table."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Before eating, someone says «Bon appétit !». You reply «Merci, vous aussi / toi aussi !» or just «Bon appétit !» back. Wait for the host before you start."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Keep both hands on the table, wrists resting on the edge. Hands in your lap looks odd (the opposite of British and American manners!). Put your bread straight on the tablecloth and break it with your hands; don't cut it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "At a French dinner, where do your hands go between bites?", "choices": ["In your lap", "Wrists resting on the table", "Arms crossed"], "answer": "Wrists resting on the table", "right": "Exactly! Hands visible, wrists on the table edge.", "wrong": "In France, your hands stay visible: wrists rest on the edge of the table. Hands in your lap is the Anglo-Saxon habit."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Asking for things: «Tu peux me passer le sel ?» (casual). «Vous pourriez me passer le pain, s'il vous plaît ?» (polite: «pourriez» is the conditional, which softens the request)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You're with your partner's parents (vous) and you want the bread. What do you say?", "choices": ["Passe-moi le pain !", "Vous pourriez me passer le pain, s'il vous plaît ?", "Donnez pain."], "answer": "Vous pourriez me passer le pain, s'il vous plaît ?", "right": "Parfait! «Vous» + conditional + s'il vous plaît: impeccable.", "wrong": "With in-laws, be polite: «Vous pourriez me passer le pain, s'il vous plaît ?». «Passe-moi» uses «tu» and is a direct command."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«L'apéro» means drinks and nibbles before dinner. It's a beloved ritual: «On prend l'apéro ?». An «apéro dînatoire» is so generous it replaces dinner. When toasting: «Santé !» or «À la vôtre / À la tienne !». Look people in the eyes as you clink glasses. Superstition says it's bad luck otherwise!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Allez, santé ! — À la tienne !", "en": "Come on, cheers! — Cheers to you!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A friend (tu) clinks your glass and says «Santé !». What do you reply?", "choices": ["À la tienne !", "À la vôtre, Monsieur !", "Bon appétit !"], "answer": "À la tienne !", "right": "Bravo! «À la tienne» goes with «tu», «À la vôtre» with «vous».", "wrong": "With a friend, reply «À la tienne !» (tu). «À la vôtre» is for «vous» or a group. «Bon appétit» is for food."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Compliment the cook, because it matters: «C'est délicieux !», «C'est vraiment bon !». When you're full: «Je n'ai plus faim, merci», «J'ai très bien mangé», or casually «Je suis calé(e) !»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Avoid «Je suis plein(e)» for 'I'm full'. It sounds odd and can be read as 'I'm drunk' (and «pleine» is used for pregnant animals!). Say «J'ai très bien mangé» instead."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What's a polite way to refuse a second helping?", "choices": ["Je suis plein.", "Merci, j'ai très bien mangé.", "Non, c'est bon, beurk."], "answer": "Merci, j'ai très bien mangé.", "right": "Parfait! Polite, and a compliment at the same time.", "wrong": "Say «Merci, j'ai très bien mangé.» «Je suis plein» sounds odd, and «beurk» means 'yuck'!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "At a restaurant, tap water is free: «Une carafe d'eau, s'il vous plaît». The waiter won't bring the bill until you ask, because rushing you would be rude: «L'addition, s'il vous plaît.» Service is included, so a tip is optional and small."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Bon appétit», hands on the table, «Vous pourriez me passer… ?», «On prend l'apéro ?», «Santé ! / À la tienne !», «C'est délicieux !», «J'ai très bien mangé», «Une carafe d'eau», «L'addition, s'il vous plaît»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The bill, please.", "answer": "L'addition, s'il vous plaît.", "accept": ["l'addition, s'il vous plaît", "l'addition s'il vous plaît", "l'addition, s'il vous plait", "l'addition s'il vous plait", "l'addition, s'il te plaît"], "hint": "l' + the word for 'bill' + s'il vous plaît", "right": "Parfait! Now the waiter will come.", "wrong": "Answer: «L'addition, s'il vous plaît.» (A 'bill' at a restaurant is «l'addition», not «la facture».)"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud at the table: «Bon appétit ! Mmm, c'est délicieux !»", "fr": "Bon appétit ! Mmm, c'est délicieux !"}]}, "49-3": {"stageId": "49-3", "title": "Le travail & la paperasse", "goal": "Understand everyday French work culture and admin vocabulary: RTT, faire le pont, la pause déj, la Sécu, la rentrée.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Living in France means meeting two things: French work culture and «la paperasse» (familiar for paperwork and red tape). Here are the words everyone uses."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The legal working week is 35 hours. Many people who work more get «des RTT» (from «réduction du temps de travail»): extra days off. «Je suis en RTT vendredi» = I've got Friday off."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Faire le pont» = 'to make the bridge'. When a public holiday falls on a Thursday, many people take the Friday off too, bridging to the weekend. May in France is famous for its «ponts»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "14 July falls on a Thursday. Your colleague says «On fait le pont ?». What does she mean?", "choices": ["Shall we cross the bridge?", "Shall we take Friday off too?", "Shall we work late?"], "answer": "Shall we take Friday off too?", "right": "Exactly! A four-day weekend. Very French.", "wrong": "«Faire le pont» = take the day between a public holiday and the weekend off, so here it's Friday."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est la pause déj, on va manger ?", "en": "It's lunch break, shall we go and eat?", "note": "«Déj» is short for «déjeuner» (lunch)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Lunch is a real break: often an hour, sitting down, with colleagues. Eating at your desk is less common than in the UK or the US. Careful: in Québec, Belgium and Switzerland, «déjeuner» is breakfast and «dîner» is lunch!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Admin survival kit: «Il faut prendre rendez-vous» (you need an appointment for almost everything), «remplir un formulaire» (fill in a form), «une pièce d'identité» (ID), «un justificatif de domicile» (proof of address, usually a recent utility bill)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The préfecture asks for «un justificatif de domicile». What do you bring?", "choices": ["Your passport", "A recent electricity bill", "Your diploma"], "answer": "A recent electricity bill", "right": "Yes! A recent bill (often under 3 months old) proves your address.", "wrong": "«Un justificatif de domicile» proves where you live, typically a recent electricity, gas or internet bill. Your passport is «une pièce d'identité»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«La Sécu» = «la Sécurité sociale», the public health insurance. You show «la carte Vitale» (your health card) at the doctor's, and «la Sécu rembourse» = it pays you back part of the cost. A «mutuelle» (top-up insurance) covers the rest."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «La Sécu rembourse» mean?", "choices": ["Social security pays you back.", "The security guard gives refunds.", "The bank pays for you."], "answer": "Social security pays you back.", "right": "Correct! «Rembourser» = to reimburse, pay back.", "wrong": "«La Sécu» = la Sécurité sociale (health insurance), and «rembourser» = to pay back. So: social security reimburses you."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«La rentrée» is early September, when everyone comes back from summer: school, work, politics, new TV shows and books. For the French, it feels more like a fresh start than New Year."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Bonne rentrée à tous !", "en": "Have a good start back, everyone!", "note": "You hear it everywhere in September."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «la rentrée»?", "choices": ["Coming home each evening", "The return to school and work after the summer", "The entrance of a building"], "answer": "The return to school and work after the summer", "right": "Parfait! It's a big cultural moment in September.", "wrong": "«La rentrée» is the September return to school and work after the summer holidays. The entrance of a building is «l'entrée»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «la paperasse», «les RTT», «faire le pont», «la pause déj», «prendre rendez-vous», «remplir un formulaire», «un justificatif de domicile», «la Sécu rembourse», «la rentrée»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: You need to make an appointment.", "answer": "Il faut prendre rendez-vous.", "accept": ["il faut prendre rendez-vous", "il faut prendre un rendez-vous", "il faut prendre rdv", "Il faut prendre un rendez-vous."], "hint": "Il faut + 'take' + appointment", "right": "Bravo! In French you 'take' an appointment.", "wrong": "Answer: «Il faut prendre rendez-vous.» In French, you «prendre» (take) an appointment, not «faire»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je suis en RTT vendredi, on fait le pont !»", "fr": "Je suis en RTT vendredi, on fait le pont !"}]}, "49-4": {"stageId": "49-4", "title": "Les références de tout le monde", "goal": "Recognise the shared cultural references every French person knows, from «métro, boulot, dodo» to la galette des rois and le système D.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Some phrases are instantly understood by every French person because they point to shared experiences. Knowing them makes your French feel like it's from the inside."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Métro, boulot, dodo.", "en": "Commute, work, sleep (the daily grind).", "note": "From a 1950s poem. «Dodo» is a baby word for sleep."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A friend sighs «Métro, boulot, dodo…». What does she mean?", "choices": ["She loves the metro.", "Her life is a monotonous routine.", "She's going to bed."], "answer": "Her life is a monotonous routine.", "right": "Exactly! It's the classic complaint about the daily grind.", "wrong": "«Métro, boulot, dodo» sums up a repetitive routine: commute, work, sleep, repeat."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The calendar: «les grandes vacances» are the long summer holidays (July and August, about two months for schools). «Le 14 Juillet» is the national holiday, with a military parade and fireworks. In French it's «la fête nationale». Nobody says 'Bastille Day'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In January (around Epiphany), people share «la galette des rois»: a puff pastry cake with almond cream («frangipane») and a small charm hidden inside, «la fève». Whoever finds it becomes king or queen and wears the paper crown. The youngest child hides under the table and says who gets each slice."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You find «la fève» in your slice of galette. What happens?", "choices": ["You pay for the cake.", "You become king or queen and wear the crown.", "You have to sing a song."], "answer": "You become king or queen and wear the crown.", "right": "Vive le roi! (Tradition says you buy the next galette, too.)", "wrong": "Finding «la fève» makes you king or queen for the day, with the paper crown."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Le bac» (baccalauréat) is the exam at the end of high school. «Il a eu son bac» = he passed it. Job ads count years of study after it: «bac +3» (bachelor's), «bac +5» (master's)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A job ad asks for «bac +5». What do they want?", "choices": ["Five years of experience", "Five years of study after the bac (master's level)", "Candidates aged 25"], "answer": "Five years of study after the bac (master's level)", "right": "Correct! «Bac +5» = master's level.", "wrong": "«Bac +5» means five years of higher education after the baccalauréat, which is master's level."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A stereotype the French happily own: «Les Français adorent râler». «Râler» = to grumble or moan. It's almost a national sport, and even a way of bonding."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Le système D»: the D stands for «se débrouiller», to manage or get by. It means making do with what you have, finding clever workarounds. «Je me débrouille» = I manage, I get by."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pas de tournevis ? Système D : un couteau fera l'affaire.", "en": "No screwdriver? Make do: a knife will do the job."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «le système D» about?", "choices": ["Bureaucracy", "Resourcefulness, making do", "Holidays"], "answer": "Resourcefulness, making do", "right": "Bravo! «Débrouille» = resourcefulness.", "wrong": "«Le système D» (from «se débrouiller») is about resourcefulness: making do with what's available."}, {"t": "say", "text": "And yes, the French queue: «Il faut faire la queue». Practise saying «queue» [kø] with rounded lips."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «métro, boulot, dodo», «les grandes vacances», «le 14 Juillet», «la galette des rois» and «la fève», «avoir son bac / bac +5», «râler», «le système D», «faire la queue»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: He passed his bac.", "answer": "Il a eu son bac.", "accept": ["il a eu son bac", "il a obtenu son bac", "il a réussi son bac", "Il a obtenu son bac.", "Il a réussi son bac."], "hint": "Use «avoir» in the passé composé", "right": "Parfait! «Avoir son bac» is the natural phrase.", "wrong": "Answer: «Il a eu son bac.» Careful: «passer le bac» means to TAKE the exam, not to pass it!"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with a sigh: «Métro, boulot, dodo… Vivement les grandes vacances !»", "fr": "Métro, boulot, dodo… Vivement les grandes vacances !"}]}, "49-5": {"stageId": "49-5", "title": "Tu ou vous ?", "goal": "Choose confidently between «tu» and «vous», know who proposes switching, and stay consistent once you've chosen.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Tu» or «vous»? It's not only about age. It's about closeness and respect. Get it wrong one way and you seem cold; get it wrong the other way and you seem rude."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Vous»: strangers, shop staff, officials, older people you don't know, your boss (by default), often your in-laws at first. «Tu»: friends, family, children, people your age in casual settings, and pets."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A shop assistant asks what you'd like. What will they say?", "choices": ["Tu veux quoi ?", "Vous désirez ?", "Qu'est-ce que tu désires ?"], "answer": "Vous désirez ?", "right": "Exactly! Customer service always uses «vous».", "wrong": "Shop staff use «vous» with customers: «Vous désirez ?» (What would you like?)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Switching to «tu» is proposed, usually by the older or more senior person: «On peut se tutoyer ?», «On se tutoie ?», «Tutoie-moi, voyons !». The verbs are «tutoyer» (to say tu) and «vouvoyer» (to say vous)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Oh, tutoie-moi, voyons ! On a le même âge.", "en": "Oh, call me «tu», come on! We're the same age."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your boss says «On peut se tutoyer, si tu veux.» What should you do?", "choices": ["Keep saying vous to show respect", "Switch to tu", "Use tu only in emails"], "answer": "Switch to tu", "right": "Yes! Accept the offer. Refusing can feel cold.", "wrong": "When someone senior offers «tu», accept and switch. Carrying on with «vous» can come across as keeping your distance."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "When in doubt, use «vous». Nobody is offended by «vous», but an early «tu» can offend. Workplaces vary: start-ups and young teams use «tu» freely («Entre collègues, on se tutoie souvent»), while banks, law firms and government stay with «vous» longer."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You need directions from a stranger in the street. What do you say?", "choices": ["Excusez-moi de vous déranger…", "Excuse-moi, tu sais où…", "Hé, toi !"], "answer": "Excusez-moi de vous déranger…", "right": "Parfait! Polite «vous» with a stranger.", "wrong": "Strangers get «vous»: «Excusez-moi de vous déranger…» (Sorry to bother you…)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Excusez-moi de vous déranger, auriez-vous un instant ?", "en": "Sorry to bother you, would you have a moment?", "note": "Very polite, with inversion and the conditional."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Be consistent! Every form follows your choice. «Vous» goes with «votre, vos» and «Asseyez-vous». «Tu» goes with «ton, ta, tes» and «Assieds-toi». Mixing them is a classic learner mistake."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence is consistent?", "choices": ["Vous pouvez me donner ton numéro ?", "Tu peux me donner votre numéro ?", "Vous pouvez me donner votre numéro ?"], "answer": "Vous pouvez me donner votre numéro ?", "right": "Bravo! «Vous» goes with «votre».", "wrong": "Keep it consistent: «vous» goes with «votre», «tu» with «ton». So: «Vous pouvez me donner votre numéro ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Young people use «tu» with each other right away, even as strangers, and online too. But a 20-year-old saying «tu» to a 70-year-old stranger is rude. At school it's lopsided: the teacher says «tu» to young pupils, and they say «vous» back."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: use «vous» by default with strangers, staff, officials and your boss. The senior person proposes «tu» («On peut se tutoyer ?»), and you accept. Stay consistent: «vous / votre», «tu / ton». When in doubt, «vous»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Can we say «tu» to each other?", "answer": "On peut se tutoyer ?", "accept": ["on peut se tutoyer", "on peut se tutoyer?", "est-ce qu'on peut se tutoyer", "est-ce qu'on peut se tutoyer ?", "on se tutoie", "on se tutoie ?", "on pourrait se tutoyer"], "hint": "on + pouvoir + se + the verb for saying 'tu'", "right": "Parfait! That's the natural way to propose it.", "wrong": "Answer: «On peut se tutoyer ?» (or «On se tutoie ?»). «Tutoyer» = to say «tu»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, politely: «Excusez-moi de vous déranger, auriez-vous un instant ?»", "fr": "Excusez-moi de vous déranger, auriez-vous un instant ?"}]}, "50-1": {"stageId": "50-1", "title": "L'argot de tous les jours", "goal": "Understand the everyday slang words natives use all day (bosser, boulot, fric, thune, bouffe, mec, nana, bagnole, fringues) and know their standard equivalents.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Real spoken French is full of familiar words you'll rarely see in a textbook but hear every day. Your goal: understand them all, and use them only with friends."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Work: «bosser» = to work, «le boulot» = job or work. «Je bosse demain», «J'ai trouvé un boulot». Standard: «travailler», «le travail», «un emploi»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je bosse tout le week-end, j'en ai marre.", "en": "I'm working all weekend, I'm fed up."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Il cherche du boulot» mean?", "choices": ["He's looking for a ball.", "He's looking for work.", "He's looking for a bowl."], "answer": "He's looking for work.", "right": "Exactly! «Le boulot» = work, a job.", "wrong": "«Le boulot» is familiar for work or a job. «Il cherche du boulot» = He's looking for work."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Money: «le fric», «la thune» (or «les thunes»), «le blé». «J'ai plus de thune» = I've got no money left. Food: «la bouffe» (food) and «bouffer» (to eat, to scoff)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «J'ai pas de fric» mean?", "choices": ["I don't have a fridge.", "I don't have any money.", "I don't have any friends."], "answer": "I don't have any money.", "right": "Yes! «Le fric» = money.", "wrong": "«Le fric» is slang for money. A fridge is «un frigo», another familiar word!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "People and things: «un mec» (a guy), «une nana» (a girl or woman, friendly, a bit old-fashioned), «une bagnole» (a car), «les fringues» (clothes), «un truc» (a thing, a whatsit)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Mon mec» and «ma nana» can mean 'my boyfriend' and 'my girlfriend'! «C'est mon mec» = That's my boyfriend."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is the casual way to say 'I bought new clothes'?", "choices": ["J'ai acheté de nouvelles fringues.", "J'ai acheté de nouvelles bagnoles.", "J'ai acheté de la nouvelle bouffe."], "answer": "J'ai acheté de nouvelles fringues.", "right": "Bravo! «Les fringues» = clothes.", "wrong": "«Les fringues» = clothes. «Bagnoles» are cars and «la bouffe» is food."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le mec avec la grosse bagnole, c'est le copain de ma sœur.", "en": "The guy with the big car is my sister's boyfriend."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word is NOT slang for money?", "choices": ["la thune", "le fric", "la bouffe"], "answer": "la bouffe", "right": "Correct! «La bouffe» is food.", "wrong": "«La thune» and «le fric» both mean money. «La bouffe» means food."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Register check: all these words are «familier». They're fine with friends or a colleague at the coffee machine. Avoid them in interviews, with officials, and in writing. Standard words: travailler, travail, argent, nourriture, voiture, vêtements, homme, femme."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: bosser / le boulot → travailler / le travail. Le fric / la thune → l'argent. La bouffe → la nourriture. Un mec / une nana → un homme / une femme. Une bagnole → une voiture. Les fringues → les vêtements."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in slang: I'm working tomorrow.", "answer": "Je bosse demain.", "accept": ["je bosse demain", "demain je bosse", "Demain, je bosse.", "demain, je bosse"], "hint": "The slang verb for 'to work'", "right": "Super! Very natural.", "wrong": "Answer: «Je bosse demain.» «Bosser» = to work (familier)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, casually: «Je bosse demain, j'ai besoin de thune !»", "fr": "Je bosse demain, j'ai besoin de thune !"}]}, "50-2": {"stageId": "50-2", "title": "Le verlan (l'envers)", "goal": "Understand how verlan works and recognise the most common verlan words (ouf, chelou, relou, vénère, meuf, zarbi, téma, laisse béton).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Verlan» is the word «l'envers» ('the reverse') flipped: «l'envers» → «vers-l'en» → «verlan». You swap the syllables of a word. It was born in the suburbs («les banlieues») and is now everywhere in young people's speech."}, {"t": "say", "text": "How it works: split the word into syllables and reverse them. «fou» → «ouf», «louche» (shady) → «chelou», «lourd» (tiresome) → «relou», «énervé» → «vénère», «bizarre» → «zarbi», «mater» (to look at) → «téma», «femme» → «meuf»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est ouf !", "en": "That's crazy!", "note": "From «fou». Now used by almost everyone under 40, even on TV."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Chelou» comes from which word?", "choices": ["louche", "cheval", "chez lui"], "answer": "louche", "right": "Exactly! «lou-che» → «che-lou». Weird or shady.", "wrong": "«Chelou» is «louche» flipped: «lou-che» → «che-lou». It means weird, dodgy."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Une meuf» = a girl or woman (from «femme»), and «ma meuf» = my girlfriend. Some verlan words have even been flipped again: «meuf» → «feumeu»! You'll mostly hear plain «meuf»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Elle est vénère» mean?", "choices": ["She's venerable.", "She's angry.", "She's tired."], "answer": "She's angry.", "right": "Bravo! «Énervé» → «vénère»: angry, wound up.", "wrong": "«Vénère» comes from «énervé» (annoyed, angry). «Elle est vénère» = She's really angry."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Relou» = annoying, from «lourd». In standard French, «Il est lourd» already means he's tiresome or pushy. Verlan just makes it sound cooler."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est relou, il arrête pas de m'appeler.", "en": "He's so annoying, he won't stop calling me."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Téma ça !» mean?", "choices": ["Take that!", "Look at that!", "Tell me that!"], "answer": "Look at that!", "right": "Yes! «Mater» → «téma»: to look, to check out.", "wrong": "«Téma» is «mater» (to look at, slang) flipped. «Téma ça !» = Look at that!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Laisse béton» = «laisse tomber» (forget it). It's older verlan, made famous by a 1977 song by Renaud. Today it sounds a bit retro or jokey."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «C'est zarbi» mean?", "choices": ["It's weird.", "It's a zebra.", "It's a bar."], "answer": "It's weird.", "right": "Exactly! «Bizarre» → «zarbi».", "wrong": "«Zarbi» is «bizarre» flipped: «C'est zarbi» = It's weird."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Don't invent your own verlan! Only a few dozen words are really common. Making up new ones will just sound strange. Stick to the classics."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Register: verlan is very familiar, youth and street speech. Using «C'est ouf» with friends is fine and fun. But verlan with older people, at work or in writing sounds forced or disrespectful."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: ouf (fou), chelou (louche), relou (lourd), vénère (énervé), meuf (femme), zarbi (bizarre), téma (mater), laisse béton (laisse tomber)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in verlan: It's weird. (Flip «louche».)", "answer": "C'est chelou.", "accept": ["c'est chelou", "c'est chelou !", "c'est zarbi", "C'est zarbi."], "hint": "lou-che → ?", "right": "Trop bien! You've cracked the code.", "wrong": "Answer: «C'est chelou.» (from «louche»). «C'est zarbi» also works."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with attitude: «C'est ouf ! Il est trop relou, lui.»", "fr": "C'est ouf ! Il est trop relou, lui."}]}, "50-3": {"stageId": "50-3", "title": "Kiffer, galérer & co", "goal": "Understand and use the key familiar verbs of spoken French: kiffer, galérer, piger, capter, se casser, flipper, saouler, bouffer.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Familiar verbs are the backbone of casual French. Learn these and suddenly half of what young French people say makes sense."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Kiffer» = to love, to really enjoy: «Je kiffe cette chanson.» «Galérer» = to struggle, to have a hard time (from «une galère», a galley ship). «C'est la galère» = It's a nightmare."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai galéré pour trouver un appart.", "en": "I really struggled to find a flat.", "note": "«Appart» = appartement. «Galérer pour» + infinitive."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «J'ai galéré» mean?", "choices": ["I had a blast.", "I really struggled.", "I went sailing."], "answer": "I really struggled.", "right": "Exactly! «Galérer» = to struggle.", "wrong": "«Galérer» means to struggle or have a hard time. Having a blast would be «Je me suis éclaté» or «J'ai kiffé»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Understanding: «piger» = to get it («T'as pigé ?»). «Capter» = to get it, OR to have phone signal, OR to catch up with someone: «Je capte rien» (I don't get it / I've got no signal), «On se capte plus tard ?» (Catch you later?)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What can «Je capte rien» mean?", "choices": ["I caught nothing (fishing).", "I don't get it at all / I've got no signal.", "I can't hear the music."], "answer": "I don't get it at all / I've got no signal.", "right": "Yes! Context tells you which one.", "wrong": "«Capter» = to understand OR to get a signal. «Je capte rien» = I don't get anything / no signal."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Leaving: «se casser» = to leave, clear off. «Je me casse» = I'm off. «Je me suis cassé» = I got out of there. «Casse-toi !» is rude: get lost."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch out: «Je me suis cassé la jambe» = I broke my leg (standard French!). With a body part it means 'break'. Alone, «Je me suis cassé» (slang) means 'I left'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Il s'est cassé le bras» mean?", "choices": ["He left his arm behind.", "He broke his arm.", "He left in a hurry."], "answer": "He broke his arm.", "right": "Correct! With a body part, «se casser» = to break.", "wrong": "«Se casser le bras» = to break one's arm. Only «se casser» on its own means 'to leave' in slang."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Feelings: «flipper» = to freak out, be scared («Arrête de flipper !», «Ça me fait flipper»). «Saouler» (also spelled «soûler») = to get on your nerves («Tu me saoules !»). «Bouffer» = to eat, to scoff."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Arrête de flipper, ça va bien se passer.", "en": "Stop freaking out, it'll be fine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend won't stop talking about crypto. «Il me ___ avec ses histoires.»", "choices": ["saoule", "kiffe", "capte"], "answer": "saoule", "right": "Bravo! «Il me saoule» = he's getting on my nerves.", "wrong": "It's «saoule»: «Il me saoule avec ses histoires» = he's getting on my nerves with his stories."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "They're all regular -er verbs: «je kiffe, j'ai kiffé». «Galérer» changes its accent like «préférer»: «je galère» but «j'ai galéré»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: kiffer (love), galérer (struggle), piger / capter (get it), se casser (leave), flipper (freak out), saouler (annoy), bouffer (eat). All familiar: friends only!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (casual): I love this song.", "answer": "Je kiffe cette chanson.", "accept": ["je kiffe cette chanson", "je kiffe trop cette chanson", "Je kiffe trop cette chanson.", "je kiffe grave cette chanson"], "hint": "The slang verb from Arabic «kif»", "right": "Trop bien! Very natural.", "wrong": "Answer: «Je kiffe cette chanson.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «J'ai galéré, mais j'ai pigé ! Je kiffe le français.»", "fr": "J'ai galéré, mais j'ai pigé ! Je kiffe le français."}]}, "50-4": {"stageId": "50-4", "title": "Les intensifs", "goal": "Intensify like a native with très, super, hyper, trop, grave and vachement, and react with top, nul and un truc de fou, knowing the register of each.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "English has 'very, really, so, super'. French has a ladder: «très» (neutral) → «vraiment», «super», «hyper» (everyday) → «trop», «grave», «vachement» (familiar)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est trop bien !", "en": "It's so good!", "note": "In casual speech, «trop» = 'so, really', not only 'too much'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«C'est trop cher» can mean 'too expensive' (standard) or 'so expensive' (casual). Context and tone decide. «Il est trop mignon !» is pure praise: He's so cute!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A teenager says «Ce film est trop bien !». What does she mean?", "choices": ["It's too good to be true.", "It's really great.", "It's overrated."], "answer": "It's really great.", "right": "Exactly! Casual «trop» = so, really.", "wrong": "In casual speech, «trop bien» just means 'really great'. «Trop» works as an intensifier here."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Super» and «hyper» go before adjectives: «Il fait super chaud», «Je suis hyper fatigué(e)». Adults use them too. «Vachement» (yes, from «vache», cow!) also means really: «C'est vachement cher». It's a bit old-school but still very much alive."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is the safest, most neutral choice at work?", "choices": ["C'est très intéressant.", "C'est grave intéressant.", "C'est vachement intéressant."], "answer": "C'est très intéressant.", "right": "Parfait! «Très» works in any register.", "wrong": "At work, «très» (or «vraiment») is safest. «Grave» is youth slang, and «vachement» is familiar."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Grave» as an intensifier is young and familiar. It goes before an adjective or after a verb: «C'est grave bien», «Je kiffe grave», «J'ai grave la flemme»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai grave la flemme d'aller au sport.", "en": "I really can't be bothered to go to the gym."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Big reactions: «C'est top !» (great), «C'est génial !», «Un truc de fou !» (something crazy or amazing). Negative: «C'est nul !» (it's rubbish), «C'est naze» (familiar)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Un truc de fou !» describe?", "choices": ["A mad person", "Something amazing or crazy", "A broken object"], "answer": "Something amazing or crazy", "right": "Yes! «Truc» = thing, «de fou» = crazy, so an incredible thing.", "wrong": "«Un truc de fou» = something crazy or amazing. «Truc» = thing."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Don't stack intensifiers: ✗ «très trop bien». And before a noun, «trop de» means too much / too many: «Il y a trop de monde» = There are too many people."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'It's really expensive' using «vache»?", "choices": ["C'est vachement cher.", "C'est vache cher.", "C'est cher vachement."], "answer": "C'est vachement cher.", "right": "Bravo! The adverb «vachement» goes before the adjective.", "wrong": "The adverb is «vachement», and it goes before the adjective: «C'est vachement cher.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: neutral «très / vraiment». Everyday «super / hyper». Familiar «trop / vachement / grave». Reactions: «C'est top !», «Un truc de fou !», «C'est nul !»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (casual, with «trop»): It's so good!", "answer": "C'est trop bien !", "accept": ["c'est trop bien", "c'est trop bien!", "c'est trop bien !", "c'est trop cool"], "hint": "c'est + trop + 'good'", "right": "Trop bien, justement!", "wrong": "Answer: «C'est trop bien !»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il fait super chaud, mais c'est trop bien !»", "fr": "Il fait super chaud, mais c'est trop bien !"}]}, "50-5": {"stageId": "50-5", "title": "Registres : quand ne pas le dire", "goal": "Switch between familier, courant and soutenu, turning slang into standard and formal French depending on who you're talking to.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Knowing slang is half the job. The other half is knowing when NOT to use it. French has three main registers: «familier» (friends), «courant» (everyday standard), «soutenu» (formal, written)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "une bagnole → une voiture → un véhicule", "en": "a car: slang → standard → formal or official", "note": "Same thing, three registers."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The golden rule: match your listener. With friends, familier is fine. With shops, strangers and new colleagues, use courant. For interviews, officials, letters and client emails, use courant or soutenu."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a job interview, you're asked why you left your last job. What do you say?", "choices": ["Je me suis cassé, le boulot me saoulait.", "J'ai quitté ce poste car je souhaitais évoluer.", "J'en avais marre, c'était relou."], "answer": "J'ai quitté ce poste car je souhaitais évoluer.", "right": "Parfait! Professional and positive.", "wrong": "In an interview, slang («se casser», «saouler», «relou») sounds unprofessional. Say: «J'ai quitté ce poste car je souhaitais évoluer.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Key conversions: «Je me casse» → «Je m'en vais / Je dois y aller». «C'est relou» → «C'est pénible». «Je suis fauché» → «Je n'ai pas d'argent». «Il est vénère» → «Il est en colère». «Ça me saoule» → «Cela m'agace»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What's the formal equivalent of «Ça me saoule»?", "choices": ["Cela m'agace.", "Ça me kiffe.", "Je suis saoul."], "answer": "Cela m'agace.", "right": "Bravo! «Cela» + «agacer»: formal.", "wrong": "The formal version is «Cela m'agace.» Careful: «Je suis saoul» means 'I'm drunk'!"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Register isn't just vocabulary. Grammar shows it too: dropping «ne» («je sais pas»), «ça» vs «cela», «on» vs «nous», «t'as» vs «tu as», questions by intonation vs inversion. In professional writing, put «ne» back and use «nous»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je ne sais pas, avez-vous vu cela ?", "en": "I don't know, have you seen that?", "note": "The spoken familiar version: «Je sais pas, t'as vu ça ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In an email to a client, which is correct?", "choices": ["Je sais pas si on peut.", "Je ne sais pas si nous pouvons.", "J'sais pas si on peut."], "answer": "Je ne sais pas si nous pouvons.", "right": "Exactly! Full negation and «nous» for a written, professional tone.", "wrong": "In professional writing, keep «ne» and use «nous»: «Je ne sais pas si nous pouvons.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "People words: «un mec» → «un homme» → «un monsieur». When talking to staff or officials about someone, say «ce monsieur» or «cette dame». «Le mec là-bas» would sound disrespectful."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You're speaking to a receptionist: «___ là-bas m'a dit de venir ici.»", "choices": ["Le mec", "Le monsieur", "Le type"], "answer": "Le monsieur", "right": "Parfait! Polite and natural.", "wrong": "With a receptionist, say «Le monsieur là-bas…». «Le mec» and «le type» are familiar."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Too much slang from a learner can backfire: it can sound funny or forced. Aim to understand everything, use a few safe words with friends («c'est ouf», «j'ai la flemme», «bosser»), and always keep the courant version ready."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Upgrade «Je bosse dans une boîte de com» for a formal context.", "choices": ["Je travaille dans une agence de communication.", "Je bosse dans une agence de communication.", "Je taffe dans une boîte de com."], "answer": "Je travaille dans une agence de communication.", "right": "Bravo! «Travailler» and the full name: perfectly professional.", "wrong": "«Bosser», «taffer» and «une boîte de com» are all familiar. Formal: «Je travaille dans une agence de communication.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: familier → courant → soutenu. Se casser → s'en aller. Relou → pénible. Fauché → sans argent. Vénère → en colère. Ça me saoule → cela m'agace. Mec → homme → monsieur. Bagnole → voiture → véhicule. Match your listener!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Rewrite in standard French: «Il est vénère.»", "answer": "Il est en colère.", "accept": ["il est en colère", "il est fâché", "Il est fâché.", "il est très en colère"], "hint": "être en + the noun for 'anger'", "right": "Parfait! You've switched register like a native.", "wrong": "Answer: «Il est en colère.» (or «Il est fâché.»)"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, in polished register: «Excusez-moi, je dois y aller. Bonne journée !»", "fr": "Excusez-moi, je dois y aller. Bonne journée !"}]}, "51-1": {"stageId": "51-1", "title": "Le corps", "goal": "Understand and use eight everyday French idioms built on body parts, and know when they fit.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Native French is full of images, and the body is everywhere. A Parisian won't say 'it's very expensive' — she'll say it costs 'the eyes of the head': «Ça coûte les yeux de la tête.» Today we collect eight of these little pictures."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce sac coûte les yeux de la tête.", "en": "This bag costs an arm and a leg.", "note": "English pays with an arm and a leg; French pays with the eyes."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a mis les pieds dans le plat en parlant de son ex.", "en": "He put his foot in it by mentioning his ex.", "note": "Literally 'he put his feet in the dish' — a social blunder."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend sighs: «Tu me casses les pieds !» What does she mean?", "choices": ["You're stepping on my feet.", "You're getting on my nerves.", "You broke my foot."], "answer": "You're getting on my nerves.", "right": "Exactly! «Casser les pieds à quelqu'un» = to annoy someone. Very common, familier.", "wrong": "It's an idiom: «Tu me casses les pieds !» = 'You're getting on my nerves!' Nobody's feet are hurt. A related adjective: «Il est casse-pieds» = he's a pain."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Il a un poil dans la main» means he's lazy. The image: his hand has done so little work that a hair has had time to grow on his palm!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your colleague never lifts a finger. Which idiom fits?", "choices": ["Il a le bras long.", "Il a un poil dans la main.", "Il n'a pas froid aux yeux."], "answer": "Il a un poil dans la main.", "right": "Parfait ! A hair growing in the palm = he never works.", "wrong": "The lazy one is «Il a un poil dans la main.» «Avoir le bras long» = to have influence; «ne pas avoir froid aux yeux» = to be fearless."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two more: «avoir le bras long» — a long arm reaches far, so it means having connections or influence. And «ne pas avoir froid aux yeux» — 'not to have cold eyes' — means to be bold, fearless."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle n'a pas froid aux yeux : elle a démissionné pour faire le tour du monde.", "en": "She's fearless: she quit her job to travel around the world."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Son oncle a le bras long, il lui a trouvé un stage.", "en": "His uncle has connections; he found him an internship."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Tenir la jambe à quelqu'un» = to keep someone talking when they want to leave (they're 'holding your leg'). And «se mettre le doigt dans l'œil» = to be totally wrong. Natives intensify it: «…jusqu'au coude !» (up to the elbow!). Both are familier — great with friends, not in a job interview."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Si tu crois qu'il va t'aider, tu te mets le doigt dans l'œil.» Meaning?", "choices": ["You're about to cry.", "You're completely wrong.", "You're not paying attention."], "answer": "You're completely wrong.", "right": "Oui ! «Se mettre le doigt dans l'œil» = to be badly mistaken.", "wrong": "«Se mettre le doigt dans l'œil» = to be completely wrong: 'If you think he'll help you, you're kidding yourself.'"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "At dinner, Paul mentions the divorce nobody was supposed to know about. You say:", "choices": ["Il m'a tenu la jambe.", "Il a le bras long.", "Il a mis les pieds dans le plat."], "answer": "Il a mis les pieds dans le plat.", "right": "Bravo ! That's the classic blunder idiom.", "wrong": "A tactless blunder = «mettre les pieds dans le plat». «Tenir la jambe» = to talk someone's ear off."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «les yeux de la tête» (very expensive), «casser les pieds» (annoy), «mettre les pieds dans le plat» (blunder), «un poil dans la main» (lazy), «le bras long» (influence), «tenir la jambe» (talk your ear off), «le doigt dans l'œil» (totally wrong), «pas froid aux yeux» (fearless)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It costs an arm and a leg.", "answer": "Ça coûte les yeux de la tête.", "accept": ["ça coûte les yeux de la tête", "ça coute les yeux de la tête", "Ça coute les yeux de la tête."], "hint": "Think 'the eyes of the head'.", "right": "Excellent ! You're paying with your eyes now, like a real French speaker.", "wrong": "The idiom is «Ça coûte les yeux de la tête.» — literally 'it costs the eyes of the head'."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Elle m'a tenu la jambe pendant une heure !»", "fr": "Elle m'a tenu la jambe pendant une heure !"}]}, "51-2": {"stageId": "51-2", "title": "La nourriture", "goal": "Master eight food-based French idioms and use them naturally in conversation.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "France is obsessed with food — so of course its idioms are too! Peaches, salads, apples, beans and onions all carry hidden meanings. Let's start with energy: «J'ai la pêche !» = I'm full of beans!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai bien dormi, j'ai la pêche ce matin !", "en": "I slept well, I'm full of energy this morning!", "note": "Synonym: «J'ai la frite !» — both familier."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now a dramatic one: «tomber dans les pommes» = to faint. Despite the apples, it has nothing to do with fruit — it probably comes from an old expression «être dans les pâmes» (to be in a swoon)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il faisait si chaud qu'elle est tombée dans les pommes.» What happened?", "choices": ["She fell into the apple basket.", "She fainted.", "She got angry."], "answer": "She fainted.", "right": "Oui ! «Tomber dans les pommes» = to pass out.", "wrong": "«Tomber dans les pommes» = to faint: 'It was so hot she passed out.'"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Arrête de raconter des salades, je sais que tu n'étais pas malade.", "en": "Stop telling stories, I know you weren't sick.", "note": "«Raconter des salades» = to tell lies or tall tales."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Workload and money: «J'ai du pain sur la planche» = I've got a lot on my plate (bread on the board, waiting to be baked). And «Ça met du beurre dans les épinards» = it brings in a bit of extra money, it makes life easier — butter makes spinach taste better!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your weekend tutoring job brings in a little extra cash. You say:", "choices": ["J'ai du pain sur la planche.", "Ça met du beurre dans les épinards.", "C'est la fin des haricots."], "answer": "Ça met du beurre dans les épinards.", "right": "Exactement ! Extra money that makes life nicer = butter in the spinach.", "wrong": "Extra income = «Ça met du beurre dans les épinards.» «Du pain sur la planche» = lots of work; «la fin des haricots» = all is lost."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«C'est la fin des haricots» = it's all over, we're done for (beans were the last food left when everything else ran out). «Haut comme trois pommes» = tiny, knee-high — used for small children."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je la connais depuis qu'elle est haute comme trois pommes.", "en": "I've known her since she was knee-high.", "note": "Note the agreement: «haute» for a girl."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Oignons» here means 'business, affairs'. «C'est pas mes oignons» = it's none of my business. Grammatically correct French would be «Ce ne sont pas mes oignons», but in speech everyone says «c'est pas». Ruder version: «Occupe-toi de tes oignons !» = Mind your own business!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A nosy neighbour asks how much you earn. Cheeky reply?", "choices": ["Occupe-toi de tes oignons !", "J'ai la pêche !", "Il est haut comme trois pommes."], "answer": "Occupe-toi de tes oignons !", "right": "Ha, parfait ! Cheeky but very natural. (Use «Occupez-vous de vos oignons» if you «vous» them.)", "wrong": "'Mind your own business' = «Occupe-toi de tes oignons !» The onions are your private affairs."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which idiom means you're overloaded with work?", "choices": ["J'ai du pain sur la planche.", "Je raconte des salades.", "Je suis tombé dans les pommes."], "answer": "J'ai du pain sur la planche.", "right": "Bravo ! Bread on the board = lots of work ahead.", "wrong": "Lots of work = «J'ai du pain sur la planche.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «avoir la pêche» (energy), «raconter des salades» (lie), «tomber dans les pommes» (faint), «la fin des haricots» (all is lost), «haut comme trois pommes» (tiny), «du pain sur la planche» (lots of work), «du beurre dans les épinards» (extra money), «pas mes oignons» (not my business)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I've got a lot on my plate.", "answer": "J'ai du pain sur la planche.", "accept": ["j'ai du pain sur la planche", "J’ai du pain sur la planche."], "hint": "Bread… on a board.", "right": "Super ! Now go get that bread baked.", "wrong": "It's «J'ai du pain sur la planche.» — bread waiting on the board."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Arrête de raconter des salades !»", "fr": "Arrête de raconter des salades !"}]}, "51-3": {"stageId": "51-3", "title": "Les animaux", "goal": "Use eight animal idioms and avoid translating English animal idioms literally.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "English and French both love animal idioms — but the animals don't always match! In English you have a frog in your throat; in French it's a cat: «J'ai un chat dans la gorge.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Excusez-moi, j'ai un chat dans la gorge.", "en": "Excuse me, I've got a frog in my throat."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How does a French speaker say 'When pigs fly!'?", "choices": ["Quand les cochons voleront !", "Quand les poules auront des dents !", "Quand les chats aboieront !"], "answer": "Quand les poules auront des dents !", "right": "Exactement ! Hens will never have teeth — so: never.", "wrong": "The French version is «Quand les poules auront des dents !» (when hens have teeth). Flying pigs mean nothing in French."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Poser un lapin à quelqu'un» = to stand someone up. Literally 'to put a rabbit on someone'. Nobody really knows why — just learn it, natives use it all the time."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On avait rendez-vous à huit heures, et il m'a posé un lapin.", "en": "We were supposed to meet at eight, and he stood me up."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il m'a posé un lapin.» What happened?", "choices": ["He gave me a pet.", "He didn't show up.", "He told me a lie."], "answer": "He didn't show up.", "right": "Oui ! He stood you up. Poor you.", "wrong": "«Poser un lapin» = to stand someone up — he never came."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Weather and mood: «Il fait un froid de canard» = it's freezing (duck-hunting weather!). «J'ai le cafard» = I'm feeling down — «le cafard» is literally a cockroach, an image borrowed from Baudelaire for gloom."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You feel a bit blue today. You say:", "choices": ["J'ai le cafard.", "J'ai un chat dans la gorge.", "Il fait un froid de canard."], "answer": "J'ai le cafard.", "right": "Bravo ! «Avoir le cafard» = to feel down. (I hope you don't, though!)", "wrong": "Feeling down = «J'ai le cafard.» A cat in the throat = hoarse; «froid de canard» = freezing weather."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Une poule mouillée» = a wet hen = a coward, a 'chicken'. And when someone asks you a riddle and you have no idea: «Je donne ma langue au chat» = I give up, tell me!"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Revenons à nos moutons» = let's get back to the point. It comes from a 15th-century farce where a judge kept saying it to drag the lawyers back to the case of stolen sheep. Perfect in meetings when people drift off-topic!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The meeting has drifted to holiday plans. The manager says:", "choices": ["Je donne ma langue au chat.", "C'est une poule mouillée.", "Revenons à nos moutons."], "answer": "Revenons à nos moutons.", "right": "Parfait ! Back to our sheep — back to business.", "wrong": "To get back on topic: «Revenons à nos moutons.» «Donner sa langue au chat» = to give up guessing."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «poser un lapin» (stand up), «un chat dans la gorge» (frog in throat), «un froid de canard» (freezing), «une poule mouillée» (coward), «avoir le cafard» (feel down), «quand les poules auront des dents» (never), «donner sa langue au chat» (give up guessing), «revenons à nos moutons» (back to the point)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: He stood me up.", "answer": "Il m'a posé un lapin.", "accept": ["il m'a posé un lapin", "il m’a posé un lapin", "Il m'a posé un lapin !"], "hint": "A rabbit is involved.", "right": "Très bien ! A rabbit, not a no-show.", "wrong": "It's «Il m'a posé un lapin.» — passé composé of «poser un lapin»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je donne ma langue au chat !»", "fr": "Je donne ma langue au chat !"}]}, "51-4": {"stageId": "51-4", "title": "La vie quotidienne", "goal": "Describe everyday situations — weather, love, sleep, timing — with natural French idioms.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Everyday life in French is full of pictures. Sunday morning? You don't 'sleep in' — you do the 'fat morning': «Je fais la grasse matinée.» Natives shorten it: «la grasse mat'»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Dimanche, je fais la grasse matinée jusqu'à midi.", "en": "On Sunday, I sleep in until noon."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Weather: English rains cats and dogs, French rains ropes: «Il pleut des cordes.» Picture the rain so heavy it looks like ropes hanging from the sky."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say 'It's raining cats and dogs'?", "choices": ["Il pleut des chats et des chiens.", "Il pleut des cordes.", "Il fait un froid de canard."], "answer": "Il pleut des cordes.", "right": "Bravo ! Ropes, not pets. You can also say «Il pleut à verse.»", "wrong": "Never translate the animals! It's «Il pleut des cordes.» (or «Il pleut à verse»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Love: «le coup de foudre» = love at first sight. «La foudre» is lightning — love strikes you like a bolt from the sky. And a dreamy person «est dans la lune» — on the moon, i.e. daydreaming."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quand je l'ai vue, ça a été le coup de foudre.", "en": "When I saw her, it was love at first sight."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your student isn't listening, staring out of the window. He…", "choices": ["a le coup de foudre.", "est dans la lune.", "fait la grasse matinée."], "answer": "est dans la lune.", "right": "Oui ! He's on the moon — daydreaming.", "wrong": "Daydreaming = «être dans la lune»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Ça tombe bien !» = Good timing! / That works out! Its opposite: «Ça tombe mal.» = Bad timing. Example: «Tu as besoin d'une voiture ? Ça tombe bien, je vends la mienne !»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Tirer les vers du nez à quelqu'un» = to worm information out of someone (literally to pull the worms out of their nose!). And English kills two birds with one stone; French simply makes two hits with one stone: «faire d'une pierre deux coups»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You pick up your parcel and visit your grandma on the same trip. You…", "choices": ["as fait d'une pierre deux coups.", "as tué deux oiseaux.", "as tiré les vers du nez."], "answer": "as fait d'une pierre deux coups.", "right": "Exactement ! Two hits, one stone — and no birds harmed.", "wrong": "The French idiom is «faire d'une pierre deux coups». No birds in French!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Finally, to reassure someone: «Ce n'est pas la mer à boire» — it's not like drinking the sea, i.e. it's not that hard. In speech: «C'est pas la mer à boire.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend is scared of a small exam. You reassure her:", "choices": ["Ça tombe mal.", "C'est pas la mer à boire.", "Il pleut des cordes."], "answer": "C'est pas la mer à boire.", "right": "Parfait ! You don't have to drink the sea — it's doable.", "wrong": "To say 'it's not that hard': «C'est pas la mer à boire.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «la grasse matinée» (sleep in), «dans la lune» (daydreaming), «le coup de foudre» (love at first sight), «il pleut des cordes» (pouring), «ça tombe bien» (good timing), «tirer les vers du nez» (get info out of someone), «d'une pierre deux coups» (two birds, one stone), «pas la mer à boire» (not that hard)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I killed two birds with one stone.", "answer": "J'ai fait d'une pierre deux coups.", "accept": ["j'ai fait d'une pierre deux coups", "j’ai fait d’une pierre deux coups"], "hint": "One stone, two hits.", "right": "Super ! Efficient and idiomatic.", "wrong": "It's «J'ai fait d'une pierre deux coups.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Il pleut des cordes, je fais la grasse matinée !»", "fr": "Il pleut des cordes, je fais la grasse matinée !"}]}, "51-5": {"stageId": "51-5", "title": "Argumenter avec des images", "goal": "Use idioms to debate, negotiate, and push back in French discussions.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In a French debate — and French people love debating — idioms are weapons. If an argument is weak, you say it doesn't hold the road: «Ça ne tient pas la route.» English says it doesn't hold water."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ton argument ne tient pas la route.", "en": "Your argument doesn't hold water."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Getting to the point: «Je ne vais pas y aller par quatre chemins» = I won't beat around the bush (I won't take four paths). And to someone dodging: «Arrête de tourner autour du pot !» = stop circling the pot!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which phrase announces that YOU are going to be direct?", "choices": ["Je ne vais pas y aller par quatre chemins.", "Arrête de tourner autour du pot.", "Tu enfonces une porte ouverte."], "answer": "Je ne vais pas y aller par quatre chemins.", "right": "Oui ! You announce you'll be blunt. «Tourner autour du pot» is what you accuse others of.", "wrong": "«Je ne vais pas y aller par quatre chemins» = I'll be direct. «Arrête de tourner autour du pot» tells someone else to stop dodging."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Negotiating: «Coupons la poire en deux» = let's split the pear in two = let's meet halfway. And «mettre de l'eau dans son vin» = to water down one's wine = to tone down one's demands, to compromise."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu veux 100 euros, moi 60 : coupons la poire en deux, 80.", "en": "You want 100 euros, I want 60: let's meet halfway, 80."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Two parties both soften their positions. They…", "choices": ["ont mis de l'eau dans leur vin.", "ont eu le dernier mot.", "ont enfoncé une porte ouverte."], "answer": "ont mis de l'eau dans leur vin.", "right": "Bravo ! Watering the wine = becoming less extreme.", "wrong": "Compromising = «mettre de l'eau dans son vin»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Calling out a useless point: «Tu enfonces une porte ouverte» = you're breaking down an open door = you're stating the obvious. And the stubborn debater «veut toujours avoir le dernier mot» — always wants the last word."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone solemnly declares: «Il faut de l'argent pour acheter une maison.» You reply:", "choices": ["Coupons la poire en deux.", "Tu enfonces une porte ouverte.", "Ça tombe bien."], "answer": "Tu enfonces une porte ouverte.", "right": "Ha, exactement ! Obvious statement = pushing an open door.", "wrong": "Stating the obvious = «enfoncer une porte ouverte»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "A sophisticated one for essays and meetings: «C'est l'arbre qui cache la forêt» — a small visible detail hiding a much bigger problem. Variant: «Il ne faut pas que l'arbre cache la forêt» = don't let the detail hide the big picture."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce petit bug, c'est l'arbre qui cache la forêt : tout le système est mal conçu.", "en": "This little bug is just the tip of the iceberg: the whole system is badly designed."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ça ne tient pas la route» is best translated as:", "choices": ["It's not on the road.", "It doesn't hold water.", "It's a long way off."], "answer": "It doesn't hold water.", "right": "Parfait ! An argument that doesn't hold up.", "wrong": "«Ça ne tient pas la route» = it doesn't hold water / doesn't stand up."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «ne pas tenir la route» (weak argument), «couper la poire en deux» (meet halfway), «ne pas y aller par quatre chemins» (be direct), «tourner autour du pot» (dodge), «enfoncer une porte ouverte» (state the obvious), «avoir le dernier mot» (last word), «l'arbre qui cache la forêt» (hidden bigger problem), «mettre de l'eau dans son vin» (compromise)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Let's meet halfway.", "answer": "Coupons la poire en deux.", "accept": ["coupons la poire en deux", "On coupe la poire en deux.", "on coupe la poire en deux"], "hint": "Split a fruit.", "right": "Deal ! Very natural.", "wrong": "It's «Coupons la poire en deux.» (or, casually, «On coupe la poire en deux»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Arrête de tourner autour du pot !»", "fr": "Arrête de tourner autour du pot !"}]}, "52-1": {"stageId": "52-1", "title": "Le monologue intérieur", "goal": "Build a French inner voice with the little phrases natives use when talking to themselves.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The secret to thinking in French: switch your inner voice. When you wake up, plan your day, lose your keys — say it to yourself in French. Natives start a thought with: «Bon, alors…» = Right, so…"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Bon, alors… par où commencer ?", "en": "Right, so… where do I start?"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Voyons voir… il me faut du lait, du pain et des œufs.", "en": "Let's see… I need milk, bread and eggs.", "note": "«Voyons voir» is redundant on purpose — very natural."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You open the fridge, wondering what to cook. Most natural self-talk?", "choices": ["Voyons voir…", "Revenons à nos moutons.", "Ceci dit…"], "answer": "Voyons voir…", "right": "Oui ! «Voyons voir…» is the classic 'let's see' while thinking.", "wrong": "When you're thinking something through: «Voyons voir…»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Little emergencies: «Attends, j'ai oublié mes clés !» — yes, you even say «attends» to yourself! And when you're late: «Mince !» — the polite version of a swear word (= darn!). «Zut !» works too."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You spill coffee in front of your boss. Which exclamation is safe?", "choices": ["Mince !", "Merde !", "Tant mieux !"], "answer": "Mince !", "right": "Bien joué ! «Mince» (or «zut») is the polite 'darn'.", "wrong": "«Mince !» is the safe, polite exclamation. «Merde !» is vulgar, and «tant mieux» means 'so much the better'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Reminding yourself: «Il faut que je pense à appeler maman.» = I need to remember to call mum. «Penser à + infinitive» = to remember to do. Note the subjunctive after «il faut que»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il faut que je pense à acheter du pain.» means:", "choices": ["I have to think about bread.", "I need to remember to buy bread.", "I think I'll buy bread."], "answer": "I need to remember to buy bread.", "right": "Exactement ! «Penser à faire» = remember to do.", "wrong": "«Penser à + infinitif» = to remember to do something: 'I need to remember to buy bread.'"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Motivating yourself: «Allez, au boulot !» = Come on, back to work! («le boulot» = familier for work). Letting go: «Tant pis, je le ferai demain.» = Oh well, I'll do it tomorrow."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Don't mix up «tant pis» (oh well, too bad — resigned) and «tant mieux» (so much the better — happy). «Il pleut ? Tant pis, je prends le parapluie.» / «Il fait beau ? Tant mieux !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The shop is closed. You shrug and think:", "choices": ["Tant mieux, j'irai demain.", "Tant pis, j'irai demain.", "Allez, au boulot !"], "answer": "Tant pis, j'irai demain.", "right": "Parfait ! Resigned acceptance = «tant pis».", "wrong": "For an annoying situation you accept: «Tant pis». «Tant mieux» is for good news."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap of your inner voice: «Bon, alors…», «Voyons voir…», «Attends…», «Qu'est-ce que je fais maintenant ?», «Il faut que je pense à…», «Allez, au boulot !», «Mince !», «Tant pis». Challenge: narrate tomorrow morning to yourself with these!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: Oh well, I'll do it tomorrow.", "answer": "Tant pis, je le ferai demain.", "accept": ["tant pis, je le ferai demain", "tant pis je le ferai demain"], "hint": "Tant… + futur of «faire».", "right": "Très bien ! «Je le ferai» — futur simple of «faire».", "wrong": "It's «Tant pis, je le ferai demain.» — «ferai» is the future of «faire»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, like you're talking to yourself: «Mince, je suis en retard ! Allez, au boulot !»", "fr": "Mince, je suis en retard ! Allez, au boulot !"}]}, "52-2": {"stageId": "52-2", "title": "Raconter ce qu'on fait, en direct", "goal": "Narrate what you are doing, have just done, and are about to do — in real time, in French.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "To think in French, narrate your life like a live commentator. French has no '-ing' tense, so to stress that something is happening right now, use «être en train de + infinitive»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je ne peux pas te parler, je suis en train de cuisiner.", "en": "I can't talk, I'm (busy) cooking."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The time machine: just past = «venir de + infinitive» («Je viens de rentrer» = I've just got home). Near future = «aller + infinitive» («Je vais prendre une douche»). Imminent = «être sur le point de» («Je suis sur le point de partir» = I'm about to leave)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I've just arrived.' Most natural French?", "choices": ["Je suis juste arrivé.", "Je viens d'arriver.", "Je vais arriver."], "answer": "Je viens d'arriver.", "right": "Parfait ! «Venir de + infinitif» is THE way to say 'have just'.", "wrong": "'Have just done' = «venir de + infinitif»: «Je viens d'arriver.» «Je suis juste arrivé» is an anglicism."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your coat is on, keys in hand. You're…", "choices": ["en train de partir.", "sur le point de partir.", "venu de partir."], "answer": "sur le point de partir.", "right": "Oui ! «Sur le point de» = on the verge of.", "wrong": "About to do something = «être sur le point de»: «Je suis sur le point de partir.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Big trap: 'I've been waiting for an hour'. English uses a perfect tense, French uses the PRESENT, because the waiting is still going on: «Ça fait une heure que j'attends.» or «J'attends depuis une heure.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ça fait vingt minutes que je t'___ !»", "choices": ["attends", "ai attendu", "attendais"], "answer": "attends", "right": "Bravo ! Action still going on → present tense.", "wrong": "With «ça fait… que» or «depuis» for an ongoing action, French uses the present: «Ça fait vingt minutes que je t'attends !»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je n'arrête pas de penser à ça.", "en": "I can't stop thinking about it.", "note": "«Ne pas arrêter de + infinitif» = to keep doing something."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Narrating your thoughts: «Je me demande si…» = I wonder if… and «J'ai l'impression que…» = I get the feeling that… Both are perfect inner-voice starters."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je me demande ___ c'est une bonne idée.»", "choices": ["que", "si", "quoi"], "answer": "si", "right": "Exactement ! 'Wonder if/whether' = «se demander si».", "wrong": "After «se demander», 'if/whether' = «si»: «Je me demande si c'est une bonne idée.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Daily drill: while you do chores, describe them out loud. «Je suis en train de faire la vaisselle. Je viens de finir. Je vais ranger la cuisine.» Ten seconds a day rewires your brain."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «en train de» (right now), «venir de» (just), «aller + inf.» (going to), «sur le point de» (about to), «ça fait… que + présent» (been doing), «ne pas arrêter de» (keep doing), «je me demande si», «j'ai l'impression que»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I've been waiting for an hour. (use «ça fait»)", "answer": "Ça fait une heure que j'attends.", "accept": ["ça fait une heure que j'attends", "ca fait une heure que j'attends", "ça fait une heure que j’attends", "J'attends depuis une heure.", "j'attends depuis une heure"], "hint": "Ça fait + time + que + present.", "right": "Parfait ! Present tense, because you're still waiting.", "wrong": "It's «Ça fait une heure que j'attends.» — present tense for an ongoing action."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je viens de rentrer et je vais prendre une douche.»", "fr": "Je viens de rentrer et je vais prendre une douche."}]}, "52-3": {"stageId": "52-3", "title": "Nuancer sa pensée", "goal": "Add nuance and sophistication to your opinions with high-level French discourse markers.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Au niveau C1–C2, ce qui distingue un natif, c'est la nuance. Instead of blunt 'yes/no', natives soften, concede and qualify. First, honesty: «À vrai dire…» = To tell the truth…"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "À vrai dire, je n'en sais rien.", "en": "To tell the truth, I have no idea.", "note": "«en» replaces «de ça»: I know nothing of it."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Si je ne m'abuse» = if I'm not mistaken. It's soutenu and a little playful — «s'abuser» means to be wrong. Everyday equivalent: «si je ne me trompe pas»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is the most formal way to say 'if I'm not mistaken'?", "choices": ["Si je ne m'abuse", "Si je me trompe pas", "Si j'ai bon"], "answer": "Si je ne m'abuse", "right": "Bravo ! Elegant, slightly literary — great for impressing.", "wrong": "The soutenu version is «si je ne m'abuse». «Si je me trompe pas» (no «ne») is casual speech."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Concession markers — 'still, all the same': «Toujours est-il que…» = the fact remains that… «N'empêche que…» (more oral) = all the same… «Il n'en reste pas moins que…» (formal) = nonetheless…"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "On a bien joué. Toujours est-il qu'on a perdu.", "en": "We played well. The fact remains that we lost."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Toujours est-il que» means:", "choices": ["It's always the case that", "The fact remains that", "Is it always true that"], "answer": "The fact remains that", "right": "Exactement ! The inversion «est-il» doesn't make it a question.", "wrong": "«Toujours est-il que» = 'the fact remains that'. It is not about 'always', and it's not a question despite the inversion."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Encore faut-il que + subjonctif» = 'X still has to happen, though' — a condition that may not be met. After «encore» at the start of a clause, formal French inverts subject and verb: «faut-il»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«C'est un bon plan. Encore faut-il qu'il ___ d'accord.»", "choices": ["est", "soit", "sera"], "answer": "soit", "right": "Parfait ! «Il faut que» → subjunctive: «qu'il soit».", "wrong": "«Faut-il que» triggers the subjunctive: «Encore faut-il qu'il soit d'accord.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Quitte à + infinitive, autant + infinitive» = if we're going to X anyway, we may as well Y. «Quitte à partir, autant partir tôt.» Very native, very elegant."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Quitte à payer, ___ prendre la meilleure place.»", "choices": ["autant", "pourtant", "tant"], "answer": "autant", "right": "Oui ! «Quitte à…, autant…» — a fixed pair.", "wrong": "The pair is «Quitte à…, autant…»: 'If we're paying anyway, may as well get the best seat.'"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Ce n'est pas tant X que Y» = it's not so much X as Y. «Ce n'est pas tant le prix que la qualité qui me gêne.» This structure instantly makes your French sound refined."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «à vrai dire», «si je ne m'abuse» (soutenu), «toujours est-il que», «n'empêche que» (oral), «il n'en reste pas moins que» (formal), «encore faut-il que + subj.», «quitte à…, autant…», «ce n'est pas tant… que…»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: To tell the truth, I have no idea.", "answer": "À vrai dire, je n'en sais rien.", "accept": ["à vrai dire, je n'en sais rien", "a vrai dire, je n'en sais rien", "à vrai dire je n'en sais rien", "à vrai dire, je n’en sais rien"], "hint": "À vrai… + «en» + «rien».", "right": "Excellent ! Don't forget that little «en».", "wrong": "It's «À vrai dire, je n'en sais rien.» — «en» = about it."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «N'empêche que c'était une bonne idée.»", "fr": "N'empêche que c'était une bonne idée."}]}, "52-4": {"stageId": "52-4", "title": "Les connecteurs du natif", "goal": "Use the small spoken connectors that make French conversation flow naturally.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Textbooks teach «cependant» and «néanmoins». But in real conversation, natives glue their sentences together with tiny words: «d'ailleurs», «par contre», «sinon», «en gros», «au fait»… Master these and you'll instantly sound fluent."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est bon. Par contre, c'est cher.", "en": "It's good. On the other hand, it's expensive.", "note": "Purists prefer «en revanche» in writing, but «par contre» rules in speech."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Sinon» literally means 'otherwise', but natives use it to change the subject: «Sinon, ça va ?» = Other than that, how are you? / Anyway, how's things?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "After discussing work, your friend says «Sinon, ça va ?». She means:", "choices": ["Otherwise, it won't work?", "Anyway, how are you doing?", "If not, is it OK?"], "answer": "Anyway, how are you doing?", "right": "Exactement ! «Sinon» = changing topic, 'apart from that'.", "wrong": "In conversation, «Sinon, ça va ?» = 'Anyway / apart from that, how are you?'"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Huge trap: «au fait» (by the way — new topic) vs «en fait» (actually — correcting). «Au fait, tu as vu Marie ?» vs «En fait, je ne suis pas d'accord.» Pronunciation: the t of «fait» is usually sounded in both: [ofɛt], [ɑ̃fɛt]."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You suddenly remember something unrelated. You start with:", "choices": ["En fait,", "Au fait,", "Par contre,"], "answer": "Au fait,", "right": "Bravo ! «Au fait» = by the way.", "wrong": "'By the way' = «Au fait». «En fait» = 'actually, in fact'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Summarising: «En gros» = basically, roughly. «Autrement dit» = in other words. And «d'ailleurs» = besides / actually, adding a supporting point: «D'ailleurs, je l'ai vu hier.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "En gros, il faut tout refaire. Autrement dit, on a perdu une semaine.", "en": "Basically, we have to redo everything. In other words, we've lost a week."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Quand même» is magic. It can mean 'all the same' («Il est venu quand même» = he came anyway) or add emphasis/indignation: «C'est quand même cher !» = It's really quite expensive, you have to admit!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il pleuvait, mais on est sortis quand même.» Here «quand même» means:", "choices": ["anyway", "when", "even if"], "answer": "anyway", "right": "Oui ! = 'we went out anyway'.", "wrong": "Here «quand même» = 'anyway, all the same': we went out despite the rain."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which connector concedes a point: 'That said, he's right'?", "choices": ["Sinon, il a raison.", "Ceci dit, il a raison.", "Au fait, il a raison."], "answer": "Ceci dit, il a raison.", "right": "Parfait ! «Ceci dit» (or «cela dit») = that said.", "wrong": "'That said' = «Ceci dit» / «Cela dit»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «d'ailleurs» (besides), «par contre» (on the other hand), «sinon» (anyway / apart from that), «en gros» (basically), «autrement dit» (in other words), «quand même» (anyway / emphasis), «au fait» (by the way), «ceci dit» (that said)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: By the way, have you seen Marie? (use «tu»)", "answer": "Au fait, tu as vu Marie ?", "accept": ["au fait, tu as vu marie", "au fait tu as vu marie", "Au fait, tu as vu Marie?", "au fait, as-tu vu marie", "au fait, tu as vu marie ?"], "hint": "Au… not En…", "right": "Super ! Natural spoken French.", "wrong": "It's «Au fait, tu as vu Marie ?» — «au fait» for 'by the way'."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «C'est bon. Par contre, c'est quand même cher !»", "fr": "C'est bon. Par contre, c'est quand même cher !"}]}, "52-5": {"stageId": "52-5", "title": "Penser sans traduire : expliquer en français", "goal": "Explain any word you don't know using French definitions and circumlocution instead of translating.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Even natives forget words! The key skill is not knowing every word, but explaining around it — in French, never stopping to translate. Three magic templates: «C'est ce qu'on utilise pour/quand…», «C'est quelqu'un qui…», «C'est un truc qui sert à…»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Un parapluie, c'est ce qu'on utilise quand il pleut.", "en": "An umbrella is what you use when it rains."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Un boulanger, c'est la personne qui fait le pain.", "en": "A baker is the person who makes bread."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You forgot the word 'mécanicien'. Best way to describe it?", "choices": ["C'est un truc qui répare les voitures.", "C'est quelqu'un qui répare les voitures.", "C'est ce que répare les voitures."], "answer": "C'est quelqu'un qui répare les voitures.", "right": "Parfait ! People → «quelqu'un qui» or «la personne qui».", "wrong": "For a person, use «quelqu'un qui» or «la personne qui»: «C'est quelqu'un qui répare les voitures.» «Un truc» is for things."}, {"t": "say", "text": "For objects: «C'est un truc qui sert à + infinitive» = it's a thing used for… «Un truc» (or «un machin») is informal but everyone uses it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Describe a corkscrew: «C'est un truc qui sert ___ ouvrir les bouteilles.»", "choices": ["pour", "à", "de"], "answer": "à", "right": "Bravo ! «Servir à + infinitif» = to be used for.", "wrong": "The verb is «servir à + infinitif»: «un truc qui sert à ouvrir les bouteilles.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Grammar check: «ce qu'on utilise» — «ce que» because «on» is the subject and the thing is the object. But «ce qui sert à…» — «ce qui» when the thing is the subject."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Une clé, c'est ___ on utilise pour ouvrir une porte.»", "choices": ["ce qui", "ce qu'", "qu'est-ce qu'"], "answer": "ce qu'", "right": "Exactement ! «On» is the subject → «ce qu'on utilise».", "wrong": "«On» is already the subject, so the object pronoun is «ce que» → «ce qu'on utilise»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "You can also define by opposites or synonyms: «C'est le contraire de facile» → difficile. «C'est comme… une sorte de…» And buy time like a native: «Comment dire ?», «Ça s'appelle comment, déjà ?», «Je ne trouve pas le mot.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Déjà» at the end of a question doesn't mean 'already' — it means 'again', asking to be reminded: «Il s'appelle comment, déjà ?» = What's his name again?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«C'est comment, son nom, déjà ?» means:", "choices": ["What's his name again?", "What's his name already?", "Is that his name yet?"], "answer": "What's his name again?", "right": "Oui ! «Déjà» = 'again' in reminder questions.", "wrong": "Here «déjà» = 'again': 'What's his name again?'"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «C'est ce qu'on utilise pour/quand…», «C'est la personne / quelqu'un qui…», «C'est un truc qui sert à…», «C'est le contraire de…», «Comment dire ?», «Ça s'appelle comment, déjà ?», «Je ne trouve pas le mot.» Never freeze — describe!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: It's someone who fixes cars.", "answer": "C'est quelqu'un qui répare les voitures.", "accept": ["c'est quelqu'un qui répare les voitures", "c’est quelqu’un qui répare les voitures", "c'est une personne qui répare les voitures", "c'est la personne qui répare les voitures"], "hint": "quelqu'un qui + réparer", "right": "Très bien ! That's «un garagiste» or «un mécanicien».", "wrong": "It's «C'est quelqu'un qui répare les voitures.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ça s'appelle comment, déjà ? C'est un truc qui sert à ouvrir les bouteilles.»", "fr": "Ça s'appelle comment, déjà ? C'est un truc qui sert à ouvrir les bouteilles."}]}, "53-1": {"stageId": "53-1", "title": "Phonetics: liaison, enchaînement, e muet, nasals, intonation", "goal": "Hear and produce French as it is really spoken: linked words, dropped e's, the three nasal vowels and question intonation.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Real French flows like one long word. A final consonant that's normally silent can wake up before a vowel — that's «la liaison». «les amis» sounds like [le-za-mi]: the s becomes a z and jumps onto the next word."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "les‿amis", "en": "the friends", "note": "Say [lezami], not [le ami]."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Liaison can change meaning! «ils‿ont» [il-zɔ̃] = they have, with a buzzing Z. «ils sont» [il-sɔ̃] = they are, with a hissing S. Put your hand on your throat: Z vibrates, S doesn't."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You hear [il-zɔ̃ fɛ̃]. What did they say?", "choices": ["Ils sont faim.", "Ils ont faim.", "Il sont fin."], "answer": "Ils ont faim.", "right": "Bravo ! The buzzing [z] = liaison of «ils ont» = they're hungry.", "wrong": "A [z] sound = «ils‿ont» (have). A hissing [s] = «ils sont» (are). So: «Ils ont faim.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some words start with an 'h aspiré' that blocks liaison: «les héros» = [le e-ʁo], NO z. Otherwise «les‿héros» would sound like «les zéros» — the zeros! Compare «les‿hôtels» [le-zo-tɛl], which does link."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In which phrase must you NOT make a liaison?", "choices": ["les amis", "les hôtels", "les héros"], "answer": "les héros", "right": "Exactement ! H aspiré — otherwise your heroes become zeros.", "wrong": "«héros» has an h aspiré, so no liaison: [le eʁo]. «les‿amis» and «les‿hôtels» do link."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In liaison, d sounds like [t]: «un grand‿homme» = [œ̃ gʁɑ̃-tɔm]. And s/x sound like [z]: «deux‿enfants» = [dø-zɑ̃fɑ̃]. Related: «enchaînement» — a pronounced final consonant simply slides onto the next vowel: «il‿arrive» = [i-la-ʁiv]."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How does the d sound in «un grand homme»?", "choices": ["[d]", "[t]", "silent"], "answer": "[t]", "right": "Parfait ! [gʁɑ̃tɔm].", "wrong": "In liaison, d becomes [t]: «un grand‿homme» = [œ̃ gʁɑ̃tɔm]."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Le e muet» — the unstable e — often disappears at real speed. «Je ne sais pas» becomes «J'sais pas» [ʃe pa] (the j even turns into ch!). «un petit café» becomes «un p'tit café» [œ̃ pti kafe]."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'sais pas. — Un p'tit café ?", "en": "Dunno. — A little coffee?", "note": "This is how everyone talks; write the full forms, but recognise the short ones."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Three nasal vowels — air goes through the nose, and you NEVER pronounce the n: «vent» [ɑ̃] — open mouth like 'ah'. «vont» [ɔ̃] — rounded lips like 'oh'. «vin» [ɛ̃] — spread lips, like the 'a' in 'cat'."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word has the spread-lip nasal [ɛ̃] (like 'cat' through the nose)?", "choices": ["vent", "vont", "vin"], "answer": "vin", "right": "Oui ! «vin» = wine. Smile slightly as you say it.", "wrong": "«vin» = [ɛ̃], spread lips. «vent» = [ɑ̃] (open 'ah'), «vont» = [ɔ̃] (rounded 'oh')."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Intonation: in everyday French, a yes/no question is often just a statement with a rising voice. «Tu viens.» (falling = You're coming.) / «Tu viens ?» (rising = Are you coming?). Recap: liaison (les‿amis), h aspiré (les héros), d → [t], dropped e (j'sais pas), three nasals, rising questions."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: a great man (the phrase with a [t] liaison)", "answer": "un grand homme", "accept": ["un grand homme", "Un grand homme.", "un grand homme."], "hint": "grand + homme", "right": "Super ! And remember to say it [gʁɑ̃tɔm].", "wrong": "It's «un grand homme», pronounced [œ̃ gʁɑ̃tɔm]."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, linking everything, voice rising at the end: «Les amis, ils ont du vin. Tu viens ?»", "fr": "Les amis, ils ont du vin. Tu viens ?"}]}, "53-2": {"stageId": "53-2", "title": "Numbers at real speed (and septante/nonante)", "goal": "Understand and say French numbers 70–99, prices and phone numbers at native speed, including Belgian and Swiss forms.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French numbers from 70 to 99 are famous for doing maths in real time. 70 = «soixante-dix» (sixty-ten), 80 = «quatre-vingts» (four twenties), 90 = «quatre-vingt-dix» (four twenties ten). At real speed you must decode them instantly — let's train."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "soixante et onze", "en": "71", "note": "Like 21, 31, 41… 71 takes «et»: sixty AND eleven."}, {"t": "say", "text": "But from 81 on, there's no «et»: «quatre-vingt-un» (81), «quatre-vingt-onze» (91). And spelling: «quatre-vingts» takes an s only when it ends the number: 80 = «quatre-vingts», but 85 = «quatre-vingt-cinq»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you write 71?", "choices": ["soixante-onze", "soixante et onze", "soixante-dix-un"], "answer": "soixante et onze", "right": "Bravo ! Sixty AND eleven. (Since the 1990 spelling reform, «soixante-et-onze» is also accepted.)", "wrong": "71 = «soixante et onze»: 60 + «et» + 11."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which spelling is correct for 80?", "choices": ["quatre-vingt", "quatre-vingts", "quatre-vingts-un"], "answer": "quatre-vingts", "right": "Parfait ! The s appears only when nothing follows.", "wrong": "80 = «quatre-vingts» (with s). But 81 = «quatre-vingt-un» — the s disappears when another number follows."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The cashier says «quatre-vingt-dix-sept». That's:", "choices": ["87", "97", "77"], "answer": "97", "right": "Exactement ! 4×20 + 10 + 7 = 97.", "wrong": "«quatre-vingt-dix-sept» = 4×20 + 10 + 7 = 97. 87 would be «quatre-vingt-sept»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Good news: in Belgium and Switzerland, they use logical words! 70 = «septante», 90 = «nonante». So 75 = «septante-cinq», 91 = «nonante et un». For 80, Belgians say «quatre-vingts», but in some Swiss cantons (Vaud, Valais, Fribourg) you'll hear «huitante»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In Brussels, someone says «nonante-deux». That's:", "choices": ["92", "72", "82"], "answer": "92", "right": "Oui ! «nonante» = 90, so 92.", "wrong": "«nonante» = 90 in Belgium and Switzerland, so «nonante-deux» = 92."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Prices: French drops 'and' and 'cents'. €12.90 = «douze euros quatre-vingt-dix». Phone numbers are read in pairs: 06 12 45 78 90 = «zéro six, douze, quarante-cinq, soixante-dix-huit, quatre-vingt-dix»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ça fait douze euros quatre-vingt-dix.", "en": "That's €12.90."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How would a French person read the phone digits «78»?", "choices": ["sept, huit", "soixante-dix-huit", "septante-huit"], "answer": "soixante-dix-huit", "right": "Bravo ! In France, phone numbers go in pairs: 78 = «soixante-dix-huit».", "wrong": "French phone numbers are read in pairs, so 78 = «soixante-dix-huit» («septante-huit» only in Belgium/Switzerland)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: 71 «soixante et onze», 80 «quatre-vingts», 81 «quatre-vingt-un», 91 «quatre-vingt-onze». Belgium/Switzerland: «septante», «nonante» (+ «huitante» in parts of Switzerland). Prices: «douze euros quatre-vingt-dix». Phones: pairs."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write 91 in words (France).", "answer": "quatre-vingt-onze", "accept": ["quatre-vingt-onze", "quatre vingt onze"], "hint": "4×20 + 11, no «et», no s.", "right": "Excellent ! No «et» and no s.", "wrong": "91 = «quatre-vingt-onze»: no «et», and no s on «vingt» because a number follows."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say this phone number out loud, in pairs: 06 12 45 78 90", "fr": "zéro six, douze, quarante-cinq, soixante-dix-huit, quatre-vingt-dix"}]}, "54-1": {"stageId": "54-1", "title": "Noun gender patterns", "goal": "Predict the gender of most French nouns from their endings and master the classic exceptions and double-gender words.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Gender is the English speaker's nightmare — but it's far less random than it seems. Endings are strong clues. Learn the patterns, then memorise the exceptions."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Masculine endings: «-age» (le fromage, le voyage), «-ment» (le moment), «-eau» (le bateau), «-isme» (le tourisme). Feminine endings: «-tion» (la nation), «-té» (la liberté), «-ence/-ance» (la patience), «-ette» (la baguette)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "le fromage, le voyage, le garage", "en": "cheese, trip, garage", "note": "«-age» → masculine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«-age» words are masculine… but which one is a famous feminine exception?", "choices": ["le fromage", "la plage", "le message"], "answer": "la plage", "right": "Bravo ! «la plage», along with «la page», «la cage», «l'image», «la nage», «la rage».", "wrong": "«la plage» is feminine — an exception to the «-age» rule, like «la page», «la cage», «l'image»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«liberté» ends in «-té». So:", "choices": ["le liberté", "la liberté", "les deux"], "answer": "la liberté", "right": "Oui ! «-té» → feminine: la liberté, la beauté, la société.", "wrong": "«-té» nouns are feminine: «la liberté». Exceptions: «l'été», «le côté», «le pâté», «le comité»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Exceptions to keep handy: «l'été» (m.) — summer is masculine, like all seasons; «le silence» (m.) despite «-ence»; «l'eau» and «la peau» (f.) despite «-eau»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Big trap: words ending in «-ème» that come from Greek are masculine, even though they end in e: «le problème», «le système», «le thème», «le programme», «le drame»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «C'est ___ grand problème.»", "choices": ["une", "un", "des"], "answer": "un", "right": "Parfait ! «le problème» is masculine (Greek origin).", "wrong": "«problème» is masculine: «un grand problème». Same for «le système», «le thème»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Some words change meaning with gender: «le tour» (trip, turn) / «la tour» (tower); «le poste» (job, TV set) / «la poste» (post office); «le livre» (book) / «la livre» (pound); «le mode» (method) / «la mode» (fashion)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je fais le tour de la tour Eiffel.", "en": "I'm walking around the Eiffel Tower."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je vais à la poste.» means:", "choices": ["I'm going to my job.", "I'm going to the post office.", "I'm going to the TV."], "answer": "I'm going to the post office.", "right": "Exactement ! «la poste» = post office; «le poste» = job/position or set.", "wrong": "Feminine «la poste» = the post office. Masculine «le poste» = a job or a (TV/radio) set."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «-age, -ment, -eau, -isme, -ème» → usually masculine; «-tion, -té, -ence, -ette» → usually feminine. Exceptions: la plage, l'été (m.), le silence, l'eau (f.). Double-gender: le/la tour, le/la poste, le/la livre, le/la mode."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The problem is the cheese. (use «c'est»)", "answer": "Le problème, c'est le fromage.", "accept": ["le problème, c'est le fromage", "le problème c'est le fromage", "le problème, c’est le fromage", "le problème est le fromage"], "hint": "Both nouns are masculine.", "right": "Très bien ! Two masculines, perfectly agreed.", "wrong": "It's «Le problème, c'est le fromage.» — both «problème» and «fromage» are masculine."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Un beau voyage, une belle plage et un long été.»", "fr": "Un beau voyage, une belle plage et un long été."}]}, "54-2": {"stageId": "54-2", "title": "Prepositions with countries & cities", "goal": "Choose correctly between à, en, au, aux and de, d', du, des with cities, countries and islands.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "English uses 'in/to' for everything. French changes the preposition depending on the place. Rule one: cities take «à». «J'habite à Paris», «Je vais à Montréal»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Countries have a gender. Most ending in «-e» are feminine → «en»: «en France», «en Italie», «en Chine». Masculine → «au»: «au Canada», «au Japon», «au Sénégal». Plural → «aux»: «aux États-Unis», «aux Pays-Bas»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle travaille aux États-Unis.", "en": "She works in the US."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le Mexique» ends in -e, but it's masculine. So:", "choices": ["en Mexique", "au Mexique", "à Mexique"], "answer": "au Mexique", "right": "Bravo ! Masculine exceptions: le Mexique, le Cambodge, le Mozambique, le Zimbabwe.", "wrong": "«le Mexique» is masculine despite the -e, so: «au Mexique». Same for «au Cambodge», «au Mozambique»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Trap: masculine countries starting with a vowel take «en», for easier pronunciation: «en Iran», «en Irak», «en Israël», «en Afghanistan», «en Équateur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I grew up in Iran.'", "choices": ["J'ai grandi au Iran.", "J'ai grandi en Iran.", "J'ai grandi à Iran."], "answer": "J'ai grandi en Iran.", "right": "Parfait ! Masculine but vowel-initial → «en».", "wrong": "«Iran» is masculine but starts with a vowel, so: «en Iran». «au Iran» is impossible."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Coming FROM: feminine → «de/d'» («Je reviens de France», «d'Italie»); masculine → «du» («Il vient du Sénégal»); plural → «des» («des États-Unis»); cities → «de» («de Lyon»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je reviens ___ Italie.»", "choices": ["de l'", "d'", "du"], "answer": "d'", "right": "Exactement ! Feminine country → «de», elided before a vowel: «d'Italie».", "wrong": "Feminine countries take plain «de» without article: «d'Italie» (elided before the vowel)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il vient ___ Sénégal.»", "choices": ["de", "du", "en"], "answer": "du", "right": "Oui ! «le Sénégal» is masculine → «du».", "wrong": "Masculine country → «du» (= de + le): «Il vient du Sénégal»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Provinces and regions follow the same logic: «au Québec», «en Bretagne», «en Californie», «au Texas». Small islands often take «à»: «à Cuba», «à Madagascar», «à La Réunion», but bigger European ones take «en»: «en Corse», «en Sicile». And some cities have an article: «au Caire», «au Havre»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ils vivent à La Réunion.", "en": "They live on Réunion."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap — to/in: city «à», feminine «en», masculine «au», plural «aux», vowel-initial masculine «en». From: «de/d'», «du», «des». Watch: le Mexique, le Cambodge, au Québec, à La Réunion."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I'm going to Canada.", "answer": "Je vais au Canada.", "accept": ["je vais au canada", "je vais au Canada"], "hint": "le Canada is masculine.", "right": "Super ! Masculine → «au».", "wrong": "«le Canada» is masculine: «Je vais au Canada.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je suis né au Mexique, j'ai grandi en Iran et j'habite aux États-Unis.»", "fr": "Je suis né au Mexique, j'ai grandi en Iran et j'habite aux États-Unis."}]}, "54-3": {"stageId": "54-3", "title": "Faux amis (false friends)", "goal": "Avoid the most dangerous French–English false friends and use the correct French word instead.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«Les faux amis» look like English words but betray you. Some just cause confusion; others cause real embarrassment. Let's defuse the classics."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«actuellement» = currently, NOT 'actually'. 'Actually' = «en fait». And «éventuellement» = possibly, NOT 'eventually'. 'Eventually' = «finalement» or «à la fin»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est actuellement au Japon.", "en": "He's currently in Japan."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Actually, I don't agree.'", "choices": ["Actuellement, je ne suis pas d'accord.", "En fait, je ne suis pas d'accord.", "Éventuellement, je ne suis pas d'accord."], "answer": "En fait, je ne suis pas d'accord.", "right": "Bravo ! 'Actually' = «en fait».", "wrong": "'Actually' = «en fait». «Actuellement» means 'currently'."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Places: «la librairie» = bookshop (where you buy books). 'Library' = «la bibliothèque» (where you borrow them). And «la location» = rental («une voiture de location» = a rental car). 'Location' = «l'emplacement» or «le lieu»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You want to borrow books for free. You go to:", "choices": ["la librairie", "la bibliothèque", "la location"], "answer": "la bibliothèque", "right": "Exactement ! Borrow = «bibliothèque», buy = «librairie».", "wrong": "A library = «la bibliothèque». «La librairie» is a bookshop where you pay."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«attendre» = to wait (for), NOT 'to attend'. 'To attend a meeting' = «assister à une réunion». And «sensible» = sensitive. 'Sensible' (reasonable) = «raisonnable» or «sensé»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I'll attend the meeting.'", "choices": ["J'attendrai la réunion.", "J'assisterai à la réunion.", "Je serai sensible à la réunion."], "answer": "J'assisterai à la réunion.", "right": "Parfait ! 'Attend' = «assister à».", "wrong": "«attendre» means 'to wait'. To attend = «assister à»: «J'assisterai à la réunion.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«La monnaie» = change, coins, or a currency («Vous avez de la monnaie ?» = Do you have change?). 'Money' in general = «l'argent». So «Je n'ai pas d'argent» = I have no money."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The embarrassing one: «un préservatif» is a condom! Food preservatives = «des conservateurs». So never ask a French grocer for jam «sans préservatifs»…"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'This jam has no preservatives.'", "choices": ["Cette confiture est sans préservatifs.", "Cette confiture est sans conservateurs.", "Cette confiture est sans monnaie."], "answer": "Cette confiture est sans conservateurs.", "right": "Ouf ! Well done. «conservateurs» = preservatives.", "wrong": "Careful! «préservatif» = condom. Preservatives = «conservateurs»: «sans conservateurs»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: actuellement (currently) ≠ actually (en fait); éventuellement (possibly) ≠ eventually (finalement); librairie (bookshop) ≠ library (bibliothèque); sensible (sensitive) ≠ sensible (raisonnable); attendre (wait) ≠ attend (assister à); monnaie (change) ≠ money (argent); préservatif ≠ preservative (conservateur); location (rental) ≠ location (lieu)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I'm going to the library.", "answer": "Je vais à la bibliothèque.", "accept": ["je vais à la bibliothèque", "je vais a la bibliotheque", "je vais a la bibliothèque"], "hint": "Not «librairie»!", "right": "Très bien ! You avoided the trap.", "wrong": "A library = «la bibliothèque»: «Je vais à la bibliothèque.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «En fait, je suis actuellement à la bibliothèque.»", "fr": "En fait, je suis actuellement à la bibliothèque."}]}, "55-1": {"stageId": "55-1", "title": "Phone & admin French", "goal": "Handle a French phone call and everyday paperwork with the fixed phrases natives expect.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Calling a French office or doing paperwork follows a script. Learn the set phrases and you'll sound calm and capable. The first one a receptionist will say is «C'est de la part de qui ?», literally 'it's on behalf of whom?', meaning 'Who's calling?'"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "— Bonjour, je voudrais parler à Madame Martin, s'il vous plaît. — C'est de la part de qui ?", "en": "— Hello, I'd like to speak to Mrs Martin, please. — Who's calling?", "note": "Reply: «C'est de la part de Tom Smith» or just «De la part de Tom Smith»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The receptionist asks «C'est de la part de qui ?». Best reply?", "choices": ["C'est moi qui parle.", "De la part de Julie Dubois.", "Je suis à la part de Julie."], "answer": "De la part de Julie Dubois.", "right": "Parfait ! You echo the structure: «De la part de» + your name.", "wrong": "Echo the question's structure: «De la part de Julie Dubois.» That's the standard, polite answer."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Next you'll hear «Ne quittez pas» (hold the line, literally 'don't leave') and then «Je vous le passe» (I'll put him through) or «Je vous la passe» (I'll put her through). If the person is out: «Elle est absente pour le moment. Voulez-vous laisser un message ?»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Ne quittez pas» has nothing to do with quitting. And in «Je vous le passe», «le» is the person you want and «vous» is you: 'I pass him to you.' For a woman it becomes «Je vous la passe»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You asked for Madame Martin. She's available. The receptionist says:", "choices": ["Je vous la passe.", "Je vous le passe.", "Je la vous passe."], "answer": "Je vous la passe.", "right": "Exactement ! Feminine «la» for Madame Martin, and «vous» comes before «la».", "wrong": "Madame Martin is a woman, so «la». Pronoun order is «vous» before «la»: «Je vous la passe.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Names always get spelled: «Vous pouvez me l'épeler ?» (Can you spell it?). Know your French letters: G is «gé», J is «ji», W is «double vé». For doubles say «deux L», and for accents «e accent aigu». To be clear, natives use names: «S comme Suzanne»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Smith : S comme Suzanne, M comme Marcel, I, T comme Thérèse, H.", "en": "Smith: S as in Suzanne, M as in Marcel, I, T as in Thérèse, H."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you say the letter J when spelling in French?", "choices": ["«gé»", "«ji»", "«jé»"], "answer": "«ji»", "right": "Oui ! J is «ji» and G is «gé», the opposite of what English ears expect.", "wrong": "In French, G is «gé» and J is «ji». English speakers swap them all the time, which causes a lot of email typos!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Admin words you'll need: «un justificatif de domicile» (proof of address, e.g. an electricity bill under 3 months old), «un RIB» (relevé d'identité bancaire, the slip with your IBAN, wanted by employers and landlords), and «la carte Vitale» (the green health insurance card for doctors and pharmacies)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your new employer needs your bank details to pay your salary. They ask for:", "choices": ["un RIB", "une carte Vitale", "un justificatif de domicile"], "answer": "un RIB", "right": "Bravo ! The «RIB» has your IBAN and BIC on it.", "wrong": "Bank details come on a «RIB» (relevé d'identité bancaire). The «carte Vitale» is for health, and the «justificatif de domicile» proves where you live."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "At the pharmacy: «Vous avez votre ___ ?»", "choices": ["carte Vitale", "RIB", "justificatif de domicile"], "answer": "carte Vitale", "right": "C'est ça ! Pharmacies and doctors scan your «carte Vitale» for reimbursement.", "wrong": "For anything health-related it's the «carte Vitale», the green Sécurité sociale card."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Formal letters open with «Madame, Monsieur,» and close with the famous «Je vous prie d'agréer, Madame, Monsieur, l'expression de mes salutations distinguées.» For emails, «Cordialement» or «Bien cordialement» is enough."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «C'est de la part de qui ?», «Ne quittez pas», «Je vous le/la passe», «Vous pouvez me l'épeler ?», plus «RIB», «justificatif de domicile» and «carte Vitale». You're ready for French bureaucracy!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (you're the receptionist): Hold the line, I'll put him through.", "answer": "Ne quittez pas, je vous le passe.", "accept": ["ne quittez pas, je vous le passe", "ne quittez pas je vous le passe", "Ne quittez pas. Je vous le passe.", "ne quittez pas. je vous le passe"], "hint": "Don't leave… I pass him to you.", "right": "Impeccable, you sound like a real standardiste !", "wrong": "The phrase is «Ne quittez pas, je vous le passe.» with «vous» before «le»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Bonjour, je voudrais parler à Madame Martin, s'il vous plaît. C'est de la part de Tom Smith.»", "fr": "Bonjour, je voudrais parler à Madame Martin, s'il vous plaît. C'est de la part de Tom Smith."}]}, "55-2": {"stageId": "55-2", "title": "Texting & online writing", "goal": "Read and write casual French texts using the most common abbreviations, and know when not to use them.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French texting («le langage SMS») drops letters and spells by sound. «slt cv ?» is just «Salut, ça va ?». Once you can decode it, group chats suddenly make sense."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "slt cv ? tu fé koi ce soir ?", "en": "hi, how's it going? what are you doing tonight?", "note": "«fé koi» = «fais quoi», spelled the way it sounds."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The essentials: «mdr» = mort de rire (lol), «ptdr» = pété de rire (rofl), «jsp» = je sais pas (dunno), «tkt» = t'inquiète (don't worry), «pk» or «pq» = pourquoi, «dsl» = désolé, «stp» = s'il te plaît, «bcp» = beaucoup, «rdv» = rendez-vous."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend writes «dsl jsp». What do they mean?", "choices": ["Sorry, I don't know.", "Lol, see you later.", "Don't worry, why?"], "answer": "Sorry, I don't know.", "right": "Exactement ! «dsl» = désolé, «jsp» = je sais pas.", "wrong": "«dsl» = désolé (sorry) and «jsp» = je sais pas (I don't know)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Read it aloud and it clicks: «c» = c'est, «g» = j'ai, «koi» = quoi, «2m1» = demain, «a+» = à plus (see ya)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«tkt g 1 plan». What does «tkt» stand for?", "choices": ["t'inquiète", "t'es quand ?", "tu kiffes trop"], "answer": "t'inquiète", "right": "Oui ! «tkt» = «t'inquiète (pas)», and «g 1 plan» = «j'ai un plan». Don't worry, I've got a plan.", "wrong": "«tkt» = «t'inquiète», the spoken short form of «ne t'inquiète pas» (don't worry)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Sign-offs: «bisous», «bises» or «grosses bises» are normal between friends and family, even when writing to a male friend. Guys also often write «a+» or «ciao». None of this goes to your boss or your landlord!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "rdv à 18h devant le ciné ? bisous", "en": "meet at 6pm outside the cinema? love"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sign-off is right for an email to your landlord?", "choices": ["Cordialement,", "Bisous !", "a+"], "answer": "Cordialement,", "right": "Parfait ! «Cordialement» is the neutral, professional choice.", "wrong": "Texting codes and «bisous» are for friends. With a landlord, use «Cordialement,»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Notice that texting mirrors speech: the «ne» disappears («jsp» comes from «je sais pas»), and questions use intonation, not inversion. So «pk tu viens pas ?» is spoken French written fast."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«pk tu viens pas ?» in standard written French is:", "choices": ["Pourquoi tu ne viens pas ?", "Pourquoi tu viens, quand ?", "Parce que tu ne viens pas."], "answer": "Pourquoi tu ne viens pas ?", "right": "Très bien ! «pk» = pourquoi, and you put the «ne» back.", "wrong": "«pk» = pourquoi (not «parce que»). Standard: «Pourquoi tu ne viens pas ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «slt», «cv», «mdr», «jsp», «tkt», «pk», «dsl», «rdv», «bisous». They're great with friends and off-limits in formal writing."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Decode into full standard French: «slt cv ?»", "answer": "Salut, ça va ?", "accept": ["salut, ça va", "salut ça va", "Salut ça va ?", "salut, ça va ?", "salut ça va ?"], "hint": "A greeting + 'how's it going?'", "right": "Bravo ! «Salut, ça va ?»", "wrong": "«slt» = «Salut», «cv» = «ça va»: «Salut, ça va ?»"}, {"t": "type", "q": "What's the texting abbreviation for «rendez-vous»?", "answer": "rdv", "accept": ["rdv", "RDV"], "hint": "Three consonants.", "right": "Oui ! «rdv à 20h ?»", "wrong": "It's «rdv», as in «rdv à 20h ?»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud (the spoken version of a text): «Salut, ça va ? T'inquiète, j'arrive dans dix minutes. Bisous !»", "fr": "Salut, ça va ? T'inquiète, j'arrive dans dix minutes. Bisous !"}]}, "55-3": {"stageId": "55-3", "title": "Storytelling & emotional nuance", "goal": "Tell a lively anecdote the French way and name your feelings precisely.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "When French people tell a story, they often switch to the present tense for drama and use connectors like «et là» (and then), «d'un coup» (suddenly) and «bref» (anyway)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Et là, tu sais pas ce qu'il me fait ? Il se lève et il part sans payer !", "en": "And then, guess what he does? He gets up and leaves without paying!", "note": "Narrative present + dropped «ne» = lively spoken style."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which connector means 'suddenly'?", "choices": ["Bref", "D'un coup", "Du coup"], "answer": "D'un coup", "right": "Exactement ! «D'un coup» (or «tout à coup») = suddenly.", "wrong": "«Du coup» = so / as a result. «D'un coup» = suddenly. One little word, a totally different meaning!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "D'un coup, la lumière s'est éteinte.", "en": "Suddenly, the light went out."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Bref» wraps things up or cuts a digression: «Bref, je te passe les détails» (Anyway, I'll spare you the details). And «du coup» is the filler of the decade: 'so…'. Use it, just not in every sentence!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the feelings: «dégoûté» (gutted), «vexé» (hurt, offended), «soulagé» (relieved), «gêné» (embarrassed), «aux anges» (over the moon)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your team lost the final in the last minute. You say:", "choices": ["J'étais aux anges.", "J'étais dégoûté.", "J'étais soulagé."], "answer": "J'étais dégoûté.", "right": "Oui ! In everyday speech «dégoûté» = gutted, bitterly disappointed.", "wrong": "Here «dégoûté» means gutted: «J'étais dégoûté.» «Aux anges» = overjoyed, «soulagé» = relieved."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False friends: «embarrassé» is more 'awkward, perplexed'. For red-faced embarrassment say «gêné» or «j'avais honte». And «vexer» doesn't mean 'to annoy'. It means to hurt someone's pride."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You tripped in front of the whole class. You were…", "choices": ["trop gêné", "trop vexé", "trop soulagé"], "answer": "trop gêné", "right": "C'est ça ! «J'étais trop gêné» = I was so embarrassed.", "wrong": "Embarrassment is «gêné». «Vexé» means hurt by someone's words or actions."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ça m'a vexé qu'il ne m'ait pas invité.", "en": "It hurt my feelings that he didn't invite me.", "note": "Emotion + «que» → subjunctive."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You thought you'd failed the exam, but you passed. Phew!", "choices": ["Je suis soulagé !", "Je suis dégoûté !", "Je suis vexé !"], "answer": "Je suis soulagé !", "right": "Bravo ! «Soulagé» = relieved.", "wrong": "Relief is «soulagé»: «Je suis soulagé !»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle a dit oui ! Je suis aux anges.", "en": "She said yes! I'm over the moon."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: tell it in the present, then add «et là», «d'un coup» and «bref, je te passe les détails». Name the feeling: «dégoûté», «vexé», «soulagé», «gêné», «aux anges»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (to a friend): Anyway, I'll spare you the details.", "answer": "Bref, je te passe les détails.", "accept": ["bref, je te passe les détails", "bref je te passe les détails", "Bref, je vous passe les détails.", "bref, je vous passe les détails"], "hint": "Start with the classic wrap-up word.", "right": "Parfait, très naturel !", "wrong": "Natives say: «Bref, je te passe les détails.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with drama: «Et là, d'un coup, la lumière s'est éteinte. J'étais mort de peur !»", "fr": "Et là, d'un coup, la lumière s'est éteinte. J'étais mort de peur !"}]}, "55-4": {"stageId": "55-4", "title": "Gestures, Body Language & Interaction Codes", "goal": "Recognise key French gestures and use the listening and interrupting phrases that make conversation flow.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French is spoken with the face and shoulders too. «Hausser les épaules» (to shrug) shows indifference, and «faire la moue» (to pout) often comes with the famous «Bof» (meh)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "— Le film t'a plu ? — Bof…", "en": "— Did you like the film? — Meh…", "note": "Usually with a pout and a little shrug."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone asks if you liked a so-so restaurant. The very French reply:", "choices": ["Bof.", "Mon œil !", "Ah bon ?"], "answer": "Bof.", "right": "Exactement ! «Bof» + a pout = not great, not awful.", "wrong": "Lukewarm opinion = «Bof». «Mon œil !» = I don't believe you, «Ah bon ?» = Really?"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Mon œil !» comes with a gesture: you pull down your lower eyelid with your index finger. It means 'Yeah, right!' Another one: stroking your cheek with the back of your fingers means «La barbe !» (What a bore!)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend claims he ran a marathon in two hours. You pull down your eyelid and say:", "choices": ["Mon œil !", "Je vois.", "Bof."], "answer": "Mon œil !", "right": "Ha, oui ! «Mon œil !» = Yeah, right!", "wrong": "Disbelief = «Mon œil !», with the eyelid gesture."}, {"t": "say", "text": "French listeners react all the time. Silence feels cold. Keep the conversation going with «Je vois» (I see), «Ah bon ?» (Really?), «C'est vrai ?», «Ah oui ?», «D'accord» and lots of «mmh»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Intonation matters: a rising «Ah bon ?» shows surprise, while a falling «Ah bon.» means 'oh, OK then' (resigned)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your colleague says «Paul démissionne demain.» You're surprised:", "choices": ["Ah bon ?", "Bof.", "Laisse-moi finir !"], "answer": "Ah bon ?", "right": "Parfait ! A rising «Ah bon ?» = Really?", "wrong": "Surprise = «Ah bon ?» with rising intonation."}, {"t": "say", "text": "French conversation overlaps a lot, and among friends interrupting isn't rude in itself. In formal settings, soften it: «Pardon de vous interrompre, mais…» or «Si je peux me permettre…». To keep the floor: «Laisse-moi finir !» or «Attends, j'ai pas fini !»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pardon de vous interrompre, mais la réunion commence dans cinq minutes.", "en": "Sorry to interrupt, but the meeting starts in five minutes."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a meeting, you want to politely cut in on your boss:", "choices": ["Pardon de vous interrompre, mais…", "Laisse-moi finir !", "Mon œil !"], "answer": "Pardon de vous interrompre, mais…", "right": "Très bien ! Polite, with «vous».", "wrong": "With your boss, use the polite formula: «Pardon de vous interrompre, mais…»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Greetings: a handshake in business, «la bise» (cheek kisses) among friends. That's 2 in Paris, often 3 in parts of the south and up to 4 in some regions. When unsure, let the other person lead."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «hausser les épaules» mean?", "choices": ["to shrug", "to pout", "to nod"], "answer": "to shrug", "right": "Oui ! «Il a haussé les épaules» = he shrugged.", "wrong": "«Hausser les épaules» = to shrug. To pout is «faire la moue», and to nod is «hocher la tête»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «Bof» + pout, «Mon œil !» + eyelid, listening signals («Je vois», «Ah bon ?»), and turn-taking («Pardon de vous interrompre», «Laisse-moi finir !»)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French (to a friend): Let me finish!", "answer": "Laisse-moi finir !", "accept": ["laisse-moi finir", "laisse-moi finir !", "Laisse-moi finir!", "Laissez-moi finir !", "laissez-moi finir"], "hint": "Imperative of laisser + moi + infinitive.", "right": "Bravo ! Note the hyphen in «Laisse-moi».", "wrong": "«Laisse-moi finir !» (with «vous»: «Laissez-moi finir !»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, politely: «Pardon de vous interrompre, mais je voudrais ajouter quelque chose.»", "fr": "Pardon de vous interrompre, mais je voudrais ajouter quelque chose."}]}, "56-1": {"stageId": "56-1", "title": "Participe passé agreement with avoir", "goal": "Make the past participle agree with a preceding direct object after avoir, and know when it never agrees.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The golden rule: with «avoir», the past participle never agrees with the subject. It agrees with the direct object (COD) only if that object comes BEFORE the verb. «Elle a mis sa veste»: the object comes after, so no agreement. «Elle l'a mise»: «l'» = la veste comes before, so «mise»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle a mis sa veste. → Elle l'a mise.", "en": "She put on her jacket. → She put it on.", "note": "You can HEAR it: «mise» ends in a [z] sound."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Cette lettre, je l'ai ___ hier.»", "choices": ["écrit", "écrite", "écrits"], "answer": "écrite", "right": "Parfait ! «l'» = cette lettre (feminine), placed before → «écrite», with an audible t.", "wrong": "«l'» stands for «cette lettre» (feminine singular) and comes before «ai», so: «je l'ai écrite»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "A direct object comes before the verb in three situations: 1) object pronouns «me, te, le, la, les, nous, vous», 2) the relative pronoun «que», 3) questions with «quel(le)(s)…» or «combien de…»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les fleurs que tu m'as offertes sont magnifiques.", "en": "The flowers you gave me are gorgeous.", "note": "«que» = les fleurs (feminine plural) → «offertes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Quelles robes as-tu ___ ?»", "choices": ["acheté", "achetées", "achetés"], "answer": "achetées", "right": "Bravo ! «Quelles robes» (feminine plural) comes first → «achetées».", "wrong": "The object «quelles robes» is feminine plural and comes before the verb: «achetées»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tes cousins ? Je les ai ___ au cinéma.»", "choices": ["vu", "vue", "vus"], "answer": "vus", "right": "Exactement ! «les» = tes cousins (masculine plural) → «vus».", "wrong": "«les» refers to «tes cousins», masculine plural, placed before → «vus»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Two traps that never agree: 1) «en»: «Des films, j'en ai vu beaucoup» (no -s). 2) Indirect objects «lui, leur»: «Je leur ai parlé». Only DIRECT objects trigger agreement."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Des pommes ? J'en ai ___ trois.»", "choices": ["mangé", "mangées", "mangés"], "answer": "mangé", "right": "Oui ! «en» doesn't trigger agreement → «mangé».", "wrong": "With «en» the participle stays invariable: «J'en ai mangé trois.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ma sœur ? Je lui ai ___ hier.»", "choices": ["téléphoné", "téléphonée", "téléphonées"], "answer": "téléphoné", "right": "Très bien ! «lui» is indirect (téléphoner À quelqu'un) → no agreement.", "wrong": "It's «téléphoner à quelqu'un», so «lui» is an indirect object. No agreement: «Je lui ai téléphoné.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Combien de pages as-tu lues ?", "en": "How many pages did you read?", "note": "«combien de pages» comes first → «lues»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Native reality: in casual speech many French people skip the audible agreement («la lettre que j'ai écrit»). In writing and careful speech it's expected, and getting the audible ones right («mise, écrite, faite, prise, ouverte») marks a C2 speaker."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: direct object BEFORE the verb (pronoun, «que», «quel/combien») → agree. Object after the verb → no agreement. «en», «lui», «leur» → never."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: The photos? I took them yesterday. (prendre)", "answer": "Les photos ? Je les ai prises hier.", "accept": ["les photos ? je les ai prises hier", "les photos? je les ai prises hier", "Les photos ? Je les ai prises hier", "les photos, je les ai prises hier", "Je les ai prises hier.", "je les ai prises hier"], "hint": "«les» = les photos (feminine plural).", "right": "Impeccable ! «prises», and you can hear the [z].", "wrong": "«les» = les photos (feminine plural) before the verb → «Je les ai prises hier.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud and make the agreements audible: «La décision qu'elle a prise, je l'ai trouvée courageuse.»", "fr": "La décision qu'elle a prise, je l'ai trouvée courageuse."}]}, "56-2": {"stageId": "56-2", "title": "Pronouns y, en & double pronouns", "goal": "Use y and en confidently, order double pronouns correctly, and master the everyday idioms built on them.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«y» replaces «à + thing» or a place. «en» replaces «de + noun» and anything with a quantity. Natives use them constantly, often in fixed expressions."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu vas à Lyon ? — Oui, j'y vais demain.", "en": "Are you going to Lyon? — Yes, I'm going (there) tomorrow."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu as des enfants ? — Oui, j'en ai deux.", "en": "Do you have children? — Yes, I have two.", "note": "With «en», the number stays at the end."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu penses à ton examen ?» — «Oui, j'___ pense tout le temps.»", "choices": ["y", "en", "lui"], "answer": "y", "right": "Parfait ! «penser à» + thing → «y».", "wrong": "«penser à quelque chose» → «j'y pense». For a person you'd say «je pense à lui»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu veux du café ?» — «Oui, j'___ veux bien.»", "choices": ["y", "en", "le"], "answer": "en", "right": "Oui ! «du café» (partitive) → «en».", "wrong": "Partitive «du café» is replaced by «en»: «J'en veux bien.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Double pronoun order: «me/te/se/nous/vous» → «le/la/les» → «lui/leur» → «y» → «en». So: «Je le lui ai dit», «Il y en a», «Je vous en prie»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Memory trick: «y» always comes before «en», as in «il y en a». Think of the Japanese currency: Y-EN!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "I gave the book to Marie → «Je ___ ai donné.»", "choices": ["le lui", "lui le", "la lui"], "answer": "le lui", "right": "Exactement ! «le» (the book) before «lui» (to Marie).", "wrong": "«le/la/les» come before «lui/leur»: «Je le lui ai donné.» It's «le» because «livre» is masculine."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In the affirmative imperative, pronouns go after the verb with hyphens, direct before indirect, and «me» becomes «moi»: «Donne-le-moi !» In the negative imperative, normal order returns: «Ne me le donne pas !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Tell a friend: 'Give them to me!' (the keys)", "choices": ["Donne-les-moi !", "Donne-moi-les !", "Me les donne !"], "answer": "Donne-les-moi !", "right": "Bravo ! Standard order: verb-direct-indirect.", "wrong": "Standard French is «Donne-les-moi !». You may hear «Donne-moi-les» in casual speech, but it's not correct."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Frozen idioms: «J'en ai marre» (I'm fed up), «Vas-y !» (Go on!), «Ça y est !» (Done! / That's it!), «On s'en va» (We're leaving), «Ne m'en parle pas !» (Don't get me started!), «Il y en a qui…» (Some people…)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend hesitates at the edge of the pool. You shout:", "choices": ["Vas-y !", "Va-y !", "Y va !"], "answer": "Vas-y !", "right": "Oui ! The «s» comes back before «y» for the sound link: «Vas-y».", "wrong": "The imperative «va» takes an «s» before «y» or «en», for the liaison: «Vas-y !» (and «Manges-en !»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «y» = à + thing or place, «en» = de + noun or quantity. Order: me/te → le/la/les → lui/leur → y → en. Imperative: «Donne-le-moi !». Idioms: «J'en ai marre», «Ça y est», «Vas-y»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I told him. (dire, passé composé, double pronoun)", "answer": "Je le lui ai dit.", "accept": ["je le lui ai dit", "Je le lui ai dit"], "hint": "le + lui, then the auxiliary.", "right": "Parfait ! «Je le lui ai dit.»", "wrong": "«le» (it) before «lui» (to him): «Je le lui ai dit.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud with feeling: «J'en ai marre ! Ça y est, on s'en va.»", "fr": "J'en ai marre ! Ça y est, on s'en va."}]}, "56-3": {"stageId": "56-3", "title": "Quantities & Partitive Traps", "goal": "Choose correctly between de, du/des and the article after quantities, negations and ne…que.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "After expressions of quantity, use bare «de», with no article: «beaucoup de monde», «un peu de temps», «trop de travail», «assez d'argent»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il y a beaucoup de monde ce soir.", "en": "There are a lot of people tonight.", "note": "«le monde» = people here, and it's singular."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai beaucoup ___ amis.»", "choices": ["des", "d'", "de les"], "answer": "d'", "right": "Exactement ! «beaucoup de» → «beaucoup d'amis».", "wrong": "After «beaucoup», use bare «de» (elided before a vowel): «beaucoup d'amis»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Exceptions that keep the article: «la plupart des», «bien des», «la majorité des». And «la plupart des gens» takes a plural verb: «La plupart des gens pensent que…»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ gens pensent que c'est trop cher.»", "choices": ["La plupart de", "La plupart des", "Beaucoup des"], "answer": "La plupart des", "right": "Bravo ! «La plupart des» keeps the article.", "wrong": "«la plupart» is an exception: «La plupart des gens…». But «beaucoup de gens»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Negation: «un, une, des, du, de la» become «de» after «pas»: «Je n'ai pas de voiture». BUT with «être» nothing changes: «Ce n'est pas du café, c'est du thé». And «ne… que» isn't a real negation, so the article stays: «Il ne mange que des légumes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je n'ai pas ___ temps.»", "choices": ["du", "de", "le"], "answer": "de", "right": "Oui ! After «pas», «du» becomes «de».", "wrong": "«J'ai du temps» → «Je n'ai pas de temps»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ce n'est pas ___ vin, c'est du jus de raisin.»", "choices": ["de", "du", "le"], "answer": "du", "right": "Très bien ! With «être», the article doesn't change.", "wrong": "«être» is the exception: «Ce n'est pas du vin.» You're saying what it IS or ISN'T, not denying a quantity."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il ne boit que ___ eau.»", "choices": ["d'", "de l'", "l'"], "answer": "de l'", "right": "Parfait ! «ne… que» = only, so the partitive stays.", "wrong": "«ne… que» means 'only', it isn't a negation: «Il ne boit que de l'eau.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«peu de» vs «un peu de»: «Il a peu d'amis» = few (negative). «J'ai un peu de temps» = a little (positive). «un peu de» only works with uncountables, so for 'a few friends' say «quelques amis»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Positive meaning: 'I have a few friends here.'", "choices": ["J'ai peu d'amis ici.", "J'ai quelques amis ici.", "J'ai un peu d'amis ici."], "answer": "J'ai quelques amis ici.", "right": "Exactement ! «quelques» for countable plurals.", "wrong": "«peu d'amis» = few (sad!), and «un peu d'amis» is wrong. 'A few friends' = «quelques amis»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Refined detail: before a plural adjective + noun, written French uses «de»: «de bonnes idées». In everyday speech «des bonnes idées» is very common."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: quantity + «de»; «la plupart des / bien des»; «pas de» except with «être»; «ne… que» keeps the article; «peu de» ≠ «un peu de»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: He doesn't have a car.", "answer": "Il n'a pas de voiture.", "accept": ["il n'a pas de voiture", "Il n'a pas de voiture", "il a pas de voiture"], "hint": "une → de after pas.", "right": "Bravo ! «pas de voiture».", "wrong": "«une voiture» becomes «de voiture» after «pas»: «Il n'a pas de voiture.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «La plupart des gens ont peu de temps, mais beaucoup de projets.»", "fr": "La plupart des gens ont peu de temps, mais beaucoup de projets."}]}, "56-4": {"stageId": "56-4", "title": "Mise en relief (emphasis)", "goal": "Emphasise words the French way with c'est… qui/que, ce qui/ce que/ce dont… c'est, and dislocation.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In English you stress a word with your voice: 'YOU said it!' French can't really do that, so it restructures the sentence: «C'est toi qui l'as dit !»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est toi qui l'as dit !", "en": "YOU said it! / You're the one who said it!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "With «C'est + person + qui», the verb agrees with the person being stressed: «C'est moi qui paie», «C'est nous qui avons gagné», «C'est vous qui avez raison»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«C'est moi qui ___ raison.»", "choices": ["a", "ai", "as"], "answer": "ai", "right": "Parfait ! «moi» → «ai».", "wrong": "The verb agrees with «moi»: «C'est moi qui ai raison.» A very common native slip is «qui a»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To stress anything else (a time, place or object), use «C'est… que»: «C'est demain qu'on part», «C'est à Paris que je l'ai rencontrée»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Stress 'tomorrow': 'We're leaving TOMORROW.'", "choices": ["C'est demain qu'on part.", "C'est demain qui on part.", "Demain, c'est qu'on part."], "answer": "C'est demain qu'on part.", "right": "Bravo ! «C'est… que» for non-subjects.", "wrong": "«demain» isn't the subject, so use «que»: «C'est demain qu'on part.»"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Keep the preposition: «C'est à toi que je parle», not «C'est toi que je parle»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Pseudo-clefts: «Ce qui compte, c'est la santé» (subject), «Ce que je veux, c'est partir» (object), «Ce dont j'ai besoin, c'est de vacances» (verbs with «de»: avoir besoin de, avoir envie de, parler de). The «de» comes back after «c'est»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ j'ai envie, c'est d'un bon café.»", "choices": ["Ce que", "Ce dont", "Ce qui"], "answer": "Ce dont", "right": "Exactement ! «avoir envie DE» → «ce dont».", "wrong": "«avoir envie de» needs «dont»: «Ce dont j'ai envie, c'est d'un bon café.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ m'énerve, c'est son retard.»", "choices": ["Ce qui", "Ce que", "Ce dont"], "answer": "Ce qui", "right": "Oui ! It's the subject of «m'énerve» → «ce qui».", "wrong": "The gap is the subject of «énerve» (something annoys me), so: «Ce qui m'énerve, c'est son retard.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Dislocation is the spoken favourite: you put the topic at the start or the end and repeat it with a pronoun. «Le foot, j'y connais rien.» «Elle est géniale, ta sœur.» «Moi, je trouve que…»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ton frère, je l'adore. / Je l'adore, ton frère.", "en": "I love your brother."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which dislocation is correct?", "choices": ["Le chocolat, je l'adore.", "Le chocolat, j'en adore.", "Le chocolat, je lui adore."], "answer": "Le chocolat, je l'adore.", "right": "Parfait ! «adorer quelque chose» → direct object «le / l'».", "wrong": "«adorer» takes a direct object, so the topic comes back as «l'»: «Le chocolat, je l'adore.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «C'est moi qui…» (verb agrees), «C'est demain que…», «Ce qui / Ce que / Ce dont…, c'est…», and dislocation «Ta sœur, elle est géniale»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French, stressing the person: It's on me. (I'm the one paying.)", "answer": "C'est moi qui paie.", "accept": ["c'est moi qui paie", "c'est moi qui paye", "C'est moi qui paye."], "hint": "C'est + moi + qui + verb in the je form.", "right": "Bravo, et merci pour le café !", "wrong": "«C'est moi qui paie.» (or «paye»), with the verb in the «je» form."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Ce qui compte, c'est la santé. Le reste, on s'en fiche.»", "fr": "Ce qui compte, c'est la santé. Le reste, on s'en fiche."}]}, "57-1": {"stageId": "57-1", "title": "Francophonie: Québec, Belgium, Switzerland, Africa", "goal": "Recognise signature words and structures of French from Québec, Belgium, Switzerland and francophone Africa.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Over 300 million people speak French, and Paris French is just one variety. Today we'll travel to Québec, Belgium, Switzerland and West Africa."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Québec: «un char» (a car), «stationner» (to park), «c'est plate» (it's boring), «magasiner» (to go shopping), «ma blonde» (my girlfriend), «mon chum» (my boyfriend or buddy). Questions often add «-tu»: «Tu veux-tu un café ?»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Mon char est stationné en face.", "en": "My car's parked opposite.", "note": "In France: «Ma voiture est garée en face.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A Quebecer says «Ce film-là, c'est plate.» Meaning?", "choices": ["It's boring.", "It's on a flat screen.", "It's great."], "answer": "It's boring.", "right": "Exactement ! «C'est plate» = it's boring (pronounced «platte»).", "wrong": "In Québec, «c'est plate» = it's boring. In France you'd say «c'est ennuyeux» or «c'est nul»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In Québec, Belgium and Switzerland: «déjeuner» = breakfast, «dîner» = lunch, «souper» = dinner. Check the time before you accept a «dîner» invitation!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Belgium and Switzerland: «septante» (70) and «nonante» (90), plus «huitante» (80) in some Swiss cantons. Belgians say «À tantôt !» (see you later today) and use «savoir» for 'to be able to': «Je ne sais pas venir» = I can't come. The Swiss call a mobile phone «un natel»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In Brussels, 97 is said:", "choices": ["nonante-sept", "quatre-vingt-dix-sept", "huitante-dix-sept"], "answer": "nonante-sept", "right": "Bravo ! Much more logical than «quatre-vingt-dix-sept», non ?", "wrong": "Belgians say «nonante-sept». «Quatre-vingt-dix-sept» is France, and «huitante-dix-sept» doesn't exist."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A Belgian colleague says «Je ne sais pas venir demain.» She means:", "choices": ["She can't come tomorrow.", "She doesn't know if she'll come.", "She doesn't know the way."], "answer": "She can't come tomorrow.", "right": "Oui ! In Belgium «savoir» often = «pouvoir».", "wrong": "Belgian «savoir» can mean 'to be able to': «Je ne sais pas venir» = «Je ne peux pas venir»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai oublié mon natel à la maison.", "en": "I forgot my mobile at home.", "note": "Swiss French. France: «portable», Belgium: «GSM», Québec: «cellulaire» or «cell»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "West and Central Africa (Côte d'Ivoire, Cameroun, Sénégal…): «On dit quoi ?» (What's up?), «gâter» = to break or damage («Il a gâté mon téléphone»), «un maquis» (an open-air eatery in Côte d'Ivoire), «s'enjailler» (to have fun, Ivorian slang)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In Abidjan: «Mon petit frère a gâté la télé.»", "choices": ["He broke the TV.", "He spoiled the TV with gifts.", "He turned off the TV."], "answer": "He broke the TV.", "right": "C'est ça ! «gâter» = to break or damage in much of francophone Africa.", "wrong": "In African French «gâter» often means to break or damage: «Il a gâté la télé» = he broke the TV."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word for 'mobile phone' would you hear in Geneva?", "choices": ["natel", "char", "cellulaire"], "answer": "natel", "right": "Parfait ! «natel» is Swiss.", "wrong": "In Switzerland it's «natel». «Cellulaire» is Québec, and «char» means car in Québec."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "These aren't 'mistakes'. They're legitimate norms of French. A C2 speaker understands them all and adapts, the way you'd adapt between British and American English."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: Québec «char, plate, -tu», Belgium «septante, nonante, à tantôt, savoir = pouvoir», Switzerland «natel, huitante», Africa «On dit quoi ?, gâter»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Translate into France French: «Mon char est stationné en face.»", "answer": "Ma voiture est garée en face.", "accept": ["ma voiture est garée en face", "Ma voiture est garée en face", "ma voiture est stationnée en face", "Ma voiture est stationnée en face."], "hint": "car → voiture (feminine!), park → garer.", "right": "Bravo ! «voiture» is feminine, so «garée».", "wrong": "France French: «Ma voiture est garée en face.» Watch the feminine agreement."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Try a Québécois accent! «Tu veux-tu un café ? Il fait frette à matin !» (Want a coffee? It's freezing this morning!)", "fr": "Tu veux-tu un café ? Il fait frette à matin !"}]}, "57-2": {"stageId": "57-2", "title": "Proverbs & Everyday Sayings", "goal": "Understand and use the most common French proverbs and match them to their English equivalents.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Proverbs are wisdom in a nutshell, and they rarely translate word for word. Learn the French image and its English partner together."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Petit à petit, l'oiseau fait son nid.", "en": "Little by little, the bird builds its nest. ≈ Slow and steady wins the race."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Quand le chat n'est pas là, les souris dansent.", "en": "When the cat's away, the mice will play."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your boss is on holiday and the office is partying. Which proverb?", "choices": ["Quand le chat n'est pas là, les souris dansent.", "Petit à petit, l'oiseau fait son nid.", "Mieux vaut tard que jamais."], "answer": "Quand le chat n'est pas là, les souris dansent.", "right": "Ha, exactement !", "wrong": "Boss away, staff partying: «Quand le chat n'est pas là, les souris dansent.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "More classics: «Il ne faut pas vendre la peau de l'ours avant de l'avoir tué» (don't sell the bear's skin before killing it = don't count your chickens), «Qui ne risque rien n'a rien» (nothing ventured, nothing gained), «L'habit ne fait pas le moine» (the habit doesn't make the monk = don't judge a book by its cover)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend is spending his bonus before he's even been promoted.", "choices": ["Il ne faut pas vendre la peau de l'ours avant de l'avoir tué.", "Qui ne risque rien n'a rien.", "Les chiens ne font pas des chats."], "answer": "Il ne faut pas vendre la peau de l'ours avant de l'avoir tué.", "right": "Parfait ! Don't count your chickens.", "wrong": "Celebrating too early = «Il ne faut pas vendre la peau de l'ours avant de l'avoir tué.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Don't judge a book by its cover' ≈", "choices": ["L'habit ne fait pas le moine.", "Pierre qui roule n'amasse pas mousse.", "Mieux vaut tard que jamais."], "answer": "L'habit ne fait pas le moine.", "right": "Bravo ! Appearances can deceive.", "wrong": "The French image is clothing: «L'habit ne fait pas le moine.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "And: «Les chiens ne font pas des chats» (dogs don't make cats = the apple doesn't fall far from the tree), «Mieux vaut tard que jamais» (better late than never), «Pierre qui roule n'amasse pas mousse» (a rolling stone gathers no moss)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Proverbs keep old grammar: «Mieux vaut…» has no «il», and «Qui ne risque rien…» uses «qui» = whoever. Natives also often say just the first half: «Quand le chat n'est pas là…», «Qui ne risque rien…»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Complete: «Qui ne risque rien ___»", "choices": ["n'a rien.", "ne gagne pas.", "a tout."], "answer": "n'a rien.", "right": "Oui ! «Qui ne risque rien n'a rien.»", "wrong": "The fixed form is «Qui ne risque rien n'a rien.» Proverbs don't change."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend's son is just as stubborn as his dad. You smile and say:", "choices": ["Les chiens ne font pas des chats.", "L'habit ne fait pas le moine.", "Petit à petit, l'oiseau fait son nid."], "answer": "Les chiens ne font pas des chats.", "right": "Exactement ! You could also say «Tel père, tel fils».", "wrong": "Like father, like son: «Les chiens ne font pas des chats» (or «Tel père, tel fils»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a enfin rendu son mémoire ? Mieux vaut tard que jamais !", "en": "He finally handed in his dissertation? Better late than never!"}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Two more worth knowing: «Il ne faut pas mettre la charrue avant les bœufs» (don't put the cart before the horse) and «Après la pluie, le beau temps» (every cloud has a silver lining)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: oiseau/nid, chat/souris, peau de l'ours, qui ne risque rien, habit/moine, mieux vaut tard, chiens/chats, pierre qui roule. Use them sparingly, at the right moment, and you'll sound truly native."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the French proverb: Better late than never.", "answer": "Mieux vaut tard que jamais.", "accept": ["mieux vaut tard que jamais", "Mieux vaut tard que jamais", "Mieux vaut tard que jamais !", "il vaut mieux tard que jamais"], "hint": "Starts with «Mieux vaut…».", "right": "Parfait !", "wrong": "«Mieux vaut tard que jamais.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud, with conviction: «Qui ne risque rien n'a rien !»", "fr": "Qui ne risque rien n'a rien !"}]}, "57-3": {"stageId": "57-3", "title": "Euphemisms, Litotes & Understatement", "goal": "Decode French understatement (litotes) and euphemisms in conversation, the news and the workplace.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French loves understatement: say less to mean more. A litote denies the opposite. «C'est pas mal du tout» = it's really good. The most famous one is from Corneille, where Chimène says «Va, je ne te hais point» ('I don't hate you'), meaning 'I love you'."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est pas mal du tout, ton gâteau !", "en": "Your cake is really good!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "It works the other way too. «Ce n'est pas terrible» = it's pretty bad. Here «terrible» means 'great', so «pas terrible» never means 'not terrible'!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A friend says about a restaurant: «C'était pas terrible.» Should you go?", "choices": ["Probably not, it was rather bad.", "Yes, it wasn't bad at all.", "Yes, it was excellent."], "answer": "Probably not, it was rather bad.", "right": "Exactement ! «Pas terrible» = disappointing.", "wrong": "Trap! «Pas terrible» = not great, pretty bad. Skip that restaurant."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il n'est pas très malin.» means…", "choices": ["He's rather stupid.", "He's quite clever.", "He's not very evil."], "answer": "He's rather stupid.", "right": "Oui ! A polite way to say he's not bright.", "wrong": "«malin» = clever or smart, so «pas très malin» is a soft way of saying 'rather stupid'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Everyday litotes: «C'est pas évident» = it's hard, «C'est pas donné» = it's expensive, «C'est pas bête !» = good idea!, «Je ne suis pas contre» = I'd love to."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Cet hôtel, c'est pas donné.»", "choices": ["It's expensive.", "It's cheap.", "It's not available."], "answer": "It's expensive.", "right": "Bravo ! «Pas donné» = far from free.", "wrong": "«donné» = given away, so «pas donné» = expensive."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Euphemisms soften harsh realities. For death: «Il nous a quittés», «Il s'est éteint», and in obituaries «après une longue maladie» (often cancer). In the news: «un plan social» (mass layoffs), «les demandeurs d'emploi» (the unemployed), «les personnes à mobilité réduite» (people with reduced mobility)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Notre collègue nous a quittés la semaine dernière, après une longue maladie.", "en": "Our colleague passed away last week, after a long illness."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A news report says a company has announced «un plan social». That means:", "choices": ["mass layoffs", "a social event", "a welfare benefit"], "answer": "mass layoffs", "right": "Exactement ! A classic corporate euphemism.", "wrong": "«Un plan social» (officially «plan de sauvegarde de l'emploi») = mass redundancies."}, {"t": "say", "text": "At work: «C'est perfectible» (there's room for improvement = it's not good enough), «C'est intéressant…» (polite doubt), «On va y réfléchir» (often a soft no)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your manager says your report is «perfectible». The real message:", "choices": ["It needs work.", "It's perfect.", "It's finished."], "answer": "It needs work.", "right": "Oui ! Politely critical.", "wrong": "«Perfectible» = can be improved, a gentle way of saying it needs more work."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: litotes («pas mal», «pas terrible», «pas donné», «pas bête») and euphemisms («nous a quittés», «plan social», «perfectible»). Always listen for what ISN'T said."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French using a litote (familiar, no «ne»): It's really not bad at all!", "answer": "C'est pas mal du tout !", "accept": ["c'est pas mal du tout", "Ce n'est pas mal du tout !", "ce n'est pas mal du tout", "C'est pas mal du tout!"], "hint": "pas mal + du tout", "right": "Parfait, very French!", "wrong": "«C'est pas mal du tout !» (written: «Ce n'est pas mal du tout !»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say Corneille's famous litote: «Va, je ne te hais point.»", "fr": "Va, je ne te hais point."}]}, "58-1": {"stageId": "58-1", "title": "Subjonctif passé", "goal": "Form the past subjunctive and use it for actions completed before the main verb.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "The «subjonctif passé» = «avoir» or «être» in the present subjunctive + past participle: «que j'aie fini», «que tu sois venu(e)». Use it when the subjunctive action is already completed."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis ravi que tu sois venu.", "en": "I'm delighted you came.", "note": "The coming happened before the delight."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je regrette qu'elle ___ partie si tôt.»", "choices": ["soit", "ait", "est"], "answer": "soit", "right": "Parfait ! «partir» takes «être» → «qu'elle soit partie».", "wrong": "«partir» uses «être», and after «regretter que» you need the subjunctive: «qu'elle soit partie»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Compare: «Je suis content que tu viennes» (now or in the future) vs «Je suis content que tu sois venu» (already done)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I'm sorry you had to wait.' (The waiting is over.)", "choices": ["Je suis désolé que vous attendiez.", "Je suis désolé que vous ayez attendu.", "Je suis désolé que vous avez attendu."], "answer": "Je suis désolé que vous ayez attendu.", "right": "Bravo ! It's finished, so you need the past subjunctive.", "wrong": "The wait is over, so: «que vous ayez attendu». «avez» is indicative, which is wrong after «désolé que»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Know the auxiliaries cold. «avoir»: que j'aie, tu aies, il ait, nous ayons, vous ayez, ils aient. «être»: que je sois, tu sois, il soit, nous soyons, vous soyez, ils soient."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Spelling traps: «que j'aie» (with -e) sounds like «j'ai», and «qu'il ait» ends in -t. Never write «ayions» or «soyions»: no «i» after the «y»!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Bien qu'il ___ beaucoup travaillé, il a échoué.»", "choices": ["a", "ait", "aie"], "answer": "ait", "right": "Exactement ! «bien que» + subjunctive, «il» → «ait».", "wrong": "«bien que» always takes the subjunctive, and for «il» it's «ait»: «Bien qu'il ait beaucoup travaillé…»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Same triggers as the present subjunctive: emotion, doubt («Je ne crois pas qu'ils aient compris»), «bien que», «pourvu que», superlatives («le meilleur film que j'aie jamais vu») and deadlines («Il faut que tu aies terminé avant midi»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«C'est le plus beau pays que nous ___ visité.»", "choices": ["ayons", "avons", "ayions"], "answer": "ayons", "right": "Parfait ! A superlative + «que» takes the subjunctive in careful French.", "wrong": "After a superlative, use the subjunctive: «que nous ayons visité». «ayions» doesn't exist."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je ne pense pas qu'ils ___ arrivés.»", "choices": ["soient", "aient", "sont"], "answer": "soient", "right": "Oui ! Negative opinion → subjunctive. «arriver» → «être».", "wrong": "«Je ne pense pas que» + subjunctive, and «arriver» uses «être»: «qu'ils soient arrivés»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pourvu qu'il ne se soit rien passé !", "en": "Let's hope nothing has happened!", "note": "Pronominal verbs take «être»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «aie/sois» + participle for completed actions after subjunctive triggers. Strictly, «après que» takes the indicative («après qu'il est parti»), though many natives use the subjunctive there."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: I'm delighted that you came. (to a woman, tu)", "answer": "Je suis ravi que tu sois venue.", "accept": ["je suis ravi que tu sois venue", "je suis ravie que tu sois venue", "Je suis ravie que tu sois venue.", "Je suis ravi que tu sois venue"], "hint": "ravi(e) que + être subjunctive + venue", "right": "Magnifique ! «sois venue», with feminine agreement.", "wrong": "«Je suis ravi(e) que tu sois venue.» Use «être» because it's «venir», and add the feminine -e."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Bien qu'elle soit arrivée en retard, elle a tout compris.»", "fr": "Bien qu'elle soit arrivée en retard, elle a tout compris."}]}, "58-2": {"stageId": "58-2", "title": "Discours indirect & concordance des temps", "goal": "Report statements, questions and orders in the past, shifting tenses, question words and time markers correctly.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "When the reporting verb is in the past («il a dit», «elle a demandé»), tenses shift back: présent → imparfait, passé composé → plus-que-parfait, futur → conditionnel. Imparfait, plus-que-parfait and conditionnel stay the same."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a dit : « Je viendrai. » → Il a dit qu'il viendrait.", "en": "He said, 'I'll come.' → He said he would come.", "note": "futur → conditionnel"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai faim.» → Elle a dit qu'elle ___ faim.", "choices": ["a", "avait", "aurait"], "answer": "avait", "right": "Parfait ! présent → imparfait.", "wrong": "After a past reporting verb, the present becomes the imparfait: «qu'elle avait faim»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«J'ai tout fini.» → Il a affirmé qu'il ___ tout fini.", "choices": ["a", "avait", "aurait"], "answer": "avait", "right": "Bravo ! passé composé → plus-que-parfait.", "wrong": "The passé composé shifts to the plus-que-parfait: «qu'il avait tout fini»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Questions: yes/no questions → «si». «Qu'est-ce que…?» → «ce que». «Qu'est-ce qui…?» → «ce qui». Other question words stay («où, quand, pourquoi, comment»). No inversion and no «est-ce que» in indirect questions!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Qu'est-ce que tu fais ?» → Il voulait savoir ___ je faisais.", "choices": ["qu'est-ce que", "ce que", "que"], "answer": "ce que", "right": "Exactement ! «qu'est-ce que» → «ce que».", "wrong": "In indirect speech «qu'est-ce que» becomes «ce que»: «Il voulait savoir ce que je faisais.»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu as faim ?» → Elle m'a demandé ___ j'avais faim.", "choices": ["si", "que", "est-ce que"], "answer": "si", "right": "Oui ! Yes/no question → «si».", "wrong": "A yes/no question is reported with «si»: «Elle m'a demandé si j'avais faim.»"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je me demandais ce qui se passait.", "en": "I was wondering what was happening.", "note": "«Qu'est-ce qui se passe ?» → «ce qui se passait»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Orders become «de» + infinitive: «Tais-toi !» → «Elle m'a dit de me taire.» Notice that the pronoun changes too."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Time words shift too: aujourd'hui → ce jour-là, demain → le lendemain, hier → la veille, la semaine prochaine → la semaine suivante, ici → là. In speech, if something is still true, natives often keep the present: «Il m'a dit qu'il vient demain»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je pars demain.» (reported a month later) → Il a dit qu'il partait ___.", "choices": ["demain", "le lendemain", "la veille"], "answer": "le lendemain", "right": "Parfait ! Seen from later, «demain» → «le lendemain».", "wrong": "From a later point of view, «demain» becomes «le lendemain» (the next day). «La veille» = the day before."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Où vas-tu ?» → Il m'a demandé…", "choices": ["où j'allais.", "où allais-je.", "où est-ce que j'allais."], "answer": "où j'allais.", "right": "Bravo ! Normal word order, no inversion.", "wrong": "Indirect questions use normal word order: «Il m'a demandé où j'allais.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: shift the tenses back, use «si / ce que / ce qui», normal word order, orders → «de» + infinitive, and adjust time words (le lendemain, la veille)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Report in French: «Je viendrai.» → He said that he would come.", "answer": "Il a dit qu'il viendrait.", "accept": ["il a dit qu'il viendrait", "Il a dit qu'il viendrait"], "hint": "futur → conditionnel", "right": "Impeccable !", "wrong": "«Il a dit qu'il viendrait.» The future «viendrai» becomes the conditional «viendrait»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Elle m'a demandé si j'avais faim et ce que je voulais manger.»", "fr": "Elle m'a demandé si j'avais faim et ce que je voulais manger."}]}, "58-3": {"stageId": "58-3", "title": "Indefinites: tout, chaque, aucun, n'importe…", "goal": "Use tout (adjective, pronoun, adverb), chaque/chacun, aucun and n'importe… correctly, including pronunciation and agreement traps.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "«tout» has three jobs: adjective («tous les jours»), pronoun («Ils sont tous partis») and adverb meaning 'quite, completely' («Il est tout content»)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ils sont tous partis.", "en": "They all left.", "note": "As a pronoun, «tous» has a pronounced s: [tus]. As an adjective («tous les jours») the s is silent: [tu]."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In «Ils sont tous là», is the s of «tous» pronounced?", "choices": ["Yes, [tus]", "No, [tu]", "Only before a vowel"], "answer": "Yes, [tus]", "right": "Exactement ! Pronoun «tous» → [tus].", "wrong": "Here «tous» is a pronoun (= all of them), so you pronounce the s: [tus]."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Adverb «tout» (= very, completely) normally doesn't change: «Elle est tout étonnée». BUT it agrees before a feminine adjective starting with a consonant or aspirated h: «Elle est toute petite», «Elles sont toutes honteuses»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle est ___ contente.»", "choices": ["tout", "toute", "tous"], "answer": "toute", "right": "Bravo ! Feminine adjective starting with a consonant → «toute».", "wrong": "«contente» is feminine and starts with a consonant, so the adverb agrees: «toute contente»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elles sont ___ émues.» (meaning 'very moved')", "choices": ["tout", "toutes", "tous"], "answer": "tout", "right": "Parfait ! Before a vowel the adverb stays «tout».", "wrong": "«émues» starts with a vowel, so the adverb stays «tout». «toutes émues» would mean 'all of them moved'."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Elles sont toutes contentes» is ambiguous: 'they're all happy' or 'they're very happy'. Context decides."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«chaque» is an adjective, always singular and always before a noun: «chaque jour». «chacun / chacune» is the pronoun: «Ces livres coûtent 10 € chacun». Never end a sentence with «chaque»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ces billets coûtent 20 € ___.»", "choices": ["chaque", "chacun", "chacuns"], "answer": "chacun", "right": "Oui ! A pronoun is needed → «chacun».", "wrong": "No noun follows, so you need the pronoun «chacun». «Chaque» must be followed by a noun."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«aucun(e)» goes with «ne» and is almost always singular: «Je n'ai aucune idée», «Aucun élève n'est venu». Never add «pas»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je n'ai ___ idée.»", "choices": ["aucune", "aucunes", "pas aucune"], "answer": "aucune", "right": "Exactement ! Singular, feminine, no «pas».", "wrong": "«aucun» is singular and replaces «pas»: «Je n'ai aucune idée.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«n'importe qui / quoi / où / quand / comment» = anyone, anything… or 'just any', often with a negative tone: «Ne parle pas à n'importe qui», «Tu dis n'importe quoi !». Formal register: «quiconque» = whoever, and «quelconque» after a noun = some… or other, or mediocre («un prétexte quelconque»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend is talking nonsense. You say:", "choices": ["Tu dis n'importe quoi !", "Tu dis quiconque !", "Tu dis quelque chose quelconque !"], "answer": "Tu dis n'importe quoi !", "right": "Ha, oui ! Very common and very French.", "wrong": "'You're talking nonsense' = «Tu dis n'importe quoi !»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «tous» [tus] as a pronoun, adverb «tout/toute», «chaque» + noun vs «chacun», «aucun» + ne (singular), «n'importe qui/quoi», and formal «quiconque»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: They (women) all left.", "answer": "Elles sont toutes parties.", "accept": ["elles sont toutes parties", "Elles sont toutes parties"], "hint": "être + toutes + participle with feminine plural agreement.", "right": "Parfait ! «toutes parties», with double agreement.", "wrong": "«Elles sont toutes parties.» Both «toutes» and «parties» are feminine plural."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Chacun fait ce qu'il veut, mais pas n'importe comment.»", "fr": "Chacun fait ce qu'il veut, mais pas n'importe comment."}]}, "58-4": {"stageId": "58-4", "title": "Idiomatic Pronominal Verbs", "goal": "Understand and use idiomatic pronominal verbs with en and y (s'en aller, s'en sortir, s'en faire, s'en vouloir, s'y prendre…).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Add «se» + «en» or «y» to a verb and you often get a whole new meaning. Learn these as fixed chunks."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The key ones: «s'en aller» (to leave), «s'en sortir» (to manage, pull through), «s'en faire» (to worry), «s'en vouloir» (to blame yourself), «s'en prendre à qqn» (to lash out at someone), «s'y prendre» (to go about it), «se douter de» (to suspect), «se faire avoir» (to be fooled, ripped off)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ne t'en fais pas, tu vas t'en sortir.", "en": "Don't worry, you'll manage."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Don't worry!' (to a friend)", "choices": ["Ne t'en fais pas !", "Ne te fais pas !", "Ne t'y fais pas !"], "answer": "Ne t'en fais pas !", "right": "Parfait ! «s'en faire» = to worry.", "wrong": "The idiom is «s'en faire»: «Ne t'en fais pas !» (casual: «T'en fais pas !»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«se douter» means to suspect that something IS true, almost the opposite of «douter»! «Je m'en doutais» = I thought as much. «J'en doute» = I doubt it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'I thought as much!'", "choices": ["Je m'en doutais !", "Je doutais !", "J'en doutais !"], "answer": "Je m'en doutais !", "right": "Exactement ! «se douter de» = to suspect.", "wrong": "«Je m'en doutais !» = I figured. «J'en doutais» means the opposite: I doubted it."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je m'en veux de ne pas l'avoir appelé.", "en": "I'm kicking myself for not calling him.", "note": "«en vouloir à qqn» = to hold a grudge against someone: «Tu m'en veux ?»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'Why are you taking it out on me? It's not my fault!'", "choices": ["Pourquoi tu t'en prends à moi ?", "Pourquoi tu t'en vas de moi ?", "Pourquoi tu t'y prends à moi ?"], "answer": "Pourquoi tu t'en prends à moi ?", "right": "Bravo ! «s'en prendre à qqn» = to lash out at someone.", "wrong": "Lashing out at someone = «s'en prendre à quelqu'un»: «Pourquoi tu t'en prends à moi ?»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«s'y prendre» = to go about it: «Je ne sais pas comment m'y prendre», «Il s'y prend mal». «se faire avoir» = to get conned: «Je me suis fait avoir !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "You paid €15 for a coffee in a tourist trap (you're a woman):", "choices": ["Je me suis fait avoir.", "Je me suis faite avoir.", "Je m'en suis fait."], "answer": "Je me suis fait avoir.", "right": "Oui ! «fait» + infinitive never agrees, even for a woman.", "wrong": "«fait» followed by an infinitive stays invariable: «Je me suis fait avoir», even when a woman says it."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "In the passé composé, «en» goes before the auxiliary: «Il s'en est allé», «Elle s'en est sortie». In the imperative: «Va-t'en !», «Allez-vous-en !», «Ne t'en va pas !»"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Tell someone (tu) to go away:", "choices": ["Va-t'en !", "Va-toi-en !", "Vas-en-toi !"], "answer": "Va-t'en !", "right": "Parfait ! «te» → «t'» before «en».", "wrong": "«s'en aller» in the imperative: «Va-t'en !» («te» elides to «t'»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «s'en aller, s'en sortir, s'en faire, s'en vouloir, s'en prendre à, s'y prendre, se douter de, se faire avoir». These chunks make your French sound native."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: She pulled through. (s'en sortir, passé composé)", "answer": "Elle s'en est sortie.", "accept": ["elle s'en est sortie", "Elle s'en est sortie"], "hint": "s'en + être + participle agreeing with elle.", "right": "Bravo ! «s'en est sortie», with feminine agreement.", "wrong": "«Elle s'en est sortie.» Use «être», put «en» before the auxiliary, and make it agree: «sortie»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je m'en veux, je me suis fait avoir comme un débutant.»", "fr": "Je m'en veux, je me suis fait avoir comme un débutant."}]}, "58-5": {"stageId": "58-5", "title": "Word Families & Derivation", "goal": "Use common prefixes and suffixes to build and decode French words, and predict their gender.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French builds words like Lego: root + prefix + suffix. Learn the pieces and your vocabulary multiplies."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Negative prefixes: «in-» becomes «im-» before b, p, m («impossible»), «il-» before l («illisible»), «ir-» before r («irremplaçable»). «dé-/dés-» undoes: «faire → défaire», «habiller → déshabiller». «re-/r-/ré-» = again: «faire → refaire», «ouvrir → rouvrir», «écrire → réécrire»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ton écriture est illisible !", "en": "Your handwriting is illegible!"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The opposite of «lisible»:", "choices": ["illisible", "inlisible", "délisible"], "answer": "illisible", "right": "Parfait ! «in-» becomes «il-» before l.", "wrong": "Before l, «in-» becomes «il-»: «illisible»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "'to reopen'", "choices": ["rouvrir", "réouvrir", "reouvrir"], "answer": "rouvrir", "right": "Bravo ! Before a vowel, «re-» often shrinks to «r-».", "wrong": "The verb is «rouvrir» (like «rentrer», «rallumer»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "A fun irregularity: the verb is «rouvrir», but the noun is «la réouverture». Language isn't always logical!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Noun suffixes reveal gender: «-ment» and «-age» are masculine («le licenciement», «le bricolage»). «-ité/-té», «-tion», «-ure» and quality nouns in «-eur» are feminine («la faisabilité», «l'ouverture», «la blancheur», «la longueur»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Nouns formed with the suffixes «-age» and «-ment» (le bricolage, le licenciement) are usually…", "choices": ["masculine", "feminine", "either"], "answer": "masculine", "right": "Exactement ! A great shortcut for gender.", "wrong": "Suffix «-age» and «-ment» → masculine. Beware: «la page, la plage, l'image, la cage» aren't built with that suffix, which is why they're feminine."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The noun for the quality of «doux»:", "choices": ["la douceur", "le doucement", "la doucité"], "answer": "la douceur", "right": "Oui ! Quality noun in «-eur», feminine.", "wrong": "Quality nouns often take «-eur»: «doux → la douceur», like «blanc → la blancheur»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "The noun from «faisable»:", "choices": ["la faisabilité", "le faisement", "la faisableté"], "answer": "la faisabilité", "right": "Parfait ! «-able» → «-abilité».", "wrong": "«-able» adjectives give «-abilité» nouns: «faisable → la faisabilité»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Adjective suffixes: «-able / -ible» = can be done («lavable», «buvable»), «-eux» = full of («courage → courageux»). And adverbs add «-ment» to the feminine: «lente → lentement»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ce projet est irréalisable : personne n'a étudié sa faisabilité.", "en": "This project is unfeasible: nobody studied its feasibility."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«défaire sa valise» means…", "choices": ["to unpack your suitcase", "to pack your suitcase", "to lose your suitcase"], "answer": "to unpack your suitcase", "right": "Bravo ! «dé-» undoes «faire sa valise» (to pack).", "wrong": "«faire sa valise» = to pack, so «défaire sa valise» = to unpack."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «il-/im-/ir-/in-» for negation, «dé-» to undo, «re-/r-» for again. «-ment / -age» = masculine, «-ité / -eur / -tion» = feminine. Root + piece = a new word!"}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write the French adjective: irreplaceable (masculine).", "answer": "irremplaçable", "accept": ["irremplaçable", "Irremplaçable"], "hint": "ir- + remplaçable (don't forget the cédille).", "right": "Parfait, cédille included !", "wrong": "«irremplaçable»: «ir-» + «remplaçable», with a double r and a «ç»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Mon voisin est irremplaçable : il adore le bricolage !»", "fr": "Mon voisin est irremplaçable : il adore le bricolage !"}]}, "59-1": {"stageId": "59-1", "title": "Passé simple: recognising it in reading", "goal": "Recognise the passé simple in novels, history books and tales, identify the infinitive behind tricky forms, and convert it to the spoken passé composé.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Welcome to literary French! The «passé simple» is the past tense of written narrative: novels, fairy tales, biographies, history books. You almost never need to speak it, but you must recognise it instantly. It does the job of the «passé composé»: completed events in a story. The «imparfait» still paints the background."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le roi entra, regarda la foule et sourit.", "en": "The king came in, looked at the crowd and smiled.", "note": "Three passé simple forms: entrer → entra, regarder → regarda, sourire → sourit."}, {"t": "say", "text": "There are three ending families. -er verbs take -a: «il parla, ils parlèrent». Most -ir/-re verbs take -i: «il finit, il prit, ils prirent». A third group takes -u: «il fut, il eut, il put, il voulut». And «venir/tenir» have their own: «il vint, ils vinrent»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Focus on «il/elle» and «ils/elles»: that's 95% of what you'll meet in books. Endings like «-èrent», «-irent», «-urent», «-inrent» scream passé simple."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle mourut en 1903.» What does «mourut» mean?", "choices": ["she was dying", "she died", "she will die"], "answer": "she died", "right": "Exactly! «mourut» is the passé simple of «mourir»: a single completed event.", "wrong": "«mourut» is the passé simple of «mourir» → «she died». «she was dying» would be the imparfait «elle mourait»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The hardest forms look nothing like their infinitive. Learn them as a set: «il fut» (être), «il eut» (avoir), «il fit» (faire), «il naquit» (naître), «il vit» (voir), «il mit» (mettre), «il dut» (devoir), «il sut» (savoir)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ils eurent beaucoup d'enfants.» What is the infinitive of «eurent»?", "choices": ["être", "avoir", "aller"], "answer": "avoir", "right": "Yes! «ils eurent» = they had. It's the famous fairy-tale ending: «Ils vécurent heureux et eurent beaucoup d'enfants».", "wrong": "«eurent» comes from «avoir» (il eut, ils eurent). «être» gives «il fut, ils furent»; «aller» gives «il alla, ils allèrent»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Watch the look-alikes. «il vit» = he saw (voir, passé simple) OR he lives (vivre, present). «il finit» is identical in the present and the passé simple. And «il fut» (he was) must not be confused with «qu'il fût» with a circumflex, which is the imperfect subjunctive. Context decides."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Victor Hugo naquit à Besançon en 1802.", "en": "Victor Hugo was born in Besançon in 1802.", "note": "naître → il naquit. Biographies love the passé simple."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which of these is in the passé simple?", "choices": ["ils parlaient", "ils parlèrent", "ils ont parlé"], "answer": "ils parlèrent", "right": "Bravo! «-èrent» is the plural passé simple ending of -er verbs.", "wrong": "«ils parlèrent» is the passé simple. «ils parlaient» is the imparfait and «ils ont parlé» is the passé composé."}, {"t": "say", "text": "In a story, imparfait and passé simple work as a team, just like imparfait and passé composé in speech: the imparfait sets the scene, the passé simple moves the action forward."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il pleuvait quand elle arriva.", "en": "It was raining when she arrived.", "note": "Background (pleuvait) + event (arriva)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Telling a friend about it out loud, how would a French person say «Elle arriva à midi»?", "choices": ["Elle arrivait à midi.", "Elle est arrivée à midi.", "Elle arriva à midi."], "answer": "Elle est arrivée à midi.", "right": "Parfait! In conversation, the passé composé replaces the passé simple.", "wrong": "In speech, French uses the passé composé: «Elle est arrivée à midi.» The passé simple sounds bookish (or deliberately funny) out loud, and «arrivait» would mean she used to arrive / was arriving."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ils vinrent nous voir.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["They came to see us.", "They will come to see us.", "They sold us."], "answer": "They came to see us.", "right": "Yes! «vinrent» is the passé simple of «venir».", "wrong": "«ils vinrent» = they came (venir). «They sold us» would be «ils nous vendirent»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: the passé simple is the narrative past of writing. Spot the endings -a/-èrent, -it/-irent, -ut/-urent, -int/-inrent, learn the irregulars (fut, eut, fit, naquit, vit, mit), and in speech switch to the passé composé."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Rewrite in the passé composé: «Il fit ses devoirs.»", "answer": "Il a fait ses devoirs.", "accept": ["il a fait ses devoirs", "Il a fait ses devoirs"], "hint": "fit comes from faire", "right": "Excellent! «il fit» → «il a fait».", "wrong": "«fit» is the passé simple of «faire», so the passé composé is «Il a fait ses devoirs.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Victor Hugo naquit à Besançon en 1802.»", "fr": "Victor Hugo naquit à Besançon en 1802."}]}, "59-2": {"stageId": "59-2", "title": "Nominalisation & logical connectors", "goal": "Turn verbs and adjectives into nouns the way French journalists and academics do, and link ideas precisely with formal connectors.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Formal written French loves nouns. Instead of «Les prix augmentent», a journalist writes «l'augmentation des prix» or «la hausse des prix». This is «la nominalisation»: it makes sentences compact, objective and easy to put in a headline."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "La hausse des prix inquiète les ménages.", "en": "The rise in prices worries households.", "note": "hausser → la hausse; its opposite is la baisse."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The main suffixes: «-tion/-ation» (augmenter → l'augmentation), «-ment» (licencier → le licenciement), «-ure» (fermer → la fermeture), «-age» (nettoyer → le nettoyage), and sometimes no suffix at all (refuser → le refus, baisser → la baisse). For adjectives: «-ité» (rapide → la rapidité), «-esse» (faible → la faiblesse), «-eur» (long → la longueur)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Nominalise «Les usines ferment.»", "choices": ["la fermation des usines", "la fermeture des usines", "le fermement des usines"], "answer": "la fermeture des usines", "right": "Yes! «fermer» → «la fermeture». Each verb has its own noun, so learn them in pairs.", "wrong": "The noun of «fermer» is «la fermeture»: «la fermeture des usines». «fermation» doesn't exist, and «fermement» is an adverb (firmly)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "When you nominalise, the subject becomes a complement with «de»: «Le ministre démissionne» → «la démission du ministre». Don't forget the contractions «du» and «des»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le gouvernement veut réformer les retraites.» Which noun phrase is correct?", "choices": ["la réformation des retraites", "la réforme des retraites", "le réformement des retraites"], "answer": "la réforme des retraites", "right": "Perfect! «réformer» → «la réforme», a word you'll see in every French newspaper.", "wrong": "The noun is «la réforme»: «la réforme des retraites». «la Réformation» only refers to the Protestant Reformation."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the connectors, the joints of an argument. Contrast: «en revanche», «néanmoins», «pourtant», «certes…, mais…». Cause: «dans la mesure où» (insofar as, since), «étant donné que». Consequence: «d'où» + noun (hence), «si bien que» + clause (so that, with the result that), «par conséquent»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Certes, le projet coûte cher, mais il créera des emplois.", "en": "Admittedly, the project is expensive, but it will create jobs.", "note": "«Certes» concedes a point before you counter it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les ventes ont chuté, ___ la fermeture de deux magasins.»", "choices": ["d'où", "si bien que", "néanmoins"], "answer": "d'où", "right": "Well done! «d'où» + noun = hence. It's perfect before a nominalisation.", "wrong": "Before a noun, use «d'où»: «d'où la fermeture de deux magasins». «si bien que» needs a full clause: «si bien que deux magasins ont fermé»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "English «so that» hides two different ideas. Result → «si bien que» + indicative: «si bien que la route a été coupée». Purpose → «pour que / afin que» + subjunctive: «pour que tout le monde comprenne»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a beaucoup plu, si bien que la route a été coupée.", "en": "It rained heavily, so the road was closed.", "note": "Result: indicative after «si bien que»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Paris est très cher. ___, la province offre une meilleure qualité de vie.»", "choices": ["En revanche", "D'où", "Dans la mesure où"], "answer": "En revanche", "right": "Exactly! «En revanche» contrasts two facts: on the other hand.", "wrong": "You need contrast here: «En revanche» = on the other hand. «D'où» introduces a consequence, and «dans la mesure où» introduces a cause."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «dans la mesure où» mean?", "choices": ["insofar as", "as soon as", "although"], "answer": "insofar as", "right": "Yes! «dans la mesure où» = insofar as / since, followed by the indicative.", "wrong": "«dans la mesure où» means insofar as / since. «as soon as» = «dès que»; «although» = «bien que»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: nominalise with -tion, -ment, -ure, -age, -ité, -esse (or no suffix), put the old subject after «de», and link your ideas with «certes… mais», «en revanche», «néanmoins», «d'où», «si bien que» and «dans la mesure où»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Nominalise this headline: «Le chômage augmente.»", "answer": "L'augmentation du chômage", "accept": ["l'augmentation du chômage", "La hausse du chômage", "la hausse du chômage", "L'augmentation du chômage."], "hint": "augmenter → l'aug…; de + le = du", "right": "Très bien! «l'augmentation du chômage» (or «la hausse du chômage»).", "wrong": "«augmenter» → «l'augmentation», and «de + le chômage» → «du chômage»: «L'augmentation du chômage»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Certes, le projet coûte cher, mais il créera des emplois.»", "fr": "Certes, le projet coûte cher, mais il créera des emplois."}]}, "59-3": {"stageId": "59-3", "title": "Journalistic & Press French", "goal": "Decode the conventions of French news writing: the reporting conditional, political metonymies, police vocabulary, inverted source clauses and verbless headlines.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French newspapers have their own code. Four keys: the conditional for unconfirmed information, place names standing for institutions, police and court vocabulary, and compact verbless headlines. Let's crack them."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le suspect aurait pris la fuite.", "en": "The suspect reportedly fled.", "note": "Conditionnel journalistique: information not yet confirmed."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le ministre serait sur le point de démissionner.» What is the journalist saying?", "choices": ["The minister would resign if necessary.", "The minister is reportedly about to resign.", "The minister has resigned."], "answer": "The minister is reportedly about to resign.", "right": "Exactly! In the press, the conditional means reportedly / allegedly, not would.", "wrong": "This is the journalistic conditional: the info is unconfirmed. «serait sur le point de» = is reportedly about to."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Present conditional for a present or future rumour («il serait malade»), past conditional for a past one («il aurait pris la fuite»). English uses «reportedly», «allegedly», «is said to»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Metonymies: «l'Élysée» = the President; «Matignon» = the Prime Minister's office; «Bercy» = the Finance Ministry; «le Quai d'Orsay» = the Foreign Ministry; «la place Beauvau» = the Interior Ministry; «l'Hexagone» = mainland France (because of its six-sided shape)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Bercy table sur une croissance de 1 %.» Who is «Bercy»?", "choices": ["the Finance Ministry", "the Prime Minister's office", "the Senate"], "answer": "the Finance Ministry", "right": "Yes! And «tabler sur» = to count on, to forecast.", "wrong": "«Bercy» is the Finance Ministry (it's in the Bercy district of Paris). The Prime Minister is «Matignon»; the Senate sits at «le palais du Luxembourg»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Police and justice: «interpeller» = to take in for questioning; «placé en garde à vue» = held in police custody; «mis en examen» = formally placed under investigation (roughly, charged); «le bilan» = the toll; «le bilan s'alourdit» = the toll is rising."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Un homme a été interpellé puis placé en garde à vue.", "en": "A man was taken in by police and then held in custody.", "note": "The press loves the passive: the agent (the police) is obvious."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le bilan s'alourdit.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["The death toll is rising.", "The bill is getting heavier.", "The report has been delayed."], "answer": "The death toll is rising.", "right": "Correct! «le bilan» = the toll, «s'alourdir» = to get heavier.", "wrong": "After an accident or disaster, «le bilan» is the toll, and «s'alourdit» = is getting heavier: The death toll is rising."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Sources often come after the information, with inversion: «a-t-on appris de source policière» (police sources said), «indique-t-on à Matignon». Also: «selon nos informations» (according to our sources), «de source proche du dossier» (from a source close to the case)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Trois personnes ont été blessées, a-t-on appris de source policière.", "en": "Three people were injured, police sources said."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Headlines drop the verb and use a colon: «Remaniement : trois ministres sur la sellette» (Reshuffle: three ministers in the hot seat). Topic on the left, news on the right."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Grève : la SNCF annonce un trafic perturbé.» What does the part before the colon give you?", "choices": ["the topic, then the news", "a quotation", "a list"], "answer": "the topic, then the news", "right": "Exactly! «Grève» is the topic; the rest is the news.", "wrong": "In French headlines, the word before the colon is the topic («Grève» = strike), and what follows is the news itself."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: conditional = reportedly; Élysée, Matignon, Bercy, Quai d'Orsay, Beauvau, l'Hexagone; interpellé, garde à vue, mis en examen, bilan; «a-t-on appris»; and «Topic : news» headlines."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write it in press French: «The suspect reportedly fled.»", "answer": "Le suspect aurait pris la fuite.", "accept": ["le suspect aurait pris la fuite", "Le suspect aurait pris la fuite", "Le suspect se serait enfui.", "le suspect se serait enfui"], "hint": "Past conditional of «prendre la fuite»", "right": "Parfait! The past conditional marks unconfirmed past information.", "wrong": "Use the past conditional: «Le suspect aurait pris la fuite.» (or «se serait enfui»)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Trois personnes ont été blessées, a-t-on appris de source policière.»", "fr": "Trois personnes ont été blessées, a-t-on appris de source policière."}]}, "59-4": {"stageId": "59-4", "title": "Homophones grammaticaux et orthographe de dictée", "goal": "Spell the classic French homophones correctly (a/à, ou/où, -er/-é, leur/leurs, quel que, s'est/c'est, sur/sûr) using quick substitution tests.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In a French dictée, many words sound identical but are spelt differently: «a/à», «ou/où», «-er/-é», «leur/leurs», «ces/ses/c'est/s'est», «quelle/qu'elle», «sur/sûr». Even French natives make these mistakes. The secret weapon: substitution tests."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«a» or «à»? Replace with «avait»: if it works, write «a». «ou» or «où»? Replace with «ou bien»: if it works, write «ou». «-er» or «-é»? Replace with «vendre/vendu»: «Vous devez réserver» → «vous devez vendre» → -er; «j'ai réservé» → «j'ai vendu» → -é."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il a mal à la tête.", "en": "He has a headache.", "note": "«Il avait mal» works → «a». «avait la tête» doesn't → «à»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je dois ___ ce dossier avant midi.» (envoyer)", "choices": ["envoyer", "envoyé", "envoyez"], "answer": "envoyer", "right": "Yes! «Je dois vendre» works, so it's the infinitive: «envoyer».", "wrong": "After «dois», you need an infinitive. Test: «je dois vendre» (not «vendu») → «envoyer»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«leur» before a verb = to them, and it never takes an -s: «Je leur parle». «leur/leurs» before a noun = their, and agrees: «leur fils», «leurs cadeaux». And «qu'elle» = «que + elle»: test with «qu'il»: «Je pense qu'elle a raison» → «qu'il a raison» ✓."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je leur ai donné leurs cadeaux.", "en": "I gave them their presents.", "note": "First «leur» = to them (no s); second = their, plural noun."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«quel que» + «être» in the subjunctive = whatever; «quel» agrees with the subject that follows: «Quel que soit le prix», «Quelle que soit ta décision», «Quelles que soient vos raisons». Written as one word, «quelque(s)» + noun = some: «quelques amis»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ que soient vos raisons, c'est non.»", "choices": ["Quelles", "Quelque", "Qu'elles"], "answer": "Quelles", "right": "Bravo! «quel que» agrees with «raisons» (feminine plural): «Quelles que soient».", "wrong": "Before «soit/soient», write «quel que» in two words, agreeing with the subject: «raisons» is feminine plural → «Quelles que soient vos raisons»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«ces» = these (ceux-là); «ses» = his/her (les siens); «c'est» = cela est; «s'est» = a reflexive verb in the passé composé (elle se trompe → elle s'est trompée). And «sûr» (certain, feminine «sûre») vs «sur» (on)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle ___ trompée de route.»", "choices": ["c'est", "s'est", "ses"], "answer": "s'est", "right": "Exactly! «se tromper» → «elle s'est trompée».", "wrong": "The verb is «se tromper», so the passé composé is «elle s'est trompée». «c'est» = cela est; «ses» = his/her."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis sûr qu'il est sur le toit.", "en": "I'm sure he's on the roof.", "note": "sûr = certain (with circumflex); sur = on."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The famous trap: «Elle s'est rendu compte», never «rendue». «compte» is the direct object placed after the verb, so the participle doesn't agree. Same thing in «Elle s'est lavé les mains»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle s'est ___ compte de son erreur.»", "choices": ["rendu", "rendue", "rendus"], "answer": "rendu", "right": "Superb! «compte» is the direct object, after the verb → no agreement.", "wrong": "In «se rendre compte», «compte» is the direct object, placed after the verb, so the participle stays invariable: «Elle s'est rendu compte»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le jour ___ je l'ai rencontrée…»", "choices": ["ou", "où", "d'ou"], "answer": "où", "right": "Yes! «où» here is the relative pronoun of time (the day when).", "wrong": "Test: «le jour ou bien je l'ai rencontrée» makes no sense → «où», the relative of place AND time: «le jour où»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: a/avait, ou/ou bien, -er/vendre vs -é/vendu, «leur» + verb never with -s, «quel que soit» agrees, «s'est» for reflexive verbs, «sûr» = certain, and «elle s'est rendu compte»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «I gave them their presents.»", "answer": "Je leur ai donné leurs cadeaux.", "accept": ["je leur ai donné leurs cadeaux", "Je leur ai donné leurs cadeaux"], "hint": "leur (to them) + leurs (their, plural)", "right": "Impeccable! «leur» without s before the verb, «leurs» before the plural noun.", "wrong": "«Je leur ai donné leurs cadeaux.» «leur» (to them) never takes -s; «leurs» (their) agrees with «cadeaux»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Quelle que soit ta décision, je te soutiendrai.»", "fr": "Quelle que soit ta décision, je te soutiendrai."}]}, "60-1": {"stageId": "60-1", "title": "Verbes à préposition (penser à/de, manquer à)", "goal": "Use the right preposition after key verbs (penser à/de, manquer à/de, dépendre de, attendre…) and the matching pronouns y, en and lui.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Many French verbs need a specific preposition, and changing it changes the meaning. «penser à» = to think about; «penser de» = to have an opinion of. «manquer à» = to be missed by; «manquer de» = to lack."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je pense souvent à mes grands-parents.", "en": "I often think about my grandparents.", "note": "penser à = have someone/something in mind."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Que penses-tu de son dernier roman ?", "en": "What do you think of his latest novel?", "note": "penser de = give an opinion."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Qu'est-ce que tu penses ___ ce film ?»", "choices": ["à", "de", "sur"], "answer": "de", "right": "Yes! You're asking for an opinion → «penser de».", "wrong": "For an opinion, use «penser de»: «Qu'est-ce que tu penses de ce film ?» «penser à» = to think about, have in mind."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«manquer à» works backwards compared with English: the person or thing you miss is the subject. «Tu me manques» = I miss you (literally: you are missing to me). «Paris me manque» = I miss Paris. «Mes enfants me manquent» = I miss my children."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«I miss my sister.»", "choices": ["Je manque ma sœur.", "Ma sœur me manque.", "Je me manque de ma sœur."], "answer": "Ma sœur me manque.", "right": "Parfait! The one who is missed is the subject: «Ma sœur me manque».", "wrong": "Flip the sentence: the person missed is the subject. «Ma sœur me manque.» «Je manque ma sœur» would mean I miss (fail to catch) my sister, like missing a train."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Ask yourself: who is absent? That's the subject. «Je te manque ?» = Do you miss me? And with a direct object, «manquer» = to miss (fail to catch): «J'ai manqué le train». «manquer de» = to lack: «Il manque de confiance en lui»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Traps for English speakers: no preposition in French but one in English: «attendre» (wait for), «chercher» (look for), «écouter» (listen to), «regarder» (look at), «payer» (pay for). And the opposite: «dépendre de» (depend on), «profiter de» (make the most of), «se souvenir de» (remember), «téléphoner à», «ressembler à», «obéir à»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«I've been waiting for your answer since yesterday.»", "choices": ["J'attends pour ta réponse depuis hier.", "J'attends ta réponse depuis hier.", "J'attends à ta réponse depuis hier."], "answer": "J'attends ta réponse depuis hier.", "right": "Exactly! «attendre» takes a direct object: no «pour».", "wrong": "«attendre» already contains the meaning of for: «J'attends ta réponse depuis hier.» Never «attendre pour»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Pronouns follow the preposition. «à» + thing → «y»: «J'y pense». «de» + thing → «en»: «Je m'en souviens». «à» + person: usually «lui/leur» («Je lui téléphone», «Tu lui manques»), but with «penser à» you keep «à lui / à elle»: «Je pense à elle»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu te souviens de ce voyage ? — Oui, je m'en souviens très bien.", "en": "Do you remember that trip? — Yes, I remember it very well.", "note": "se souvenir de + thing → en."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Tu penses à ton examen ? — Oui, j'___ pense tout le temps.»", "choices": ["y", "en", "lui"], "answer": "y", "right": "Bravo! «penser à» + thing → «y»: «j'y pense».", "wrong": "«à + thing» is replaced by «y»: «j'y pense». «en» replaces «de + thing», and «lui» is for people."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ça dépend ___ toi.»", "choices": ["de", "à", "sur"], "answer": "de", "right": "Yes! «dépendre de», whatever English says.", "wrong": "English says depend on, but French says «dépendre de»: «Ça dépend de toi.»"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «penser à» (think about) vs «penser de» (opinion); «X me manque» = I miss X; «manquer de» = lack; «attendre/chercher/écouter» take no preposition; «dépendre de, profiter de, se souvenir de»; and pronouns y (à + thing), en (de + thing)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «I miss you.» (to a friend, tu)", "answer": "Tu me manques.", "accept": ["tu me manques", "Tu me manques", "Vous me manquez.", "vous me manquez"], "hint": "The person missed is the subject.", "right": "Magnifique! «Tu me manques» – the person missed is the subject.", "wrong": "Reverse the English: «Tu me manques.» (you are missing to me)."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je pense souvent à mes grands-parents, ils me manquent.»", "fr": "Je pense souvent à mes grands-parents, ils me manquent."}]}, "60-2": {"stageId": "60-2", "title": "Le ne explétif", "goal": "Recognise and use the non-negative «ne explétif» after verbs of fear, avant que, à moins que and comparisons, without confusing it with real negation.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Here is a very French subtlety: a «ne» that does NOT negate. «Je crains qu'il ne soit trop tard» means I'm afraid it IS too late. This is the «ne explétif»: optional, elegant, typical of careful speech and writing (soutenu)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je crains qu'il ne soit trop tard.", "en": "I'm afraid it's too late.", "note": "No «pas» → no negation."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Where does it appear? After verbs of fear: «craindre que», «avoir peur que», «de peur que», «de crainte que»; after «avant que» and «à moins que»; after «éviter que» and «empêcher que»; and in comparisons of inequality: «plus… que je ne le pensais»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je crains qu'il ne vienne.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["I'm afraid he will come.", "I'm afraid he won't come.", "I'm afraid he came."], "answer": "I'm afraid he will come.", "right": "Exactly! The lone «ne» is explétif: no negative meaning.", "wrong": "Without «pas», the «ne» is explétif and adds no negation: «Je crains qu'il ne vienne» = I'm afraid he WILL come."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Real negation needs «pas» (or «jamais», «plus»…): «Je crains qu'il ne vienne pas» = I'm afraid he won't come. Rule of thumb: lone «ne» = style; «ne… pas» = negation."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence means «I'm afraid he won't come»?", "choices": ["Je crains qu'il ne vienne.", "Je crains qu'il ne vienne pas.", "Je crains qu'il vienne."], "answer": "Je crains qu'il ne vienne pas.", "right": "Yes! Only «ne… pas» gives a real negation.", "wrong": "For a real negation you need «pas»: «Je crains qu'il ne vienne pas.» The other two both mean I'm afraid he will come."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Rentrons avant qu'il ne fasse nuit.", "en": "Let's go home before it gets dark.", "note": "avant que + subjunctive, with optional ne explétif."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Use it with «avant que», but never with «après que» (which takes the indicative anyway). And careful writers avoid it after «sans que», which is already negative: «Il est parti sans qu'on le sache»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est plus compliqué que je ne le pensais.", "en": "It's more complicated than I thought.", "note": "Comparison + ne explétif + the neutral pronoun «le»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il est plus intelligent qu'il n'en a l'air.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["He's smarter than he looks.", "He's not as smart as he looks.", "He's smart but doesn't look it at all."], "answer": "He's smarter than he looks.", "right": "Bravo! The «ne» after a comparison is explétif.", "wrong": "In comparisons, «ne» is explétif: «plus intelligent qu'il n'en a l'air» = smarter than he looks."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Rentrons avant qu'il fasse nuit.» (without «ne») is…", "choices": ["wrong", "correct, just less formal", "a negation"], "answer": "correct, just less formal", "right": "Exactly! The ne explétif is optional; leaving it out is perfectly correct, just less formal.", "wrong": "The ne explétif is never compulsory. Without it, the sentence is correct and simply sounds more everyday."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "After which conjunction would you NOT use a ne explétif?", "choices": ["à moins que", "de peur que", "bien que"], "answer": "bien que", "right": "Right! «bien que» expresses concession, not fear or prevention: no ne explétif.", "wrong": "«à moins que» and «de peur que» accept the ne explétif. «bien que» (although) never does: «bien qu'il soit tard»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: a lone «ne» after craindre que, avoir peur que, de peur que, avant que, à moins que, éviter que, and in comparisons, is stylistic, never negative. Add «pas» for a real negation. It's optional and marks a polished register."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in formal French: «Unless you'd rather wait…» (vous, with ne explétif)", "answer": "À moins que vous ne préfériez attendre…", "accept": ["à moins que vous ne préfériez attendre", "À moins que vous ne préfériez attendre", "À moins que vous ne préfériez attendre...", "a moins que vous ne préfériez attendre", "À moins que vous préfériez attendre…", "à moins que vous préfériez attendre", "à moins que vous ne préfériez attendre…"], "hint": "à moins que + ne + subjunctive of préférer", "right": "Très élégant! «à moins que vous ne préfériez» – subjunctive plus ne explétif.", "wrong": "«À moins que vous ne préfériez attendre…» – «à moins que» takes the subjunctive (préfériez) and often a ne explétif."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je crains qu'il ne soit trop tard.»", "fr": "Je crains qu'il ne soit trop tard."}]}, "60-3": {"stageId": "60-3", "title": "Les collocations (prendre une décision, nouer des liens)", "goal": "Use natural French word partnerships (verb + noun, noun + adjective) instead of word-for-word English translations.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Collocations are the word pairs natives use automatically. English «make, take, do, have» don't translate one-to-one: in French you «prends une décision» (you don't «make» it), and you «poses une question» (you don't «ask» it). Getting these right is what makes you sound native."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il faut prendre une décision avant vendredi.", "en": "We have to make a decision before Friday."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«to ask a question»", "choices": ["demander une question", "poser une question", "faire une question"], "answer": "poser une question", "right": "Parfait! A question is «posée» in French.", "wrong": "The collocation is «poser une question». «demander» is for asking FOR something: «demander l'heure», «demander un service»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«demander» = to ask for (demander de l'aide, demander pardon). «poser» = to ask a question. «Puis-je vous poser une question ?» is the classic polite formula."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More key pairs: «nouer des liens» (to form bonds), «relever un défi» (to take up a challenge), «tirer des conclusions» (to draw conclusions), «porter plainte» (to press charges), «émettre une hypothèse» (to put forward a hypothesis), «prêter attention» (to pay attention), «commettre une erreur» (to make a mistake, formal)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Pendant son séjour, elle a noué des liens avec les habitants.", "en": "During her stay, she formed bonds with the locals.", "note": "«nouer» = to tie (a knot)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«to draw conclusions»", "choices": ["dessiner des conclusions", "tirer des conclusions", "prendre des conclusions"], "answer": "tirer des conclusions", "right": "Yes! «tirer» = to pull, to draw out.", "wrong": "«to draw» here isn't drawing a picture: it's «tirer des conclusions». «dessiner» is for pictures."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Noun + adjective pairs too, often as intensifiers: «une pluie battante» (pouring rain), «un froid de canard» (freezing cold), «une faim de loup» (ravenous hunger), «une peur bleue» (a terrible fright), «une nuit blanche» (a sleepless night), «un gros fumeur» (a heavy smoker), «un célibataire endurci» (a confirmed bachelor)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«a heavy smoker»", "choices": ["un lourd fumeur", "un gros fumeur", "un fumeur épais"], "answer": "un gros fumeur", "right": "Exactly! «gros» is the natural intensifier here (also «gros buveur», «gros mangeur»).", "wrong": "French says «un gros fumeur» («lourd» doesn't work here). In a formal register: «un fumeur invétéré»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Nous sommes sortis sous une pluie battante.", "en": "We went out in the pouring rain."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«to take up a challenge»", "choices": ["relever un défi", "prendre un défi", "lever un défi"], "answer": "relever un défi", "right": "Bravo! «relever un défi» – also «relever le défi» (to rise to the challenge).", "wrong": "The collocation is «relever un défi». «prendre un défi» is an anglicism."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Learn nouns together with their verb, as a single block: not «décision» alone but «prendre une décision». Keep a notebook column «verb + noun»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«She pressed charges against her neighbour.»", "choices": ["Elle a porté plainte contre son voisin.", "Elle a pressé des charges contre son voisin.", "Elle a fait plainte contre son voisin."], "answer": "Elle a porté plainte contre son voisin.", "right": "Perfect! «porter plainte contre quelqu'un».", "wrong": "In French you «portes plainte»: «Elle a porté plainte contre son voisin.» «presser des charges» is a word-for-word translation."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: prendre une décision, poser une question, nouer des liens, relever un défi, tirer des conclusions, porter plainte, émettre une hypothèse; une pluie battante, une peur bleue, un gros fumeur. Learn them as blocks."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «We must make a decision.»", "answer": "Nous devons prendre une décision.", "accept": ["nous devons prendre une décision", "Nous devons prendre une décision", "On doit prendre une décision.", "on doit prendre une décision", "Il faut prendre une décision.", "il faut prendre une décision"], "hint": "Not «faire»…", "right": "Excellent! «prendre une décision», never «faire».", "wrong": "A decision is taken in French: «Nous devons prendre une décision.»"}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Elle a relevé le défi et noué des liens avec toute l'équipe.»", "fr": "Elle a relevé le défi et noué des liens avec toute l'équipe."}]}, "60-4": {"stageId": "60-4", "title": "Marqueurs discursifs (quand même, pourtant, du coup, d'ailleurs)", "goal": "Use the little words that give spoken French its flavour (quand même, pourtant, du coup, d'ailleurs, en fait, en effet, bref) with the right meaning and register.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "Discourse markers are the little words natives sprinkle everywhere: «quand même», «pourtant», «du coup», «d'ailleurs», «en fait», «en effet», «bref». They don't add facts; they show your attitude and connect ideas. Master them and you instantly sound more French."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«quand même» has two lives. 1) anyway, all the same: despite an obstacle. 2) indignation or insistence: honestly!, come on!, really!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il était malade, mais il est venu quand même.", "en": "He was ill but came anyway.", "note": "Meaning 1: despite that."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Tu aurais pu prévenir, quand même !", "en": "You could have told me, honestly!", "note": "Meaning 2: reproach."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle a beaucoup révisé ; ___, elle a échoué.»", "choices": ["pourtant", "du coup", "d'ailleurs"], "answer": "pourtant", "right": "Yes! «pourtant» = yet, and yet: a surprising contrast.", "wrong": "Revising a lot and failing is a contrast → «pourtant» (yet). «du coup» would mean she failed BECAUSE she revised!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "«du coup» = so, as a result. It's very common, even overused, in everyday speech (familier/courant). In formal writing, prefer «donc», «par conséquent» or «c'est pourquoi»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il y avait grève, du coup j'ai pris un Vélib'.", "en": "There was a strike, so I took a Vélib' (city bike)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a formal cover letter, which replaces «du coup»?", "choices": ["par conséquent", "quand même", "bref"], "answer": "par conséquent", "right": "Exactly! «par conséquent» expresses consequence in a formal register.", "wrong": "«du coup» is too casual for a formal letter; use «par conséquent» (or «donc», «c'est pourquoi»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False twins! «en fait» = actually, in fact: it corrects or contradicts. «en effet» = indeed: it confirms what was just said. Mixing them up is a classic learner mistake."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Je croyais que c'était lundi. ___, c'était mardi.»", "choices": ["En fait", "En effet", "D'ailleurs"], "answer": "En fait", "right": "Perfect! You're correcting a wrong belief → «en fait».", "wrong": "This corrects a mistaken belief, so use «En fait» (actually). «En effet» would confirm it, which makes no sense here."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«d'ailleurs» adds a supporting point: besides, incidentally, in fact. «bref» wraps up a story: anyway, long story short."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je le connais mal ; d'ailleurs, je ne l'ai vu qu'une fois.", "en": "I barely know him; in fact, I've only met him once."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le train était en retard, il pleuvait, j'avais oublié mon parapluie… ___, une journée horrible.»", "choices": ["Bref", "Pourtant", "En effet"], "answer": "Bref", "right": "Yes! «Bref» sums up: long story short.", "wrong": "After a list of details, «Bref» sums it all up: long story short, a horrible day."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: quand même = anyway / honestly!; pourtant = yet; du coup = so (casual); d'ailleurs = besides; en fait = actually (correction); en effet = indeed (confirmation); bref = long story short."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «He was ill but he came anyway.»", "answer": "Il était malade, mais il est venu quand même.", "accept": ["il était malade, mais il est venu quand même", "il était malade mais il est venu quand même", "Il était malade mais il est venu quand même.", "Il était malade, mais il est venu quand même"], "hint": "anyway = quand même, at the end", "right": "Bravo! «quand même» at the end = anyway.", "wrong": "«Il était malade, mais il est venu quand même.» Imparfait for the state, passé composé for the event, «quand même» at the end."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «En fait, je pensais que c'était demain. Bref, on a raté le train.»", "fr": "En fait, je pensais que c'était demain. Bref, on a raté le train."}]}, "60-5": {"stageId": "60-5", "title": "Precise Emotions & Character Vocabulary", "goal": "Express precise feelings and personality traits (dépité, désemparé, excédé, navré, rancunier, susceptible, pudique, blasé) and avoid false friends.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "At C2 level, «content», «triste» and «fâché» are no longer enough. French has precise words for the shades of feeling. Let's start with emotions: «dépité» (crestfallen and vexed after a letdown), «désemparé» (helpless, at a loss), «excédé» (exasperated, at the end of one's tether), «navré» (deeply sorry, formal), «blasé» (jaded, unimpressed)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Après le refus de l'éditeur, il est resté dépité.", "en": "After the publisher turned him down, he was crestfallen.", "note": "«dépité» mixes disappointment and wounded pride."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend has just lost her job and has no idea what to do next. She is…", "choices": ["désemparée", "blasée", "rancunière"], "answer": "désemparée", "right": "Exactly! «désemparé(e)» = helpless, not knowing what to do.", "wrong": "Not knowing what to do = «désemparée». «blasée» = jaded, «rancunière» = holding a grudge."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now personality: «rancunier/rancunière» (holds grudges), «susceptible» (touchy, easily offended), «pudique» (reserved about feelings or the body), «exigeant» (demanding), «taquin» (teasing)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False friends! «susceptible» ≠ susceptible to: it means touchy. «sensible» = sensitive (sensible = «raisonnable»). «pudique» ≠ prudish: it's discreet, reserved; prudish is «pudibond» or «prude»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ne lui fais pas de remarque sur sa coiffure, il est très ___.»", "choices": ["susceptible", "blasé", "navré"], "answer": "susceptible", "right": "Yes! «susceptible» = touchy, easily offended.", "wrong": "Someone who takes offence easily is «susceptible» in French (a false friend!)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je suis navré pour ce contretemps.", "en": "I'm terribly sorry for this setback.", "note": "«navré» is stronger and more formal than «désolé»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a formal email, how do you apologise for a delay?", "choices": ["Je suis navré du retard.", "Je suis blasé du retard.", "Je suis dépité du retard."], "answer": "Je suis navré du retard.", "right": "Parfait! «navré» = deeply sorry, ideal in a formal apology.", "wrong": "Use «navré» to apologise: «Je suis navré du retard.» «dépité» would mean you're the one who is vexed by it!"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Anger has a scale: «agacé» (irritated) → «énervé» (annoyed, wound up) → «excédé» (exasperated) → «hors de moi» (beside myself)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is the strongest?", "choices": ["agacé", "énervé", "excédé"], "answer": "excédé", "right": "Right! «excédé» = pushed beyond the limit.", "wrong": "The scale goes «agacé» < «énervé» < «excédé». «excédé» = at the end of one's tether."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Elle est très pudique : elle parle rarement de ses sentiments.", "en": "She's very reserved: she rarely talks about her feelings."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il n'oublie jamais une offense.» He is very…", "choices": ["rancunier", "pudique", "désemparé"], "answer": "rancunier", "right": "Bravo! «rancunier» comes from «la rancune» (a grudge).", "wrong": "Never forgetting an offence = «rancunier» (from «la rancune», a grudge)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: dépité, désemparé, excédé, navré, blasé for feelings; rancunier, susceptible (touchy!), pudique (reserved, not prudish) for character; and the scale agacé → énervé → excédé → hors de moi."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «She's very touchy.»", "answer": "Elle est très susceptible.", "accept": ["elle est très susceptible", "Elle est très susceptible"], "hint": "A false friend!", "right": "Excellent! «susceptible» = touchy.", "wrong": "«Elle est très susceptible.» – in French, «susceptible» means touchy."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je suis navré, je me sens complètement désemparé.»", "fr": "Je suis navré, je me sens complètement désemparé."}]}, "61-1": {"stageId": "61-1", "title": "Atténuation et stratégies de politesse", "goal": "Soften requests, disagreements and reminders the French way, using the conditional, the imparfait de politesse, hedges and polite formulas.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French politeness relies on softening. Too much directness can sound rude, even when it's perfectly correct. The tools: the conditional («Auriez-vous…»), the imparfait de politesse («Je voulais…»), minimisers («un petit»), hedges («il me semble», «sauf erreur de ma part») and formulas like «je me permets»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je voulais vous demander un petit service.", "en": "I wanted to ask you a small favour.", "note": "The imparfait softens the request; «petit» minimises it."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which request is the most polite?", "choices": ["Rappelez-moi.", "Vous pouvez me rappeler ?", "Auriez-vous l'amabilité de me rappeler ?"], "answer": "Auriez-vous l'amabilité de me rappeler ?", "right": "Exactly! Conditional + «l'amabilité» = maximum politeness.", "wrong": "The most polite is «Auriez-vous l'amabilité de me rappeler ?» – conditional plus inversion plus a courtesy noun. The imperative is the most direct."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To disagree or correct someone without offending: «Il me semble que…» (it seems to me), «Sauf erreur de ma part…» (unless I'm mistaken), «Ce n'est pas tout à fait ce que j'avais en tête» (it's not quite what I had in mind), «Si je puis me permettre…» (if I may), «Je ne suis pas sûr que + subjonctif»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Sauf erreur de ma part, la réunion est à 14 h.", "en": "Unless I'm mistaken, the meeting is at 2 pm.", "note": "You correct someone while taking the blame on yourself."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you politely tell your boss a figure looks wrong?", "choices": ["Ce chiffre est faux.", "Il me semble que ce chiffre est un peu élevé.", "Vous vous trompez sur ce chiffre."], "answer": "Il me semble que ce chiffre est un peu élevé.", "right": "Perfect! «Il me semble» plus «un peu» softens the criticism twice.", "wrong": "Soften it: «Il me semble que ce chiffre est un peu élevé.» The other two are blunt accusations."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«Je me permets de vous relancer» is THE classic email formula for chasing a reply (relancer = to follow up). Also: «Je reviens vers vous concernant…» (I'm getting back to you about…)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je me permets de vous relancer concernant ma demande du 3 mars.", "en": "I'm taking the liberty of following up on my request of 3 March."}, {"t": "say", "text": "French also loves understatement (la litote): «Ce n'est pas terrible» = it's bad; «Ce n'est pas mal» = it's good; and «Ce serait bien de revoir ce passage» = this passage needs redoing. Learn to hear the real message."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your editor writes: «Ce serait bien de revoir ce passage.» What does she mean?", "choices": ["This passage is great.", "This passage needs reworking.", "Rereading this passage would be pleasant."], "answer": "This passage needs reworking.", "right": "Yes! It's a polite way of saying: please rewrite it.", "wrong": "It's a softened instruction: This passage needs reworking. The conditional «ce serait bien» hides a request."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What register is «Si je puis me permettre…»?", "choices": ["familier", "courant", "soutenu"], "answer": "soutenu", "right": "Exactly! «puis» is an old form of «peux», used only in formal language.", "wrong": "It's soutenu: «puis» is a literary form of «peux» («puis-je…?»)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Email sign-offs by formality: «Bien à vous» / «Bien cordialement» (standard professional) → «Je vous prie d'agréer, Madame, Monsieur, l'expression de mes salutations distinguées» (formal letter)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: soften with the conditional, the imparfait de politesse and «un petit»; hedge with «il me semble», «sauf erreur de ma part», «pas tout à fait»; follow up with «je me permets de vous relancer»; and decode understatements."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «Would you be so kind as to call me back?»", "answer": "Auriez-vous l'amabilité de me rappeler ?", "accept": ["auriez-vous l'amabilité de me rappeler", "Auriez-vous l'amabilité de me rappeler?", "Auriez-vous l'amabilité de me rappeler", "Auriez-vous la gentillesse de me rappeler ?", "auriez-vous la gentillesse de me rappeler"], "hint": "Conditional of avoir + l'amabilité", "right": "Très courtois! Conditional plus inversion plus «l'amabilité».", "wrong": "«Auriez-vous l'amabilité de me rappeler ?» – «auriez-vous» is the conditional of «avoir» with inversion."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Si je puis me permettre, ce n'est pas tout à fait ce que j'avais en tête.»", "fr": "Si je puis me permettre, ce n'est pas tout à fait ce que j'avais en tête."}]}, "61-2": {"stageId": "61-2", "title": "Argumentation et prise de parole (exposé, débat)", "goal": "Structure a French-style exposé or debate contribution: problématique, announced plan, concession, objection, examples, floor-holding and conclusion.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French schools teach a very codified way of arguing. An exposé or dissertation has a «problématique» (a guiding question), an announced «plan» in two or three parts, clear transitions, and a conclusion that ends with an «ouverture» (a wider question)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Dans quelle mesure le télétravail transforme-t-il notre rapport au temps ?", "en": "To what extent does remote work transform our relationship with time?", "note": "A typical problématique: «Dans quelle mesure…» plus inversion."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Announce your plan: «Dans un premier temps, nous verrons… ; dans un second temps, nous examinerons… ; enfin…». Transitions: «Ce qui nous amène à nous demander si…» (which leads us to ask whether…)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which phrase opens the first part of an exposé?", "choices": ["Dans un premier temps, nous verrons…", "En définitive…", "Qui plus est…"], "answer": "Dans un premier temps, nous verrons…", "right": "Yes! «Dans un premier temps» = first of all, to begin with.", "wrong": "«Dans un premier temps, nous verrons…» opens part one. «En définitive» concludes; «Qui plus est» adds an argument."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The heart of French argumentation is concession then refutation: «Certes…, mais…», «On pourrait objecter que… Cependant…», «Il n'en demeure pas moins que…» (the fact remains that…). You show you've considered the other side, then beat it."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Certes, le télétravail offre de la souplesse, mais il peut isoler les salariés.", "en": "Admittedly, remote work offers flexibility, but it can isolate employees."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "How do you anticipate an objection?", "choices": ["On pourrait objecter que…", "Prenons l'exemple de…", "En conclusion…"], "answer": "On pourrait objecter que…", "right": "Exactly! «On pourrait objecter que…» = one might object that…", "wrong": "«On pourrait objecter que…» raises the opposing view before you answer it."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To add and illustrate: «Qui plus est» / «De surcroît» (what's more), «Prenons l'exemple de la Finlande», «À titre d'exemple» (by way of example), «C'est notamment le cas de…» (this is particularly the case of…)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Holding the floor in a debate: «Si vous me permettez de terminer…» (if you'll allow me to finish), «Permettez-moi de nuancer» (let me qualify that), «Je ne partage pas votre point de vue». Firm, but always with «vous»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Someone interrupts you in a televised debate. You say:", "choices": ["Si vous me permettez de terminer…", "Tais-toi !", "Bref, quoi."], "answer": "Si vous me permettez de terminer…", "right": "Parfait! Polite but firm: the classic way to keep the floor.", "wrong": "In a debate, stay courteous: «Si vous me permettez de terminer…». «Tais-toi !» is rude and familier."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What does «Qui plus est» mean?", "choices": ["What's more", "Who is more", "However"], "answer": "What's more", "right": "Yes! It adds a stronger argument.", "wrong": "«Qui plus est» = what's more, moreover. It's a fixed expression; «qui» doesn't mean who here."}, {"t": "say", "text": "To conclude: «En définitive», «Pour conclure», «En somme». Then the ouverture: «On peut dès lors se demander si…» (one may therefore wonder whether…)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il n'en demeure pas moins que…» means:", "choices": ["The fact remains that…", "It is less and less true that…", "There is nothing left of…"], "answer": "The fact remains that…", "right": "Bravo! A powerful phrase for coming back to your main point after a concession.", "wrong": "«Il n'en demeure pas moins que…» = the fact remains that… / nevertheless…"}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: problématique («Dans quelle mesure…?»), announced plan («Dans un premier temps…»), concession («Certes… mais»), objection («On pourrait objecter que»), additions («Qui plus est»), floor-holding («Si vous me permettez de terminer»), and a conclusion with an ouverture."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «Admittedly, it's expensive, but it's useful.»", "answer": "Certes, c'est cher, mais c'est utile.", "accept": ["certes, c'est cher, mais c'est utile", "certes c'est cher mais c'est utile", "Certes, c'est cher mais c'est utile.", "Certes, c'est cher, mais c'est utile"], "hint": "Certes…, mais…", "right": "Excellent! The classic concession structure.", "wrong": "«Certes, c'est cher, mais c'est utile.» – «Certes» concedes, «mais» counters."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Dans un premier temps, nous verrons les avantages ; puis nous examinerons les limites.»", "fr": "Dans un premier temps, nous verrons les avantages ; puis nous examinerons les limites."}]}, "61-3": {"stageId": "61-3", "title": "Les registres en triplets (bouffer / manger / se sustenter)", "goal": "Recognise and choose between familier, courant and soutenu equivalents for everyday words.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French registers are sharply separated: familier (friends, slang), courant (neutral, everyday), soutenu (formal, literary). The same idea often has three words. Choosing the wrong one is like wearing a tracksuit to a wedding, or a tuxedo to the beach."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Classic triplets (familier / courant / soutenu): «bouffer / manger / se sustenter», «bosser / travailler / œuvrer», «le fric / l'argent / les fonds», «la bagnole / la voiture / le véhicule», «un bouquin / un livre / un ouvrage»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which word is the familier version of «livre»?", "choices": ["un ouvrage", "un bouquin", "un tome"], "answer": "un bouquin", "right": "Yes! «un bouquin» is everyday slang; hence «un bouquiniste», a second-hand bookseller.", "wrong": "«un bouquin» is the familier word. «un ouvrage» is soutenu, and «un tome» is a volume of a series."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "J'ai pas pigé ce qu'il voulait.", "en": "I didn't get what he wanted.", "note": "Familier: «piger» plus the dropped «ne». Courant: «Je n'ai pas compris». Soutenu: «Je n'ai pas saisi»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Familier is also grammar, not just vocabulary: dropping «ne», «on» for «nous», «ça» for «cela». And soutenu words in casual speech often sound ironic: «se sustenter» is mostly used jokingly."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "In a CV or cover letter, you write:", "choices": ["J'ai bossé trois ans chez Renault.", "J'ai travaillé trois ans chez Renault.", "J'ai œuvré trois ans chez Renault."], "answer": "J'ai travaillé trois ans chez Renault.", "right": "Exactly! Professional writing is courant to soutenu, and «travailler» is the natural choice.", "wrong": "«bosser» is too familier for a CV, and «œuvrer» sounds grandiose (it suits causes: «œuvrer pour la paix»). Use «J'ai travaillé»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "More triplets: «se planter / se tromper / commettre une erreur», «flipper / avoir peur / redouter», «piger / comprendre / saisir», «crever / mourir / décéder»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What register is «redouter»?", "choices": ["familier", "courant", "soutenu"], "answer": "soutenu", "right": "Right! «redouter» = to dread, a formal register.", "wrong": "«redouter» is soutenu. The familier word is «flipper», the courant one is «avoir peur»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il est décédé hier.", "en": "He passed away yesterday.", "note": "«décéder» is formal and administrative; «crever» would be very familier, even offensive."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your friend texts: «Je flippe pour l'exam.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["I'm freaking out about the exam.", "I'm flipping through the exam.", "I'm excited about the exam."], "answer": "I'm freaking out about the exam.", "right": "Yes! «flipper» = to freak out; and «l'exam» is the familier short form of «l'examen».", "wrong": "«flipper» (familier) = to be scared, to freak out: I'm freaking out about the exam."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which is the soutenu equivalent of «le fric»?", "choices": ["les fonds", "la thune", "le blé"], "answer": "les fonds", "right": "Exactly! «les fonds» = funds, formal and financial.", "wrong": "«la thune» and «le blé» are also slang for money! The formal word is «les fonds»."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Slang has many synonyms: money = «fric, thune, blé, oseille»; car = «bagnole, caisse». Recognise them all, but use them only with friends."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: every idea has a familier / courant / soutenu version. Familier with friends, courant almost everywhere, soutenu in formal writing (or for humour). Watch the grammar markers too: a dropped «ne» is familier."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Rewrite in courant register: «J'ai pas pigé.»", "answer": "Je n'ai pas compris.", "accept": ["je n'ai pas compris", "Je n'ai pas compris"], "hint": "Restore the ne and use the neutral verb", "right": "Parfait! Restored «ne» plus the neutral verb «comprendre».", "wrong": "Courant: «Je n'ai pas compris.» – put back the «ne» and replace «piger» with «comprendre»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «En langage soutenu, on ne bouffe pas : on se sustente.»", "fr": "En langage soutenu, on ne bouffe pas : on se sustente."}]}, "61-4": {"stageId": "61-4", "title": "Law, Politics & Society for Debates", "goal": "Understand and use the key vocabulary of French institutions, law-making and social life (projet de loi, 49.3, motion de censure, syndicat, laïcité…).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "To follow a French debate, you need the institutions. Parliament has two chambers: «l'Assemblée nationale» (with «les députés») and «le Sénat» (with «les sénateurs»). The President sits at «l'Élysée», the Prime Minister at «Matignon», and «le Conseil constitutionnel» checks that laws respect the Constitution."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Making laws: «un projet de loi» is a bill from the government; «une proposition de loi» comes from MPs. Parliament «vote» or «adopte» the text, MPs table «des amendements», and the President «promulgue» the law."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A bill introduced by MPs is called…", "choices": ["une proposition de loi", "un projet de loi", "un décret"], "answer": "une proposition de loi", "right": "Exactly! Proposition = from Parliament; projet = from the government.", "wrong": "From MPs: «une proposition de loi». From the government: «un projet de loi». «un décret» is issued by the executive without a parliamentary vote."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The famous «49.3»: article 49, paragraph 3 of the Constitution lets the government pass a text without a vote, unless the Assembly adopts a «motion de censure» (a no-confidence motion), which would bring down the government."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le gouvernement a eu recours au 49.3 pour faire adopter la réforme.", "en": "The government used article 49.3 to get the reform passed.", "note": "«avoir recours à» / «recourir à» = to resort to."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«déposer une motion de censure» means…", "choices": ["to table a motion of no confidence", "to censor a newspaper", "to deposit money in a bank"], "answer": "to table a motion of no confidence", "right": "Yes! «déposer» = to table (a motion, a bill, a complaint).", "wrong": "In politics, «déposer une motion de censure» = to table a motion of no confidence against the government."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Society: «un syndicat» (a trade union), «une grève» (a strike), «faire grève» (to go on strike), «une manifestation», familier «une manif» (a protest), «manifester» (to demonstrate), «la laïcité» (state secularism, based on the 1905 law separating Church and State)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Les syndicats appellent à la grève et à manifester jeudi.", "en": "The unions are calling for a strike and for people to demonstrate on Thursday."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "False friends: «une manifestation» is usually a protest; «un député» is an MP, not a deputy; «la laïcité» has nothing to do with the laity; «voter une loi» = to pass a law."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«a trade union»", "choices": ["une union", "un syndicat", "un syndicalisme"], "answer": "un syndicat", "right": "Parfait! «un syndicat», whose members are «des syndicalistes».", "wrong": "A trade union is «un syndicat». «le syndicalisme» is the movement itself; «une union» is a marriage or alliance."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Who checks that laws comply with the Constitution?", "choices": ["le Conseil constitutionnel", "le Sénat", "Matignon"], "answer": "le Conseil constitutionnel", "right": "Right! Its members are nicknamed «les Sages».", "wrong": "It's «le Conseil constitutionnel» (nicknamed «les Sages»). The Senate votes laws; Matignon is the Prime Minister's office."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Faut-il organiser un référendum sur cette réforme ?", "en": "Should a referendum be held on this reform?"}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is «la laïcité»?", "choices": ["state secularism", "the laziness of the state", "the laity"], "answer": "state secularism", "right": "Exactly! The neutrality of the State towards religions, a central and hotly debated French principle.", "wrong": "«la laïcité» = state secularism: the separation of religion and State (law of 1905)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: Assemblée nationale (députés), Sénat, Élysée, Matignon, Conseil constitutionnel; projet vs proposition de loi; 49.3 and motion de censure; syndicat, grève, manifestation, laïcité, référendum."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «The unions are calling for a strike.»", "answer": "Les syndicats appellent à la grève.", "accept": ["les syndicats appellent à la grève", "Les syndicats appellent à la grève", "Les syndicats appellent à faire grève.", "les syndicats appellent à faire grève"], "hint": "appeler à + la grève", "right": "Excellent! «appeler à la grève» = to call a strike.", "wrong": "«Les syndicats appellent à la grève.» Note the double «l» in «appellent»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le gouvernement a eu recours au 49.3 et l'opposition a déposé une motion de censure.»", "fr": "Le gouvernement a eu recours au 49.3 et l'opposition a déposé une motion de censure."}]}, "61-5": {"stageId": "61-5", "title": "Anglicismes et équivalents officiels", "goal": "Know the official French equivalents of common English borrowings and avoid the most criticised calques (faire sens, postuler pour, supporter une équipe).", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French borrows heavily from English, and official bodies (the Académie française and the Commission d'enrichissement de la langue française) propose French equivalents. There are two kinds of anglicism: borrowed words (email, brainstorming) and calques, where French words copy an English structure («ça fait sens», «supporter une équipe»)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Official equivalents: «un courriel» (e-mail), «un remue-méninges» (brainstorming), «une infox» (fake news), «la date butoir / la date limite» (deadline), «un mot-dièse» (hashtag), «le retour» (feedback), «le covoiturage» (car-sharing)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "What is the official French term for «fake news»?", "choices": ["une infox", "une fausse-news", "une fakeinfo"], "answer": "une infox", "right": "Yes! «infox» = «information» + «intox» (disinformation).", "wrong": "The recommended term is «une infox», a blend of «information» and «intoxication» (in the sense of disinformation)."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Reality check: in France, people say «mail» or «e-mail» far more often than «courriel», which is standard in Québec and in formal French writing. Know both and adapt to context."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Calques criticised in careful French: «Ça fait sens» → «Ça a du sens» or «C'est logique»; «postuler pour un poste» → «postuler à un poste»; «supporter une équipe» → «soutenir une équipe»; «réaliser que» (to realise) → «se rendre compte que»; «Merci pour ton feedback» → «Merci pour ton retour»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Ça a du sens.", "en": "It makes sense.", "note": "«Ça fait sens» is widespread but frowned upon by purists."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which form is recommended in careful French?", "choices": ["Ça fait sens.", "Ça a du sens.", "Ça prend du sens."], "answer": "Ça a du sens.", "right": "Exactly! «avoir du sens» is the traditional French expression.", "wrong": "«Ça fait sens» copies make sense. The recommended form is «Ça a du sens» (or «C'est logique»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«I'm applying for the job.»", "choices": ["Je postule pour le poste.", "Je postule au poste.", "J'applique pour le poste."], "answer": "Je postule au poste.", "right": "Parfait! «postuler à un poste».", "wrong": "The construction is «postuler à»: «Je postule au poste.» «appliquer» never means to apply for a job in French."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«supporter» in French means to tolerate, to put up with: «Je ne le supporte pas» = I can't stand him. So to a purist, «Je supporte le PSG» sounds like «I put up with PSG»! Say «Je soutiens le PSG» or «Je suis supporter du PSG»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Je ne supporte pas le bruit, mais je soutiens mon équipe.", "en": "I can't stand noise, but I support my team."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«deadline»", "choices": ["la date butoir", "la ligne morte", "la date finale"], "answer": "la date butoir", "right": "Yes! «la date butoir» (or «la date limite», «l'échéance»).", "wrong": "Deadline = «la date butoir» or «la date limite». «la ligne morte» is a word-for-word translation that doesn't exist."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Let's have a brainstorming session.»", "choices": ["On va faire un remue-méninges.", "On va faire une tempête de cerveaux.", "On va faire un orage d'idées."], "answer": "On va faire un remue-méninges.", "right": "Bravo! «remuer» = to stir, «les méninges» (familier) = the brain.", "wrong": "The official term is «un remue-méninges» (literally, a stirring of the brains). The others are invented translations."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: courriel, remue-méninges, infox, date butoir, mot-dièse, retour. And avoid the calques: say «ça a du sens», «postuler à», «soutenir une équipe», «se rendre compte que»."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «Thanks for your feedback.» (tu)", "answer": "Merci pour ton retour.", "accept": ["merci pour ton retour", "Merci pour ton retour", "Merci de ton retour.", "merci de ton retour", "Merci pour votre retour.", "merci pour votre retour", "Merci de votre retour."], "hint": "feedback = retour", "right": "Excellent! «retour» is the natural French word for feedback.", "wrong": "«Merci pour ton retour.» – in professional French, feedback = «un retour»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Je postule à ce poste ; la date butoir est vendredi.»", "fr": "Je postule à ce poste ; la date butoir est vendredi."}]}, "62-1": {"stageId": "62-1", "title": "Gérondif, participe présent ou adjectif verbal (fatigant/fatiguant)", "goal": "Distinguish the gérondif, the present participle and the verbal adjective, including the spelling pairs fatiguant/fatigant, convainquant/convaincant and précédant/précédent.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French has three different -ant forms. 1) The «gérondif»: «en + -ant», saying how or when, with the same subject as the main verb. 2) The «participe présent»: a verb form, invariable, often with an object, mostly written. 3) The «adjectif verbal»: a real adjective that agrees, and sometimes has a different spelling!"}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Il s'est cassé la jambe en skiant.", "en": "He broke his leg while skiing.", "note": "Gérondif: simultaneous action, same subject."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«She learnt French by watching films.»", "choices": ["Elle a appris le français en regardant des films.", "Elle a appris le français regardante des films.", "Elle a appris le français par regarder des films."], "answer": "Elle a appris le français en regardant des films.", "right": "Parfait! «en + -ant» expresses the means (by doing).", "wrong": "By + -ing = «en + participe présent»: «en regardant des films». «par + infinitive» doesn't work here."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "«tout en» + gérondif stresses simultaneity or concession: «Tout en reconnaissant ses qualités, je reste sceptique» (while acknowledging his qualities, I remain sceptical)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The participe présent acts like a verb: it's invariable and can take an object (= «qui» + verb): «Les personnes souhaitant participer…» = qui souhaitent participer. The adjectif verbal describes a quality and agrees: «des arguments convaincants», «une journée fatigante»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "The spelling trap: several pairs differ! Participle → adjective: «fatiguant → fatigant», «convainquant → convaincant», «provoquant → provocant», «communiquant → communicant», «précédant → précédent», «différant → différent», «négligeant → négligent», «excellant → excellent»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'est un voyage très fatigant.", "en": "It's a very tiring trip.", "note": "Adjective (with «très»): spelt without «u»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Ses arguments sont très ___.»", "choices": ["convaincants", "convainquants", "convainquant"], "answer": "convaincants", "right": "Excellent! An adjective (after «très»), so it's «-cant» and agrees: «convaincants».", "wrong": "After «sont très», it's an adjective: «convaincants» (spelt with «c», plural agreement). «convainquant» is the invariable participle."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Convainquant ses collègues un à un, elle a fait adopter le projet.", "en": "Convincing her colleagues one by one, she got the project approved.", "note": "Participle with an object («ses collègues») → «-quant», invariable."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«___ tout le monde avec ses questions, il a fini par se taire.»", "choices": ["Fatigant", "Fatiguant", "Fatigants"], "answer": "Fatiguant", "right": "Bravo! It has an object («tout le monde»), so it's the participle: «fatiguant».", "wrong": "It takes an object («tout le monde») = «qui fatiguait tout le monde» → present participle «Fatiguant», with «u», invariable."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The test: can you add «très»? → adjectif verbal («très fatigant»). Can you replace it with «qui + verb» and does it have an object? → participe présent («fatiguant ses amis» = qui fatigue ses amis)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le jour ___, il avait plu.»", "choices": ["précédant", "précédent", "précédents"], "answer": "précédent", "right": "Yes! It's an adjective meaning previous, agreeing with «jour»: «précédent».", "wrong": "Here it's the adjective (the previous day) → «précédent». «précédant» is the participle: «les jours précédant l'élection» (the days preceding the election)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Les personnes ___ participer doivent s'inscrire.»", "choices": ["souhaitant", "souhaitantes", "souhaitants"], "answer": "souhaitant", "right": "Exactly! A participle with a complement (= qui souhaitent): invariable.", "wrong": "«souhaitant participer» = qui souhaitent participer: it's a present participle, so it's invariable: «souhaitant»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: «en + -ant» = gérondif (how/when, same subject); participe présent = verb, invariable, often with an object; adjectif verbal = describes, agrees, and is sometimes spelt differently (fatigant, convaincant, précédent, différent)."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «He broke his leg while skiing.»", "answer": "Il s'est cassé la jambe en skiant.", "accept": ["il s'est cassé la jambe en skiant", "Il s'est cassé la jambe en skiant"], "hint": "se casser la jambe + gérondif of skier", "right": "Superbe! «en skiant» – and note «cassé» doesn't agree, since «la jambe» comes after the verb.", "wrong": "«Il s'est cassé la jambe en skiant.» Gérondif = «en» + «skiant»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Tout en reconnaissant que ses arguments sont convaincants, je reste sceptique.»", "fr": "Tout en reconnaissant que ses arguments sont convaincants, je reste sceptique."}]}, "62-2": {"stageId": "62-2", "title": "Inversion stylistique et subjonctif imparfait (reconnaissance)", "goal": "Recognise literary subject-verb inversion after adverbs (peut-être, aussi, à peine, encore) and understand the imperfect and pluperfect subjunctive in texts.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "In literary and formal French, some adverbs at the start of a sentence trigger subject-verb inversion: «peut-être», «aussi» (meaning therefore), «à peine», «sans doute», «encore», «ainsi», «du moins». It sounds elegant, and you'll meet it constantly in books and the press."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Peut-être a-t-il oublié.", "en": "Perhaps he forgot.", "note": "Courant alternatives: «Il a peut-être oublié» / «Peut-être qu'il a oublié»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Which sentence is NOT correct?", "choices": ["Peut-être a-t-il oublié.", "Peut-être qu'il a oublié.", "Peut-être il a oublié."], "answer": "Peut-être il a oublié.", "right": "Exactly! After an initial «peut-être», you need either inversion or «que».", "wrong": "An initial «peut-être» requires inversion («Peut-être a-t-il oublié») or «que» («Peut-être qu'il a oublié»). «Peut-être il a oublié» is incorrect."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "Big trap: «aussi» at the start of a sentence, followed by inversion, means therefore, not also! «Aussi avons-nous décidé de reporter la réunion» = we therefore decided to postpone the meeting."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Aussi avons-nous décidé de reporter la réunion.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["We therefore decided to postpone the meeting.", "We also decided to postpone the meeting.", "We decided to postpone the meeting as well."], "answer": "We therefore decided to postpone the meeting.", "right": "Bravo! Initial «aussi» + inversion = therefore.", "wrong": "At the start of a sentence with inversion, «aussi» means therefore / so: We therefore decided to postpone the meeting."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "À peine était-elle sortie qu'il se mit à pleuvoir.", "en": "No sooner had she gone out than it started to rain.", "note": "«À peine… que…» = no sooner… than / hardly… when."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Two more: «Encore faut-il…» = still, one has to… (a reservation). And in relative clauses, a noun subject is often placed after the verb for rhythm: «La maison où vécut Victor Hugo se visite» (rather than «où Victor Hugo vécut»)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Encore faut-il en avoir les moyens.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["Still, you need to be able to afford it.", "Again, you need the means.", "You need more money."], "answer": "Still, you need to be able to afford it.", "right": "Yes! «Encore faut-il…» adds a condition or reservation.", "wrong": "«Encore faut-il…» introduces a reservation: Still, you need to be able to afford it. «encore» doesn't mean again here."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Now the «subjonctif imparfait». In classic literature it replaces the present subjunctive when the main verb is in the past. It's built on the passé simple; the most common form is the 3rd person singular with a circumflex: «il fut → qu'il fût», «il eut → qu'il eût», «il vint → qu'il vînt», «il parla → qu'il parlât»."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Bien qu'elle fût fatiguée, elle continua.", "en": "Although she was tired, she carried on.", "note": "Modern French: «Bien qu'elle soit fatiguée, elle a continué»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il fallait qu'il vînt.» What is the modern equivalent?", "choices": ["Il fallait qu'il vienne.", "Il fallait qu'il venait.", "Il faut qu'il vint."], "answer": "Il fallait qu'il vienne.", "right": "Parfait! Today, the present subjunctive replaces the imperfect subjunctive.", "wrong": "Modern French uses the present subjunctive even after a past main verb: «Il fallait qu'il vienne.» «venait» isn't subjunctive."}, {"t": "tip", "text": "The circumflex matters: «il fut» (passé simple, he was) vs «qu'il fût» (imperfect subjunctive). And «Il eût mieux valu» is a pluperfect subjunctive used as a literary past conditional = «Il aurait mieux valu»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Il eût mieux valu se taire.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["It would have been better to keep quiet.", "It was better to keep quiet.", "He had to keep quiet."], "answer": "It would have been better to keep quiet.", "right": "Excellent! «eût valu» = «aurait valu»: the literary past conditional.", "wrong": "«Il eût mieux valu» = «Il aurait mieux valu»: It would have been better to keep quiet."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: initial peut-être, aussi (= therefore), à peine, encore, sans doute → inversion. Imperfect subjunctive = «-ât, -ît, -ût, -înt» with a circumflex (fût, eût, vînt, parlât). Recognise them when reading; in speech, use the present subjunctive."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Modernise: «Bien qu'elle fût fatiguée» →", "answer": "Bien qu'elle soit fatiguée", "accept": ["bien qu'elle soit fatiguée", "Bien qu'elle soit fatiguée.", "Bien qu'elle soit fatiguée"], "hint": "present subjunctive of être", "right": "Très bien! «fût» → «soit».", "wrong": "Replace the imperfect subjunctive with the present subjunctive: «Bien qu'elle soit fatiguée»."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «À peine était-elle sortie qu'il se mit à pleuvoir.»", "fr": "À peine était-elle sortie qu'il se mit à pleuvoir."}]}, "62-3": {"stageId": "62-3", "title": "Humour et jeux de mots (second degré, calembour, contrepèterie)", "goal": "Understand the main forms of French humour (second degré, calembours, les combles, contrepèteries) and talk about someone's sense of humour.", "turns": [{"t": "say", "text": "French humour loves wordplay and irony. Key words: «le second degré» (irony, not meant literally), «le premier degré» (literal), «un calembour» (a pun), «une contrepèterie» (a spoonerism, often with a hidden cheeky meaning), «les combles» (riddle jokes), «un pince-sans-rire» (a deadpan person) and «l'autodérision» (self-mockery)."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "C'était du second degré !", "en": "I was being ironic! / It was tongue-in-cheek!", "note": "What you say when someone takes your joke seriously."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Your colleague took your ironic remark seriously. You sigh:", "choices": ["Il prend tout au premier degré.", "Il prend tout au second degré.", "Il a beaucoup d'esprit."], "answer": "Il prend tout au premier degré.", "right": "Exactly! «au premier degré» = literally, missing the irony.", "wrong": "Someone who misses irony «prend tout au premier degré» (takes everything literally)."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Les combles» are classic riddles: «Quel est le comble pour un… ?» (What's the height of irony for a…?) The answer plays on a double meaning: «Le comble pour un électricien ? Ne pas être au courant.» («le courant» = electric current; «être au courant» = to be in the know)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Le comble pour un jardinier ? Raconter des salades.» Why is it funny?", "choices": ["«raconter des salades» means to tell tall tales, and gardeners grow salad", "gardeners hate salad", "«salade» is slang for money"], "answer": "«raconter des salades» means to tell tall tales, and gardeners grow salad", "right": "Yes! The idiom and the literal meaning collide: that's the whole mechanism of «les combles».", "wrong": "«raconter des salades» is an idiom for telling tall tales, while lettuces grow in a garden. Double meaning = comble."}, {"t": "example", "fr": "Le comble pour un boulanger ? Être dans le pétrin.", "en": "The height of irony for a baker? Being in a fix.", "note": "«le pétrin» = a kneading trough AND, figuratively, a mess."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«Un calembour» is a pun on sounds. French shop names love them: a hairdresser called «L'Hair du temps» (sounds like «l'air du temps», the spirit of the times). It's everywhere in headlines and ads."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "A hairdresser called «L'Hair du temps» is an example of…", "choices": ["un calembour", "une contrepèterie", "un pince-sans-rire"], "answer": "un calembour", "right": "Bravo! A pun based on sound: «hair» / «air».", "wrong": "A play on sounds («L'Hair» / «l'air») is «un calembour»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "«La contrepèterie» swaps sounds between words to reveal a hidden meaning, often naughty, which the listener has to find. It goes back to Rabelais («Femme folle à la messe…») and is a weekly tradition in the satirical paper «Le Canard enchaîné». A clean classic: «Sonnez, trompettes» → «Trompez, sonnettes»."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "Swap the first sounds of «Sonnez, trompettes». You get:", "choices": ["Trompez, sonnettes", "Sonnettes, trompez", "Sonnez, trompez"], "answer": "Trompez, sonnettes", "right": "Exactly! «s-» and «tr-» change places: that's the contrepèterie mechanism.", "wrong": "In a contrepèterie, sounds swap places, not whole words: «Sonnez, trompettes» → «Trompez, sonnettes»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Talking about humour: «un pince-sans-rire» (deadpan), «l'autodérision» (self-deprecating humour), «avoir de l'esprit» (to be witty), «une vanne» (familier: a dig, a joke), «chambrer quelqu'un» (familier: to tease someone)."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«C'est un pince-sans-rire.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["He has a deadpan sense of humour.", "He never laughs because he's sad.", "He pinches people for fun."], "answer": "He has a deadpan sense of humour.", "right": "Yes! He jokes with a perfectly straight face.", "wrong": "«un pince-sans-rire» is someone who makes jokes with a straight face: deadpan humour."}, {"t": "ask", "q": "«Elle a beaucoup d'esprit.» What does it mean?", "choices": ["She's very witty.", "She's very spiritual.", "She has a lot of energy."], "answer": "She's very witty.", "right": "Exactly! «avoir de l'esprit» = to be witty, quick and clever with words.", "wrong": "«avoir de l'esprit» = to be witty. Spiritual = «spirituel(le)» (which can also mean witty!), and energy = «de l'énergie»."}, {"t": "say", "text": "Recap: second degré (ironic) vs premier degré (literal); combles and calembours play on double meanings and sounds; contrepèteries swap sounds; a pince-sans-rire jokes deadpan; avoir de l'esprit = to be witty."}, {"t": "type", "q": "Write in French: «I was being ironic!» (use second degré)", "answer": "C'était du second degré !", "accept": ["c'était du second degré", "C'était du second degré!", "C'était du second degré.", "C'était du second degré"], "hint": "C'était du…", "right": "Parfait! Now nobody can take you au premier degré.", "wrong": "«C'était du second degré !» – the standard way to say you were joking or being ironic."}, {"t": "speak", "text": "Say it out loud: «Le comble pour un boulanger ? Être dans le pétrin.»", "fr": "Le comble pour un boulanger ? Être dans le pétrin."}]}};
